window.WHOLE_FOREST = {"people":[{"after":"he'd set it up before the words went. while he could still speak he used his own failing body as the lesson, told people it was impermanence standing in front of them, and said to find a true refuge inside themselves because he wouldn't be teaching much longer. then he lay there and let them look after him. ajahn pasanno, one of his western monks, said later that he did his grieving while chah was still alive and sick. that's the whole move. a glass you treat as already broken is one you can hold without fear, and he'd said so for years. this was him holding it.\nthere's no turn to report, because there was no fall. the year after he died the king came to the cremation, and a crowd somewhere between four hundred thousand and a million stood in a forest in ubon that had been a fever swamp when he sat down in it. sumedho ran amaravati, the big english monastery, until he retired in 2010. kornfield's centers in massachusetts and california are where a lot of americans first sat down to meditate. the branches are still there, run by the monks he trained and the monks they trained.","basically":"a rice farmer's son from the poor northeast of thailand who became a monk, walked the forests for years, and in 1954 sat down in a malaria swamp near his home village and let a monastery grow up around him. he taught in farm jokes, a man who wants a chicken to be a duck, a dog asleep on a pile of rice it doesn't know is food, and he told people to treat a drinking glass as already broken so they could enjoy it while it lasted. the americans who turned up in the sixties and seventies went home and built things. jack kornfield, who co-founded two of the biggest american meditation centers, was one of his monks, so if you've done a mindfulness course at work some of it came down through this man. think of a village doctor with no website who ends up with branches in a dozen countries because his students opened them.","born":1918,"died":1992,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 296 250 L 296 118 M 348 250 L 348 118'/><path d='M 262 134 C 254 96 290 72 322 84 C 352 62 394 92 384 128 C 396 150 356 166 330 154 C 304 170 262 160 262 134 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 348 190 L 386 190'/><path d='M 36 190 L 36 250 M 92 190 L 92 250'/><path d='M 30 190 L 98 190 L 98 150 L 30 150 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 22 150 L 64 120 L 106 150 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 56 190 L 56 168 L 72 168 L 72 190' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 136 166 Q 150 158 164 166 L 198 250 L 102 250 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='150' cy='150' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='145' cy='148' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='155' cy='148' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 145 155 Q 150 159 155 155' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 138 174 L 118 206'/><path d='M 162 176 L 198 148'/><path d='M 192 124 L 214 124 L 210 150 L 196 150 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 196 136 L 210 136' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='262' cy='182' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='257' cy='180' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='267' cy='180' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 258 188 L 266 188' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 262 196 L 262 232'/><path d='M 262 232 L 240 250 M 262 232 L 284 250'/><path d='M 262 206 L 246 226 M 262 206 L 278 226'/><g transform='translate(356,163) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he said to treat the glass as already broken. then he lay ten years without a word while the monastery filled up around him.","key":"ajahnchah","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajahn_Chah","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forest_Tradition_of_Ajahn_Chah","why":"the branch monasteries, country by country"},{"label":"in simple terms","url":"https://origin.dhammatalks.org/Archive/Writings/Ebooks/InSimpleTerms_251113.pdf","why":"a hundred and eight of his similes, with the glass, in thanissaro bhikkhu's translation"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ajahn_Sumedho","why":"the american who stayed ten years and took it to england"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["asked what let him get as far as he did in the practice, he said: i dared to do it. ajahn pasanno, one of his western monks, tells it.","a westerner asked him about sacred sexuality, whether sex could be part of the path. he picked his nose. ajahn amaro tells it, and the nose was the answer.","jack kornfield tells of him looking around a meditation retreat full of westerners and saying it was like a big hospital, and that he hoped they got well soon.","in 1977 he went to london at the invitation of a buddhist group, looked at how many people wanted this, and went home without sumedho, whom he left there to start a monastery. sumedho ran monasteries in england for the next thirty three years."],"name":"ajahn chah","place":"ubon ratchathani, in the northeast of thailand","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 100 104 102 130 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 18 130 C 20 108 36 98 52 97 L 60 104 L 54 130 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 97 M 68 84 L 68 97'/><path d='M 32 54 C 23 50 23 69 32 67 M 88 54 C 97 50 97 69 88 67'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 38 40 C 44 33 52 31 60 31 C 68 31 76 33 82 40' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 44 50 Q 50 47 56 50 M 64 50 Q 70 47 76 50' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 58 L 57 66 L 63 66' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 48 73 C 54 81 66 81 72 73' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 44 70 Q 46 76 48 78 M 76 70 Q 74 76 72 78' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 86 104 L 106 104 L 102 128 L 90 128 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 90 114 L 103 114' stroke-width='1.8'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"suppose you get a glass. you say, this is mine, and it isn't broken. but whatever can break will break, and the buddha saw this glass as already broken.","where":"the broken glass, in in simple terms, 108 dhamma similes, translated by thanissaro bhikkhu. the first two sentences are exact, the rest is paraphrased"},{"q":"things are simply the way they are. they don't give us suffering.","where":"a thorn, in in simple terms, 108 dhamma similes, translated by thanissaro bhikkhu. the thorn is just a thorn, he goes on; the suffering starts when you step on it"},{"q":"i haven't seen any buddhism yet.","where":"to jack kornfield, who had asked what he thought about buddhism coming to the west. attributed; the story is told by ajahn pasanno and ajahn amaro, two of his western monks, in a 2025 talk recorded at abhayagiri, the branch monastery in california"}],"start":"a still forest pool, the short book jack kornfield and paul breiter made from his talks in 1985, for the voice. then in simple terms, 108 dhamma similes, thanissaro bhikkhu's translation of his farm pictures, which is where the glass is. stillness flowing, ajahn jayasaro's biography, is enormous and free to download, for when you want all of it.","status":"approved","story":"chah chuangchot was born on june 17, 1918, in a farming village near ubon ratchathani, a town in the northeast of thailand, the poor rice growing part of the country. at nine he was put in the local monastery, learned to read and write there, and spent his early teens as a novice, a boy in robes training to be a monk. then he went home to work the farm. on april 26, 1939, at twenty, he was ordained for life and given the monk's name subhaddo. thai buddhism is theravada, the form of buddhism in thailand and the countries around it, and its monks live under the vinaya, the monks' rulebook. he spent his first years studying the scriptures and pali, the ancient indian language they're written in, and passed his exams. then his father got sick. chah nursed him, and his father died, and the question of what any of it was for would not leave him alone. in 1946 he put down the books and walked out.\nhe walked about four hundred kilometers south into central thailand and stayed at a monastery that studied the vinaya, then walked back north to find ajahn mun, the old monk the wandering forest monks of thailand looked to. ajahn means teacher. mun told him the teachings are vast but simple at the heart. get mindfulness going, watch everything arise in the mind, and right there is the path. chah said later it turned him around. for about seven years after that he lived the way the forest monks lived, in jungle with tigers and cobras in it, in caves, and in cremation grounds, the places where villages burned their dead, sitting up at night with his own fear. the story goes that on his first day in one of those grounds a child had just died, and in the night he saw a boy being born in the grave, and in the morning a villager told him the child had been a boy. it's told as a story and that's how to take it.\nin 1954 he settled in a patch of forest near his home village called pah pong, full of malaria and, people said, haunted. monks gathered, and it became wat pah pong, wat being the thai word for a monastery. the training there was hard on purpose. he kept the rulebook to the letter, pushed monks to their limits and handed them long work projects that seemed to have no point, to break their attachment to feeling calm. and he taught in pictures from the farm. one man looks at a chicken and a duck and wants the chicken to be a duck, and suffers. the other sees a chicken as a chicken and a duck as a duck, and that way there's no problem. a hungry dog lies on a pile of unhusked rice and doesn't know it's food, and goes off to eat garbage. a thorn is just a thorn. the suffering starts when you step on it. and the glass. you say the glass is yours and it isn't broken, he'd say, but anything that can break will break, if not now then later, and the [[buddha]] saw this glass as already broken, so when it went he wasn't troubled.\nin 1967 an american turned up. ajahn sumedho, born robert jackman in seattle in 1934, had done four years as a navy medic and two as a peace corps english teacher in borneo, the peace corps being the american program that sends young volunteers abroad, when he saw a monk walk by in singapore and thought, that looks interesting. he was ordained in thailand in may 1967 and came to wat pah pong the same year. chah gave him no breaks for being a foreigner. others followed, among them jack kornfield, who'd come out of college in 1967 straight into the peace corps and medical work in the mekong river valley, became a monk under chah, and went home in 1972. in 1975 chah set the westerners up in a forest nearby, wat pah nanachat, the first monastery in thailand run by and for english speaking monks, with sumedho as abbot. in 1977 a buddhist group in london invited him over. he looked at how many people wanted this and left sumedho and another monk behind to start something. in 1979 he came back for the start of chithurst, a monastery in a house in sussex, and went on to america and canada. in america he kept hammering on the rules of conduct. kornfield, translating, put his own spin on them for the audience, and chah figured it out. kornfield once asked him what he made of buddhism coming to the west. i haven't seen any buddhism yet, he said.\nhis health was going by 1980. the accounts tie it to diabetes. he spent the rains retreats of 1980 and 1981, the three months of monsoon when monks stay in one place, away from wat pah pong, and before the 1981 one was over he was operated on in bangkok. it did little. within months he stopped talking, and then his arms and legs went, until he lay nearly paralysed. on january 20, 1983, he was brought home to ubon after five months of tests in a bangkok hospital, and he stayed in that bed for nine years, nursed by his monks, while the branch monasteries multiplied. he died at twenty past five in the morning on january 16, 1992, at seventy three, when his kidneys and heart gave out. the cremation was a year later, on january 16, 1993, because of the crowds expected. the king and queen of thailand sat in a pavilion built for the day. how many came depends on who's counting. his biographer says four hundred thousand. other accounts say a million. the place he started in a fever swamp now has more than two hundred and fifty branches in thailand and over fifteen abroad.","struggle":"fear came first, and he went looking for it. the cremation grounds were where it lived, and a good part of the seven wandering years went on sitting up at night with it. his biographer ajahn jayasaro, one of his own monks, says people who knew him as a boy remembered an even temper and couldn't recall him in a fight, and that chah himself said later the one craving he had real trouble beating was sexual desire. that's as far as the record goes.\nthen the body. by 1980 he was sick, diabetes by the accounts, and by the end of 1981 he'd had an operation in bangkok that changed nothing. in the months after it he lost his speech, and then his limbs. he told the monks the illness was incurable and that they would have to look after him. for most of ten years he lay in a bed at wat pah pong, a man who had taught by talking, with the talking gone.","threads":["dying","the body","wandering","silence"],"years":"1918 to 1992"},{"after":"there isn't a turning point to be after. she didn't fall and get up. what she did was keep moving, sixty years of trains and ashrams and sitting with whoever came, prime ministers and widows and seekers from france, and treating all of them about the same.\nat the end, in 1982, the accounts say she stopped eating and nearly stopped speaking for months. she died that august. most hindus are cremated. they didn't cremate her. they carried her to kankhal and buried her by the ganges in a shrine, the way hindus bury a saint.","basically":"a village girl from bengal with almost no schooling who went into trances when people sang, and spent sixty years riding trains around india while people everywhere called her mother. a prime minister came to see her and so did widows with nothing, and she treated them about the same. think of the person at a party everybody ends up standing near without knowing why. take from her that joy can be the ground you stand on, even in a life that's run by other people.","born":1896,"died":1982,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 188 116 C 176 124 172 146 166 160 M 212 116 C 224 124 230 146 236 160' stroke-width='4' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 200 180 C 176 200 170 230 176 246 L 226 246 C 232 230 226 200 200 180 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='200' cy='120' r='14'/><path d='M 200 134 L 200 180 M 200 148 L 178 118 M 200 148 L 222 118'/><path d='M 195 125 Q 200 129 205 125'/><path d='M 150 170 C 146 160 152 150 146 140 M 250 170 C 254 160 248 150 254 140' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='70' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 70 192 L 70 234'/><path d='M 70 234 C 50 234 40 250 56 250 L 84 250 C 100 250 90 234 70 234'/><path d='M 70 208 L 56 234 M 70 208 L 84 234'/><rect x='92' y='226' width='50' height='22' rx='10' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 102 226 L 102 248 M 132 226 L 132 248' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='320' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 320 192 L 320 234'/><path d='M 320 234 C 300 234 290 250 306 250 L 334 250 C 350 250 340 234 320 234'/><path d='M 320 208 L 292 218 M 320 208 L 348 218'/><circle cx='290' cy='210' r='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='350' cy='210' r='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 290 196 L 290 190 M 350 196 L 350 190 M 282 200 L 278 196 M 358 200 L 362 196' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(117,205.8) scale(0.75)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a bengali village girl with almost no schooling, married at thirteen, and her husband ended up as her disciple.","key":"anandamayi","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anandamayi_Ma","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/7452","why":"autobiography of a yogi, the first edition; chapter 45 is about her"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"her ashrams kept strict brahmin rules about food, who could cook it and who could eat with whom, and westerners and people of lower caste often ate apart. she didn't change it. put that next to [[nanak]], the founder of the sikhs, whose whole idea was a free kitchen where everybody sits on the same floor and eats the same food.","moments":["the name anandamayi came from her early devotees in dhaka in the twenties. she didn't pick it and didn't use it. she said this body.","yogananda wrote her up as chapter 45 of autobiography of a yogi, his 1946 memoir, and called her the bengali joy-permeated mother. for a lot of americans that chapter was the first they heard of her.","she's photographed laughing more than almost any saint of her century, long hair loose, in plain white."],"name":"anandamayi ma","place":"east bengal, then dhaka, then every train line in india","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i was the same as a little girl. i grew into womanhood, and still i was the same.","where":"to yogananda, in his memoir autobiography of a yogi, chapter 45, paraphrased"},{"q":"this body.","where":"what she nearly always called herself, instead of i. it's in every record of her talks"},{"q":"this body has no plans. it goes where the kheyala takes it.","where":"her usual answer about where she'd go next, paraphrased; kheyala is a bengali word for a sudden, unwilled impulse"}],"start":"autobiography of a yogi, chapter 45, for a first look. then alexander lipski's short life and teaching of sri anandamayi ma.","status":"approved","story":"nirmala sundari was born in 1896 in a village in east bengal, now bangladesh. her family were brahmins, the priest caste. caste is the inherited ranking hindu society was built on, which decided what work you did and who you could eat with, and brahmins were at the top of it, though her family had very little money. she had almost no schooling. at about thirteen, as the custom was, she was married to ramani mohan chakrabarti. they never slept together, and the marriage stayed that way for life. her husband ended up taking initiation from her, the ceremony where you formally take someone as your spiritual teacher. her people called him bholanath, and he followed her for the rest of his life.\nin her twenties she went through years of intense spiritual practice with no teacher at all, and in 1922 initiated herself. then came the kirtan, a room of people singing the names of god over and over to drums and cymbals, faster and louder, closer to a gospel revival than a church choir. when it started she'd go into states nobody could reach her in, body rigid, or swaying, or down on the floor. in dhaka, now bangladesh's capital, people started coming to watch, then to stay. they named her anandamayi ma, the mother full of joy.\nshe never wrote anything and never set up a system. for sixty years she traveled, rarely staying anywhere long, and ashrams, communities built around a teacher, went up where she'd been, in varanasi, the holy city on the ganges, in dehradun in the himalayan foothills, and dozens more places. kamala nehru, the wife of india's first prime minister, was devoted to her, and later their daughter indira gandhi, prime minister herself and no relation to mohandas gandhi, came to her for years.\nin 1936 paramahansa yogananda, the indian monk who'd been teaching yoga in america since 1920, came to see her. he asked about her life, and she told him she'd been the same before she was born, the same as a little girl, the same as a wife, and the same now. she died in 1982 near dehradun and is buried at kankhal, next to haridwar, a holy town where the ganges comes down out of the mountains.","struggle":"in her teens and twenties, in her husband's family's house, she'd go stiff and stop answering. the relatives thought she was sick or possessed, and the story goes they called in exorcists. she was a young wife who wouldn't behave like one, in a house that had expected one.\nfor long stretches later on, the accounts say, her hand wouldn't go to her mouth, and attendants fed her. whatever you make of that, it meant she lived her whole adult life in other people's hands.","threads":["joy","wandering","family"],"years":"1896 to 1982"},{"after":"there's no comeback here and no redemption arc, because there's nothing on record to come back from. as far as anyone can tell he just kept going, the shop, the customers, the poems, in one town, for decades, with no court and no crowd, until the city was destroyed around him.\nthe morning after belonged to other people. lines attributed to [[rumi]], who was a boy of thirteen in 1221, call attar the spirit and sanai, an older sufi poet, its two eyes, and say attar crossed all seven cities of love while rumi was still at the bend of the first street. and ali-shir nava'i, a poet and statesman who wrote in turkish and lived from 1441 to 1501, built him the tomb that still stands in nishapur.","basically":"a persian pharmacist in twelfth century iran, a man behind a counter mixing medicines for a long line of customers, who wrote some of the great poems of the world in between. picture the guy at your local drugstore filling prescriptions all day and quietly writing a masterpiece on his breaks. his big one is about a crowd of birds who go looking for god and end up face to face with themselves. almost nothing about his life is certain, and he probably died when the mongols wiped out his city.","born":1145,"died":1221,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--teal)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='60' cy='52' r='18' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 0 282 C 110 276 190 196 262 138 L 400 136 L 400 300 L 0 300 Z' fill='var(--sage)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 282 C 110 276 190 196 262 138 L 398 136'/><ellipse cx='338' cy='139' rx='44' ry='8' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 314 140 q 4 -3 8 0 M 328 138 q 4 -3 8 0 M 342 141 q 4 -3 8 0 M 356 138 q 4 -3 8 0' stroke-width='1.8' opacity='.7'/><g stroke-width='2.4'><path d='M 269.5 124.5 L 258.2 120.8'/><ellipse cx='282' cy='124.5' rx='13.8' ry='10' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 274.5 120.8 L 284.5 125.8 M 273.2 125.8 L 283.2 130.8' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 290.8 108.2 L 284.5 98.2 M 293.9 107 L 292 95.8 M 297 107.6 L 299.5 97 M 299.5 109.5 L 304.5 100.8'/><circle cx='294.5' cy='113.2' r='6.2' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='296.4' cy='112' r='0.9' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 300.8 113.2 Q 308.2 114.5 312 120.8'/><path d='M 279.5 133.2 L 278.2 137 M 285.8 133.2 L 287 137'/><\/g><g stroke-width='2.4'><path d='M 230 147 L 223 142'/><ellipse cx='240' cy='147' rx='11' ry='8' fill='var(--clay)'/><circle cx='250' cy='139' r='5' fill='var(--clay)'/><circle cx='251.5' cy='138' r='0.8' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 255 139 L 259 140'/><path d='M 238 154 L 237 157 M 243 154 L 244 157'/><\/g><g stroke-width='2.4'><path d='M 202.1 166.8 C 179 140.4 174.6 171.2 201 172.3 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 201 169 L 193.3 163.5'/><ellipse cx='212' cy='169' rx='12.1' ry='8.8' fill='var(--lavender)'/><circle cx='223' cy='160.2' r='5.5' fill='var(--lavender)'/><circle cx='224.7' cy='159.1' r='0.8' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 228.5 160.2 L 232.9 161.3'/><path d='M 209.8 176.7 L 208.7 180 M 215.3 176.7 L 216.4 180'/><\/g><g stroke-width='2.4'><path d='M 175 194 L 168.7 189.5'/><ellipse cx='184' cy='194' rx='9.9' ry='7.2' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='193' cy='186.8' r='4.5' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='194.3' cy='185.9' r='0.8' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 197.5 186.8 L 201.1 187.7'/><path d='M 182.2 200.3 L 181.3 203 M 186.7 200.3 L 187.6 203'/><\/g><g stroke-width='2.4'><path d='M 147 214 L 140 209'/><ellipse cx='157' cy='214' rx='11' ry='8' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='167' cy='206' r='5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='168.5' cy='205' r='0.8' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 172 206 L 178 207' stroke-width='3.5'/><path d='M 155 221 L 154 224 M 160 221 L 161 224'/><\/g><g stroke-width='2.4'><path d='M 120 233 L 114.4 229'/><ellipse cx='128' cy='233' rx='8.8' ry='6.4' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='136' cy='226.6' r='4' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='137.2' cy='225.8' r='0.8' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 140 226.6 L 143.2 227.4'/><path d='M 126.4 238.6 L 125.6 241 M 130.4 238.6 L 131.2 241'/><\/g><g stroke-width='2.4'><path d='M 91.5 245.5 L 84.8 240.8'/><ellipse cx='101' cy='245.5' rx='10.4' ry='7.6' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='110.5' cy='237.9' r='4.8' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='111.9' cy='237' r='0.8' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 115.2 237.9 L 119 238.8'/><path d='M 99.1 252.2 L 98.2 255 M 103.8 252.2 L 104.8 255'/><\/g><circle cx='40' cy='204' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 26 201 C 20 176 60 176 54 201 C 46 194 34 194 26 201 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 218 L 40 252 M 40 252 L 30 280 M 40 252 L 50 279'/><path d='M 40 228 L 26 246 M 40 228 L 60 216'/><path d='M 58 214 L 58 208 L 62 208 L 62 214 C 68 216 68 228 60 229 C 52 228 52 216 58 214 Z' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 322 272 C 322 258 352 256 354 272 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><g transform='translate(327,236) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he ran a pharmacy in nishapur and wrote the poem where thirty birds go looking for their king and find themselves.","key":"attar","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Attar_of_Nishapur","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Conference_of_the_Birds","why":"the birds poem, the seven valleys and the ending"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tazkirat_al-Awliya","why":"his book of sufi lives"},{"label":"encyclopaedia iranica","url":"https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/attar-farid-al-din-poet","why":"the scholars' long entry on what's actually known"}],"lineage":"muslim","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["the only date attar gives about his own life is 1177, the year he says he finished the conference of the birds. that line isn't in every old copy of the poem, so even that one's shaky.","in the memorial of the saints, hasan al-basri, the great preacher of basra, asks rabia how she found the secret, and she says, you know of the how, but i know of the how-less. hasan died in 728, when rabia would have been about eleven if the usual dates are right, so it's almost certainly a story. that's the kind of book it is.","a later story, with nothing to back it up, says a mongol soldier caught the old man in 1221 and someone offered a thousand pieces of silver for him. attar told him not to sell, he'd get more. then someone offered a sack of straw, and attar said sell, that's what i'm worth. the soldier killed him.","peter brook, the british theater director, took the conference of the birds on tour through africa in 1972 and 1973, then turned it into a play that opened in france in 1979."],"name":"attar","place":"nishapur, in northeastern iran","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 20 130 C 22 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 98 104 100 130' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 35 98 48 118 60 121 C 72 118 85 98 84 66 C 76 80 44 80 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 90 C 52 100 56 108 58 112 M 70 90 C 68 100 64 108 62 112' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 50 74 C 54 70 66 70 70 74'/><path d='M 28 52 C 16 8 104 8 92 52 C 82 40 38 40 28 52 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 40 C 48 28 72 28 88 40 M 38 28 C 52 20 68 20 82 28 M 52 21 C 62 28 74 36 86 42' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 45 51 L 55 50 M 65 50 L 75 51' opacity='.8'/><path d='M 46 58 C 49 56 53 56 56 58 M 64 58 C 67 56 71 56 74 58'/><path d='M 41 60 L 38 58 M 79 60 L 82 58' opacity='.5' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 99 98 L 99 104 C 91 108 91 124 101 126 C 111 124 111 108 103 104 L 103 98 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 97 92 L 105 92 L 105 98 L 97 98 Z' fill='var(--clay)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"do not imagine only he could fall; this hidden danger lurks within us all.","where":"the conference of the birds, the story of sheikh san'an, in afkham darbandi and dick davis's translation (penguin classics)"},{"q":"she was a drop returned to truth's great sea.","where":"the conference of the birds, the story of sheikh san'an, on the christian girl's death, in afkham darbandi and dick davis's translation (penguin classics)"},{"q":"when the heart busies itself with trivial nothings, nothing will come but boredom and redundancy.","where":"the conference of the birds, in sholeh wolpé's translation (norton, 2017), as she quotes it in world literature today"}],"start":"the conference of the birds, in afkham darbandi and dick davis's penguin translation, which keeps the rhymes. read the excuses, then the story of sheikh san'an, then the last pages. then paul losensky's english translation of the memorial of the saints, his lives of the sufis, which losensky calls memorial of god's friends.","status":"approved","story":"farid ud-din attar was a persian poet from nishapur, a big trading city on the silk road in northeastern iran, and most of what gets said about his life is a guess. attar is a job, not a surname. it means a perfumer or an apothecary, the man who mixes and sells medicines. he was probably born around 1145, a date scholars worked out backward from hints, not one anybody wrote down. his own poems say he worked in a pharmacy, saw a very large number of customers, and started some of his long poems there. only two people from his own lifetime mention him at all. outside his home town he wasn't famous until the 1400s, and that's when the fantastic stories about him started piling up.\nhis big poem is the conference of the birds, a long persian poem in rhyming couplets. all the birds of the world get together because they have no king. the hoopoe, a bird with a tall crest of feathers, the one the quran, which muslims hold to be god's own speech, has carrying news to king solomon, stands up and says they do have a king. it's the simorgh, a giant bird out of old persian myth, and it lives very far away. then the excuses start. the nightingale can't leave the rose he's in love with. the parrot would rather live forever. the peacock only wants to get back into paradise. the duck is happy in its water and proud of being clean. the partridge can't stop digging for jewels, the owl can't leave his gold, and the falcon likes serving earthly kings. the hoopoe answers every one of them with a story, and the stories are most of the book.\nthe longest one is about sheikh san'an, a holy man who'd lived beside the kaaba, the shrine in mecca, for about fifty years, made the hajj, the pilgrimage to mecca, fifty times, and had four hundred students. night after night he dreams he's bowing to an idol in a christian country. so he goes there, and he sees a christian girl, and he falls completely apart. she tells him the price. burn the quran, drink wine, bow down to images, give up his faith, and herd her pigs for a year. he does it. his students go home in disgust. one loyal disciple calls them fair weather friends, goes after the sheikh, and prays for forty days until he sees the prophet muhammad in a dream telling him it's answered. the sheikh comes back to himself. the girl dreams the sun falls down beside her and tells her to go with him. she follows, becomes a muslim, and dies.\nafter that the birds finally set out, thousands of them. the hoopoe has warned them there are seven valleys on the way, quest, love, knowledge, detachment, unity, wonderment, and last, poverty and annihilation, where the self disappears. most of them never make it. they die, or give up. thirty arrive. in persian, thirty birds is si morgh. they look for their king and what they see is themselves, thirty birds, looking back. the king was them the whole time, the way the sun is still the sun when you see it in a mirror.\nhis other big book is prose, the tadhkirat al-awliya, the memorial of the saints, seventy two chapters on the lives and sayings of the great sufis, the mystics of islam, from the centuries before him. it's where the stories about [[rabia|rabia of basra]], a woman who'd been stolen as a child and sold into slavery, first turn up, some four hundred years after her. and it ends with [[hallaj|al-hallaj]], a sufi put to death in 922 after nine years in prison, who in the famous version was killed for saying ana al-haqq, i am the truth, al-haqq being one of the names of god. historians say the real reasons were messier. attar hardly ever says where he got his stories.","struggle":"the honest answer is that nobody wrote down his dark nights. what his own poems do show is a working man. he started some of his long poems in the pharmacy with customers coming through the door. he wouldn't write praise poems for rulers, which most of the poets around him did for a living. he had the shop, so he could afford to say no. he stayed a local poet, and only two people of his time bothered to mention him.\nthen in april 1221 came the sack of nishapur. the mongols, the horse armies out of the east, had lost a commander married into their ruling family in an earlier fight there, and they came back for the whole city. medieval chroniclers said more than a million and a half people died. modern historians think a hundred to two hundred thousand, which is still a whole city. attar was in his seventies. most scholars think he was killed in it. a few think he may have lived until about 1230. nobody knows how he spent his last days.","threads":["work","war","dying"],"years":"around 1145 to 1221"},{"after":"he broke off the engagement and never married. after the garden he went to a friend's country house at cassiciacum, outside milan, with his mother, his son, and a few friends, and spent the months before his baptism talking philosophy and reading the psalms out loud, the old hebrew prayer songs in the middle of the bible. then, a decade later, he wrote it all down, the pears, the woman, the lust, the lies to his mother, in a book addressed to god that anyone could read.\nmonica died at ostia, rome's port, on the way home in 387, and adeodatus died a couple of years later, still a teenager. augustine went back to africa with nothing left of that life and became the bishop who wrote about restlessness better than anyone. when he was dying, says his friend possidius, who wrote the first life of him, he had the penitential psalms copied out and hung on the wall where he could read them from bed, and he wept. those are seven psalms that are all about being sorry.","basically":"a brilliant, horny grad student from north africa who partied through his twenties, had a kid with a woman he never married, then got religion hard and became a bishop. then he wrote the first tell-all memoir in history and put all of it in. if you've ever done something shitty for no reason and lain awake wondering why, he's your guy.","born":354,"died":430,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 100 250 L 100 140'/><ellipse cx='100' cy='120' rx='60' ry='46' fill='var(--sage)'/><g fill='var(--lavender)'><circle cx='72' cy='132' r='5'/><circle cx='128' cy='110' r='5'/><circle cx='92' cy='92' r='5'/><circle cx='140' cy='140' r='5'/><\/g><circle cx='170' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 170 192 L 170 234'/><path d='M 170 234 C 150 234 140 250 156 250 L 184 250 C 200 250 190 234 170 234'/><path d='M 170 208 L 156 234 M 170 208 L 184 234'/><path d='M 214 226 L 270 226 M 220 226 L 220 250 M 264 226 L 264 250'/><path d='M 226 226 L 226 218 C 234 214 240 216 242 220 C 244 216 250 214 258 218 L 258 226' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 242 220 L 242 226'/><path d='M 296 250 L 296 180 L 390 180 L 390 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 296 205 L 390 205 M 296 228 L 390 228 M 330 180 L 330 205 M 360 205 L 360 228 M 330 228 L 330 250' opacity='.35'/><circle cx='340' cy='168' r='10'/><path d='M 335 171 Q 340 175 345 171'/><path d='M 328 180 L 330 175 M 352 180 L 350 175'/><path d='M 320 156 C 312 150 306 156 298 150 M 320 166 C 312 162 306 168 298 164' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='290' cy='146' r='3' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 293 146 L 293 134'/><g transform='translate(106,128.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he prayed for chastity, but not yet, and then wrote down every bit of it in a book people are still reading sixteen hundred years later.","key":"augustine","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augustine_of_Hippo","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3296","why":"the confessions on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"in 385 his mother arranged a marriage for him to a girl from a good family who was about two years too young to marry. the woman he'd lived with for fifteen years, the mother of his son, was sent back to africa so she wouldn't be in the way. he says she vowed never to be with another man. he kept the boy. he never tells us her name. a cold thing to have done, and he knew it.\nlater, as a bishop, he argued that the roman state should use force to bring the donatists back into line. they were a rival christian church in north africa that said priests who'd caved during the last roman persecution weren't real priests anymore. he leaned on a line from a story jesus tells in luke, about a man whose dinner guests don't show, so he sends his servant out to the roads to compel people to come in. augustine read it as permission to force people into the church. later churchmen leaned on his reading for centuries to justify forcing people back into line, and that argument had a long, ugly life after him.","moments":["a bishop told monica, when she begged him to talk sense into her son, that the child of so many tears could not be lost. augustine put it in book 3 of the confessions.","in book 9, at a window in ostia looking out on a garden, he and monica talked about what eternal life might be like and for a moment, he says, they touched it. she was dead within two weeks.","about the woman he sent away, he writes that his heart, where it clung to her, was torn and wounded and bleeding. she's in the book because he loved her."],"name":"augustine of hippo","place":"north africa, then rome and milan, then north africa again","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 34 40 L 38 8 L 60 22 L 82 8 L 86 40 C 70 34 50 34 34 40 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 60 22 L 60 36 M 53 28 L 67 28' opacity='.7'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"you have made us for yourself, and our heart is restless until it rests in you.","where":"the confessions, his memoir, book 1, chapter 1, the first paragraph"},{"q":"give me chastity and continence, but not yet.","where":"confessions, book 8, chapter 7, the prayer he says he prayed as a young man. chastity and continence mean no sex"},{"q":"late have i loved you, beauty so ancient and so new.","where":"confessions, book 10, chapter 27"}],"start":"the confessions, books 1 through 9, which are the memoir part; after that it turns into philosophy. book 8 has the garden. a modern translation, like maria boulding's or sarah ruden's.","status":"approved","story":"born in 354 in thagaste, a small town in what's now algeria, back when north africa was part of the roman empire, to a pagan father who kept the old roman gods and a christian mother, monica. brilliant and ambitious, and in his twenties a teacher of rhetoric, which meant public speaking and argument, the skill you needed back then to get anywhere in law or government. think debate coach crossed with speechwriter. at sixteen he and some friends snuck into a neighbor's orchard at night and stole a load of pears. they weren't hungry. the pears weren't even good. they threw most of them to the pigs. what got him, and what he spends pages on thirty years later, is that they did it for no reason except that it was wrong and they were together. it's the first honest look anyone wrote at why we do dumb shit just because. keying a car you don't even hate.\nat around eighteen, in carthage, the big city of roman africa, he took a woman as his concubine, a live-in partner from a lower class, the kind of arrangement an ambitious roman could have without marrying. the confessions, the tell-all memoir he wrote at forty three, never gives her name. they were together about fifteen years and had a son, adeodatus, which means given by god. for nine of those years augustine belonged to the manichees, a religion out of persia that taught the world was a war between a good power of light and an evil power of darkness, and that the body and its appetites belonged to the dark. he says later he liked that it let him believe the bad in him wasn't really him.\nhe moved to rome and then to milan, where he'd landed the job of the city's official teacher of rhetoric, and where the preaching of the bishop, ambrose, one of the most powerful churchmen in the empire, got under his skin. in 386, in a garden in milan, weeping under a fig tree, he heard a child's voice from the next house chanting take up and read, take up and read. he picked up the letters of [[paul]], the early christian whose letters make up a big part of the new testament, opened them at random, and read two lines from chapter 13 of the letter to the romans telling him to quit the partying and the sleeping around. that was it. he was done fighting. ambrose baptized him and adeodatus at easter 387, the ceremony that made them christians.\nhe went home to africa, was made a priest in hippo, a port town in what's now algeria, in 391, more or less against his will, and bishop a few years later. he wrote the confessions around 397, then the city of god, a huge book written after the goths sacked rome in 410, arguing that christians didn't need rome to last. then a mountain of everything else. he's shaped how the west thinks about sin and sex more than almost anyone after paul. he died in 430 with the vandals, a germanic tribe that had crossed over from spain, besieging the city.","struggle":"book 8 of the confessions is a man at war with his own will. he says he wanted to change and didn't want to, both at once, and that the chains were his own habits. he'd been praying give me chastity, but not yet, for years, meaning give me the strength to stop sleeping around, and meaning the second half.\nafter his mother got him engaged and the woman he lived with was sent away, which is down in the mess, he got worse, not better. he took another mistress while he waited for his fiancée to come of age. he writes that the wound from losing the first one didn't heal. it festered, and the pain went colder and more desperate.","threads":["the body","love","family"],"years":"354 to 430"},{"after":"what he did after the vision is the part worth keeping. he had asked when the world gets put right, and was told: when your teaching has spread everywhere. it hurt him how far off that sounded. he didn't write a book. he stayed what he was, the man in the community's house in medzhybizh, on the tax rolls until 1760, healing and praying and talking with whoever came.\nthe letter itself never got delivered. jacob joseph was supposed to carry it to gershon in the land of israel, the trip never happened, and he printed it in 1781, when both men were dead. by then the spreading was under way, done by other people. if the baal shem tov got any relief in his own lifetime, the record doesn't show it. he asked when things would get better, was told not yet, and went back to work.","basically":"a jewish healer and teacher in a small town in what's now ukraine, and the man hasidism traces itself back to. hasidism is the joyful, mystical movement inside judaism that began in eastern europe in the 1700s and is still a major branch of it. he taught that god is present in everything, that you come to god with joy and not by starving yourself, and that someone who can't read the holy books can be as close to god as a scholar. he left no books, and the main collection of stories about him was printed more than fifty years after he died. picture someone who died in the early 1970s, with the first book about him coming out around now, built from what people's grandparents said.","born":1700,"died":1760,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--olive)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='120' cy='160' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 107 153 C 109 140 131 140 133 153 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 110 168 C 112 184 128 184 130 168' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 120 178 L 120 218'/><path d='M 120 218 L 106 250 M 120 218 L 134 250'/><path d='M 120 188 L 104 206 M 120 188 L 142 184'/><path d='M 132 166 C 140 167 145 171 147 176'/><path d='M 144 173 L 154 173 L 152 182 L 146 182 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 150 165 C 144 157 156 153 150 143' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><circle cx='216' cy='196' r='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 216 206 L 216 232'/><path d='M 216 232 L 208 250 M 216 232 L 224 250'/><path d='M 216 214 L 230 206 M 216 216 L 236 204'/><path d='M 224 200 L 248 190' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 256 182 L 260 176 M 262 192 L 269 190 M 252 172 L 253 165' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 326 250 L 342 96 M 352 250 L 368 96'/><path d='M 328.5 226 L 354.5 226 M 331.6 196 L 357.6 196 M 334.7 166 L 360.7 166 M 337.8 136 L 363.8 136 M 341 106 L 367 106'/><path d='M 318 84 C 304 84 304 62 320 62 C 322 46 348 42 356 56 C 372 48 388 62 378 76 C 390 84 380 98 368 96 L 326 96 C 316 98 312 90 318 84 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(328,21) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a small-town healer who never wrote a book, and the stories about him turned into a whole way of being jewish.","key":"baalshemtov","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baal_Shem_Tov","why":"the plain overview, with the arguments about his dates"},{"label":"tradition journal","url":"https://traditiononline.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/06/The-Letter-of.pdf","why":"his one surviving letter in english, translated by norman lamm"},{"label":"encyclopaedia judaica","url":"https://www.encyclopedia.com/religion/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/israel-ben-eliezer-baal-shem-tov","why":"the long scholarly entry on his life and teaching"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"he earned his living selling amulets and cures and casting out demons, and not everyone was charmed. tradition hints that some of the pious circles he later led were put off at first by the miracle healing. a senior rabbi in the city of lviv warned his relatives not to go to the witch doctor who calls himself baal shem. his critics also said he was no scholar, and that he talked too much with simple people.\nthe other thing is what anyone can know. he refused to write his teaching down, so every line comes through a student, and the hasidic camps that later split from each other all claimed him. the stories were printed more than fifty years after his death by people who were open about their pious purpose, after decades of being passed along by mouth. the hasidism of rebbes and dynasties, where a community gathers around one man and the job passes from father to son, was built after he was gone, by other people. anyone who tells you what he was really like inside is guessing.","moments":["the tax rolls of medzhybizh follow him across twenty years. in 1740 the clerk writes kabbalist. in 1742 and 1758 it's baal shem. in 1760, the last time he appears, it's baal shem, doctor.","a story, and only a story: on yom kippur, the day of atonement, a shepherd boy who couldn't read the prayers sat in the baal shem tov's prayer house with his father, and at the closing service he pulled out his flute and blew it. the congregation was horrified. the baal shem tov said the boy had made things easier for him. one scholar traces the familiar version to a book printed in 1905, about a hundred and fifty years after the fact, and a tale much like it sits in a german jewish book from around 1200. [[buber|martin buber]] retold it as the little whistle.","the hasidic account of how dov ber came to him: he was in poor health, went for a cure, and expected to hear deep secrets. what he got was stories about the man's everyday life. he stayed.","the portrait most often passed around as the baal shem tov isn't him. it's hayyim samuel jacob falk, a different wonder worker, known as the baal shem of london."],"name":"the baal shem tov","place":"podolia, a borderland now in western ukraine, and for his last twenty years the town of medzhybizh","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 106 38 98 60 98 C 82 98 100 106 102 130 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 31 47 C 27 18 93 18 89 47 C 78 39 42 39 31 47 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 36 30 C 40 34 44 34 48 30 M 56 27 C 60 31 64 31 68 27 M 74 31 C 78 34 82 34 85 31' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 32 64 C 30 98 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 90 98 88 64 C 80 92 40 92 32 64 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 46 98 C 52 106 68 106 74 98 M 50 110 C 56 115 64 115 70 110' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 45 51 Q 49.5 48 54 50 M 66 50 Q 70.5 48 75 51' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.8' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.8' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 59 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 54 74 C 58 77 62 77 66 74'/><path d='M 65 75 C 74 77 84 82 90 88'/><path d='M 88 85 L 100 85 L 98 96 L 91 96 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 95 78 C 90 72 99 68 94 60' stroke-width='1.8' opacity='.5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"for in every single letter there are worlds and souls and divinity.","where":"his letter to gershon of kitov, the holy epistle, written around 1751, from the part on how to pray. translated by norman lamm in the journal tradition, fall 1974"},{"q":"i asked the messiah, 'when are you coming, sir?'","where":"the same letter, on his vision of rosh hashanah 1746. norman lamm's translation"},{"q":"don't put yourself in danger with many fasts. they lead to melancholy and sadness.","where":"his warning to his student jacob joseph of polonne, preserved in shivhei ha-besht, the story collection printed more than fifty years after he died. attributed, and paraphrased"}],"start":"the letter first. the holy epistle is short, and it's the only time you hear his own voice. then in praise of the baal shem tov, the english translation of the old story collection, read as stories. for the man behind them, moshe rosman's founder of hasidism.","status":"approved","story":"his name was israel ben eliezer, israel son of eliezer. baal shem tov is a title, hebrew for master of the good name, and people shorten it to the besht. being a baal shem was a known job among the jews of eastern europe: a folk healer who worked with the secret names of god, wrote amulets, which are small written charms worn for protection, handed out remedies and drove out demons. there were others before him and others in his own day. he was proud of the work, and signed himself israel baal shem of tluste, after a town he lived in.\nalmost everything about his first forty years comes from one book, shivhei ha-besht, in praise of the baal shem tov. it's a collection of more than two hundred stories, gathered by the son-in-law of a man who had been his scribe, and it was printed in 1814 or 1815, more than fifty years after he died. historians think there's some fact in it and argue about which parts. the way it tells it, he was born around 1700 to poor, elderly parents in okopy, a fort town in podolia, a borderland of the old polish kingdom that's now mostly in ukraine. he was orphaned as a child. he got by as a helper at a children's school and then as a watchman at a synagogue. his first wife died soon after the wedding. his second, hannah, was the sister of gershon of kitov, a learned man who, the stories say, looked down on his new brother-in-law. the couple went to live in the carpathian mountains, where he dug clay and she sold it in town, and later they kept an inn. in the mid 1730s, on his thirty-sixth birthday according to hasidic tradition, he made himself known as a healer and a teacher. nobody's sure about the beginning. one encyclopedia says he was probably born in tluste, one historian says there's no solid evidence for okopy and puts the birth across the border in moldavia, and the year 1698 that you'll often see comes from a document that scholars call a forgery.\nthen the paper trail starts. in the 1990s the historian moshe rosman went through the archive of the polish noble family that owned medzhybizh, a town in podolia, and found him in the tax rolls. from 1740 to 1760 they list a man living tax free in a house that belonged to the town's jewish community. in 1740 the clerk calls him a kabbalist, meaning someone who works with kabbalah, the jewish mystical tradition. in other years he's balszem, and in 1760 he's baal shem, doctor. so the documents don't show the rebel on the margins that later tellings made of him. they show someone the town's jewish leaders thought well enough of to house, a kind of mystic in residence. after 1760 he's gone from the rolls.\nhe taught by talking. the stories never show him preaching in a synagogue. they show him in conversation, with one person or a few, women included, and the traditional picture puts a pipe in his hand. he wrote none of it down and didn't want others to. what his students recorded later comes to this. god is present in everything, the ordinary material world included. the aim is devekut, hebrew for clinging, which means keeping your mind attached to god, and you can do that while you eat or work or do business, not only in prayer. joy is the right mood for it, so he was against the long fasts and self-punishment that pious jews of his time went in for. and he spent his time with simple people, which his critics held against him. only one document is widely accepted as his own words, a letter to gershon, who had moved to the land of israel in 1747. it's known as the holy epistle and was written around 1751. in it he says that on rosh hashanah, the jewish new year, in the autumn of 1746, his soul went up to heaven, that he reached the palace of the messiah, the anointed king jews are still waiting for, and asked him when he was coming. the answer was: when your teaching is known all over the world. he says the answer bewildered him, and that he was in anguish at how long that would take.\nhe died in medzhybizh in 1760, during the late spring festival of shavuot by the usual account. he left a circle of students and no organization. the movement was their work. dov ber of mezeritch, a preacher, gathered students of his own who carried the teaching out of ukraine into poland and russia. jacob joseph of polonne, a rabbi whose town had dismissed him in 1748, published the first hasidic book in 1780, toldot yaakov yosef, with more than 280 passages that begin with the words i heard from my teacher. the organized opposition came after he was dead as well. in the spring of 1772 the vilna gaon, the great scholar of vilna, which is now vilnius in lithuania, and his community put the hasidim under a herem, a ban. the opponents became known as the mitnagdim, which just means opponents. one of his great-grandsons was [[nachman|nachman of breslov]].","struggle":"there's no diary. the stories about him are praise, and praise doesn't record bad nights. the one place you can hear him in trouble is the letter to gershon.\nhe writes that in the 1746 vision he saw the accusing angel rise up in the middle of all the joy in heaven and bring decrees against jews: forced conversion, then death. he says he was seized with terror and offered his own life for theirs. and then, he writes, it happened. in the town of zaslav there was a blood libel, the old lie that jews kill christians to use their blood, and jews died for it, and after that in two more towns. in a second vision, on rosh hashanah in 1749, he says he saw whole countries about to be handed over for destruction. he begged that the jews fall into the hand of god and not the hand of man, and was granted that sickness would come in place of the killing. sickness spread, he writes, like nothing poland had seen. when he and his circle began saying prayers against it, he was asked in a night vision whether he hadn't chosen this himself, and told that an accuser doesn't get to turn defender. he stopped praying about it, he says, and tried only once more, in the synagogue, because he was so afraid for the people. believe in the visions or don't. this is a man who thought he had traded a massacre for a plague and then had to live through the plague.\nthe rest is smaller. he tells gershon he can't write at length because he cries when he remembers him leaving. he apologizes for not sending money: there's been plague and famine, and he's feeding the young children of the family and other poor jews besides. he says god knows he hasn't given up on coming to the land of israel to be with him, but the time isn't ripe. he died in medzhybizh about nine years later.","threads":["joy","work","fame"],"years":"around 1700 to 1760"},{"after":"he didn't get bitter and he didn't go quiet. he started a podcast, wrote books nobody in evangelicalism would touch, did a speaking tour with oprah winfrey, did shows with comedians, and kept saying the same thing he'd said in grand rapids, just without the sound system.\nthe 2018 documentary about him is called the heretic and he's fine with it. he says he's more interested in what's true than in who's allowed to say it.","basically":"an evangelical pastor from michigan who built one of the hottest churches in america before he was thirty five, then wrote a book asking whether god really sends most people to burn forever. the answer he gave, probably not, got him run out of the whole world he grew up in. now he lives in los angeles, does a podcast, and tours with comedians. if you were raised on hell and quietly stopped buying it, he's the guy who said it out loud from the stage and paid for it.","born":1970,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><ellipse cx='150' cy='232' rx='90' ry='14' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 80 240 L 70 270 M 220 240 L 230 270 M 150 246 L 150 270'/><path d='M 150 232 C 140 232 130 226 150 220 C 170 226 160 232 150 232' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='150' cy='80' r='14'/><path d='M 150 94 L 150 142'/><path d='M 150 142 L 136 176 M 150 142 L 164 176'/><path d='M 150 108 L 130 84 M 150 108 L 170 84'/><path d='M 145 85 Q 150 89 155 85'/><path d='M 150 176 L 150 216' stroke-dasharray='2 8' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 290 270 L 290 170 L 380 170 L 380 270' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 290 195 L 380 195 M 290 220 L 380 220 M 290 245 L 380 245 M 335 170 L 335 195 M 312 195 L 312 220 M 358 195 L 358 220 M 335 220 L 335 245 M 312 245 L 312 270 M 358 245 L 358 270'/><rect x='336' y='221' width='21' height='23' fill='var(--peach)'/><g transform='translate(352,143.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a megachurch pastor who wrote that hell might not be the point, and got told farewell by the whole industry.","key":"bell","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rob_Bell","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nooma","why":"the short films that made him famous in youth groups"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"the fights were all about doctrine. some people who worked for him at mars hill felt left behind when he walked out, and he's talked about that.","moments":["the first nooma film, rain, from 2002, is him carrying his crying son home in a storm and saying that's what god is like. it's ten minutes long and it wrecked a generation of youth groups.","john piper's farewell tweet came before the book was even in stores. bell's response was basically to shrug and keep the tour dates.","his book what is the bible? says the bible is a library of arguing voices and the argument is the point. it's the most useful thing anybody in a church has written for people who love the book and can't stand the rulebook."],"name":"rob bell","place":"michigan, then california","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 96 C 52 104 68 104 72 96' /><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 70 C 46 84 74 84 80 70' stroke-dasharray='1 4' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><rect x='41' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><rect x='62' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><path d='M 58 55 L 62 55 M 41 54 L 34 52 M 79 54 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"a brick wall is only as strong as its weakest brick. a trampoline gets stronger the more you jump.","where":"velvet elvis, his first book, paraphrased"},{"q":"the bible isn't one book, it's a library, and the books argue with each other. that's the point.","where":"what is the bible?, his 2017 book, paraphrased"},{"q":"love wins. that's it. that's the whole title.","where":"love wins, 2011, the book that got him thrown out"}],"start":"velvet elvis. then love wins. then his podcast, the robcast, which is where he's loosest.","status":"approved","story":"he built mars hill bible church in grand rapids, michigan into ten thousand people a week by his early thirties. a megachurch is exactly that, a church the size of an arena, with a band and big screens. he also made short films called nooma, ten minute videos of him walking around talking about god, and half the youth groups in america watched them. for about five years he was the future of evangelicalism, the born again, bible first side of american protestant christianity, the side with the megachurches and the christian radio.\nvelvet elvis, his first book, from 2005, has the trampoline. he said faith should be a trampoline, springs you can pull out and look at and bounce on, not a brick wall where if you pull one brick the whole thing comes down. to the people running evangelicalism, questioning the bricks was already the problem. that book was the warning shot.\nlove wins, from 2011, asked whether a loving god sends billions of people to hell, conscious eternal torment, awake and burning with no end, and answered, probably not. to evangelicals that isn't a side question. it's the floor. before the book was even in stores, john piper, one of the most powerful pastors in that world, tweeted three words: farewell rob bell. that was the excommunication, the old word for a church formally throwing you out. this one was a tweet, and it worked. he left mars hill the next year.\nhe's been in los angeles since, doing a podcast, touring with comedians, writing about the bible as a library of arguing voices instead of a rulebook. he's the easiest christian on these pages to love because he never stopped being funny about it.","struggle":"after love wins he was forty one with young kids, no church, no denomination to fall back on the way most pastors have, and half the people who'd loved him calling him a heretic on the internet, meaning somebody teaching what the church calls false. he's talked about the weirdness of walking away from ten thousand people who'd shown up to hear you every week.\nhe moved to los angeles and, by his own account, spent a lot of time surfing badly and figuring out who he was without a building.","threads":["doubt","fame"],"years":"born 1970"},{"after":"after wounded knee he went on living on the reservation, raising a family, healing people. after he became a catholic he gave himself to it for about forty years. he also kept the old stories, and when neihardt and later joseph epes brown, a young white scholar, came asking, he told them.\nafter black elk speaks came out, he signed a statement in 1934 saying he was a catholic and regretted the book leaving that out. scholars argue about how much of that statement was his and how much the priests'. some of his descendants have said he held both and didn't feel he had to choose. that's the most honest reading anyone's come up with.","basically":"a lakota holy man who had a huge vision as a sick nine year old, was a boy in the fight where custer's men were wiped out, toured europe with buffalo bill's wild west show, rode into the aftermath of the wounded knee massacre, and then spent decades as a catholic teacher without ever dropping the old ways. the famous book about him is partly ghostwritten, by the white poet who interviewed him. take from him that you can carry two faiths at once and not owe anyone a choice between them.","born":1863,"died":1950,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 280 L 150 180 L 280 280 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='150' cy='84' r='14'/><path d='M 150 98 L 150 146'/><path d='M 150 146 L 136 180 M 150 146 L 164 180'/><path d='M 150 112 L 130 88 M 150 112 L 170 88'/><path d='M 125 44 Q 125 26 142 28 Q 151 10 169 22 Q 183 14 187 32 Q 195 42 183 44 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 140 50 L 136 62 M 156 50 L 152 64 M 172 50 L 168 62' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 20 280 L 380 280'/><path d='M 310 280 L 310 240 L 350 240 L 350 280' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 304 242 L 330 222 L 356 242'/><path d='M 330 222 L 330 206 M 324 212 L 336 212'/><path d='M 324 280 L 324 262 L 336 262 L 336 280' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 204 236 C 206 224 222 224 226 236 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(208,210.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he had a great vision at nine, fought at the little bighorn at twelve, and spent the last half of his life as a catholic teacher, and he never said he'd given up either.","key":"blackelk","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Elk","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Elk_Speaks","why":"the famous book, and the argument about how much of it is his"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["in 1931 he had neihardt take him up harney peak, the highest mountain in the black hills, the lakota's holy ground, where in the vision he'd stood at the center of the world. he was old and nearly blind, and he prayed for his people. neihardt wrote that a little rain fell out of a nearly clear sky. the mountain has been called black elk peak since 2016.","in 1947 he told the lakota rites to joseph epes brown, the seven sacred ceremonies, the sweat lodge and the vision quest among them, and the sun dance, the big summer ceremony of fasting and dancing around a sacred tree, and that became the sacred pipe, in 1953. it's the other book, and it's closer to his own voice."],"name":"black elk","place":"the northern plains, then the pine ridge reservation in south dakota","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 60 100 L 60 118 M 54 106 L 66 106' stroke-width='2.5'/><path d='M 48 96 C 50 100 56 100 60 100 C 64 100 70 100 72 96' stroke-dasharray='0.1 4' stroke-width='3'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"anywhere is the center of the world.","where":"black elk speaks, the chapter on the great vision; the english is neihardt's"},{"q":"it is the story of all life that is holy and good to tell.","where":"black elk speaks, the opening; neihardt's english, from what black elk told him"},{"q":"the nation's hoop is broken and scattered.","where":"black elk speaks, the last pages. the scholar raymond demallie showed these lines are neihardt's, not in the interview notes"}],"start":"black elk speaks, the chapter on the great vision, read knowing neihardt's hand is on it. then raymond demallie's the sixth grandfather, which prints the interview notes. then damian costello's black elk: colonialism and lakota catholicism.","status":"approved","story":"heȟáka sápa, black elk, was born in 1863, by his own count, into the oglala, one of the seven bands of the lakota, the plains nation white americans called the sioux. crazy horse, the war leader, was his second cousin. at nine he fell sick for days, and while he lay there he had the great vision. in it he was taken up to the six grandfathers, the powers of the four directions and the sky and the earth, and shown the sacred hoop of his people, their life as one nation, with a flowering tree at its center. he told almost no one for years.\nat twelve he was at the battle of the little bighorn in june 1876, where the lakota and cheyenne wiped out custer's command, the fight white americans called custer's last stand. then the buffalo were gone and the people were on reservations. in 1886 he joined buffalo bill's wild west, a traveling show of cowboys, sharpshooters and real indians acting out battles, to see the white world for himself, and in 1887 performed in london for queen victoria, whom the lakota called grandmother england. he got separated from the show, traveled on through europe, and made it home in 1889.\non december 29, 1890, at wounded knee creek in south dakota, the us army opened fire on a band of lakota and killed hundreds, many of them women and children. black elk rode there as the killing was happening and was wounded in the fighting that followed. he'd been healing people since his late teens and went back to it. in 1904 he was baptized a catholic, took the name nicholas, and became a catechist, a lay teacher who travels around teaching the faith, going from reservation to reservation, and he did that for decades.\nin 1931 john neihardt, nebraska's poet laureate, came to pine ridge and black elk told him his life and his vision, through his son ben as interpreter. black elk speaks came out in 1932. neihardt shaped it and in places rewrote it, including its most famous lines, and he left out the catholic half of black elk's life. black elk died in 1950. in 2017 the catholic church opened his cause for sainthood, the first step toward declaring him a saint.","struggle":"the vision sat on him for years. he was told it was for his people and he didn't know how to give it to them, and he said later he'd been afraid of it, and sick, until he acted part of it out in his late teens in the horse dance, a ceremony where the vision was staged in front of the whole camp with riders on horses.\nand then everything the vision was about went under. the buffalo gone, the land taken, the people starving on the reservation, and wounded knee in december 1890. he was twenty seven.","threads":["the land","war","doubt"],"years":"1863 to 1950"},{"after":"when the german church folded to hitler, he didn't write essays about it. in 1935 he started an illegal seminary, a school for training pastors that the nazi-approved church didn't allow, at finkenwalde, a village in what's now poland, and trained young pastors in a big old house until the gestapo, hitler's secret police, shut it down in 1937. life together is the book about that house.\nwhen the july 1944 bomb plot against hitler failed and the gestapo found papers tying him to the conspiracy, it was over. he'd already been in a cell for more than a year and he spent the rest of his time there writing letters that are more alive than most people's freedom. the camp doctor wrote later that he had hardly ever seen a man die so submissive to the will of god. recent biographers doubt the doctor's story. he was thirty nine.","basically":"a german pastor in the 1930s who saw what hitler was the minute he took over and said so, while most of the churches in germany went along. he was safe in new york, went home on purpose, joined the resistance that was plotting to kill hitler, and was hanged a month before the war in europe ended. when people ask what they'd have done in 1933, he's the answer nobody wants to live. if you've ever had to choose between keeping your hands clean and doing what's needed, he chose, and he never pretended it was clean.","born":1906,"died":1945,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 200 L 90 200 L 90 230 L 20 230' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 200 L 30 170 L 50 170 L 50 200 M 60 200 L 60 150 L 78 150 L 78 200' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 20 250 C 60 240 100 260 140 250 S 220 240 260 250 S 340 260 380 250'/><path d='M 150 236 L 170 262 L 330 262 L 350 236 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 236 L 250 180'/><path d='M 250 186 L 300 210 L 250 210 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 290 236 L 290 205 L 320 205 L 320 236' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 305 205 L 305 190'/><path d='M 305 182 C 312 170 320 175 328 168' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='200' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 200 164 L 200 212'/><path d='M 200 212 L 186 246 M 200 212 L 214 246'/><path d='M 200 178 L 214 202 M 200 178 L 174 172'/><path d='M 110 140 C 130 130 150 130 165 138' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(150,209.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he was safe in new york in 1939 and went back to nazi germany on one of the last boats.","key":"bonhoeffer","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dietrich_Bonhoeffer","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Cost_of_Discipleship","why":"the book with cheap grace in it"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["in 1931 he took a confirmation class, the class where teenagers learn the faith before they join the church, of a few dozen rough kids at zion church in working class north berlin, and rented a room in the neighborhood to be near them. the class had gotten out of control before he came.","his twin sister sabine married gerhard leibholz, a law professor the nazis counted as jewish, and in 1938 bonhoeffer drove with them toward the swiss border, which they crossed late at night.","he brought recordings of black spirituals, the songs enslaved people made in america, back from harlem in 1931 and played them for his german students."],"name":"dietrich bonhoeffer","place":"berlin, harlem, berlin, prison","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='8'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='8'/><path d='M 58 56 L 62 56 M 42 55 L 34 52 M 78 55 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"cheap grace is the grace we bestow on ourselves.","where":"the cost of discipleship, chapter 1"},{"q":"i will have no right to participate in the reconstruction of christian life in germany after the war if i do not share the trials of this time with my people.","where":"a letter to reinhold niebuhr, the american theologian, 1939"},{"q":"who am i? they mock me, these lonely questions of mine. whoever i am, thou knowest, o god, i am thine.","where":"letters and papers from prison, the poem who am i, the last lines"}],"start":"the cost of discipleship, the first chapter, on cheap grace. then letters and papers from prison, the poems especially. then life together, which is short, about the little underground school for pastors he ran.","status":"approved","story":"a german pastor and theologian, meaning someone who studies and writes about god for a living, brilliant early, with a doctorate at twenty one. in 1930 he went to new york to study at union seminary, a big school for training ministers, and ended up spending his sundays in harlem at abyssinian baptist church, one of the biggest black churches in america, teaching sunday school. the black church's music and grief got into him in a way the lecture halls never did.\nwhen hitler took power in 1933 he was one of the first pastors to say out loud that the church had to oppose him. most of the german protestant church went along. a breakaway called the confessing church didn't, and he was in it. the cost of discipleship, from 1937, his book about what it actually costs to follow [[jesus]], has the line everyone quotes. cheap grace, he says, is forgiveness you hand yourself without changing anything, grace without the cross, meaning without any of the price jesus paid, and it's the deadly enemy of the church. it's the apology with nothing behind it.\nfriends got him a job in new york in june 1939 to keep him safe. he lasted a few weeks. he wrote to reinhold niebuhr, the best known american theologian of the day, that he had no right to help rebuild christian life in germany after the war if he didn't go through the trials of that time with his people. he sailed back in july. he joined the resistance, a circle of officers inside german military intelligence who tried more than once to kill hitler. he carried messages abroad for them. he knew of the plans to kill hitler and approved of them, but he took no part in the attempts himself. he was arrested in april 1943, at first for helping jews escape germany, and was hanged at flossenbürg, a nazi concentration camp, on april 9, 1945, two weeks before the americans got there.\nthe letters he wrote from prison, mostly to his best friend eberhard bethge and to his family, came out after the war as letters and papers from prison, and they're the best thing he did. he was thirty nine.","struggle":"in prison he wrote a poem called who am i. others told him they saw a calm, cheerful, confident man. inside, he wrote, he was restless and longing and sick, like a bird in a cage, struggling for breath. he asked which one was true and answered, whoever i am, god, you know i am yours.\nthe deeper trouble was joining a plot to kill a man. he'd been a pacifist, someone who won't use violence at all, and had wanted to go study with gandhi, the indian leader who fought the british without guns. back in 1933 he had written that the church's job was not just to bandage the victims under the wheel but to put a spoke in the wheel itself. he never called it innocent. he accepted the guilt and did it anyway.","threads":["prison","war","dying"],"years":"1906 to 1945"},{"after":"after the young man, he quit chasing the high. since then, he wrote, he had given up the religious that is nothing but exception and ecstasy, or it had given him up. what he had left was the everyday, and each hour's claim on him. two years later he started drafting i and thou. after landauer's rebuke he dropped the war talk. on may 2, 1919, soldiers beat landauer to death in a prison in munich, and buber gathered his friend's writings and letters and got them into print.\nthen he kept turning toward people he had every reason to turn from. in germany in 1953 he told the audience who am i to forgive, and then asked the nations to start talking to each other for real anyway. in 1962, with eichmann sentenced to hang, he and a handful of other israeli intellectuals, scholem among them, asked the president to make it life in prison. an unsigned draft in his papers says there is no man who deserves less mercy, and that we do not want this hateful person to turn us into the hangman. he lost that one too. on his eighty fifth birthday about four hundred students from the hebrew university turned up outside his house, and he is said to have valued that above the prizes.","basically":"a jewish philosopher from vienna who said there are two ways to stand in front of anything, a tree or another person. you can treat it as an it, something to size up and use, or you can turn to it as a you and actually be there. it's the difference between nodding at someone while you read your phone and the rare time the phone stays in your pocket. he put that in a short, strange book called i and thou in 1923, said god is met the same way, and then spent forty years telling his fellow jews they should share one country with the arabs, which hardly anybody on either side wanted to hear.","born":1878,"died":1965,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--olive)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 72 250 L 72 174'/><circle cx='72' cy='128' r='46' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 56 137 L 90 137' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='190' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 190 164 L 190 212'/><path d='M 190 212 L 176 250 M 190 212 L 204 250'/><path d='M 190 180 L 216 190 M 190 184 L 174 204'/><path d='M 178 157 C 176 186 204 186 202 157 C 197 165 183 165 178 157 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='290' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 290 164 L 290 212'/><path d='M 290 212 L 276 250 M 290 212 L 304 250'/><path d='M 290 180 L 264 190 M 290 184 L 306 204'/><path d='M 212 148 L 268 148' stroke-dasharray='2 8' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(60,110) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"his mother left when he was three, and he spent the rest of his life working out what it means to really meet someone.","key":"buber","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martin_Buber","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I_and_Thou","why":"the book, and what i-it and i-thou mean"},{"label":"stanford encyclopedia of philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/buber/","why":"the careful version, with his critics"},{"label":"internet encyclopedia of philosophy","url":"https://iep.utm.edu/martin-buber/","why":"a second scholarly life, easier to read"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"he got the first world war wrong. when it started in 1914 he was publicly keen on the german war effort, and talked up the comradeship it gave people. it took a friend to stop him. in 1916 gustav landauer, an anarchist and pacifist, went after him for it and called him kriegsbuber, war buber. the second charge has stuck longer. gershom scholem, who became the founding historian of jewish mysticism, wrote in 1961 that buber had built his hasidism out of the legends and skipped the movement's own books of teaching, and that he read his own philosophy into what was left. the real thing, scholem said, cared far more about getting free of this world than buber's warm everyday version lets on. [[heschel|abraham joshua heschel]], who was born into a family of hasidic rebbes, saw it the other way and said that if you want to know hasidism as it was, begin with buber. and walter kaufmann, who translated i and thou into english a second time in 1970, said the book had the oracular tone of false prophets and was more affected than honest.\nhis politics got it from both directions, and each side gets to say it in its own words. buber held that zionism was only worth having as a moral project, and feared that a victory of the jews over the arabs would be a defeat for zionism. so he argued for one shared country, and after 1948 for the refugees. the zionist leadership found him unrealistic and a thorn in its side, and the jewish press called his ally judah magnes a quisling, a traitor. hardly any arabs took up the idea, and one arab leader who did sign on to it was assassinated for that in november 1946. from the other direction, a more recent critic has argued that buber failed his own standard and talked down to the palestinians. and when he was offered the goethe prize from hamburg in 1951, many in israel said it was far too early to take an honor from germany. he accepted, and in 1953 he went to germany to collect it, along with a second one, the peace prize of the german book trade.","moments":["at eleven, spending the summer on his grandparents' estate, he used to steal into the stable to stroke a dapple-grey horse, and felt he was touching something completely other than himself that still let him near. then one day he noticed how much fun it was and became aware of his own hand. the next day the horse didn't lift its head for him. he tells it in the essay dialogue.","on september 18, 1961, dag hammarskjöld, the head of the united nations, died when his plane crashed in africa. in his briefcase were copies of i and thou in german and english. he had been working on a swedish translation during the flight.","in april 1963 [[king|martin luther king]] borrowed his words in the letter from birmingham jail: segregation, he wrote, substitutes an i-it relationship for an i-thou relationship and ends up relegating persons to the status of things. in february 1965, after king was jailed in selma, buber and other professors at the hebrew university wrote to the american president to say how glad they were that king was free again, and to ask for the release of the others.","when he died in june 1965, classes were cancelled and hundreds of students lined up to say goodbye."],"name":"martin buber","place":"vienna, lemberg, germany, then jerusalem","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 104 38 96 60 96 C 82 96 100 104 102 130 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 34 48 C 21 50 21 70 30 78 C 30 66 32 58 35 52 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 86 48 C 99 50 99 70 90 78 C 90 66 88 58 85 52 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 33 64 C 28 98 42 124 60 126 C 78 124 92 98 87 64 C 80 78 70 80 60 80 C 50 80 40 78 33 64 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 46 94 L 47 106 M 60 96 L 60 114 M 74 94 L 73 106' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 50 77 C 54 73 58 74 60 76 C 62 74 66 73 70 77'/><path d='M 50 38 Q 60 35 70 38' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 44 50 Q 49 46 55 49 M 65 49 Q 71 46 76 50' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 59 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 80 112 L 92 108 L 104 112 L 104 126 L 92 122 L 80 126 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 92 108 L 92 122'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"all real living is meeting.","where":"i and thou, part one, in ronald gregor smith's 1937 english translation"},{"q":"i possess nothing but the everyday out of which i am never taken.","where":"dialogue, the first essay in between man and man, the section called a conversion, translated by ronald gregor smith"},{"q":"we have no desire to dispossess them; we want to live with them.","where":"his open letter to gandhi, dated jerusalem, february 24, 1939, writing about the arabs of palestine"}],"start":"i and thou, part one, slowly. it's short and odd, and the first part is the one people carry around. if it won't open for you, read the essay dialogue in between man and man first, which is the same idea told through things that happened to him. then tales of the hasidim, anywhere you like.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in vienna on february 8, 1878. when he was three, by his own account and most others, his parents split up and his mother vanished from his life. his biographers say she left with a russian officer. the boy was sent to his father's parents in lemberg, a city in the austrian empire that is now lviv, in ukraine. his grandfather solomon buber sat on the chamber of commerce and audited banks, and the rest of the time he was a respected editor of midrash, the old rabbinic way of reading the hebrew bible, the jewish scriptures, by questioning every word and filling the gaps with stories. martin lived in that house of books until he was fourteen.\nas a student he joined the zionist movement, zionism being the campaign, brand new then, for a jewish homeland in palestine. its founder, theodor herzl, a journalist, made him editor of the movement's newspaper around 1901. it went wrong fast. herzl was after a state, to be won by diplomacy with emperors and sultans. buber thought the point was to bring jewish culture and spirit back to life, and that the politics came second. within a few years they had split and buber had stepped back. in zurich in 1899 he had met paula winkler, a writer from munich who was raised catholic, and they stayed together until she died in 1958. at twenty six he started reading the teachings of hasidism, the joyful, mystical jewish revival that began in eastern europe in the 1700s, and it took him over. he retold the tales of [[nachman|rabbi nachman]] in german in 1906, then the legends of the [[baalshemtov|baal shem tov]], and decades later he gathered the big collection, tales of the hasidim. young german jews from families that had mostly let judaism go, gershom scholem among them, found their way back to it through him.\nin 1923 he published ich und du, which its first english translator called i and thou. du is just the everyday, informal german word for you. it doesn't sound churchy the way thou does. the argument is easy to say and hard to do. there are two ways of facing anything. in i-it you stand back and treat what's in front of you as a thing, to look over, sort and use. in i-thou you turn toward it with all of yourself, and for a moment nobody in the meeting is an object. he says it can happen with a person, and also with a tree. it never lasts. every thou has to go back to being an it, and he calls that the exalted melancholy of our fate. you can't live without the world of it, he says, but whoever lives only there is not fully a person. god, for him, is the eternal thou, the one that never turns into an it. he said he couldn't talk about god, only about meeting god.\nthrough the 1920s he taught in frankfurt, at the university and at the lehrhaus, a free school of jewish learning for adults that his friend franz rosenzweig had started in 1920. in 1925 the two of them began translating the hebrew bible into german, a german bent far enough that you could feel the hebrew underneath. rosenzweig was dying slowly of a disease that paralyzes the body, and near the end he worked by having his wife, edith, recite the alphabet until he signaled at a letter. he died in december 1929. buber finished the job alone in 1961. when the nazis took power in 1933 he gave up his university post before they could take it from him, and that october he was forbidden to lecture. jews were being pushed out of german schools, so he reopened the lehrhaus and built an office that trained teachers and kept jewish adult education going around the country. in 1938, at about sixty, he left for jerusalem, where the hebrew university gave him a chair in sociology. that november, on kristallnacht, the night the nazis smashed and burned jewish homes, shops and synagogues across germany, the home he had left behind was ransacked.\npalestine was then under the british mandate, britain's rule there from 1920 to 1948, and he argued for one country shared by jews and arabs as equals. he had been saying it since the 1920s, alongside a small circle of intellectuals called brit shalom, and from 1942 through a small party called ihud, which he started with judah magnes, the american rabbi who ran the hebrew university, and two others. most zionists thought the idea was unrealistic. few arabs took it up. when israel was founded in 1948 he accepted the state and became its critic from inside, pressing for the arab refugees to be let back and for an end to the military rule that israel's arab citizens lived under. in 1962 he asked israel's president to spare adolf eichmann, the nazi official who had organized the deportation of jews to the death camps and had been sentenced to hang in jerusalem. the president said no. buber died at home in jerusalem on june 13, 1965, at eighty seven.","struggle":"the first wound is a balcony. his grandparents' house in lemberg had a wooden one running around the inner courtyard. he stood on it, not yet four, with a neighbor's daughter a few years older who had been told to mind him, and she said, no, she will never come back. she meant his mother. he didn't doubt it. later he made up a german word, vergegnung, a mismeeting, for a meeting between people that fails to happen. he saw his mother once more, as a grown man, when she came from far away to visit him and his wife and children. he wrote that he couldn't look into her still astonishingly beautiful eyes without hearing that word. some accounts put his age at four when she left. his own puts the balcony in his fourth year.\nthe second he called a conversion, and he tells it in the essay dialogue. one morning he had been deep in what he calls religious enthusiasm, the lifted-out-of-yourself state he went looking for in those years. that forenoon an unknown young man came to see him. buber was friendly. he talked with him attentively and openly. what he didn't do, he says, was guess the questions the young man didn't ask. not long after, one of the young man's friends told him what those questions were, and that he had come not for a chat but for a decision. by then the young man was dead. buber told his biographer maurice friedman that it was the late autumn of 1914, the opening months of the first world war, and that he died in the war. one scholar dates the visit to july 1914, and one recent account says the unasked question was whether to enlist and that he was killed at the front. you will also see it told as a suicide. that is not in buber's account. what is in it is this: when we are in despair and go to someone, what we want is a presence that tells us there is meaning anyway, and he hadn't been one.","threads":["family","exile","the land","war"],"years":"1878 to 1965"},{"after":"his after is what he did with other people's messes. a serial killer named angulimala, whose name means finger necklace because he wore one made from his victims' fingers, came at him on a road. the buddha kept walking calmly and the killer ran and couldn't catch him, and finally yelled stop. the buddha said i've stopped, you haven't. angulimala became a monk. when he went out begging, people threw rocks at him, and he came back with his head split open. the buddha told him to bear it, that this was what he'd done coming back to him. he did.\nthe monks' old rulebook says his cousin devadatta tried to kill him three times, once by setting a drunk elephant loose on him, and split his community of monks. later stories say his own clan, the shakyas, was massacred by a neighboring king in his lifetime. historians think it was a conquest, probably just after he died. he taught for forty five years through all of it and died of a bad meal, telling his cousin and attendant ananda to make sure nobody blamed the man who'd cooked it.","basically":"the son of a rich ruling family in what's now nepal, about twenty five hundred years ago, who, as the old stories tell it, had everything and walked out on it at twenty nine to figure out why everybody suffers. six years later, sitting under a tree, he figured it out, and spent the next forty five years walking around northern india teaching it. he didn't ask anyone to believe him. he told them to try it and see, which is why so much of it sits comfortably in a therapist's office today. if you want a method more than a belief, start here.","born":-480,"died":-400,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 120 250 L 120 150'/><ellipse cx='120' cy='126' rx='70' ry='53' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='120' cy='170' r='14'/><path d='M 120 184 L 120 226'/><path d='M 120 226 C 100 226 90 242 106 242 L 134 242 C 150 242 140 226 120 226'/><path d='M 120 200 L 106 226 M 120 200 L 134 226'/><path d='M 115 175 Q 120 179 125 175'/><path d='M 200 250 L 220 200 M 214 202 L 222 198 L 224 208' /><path d='M 300 250 C 300 236 340 236 340 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 240 110 C 260 100 280 110 290 100' opacity='.4'/><g transform='translate(150,121.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a rich kid who saw a sick man, an old man, and a corpse, and couldn't unsee them.","key":"buddha","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Buddha","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the dhammapada, free online","url":"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/kn/dhp/index.html","why":"his sayings in verse, in english"},{"label":"the kalama sutta, free online","url":"https://www.accesstoinsight.org/tipitaka/an/an03/an03.065.than.html","why":"the test-it-yourself talk, in english"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"as the later stories tell it, he left his wife, yasodhara, and a newborn son in the middle of the night without saying goodbye. the oldest texts say only that he left while his parents wept. the tradition frames it as necessary. his wife might have had a different word for it. years later the son, rahula, joined his order as a boy. that's his part in it, and it's a real one.","moments":["his last words were roughly: all things that come together fall apart. work it out with care. he was already lying on his right side between two trees. then he stopped.","he refused to answer whether the universe goes on forever or whether an awakened person still exists after death, and said so, because the answer didn't help anyone stop hurting. the poisoned arrow story is his answer.","kushinagar, where he died, is where mingyur rinpoche, the tibetan monk on these pages, got food poisoning on his years as a wandering beggar and nearly died, twenty five centuries later. mingyur knew exactly where he was lying."],"name":"the buddha","place":"northern india and nepal","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 100 C 52 108 74 108 90 98 L 98 130 L 44 130 Z' fill='var(--marigold)' opacity='.9'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 34 86 34 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 50 34 C 50 22 70 22 70 34 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 57 C 48 60 52 60 55 57 M 65 57 C 68 60 72 60 75 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 33 58 C 30 66 32 76 36 74 M 87 58 C 90 66 88 76 84 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"don't believe it because it's tradition, or scripture, or because your teacher said it. test it in your own life.","where":"the kalama sutta, his talk to the confused villagers, paraphrased. it's in the anguttara nikaya, the numbered talks, book 3, number 65, part of the pali canon, the oldest full set of his talks, kept in an old indian language called pali"},{"q":"a man shot with an arrow who won't be treated until he knows who shot it will die with the arrow in him.","where":"the shorter malunkya sutta, the talk to the monk who wanted answers first, in the majjhima nikaya, the middle-length talks, number 63, paraphrased"},{"q":"my teaching is a raft for crossing the river. you don't carry the raft on your head afterward.","where":"the alagaddupama sutta, the talk about grabbing a snake by the wrong end, majjhima nikaya 22, paraphrased"}],"start":"the dhammapada, a short book of his sayings in verse, the one most people start with. then the kalama sutta and the sona sutta. that one's about a monk named sona who pushed so hard at walking meditation that his feet bled, and the buddha asked him, you used to play the lute, didn't you? when the strings were too tight, did it sound right? too loose? practice is the same. in the buddha's words, a single book by bhikkhu bodhi, an american monk who's spent his life translating the talks, is the best collection.","status":"approved","story":"siddhartha gautama was a prince, or at least a rich man's son, in the foothills of the himalayas, and the story, written down centuries later, says his father tried to keep him from ever seeing anything sad. it didn't work. out in the town he saw an old man, then a sick man, then a corpse being carried off, and then a wandering monk, a holy man who'd given up everything and lived on handouts, who looked at peace. at twenty nine he left his wife and newborn son in the night to find out why we suffer and whether it can stop.\nhe tried six years of starving himself, the way the holy men of his day did, and got nowhere but thin. then he ate a bowl of rice, sat down under a fig tree, and decided not to get up until he understood. the story says he woke up that night, which is what buddha means, the one who woke up. what he found is called the four noble truths. life hurts. the hurt comes from craving, from grabbing for things to be other than they are. the craving can stop. and here's how. then he taught for forty five years.\nhe didn't ask to be believed. his talks were memorized by his monks and written down centuries later, and each one is called a sutta. in one, the kalama sutta, the kalamas, the people of a town called kesaputta, tell him that every teacher who passes through trashes all the others, so how are they supposed to know who's right. he tells them not to take anything on tradition, or scripture, or because a teacher said so, but to test it in their own lives, check it against what the wise praise, and keep what actually leads to good. another time a monk demanded answers about the afterlife and the edges of the universe before he'd practice, and the buddha said he was like a man shot with a poisoned arrow refusing treatment until he knew who shot it and what the bow was made of.\nhe died at about eighty at kushinagar, a small town in northern india, after a meal a blacksmith named cunda served him made him sick. [[mingyur|mingyur rinpoche]] nearly died of food poisoning in the same place twenty five centuries later. his last words were roughly: everything falls apart. keep going.","struggle":"six years of starving himself, down to a spine you could feel through his stomach, and it got him nothing. then he ate a bowl of rice, and the five holy men who'd been starving alongside him walked off in disgust, sure he'd gone soft.\nunder the tree, the story goes, everything came at him. mara, the demon in buddhist stories who stands for everything that keeps you stuck, threw fear at him, then desire, his whole army. the last thing was mara saying he had no right to sit there. he reached down and touched the ground. the earth was his witness. that's the answer to the doubt. no argument, just, i'm here.\nand after he woke up, he almost didn't teach. he thought nobody would get it. the story has a god named brahma come down and beg him to try, and he looked out at people like lotus flowers at different heights in a pond and figured a few had their heads above the water.","threads":["family","dying","wandering"],"years":"fifth century bc, give or take"},{"after":"after sartre he wrote the fall, the short novel of 1956, and sartre himself, when camus was dead, called it perhaps the most beautiful and the least understood of his books. then the nobel, and the first thing he did with it that matters here is a letter. on november 19, 1957, a month after the news, he wrote to louis germain, the primary school teacher from belcourt: when i heard the news, my first thought, after my mother, was of you. germain wrote back and called him my little camus. he put part of the prize money into the theatre, staging [[dostoevsky]]'s the possessed in paris in 1959 with thirty three actors, and directed it himself. and he went back to the beginning. the pages in the wreck were a novel about his own childhood, the poor boy in algiers, and he'd been writing his own start as the thing to finish.\nwith francine there is no clean ending. he stayed married and he kept casarès. the last note to casarès from lourmarin, written as he got ready to drive north, says: i am so happy at the idea of seeing you again that i am laughing as i write. sartre, who hadn't spoken to him in more than seven years, wrote three days after the crash that their quarrel had been just another way of living together and not losing sight of each other. that's the shape of it. he didn't get a morning after; he got a plane tree. what he left was the argument, that you can turn down god, and turn down the party, and still get up and push.","basically":"a french writer from algeria, raised in a poor quarter of algiers by a deaf mother who couldn't read, who turned out to be the most readable unbeliever of the twentieth century. he said no meaning gets handed down from above, that the universe doesn't answer when you ask it, and that this is no reason to quit, which is why people who do believe keep quoting him anyway. his novel the plague, about a city locked down with a disease and a doctor who keeps working, sold out again in the lockdowns of 2020. think of the friend who won't join the party and won't join the church, who says both sides are bombing civilians, and gets called a fool by one side and a traitor by the other.","born":1913,"died":1960,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='64' cy='58' r='18' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 64 28 L 64 20 M 64 96 L 64 88 M 34 58 L 26 58 M 102 58 L 94 58' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 250 L 322 100 L 338 100 L 380 250 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='226' cy='106' r='40' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 206 96 Q 222 88 238 98' stroke-width='2' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='140' cy='120' r='14'/><circle cx='144' cy='117' r='1.8' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='150' cy='118' r='1.8' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 144 126 Q 149 129 153 125' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 150 134 L 166 162'/><path d='M 150 134 L 187 112 M 152 140 L 189 124'/><path d='M 166 162 L 140 197 M 166 162 L 176 178'/><circle cx='70' cy='236' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 70 228 L 77 233 L 74 241 L 66 241 L 63 233 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.4'/><g transform='translate(318,73) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he said the one serious question is whether to kill yourself, and his whole life was the answer no.","key":"camus","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Camus","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the nobel prize","url":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/literature/1957/camus/speech/","why":"his banquet speech in stockholm, december 1957, in english"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Myth_of_Sisyphus","why":"the essay, section by section"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Plague_(novel)","why":"the novel, with the characters and how it has been read"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"the affairs, and francine. on the night of june 6, 1944, while the allies were landing in normandy, camus went to bed with maria casarès, a spanish-born actress whose father had been prime minister of the spanish republic, and who was playing the lead in his play the misunderstanding, le malentendu. he had been married to francine for four years; the war had kept them apart for two. when she came to paris he chose the marriage, on paper. in a letter he wrote, i know too well that all i need to do is say certain words and turn my back on this part of my life, and a few days later, i will try to make francine happy. four years after the break, on june 6 again, he and casarès ran into each other on the boulevard saint-germain, and from then until his death they didn't stop. 865 letters between them survive, more than a thousand pages, printed by gallimard in 2017. there were others; the accounts of francine's life speak of a string of girlfriends.\nthe cost. in 1953 francine broke down, and the breakdown is laid partly at the door of the casarès affair and his other infidelities. she was hospitalized and given insulin and electroshock, more than thirty shock treatments by one count. the accounts of what she did differ. one has her springing toward a terrace window at home, with camus catching her; another has her going off a balcony, and says nobody knows whether she meant to die or to get out of the hospital. he told casarès she would have succeeded if i had not been quick enough, and said he didn't fully understand her state. to francine he said: they think i'm the guilty one. after the nobel he wrote to a cousin about francine, whom i have never stopped loving in my bad way, and said she had forgiven him. you can take his word on the forgiveness or not. she outlived him by nineteen years and died on december 24, 1979.","moments":["in his nobel banquet speech in stockholm on december 10, 1957, he said a writer cannot put himself in the service of those who make history; he is at the service of those who suffer it. and that the silence of an unknown prisoner, abandoned to humiliations at the other end of the world, is enough to draw the writer out of his exile.","louis germain's reply to the nobel letter, dated april 30, 1959, remembered camus's mother coming to see him about the scholarship list thirty five years earlier. he still called him my little camus, and wished him luck with the play.","the night of june 6, 1944, as the allies landed in normandy, was the night he and maria casarès first went to bed. four years later, on june 6 again, they met by chance on the boulevard saint-germain.","in 1959 he spent part of the nobel money putting dostoevsky's the possessed, les possédés in french, on stage at the théâtre antoine in paris: three parts, twenty four scenes, seven sets, thirty three actors, around four hours. he directed it himself."],"name":"albert camus","place":"mondovi and algiers, then paris and lourmarin","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 14 130 C 16 102 36 94 60 94 C 84 94 104 102 106 130 Z' fill='var(--olive)'/><path d='M 36 100 L 46 80 L 58 102 Z' fill='var(--olive)'/><path d='M 84 100 L 74 80 L 62 102 Z' fill='var(--olive)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 97 M 68 84 L 68 97'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 32 44 20 60 22 C 78 20 94 32 90 60 C 90 46 80 38 60 38 C 40 38 30 46 30 60 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 33 50 C 37 32 50 27 62 28 C 76 29 84 36 87 48 C 80 39 70 36 60 36 C 48 36 40 41 33 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 44 50 Q 50 46 56 49 M 64 49 Q 70 46 76 50' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 58 L 57 66 L 62 66'/><path d='M 52 75 C 56 78 64 78 68 74'/><path d='M 67 73 L 86 67 L 87 70 L 68 76 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='1.4'/><path d='M 86 67 L 87 70' stroke='var(--clay)' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 89 65 C 93 59 87 55 91 49' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"there is but one truly serious philosophical problem, and that is suicide.","where":"the myth of sisyphus, 1942, the first sentence, in justin o'brien's 1955 translation"},{"q":"the struggle itself toward the heights is enough to fill a man's heart. one must imagine sisyphus happy.","where":"the myth of sisyphus, 1942, the last two sentences, in justin o'brien's translation"},{"q":"perhaps we cannot prevent this world from being a world in which children are tortured. but we can reduce the number of tortured children.","where":"the unbeliever and christians, his talk to the dominican friars at latour-maubourg in paris, dated 1948 in resistance, rebellion, and death, translated by justin o'brien"}],"start":"the stranger, which takes an afternoon. then the plague. then the last few pages of the myth of sisyphus, the ones about the rock, before the rest of it.","status":"approved","story":"albert camus was born on november 7, 1913, in mondovi, a farming town in the east of algeria. algeria was then run by france, and not as a colony at arm's length but, on paper, as three departments of france itself, with about a million europeans living in a country where nine people in ten were not european. the french of algeria were called pieds-noirs. his father lucien, a farm worker, was called up when the first world war started and killed in october 1914. albert was eleven months old. his mother catherine, whose people had come from the balearic islands off spain, was deaf and couldn't read or write. the family ended up in belcourt, a poor quarter of algiers, the capital, living with her mother and very little else. his grandmother's plan was for him to leave school and work with his hands. his primary school teacher, louis germain, gave him lessons for free and got him a scholarship in 1924 to the lycée, the academic high school, which was how a poor boy got out. he kept goal for the junior team of racing universitaire d'alger, the university sports club, from 1928 to 1930. then, in 1930, at seventeen, he got tuberculosis, a disease of the lungs that you lived with then rather than got over. that was the end of football. he moved out of his mother's flat and in with his uncle gustave acault, a butcher, and went on studying part time between odd jobs.\na teacher named jean grenier turned him toward philosophy. he got into the university of algiers in 1933 and finished his degree in 1936 with a thesis on plotinus, a greek philosopher of the third century, and [[augustine]], the christian bishop from north africa, and on where greek thought and christian thought met in the two of them. in 1934 he married simone hié, who was addicted to morphine, which she had first taken for period pain. he married her partly to help her get clean, found out she was sleeping with her doctor, and they split in 1936. in 1935 he joined the communist party, and was later thrown out of it for refusing to follow the line. in 1938 he went to work as a reporter for alger républicain, a left wing daily in algiers. the paper was shut down in 1940, and he flew to paris for a layout job on paris-soir, a big daily, and was later laid off. on december 3, 1940, he married francine faure, a pianist and maths teacher from oran, the port city in the west of algeria.\nthe stranger, l'étranger in french, and the myth of sisyphus, le mythe de sisyphe, both came out in 1942 from gallimard, the paris publishing house. one is a short novel and one is an essay, and they make the same argument. the essay starts: there is but one truly serious philosophical problem, and that is suicide. the problem is what he called the absurd, the gap between a person who needs life to mean something and a universe that doesn't answer. he'd begun it in 1940 as the german army came into france and millions of people fled ahead of it. his answer was no, not suicide, and not the other exit either, the jump into faith that he said [[kierkegaard]], the danish christian, had made, which he called philosophical suicide. the honest move was to keep the question open and live in revolt against the silence. the essay ends on sisyphus, the man in the greek myth who chained up death and cheated his way out of the underworld, and was sentenced by the gods to push a boulder up a mountain forever and watch it roll back down each time. the struggle itself toward the heights, camus wrote, is enough to fill a man's heart. one must imagine sisyphus happy. by then he was in the war. the germans held paris, and he worked for the paper combat, which the french resistance had been printing in secret since december 1941, under a false name and with forged identity cards. at the liberation of paris in august 1944 the paper came out into the open with camus as editor in chief, writing an editorial almost every day. in january 1945 it was printing 185,000 copies. on august 8, 1945, two days after the atomic bomb fell on hiroshima, his editorial said the civilization of the machine had just reached its ultimate degree of savagery, and that mankind had to choose between collective suicide and the intelligent use of what science had won.\nhe left the paper in 1947, the year the plague, la peste in french, came out. it's set in oran, sealed off by a disease, and told by a doctor named bernard rieux who keeps working because the work is in front of him. people read it as the occupation of france in disguise, and they still read it: in the first lockdowns of 2020, sales in italy tripled and the english publisher couldn't print it fast enough. one character, tarrou, asks whether one can be a saint without god, and calls it the only problem he's up against. that was camus's own question put plainly. he didn't believe in god and never pretended to. a french scholar who went through his years of talking with the church calls him an agnostic, someone who says he doesn't know, with real esteem for christians. in the late 1940s he spoke at latour-maubourg in paris, a house of dominican friars, an order of catholic preachers, as an unbeliever among believers, and told them that perhaps we cannot prevent this world from being a world in which children are tortured, but we can reduce the number of tortured children. the english edition dates that talk to 1948; a french scholar puts it in december 1946. after the liberation, when paris put its collaborators on trial, the catholic novelist françois mauriac argued for mercy, and camus came to side with him, writing that we are going to need charity. after those trials he turned against the death penalty for good, and in 1957 wrote reflections on the guillotine against it.\nthe rebel, l'homme révolté in french, came out in 1951, a long book about rebellion, the plain no a person says to injustice, and what happens to it when it hardens into revolution. it cost him sartre, the man the papers filed him next to under existentialism, the postwar paris philosophy that says you're free and on your own. camus refused that label all his life. in 1956 he published the fall, la chute. on october 17, 1957, the swedish academy gave him the nobel prize for literature, for his important literary production, which with clear-sighted earnestness illuminates the problems of the human conscience in our times. he was forty four, the second youngest ever to get it, and had expected it to go to andré malraux. by then algeria was at war. the fln, the national liberation front, had begun fighting for independence from france on november 1, 1954, and by 1957 everyone knew the french army was torturing suspects. camus said the colonial system was unjust and brutal and that the fln's bombs on civilians were wrong, and he tried to get both sides to agree to spare civilians. both refused. the fln thought him a fool and some pieds-noirs thought him a traitor. in stockholm that december, on algeria, he said that his mother might be on one of those trams, and that if that was justice, he preferred his mother. the line has been quoted against him ever since. he spent new year 1960 at his house in lourmarin, a village in the south of france, with francine, the twins catherine and jean, born in 1945, and his publisher and friend michel gallimard. francine and the children took the train back to paris on january 2. camus rode north with gallimard in gallimard's car. on january 4, 1960, it hit a plane tree on the main road near villeblevin. camus was in the passenger seat and died at once. he was forty six. gallimard died five days later; his wife and daughter were unhurt. in the wreck were 144 handwritten pages of an unfinished novel of his own childhood, the first man, le premier homme in french, which was published in 1994. he's buried in lourmarin.","struggle":"the body first. tuberculosis at seventeen, in 1930, took the football and took him out of his mother's flat, and in those years it was a disease you carried, not one you got over. he studied part time and worked where he could. a poor boy with bad lungs and a scholarship, in a city where his own grandmother had meant for him to work with his hands.\nthen the public fight. he met jean-paul sartre in 1943, the philosopher who became the loudest voice in paris after the war, and for a while sartre counted him his best friend. then the rebel. in april 1952 les temps modernes, sartre's magazine, ran a review by francis jeanson, a younger philosopher, that tore the book apart. camus answered in the magazine. sartre answered him in august 1952, and the opening line was: my dear camus, our friendship was not easy, but i will miss it. underneath it was politics. sartre thought communism was the only road to real change; camus had rejected it on principle. they never spoke again. camus felt it as a public humiliation, and in his letters to maria casarès he described a curious depression and said he no longer had the desire to live.\nthen algeria, from 1954 until he died. he was a frenchman born there, with a mother still living in algiers, arguing against the french army's torture and against the fln's bombs at the same time, and both sides had a name for him. he kept his mother in the argument and lost the argument.","threads":["doubt","war","the body","love","exile"],"years":"1913 to 1960"},{"after":"after the surrender he spent twenty seven years asking to go home. in 1879 he went to washington and met president rutherford hayes and gave a speech to a big crowd of congressmen and officials. he kept writing and petitioning and talking to anyone who'd listen, and the answer was always no.\nhe never got back to the wallowa. white settlers were farming it. he died at colville, and the doctor there said he died of a broken heart. i can't improve on that.","basically":"a nez perce leader from a green valley in oregon who, in 1877, when the government ordered his band off their land, ended up leading about seven hundred and fifty men, women and kids on a fighting retreat of more than a thousand miles toward canada, beating the army again and again, and gave up forty miles from the border because the children were freezing. his surrender line is one of the most quoted sentences in american history. then the government broke every promise it made him, and he spent the rest of his life asking to go home. think of a refugee column with a leader who became famous for saying he was done. take from him that saying enough, out loud, for the sake of the people behind you, is its own kind of courage.","born":1840,"died":1904,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 220 L 70 150 L 130 200 L 210 120 L 290 200 L 340 160 L 400 210 L 400 300 L 0 300 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 220 C 250 196 290 196 290 220' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='150' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 150 164 L 150 212'/><path d='M 150 212 L 136 246 M 150 212 L 164 246'/><path d='M 150 180 L 172 190 M 150 176 L 172 180'/><path d='M 140 146 C 138 136 162 136 160 146' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 160 176 L 214 180' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 318 250 L 340 210 L 362 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 334 200 L 340 210 L 346 200'/><path d='M 332 250 L 340 226 L 348 250' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.6'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='60' cy='60' r='2'/><circle cx='110' cy='40' r='2'/><circle cx='360' cy='70' r='2'/><\/g><g transform='translate(70,135.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he led his people eleven hundred miles away from the army and surrendered forty miles short of canada.","key":"chiefjoseph","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chief_Joseph","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the national park","url":"https://www.nps.gov/nepe/index.htm","why":"nez perce national historical park, with the sites of the 1877 flight"},{"label":"the nez perce tribe","url":"https://nezperce.org/","why":"the nation's own site"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["about two hundred and thirty of his people, his own daughter among them, slipped through the army lines the night of the surrender and made it to sitting bull's camp in canada.","the historian mark brown wrote that the famous picture of joseph handing over his rifle with that little speech doesn't have a word of truth in it. the last line is the part everybody keeps anyway."],"name":"chief joseph","place":"the wallowa valley in northeastern oregon, then the long flight through idaho, wyoming and montana, then kansas, indian territory, and the colville reservation in washington","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 35 70 C 33 86 35 100 37 114 M 85 70 C 87 86 85 100 83 114' stroke-width='6'/><path d='M 48 38 C 46 20 74 20 72 38 C 66 30 54 30 48 38 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"from where the sun now stands, i will fight no more forever.","where":"the last line of his surrender speech, october 5, 1877, as written down by lieutenant charles erskine scott wood"},{"q":"never sell the bones of your father and your mother.","where":"his father's dying words to him, as he told it"},{"q":"let me be a free man, free to travel, free to stop, free to work, free to think and talk and act for myself.","where":"an indian's view of indian affairs, his account published in the north american review, 1879, paraphrased"}],"start":"chief joseph's own account, an indian's view of indian affairs, from 1879. then kent nerburn's chief joseph and the flight of the nez perce.","status":"approved","story":"he was born on march 3, 1840, in the wallowa valley of northeastern oregon. his name was hin-mah-too-yah-lat-kekt, thunder rolling down the mountain. he was nez perce, a name french traders gave them. their own name is nimíipuu, the people. his father, old joseph, refused to sign an 1863 treaty that gave the wallowa away, and dying, told his son never to sell the bones of his father and mother.\nin 1877 the government ordered the band onto a small reservation in idaho, fast. while they were moving, some young men, angry over old killings, killed white settlers, and the army came. on june 17, 1877, at white bird canyon in idaho, the nez perce beat the cavalry badly. then they ran.\nabout seven hundred and fifty people, most of them not fighters, went more than eleven hundred miles over the mountains through idaho, wyoming and montana, through the new yellowstone national park, heading for canada, where [[sittingbull|sitting bull]] and his lakota had found refuge. they beat or outran several armies. historians now say joseph was more the camp's leader than its war chief, looking after the old people and the children while others led the fighting.\nat the bear paw mountains in montana, about forty miles from canada, the army caught them. after days of fighting in the cold, on october 5, 1877, joseph surrendered to colonel nelson miles. an officer named charles erskine scott wood wrote down the speech, and historians argue over how much of it is joseph's words and how much is wood's. it ends, i will fight no more forever. they promised him his people could go back to idaho. instead they were shipped to kansas and then to indian territory, now oklahoma, where many died. in 1885 he was sent to the colville reservation in washington, never back to the wallowa. he died there on september 21, 1904.","struggle":"the flight itself. four months running with families, old people and babies, through mountains, burying people along the way, with armies coming from every side. by the bear paw it was snowing, they'd lost their horses, and people were freezing in pits they'd dug in the ground.\nthen the exile. in indian territory, a hot, low country nothing like their mountains, his people got sick and died, the children especially. he'd surrendered to save them, and then had to watch them die anyway, in a place he'd been lied into.","threads":["exile","war","grief","the land"],"years":"1840 to 1904"},{"after":"the after is the tour. seventy three and robbed, he sued, won a judgment for millions he never collected, and went back to work. he rehearsed the band for months, walked out in a suit and a fedora, knelt on the stage for the songs he loved, and thanked the audience every night for coming out.\nand at the end he wasn't dodging it. in the summer of 2016 marianne ihlen was dying in norway, and he wrote to her, roughly, that he was right behind her, close enough to take her hand. he died that november. the last album is a man in his eighties telling god he's ready.","basically":"a jewish poet from montreal who became one of the great songwriters, the one who wrote hallelujah, and who spent his whole life circling god and fighting a heavy depression, with a lot of women and wine along the way. in his sixties he lived five years in a zen monastery on a mountain above los angeles, came down to find his manager had taken most of his money, and went back on tour at seventy three and found a bigger audience than he'd ever had. he's for anyone who's tried everything on the dark and is still standing there in a good suit, making something out of it.","born":1934,"died":2016,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--olive)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 130 80 L 190 160 L 240 110 L 380 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 250 250 L 250 206 L 330 206 L 330 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 242 208 L 290 180 L 338 208'/><path d='M 280 250 L 280 222 L 300 222 L 300 250' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 316 210 L 316 213'/><path d='M 309 224 C 309 216 311 213 316 213 C 321 213 323 216 323 224 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 302 226 C 310 222 322 222 330 226' stroke-width='3.5'/><path d='M 238 222 L 250 184'/><circle cx='236' cy='227' r='7' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='232' cy='240' r='10' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='233' cy='236' r='2.5' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='170' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 170 192 L 170 234'/><path d='M 170 234 C 150 234 140 250 156 250 L 184 250 C 200 250 190 234 170 234'/><path d='M 170 208 L 156 234 M 170 208 L 184 234'/><path d='M 162 176 C 164 178 166 178 168 176 M 172 176 C 174 178 176 178 178 176'/><g transform='translate(281,160.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he spent five years as a zen monk, came down to find his manager had taken his money, and went back on the road at seventy three.","key":"cohen","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leonard_Cohen","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his official site","url":"https://www.leonardcohen.com/","why":"the official leonard cohen site"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/You_Want_It_Darker","why":"his last album"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"a lot of women, openly, over decades. a lot of drink, and on tour in the nineties he said he was drinking three bottles of wine before a show. a lot of drugs, acid and speed on hydra and pills later. he never married, and his kids, adam and lorca, grew up with a father who was often somewhere else.","moments":["hallelujah took him years and, he said, dozens of verses. his record company wouldn't release the album it was on in the united states in 1984. john cale, from the velvet underground, recorded it, then jeff buckley, then it turned up in shrek, the cartoon about an ogre, and then everyone sang it.","the house on hydra cost him about fifteen hundred dollars in 1960, money his grandmother had left him.","accepting a prize in spain in 2011 he told a story about a young spanish guitarist in montreal who taught him a six chord flamenco progression over a few lessons and then didn't come back, because he'd taken his own life. cohen said all his songs were built on those chords."],"name":"leonard cohen","place":"montreal, hydra, new york, a mountain above los angeles","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 36 43 C 36 22 84 22 84 43 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 26 44 C 40 40 80 40 94 44 C 84 48 36 48 26 44 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 37 38 L 83 38' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='3'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"there is a crack in everything.","where":"the song anthem, 1992, just the start of the line; the rest is about how the light gets in"},{"q":"i'm not looking for a new religion, i'm happy with the old one.","where":"interviews in the nineties about mount baldy, paraphrased"},{"q":"hineni, here i am.","where":"you want it darker, the title song of his last album, 2016; hineni is the hebrew word abraham answers god with in the bible, and he sang it with the choir of the montreal synagogue he grew up in"}],"start":"book of longing, the poems and drawings from the mountain years. then the album you want it darker, start to finish. then the favourite game, his first novel, if you want him young.","status":"approved","story":"born in montreal in 1934 into a prominent jewish family in westmount, the well-off english-speaking part of the city. his mother's father was a rabbi. his father died when he was nine, and he said he cut open one of his father's bow ties, put a note inside, and buried it in the garden, and that was the first thing he ever wrote.\nhe was a poet first. let us compare mythologies, his first book of poems, in 1956, then novels, then a house on hydra, a small greek island with no cars, in 1960, where he lived with marianne ihlen, the norwegian woman the song so long, marianne is about, and wrote. in his early thirties, broke, he decided to try songs. the folk singer judy collins recorded his suzanne, and his first album came out in 1967.\nhe knew his zen teacher, joshu sasaki, for decades before moving up to the mount baldy zen center, a bare-bones monastery high in the mountains above los angeles, in the mid nineties. zen is the japanese school of buddhism that's mostly sitting still and paying attention. he cooked for the old man, sat in the cold, and in 1996 was ordained a monk under the name jikan, usually given as the silent one. he said he wasn't looking for a new religion. he was a jew who sat.\nhe came down in 1999. in 2004 he found out his manager had taken most of his retirement money, millions, while he was up on the mountain. in 2008, at seventy three, he went back on tour, played hundreds of shows over the next five years, and came out the other side with his money back and a new audience. his last album, you want it darker, came out in october 2016. he died seventeen days later.","struggle":"depression, for most of his life, the heavy kind, the background of everything. he tried drink, drugs, women, fame, zen, and prayer on it. he said in interviews that it finally started to lift in his sixties, and he wasn't sure which thing did it, or whether it just wore out.\nand the mountain wasn't peaceful. he got up before dawn to sit in the cold and told interviewers he was bad at it. after he came down he went to mumbai in 1999 to study with ramesh balsekar, a retired indian banker who taught that there's no separate self doing anything, still looking.","threads":["madness","money","silence","dying"],"years":"1934 to 2016"},{"after":"after the shooting he healed, with a scar by his nose for the rest of his life. black buffalo woman went back to no water. crazy horse didn't try to get the title back. he stopped sitting in the councils and went back to being what he was good at, a fighter who took nothing for himself. a year later he married black shawl.\nafter his daughter died he kept fighting, and he kept to himself more than before. there's no redemption arc. there's a man who got it wrong once, publicly, took the loss, and went on being useful to his people until they killed him for it.","basically":"the oglala lakota war leader of the 1870s, quiet, strange, and so brave the men who rode with him talked about it for the rest of their lives. he never signed a treaty, never went to washington, and never let anyone make him into a picture. he also ran off with another man's wife and got shot in the face for it. picture the guy in your unit who never talked about himself and who everybody would have followed anywhere. take from him that you don't have to explain yourself to be trusted.","born":1840,"died":1877,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 80 250 L 100 180 L 120 250 M 100 180 L 100 250'/><rect x='82' y='150' width='40' height='30' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='122' cy='165' r='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 82 150 C 70 160 70 180 76 192' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='250' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 250 164 L 250 210'/><path d='M 250 210 L 234 246 M 250 210 L 268 242'/><path d='M 250 178 L 236 200 M 250 178 L 270 186'/><path d='M 256 144 L 266 124' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 280 200 C 280 186 340 184 346 198 L 360 172 L 372 178 L 356 202 C 350 212 284 214 280 200 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 288 208 L 286 250 M 300 210 L 302 250 M 338 210 L 336 250 M 348 206 L 350 250'/><path d='M 280 200 C 270 204 268 216 272 224'/><path d='M 270 186 C 290 180 300 184 330 192' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(102,133.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"nobody ever got a confirmed photograph of him, and he's the one they're carving into a mountain.","key":"crazyhorse","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crazy_Horse","why":"the plain overview, including what's legend and what isn't"},{"label":"the battlefield","url":"https://www.nps.gov/libi/index.htm","why":"the national park service site at the little bighorn"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"around 1870 a woman named black buffalo woman, whom he'd loved since they were young, left her husband, a man named no water, and rode off with crazy horse on a war party. no water came after them, found them in a lodge, and shot crazy horse in the face from close range. the bullet broke his upper jaw. it nearly set off a feud between whole bands, and older men had to settle it with gifts of horses.\nit cost him. he'd been made a shirt wearer, one of four men chosen to keep the peace in the camp and put the people ahead of themselves, and they took it away from him for this. he'd put himself first, over a married woman, and everybody knew it. people being stupid about love, and it had a price.","moments":["the story goes that when someone wanted to take his photograph, he asked why they'd want to shorten his life by taking his shadow. that's legend. what's true is that valentine mcgillycuddy, the army doctor who knew him at fort robinson, doubted any picture of him was ever made.","in 1948 a sculptor named korczak ziółkowski started blasting a huge figure of him on horseback out of a mountain in the black hills, the lakota's holy ground. the head was finished in 1998. plenty of lakota think carving up a sacred mountain to honor a man who wouldn't be photographed is exactly backwards."],"name":"crazy horse","place":"the powder river country and the black hills, then fort robinson in nebraska","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 80 34 C 86 22 92 12 98 4 C 98 16 92 28 84 36 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 82 35 L 97 7' opacity='.5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"it is no good for the people to depend on me any longer.","where":"his dying words at fort robinson, september 1877, to his father, as recorded by those in the room"},{"q":"hoka hey, it's a good day to die.","where":"attributed to him at the little bighorn. hoka hey means something like let's go, and the line was a common lakota rallying cry, not his alone"},{"q":"one does not sell the earth the people walk on.","where":"attributed; the source is shaky, and it gets quoted anyway because it sounds like what he did"}],"start":"joseph marshall iii's the journey of crazy horse, a life of him told by a lakota writer from lakota stories. then the chapters in black elk speaks about the killing of crazy horse.","status":"approved","story":"he was born around 1840, near the black hills in what's now south dakota, into the oglala, one of the seven bands of the lakota. as a boy he was called curly, for his light wavy hair. his father was a holy man named crazy horse, and when the son proved himself as a young fighter, the father gave him that name and took another one, worm. [[blackelk|black elk]], the holy man whose vision became a famous book, was his second cousin, and was a boy in the same camps.\nthe story goes that as a young man he fasted alone and had a vision of a rider coming out of a lake, dressed plain, a small stone behind one ear and a single hawk feather in his hair, riding untouched through a storm of bullets and hail. he was told to ride like that, and to take nothing for himself. the man who first told that story to white writers wasn't lakota, and chips, the holy man who actually worked with crazy horse, said the part about the lake wasn't true. so it's a story. what's certain is that he did ride into fights plain, took no scalps or loot for himself, and gave away what he had.\nin december 1866 he led the decoys who drew captain william fetterman and eighty soldiers out of their fort in wyoming and into an ambush. none of them came back. in june 1876 he fought at the rosebud and then at the little bighorn, where custer's command was wiped out. the army hunted the free bands all that winter, and his people froze and starved.\non may 6, 1877, he led about nine hundred people into fort robinson, in nebraska, and surrendered. there were rumors, jealousies among the lakota leaders, and nervous officers who believed he'd break out. on september 5 they walked him to the guardhouse. when he saw the cell he fought, and a soldier ran a bayonet into him. he died that night, in his thirties. his parents took the body away and buried it where nobody has ever found it.","struggle":"in 1871 he married a woman named black shawl, and they had one child, a daughter named they are afraid of her. she died in 1873, a little girl, while he was away fighting. he found her burial scaffold, the raised platform the lakota laid the dead on, and lay on it beside her body for three days and nights.\nthen the winter of 1876 into 1877. soldiers burning the camps, the buffalo nearly gone, children dying of cold. he held out as long as he could, and surrendered to keep the people alive. he knew what it would cost him.","threads":["war","love","grief","silence"],"years":"around 1840 to 1877"},{"after":"he didn't end a grief observed with an answer. he ended it with something quieter. he wrote that maybe his own frantic shouting had deafened him to the voice he wanted to hear, and that the less he clawed at her memory the nearer she seemed. he kept teaching, looked after joy's two sons, and went back to church.\nhe died three years later, a week before his sixty fifth birthday, at home outside oxford. hardly anyone noticed for a day or two, because it was the day kennedy was shot.","basically":"the oxford professor who wrote the narnia books, the ones with the wardrobe and the lion, and who before that was a hard-headed atheist until his friend tolkien, the lord of the rings guy, talked him the rest of the way into christianity on a walk one night. he became the most famous defender of christianity in the english speaking world, with an answer for everything. then he married late, lost his wife to cancer four years later, and wrote a small, furious notebook saying god felt like a door slammed in his face. if you've ever had your neat answers fall apart when somebody died, he wrote the answers and then watched them fall apart.","born":1898,"died":1963,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 260 L 380 260'/><path d='M 200 80 L 172 94 L 172 252 L 200 260 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 320 80 L 348 94 L 348 252 L 320 260 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='200' y='80' width='120' height='180' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 194 80 L 326 80'/><rect x='212' y='94' width='96' height='156' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 212 236 C 240 228 280 240 308 232 L 308 250 L 212 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 260 236 L 260 140 M 254 236 L 266 236'/><path d='M 252 124 L 268 124 L 266 140 L 254 140 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><g fill='var(--paper)' stroke='none'><circle cx='228' cy='120' r='2.5'/><circle cx='290' cy='110' r='2.5'/><circle cx='240' cy='170' r='2.5'/><circle cx='292' cy='190' r='2.5'/><circle cx='226' cy='210' r='2.5'/><circle cx='280' cy='150' r='2.5'/><\/g><circle cx='100' cy='164' r='14'/><path d='M 100 178 L 100 226'/><path d='M 100 226 L 86 260 M 100 226 L 114 260'/><path d='M 100 192 L 86 216 M 100 192 L 126 186'/><path d='M 106 170 L 116 174'/><path d='M 114 168 L 122 168 L 121 176 L 115 176 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 118 164 C 114 154 122 150 118 140' stroke-dasharray='2 5' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(250,53.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"an atheist oxford don got talked into christ on a night walk with tolkien, and then his wife died and he wrote that god had bolted the door.","key":"cslewis","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._S._Lewis","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/A_Grief_Observed","why":"the notebook he kept after joy died"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"the thirty years with janie moore were private and odd, and he hid the arrangement from his father for years. he never explained it, and he didn't owe anyone an explanation.","moments":["narnia started, he said, with a picture he'd had in his head since he was about sixteen, a faun, a little goat-legged man out of the old myths, carrying an umbrella and parcels through a snowy wood.","tolkien read him the lord of the rings in pieces for years at the inklings, a group of oxford friends who met to read their work out loud to each other. tolkien said later that he owed lewis an unpayable debt, mostly for the sheer encouragement.","joy davidman first wrote to him as a reader in 1950, from new york, a jewish-born former communist who'd become a christian partly through his books."],"name":"c.s. lewis","place":"belfast, then oxford, then cambridge","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 70 78 L 86 86' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 84 78 L 96 78 L 94 92 L 86 92 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"no one ever told me that grief felt so like fear.","where":"a grief observed, the notebook he kept after joy died, the first line"},{"q":"i believe in christianity as i believe that the sun has risen, not only because i see it, but because by it i see everything else.","where":"is theology poetry?, a 1945 essay"},{"q":"the most dejected and reluctant convert in all england.","where":"surprised by joy, his memoir of how he came to believe, chapter 14, about 1929"}],"start":"a grief observed, which you'll read in one night. then surprised by joy, his memoir, chapters 14 and 15, for the conversion.","status":"approved","story":"clive staples lewis, jack to everyone, was born in belfast in 1898. his mother died of cancer when he was nine. his father sent him to english boarding schools he hated, and by his teens he was an atheist.\nhe went to the trenches of the first world war as a young officer and was wounded in france in april 1918 by a british shell that fell short. he'd made a pact with a friend in training, paddy moore, that if one of them died the other would look after the dead man's family. paddy was killed. lewis took in paddy's mother, janie moore, twenty six years older than him, and lived with her for about thirty years, until she died in 1951.\nhe became an oxford don, which is what oxford calls its teachers, teaching english literature at magdalen college. in 1929 he gave in and admitted god existed, the most dejected and reluctant convert in all england, he said. on the night of september 19, 1931, he walked addison's walk, a path along the river behind the college, with j.r.r. tolkien, who'd go on to write the lord of the rings, and hugo dyson, another literature teacher, into the small hours. lewis had always loved the old myths, the norse gods and the pagan gods who die and come back. tolkien argued that the story of christ was that same kind of story, except this one had actually happened. a few days later, riding to the zoo in the sidecar of his brother's motorbike, he found he believed it.\nthen the books. the screwtape letters, a senior devil's letters coaching a junior devil on how to ruin a man. mere christianity, which started as radio talks during the second world war explaining the faith to ordinary people. the chronicles of narnia, seven children's books about kids who get into another world. a pile of others. in 1956 he married joy davidman, an american writer, in a registry office, a quick civil ceremony at a government office, so she could stay in britain. months later she had cancer. in 1957 they were married again, by a priest this time, at her hospital bed. she went into remission and they got a few good years. she died in 1960. he died on november 22, 1963, the same day as [[huxley|aldous huxley]], the novelist who wrote brave new world, and president john kennedy.","struggle":"a grief observed is the struggle, written in notebooks after joy died. he says that when you're happy god seems to be there with open arms, and when you're desperate you get a door slammed in your face and the sound of bolting and double bolting on the inside. he wondered on paper whether god was a cosmic sadist. the man who'd explained suffering to the english in the problem of pain, his 1940 book on why a good god lets people hurt, couldn't stand what it did to him.\nhe published it in 1961 under a made-up name, n.w. clerk, so nobody would know the famous defender of the faith had come apart. the story goes that friends who didn't know started handing him copies to help with his grief.","threads":["grief","doubt","love"],"years":"1898 to 1963"},{"after":"nathan the prophet came and told him a story. a rich man with huge flocks had a guest to feed, and instead of killing one of his own sheep he took a poor man's only lamb, the one that ate off the poor man's plate and slept in his arms like a daughter. david was furious and said the man deserved to die. nathan said, you are the man. david didn't argue. he said i have sinned against the lord. the baby got sick, and david lay on the ground and fasted for seven days, and the child died anyway. he got up, washed, ate, and said he'd go to the child someday and the child wouldn't come back to him.\npsalm 51 is the after, and it doesn't make one excuse. nothing about her being where a king could see, nothing about the war. wash me, make me a clean heart, a broken spirit is what you want. it does say against you, you only, have i sinned, and uriah would have had something to say about that. but he didn't excuse it, and he didn't get to undo it. nathan told him the sword would never leave his house, and it didn't.","basically":"the shepherd boy who killed goliath with a sling, became israel's greatest king about three thousand years ago, and wrote a lot of the psalms, the prayer songs people still read at funerals. at the height of his power he had a soldier's wife brought to him, a woman who had no way to say no to a king, got her pregnant, and had her husband killed to cover it up. then a prophet looked him in the eye and said you're the man, and he didn't argue. the worst of it is on the page, and if you've ever done something you can't undo and had somebody name it to your face, psalm 51 is what he wrote after.","born":null,"died":-970,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 70 L 380 70 L 380 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 294 70 L 386 70'/><rect x='326' y='100' width='26' height='30' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 44 250 C 36 222 40 206 48 200 M 72 250 C 80 222 76 206 68 200 M 44 250 L 72 250 M 42 212 L 74 212 M 52 212 L 52 250 M 58 212 L 58 250 M 64 212 L 64 250'/><circle cx='120' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 120 192 L 120 234'/><path d='M 120 234 C 100 234 90 250 106 250 L 134 250 C 150 250 140 234 120 234'/><path d='M 120 208 L 106 234 M 120 208 L 134 234'/><path d='M 114 186 L 126 186'/><path d='M 158 250 L 158 236 L 164 243 L 170 232 L 176 243 L 182 236 L 182 250 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 194 236 C 188 226 200 218 208 224 C 214 216 228 222 224 232 C 230 238 220 246 212 242 C 204 248 192 244 194 236 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='230' cy='226' rx='6' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 202 243 L 202 250 M 216 243 L 216 250'/><circle cx='262' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 262 168 L 262 216'/><path d='M 262 216 L 248 250 M 262 216 L 276 250'/><path d='M 262 182 L 276 206 M 262 182 L 236 176'/><g transform='translate(330,45.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the shepherd who killed the giant and wrote the psalms took another man's wife, had the man killed, and then wrote psalm 51.","key":"david","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David","why":"the plain overview, the story and the history"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","why":"the king james bible on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"in the spring, when kings went out to war, david stayed home and sent the army. from the roof of the palace he saw a woman bathing, bathsheba, the wife of uriah the hittite, one of his own soldiers, who was off at the siege. david sent men to bring her and he slept with her. he was the king and she was his subject, and the text never says she had any say in it. then she sent him word that she was pregnant.\nhe called uriah home from the front so he'd sleep with his wife and the baby would pass as his. uriah wouldn't go home while his comrades were sleeping in the field. david got him drunk and he still wouldn't. so david wrote a letter to his general, joab, telling him to put uriah in the front line where the fighting was worst and then pull back from him, and he sent the letter by uriah's own hand. uriah died. when the mourning was over david married bathsheba. a king who took a woman who couldn't say no and then had her husband killed. everything after has to be read with that sitting there.","moments":["on the run, he and his men were hiding at the back of a cave at en-gedi, an oasis by the dead sea, when saul came in alone to relieve himself. david crept up and cut off a corner of his robe instead of killing him, and then felt bad about even that. 1 samuel 24.","when jonathan, saul's son and his closest friend, was killed in battle, david wrote a lament that says jonathan's love for him was more wonderful than the love of women. 2 samuel 1.","running from absalom, a man named shimei followed him along the road throwing stones and cursing him. one of david's men offered to go take his head off. david said leave him alone, maybe the lord told him to. 2 samuel 16.","the line about a man after god's own heart is from 1 samuel 13, said before any of this happened. the new testament repeats it in acts 13, in a sermon of paul's. the book knew what it was saying and kept the rest in anyway."],"name":"david","place":"bethlehem, the hills of judah, jerusalem","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 36 42 L 36 20 L 47 30 L 60 16 L 73 30 L 84 20 L 84 42 C 70 36 50 36 36 42 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i have sinned against the lord.","where":"2 samuel 12:13, the whole of his answer to nathan the prophet"},{"q":"create in me a clean heart, o god.","where":"psalm 51:10; the psalm is headed as his, from when nathan came to him"},{"q":"o my son absalom, my son, my son absalom, would i had died instead of you.","where":"2 samuel 18:33, paraphrased slightly"}],"start":"2 samuel 11 and 12 in one sitting, in the old testament, the first and bigger part of any bible. then psalm 51 straight after. then 2 samuel 18 for absalom. 1 samuel 17 for goliath if you want the part everyone knows.","status":"approved","story":"the youngest of jesse's sons in bethlehem, the small town where jesus would be born a thousand years later. he was out with the sheep when the prophet samuel came looking for a king. a prophet in the bible is someone who speaks for god, and samuel was the one who made and unmade israel's kings. they had to send for him. samuel poured oil on his head in front of his older brothers, which is how you made someone king.\nthen goliath. the philistines were the people on the coast, israel's enemies, and their champion was a giant who stood in the valley of elah for forty days daring anyone to fight him. a kid with a sling and five smooth stones from the brook walked out. one stone. king saul, israel's first king, took him in, got jealous of him, and threw a spear at him while he played the lyre, a small harp. david spent years on the run in the hills with a band of men who were in debt or in trouble, and he had saul at his mercy twice and let him go.\nhe became king, first at hebron and then in jerusalem, and when they brought the ark of the covenant into the city, the gold-covered chest that held the stone tablets of the law and was the holiest thing israel had, he danced in front of it so wildly that his wife michal said he'd made a fool of himself. about half the psalms, the hundred and fifty prayer songs in the middle of the bible, carry his name. scholars argue over how many he wrote. the twenty third, the lord is my shepherd, the one they read at funerals, carries it. so does the twenty second, the one that starts my god, my god, why have you forsaken me, which [[jesus]] quoted from the cross.\nthen 2 samuel 11, the chapter where it all goes wrong, which is in the mess below. then his son absalom took the kingdom from him, and david walked out of jerusalem barefoot and weeping up the mount of olives, the hill just east of the city. he got the kingdom back. absalom died in the fighting, and when the news came david went up to the room over the gate and cried, my son absalom, would i had died instead of you.","struggle":"most of the psalms with his name on them are a man in trouble. hiding in caves, hunted, betrayed by friends, asking god how long. psalm 13 asks it four times in two verses. these are the prayers of somebody who was scared a lot and said so.\nand then the family. his son amnon raped his half sister tamar, and 2 samuel says david was very angry, and it doesn't say he did anything. absalom, tamar's brother, waited two years and killed amnon, ran, came back, and turned the country against his father. the king who could handle a giant couldn't handle his own house.","threads":["forgiveness","family"],"year":-1000,"years":"around 1000 bc"},{"after":"after the books made him famous, he didn't just stay a pundit. he taught, for decades, and a lot of the native lawyers who fought for treaty rights and tribal courts in the eighties and nineties sat in his classes. he wrote on law, on treaties, on religion, over twenty books.\nand he kept the argument with christianity going instead of walking away from it. that's what god is red is, a man who took his father's religion seriously enough to fight with it for his whole life.","basically":"a standing rock sioux writer, lawyer and professor, the son and grandson of episcopal priests, who went to seminary himself and then wrote the funniest, angriest books ever aimed at the people who'd been managing native americans. custer died for your sins, in 1969, went after the anthropologists, the missionaries, and the government, and made white readers laugh while it cut them. then god is red, in 1973, argued that native religion was not a primitive version of christianity but a different and in some ways better way of being religious. think of a stand-up comic with a law degree and a theology degree, who knows the church from inside. take from him that you can argue with the faith you were raised in, all the way down, and still take religion seriously.","born":1933,"died":2005,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='120' cy='160' r='14'/><path d='M 120 174 L 120 216'/><path d='M 120 216 C 100 216 90 232 106 232 L 134 232 C 150 232 140 216 120 216'/><path d='M 120 190 L 106 216 M 120 190 L 134 216'/><rect x='150' y='196' width='50' height='26' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 150 222 L 210 222 L 214 232 L 146 232 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='160' y='176' width='30' height='22' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 130 170 L 140 172' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 144 166 C 148 158 142 152 148 144' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 280 250 L 280 170 L 340 170 L 340 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 272 172 L 310 120 L 348 172'/><path d='M 300 130 L 300 80 L 320 80 L 320 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 296 82 L 310 58 L 324 82'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 214 L 320 214 L 320 250' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.5'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><rect x='176' y='170' width='14' height='6'/><rect x='196' y='238' width='20' height='4'/><\/g><g transform='translate(310,43.1) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"his father and grandfather were episcopal priests, and he wrote the book that told the missionaries to go home.","key":"deloria","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vine_Deloria_Jr.","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Custer_Died_for_Your_Sins","why":"the 1969 book that made him"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/God_Is_Red","why":"his book arguing with christianity"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"in red earth, white lies, in 1995, he went after mainstream science itself, the idea that native people came over a land bridge from asia and the scientists' dates for the ice age, and argued for native oral history over them. scientists said he got a lot of it wrong, and a lot of his admirers wince at that book. he was a fighter, and sometimes he picked a fight he couldn't win.","moments":["an original copy of custer died for your sins sits in the permanent exhibit at the smithsonian's national museum of the american indian.","in 1979 he wrote the introduction to a new edition of [[blackelk|black elk speaks]], calling it a religious classic for native people."],"name":"vine deloria jr.","place":"martin, south dakota, near pine ridge, then washington, and universities in arizona and colorado","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><rect x='41' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><rect x='62' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><path d='M 58 55 L 62 55 M 41 54 L 34 52 M 79 54 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 68 76 L 86 80' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 90 76 C 94 70 90 66 94 60' opacity='.4'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"indians have been cursed above all other people in history. indians have anthropologists.","where":"custer died for your sins, 1969, the chapter anthropologists and other friends"},{"q":"christianity is a religion of time. native religions are religions of place.","where":"god is red, 1973, paraphrased; it's the spine of the whole book"},{"q":"the churches should put their theology aside and help the tribes, instead of trying to convert them.","where":"custer died for your sins, the chapter on missionaries, paraphrased"}],"start":"custer died for your sins, the chapters on anthropologists and on missionaries. then god is red, the first few chapters.","status":"approved","story":"he was born on march 26, 1933, in martin, south dakota, near the pine ridge reservation, and was an enrolled member of the standing rock sioux tribe. his grandfather, philip deloria, a yankton dakota leader, had become an episcopal priest, the american branch of the church of england. his father, vine deloria sr., was an episcopal archdeacon and missionary on the reservations. his aunt ella deloria was a well known scholar of the dakota language.\nhe served in the marines from 1954 to 1956, then went to seminary, and in 1963 earned a theology degree from augustana, a lutheran seminary in rock island, illinois. he looked like he'd be the third generation in the pulpit. instead, from 1964 to 1967 he ran the national congress of american indians, the biggest native political organization in the country. then he went to law school, and finished at the university of colorado in 1970.\nin 1969 he published custer died for your sins: an indian manifesto. custer was the cavalry commander wiped out at the little bighorn in 1876, and the title throws the country's own religion back at it. the book is eleven essays, and it's funny. the chapter on anthropologists, the scholars who kept coming out to study native people, opens by saying indians have been cursed above all other people, because indians have anthropologists. the chapter on the churches says the missionaries should stop converting indians and start helping the tribes.\nin 1973 came god is red. its argument is that christianity is a religion of time, of history marching toward an end, and native religions are religions of place, tied to particular land. and that a religion that forgot the land had helped wreck it. he taught for years at the university of arizona and the university of colorado, and trained a generation of native lawyers and scholars. he died in golden, colorado, on november 13, 2005.","struggle":"he was raised inside the church, by men who'd given their lives to it, and he went to seminary to join them. then he spent the rest of his life arguing that the whole missionary project had been a disaster for his people. i don't know what that cost at home. the books are what's left, and they don't sound like a man who found it easy.\nand he was writing into a country that mostly thought of native people as sad history. being funny about it was a choice, and it didn't come from nowhere.","threads":["anger","doubt","the land"],"years":"1933 to 2005"},{"after":"the morning after the dog bite she went home and stayed. for several years she meditated in the house while raising dipa, and when she went back it was with dipa and her sister hema in tow. that became the teaching. when she had students of her own she didn't send them off to a monastery. she gave the widow with six children a practice she could do while nursing, the businesswoman five minutes a day, the bank clerk his busyness. always know what you are doing, one student remembers her saying. you cannot separate meditation from life. being a wife and being a mother, she told another, were her first teachers.\nin calcutta her door stayed open from early morning until late at night, and nobody who climbed the stairs was turned away. when dipa worried about the crowds she said they were hungry for the dhamma, the buddha's teaching, so let them come. she never took ordination, had no center and no title, and taught from a straw mat in one room until she died. i'm living, that's all, she told engler. that's enough.","basically":"a bengali buddhist housewife, married at twelve, who lost two babies and then her husband, and was so sick with grief and heart trouble in her forties that she went off to a meditation center in burma expecting to die there, and turned out to be very good at it. thrown out of burma in 1967, she landed in one room in calcutta with her daughter and taught meditation to women with six kids and a shop to run, a few minutes at a time, while the baby nursed. a handful of young americans found her in the 1970s and went home to build the retreat center in massachusetts that a good share of what america now calls mindfulness runs through. think of the grandmother nobody outside the neighborhood has heard of who turns out to have trained every chef in town.","born":1911,"died":1989,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 112 L 104 112'/><path d='M 60 112 Q 72 92 84 112 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='72' cy='96' r='7' fill='var(--marigold)'/><rect x='40' y='226' width='36' height='24' fill='var(--clay)'/><ellipse cx='58' cy='224' rx='16' ry='6' fill='var(--teal)'/><path d='M 52 214 Q 56 206 52 198 M 64 214 Q 68 206 64 198' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 96 248 L 204 248 L 200 238 L 100 238 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 136 148 Q 150 126 164 148' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='150' cy='152' r='14'/><circle cx='145' cy='152' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='155' cy='152' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 145 158 Q 150 162 155 158' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 150 166 L 150 206'/><path d='M 138 176 Q 150 184 162 176' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 150 206 L 118 226 L 160 236 M 150 206 L 182 226 L 140 236'/><path d='M 150 178 L 132 204 L 150 210 M 150 178 L 168 204 L 150 210'/><rect x='290' y='120' width='62' height='130' fill='var(--paper)'/><g opacity='.75'><circle cx='318' cy='162' r='10'/><path d='M 318 172 L 318 212 M 318 212 L 308 250 M 318 212 L 328 250'/><path d='M 318 184 L 302 196 L 318 202'/><circle cx='303' cy='197' r='5' fill='var(--peach)'/><\/g><g opacity='.75'><circle cx='372' cy='146' r='10'/><path d='M 372 156 L 372 206 M 372 206 L 362 250 M 372 206 L 382 250 M 372 172 L 360 190 M 372 172 L 384 190'/><rect x='378' y='160' width='12' height='24' fill='var(--olive)'/><\/g><g transform='translate(306,93) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a widow with a bad heart went to learn how to die, and ended up teaching the people who brought insight meditation to america.","key":"dipama","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dipa_Ma","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"lion's roar","url":"https://www.lionsroar.com/mother-of-light-the-inspiring-story-of-dipa-ma/","why":"amy schmidt's telling of her life, from the book"},{"label":"tricycle","url":"https://tricycle.org/?p=3787","why":"jack engler's 1977 interview with her, in her own words"},{"label":"dharma seed","url":"https://dharmaseed.org/retreats/4228/","why":"her talks from the 1984 retreat in barre, recorded"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"to go to the meditation center the first time she gave her money and her belongings to a neighbor and left her daughter, still a child, to be raised by that neighbor. she meant never to come back. she was dying, as far as anyone knew, and the center was where she wanted to die. it was still a child left behind, and dipa's terror when her mother came home is in every telling. the second time she went, dipa went with her.\nthe other thing is the powers. her students tell stories, in amy schmidt's book and in jack kornfield's talks, of her reading minds, being in two places at once and traveling through time. these are students' stories, most of them told after she died, and nobody tested any of it. what's documented is what she told engler in 1977: that she had trained in the siddhis under munindra, that she had once walked into a professor's room at the university near bodh gaya and talked with him while a student sat watching her meditate, that the powers tend to inflate the ego, and that she had let them go. you can take her at her word or not. she didn't ask anyone to. she was also hard on people. four hours of sleep a night. don't be lazy, she told joseph goldstein, and once had him sit for two days straight. that's a style, not a sin, but it isn't for everyone.","moments":["her first day at the kamayut center in rangoon, a few years after her husband died, a dog sank its teeth into her leg on the path to the hall. she said she felt no pain while it held on. the monks pulled it off, the hospital gave her rabies shots, and she went home.","calcutta, 1974. she told sharon salzberg, a young american passing through, that when she got back to the united states she would be teaching with joseph. salzberg had no such plan. the next year the two of them helped found a retreat center together.","late 1970s, calcutta. jack kornfield, with a bad back, a failed relationship and no confidence in himself, knelt in front of her. she stroked his head, breathed on him and chanted loving-kindness over him for about ten minutes, then told him to go and teach, with a mother's blessings. he says he smiled through the taxi ride, the airport and the whole flight to bangkok.","barre, massachusetts, september 28, 1984. on the second day of the three month retreat a seventy three year old woman from calcutta gave the talk on taking refuge and keeping the precepts. on october 8 she told the room her own story. both talks are still on tape."],"name":"dipa ma","place":"chittagong, then rangoon, then calcutta","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 16 130 C 18 104 36 96 60 96 C 84 96 102 104 104 130 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 16 130 C 18 104 36 96 60 96 C 84 96 102 104 104 130 L 95 130 C 93 111 80 104 60 104 C 40 104 27 111 25 130 Z' fill='var(--blue)' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 97 M 68 84 L 68 97'/><path d='M 32 56 C 26 53 26 67 32 65 M 88 56 C 94 53 94 67 88 65'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 33 50 C 35 31 48 25 60 25 C 72 25 85 31 87 50 C 80 42 70 38 60 38 C 50 38 40 42 33 50 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 35 L 42 41 M 52 30 L 52 36 M 60 29 L 60 35 M 68 30 L 68 36 M 76 35 L 78 41' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 44 51 Q 50 48 56 51 M 64 51 Q 70 48 76 51' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='50' cy='58' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='58' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 79 64 79 69 74'/><path d='M 43 67 Q 45 73 49 77 M 77 67 Q 75 73 71 77' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 56 102 C 51 110 51 121 57 128 L 63 128 C 69 121 69 110 64 102 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 60 103 L 60 127' stroke-width='1.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"you can do anything you want to do. it's only your thinking you can't do it that's going to stop you.","where":"as sharon salzberg remembers it, in her account of her teachers for the insight meditation society"},{"q":"if you are busy, then busyness is the meditation.","where":"to her calcutta student dipak, a bank employee, as he remembered it for amy schmidt's dipa ma: the life and legacy of a buddhist master"},{"q":"anger is a fire, but i don't feel any heat.","where":"to jack engler in calcutta in 1977, translated from bengali, printed in tricycle magazine in spring 2004"}],"start":"amy schmidt's dipa ma: the life and legacy of a buddhist master, which is short and mostly her students talking. then jack engler's 1977 interview with her, printed in tricycle in 2004, where she speaks for herself. then the 1984 talks from barre, if you want her voice.","status":"approved","story":"she was born nani bala barua on march 25, 1911, in a village on the plains near chittagong, a port in the east of bengal, then british india and now bangladesh. her people were baruas, a community of bengali buddhists who had kept the religion going in that corner of the country long after it faded from the rest of india. they kept the rituals. meditation had nearly died out there and was left to monks and nuns. as a girl she would rather study than play, helped the monks at the temple and made little statues of [[buddha|the buddha]]. she went to the village school through the sixth standard and was married at twelve, in 1923, to rajani ranjan barua, an engineer of twenty five. a week after the wedding he left for a job in rangoon, the capital of burma, the country next door that is now called myanmar. she lived two years with his parents and joined him there at fourteen. decades later she told an interviewer he was gentle and patient, and that he was her first teacher. she asked him if she could learn to meditate. he said yes, in principle, once she had raised children.\nchildren didn't come. her mother died in 1929, when she was eighteen, and she said she caught typhoid right after and didn't get over the loss until she had a child of her own. she and her husband took in her baby brother, bijoy, and raised him. one account calls him her nephew. her husband told her to treat everyone she met as her own child. she was about thirty five when her first daughter was born, and the baby died at three months. a daughter born in 1950, after twenty seven years of marriage, lived, and was named dipa. dipa ma is what people called her after that. it means dipa's mother. the next year a son died in childbirth, and she never saw him. in 1957 her husband came home from work one afternoon feeling ill and died that day, of a heart attack. she was forty six. bijoy was grown and gone.\nfor several years after that she was in bed with heart disease and high blood pressure, and the doctors expected her to die. sharon salzberg, who became one of her students, tells it that a doctor said she would die of a broken heart unless she changed something in her mind. dipa ma herself said she dreamed of the buddha chanting verses from the dhammapada, the short collection of his sayings, about clinging and sorrow. she asked herself what she could take with her when she died, and decided on a meditation center. she gave her money and belongings to a neighbor who would look after her daughter dipa and went to the kamayut meditation center in rangoon, meaning to stay for the rest of her life. she got one day. her concentration went deep fast, and on the way to the hall a dog clamped onto her leg. she said she felt nothing while it hung on. monks pulled it off, the hospital gave her rabies shots, and she went home to recover. dipa, frightened, would not let her go back. she practiced at home for years. then anagarika munindra, a friend of the family from chittagong who was studying in rangoon, talked her into coming to thathana yeiktha, the center run by mahasi sayadaw, the burmese monk who had built a way of teaching meditation to ordinary people in big numbers, in the theravada tradition, the school of buddhism burma follows. his method was vipassana, insight meditation, sitting and walking and noting whatever the mind and body are doing, starting with the rise and fall of the belly with each breath. she was fifty three. she could barely get up a flight of stairs, and salzberg tells it that she crawled up the stairs to the hall. within days she was hardly sleeping or eating, and by her own account she reached the first of the four stages of enlightenment her tradition describes in about six days. the blood pressure settled, the palpitations eased, the weak legs came back, and the grief, she said, stopped burning.\nover the next year she went back and forth between home and the center. her sister hema, who had eight children, practiced beside her for almost a year, and so did dipa, under the center's rules of silence, no eye contact, four hours of sleep and nothing to eat after noon. munindra took her further. she learned the jhanas, the deep states of concentration, and told an interviewer in 1977 that under his direction she had once resolved to sit in the eighth and deepest one for three days, eight hours, three minutes and twenty seconds, and did, and that jhana practice doesn't end suffering. he also had her train in the siddhis, the psychic powers that an old manual of the tradition lays out in detail. she said she had tried them and let them go, because they tend to inflate the ego. when munindra went home to india in 1966 she began teaching in rangoon. then burma's government, which had already pushed out some three hundred thousand people of indian origin since 1963, ordered the remaining foreigners out in 1967. monks offered to get her a permit to stay. she chose calcutta, the big city in indian bengal, for dipa's schooling. they lived in one room above a metal grinding shop, with no running water, a charcoal stove on the floor, a toilet shared with the building and a straw mat to sleep on. women found their way up there in the daytime, since the men were at work. her first formal student was a neighbor, malati barua, a widow raising six children alone, who practiced mindfulness while nursing her baby and reached the first stage. sudipti, who ran a business and looked after a sick son and an invalid mother, got five minutes a day to start. dipak, who worked in a bank and said he had no time, was told that if he was busy, then busyness was the meditation. the rules under it were hard. students kept the five precepts, the basic buddhist moral code, and slept about four hours a night.\nthe americans came through munindra. he had gone back to bodh gaya, the town in north india where the buddha is said to have woken up, and was teaching young westerners there, among them joseph goldstein and sharon salzberg. salzberg met dipa ma there, and in 1974 went to see her in calcutta. jack engler, a psychologist doing doctoral research for the university of chicago, found her in 1975 and spent the spring and summer of 1977 interviewing her in the one room, with her words translated from bengali. jack kornfield came for a day in the late 1970s. in 1975 goldstein, salzberg, kornfield and a fourth founder started the insight meditation society in a former mansion in barre, massachusetts, and it opened in february 1976. mahasi sayadaw himself taught there in 1979. dipa ma came in 1980 and again in 1984, when she helped lead the three month autumn retreat with goldstein and salzberg, and the tapes survive. she died in calcutta on september 1, 1989, at seventy eight. a neighbor who was there said she pressed her palms together, bowed to the statue of the buddha, and was gone. several hundred people in calcutta called her their teacher. in 2005 amy schmidt, a teacher at the massachusetts center, put out a short book called dipa ma: the life and legacy of a buddhist master, most of it her students remembering.","struggle":"the losses came in a row. her mother in 1929, when she was eighteen, with typhoid right after. a daughter, born when she was about thirty five, dead at three months. a son dead at birth in 1951, whom she never saw. her husband in 1957, well in the morning and dead by night. after that she couldn't get out of bed for several years. her heart was failing, her blood pressure was high, and the doctors expected her to die. the question she put to herself then, she told jack engler, was what she could take with her when she died.\nthe first try failed. she gave everything away and went to the center meaning to die there, and a dog bite and a frightened child sent her home. for years she practiced alone in the house, with dipa refusing to let her go back. when she finally reached mahasi sayadaw's center she was fifty three and crawling up stairs. and the end of the story isn't a cure for everything. she told engler in 1977 that she still felt sadness about her husband, only that it no longer burned, and that where there is birth there is death. then burma threw her out, and she spent the last twenty two years of her life in one room in calcutta with no running water. my journey is not over, she said. there is still work to be done.","threads":["grief","family","dying","the body","work"],"years":"1911 to 1989"},{"after":"what she did was the works of mercy, literally, for forty seven years. that's an old church list that comes mostly from a speech jesus gives in matthew 25, where he says whatever you did for the least of these, you did for me. feed the hungry, shelter the homeless, visit the prisoner. she lived in the houses with the people who came, ate the same soup, and wore donated clothes.\nand she kept going to jail. in the fifties for refusing to take shelter during new york's civil defense drills, the practice runs where the whole city had to hide underground as if a nuclear bomb were coming. the last time was 1973, in california, with the farmworkers' union led by cesar chavez, the grape pickers striking for decent pay, at seventy five. there's a photograph of her sitting on a folding stool facing two armed police officers, a small old woman who isn't going anywhere.","basically":"a radical young journalist in 1910s new york, drinking with playwrights and getting arrested with the women fighting for the vote, who had an abortion, then a baby with a man she never married, and then became catholic and lost him over it. in 1933 she started a penny newspaper and a string of soup kitchens and shelters that are still running, and she lived in them, with whoever came through the door, for almost fifty years. picture a new york activist who never took a salary, never moved out of the shelter, and was still getting arrested at seventy five. she's for anyone who thinks faith should look like somebody getting fed tonight.","born":1897,"died":1980,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--sage)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><rect x='36' y='228' width='50' height='22' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 235 L 86 235 M 36 242 L 86 242' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='150' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 150 168 L 150 216'/><path d='M 150 216 L 136 250 M 150 216 L 164 250'/><path d='M 150 184 L 172 194 M 150 180 L 172 184'/><path d='M 145 159 Q 150 163 155 159'/><path d='M 166 212 L 262 212 M 174 212 L 174 250 M 254 212 L 254 250'/><path d='M 184 212 L 188 182 L 238 182 L 242 212 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 182 182 L 244 182'/><path d='M 172 180 L 200 176'/><path d='M 202 176 C 198 166 206 160 202 150 M 222 176 C 226 166 218 160 222 150' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='290' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 290 168 L 290 216'/><path d='M 290 216 L 276 250 M 290 216 L 304 250'/><path d='M 290 182 L 304 206 M 290 182 L 264 176'/><path d='M 256 174 C 256 182 272 182 272 174 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g opacity='.6'><circle cx='335' cy='154' r='11'/><path d='M 335 168 L 335 216'/><path d='M 335 216 L 321 250 M 335 216 L 349 250'/><path d='M 335 182 L 319 206 M 335 182 L 351 206'/><circle cx='372' cy='154' r='10'/><path d='M 372 168 L 372 216'/><path d='M 372 216 L 358 250 M 372 216 L 386 250'/><path d='M 372 182 L 356 206 M 372 182 L 388 206'/><\/g><g transform='translate(46,203.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"she had an abortion, a common-law husband, and a jail record before she was thirty, and then she started feeding people and never stopped.","key":"dorothyday","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dorothy_Day","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"the catholic worker","url":"https://www.catholicworker.org/","why":"the movement she started, still running, with her writings"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Long_Loneliness","why":"her autobiography"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"the abortion, the short marriage, the common-law years. she told it herself, in her own books, and called herself a sinner without making a show of it. later there were people at the catholic worker who found her bossy and hard to work for, and on a bad day she'd have agreed.","moments":["the first issue of the catholic worker, may 1, 1933, was 2,500 copies. it cost a penny. it still does.","in the fifties, when the sirens went for the air raid drills, she and a handful of others sat on benches in city hall park instead of going underground, and got arrested for it, year after year, until the city quietly stopped the drills.","[[merton|thomas merton]], the famous monk in kentucky, wrote to her for years and sent the paper his essays against war. she printed them."],"name":"dorothy day","place":"brooklyn, chicago, the lower east side of new york","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 31 48 C 36 34 84 34 89 48 C 84 42 36 42 31 48 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 43 L 44 38 M 50 40 L 54 36 M 60 39 L 64 35 M 70 40 L 74 36 M 80 43 L 84 38' opacity='.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"don't call me a saint. i don't want to be dismissed so easily.","where":"attributed; she said something like it more than once"},{"q":"we have all known the long loneliness, and the only solution is love, and love comes with community.","where":"the long loneliness, her autobiography, the last pages, paraphrased"},{"q":"i really only love god as much as i love the person i love the least.","where":"attributed to her"}],"start":"the long loneliness, her autobiography. then loaves and fishes, her book about the catholic worker itself, the houses and the people in them.","status":"approved","story":"a bohemian journalist in greenwich village, new york's artists' and radicals' neighborhood, in the late teens, writing for socialist papers and drinking with eugene o'neill, the playwright, before he was famous. in 1917 she was arrested with the suffragists, the women fighting for the right to vote, for picketing the white house, and she went on a hunger strike in the workhouse, a prison farm in virginia. she was twenty.\nafter an affair with a newspaperman went bad she had an abortion, around 1919. then a short marriage. then, on staten island, a common-law life with forster batterham, an anarchist, a man who thought there shouldn't be governments at all, who loved the beach and didn't believe in marriage or god. she was happier than she'd ever been. when their daughter tamar was born in 1926 the joy turned her toward god, and she had tamar baptized, the ceremony that makes a baby a member of the church.\nforster wouldn't marry, and she wouldn't stay without the church, so in 1927 she was baptized herself and they split. it broke her heart, and she said so for the rest of her life.\nin december 1932 peter maurin turned up at her apartment with a plan. he was a french drifter from a peasant family, twenty years older than her, who walked around new york lecturing anybody who'd listen about how to rebuild the world. on may 1, 1933, they sold the first issue of the catholic worker, a newspaper saying the church belonged on the side of the poor and the unions, in union square, the manhattan plaza where the radicals gave their speeches, for a penny. then came the soup lines, then the houses of hospitality, which were shelters where anyone who came got a bed and a meal and nobody made them earn it, then the farms. it's still going, a movement of houses all over the world run by volunteers who live in them. she stayed a pacifist, someone who won't support any war, through the second world war, even against hitler, and lost most of her readers for it. she died in 1980 at maryhouse, one of her own houses, in new york.","struggle":"in 1924 she wrote a thinly disguised novel about the years before, the eleventh virgin, and later regretted it. she didn't dress up that part of her life. she put it in the long loneliness, her autobiography, and let it stand.\nthe loneliness in the title was real. she gave up the man she loved for a church he couldn't stand, and raised tamar mostly on her own in the middle of a soup kitchen full of drunks, cranks, and saints. her diaries are full of being tired, sharp with people, and sick of everyone in the house.","threads":["work","loneliness","prison"],"years":"1897 to 1980"},{"after":"the morning after the gambling has a date. april 28, 1871, wiesbaden, broke again, a letter to anna asking for the fare home and promising not to play it away. something great has happened to me, he wrote, the vicious fantasy that plagued me for almost ten years has disappeared. now it's all over, that really was the very last time. this time it was true. he went back to germany in later years and never sat down at a table. anna, who was twenty four, took over the money when they got home that july, sold his books herself from their flat, and got him clear of debt. she said he stopped because of the second baby, and scholars argue about that. nobody argues that he stopped.\nthe morning after the jews there wasn't. he never took it back, and the line about equal rights and the letter about the funeral are as far as it went. what he did with the last ten years was the brothers karamazov, which puts the case against god in the mouth of one brother and the answer in the life of a monk and lets you score it. on his last day, february 9, 1881, after the third bleed, he had the parable of the prodigal son read to his children, the story jesus tells about a son who wastes everything, comes home, and is met with a feast. his last words were to anna, that he had been faithful to her. the stone over him carries the line from the new testament about a grain of wheat that has to fall into the ground and die before it bears anything.","basically":"a russian novelist from the 1800s, the one who wrote crime and punishment and the brothers karamazov. at twenty eight he was marched out to be shot for reading a banned letter aloud at a discussion group, reprieved with a minute to go, and sent to a prison camp in siberia for four years. he came back a believer, russian orthodox, which is the eastern branch of christianity, the one with the icons and the chanted services, that split from the pope's church in the middle ages and became russia's official religion. he was also a roulette addict who pawned his wife's clothes, and in the 1870s he wrote a column that said ugly things about jews. picture the most famous writer alive, a man who has survived a death sentence and ten years of addiction, who also keeps a weekly column his best readers wish he'd stop writing.","born":1821,"died":1881,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><g stroke-width='2' opacity='.5'><circle cx='60' cy='60' r='2.5'/><circle cx='140' cy='40' r='2.5'/><circle cx='210' cy='74' r='2.5'/><circle cx='330' cy='50' r='2.5'/><circle cx='96' cy='124' r='2.5'/><circle cx='180' cy='22' r='2.5'/><circle cx='372' cy='100' r='2.5'/><circle cx='262' cy='36' r='2.5'/><\/g><g opacity='.55'><circle cx='40' cy='176' r='9'/><path d='M 40 185 L 40 222 M 40 222 L 32 250 M 40 222 L 48 250 M 40 196 L 58 190'/><path d='M 52 198 L 80 182' stroke-width='2.6'/><circle cx='72' cy='180' r='9'/><path d='M 72 189 L 72 224 M 72 224 L 64 250 M 72 224 L 80 250 M 72 200 L 90 194'/><path d='M 84 202 L 112 186' stroke-width='2.6'/><\/g><path d='M 150 120 L 150 250 M 190 120 L 190 250 M 230 120 L 230 250'/><g opacity='.65'><circle cx='150' cy='152' r='10' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='146.5' cy='151' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='153.5' cy='151' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 150 162 L 150 212 M 150 212 L 142 250 M 150 212 L 158 250 M 150 175 L 140 190 M 150 175 L 160 190'/><path d='M 142 183 L 158 183 M 142 195 L 158 195' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='190' cy='152' r='10' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='186.5' cy='151' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='193.5' cy='151' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 190 162 L 190 212 M 190 212 L 182 250 M 190 212 L 198 250 M 190 175 L 180 190 M 190 175 L 200 190'/><path d='M 182 183 L 198 183 M 182 195 L 198 195' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='230' cy='152' r='10' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='226.5' cy='151' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='233.5' cy='151' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 230 162 L 230 212 M 230 212 L 222 250 M 230 212 L 238 250 M 230 175 L 220 190 M 230 175 L 240 190'/><path d='M 222 183 L 238 183 M 222 195 L 238 195' stroke-width='2'/><\/g><circle cx='300' cy='140' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 292 149 Q 300 160 308 149' stroke-width='2.2' fill='var(--clay)'/><circle cx='295' cy='138' r='1.7' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='305' cy='138' r='1.7' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 296 146 L 304 146' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 300 154 L 300 212 M 300 212 L 288 250 M 300 212 L 312 250'/><path d='M 300 168 L 286 190 L 300 198 M 300 168 L 314 190 L 300 198'/><circle cx='362' cy='170' r='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='358.5' cy='169' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='365.5' cy='169' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 362 180 L 362 220 M 362 220 L 350 250 M 362 220 L 376 248 M 362 192 L 380 164 M 362 196 L 346 210'/><rect x='372' y='142' width='16' height='22' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 340 200 L 330 200 M 342 210 L 334 210' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(218,93) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a minute from the firing squad at twenty eight, and he spent the rest of his life working out what the minute was worth.","key":"dostoevsky","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fyodor_Dostoevsky","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Brothers_Karamazov","why":"the novel, with the inquisitor and zosima"},{"label":"lapham's quarterly","url":"https://www.laphamsquarterly.org/time/under-gun","why":"the letter to his brother from the night of the mock execution"},{"label":"the new york review of books","url":"https://www.nybooks.com/articles/1980/12/04/his-jewish-problem/","why":"joseph frank on dostoevsky and the jews, from 1980"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"roulette, for about ten years. it started in the casino at wiesbaden in june 1862, and in the autumn of 1863 he lost nearly all he had there and at baden-baden. in august 1865 he was stuck in a wiesbaden hotel for two months after gambling away the money for the trip. the staff stopped serving him anything but tea, he wrote begging letters to turgenev and others, and in the end the priest of the russian church in town lent him the fare out, which he paid back months later. the worst of it was the honeymoon. anna kept part of the housekeeping money aside for his play. when he'd lost it he came back saying he was not worthy of her, that he was a swine and she an angel, and asked for more, and she mostly gave it, because she was afraid a refusal would bring on a fit. her clothes and her jewellery went to the pawnshop. by one account she was made to pawn her underwear. by her own account she kept the gambling in a separate box from the man, as if it were something outside him. that's a kind thing to do for a husband. it is also what addiction asks the people around it to do.\nthe other thing is the jews. he used the russian slur for them, yid in english, in print and in letters, and in march 1877 he gave six sections of his column to what he called the jewish question. joseph frank, his biographer, calls the first two parts of it a compendium of slurs. in frank's telling, dostoevsky in the 1860s had the jew down as the financier and manipulator, the occult master of the stock exchange, and by the 1870s as the revolutionary behind the upheaval. in a letter he called odessa the city of the yids and said the yid had everything to gain from the chaos. the brothers karamazov itself has a scene where a character repeats the blood libel, the medieval lie that jews murdered christian children. he denied all of it. there has never been such a hatred in my heart, he wrote, and when a jewish reader named arkady kovner wrote to him in 1877 to say otherwise, he answered that he was no enemy of the jews and that a people who had lasted so long showed exceptional vitality. in the same 1877 column he said he favoured full equal rights for jews because that is christ's law, and printed a letter from a jewish girl about a doctor's funeral that jews and christians had attended together, saying maybe that pointed at an answer. david goldstein, who wrote the book on it, allows him almost no credit for any of that, and frank calls goldstein's book an implacable indictment. frank's own verdict is that he was unquestionably antisemitic and that it should not be overlooked or excused. then he argues that this was the one prejudice dostoevsky felt guilty about, which in frank's view makes it less repugnant. you can decide how much that's worth.","moments":["as a boy at the family's country place he thought he heard that a wolf was loose and ran in a panic. a serf called marey, one of the peasants his family owned, stopped his work and calmed him down. years later he wrote it up as a story, the peasant marey.","on the road to the camp, at tobolsk, the wives of the decembrists came to the prisoners with food and warm clothes, and gave each man a new testament. inside every copy they had hidden a ten rouble note.","one reporter counted more than a hundred thousand people at the funeral. the soberer counts say forty or fifty thousand.","ludwig wittgenstein, the austrian philosopher, read the brothers karamazov so often he knew whole passages by heart, and carried a copy to the front in the first world war."],"name":"fyodor dostoevsky","place":"moscow, st petersburg, and omsk in siberia","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 16 130 C 18 104 36 96 60 96 C 84 96 102 104 104 130 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 52 86 L 52 97 M 68 86 L 68 97'/><path d='M 60 97 L 60 130' stroke-width='1.8' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 32 56 C 25 52 25 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 95 52 95 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 38 42 C 44 31 54 29 60 30 C 68 29 80 33 84 44 C 78 39 70 36 62 36 C 54 36 45 38 38 42 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 92 50 106 60 108 C 70 106 82 92 84 66 C 78 78 68 82 60 82 C 52 82 42 78 36 66 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 44 51 Q 50 46 56 50 M 64 50 Q 70 46 76 51'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='2.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='2.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 59 L 57 69 L 62 69' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 51 75 Q 60 71 69 75'/><path d='M 80 104 L 108 100 L 111 121 L 83 125 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 95 102 L 97 123' stroke-width='1.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"life is a gift, life is happiness, each minute might have been an age of happiness.","where":"his letter to his brother mikhail, written on december 22, 1849, the day of the mock execution, as printed by lapham's quarterly"},{"q":"a child of disbelief and doubt, i am that today and i know will be one to the grave.","where":"his letter to natalya fonvizina, the woman who gave him his new testament on the road to siberia, as quoted by the scholar gary saul morson. the same letter says that if christ were outside the truth he would rather stay with christ"},{"q":"hell is the suffering of being unable to love.","where":"paraphrased from the talks of the elder zosima in the brothers karamazov, book six"}],"start":"the letter to his brother from december 22, 1849, first. then crime and punishment, which moves like a thriller. then the brothers karamazov, and don't skip the monk. the house of the dead for siberia.","status":"approved","story":"fyodor dostoevsky was born in moscow on november 11, 1821, the second child of a doctor at a hospital for the poor. the family lived in a house on the hospital grounds, and as a boy he met the patients while he played in the gardens. his mother taught him to read at four, using the bible. summers were spent at the family's small country estate, worked by serfs, the peasants who came with russian land and were owned along with it. his mother died of tuberculosis in february 1837, when he was fifteen, and in january 1838 he was sent to the military engineering school in st petersburg, which he hated. in june 1839 his father died. the official cause was a stroke. a neighbour accused the family's serfs of murdering him, they were tried and acquitted, and fyodor's older brother mikhail kept the story alive. in 1845, at twenty three, he finished his first novel, poor folk. a friend carried it to a poet, the poet carried it to vissarion belinsky, the biggest critic in russia, and belinsky called it the country's first social novel. it came out in january 1846 and sold.\nhe started going to the fridays of mikhail petrashevsky, a circle of young men in st petersburg who met to talk about socialism and the western books the censors banned. in 1848 a harder man inside the circle, nikolai speshnev, formed a secret society within it, and dostoevsky joined that too. 1848 was the year of revolutions across europe, and the tsar, russia's emperor, who answered to nobody, was frightened. a police agent joined the fridays, and in april 1849 sixty men were arrested. dostoevsky was held in the peter and paul fortress, the old fortress prison in st petersburg. his crime was reading aloud, at one of those evenings, a letter the critic belinsky had written to the novelist nikolai gogol tearing into gogol for backing the tsar and the church. fifteen of them were sentenced to be shot. on december 22, 1849, they were driven to semyonov square, a parade ground in the city, and had the death sentence read out. they kissed a cross. swords were broken over their heads. three men were tied to posts. dostoevsky was in the next three, and by his own reckoning had about a minute left to live, when the retreat was sounded and they were told the emperor had granted them their lives. it was a mock execution, staged on purpose. the real sentence was four years of hard labour in siberia, the frozen east of the empire where russia sent its convicts, and then the army as a private. that same day he wrote to mikhail. life is a gift, he wrote, life is happiness, each minute might have been an age of happiness.\nthey went east by sleigh, fourteen days to tobolsk, a town in western siberia. there the wives of the decembrists, officers who had risen against the tsar in 1825 and been sent to siberia for it, came to the prisoners with food and warm clothes, and gave each man a new testament, the christian half of the bible, with a ten rouble note inside. eleven days on he reached the prison camp at omsk. he did his four years and came out on february 14, 1854. the camp is in his book the house of the dead, which he wrote as the notebooks of a made up convict. after that he was a private in a line battalion at semipalatinsk, where he met maria isaeva and married her in february 1857. she'd turned him down first. about five years after the gift of the book he wrote to natalya fonvizina, the woman who had handed it to him, when she was low, and that letter gets quoted everywhere. he was a child of disbelief and doubt, he told her, and would be to the grave. and still, if someone proved to him that christ was outside the truth, he would rather stay with christ than with the truth. in 1859 the army let him go on grounds of health, and he went back to st petersburg.\nthe 1860s went wrong. he and mikhail ran a magazine, the house of the dead came out in it, the government shut the magazine down, and the second one failed. in june 1862 he went abroad for the first time, walked into the casino at wiesbaden, a german spa town, and found roulette, the game where a ball rattles round a numbered wheel. in the autumn of 1863, travelling with polina suslova, a young woman he was having an affair with, he lost nearly all he had at the tables. in 1864 his wife and his brother both died, and he became the support of mikhail's family with the magazine's debts on top. in the summer of 1865 he was stuck for two months in a wiesbaden hotel with no money, and started crime and punishment while he waited for someone to send the fare home. it's about a poor ex student in st petersburg who murders a pawnbroker to prove that an extraordinary man can step over the rules, and then finds he can't live inside what he's done. sonya, a girl forced into prostitution to feed her family, reads him the story of lazarus, the man jesus raised from the dead, and in the end he gives himself up and she follows him to siberia. he owed money everywhere and had promised a publisher a second novel by november 1866. on october 4 a stenographer of twenty named anna snitkina came to take dictation. the gambler, a short novel about a man who can't leave the wheel, was finished on october 30, twenty six days later. they married on february 15, 1867.\ntwo months after the wedding they left for europe, meaning to stay three months. they stayed four years. he lost everything again at baden-baden, another german spa, and anna's clothes and jewellery went to the pawnshop. their daughter sofya was born in geneva on march 5, 1868, and died of pneumonia on may 24, three months old. he finished the idiot, his novel about a good man trying to live like christ and what the people around him make of that, in january 1869. a second daughter, lyubov, was born that september. on april 28, 1871, broke again at wiesbaden, he wrote to anna that something great had happened to him, and he never played again. they got back to st petersburg on july 8, 1871. anna took the money out of his hands. from 1873 he wrote a column, a writer's diary, part fiction and part opinion, and by 1876 it was outselling his novels. in may 1878 his son alyosha, not yet three, died of an epileptic seizure, and that year he went to optina, a monastery famous for its elders, monks people travelled to for counsel, and sat with the elder ambrose. the russian word for such a monk is starets. the brothers karamazov ran in instalments from january 1879 to november 1880. it's about a bad old father and his sons, and the old man ends up murdered. in the middle of it one son, ivan karamazov, tells the youngest, alyosha karamazov, a story he's made up called the grand inquisitor. christ comes back to earth in spain in the days of the inquisition, the church court that hunted heretics, and a cardinal has him arrested and tells him he got it wrong when he gave people freedom, because people can't carry it. the answer to that is the elder zosima, a monk built partly on the elder ambrose, who teaches that everyone is guilty before everyone for everything, and that hell is the suffering of being unable to love. on june 8, 1880, he gave a speech in moscow about the poet alexander pushkin that brought the hall down, and ivan turgenev, a novelist he'd held a grudge against for years, hugged him. he died in st petersburg on february 9, 1881, of a bleed in the lungs, less than four months after the last instalment. tens of thousands came to the funeral.","struggle":"the epilepsy. nobody is sure when it started. one story puts the first signs at seventeen, when he heard his father was dead, and the only account of that, from his daughter, isn't trusted. what's certain is that from his twenties on he had a seizure about once a month, four or five hundred in a life, and in march 1877 alone he had four. he told his friend nikolai strakhov that in the brief moments before a fit he felt a contentedness unthinkable under normal conditions, and that a man would give ten years of his life, maybe all of it, for a few seconds of it. then the fit, and then days of being hard to live with. when he screamed at her, anna said, it was the illness, not bad temper.\nthe doubt. the man who wrote the most famous argument about god in any novel called himself a child of disbelief and doubt and said it would be so to the grave. he put the strongest case against god he could build into the mouth of ivan karamazov, and people have argued ever since about whether the answer he gave is as strong as the question.\nand the grief. 1864 took his wife and his brother. 1868 took sofya, at three months, in geneva. anna said he sobbed like a woman in despair, and that the two of them were closer for it. 1878 took alyosha, at two, with a seizure like his father's. after that one he went to the monastery.","threads":["prison","addiction","grief","doubt","family"],"years":"1821 to 1881"},{"after":"after wounded knee he kept practicing medicine, and when the reservation job went bad he found other ways to work. he started thirty two native ymca chapters, the young men's christian associations, in the 1890s, and lectured all over the country. he kept writing, calm and clear, for people who'd never heard a native person explain anything.\nafter the separation he mostly lived apart from his family, and kept lecturing and writing. he said what he had to say in the books and didn't add a public reckoning about the marriage. that's his after.","basically":"a santee dakota boy raised the old way in canada until he was fifteen, when his father, whom everyone thought was dead, walked into camp in white man's clothes and took him off to school. he became one of the first native doctors in america, and his first job was on the pine ridge reservation weeks before the massacre at wounded knee. after that he spent his life explaining native religion to white america in plain, calm books. think of the kid who gets out, gets the degree, and comes home to the worst day in his people's history. take from him that you can carry two worlds and say out loud what each one got wrong.","born":1858,"died":1939,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 170 L 130 170 L 130 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 172 L 85 126 L 140 172'/><path d='M 85 126 L 85 104 M 77 112 L 93 112'/><path d='M 70 250 L 70 206 L 100 206 L 100 250' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.25'/><path d='M 85 212 L 76 240 L 94 240 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='250' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 250 164 L 250 210'/><path d='M 250 210 L 266 246 M 250 210 L 232 242'/><path d='M 250 178 L 264 200 M 250 178 L 230 186'/><ellipse cx='230' cy='186' rx='12' ry='8' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='262' y='202' width='22' height='16' rx='3' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='180' cy='60' r='3'/><circle cx='240' cy='40' r='3'/><circle cx='320' cy='80' r='3'/><circle cx='360' cy='40' r='3'/><circle cx='200' cy='110' r='3'/><circle cx='300' cy='130' r='3'/><\/g><path d='M 160 250 C 200 244 320 244 380 250' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(85,89.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he was the new doctor at pine ridge when wounded knee happened, and he went out in the snow and brought back the living.","key":"eastman","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Eastman","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the soul of the indian, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/340","why":"his short book on native religion, at project gutenberg"},{"label":"indian boyhood, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/337","why":"his memoir of growing up dakota, at project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["his first name, hakadah, the pitiful last, was a kind of grief for his mother. the second, ohiyesa, the winner, he earned as a small boy at a ball game.","in the soul of the indian he says the old dakota didn't argue about religion. they thought each person's way to the holy was their own."],"name":"charles eastman, ohiyesa","place":"minnesota, then canada, then dartmouth and boston, then the pine ridge reservation, then everywhere lecturing","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 46 98 C 44 112 54 118 60 118 C 66 118 76 112 74 98'/><circle cx='60' cy='121' r='3.5' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"all this was a severe ordeal for one who had so lately put all his faith in the christian love and lofty ideals of the white man.","where":"from the deep woods to civilization, his 1916 memoir, the chapter on the ghost dance war"},{"q":"the worship of the great mystery was silent, solitary, free from all self-seeking.","where":"the soul of the indian, 1911, the first chapter, paraphrased. the great mystery is wakan tanka, the lakota and dakota name for the holy"},{"q":"i am an indian, and though i've learned a lot from civilization, i've never lost my indian sense of right and justice.","where":"from the deep woods to civilization, the last pages, paraphrased"}],"start":"the soul of the indian, which is short and free online. then from the deep woods to civilization, the chapters on pine ridge, for wounded knee in his own words.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in february 1858 near redwood falls, minnesota, into the santee, the eastern dakota, one of the three great branches of the people called the sioux. his mother died soon after, so they called him hakadah, the pitiful last. his grandmother raised him. in 1862, with the government months late on the food and money it owed them, and the dakota starving, the dakota war of 1862 broke out in minnesota. it was crushed, and on december 26, 1862, thirty eight dakota men were hanged together at mankato, the biggest mass execution in american history. his family fled to canada. his father had been taken prisoner, and they believed he'd been hanged.\nhe grew up in canada as a hunter, living the old way. the story goes he got the name ohiyesa, the winner, when his band won a lacrosse match. at fifteen his father showed up alive. he'd become a christian, taken the name jacob eastman, and was farming in dakota territory, and he took the boy to school. ohiyesa became charles eastman, went through dartmouth college, graduated in 1887, and finished medical school at boston university in 1890, one of the first native americans to become a doctor.\nthat fall he went to the pine ridge reservation in south dakota as the government doctor. on december 29, 1890, soldiers killed hundreds of lakota at wounded knee creek. the wounded were brought to the episcopal mission chapel, where the christmas tree was still standing. eastman and the missionaries tore out the pews, covered the floor with hay and quilts, and laid them in rows. on the third day, when the blizzard cleared, he went out with a search party to the field and found bodies strewn as far as three miles away, and a few still alive under the snow, among them a baby girl about a year old, wrapped up and unhurt. he carried her back.\nat pine ridge he met elaine goodale, a white teacher who ran the government schools in the dakotas, and they married in 1891. they had six children, and she helped shape the books he wrote, among them indian boyhood in 1902 and the soul of the indian in 1911, his short plain explanation of native religion for people who'd never heard it explained. he helped start the society of american indians in 1911, the first national group run by native people, and helped get the boy scouts going. he died in detroit on january 8, 1939.\nhe and elaine separated in 1921, and neither of them said much in public about why.","struggle":"he lost everyone at four. his mother was already gone, his father taken and presumed hanged, and his people driven out of minnesota for good. he grew up a refugee in another country.\nthen wounded knee, weeks into his first job, and it shook the thing he'd built his life on. he'd become a christian and a doctor and believed what his teachers told him about civilization, and he spent that week pulling children out from under the snow where soldiers of that civilization had left them. he never stopped being a christian. he did stop believing that the white way was the higher one.\nmuch later his daughter irene died of influenza in 1918, and not long after, the marriage came apart.","threads":["war","grief","doubt","family"],"years":"1858 to 1939"},{"after":"the dominicans, his order, kept reading him quietly for seven hundred years. copies survived in convents. in the twentieth century d.t. suzuki, the japanese scholar who brought zen to america, reached for him, thomas merton, the kentucky monk on these pages, read him, and he became the bridge between christians and buddhists that the church had tried to shut down.\nin 1992 the dominican order formally asked the vatican to lift the condemnation. it's still technically on the books. nobody cares. that's a kind of after too.","basically":"a german friar around the year 1300, one of the dominicans, an order of preaching monks, who was among the top professors in europe and then started telling ordinary people in their own language that god was nearer to them than they were to themselves. the church put him on trial for it and he died before the verdict. picture a star professor who drops the jargon, starts preaching at the laundromat, and gets reported to headquarters. if every idea you've got about god has started getting in the way of god, he's the one who told you to drop those too.","born":1260,"died":1328,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 260 L 380 260'/><circle cx='200' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 200 164 L 200 212'/><path d='M 200 212 L 186 246 M 200 212 L 214 246'/><path d='M 200 178 L 180 154 M 200 178 L 220 154'/><path d='M 140 70 C 170 40 230 40 260 70 C 230 100 170 100 140 70 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='200' cy='70' r='13' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='200' cy='70' r='5' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 200 90 L 200 132' stroke-dasharray='2 7' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 20 260 C 100 258 150 262 380 260'/><path d='M 300 260 L 300 220 L 340 220 L 340 260 M 310 220 L 320 200 L 330 220' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(360,230.3) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a dominican friar who prayed to be rid of god, got put on trial for heresy, and died before the verdict.","key":"eckhart","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meister_Eckhart","why":"the plain overview of his life and trial"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["meister means master, the german version of the title he earned at the university of paris, where he held the top chair in theology twice. only thomas aquinas, the dominican whose big system the catholic church still teaches, had done that before him.","his sermon on martha and mary flips a story from luke's gospel that everyone in church knows. jesus visits two sisters. martha runs around doing all the cooking and complains, mary sits at his feet listening, and jesus says mary picked the better part. eckhart says martha was the one who'd arrived, because she could be in the kitchen and with god at once. mary was still a beginner.","he preached in german to nuns and ordinary people when theology was done in latin, the church's language. that's why the wild stuff survived. the women wrote it down."],"name":"meister eckhart","place":"thuringia, paris, cologne","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 33 42 40 38 46 40 L 44 52 Z M 88 50 C 87 42 80 38 74 40 L 76 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 57 C 48 60 52 60 55 57 M 65 57 C 68 60 72 60 75 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the eye through which i see god is the same eye through which god sees me.","where":"his german sermons, number 57 in maurice walshe's english edition"},{"q":"i pray god to rid me of god.","where":"his sermon on poverty of spirit, known by its latin first line, beati pauperes spiritu, blessed are the poor in spirit, which is how jesus opens his most famous sermon"},{"q":"if the only prayer you ever say in your life is thank you, it will be enough.","where":"attributed to him everywhere; the idea is his, the sentence probably isn't"}],"start":"the german sermons, in maurice walshe's or oliver davies's english. if you only read one, read the sermon on poverty of spirit, beati pauperes spiritu, where he says the truly poor person wants nothing, knows nothing, and has nothing, not even god. the talks of instruction are the practical side, advice he gave the young friars when he was in charge of them.","status":"approved","story":"he was one of the most senior dominicans in europe. the dominicans are an order of friars, which means monks who live out in the towns and preach instead of staying behind monastery walls. he held a professor's chair at the university of paris, the harvard of medieval europe, twice, which almost nobody got. then he started preaching in german to nuns and ordinary townspeople, and what came out of him in plain speech was wilder than anything he'd written in latin, the language of scholars and the church, which most people couldn't read.\nhe said there's a spark in the soul that's never been separate from god. he said the eye you see god with is the same eye god sees you with. he said he prayed to god to rid him of god, meaning every idea of god you've got is in the way of the real thing, like a map you keep staring at while the road goes by. and the line everyone quotes, which is his idea if not his sentence, says that if the only prayer you ever said was thank you, it would be enough.\nin 1326 the archbishop of cologne, the top churchman in that part of germany, put him on trial for heresy. heresy means teaching something the church has ruled false, and it could get you burned. sixteen years before, a french mystic named marguerite porete had been burned alive in paris for a book about the soul losing itself in god. eckhart appealed over the archbishop's head to the pope, who lived in avignon, in southern france, back then, not rome, and went there to defend himself. he died there before it was settled. in 1329 the pope condemned a list of his statements anyway. the dominicans quietly kept reading him for seven hundred years.\nwhen d.t. suzuki, the japanese scholar who did more than anyone to bring zen buddhism to america, wanted to explain zen to christians in the fifties, eckhart was the one he reached for. zen is the japanese school of buddhism built on sitting still and seeing straight through. [[merton]] read him. [[rohr]] reads him. [[huxley]] put him all over the perennial philosophy, his 1945 book arguing that all the religions share one core. he's what christianity sounds like when it stops explaining.","struggle":"he was in his sixties when the archbishop of cologne charged him with heresy, teaching what the church had ruled false, and he went to avignon, where the pope lived then, to argue his own case in front of the pope's men. he wrote that he could be wrong but he couldn't be a heretic, because being wrong is a matter of the mind and heresy is a matter of the will. he meant he'd never set out to go against the church, and if he'd made a mistake, show him and he'd take it back.\nhe died before they finished. the pope's condemnation came a year later, so the last months of a man who'd taught that god was nearer to you than you are to yourself were spent in rooms full of church lawyers picking through his sentences one at a time.","threads":["silence","doubt"],"years":"around 1260 to 1328"},{"after":"in nicopolis he lived in a small house with almost nothing in it, a pallet and a mat and a lamp, and taught for the rest of his life. he didn't write, he didn't go into politics, he didn't ask for anything. he sat in a room and argued with young men about how to be free.\na greek scholar named simplicius, writing a few hundred years later, says that in old age epictetus took in a baby whose parents were going to leave it out to die, which romans did with babies they didn't want, legally, all the time. he got a woman to help him raise it. that's all we know about it. a man who'd been owned took in someone nobody wanted.","basically":"a slave with a busted leg who became the most useful teacher rome ever had. everything on a therapist's whiteboard about controlling what you can and letting go of the rest, he said first, and he said it as a man who'd been property. think of him as the original 'not my circus' guy, except he'd earned it.","born":50,"died":135,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 246 L 380 246'/><circle cx='140' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 140 164 L 140 212'/><path d='M 140 212 L 126 246 M 140 212 L 154 246'/><path d='M 140 178 L 124 202 M 140 178 L 156 202'/><path d='M 135 155 Q 140 159 145 155'/><path d='M 162 176 L 170 246 M 154 174 L 170 176' stroke-width='3.5'/><path d='M 190 210 L 250 210 M 196 210 L 196 246 M 244 210 L 244 246'/><path d='M 206 210 C 206 200 234 200 234 210 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 234 204 L 242 202'/><path d='M 220 200 C 214 192 220 186 220 182 C 224 188 228 192 220 200 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><rect x='280' y='80' width='90' height='90' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 325 80 L 325 170' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(338,100) scale(.42)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 60'/><path d='M 0 60 L -16 96 M 0 60 L 18 92'/><path d='M 0 28 L -14 50 M 0 28 L 20 36'/><\/g><path d='M 350 108 L 358 118 L 350 118 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><g transform='translate(292,148.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he was born a slave, walked with a limp, never wrote a word, and an emperor carried his lectures around with him.","key":"epictetus","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Epictetus","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the handbook, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/45109","why":"the enchiridion at project gutenberg, twenty minutes to read"},{"label":"the discourses, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10661","why":"a selection from his class notes at project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"the handbook has some cold lines, the one that tells you when you kiss your child to remind yourself you're kissing someone who'll die, and people reasonably find that hard to take. he meant it as protection. it can read like armor.","moments":["someone stole his iron lamp. he said he'd lost it because the thief was better at staying awake than he was, and bought a clay one. after he died, says lucian, a satirist of the time, a collector paid three thousand drachmas, a small fortune, for the clay lamp, hoping some of the wisdom would come with it.","a father told him he'd run out of the house when his little daughter was sick because he couldn't stand watching. epictetus took him apart gently for a whole chapter, asking whether love was the thing that made him leave her. discourses, book 1, chapter 11.","the teacher he learned from, musonius rufus, used to tell students that if they were comfortable in his lectures he was doing it wrong. epictetus kept that."],"name":"epictetus","place":"hierapolis in phrygia, then rome, then nicopolis in northwest greece","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 40 50 C 44 42 54 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 66 44 76 42 80 50' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 104 130 L 108 40' stroke-width='4'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"some things are up to us and some are not.","where":"the enchiridion, his short handbook, section 1, the first line"},{"q":"it isn't things that upset people, it's what they think about things.","where":"the enchiridion, section 5, paraphrased"},{"q":"a philosopher's school is a hospital. you shouldn't walk out feeling good, you should walk out having been treated.","where":"the discourses, his student's notes of his classes, book 3, chapter 23, paraphrased"}],"start":"the enchiridion, which you can read in twenty minutes. then the discourses, book 1, chapter 1. robin hard's or robin waterfield's translations are plain.","status":"approved","story":"he was born a slave in hierapolis, in what's now turkey, around the year 50. his name just means acquired. his owner in rome was epaphroditus, himself a former slave who'd risen to be one of the secretaries of nero, the emperor remembered for murdering his own mother.\nhe was lame in one leg. the story goes that his master was twisting it to see what he'd do, and epictetus said calmly, you're going to break it, and when it broke he said, i told you you would. a later writer says it was rheumatism. either way he walked with a limp his whole life.\nwhile still a slave he was allowed to study with musonius rufus, the leading stoic teacher in rome. the stoics were a greek school of philosophy that said you can't control what happens to you, only what you make of it, and that that's enough to live free and decent. modern cognitive behavioral therapy borrowed straight from them, and its founders said so. at some point he was freed and started teaching himself. around 93 the emperor domitian, who was paranoid about critics, threw the philosophers out of rome, and epictetus went to nicopolis on the greek coast and opened a school. people came from all over to sit in it.\nhe wrote nothing. a young student named arrian, who later governed a province and wrote the history of alexander the great, took down what he said as close to word for word as he could. that's the discourses, four books of class notes surviving, and the enchiridion, which means handbook, a short summary you can read in twenty minutes. the first line of the handbook is the whole program. some things are up to us and some are not.","struggle":"he spent the first part of his life as property. someone else decided where he slept, what he did, and what happened to his body, and the leg is the proof. everything he taught about what's up to you and what isn't, he learned in a house where almost nothing was.\nthen freedom, and then exile. he'd built a life teaching in rome and in his forties an emperor emptied the city of philosophers and he had to start over on a foreign coast with nothing. he never went back.","threads":["exile","the body"],"years":"around 50 to around 135"},{"after":"after fannie died he kept working, married again, and kept praying for people. after wounded knee in 1973 he didn't cash in on being the famous elder. he went back to pine ridge and kept doing ceremonies and hosting the treaty talks that came out of the occupation.\nat his funeral in 1989, russell means, one of the american indian movement's leaders, gave the eulogy and said fools crow was the reason the occupation ended without a massacre. that's not a bad thing to have said over you.","basically":"an oglala lakota holy man and ceremonial chief, born around 1890 and living to about ninety nine, who turned up at the center of almost every big lakota moment of the twentieth century. he quit school in third grade, toured with buffalo bill's wild west as a young man, led the sun dance for decades, stood between the militants and the federal government at wounded knee in 1973, prayed to open the united states senate in lakota, and told congress to give back the black hills. think of the old grandfather at the table everybody listens to, the cops included. take from him that the quiet old man is sometimes the one holding the whole thing together.","born":1890,"died":1989,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 230 250 L 230 80 M 230 110 L 210 90 M 230 104 L 254 86'/><path d='M 210 90 L 206 104 M 254 86 L 258 100 M 230 84 L 240 96' stroke='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='140' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 140 164 L 140 212'/><path d='M 140 212 L 126 246 M 140 212 L 154 246'/><path d='M 140 180 L 162 190 M 140 176 L 162 180'/><path d='M 150 180 L 200 168' stroke-width='3'/><rect x='196' y='162' width='9' height='9' rx='2' fill='var(--peach)'/><g transform='translate(300,196) scale(.35)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -20 4 M 0 28 L 20 4'/><\/g><g transform='translate(340,196) scale(.35)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -20 4 M 0 28 L 20 4'/><\/g><path d='M 40 250 C 40 220 80 220 80 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 60 222 L 60 212'/><g transform='translate(254,72.5) scale(0.5)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"black elk's nephew kept the sun dance going at pine ridge, and in 1973 he helped talk the guns down at wounded knee.","key":"foolscrow","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frank_Fools_Crow","why":"the plain overview of his life"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["the senate prayer in 1975 was in lakota, start to finish. he said what he had to say in his own language, and let them catch up.","the books about him were made by thomas mails, a white writer and illustrator who interviewed him in the 1970s. fools crow, in 1979, and fools crow: wisdom and power, in 1990."],"name":"frank fools crow","place":"the pine ridge reservation in south dakota, near wounded knee and kyle","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 40 50 C 44 42 54 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 66 44 76 42 80 50' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 35 70 C 33 86 35 100 37 114 M 85 70 C 87 86 85 100 83 114' stroke-width='6'/><path d='M 18 120 L 72 106' stroke-width='3'/><rect x='70' y='100' width='9' height='9' rx='2' fill='var(--peach)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"survival of the world depends on our sharing what we have and working together.","where":"attributed, quoted in accounts of his life"},{"q":"we are little hollow bones for the higher powers to work through.","where":"fools crow: wisdom and power, the book thomas mails made with him, paraphrased"},{"q":"the black hills are not for sale.","where":"the message of his testimony to congress in september 1976, paraphrased"}],"start":"thomas mails's fools crow, from 1979, built from interviews with him, a white writer's book in his words. then fools crow: wisdom and power.","status":"approved","story":"he was born around 1890 on the pine ridge reservation, near wounded knee creek, into the oglala lakota. [[blackelk|black elk]], the holy man whose vision became a famous book, was his uncle. he quit school in the third grade to help support his family, and as a young man he traveled around the country with buffalo bill's wild west.\nhe became a holy man and healer, and in time the ceremonial chief of the lakota, the one who leads the ceremonies for the whole people rather than one family. for years he was the intercessor at the sun dance, the great summer ceremony, meaning the holy man who prays for the people and for the dancers. the government had banned the sun dance for decades, and native religion wasn't protected by law until the american indian religious freedom act of 1978. he was one of the men who kept it going and brought it back into the open.\non february 27, 1973, members of the american indian movement, a young native activist group, and local oglala took over the village of wounded knee, the site of the 1890 massacre. they were protesting the tribal chairman, dick wilson, and the government's broken treaties. federal agents surrounded them, and it went on seventy one days, with gunfire most nights. fools crow, the senior elder, backed the protest, went to the united nations in new york to ask for recognition of an independent oglala nation, and helped negotiate the end in may.\non september 5, 1975, he gave the opening prayer in the united states senate, in lakota. on september 10, 1976, he spoke to a congressional subcommittee and asked for the black hills to be returned to his people. the fort laramie treaty of 1868 had promised the hills to the lakota forever. in 1980 the supreme court agreed they'd been taken illegally and awarded money instead. the lakota have refused the money ever since. he died near kyle, on pine ridge, on november 27, 1989.","struggle":"he lived his whole life on a reservation that was, for most of it, one of the poorest places in the united states, and he prayed and doctored for people there who mostly couldn't pay him. the ceremonies he was trained in were outlawed when he was young, and he carried them anyway.\nhis first wife, fannie afraid, died in 1954. his second wife, kate, died in october 1988, and he died a year later.","threads":["nonviolence","the land","grief"],"years":"around 1890 to 1989"},{"after":"he didn't fight for the order. he went off to pray, and at the end, sick and nearly blind, in a hut by san damiano, he wrote the canticle of the sun. brother sun, sister moon, brother wind, sister water, brother fire, mother earth. a praise song for the light from a man who could barely see it.\nnear the end he added one more verse, praising god for sister bodily death. then he asked to be laid naked on the bare ground at the portiuncula, the little chapel below assisi where it had all started, and he died there on october 3, 1226.","basically":"a rich merchant's kid from a small italian town around 1200 who wanted to be a knight, came home from a war and a year in prison not quite right, and gave away everything he had down to his clothes. he started a band of barefoot beggar brothers who owned nothing, and the stories have him preaching to birds and making a deal with a wolf. picture a trust fund kid who walks out of the family business to live under bridges on purpose, and turns out to be the happiest person anyone's ever met. he's for anyone who suspects the stuff is what's in the way.","born":1181,"died":1226,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='200' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 200 168 L 200 216'/><path d='M 200 216 L 186 250 M 200 216 L 214 250'/><path d='M 200 182 L 180 158 M 200 182 L 220 158'/><path d='M 195 159 Q 200 163 205 159'/><path d='M 226 250 L 230 240 L 276 240 L 280 250 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 230 240 L 234 232 L 272 232 L 276 240 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='264' cy='226' r='6' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 260 220 L 264 222 L 268 220'/><circle cx='50' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 50 164 L 50 210'/><path d='M 50 210 L 66 246 M 50 210 L 32 242'/><path d='M 50 178 L 64 200 M 50 178 L 30 186'/><path d='M 76 170 L 88 166 M 78 182 L 90 182' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 300 70 Q 306 64 312 70 Q 318 64 324 70 M 330 96 Q 335 91 340 96 Q 345 91 350 96 M 280 104 Q 285 99 290 104 Q 295 99 300 104'/><circle cx='140' cy='56' r='16' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 164 56 L 174 56'/><path d='M 116 56 L 106 56'/><path d='M 140 80 L 140 90'/><path d='M 140 32 L 140 22'/><path d='M 157 73 L 164 80'/><path d='M 123 73 L 116 80'/><path d='M 157 39 L 164 32'/><path d='M 123 39 L 116 32'/><g transform='translate(234,211.8) scale(0.75)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a rich kid took off every stitch in front of the bishop, handed the clothes back to his father, and walked out owning nothing.","key":"francis","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francis_of_Assisi","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canticle_of_the_Sun","why":"the praise song he wrote near the end"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fioretti_di_San_Francesco","why":"the little flowers, the old book of stories about him"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"what he did to himself, mostly. he fasted and went cold and slept on the ground until his body broke. near the end, the story goes, he apologized to it, to brother ass, which was his name for his body, for how he'd treated it.","moments":["at christmas 1223, at greccio, a hill town south of assisi, he set up a manger with a real ox and donkey so people could see how poor the birth of jesus was. every nativity scene on every lawn comes from that.","the story goes that he preached to a flock of birds near bevagna, told them to be grateful for their feathers, and they stayed to listen.","the wolf of gubbio, from the little flowers, a book of stories about him collected about a century after he died: a wolf was eating people, francis walked out and made a deal with it, the town fed it, and it died of old age. legend, and a pretty good plan."],"name":"francis of assisi","place":"assisi, italy, and the roads out of it","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 33 42 40 38 46 40 L 44 52 Z M 88 50 C 87 42 80 38 74 40 L 76 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 82 106 C 84 96 96 94 100 102 C 104 100 108 100 110 102 C 106 104 102 106 100 106 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='98' cy='101' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"praised be you, my lord, with all your creatures, especially sir brother sun.","where":"the canticle of the sun, the praise song he wrote near the end, 1224 or 1225"},{"q":"what had seemed bitter to me was turned into sweetness of soul and body.","where":"his testament, the last words he left his brothers, 1226, about the lepers"},{"q":"preach the gospel at all times, and if necessary use words.","where":"attributed to him everywhere; there's no record he said it"}],"start":"the little flowers of st francis, the old book of stories about him, for the legends. then the canticle of the sun, which is one page. then eager to love, richard rohr's book, for a modern franciscan reading him.","status":"approved","story":"francesco bernardone was the son of a rich cloth merchant in assisi, and he spent his youth spending it. parties, songs, good clothes. he wanted to be a knight. in 1202 assisi went to war with perugia, the bigger town next door, he was captured, and he spent about a year in a perugian prison. he came home sick and not quite the same.\na few years later, riding outside assisi, he met a leper. leprosy is a disease that eats away at skin and nerves, and lepers were kept outside the town and nobody touched them. instead of riding past he got down and kissed him. he said at the end of his life that what had seemed bitter was turned into sweetness. in the crumbling little church of san damiano outside town he heard the painted cross over the altar tell him to go and repair its house, which as he could see was falling down. he took it literally, sold some of his father's cloth, and tried to give the money to the priest.\nhis father hauled him before the bishop to get the money back. in front of the town francis gave back the money, took off all his clothes, handed them to his father, and said that from now on he'd only say our father who art in heaven, the first line of the lord's prayer. god was the only father he had now. the bishop covered him with his own cloak.\nbrothers gathered around him, owning nothing, begging and preaching. they became the franciscans, the friars, which is from the word for brothers. friars are like monks except they don't stay behind walls; they're out on the roads. in 1219, in the middle of the crusades, the christian wars to take the holy land, he walked across the battle lines in egypt to talk to the sultan, al-kamil, the muslim ruler the crusaders were fighting, and came back alive. in 1224 on la verna, a mountain in tuscany, he received the stigmata, which means wounds showed up in his hands and feet and side like the nail and spear wounds of jesus on the cross. he died in 1226. [[rohr|richard rohr]] is one of his friars, eight hundred years on.","struggle":"the order got away from him. thousands of men joined within ten years, and a lot of them wanted buildings, books, rules, and a little security. he wanted none of it. around 1220 he handed the running of the order to someone else and watched his rule, the short set of instructions the brothers lived by, which had been mostly jesus's own words about taking nothing for the road, get rewritten into something the lawyers could live with.\nand his body gave out. his eyes got so bad that by the end he was nearly blind. the doctors burned his temples with a hot iron to treat them. the story goes that he talked to the fire first, and asked brother fire to be gentle.","threads":["money","the body","joy"],"years":"1181 or 1182 to 1226"},{"after":"he went back to work. by 1946 he was running the neurology department. in 1947 he married elly schwindt and they had a daughter, gabriele. he spent fifty years telling people who'd never been near a camp that their suffering could mean something if they could find what it was for, and he meant their small suffering too.\nhe wouldn't blame the austrians as a people, which angered some other survivors. he said there were two races of men, the decent and the indecent, and that both were everywhere, including among the guards and among the prisoners. he stayed in vienna, the city that had sent his family to die, for the rest of his life.","basically":"a jewish psychiatrist from vienna who survived four nazi camps, came home to find his wife, his parents, and his brother all dead, and then wrote a short book in nine days saying a person can bear almost anything if they have a reason to. it's sold millions and it's on every therapist's shelf, and a lot of the modern talk about finding your why traces back to him. he earned every word of it. take from him that you don't always get to pick what happens to you, and you can still pick what it's for.","born":1905,"died":1997,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--olive)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='60' cy='50' r='2.5'/><circle cx='120' cy='80' r='2.5'/><circle cx='200' cy='40' r='2.5'/><circle cx='250' cy='90' r='2.5'/><circle cx='40' cy='120' r='2.5'/><\/g><path d='M 90 250 C 96 282 184 282 190 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='140' cy='176' r='14'/><path d='M 140 190 L 140 238'/><path d='M 140 238 L 126 272 M 140 238 L 154 272'/><path d='M 140 204 L 124 228 M 140 204 L 156 228'/><path d='M 170 250 L 190 190'/><path d='M 176 184 C 186 182 198 188 204 196'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 222 L 344 222 L 344 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 294 224 L 322 204 L 350 224'/><rect x='314' y='230' width='12' height='10' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 294 232 L 282 228 M 296 242 L 284 244' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(313,188.1) scale(0.7)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he lost his wife, his parents, and his brother to the camps, and then wrote the book about meaning in nine days.","key":"frankl","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Viktor_Frankl","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Man%27s_Search_for_Meaning","why":"the book"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"the honest criticism is about the book. he was in auschwitz itself only a few days before he was moved on, which the book doesn't make obvious, and in the last camp he worked as a doctor, which was hard but better than most had it. some survivors and scholars, lawrence langer, who writes about holocaust testimony, among them, think the book makes survival sound like a matter of attitude, which is unfair to the millions who had the right attitude and died anyway. that's fair to say. it doesn't unmake what happened to him.","moments":["by his own telling, in 1941 he had a visa for america and let it lapse so he wouldn't leave his parents. he says he found a piece of marble at home from a destroyed synagogue, carved with part of the commandment to honor your father and mother, one of the ten commandments, and took it as his answer.","digging in a ditch at dawn, thinking about tilly without knowing if she was alive, he saw a light come on in a farmhouse on the horizon. he wrote that it felt like an answer.","a fellow prisoner told him he'd dreamed the war would end on march 30. when the day came and it didn't, the man got sick, and on march 31 he died. frankl thought losing the date is what killed him."],"name":"viktor frankl","place":"vienna, the camps, vienna again","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 32 56 C 22 50 22 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 98 50 98 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='8'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='8'/><path d='M 58 56 L 62 56 M 42 55 L 34 52 M 78 55 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"everything can be taken from a man but one thing, the last of the human freedoms, to choose one's attitude in any given set of circumstances.","where":"man's search for meaning, part one"},{"q":"he who has a why to live can bear almost any how.","where":"a line from nietzsche, the german philosopher, which frankl quotes in man's search for meaning and made famous"},{"q":"between stimulus and response there is a space.","where":"attributed to him everywhere; it isn't in his books, and he probably never said it"}],"start":"man's search for meaning, part one, in one sitting. then the page about the farmhouse window again. then recollections, his short memoir, for what came after.","status":"approved","story":"a viennese psychiatrist and neurologist, born in 1905. as a teenager he wrote to sigmund freud, the father of psychoanalysis, and freud wrote back. in the thirties he set up free counseling for students around report card time, when student suicides spiked, and ran a ward for women who'd tried to kill themselves, and he kept working on the idea that what people need most is a reason.\nin 1942 he, his new wife tilly, and his parents were deported to theresienstadt, a ghetto and camp the nazis ran in czechoslovakia. his father died there. in 1944 he and tilly were sent to auschwitz, the biggest of the death camps, and from there he went on to kaufering and türkheim, work camps attached to dachau in bavaria. his mother was killed in the gas chamber at auschwitz. his brother walter died in the camps. tilly died at bergen-belsen, a camp in northern germany. he learned all of it after the americans freed him in april 1945.\nback in vienna, in 1946, he dictated man's search for meaning in nine days. he wanted it to come out anonymously, under his prisoner number. his name went on it in the end, and it's sold millions. the first half is the camps. the second half is logotherapy, the kind of therapy he built, from the greek word logos, meaning. freud had said people were driven by pleasure, and alfred adler, another viennese psychiatrist, said power. frankl said the deepest drive is the need for a reason, and the therapist's job is to help you find yours.\nhe ran the neurology department at the vienna poliklinik, a big city hospital, for twenty five years, remarried, had a daughter, kept climbing mountains, and got a pilot's license at sixty seven. he died in vienna in 1997, at ninety two.","struggle":"the manuscript of his first book was sewn into the lining of his coat when he got to auschwitz, and it was taken with everything else. in the last camp, sick with typhus, a fever carried by lice that killed huge numbers in the camps, he started rebuilding it in shorthand on scraps of paper, partly to keep himself awake at night so he wouldn't die in his sleep.\nthen he came home. tilly, his mother, his brother, all dead. he wrote to friends that year, roughly, that he was terribly tired and terribly lonely, and that only the work kept him going. the book came out of that year as much as out of the camps.","threads":["prison","grief","war"],"years":"1905 to 1997"},{"after":"what he did with his failures was publish them. the autobiography is called the story of my experiments with truth, and it's mostly a list of experiments that didn't work. the meat he ate in secret as a teenager, which in his family was close to unthinkable. the brothel a friend took him to, where he froze and got shown the door. the night his father died. he thought hiding it was worse than doing it.\nwhat he did with the killings was fast, twice, at seventy eight, until the killing stopped in calcutta and then in delhi. it stopped. two weeks after the second fast a hindu nationalist shot him for it.","basically":"a skinny indian lawyer who figured out how to beat the british empire without firing a shot, by getting millions of ordinary people to break unfair laws on purpose and take the beating without hitting back. every protest you've seen where people sit down in the road and let themselves get dragged off runs on his method. he was also hard on the people closest to him and did something in old age that's genuinely creepy, and he'd tell you to look at all of it. take from him that you can refuse to hate someone and still refuse to obey them.","born":1869,"died":1948,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 260 230 C 290 220 320 240 350 230 S 390 225 400 230'/><path d='M 270 250 C 300 240 330 260 360 250 S 395 245 400 250'/><path d='M 20 270 L 260 270'/><circle cx='200' cy='160' r='14'/><path d='M 200 174 L 200 220'/><path d='M 200 220 L 184 256 M 200 220 L 218 252'/><path d='M 200 188 L 186 210 M 200 188 L 220 196'/><path d='M 222 196 L 222 270'/><g opacity='.6'><circle cx='140' cy='178' r='10'/><path d='M 140 192 L 140 238'/><path d='M 140 238 L 124 274 M 140 238 L 158 270'/><path d='M 140 206 L 126 228 M 140 206 L 160 214'/><circle cx='90' cy='186' r='9'/><path d='M 90 200 L 90 246'/><path d='M 90 246 L 74 282 M 90 246 L 108 278'/><path d='M 90 214 L 76 236 M 90 214 L 110 222'/><circle cx='45' cy='192' r='8'/><path d='M 45 206 L 45 252'/><path d='M 45 252 L 29 288 M 45 252 L 63 284'/><path d='M 45 220 L 31 242 M 45 220 L 65 228'/><\/g><path d='M 300 270 L 320 250 L 340 270 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='340' cy='70' r='22' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 370 70 L 380 70'/><path d='M 310 70 L 300 70'/><path d='M 340 100 L 340 110'/><path d='M 340 40 L 340 30'/><path d='M 361 91 L 368 98'/><path d='M 319 91 L 312 98'/><path d='M 361 49 L 368 42'/><path d='M 319 49 L 312 42'/><g transform='translate(240,250.5) scale(1.3)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he read a hindu scripture and jesus's big sermon side by side every day, and took the british empire apart with both.","key":"gandhi","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mahatma_Gandhi","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Story_of_My_Experiments_with_Truth","why":"his autobiography"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"in his late seventies he had young women, including his nineteen year old grandniece manu, sleep naked beside him, calling it a test of his celibacy, his vow to give up sex. several people close to him quit over it. and in south africa as a young man he wrote things about black africans that are flatly racist, and fought for indians to be treated better than them rather than for everyone to be treated the same. he grew out of the second one. he never gave up the first.","moments":["the salt march in 1930. the british made it a crime for indians to make their own salt so they'd have to buy it taxed, and he walked two hundred and forty miles to the sea to pick up a handful of it. it took twenty four days and by the end there were tens of thousands walking with him.","he read the gita first in english, in london, because two theosophists, westerners who were into eastern religion, asked him about it and he was embarrassed not to know his own scripture.","he was the first person to be thrown out of a first class carriage and turn it into a fifty year career."],"name":"mohandas gandhi","place":"gujarat, london, south africa, india","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 32 56 C 22 50 22 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 98 50 98 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='8'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='8'/><path d='M 58 56 L 62 56 M 42 55 L 34 52 M 78 55 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 46 72 C 52 66 58 70 60 72 C 62 70 68 66 74 72 C 68 76 62 74 60 74 C 58 74 52 76 46 72 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"my life is my message.","where":"his answer when a reporter asked for one"},{"q":"the sermon on the mount went straight to my heart. the gita and the sermon said the same thing.","where":"his autobiography, the story of my experiments with truth, paraphrased"},{"q":"be the change you want to see in the world is something he never said. what he said was that if we could change ourselves, the world would change too.","where":"his collected works, the hundred volume set of everything he wrote, and a lot of posters that got it wrong"}],"start":"his autobiography, the story of my experiments with truth. then the gita, chapter 2, which is the one he lived on, especially the verses at the end about a person who stays steady no matter what happens. then the sermon on the mount, which is matthew chapters 5 through 7 in any bible.","status":"approved","story":"a shy young lawyer who got thrown off a train in south africa in 1893, at a station called pietermaritzburg, for being brown in a first class seat, and spent a freezing night in the waiting room deciding whether to go home or fight. what he did about it took fifty years.\nhe first read the [[krishna|bhagavad gita]], the hindu scripture where the god krishna talks a prince through his terror on the morning of a battle, in london, in english, because some theosophists asked him about it and he was embarrassed not to know it. theosophists were a club of westerners who were into eastern religion. it became his daily bread. around the same time he read the sermon on the mount, the three chapters in matthew where [[jesus]] says turn the other cheek and love your enemies, and said it went straight to his heart. later, in south africa, he read [[tolstoy]]'s the kingdom of god is within you, the russian novelist's book arguing that jesus meant all that literally, and it overwhelmed him.\nput the three together and you get satyagraha, which means truth force, the whole method. you break the unjust law in the open and take whatever they do to you without hitting back. then you keep coming, until the other side can't stand what it's doing to you, and neither can anyone watching.\nhe and tolstoy wrote to each other in the last year of tolstoy's life. he named his commune in south africa, a farm where protesters and their families lived together, tolstoy farm. decades later [[king|martin luther king]] read him and went to india to see what was left.\nin 1930 he walked two hundred and forty miles to the sea to make salt because the british said he couldn't. the british taxed salt and made it a crime for indians to make their own, in a hot country where everyone sweats it out. he picked up a lump of salty mud on the beach, and people all over india started making salt too. in 1947 india was free and immediately tore itself in half along religious lines, when the british split it into mostly hindu india and mostly muslim pakistan and neighbors started killing each other by the hundreds of thousands. he fasted nearly to death to stop the killing. a hindu nationalist, a man who thought gandhi had gone soft on muslims, shot him on january 30, 1948, on his way to evening prayers.","struggle":"when he was sixteen his father was dying and gandhi, already married, as boys often were in india then, left the sickbed to go to bed with his wife. his father died while he was gone. he never forgave himself and he tells it in the autobiography without flinching. he traced the vow of celibacy he took at thirty seven back to that night.\nhis oldest son harilal became an alcoholic, converted to islam to spite him, and turned up at gandhi's funeral so wrecked that almost nobody recognized him. the man who could bring an empire to the table couldn't reach his own kid.\nand then, as independence came, india started killing itself, hindus and muslims, hundreds of thousands dead. he'd spent his life on this. he walked into the worst of it, village to village in bengal, on foot, an old man, and said he'd rather die than watch.","threads":["nonviolence","the body","family"],"years":"1869 to 1948"},{"after":"he didn't give up on the country that jailed him. he had taken its oath, and when he came home from exile in december 1972 crowds met him, and within a year the government arrested him again. he kept saying the same thing into his nineties. in 1985, at ninety five, living in a hospital in afghanistan, he sent word through his son that the world could only survive through nonviolence.\nthere was no turnaround in the politics. the frontier stayed in pakistan, the red shirts were gone, and the province became a staging ground for the soviet war next door. near the end, as easwaran tells it, he said no true effort is in vain, that grain sits in the earth a while and then comes up a hundredfold. then he had himself buried on the afghan side of the border, which his people read as saying the pashtuns on both sides were one people, and two armies held their fire so the crowds could get him there.","basically":"a landowner's son from the pashtuns, the hill people on both sides of the border between what's now pakistan and afghanistan, where an insult could be paid back in blood generations later. in 1929 he started an army that swore off violence, men in red shirts, somewhere over a hundred thousand of them by the usual estimates, and he said he got the idea from the prophet muhammad. he was in [[gandhi]]'s inner circle, which is how he got called the frontier gandhi, and he spent something like a third of his long life locked up, first by the british and then by pakistan. picture somebody walking into a city where every family has a feud going, getting the hardest guys on every block to sign a pledge never to retaliate, and then sending them out to give two hours a day to their neighbors.","born":1890,"died":1988,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--teal)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 150 170 L 200 125 L 235 150 L 290 95 L 335 140 L 365 118 L 390 135' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='60' cy='50' r='18' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 20 260 L 380 260'/><path d='M 110 76 L 96 90 L 92 170 L 128 170 L 124 90 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><circle cx='110' cy='62' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 97 56 C 99 45 121 45 123 56 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 102 170 L 96 258 M 118 170 L 124 258'/><path d='M 100 92 L 66 116 M 120 92 L 154 116'/><path d='M 66 116 L 58 112 M 66 116 L 58 119 M 66 116 L 61 124 M 154 116 L 162 112 M 154 116 L 162 119 M 154 116 L 159 124'/><circle cx='225' cy='186' r='14'/><path d='M 225 200 L 225 228 M 225 228 L 215 258 M 225 228 L 235 258'/><path d='M 225 208 L 211 210'/><path d='M 231 213 L 195 204' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='270' cy='186' r='14'/><path d='M 270 200 L 270 228 M 270 228 L 260 258 M 270 228 L 280 258'/><path d='M 270 208 L 264 222'/><path d='M 268 213 L 252 250' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='315' cy='186' r='14'/><path d='M 315 200 L 315 228 M 315 228 L 305 258 M 315 228 L 325 258'/><path d='M 315 208 L 301 210'/><path d='M 321 213 L 285 204' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='360' cy='186' r='14'/><path d='M 360 200 L 360 228 M 360 228 L 350 258 M 360 228 L 370 258'/><path d='M 360 208 L 354 222'/><path d='M 358 213 L 342 250' stroke-width='4'/><g transform='translate(278,68) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he talked men raised on the blood feud into swearing they would never hit back, and they kept it while the bullets came.","key":"ghaffarkhan","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abdul_Ghaffar_Khan","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khudai_Khidmatgar","why":"the nonviolent army he founded, the oath and what happened to it"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Qissa_Khwani_massacre","why":"the 1930 shooting in peshawar and the disputed death toll"},{"label":"christian science monitor, 1985","url":"https://www.csmonitor.com/1985/0220/dbaman.html","why":"a news piece written while he was still alive, at ninety five"}],"lineage":"muslim","mess":"a lot of people in pakistan never forgave him. he sided with the congress party against the muslim league, the party that won pakistan, and against its leader, jinnah. he boycotted the vote that put the frontier in pakistan and backed pashtunistan, a separate state for the pashtuns, which to his critics sounded like tearing the new country apart before it had started. then in 1948 he told pakistan's assembly that all he wanted was for his province to be renamed pashtunistan, which isn't what the bannu resolution had asked for a year earlier. he spent his exile in afghanistan, a country that refused to recognize its border with pakistan at all, and in 1985 he was living in a hospital there under a soviet backed government. critics called him a traitor, some pakistani officials suspected him of communist sympathies, and his choice to be buried in afghanistan got read as refusing to lie in pakistani soil.\nand the movement didn't last. pakistan broke it after 1948, and when the ban was finally lifted in 1972 there was not much left to lift it from.","moments":["in october 1938 gandhi spoke at a public meeting on the frontier, and ghaffar khan stood next to him translating for the crowd.","in 1962 amnesty international, a group that campaigns for people jailed for what they believe, named him its prisoner of the year. he was in a pakistani prison at the time.","in 1984 he was nominated for the nobel peace prize, and in 1987 india gave him the bharat ratna, its highest civilian award.","when he died in january 1988, india's prime minister went to peshawar to pay tribute, and india declared five days of mourning for a man who was a citizen of pakistan."],"name":"abdul ghaffar khan","place":"utmanzai and peshawar, on the north-west frontier of british india, now pakistan, with years of exile in afghanistan","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 52 96 L 60 108 L 68 96 M 60 108 L 60 126'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 32 56 C 27 56 27 66 33 66 M 88 56 C 93 56 93 66 87 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 86 50 95 60 95 C 70 95 82 86 84 66 C 76 78 44 78 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 81 L 45 85 M 52 85 L 52 89 M 60 86 L 60 90 M 68 85 L 68 89 M 76 81 L 75 85' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 34 50 C 33 44 35 40 38 38 M 86 50 C 87 44 85 40 82 38' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 45 48 L 55 47 M 65 47 L 75 48' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 46 54 C 49 57 53 57 56 54 M 64 54 C 67 57 71 57 74 54'/><path d='M 60 54 C 58 62 52 68 55 72 C 58 75 63 75 66 72'/><path d='M 53 79 C 57 81 63 81 67 79'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"it is the weapon of the prophet, but you are not aware of it. that weapon is patience and righteousness.","where":"attributed, from what he told the khudai khidmatgar, his nonviolent army. the wording is the same in every account i found, but none gives the day or the place"},{"q":"there is nothing surprising in a muslim or a pathan like me subscribing to the creed of nonviolence. it was followed fourteen hundred years ago by the prophet all the time he was in mecca.","where":"the first sentence as quoted in eknath easwaran's biography of him, nonviolent soldier of islam, the second as it's quoted alongside it in later accounts; neither gives the original occasion"},{"q":"i am a simple soldier and khudai khidmatgar, and i only want to serve.","where":"his answer when the congress party offered him its presidency in 1931, as reported"}],"start":"eknath easwaran's nonviolent soldier of islam, a short warm biography first published in 1984 as a man to match his mountains. then my life and struggle, his own memoir, which came out in english in 1969.","status":"approved","story":"abdul ghaffar khan was born in 1890 in utmanzai, a village in the north-west frontier province, the corner of british india, the part of south asia britain ruled, that ran up against afghanistan. his father owned land there. the family were pashtuns, whom the british and the indians called pathans, and pashtuns live by pashtunwali, their old code of honor, where badal, revenge, has no time limit on paying back a wrong. he went to a school run by british christian missionaries, and when he finished he was offered a commission in an elite regiment of the british indian army. he turned it down, because even its indian officers were treated as second class citizens in their own country.\nin 1910, at twenty, he opened a school in utmanzai, and by 1915 the british had shut it down. in 1921 he started a string of new ones, open to everyone, girls included. he went from village to village for years, more than five hundred of them by one count. in 1929, the year after he came home from the hajj, the pilgrimage to mecca, he founded the khudai khidmatgar, which means the servants of god. members swore in god's name to serve without self interest, to take no revenge, to stay nonviolent, to forgive, and to give two hours a day to work for other people. their first white shirts got filthy fast, so a couple of men had theirs dyed at the local tannery, and the brick red stuck. everyone called them the red shirts. sources put them at over a hundred thousand, and one count says three hundred thousand, but those are estimates.\nhe told people there was nothing surprising about a muslim and a pathan taking up nonviolence. he called it the prophet muhammad's own weapon, patience and righteousness, and said the prophet had lived by it all through his years in mecca. on april 23, 1930, the british arrested him after a speech in utmanzai. in peshawar, the frontier's big city, a crowd gathered in the qissa khwani bazaar, the storytellers' market. armored cars drove into the square at speed and ran people down, and then troops fired on a crowd that had no weapons, on and off for hours. the british count was twenty dead. indian and pakistani sources say up to four hundred, and ghaffar khan himself put it at two hundred to two hundred and fifty. nobody really knows. two platoons of the garhwal rifles, a regiment from garhwal in northern india, refused to be sent into peshawar against the crowd, and their leaders got years in prison for it.\nhe and [[gandhi]] became close friends, the kind where neither one held back. in 1931 the congress party, the movement leading the fight for india's independence, offered him its presidency, and he said no, he was a simple soldier and only wanted to serve. gandhi toured the frontier with him twice in 1938. then in 1947 britain left and split the country in two, india and pakistan, a new state for muslims. he was against it. when the congress leaders accepted the plan without consulting his people, he is reported to have told them, you have thrown us to the wolves. that july the frontier voted to join pakistan in a referendum his movement boycotted.\npakistan jailed him again and again. by the end he had spent somewhere between twenty seven and thirty years in prison or under house arrest, depending on who's counting. he died in peshawar on january 20, 1988, at ninety seven, and his will said to bury him at his house in jalalabad, across the border in afghanistan. the soviet war in afghanistan was still going, and both sides called a ceasefire so the crowds could march him through the khyber pass. the reports say two bombs went off during the funeral and killed fifteen people.","struggle":"he lost two wives young. the first died in the flu epidemic of 1918. the second died in 1926 after a fall down the stairs. he was thirty six.\nthe worst of it came after the british left. in june 1947 his movement put out the bannu resolution, asking that pashtuns get the choice of a country of their own, and when the frontier referendum offered only india or pakistan, they stayed home. pakistan won with ninety nine percent on about half the turnout. he took the oath of loyalty to pakistan in february 1948 anyway, and by the end of that year he was under house arrest. on august 12, 1948, police fired on his supporters at babrra, a field in his home district, where they were protesting the arrests. the government said about fifteen died. his movement said about a hundred and fifty, and another account says over six hundred. in september the red shirts were banned.\nhe was held from 1948 to 1954, arrested again in 1956, and locked up from 1958 until he got sick in 1964, when he was let out for treatment and went into exile in afghanistan until 1972. he said later that the british had put him in prison many times, but the treatment he got in this islamic state of ours he would not even like to mention.","threads":["nonviolence","prison","exile"],"years":"1890 to 1988"},{"after":"for about ten years he lived on the move and plain, having given away his money except what his children needed. he left his brother ahmad ghazali, a sufi himself, teaching in his place. he says he spent nearly two years in damascus, climbing the minaret of the umayyad mosque, the great mosque of damascus, and shutting himself in all day to be alone. in jerusalem he went into the dome of the rock, the shrine over the stone that muslims and jews both hold sacred, every day and shut himself in there too. at the tomb of abraham in hebron he vowed never again to serve rulers or teach at state schools. he made the pilgrimage to mecca and medina in 1096, and his children's pleading, he says, finally pulled him home to tus, where he set up a little school and a sufi house. somewhere in those years he wrote the ihya.\nin 1106 he broke the vow. the nizamiyya in nishapur, the college where he'd been a student, got him back. he told his followers that people were confused about religion, the court was leaning on him, and a saying of the prophet muhammad promised a renewer at the start of every century, and a new century of the muslim calendar had just begun. it didn't last. he went home to tus to teach at his own small school, said no when baghdad asked him back in 1110, and died there in december 1111. the ihya became the most read book in islam after the quran and the collected sayings of the prophet muhammad.","basically":"a muslim scholar in the eleventh century who had the best teaching job in the religion by his early thirties, three hundred students, a college in baghdad paid for by the government. then, by his own account, he saw that he'd been teaching for the applause, and one day his tongue just stopped working in front of the class. he walked away, lived plain for about ten years, and wrote the ihya, his forty book guide to living islam from the inside, which muslims have kept reading ever since. picture a star harvard professor who freezes at the podium, quits, disappears, and comes back years later with the book everybody's grandmother owns.","born":1058,"died":1111,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--teal)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 200 L 380 200'/><path d='M 290 200 L 290 108 L 346 108 L 346 200 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 298 200 L 338 200 L 320 128 L 316 128 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 290 150 C 304 140 330 140 346 150' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='334' cy='124' r='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 50 195 L 140 195 L 140 214 L 50 214 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><circle cx='90' cy='98' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 76 94 C 74 76 106 76 104 94 C 96 88 84 88 76 94 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='95' cy='104' rx='3' ry='4'/><path d='M 90 112 L 90 160 M 90 160 L 78 195 M 90 160 L 102 195 M 90 126 L 74 150 M 90 126 L 112 118'/><path d='M 118 195 L 126 150 M 112 150 L 140 146' /><path d='M 113 148 L 126 140 L 139 146' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 104 90 L 118 76' stroke-dasharray='4 6'/><ellipse cx='146' cy='62' rx='30' ry='18' stroke-dasharray='6 7'/><circle cx='170' cy='222' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 170 236 L 170 262 M 170 262 L 150 264 M 170 246 L 154 254'/><circle cx='215' cy='222' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 215 236 L 215 262 M 215 262 L 195 264 M 215 246 L 199 254'/><circle cx='260' cy='222' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 260 236 L 260 262 M 260 262 L 240 264 M 260 246 L 244 254'/><path d='M 140 264 L 330 264' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(296,235) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the star professor of baghdad opened his mouth to lecture and nothing came out, so he left.","key":"ghazali","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Ghazali","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"deliverance from error, free online","url":"https://www.ghazali.org/works/watt3.htm","why":"watt's english translation of his own account of the breakdown"},{"label":"stanford encyclopedia of philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/al-ghazali/","why":"the careful scholarly version, including the philosophy fight"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Incoherence_of_the_Philosophers","why":"the book that started the argument with ibn rushd"}],"lineage":"muslim","mess":"he could be brutal with a pen. he ruled that anyone who publicly taught those three philosophers' positions was an unbeliever and an apostate who could be killed. in the infamies of the esotericists, his attack on the ismailis, a branch of shia islam, the second big family of muslims, he called them unbelievers whose blood may be spilled. later, in a short book called faysal al-tafriqa, he pulled the other way and said there are many ways to practice islam for someone who believes in god and in muhammad. both are him.\nthen there's the long argument over whether he turned islam against philosophy and science. the old story, told by a lot of western scholars, is that after him the muslim world stopped thinking. historians dispute that hard now. in the incoherence itself he says the math proofs of astronomy leave no room for doubt, and philosophy kept going in muslim schools for centuries, maybe stronger, because he pulled its logic into theology. and his book on marriage in the ihya is a medieval man's book. the wife obeys when he commands and guards his property while he's away. it passes on a saying credited to the prophet muhammad that marriage is enslavement, so a father should be careful whose hands he puts his daughter in, and it repeats the line that women are deficient in intelligence and religion. it also tells husbands to put up with harm from their wives and to joke with them.","moments":["there's a story, told by later writers, that robbers on the road took his notebooks when he was a student, and when he begged for them back they mocked him for a scholar whose knowledge lived in a bag. he spent the next three years memorizing it all.","in july 1091, around thirty three, he took over the nizamiyya in baghdad, the most celebrated college in the muslim world, and filled the room with three hundred students.","in damascus the man who'd run the top college in baghdad spent whole days locked alone at the top of a minaret, the tower a mosque's call to prayer comes from.","in 1180, almost seventy years after he died, ibn rushd answered him from muslim spain with the incoherence of the incoherence, and the two dead men's argument was still being translated into latin in 1526."],"name":"al-ghazali","place":"tus and nishapur in persia, then baghdad, then the road, then tus again","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 36 94 48 110 60 112 C 72 110 84 94 84 66 C 76 80 44 80 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 88 C 54 96 66 96 70 88 M 46 80 L 48 94 M 74 80 L 72 94' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 28 50 C 22 18 98 18 92 50 C 84 42 36 42 28 50 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 40 C 48 28 72 28 88 40 M 38 30 C 52 22 70 22 84 32 M 30 46 C 50 34 70 34 90 46' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 46 56 C 49 58 52 58 55 56 M 65 56 C 68 58 71 58 74 56'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 73 C 57 76 63 76 67 73'/><path d='M 66 104 L 94 100 L 96 124 L 68 128 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 71 106 L 74 126' opacity='.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"god caused my tongue to dry up so that i was prevented from lecturing.","where":"deliverance from error (al-munqidh min al-dalal), the section on the ways of the mystics, in w. montgomery watt's translation"},{"q":"the impulse moving me was the desire for an influential position and public recognition.","where":"deliverance from error, on his motives for teaching in baghdad, in w. montgomery watt's translation"},{"q":"what is most distinctive of mysticism is something which cannot be apprehended by study, but only by immediate experience.","where":"deliverance from error, on the sufis, in w. montgomery watt's translation. the arabic word he uses, dhawq, literally means tasting"}],"start":"deliverance from error, his short autobiography, in w. montgomery watt's translation. it's an afternoon, and the part about the sufis and his breakdown is the heart of it.","status":"approved","story":"abu hamid al-ghazali was born around 1058 in tus, a town in the far northeast of persia, in what's now iran. he and his little brother grew up orphans. he later wrote that from before he was twenty he'd thrown himself into every school of thought he could find, because he wanted to know things as they really are, not just take what he'd been handed.\nhe studied in nishapur, the big city of learning in that region, under al-juwayni, the leading muslim theologian of the day, meaning someone who argues about god with logic for a living. juwayni is supposed to have called him a sea you could drown in. after juwayni died in 1085, ghazali joined the circle around nizam al-mulk, the prime minister of the seljuks, a turkic dynasty whose empire ran from what's now turkey to afghanistan. in july 1091 nizam al-mulk put him in charge of the nizamiyya in baghdad, the most celebrated of the colleges he'd founded, in a city that had been the great center of muslim learning for about three hundred years. ghazali was in his early thirties, going by the usual birth date, and lecturing to three hundred students.\nhe was writing too. around 1095 he put out the incoherence of the philosophers, an attack on the muslim thinkers who followed the old greek philosophers, especially avicenna, the great doctor and philosopher who'd died in 1037. he went through twenty of their teachings and on three he said they'd left islam altogether: that the world never had a beginning, that god knows only kinds of things and not each single thing, and that bodies won't rise again after death. his most famous move is about cause and effect. when fire touches cotton, he said, it's god who burns the cotton, not the fire.\nin 1180, almost seventy years after ghazali died, ibn rushd, a philosopher in muslim spain, wrote a whole book back at him called the incoherence of the incoherence, quoting him passage by passage and answering each one. it was translated into latin twice, in 1328 and 1526, and christian europe read it. the argument between those two books is one of the big fights in the history of ideas.\nsomewhere in the middle of all that success, in 1095, he broke. what happened is below. then he left baghdad, and the rest of his life came out of that walk out the door.","struggle":"he'd already cracked once, earlier, though he doesn't say when. he says he came to doubt everything, first his own eyes and then his own reason, and for almost two months he was a sceptic in fact, though he never said so out loud. it ended, he wrote, not with an argument but with a light god cast into his breast.\nthe big one came in 1095. he'd been reading the sufis, the people inside islam who go after god directly, and saw that what they had couldn't be learned from books, only lived. looking at his own teaching, he decided it was mostly about rank and being known, and that the subjects did nothing for anyone's eternal life. for nearly six months starting in july 1095 he was pulled back and forth between the world and the next. then, he says, it stopped being a choice. one day he tried to lecture to please his students and his tongue wouldn't make a single word. grief set in, and he could hardly swallow or keep down a mouthful of food. the doctors gave up and told him the trouble came from his heart, and the only cure was for the anxiety in it to be calmed.\nin november 1095 he announced he was going on pilgrimage to mecca, which was a respectable reason to leave. privately he'd arranged to go somewhere else. people far from baghdad guessed he was scared of the government. people close to the court said it had to be some evil that had fallen on the scholars. some scholars since have wondered whether he was really running from danger. his patron nizam al-mulk had been stabbed to death in 1092 by a man from the order of assassins, and ghazali wrote his own story down when he was past fifty, partly to answer critics, so it isn't a neutral record.","threads":["doubt","fame","wandering","silence"],"years":"around 1058 to 1111"},{"after":"he used what time he had. he did interviews about dying, gave talks, and helped raise money for an endowed professorship at hopkins to keep the research going after him.\nhe didn't describe his death as a tragedy or pretend it wasn't coming. he said he felt more awake and more grateful than he'd ever been. that's the most useful data he left.","basically":"a johns hopkins pharmacologist who spent thirty years studying things like caffeine addiction, the least glamorous research there is, then ran the 2006 psilocybin study that got psychedelic science taken seriously again. he later gave it to people with cancer who were terrified of dying, and it helped. then he got stage four cancer himself and faced it the way his volunteers had. if you've wondered whether any of this is real or just hype, he's the careful one who checked.","born":1946,"died":2023,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 250 L 60 200 L 230 200 L 230 250' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 50 200 L 50 176 C 50 168 64 168 64 176 L 64 200' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='80' cy='186' r='14'/><path d='M 72 184 L 88 184' stroke-width='5'/><path d='M 66 176 C 70 168 90 168 94 176'/><path d='M 94 190 L 160 194 L 220 196 M 130 192 L 140 176 M 160 194 L 176 178'/><circle cx='300' cy='160' r='14'/><path d='M 300 174 L 300 216'/><path d='M 300 216 C 280 216 270 232 286 232 L 314 232 C 330 232 320 216 300 216'/><path d='M 300 190 L 272 200 M 300 190 L 328 200'/><rect x='316' y='188' width='16' height='20' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 250 L 244 226 L 264 226 L 258 250 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 254 226 L 250 206 M 254 226 L 262 204'/><circle cx='250' cy='204' r='5' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='262' cy='202' r='5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><g transform='translate(20,225.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a careful drug scientist restarted psychedelic research in 2006, then got stage four cancer and called it a call to celebration.","key":"griffiths","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roland_R._Griffiths","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"npr","url":"https://www.npr.org/2023/05/14/1175762261/cancer-patients-psychedelics-medical-spirituality","why":"the 2023 story on his own diagnosis and what he said about it"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"critics of the field say the early studies were small, that people guess whether they got the drug, and that the hype has run ahead of the evidence. he said many of those things himself. and the risks are real even in careful studies. some volunteers had hours of fear, anxiety, or paranoia, which is why the guides are there.","moments":["his 2006 study was designed partly to answer walter pahnke's good friday experiment from 1962, the harvard study where divinity students got psilocybin in a chapel.","the setup in his sessions was deliberately cozy, with a couch, eyeshades, headphones with music, two guides in the room, and flowers.","his team also ran a study using psilocybin to help people quit smoking. in the small first study, most of them quit."],"name":"roland griffiths","place":"baltimore, at johns hopkins","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 57 56 L 63 56 M 43 55 L 34 53 M 77 55 L 86 53' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"psilocybin can occasion mystical-type experiences having substantial and sustained personal meaning and spiritual significance.","where":"the title of his 2006 paper in psychopharmacology, a science journal"},{"q":"it has been a call to celebration.","where":"on his stage four diagnosis, speaking to npr, the american public radio network, may 2023"},{"q":"i'm truly grateful for the diagnosis.","where":"the same interview"}],"start":"the 2006 paper is readable if you skip the statistics. then michael pollan's how to change your mind, chapter 1, which is about him and his volunteers.","status":"approved","story":"he was a psychopharmacologist, a scientist who studies how drugs act on the mind, at johns hopkins, one of the top medical schools in america, in baltimore. for decades he did the dull, respected work, on caffeine withdrawal, sedatives, and what makes a drug addictive. he was also a longtime meditator, and he got curious about what meditators reported and whether you could study it.\nin 2006 he published the study that restarted the field. thirty six healthy adults, most of whom had never taken a psychedelic, got psilocybin in a living room style setup with a trained guide, eyeshades, and music, and a comparison drug on another day. two months later about two thirds rated the psilocybin day among the five most meaningful experiences of their lives, and about a third said it was the single most meaningful. the paper's title said psilocybin can occasion mystical-type experiences with lasting personal meaning. the careful journals ran it. nobody had done that in thirty five years.\nto measure what people went through he used the mystical experience questionnaire, a checklist going back to a 1962 harvard study, that asks things like whether you felt unity with everything and whether it felt more real than real. then his team gave psilocybin to people with life threatening cancer who were depressed and afraid. the 2016 study found the fear and depression dropped sharply and mostly stayed down six months later. in 2019 hopkins opened a whole center for psychedelic research with him running it.\nat the end of 2021 he was diagnosed with stage four colon cancer. he spent the last year and a half of his life talking about it in public, calm and grateful, and raising money for research. he died in october 2023, at seventy seven.","struggle":"he was fifty something and at the top of a respectable field when he decided to study the drugs that had wrecked careers in the sixties. colleagues thought he'd lost it. it took years to get approval, and every step had to be twice as careful as normal research because one bad headline could close it all down again.\nthen the cancer. he said the first reaction was shock, the kind anyone would have. what he said after that surprised people. he said he'd been handed the same test his volunteers had been handed, and that meditation, and his own experience with psychedelics, which he went public about in 2022, had helped him meet it.","threads":["psychedelics","dying","joy"],"years":"1946 to 2023"},{"after":"after lsd was banned he didn't go underground and didn't quit. he built something legal, taught thousands of people to guide it, and kept writing.\nwhen the research came back in the 2000s, the new scientists at johns hopkins and elsewhere were reading his old notes. he lived to see the thing he'd been doing in prague in 1960 become a front page story again. he married again in 2016, at eighty four.","basically":"a psychiatrist from communist czechoslovakia who ran supervised lsd therapy for years, first in prague and then in a state hospital in maryland, and probably watched more people on psychedelics than anyone alive. when the drug was outlawed he and his wife built a way to get somewhere similar by breathing hard to loud music for hours. picture the most experienced guide in the room, the one who's seen every kind of night and doesn't panic. if you want the long view from someone who stayed sober and took notes, he's it.","born":1931,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 250 L 250 250 L 250 240 L 60 240 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='80' cy='226' r='14'/><path d='M 94 228 L 170 230 L 236 234 M 130 229 L 150 214 M 170 230 L 190 214'/><circle cx='280' cy='170' r='14'/><path d='M 280 184 L 280 226'/><path d='M 280 226 C 260 226 250 242 266 242 L 294 242 C 310 242 300 226 280 226'/><path d='M 280 200 L 266 226 M 280 200 L 294 226'/><path d='M 275 175 Q 280 179 285 175'/><path d='M 320 250 L 320 196 L 360 196 L 360 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='340' cy='212' r='7'/><circle cx='340' cy='236' r='10'/><path d='M 310 176 C 302 166 302 156 310 146 M 296 184 C 284 168 284 150 296 134' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 90 200 C 100 180 120 190 130 170 C 140 150 160 160 170 140' opacity='.4'/><g transform='translate(334,174.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a czech psychiatrist sat through thousands of lsd sessions as the sober one in the room, and when the drug was banned he found a way in with breathing.","key":"grof","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stanislav_Grof","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holotropic_Breathwork","why":"the breathwork page, including the practice he built after lsd was banned and its risks"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"critics say his maps of the mind, especially his claim that people relive their own birth, go further than the evidence. and the breathwork isn't harmless. breathing that hard for that long can bring on panic, and it's not for anyone with heart trouble, epilepsy, serious mental illness, or a pregnancy.","moments":["his first session, in 1956, involved a stroboscope, a flashing light the researchers were testing on him. he said he was catapulted out of his body.","at esalen he gave workshops next to the famous names of the human potential movement, the sixties and seventies scene of encounter groups and bodywork.","in 1977 he helped start the international transpersonal association, for people studying experiences beyond the ordinary sense of self."],"name":"stanislav grof","place":"prague, then baltimore, then big sur, california","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"psychedelics, used responsibly, could be for psychiatry what the microscope is for biology or the telescope is for astronomy.","where":"lsd psychotherapy, his 1980 book, and many talks, paraphrased"},{"q":"a spiritual crisis can be a breakthrough, if it's supported instead of suppressed.","where":"spiritual emergency, the 1989 book he edited with christina grof, paraphrased"},{"q":"you don't need the drug to get there. breath and music will take you a long way.","where":"his talks on holotropic breathwork, paraphrased"}],"start":"the way of the psychonaut, his 2019 two volume summing up, or for something shorter, when the impossible happens, from 2006. read spiritual emergency if someone you love is going through something psychiatry can't name.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in prague in 1931 and trained as a doctor and psychiatrist. in 1956, as a young resident, he volunteered for an lsd session in a study at his clinic, supplied by [[hofmann|albert hofmann]]'s company, sandoz, and it changed the direction of his life.\nat the psychiatric research institute in prague in the sixties he led a program giving lsd to psychiatric patients, many sessions each, in a careful clinical setup, and wrote down everything. in 1967 he came to the united states, to johns hopkins, the big medical school in baltimore, and then became chief of psychiatric research at the maryland psychiatric research center. there he ran lsd therapy for alcoholics, heroin addicts, and people dying of cancer. between prague and maryland he said he'd been part of more than four thousand sessions.\nwhen the controlled substances act of 1970 put lsd in schedule i, the strictest category in us drug law, and research dried up in the early seventies, he moved to esalen, a retreat center on the cliffs of big sur in california, and lived there as scholar in residence from 1973 to 1987. there he and his wife christina worked out holotropic breathwork, which means breathing toward wholeness. you breathe fast and deep for a long time, lying down, with loud music and a partner sitting with you. people report experiences a lot like the ones he'd seen with lsd.\nhis big idea is that what psychiatry calls a breakdown is sometimes a breakthrough trying to happen, and that people in that state need a guide more than a sedative. he wrote more than thirty books, and he's still at it in his nineties.","struggle":"his career was cut in half twice. first by the soviet invasion of czechoslovakia in 1968, which meant he couldn't safely go home, and then by the american ban on psychedelics, which ended the maryland program in the seventies. he'd spent almost twenty years building a method, and two governments took it away.\nchristina, his wife and partner in all of it, went through what they came to call a spiritual emergency of her own, years of overwhelming states that ordinary psychiatry would have medicated. she also struggled with alcohol. they founded the spiritual emergence network in 1980 to help people in her position. she died in 2014.","threads":["psychedelics","the body","madness"],"years":"born 1931"},{"after":"there's no conversion in his story and no fall. he stayed in shiraz, wrote, outlasted the puritan, and got old. the after is what the book did once he was gone.\nsix hundred years on, on yalda, the longest night of the year, a winter party older than islam, iranian families sit up late and open his divan to read their fortunes. people quote him in ordinary conversation the way english speakers quote shakespeare without noticing. goethe built a whole book on him. the poet the clerics didn't want to bury is in every house.","basically":"a fourteenth century persian poet who wrote short love poems full of wine and longing that might be about god and might be about the bar, and six hundred years later iranians still keep his book next to the quran and flip it open to tell their fortunes. he's quoted in iran the way shakespeare is quoted in english, by people who don't know they're doing it. he also went after preachers who drank in private and scolded in public. and most of the soft, sweet hafiz quotes on your feed were written by an american in 1999.","born":1315,"died":1390,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--teal)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 342 30 C 322 38 318 70 342 82 C 314 86 300 50 320 34 C 326 30 334 28 342 30 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 20 242 L 380 242'/><path d='M 60 242 L 60 226 M 60 226 C 30 190 44 110 60 60 C 76 110 90 190 60 226 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='190' cy='170' r='14'/><path d='M 190 184 L 190 226'/><path d='M 190 226 C 170 226 160 242 176 242 L 204 242 C 220 242 210 226 190 226'/><path d='M 190 200 L 170 176 M 190 200 L 210 176'/><path d='M 185 175 Q 190 179 195 175'/><path d='M 204 170 L 222 170 C 222 182 204 182 204 170 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 132 240 L 152 232 L 152 242 Z M 152 232 L 172 240 L 152 242 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 286 242 C 280 222 294 206 312 212 C 330 204 348 222 340 242' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='296' cy='216' r='6' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='334' cy='222' r='6' fill='var(--peach)'/><g transform='translate(316,193.8) scale(0.75)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"half the hafiz on the internet isn't hafiz, and the real one is better and meaner.","key":"hafiz","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hafez","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/hafez","why":"a short life, with some real translations"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divan_of_Hafez","why":"his book, the one families open for fortunes"}],"lineage":"muslim","mess":"the wine might have been real. persians have argued about it for six centuries, and he'd have liked that they couldn't settle it.","moments":["the story goes that when timur, the central asian conqueror known in english as tamerlane, took shiraz, he summoned hafiz and asked how a beggar poet dared give away samarkand and bukhara, the cities timur had conquered half the world to adorn, for a mole on somebody's cheek. hafiz said, sire, that kind of generosity is why i'm so poor. it almost certainly never happened. it's too good to leave out.","the last couplet of nearly every ghazal has his own name in it. hafiz, stop complaining. hafiz, don't tell anyone. the poet talking to himself in front of everybody.","people go to his tomb in shiraz, put a hand on the stone, and read to each other. young couples, old men, whole families."],"name":"hafiz","place":"shiraz, persia, which he hardly ever left","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 28 54 C 26 24 94 24 92 54 C 84 40 36 40 28 54 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 44 C 50 34 70 34 84 44 M 32 50 C 46 38 74 38 88 50' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 88 104 L 110 104 C 110 117 88 117 88 104 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 99 115 L 99 124 M 92 125 L 106 125'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"o cupbearer, bring the cup around and pass it, for love looked easy at first and then came the trouble.","where":"the first ghazal of the divan, his collected poems, paraphrased; it opens in arabic before it turns to persian"},{"q":"if that beauty of shiraz would take my heart in hand, i'd give samarkand and bukhara for the mole on that cheek.","where":"the ghazal that opens agar an turk-e shirazi, paraphrased. samarkand and bukhara were two of the richest cities in central asia"},{"q":"even after all this time the sun never says to the earth, you owe me.","where":"not hafiz. daniel ladinsky, the gift, 1999. it's on a million posters with hafiz's name under it"}],"start":"faces of love, dick davis's translations, which have the real hafiz with notes. read ladinsky too, if you like him, knowing whose poems they are.","status":"approved","story":"shams ad-din muhammad was born around 1315 in shiraz, a city in the south of what's now iran, and lived nearly his whole life there. hafiz is a title. it means someone who has the whole quran, the muslim holy book, by heart. he did, and he says in one poem he knew it in fourteen different traditional ways of chanting it.\nhe was a court poet, which meant he was paid by whoever ruled the city, and in fourteenth century shiraz that meant a string of rulers taking it from each other. one prince would drink with him and the next would close the taverns. in 1353 a hard puritan named mubariz al-din took the city and shut the wine shops, and hafiz wrote about preachers who say one thing from the pulpit and do another in private.\nwhat he left is the divan, which just means collected poems. about five hundred ghazals, a persian kind of poem, a handful of couplets that each stand on their own and all ring on the same end sound. his are about wine and love and the beloved, the one the poet longs for, and you can't tell whether the wine is wine or god or both. iranians have argued about that for six hundred years. families keep the divan next to the quran and open it at random to tell their fortunes. his tomb in shiraz is a garden, and people go at dusk and read him to each other.\ngoethe, germany's great poet, read him in german in 1814, in a translation by an austrian scholar named joseph von hammer, and it set him writing again in his sixties. the west-eastern divan, 1819, a whole book of poems written back to hafiz, is his answer. ralph waldo emerson, the american essayist, translated him into english, from the german. then in 1999 an american named daniel ladinsky published the gift, poems by hafiz. they aren't. they're ladinsky's own poems, and they're what almost everybody quotes.","struggle":"he lived through a long war for his own city, and the poets rose and fell with whoever held it. the stories say he fell out with more than one ruler and spent some time away from shiraz. his poems complain about closed taverns, jealous rivals, patrons who didn't pay, and hypocrites in turbans.\nand the clerics never trusted him. the story goes that when he died some of them didn't want him buried with prayers, because of the wine. somebody opened the divan at random, the way people still do, and the line said don't hold back your steps from hafiz's funeral, because though he's drowning in sin he's on his way to paradise. they buried him.","threads":["love","joy"],"years":"around 1315 to 1390"},{"after":"the morning after the sickness is the whole second half of his life. he went home. the village that had seen him leave as a frightened boy had him back in 1716, and he took on a ruined temple and stayed. the restoring took about twenty years, the teaching about fifty. the boy who tested a god with incense smoke became the old man that people came from across japan to sit in front of, and he met them where they were, monks with koans, laypeople with pictures. he was still giving out the hermit's breathing exercise in print at seventy one, and past eighty he wrote that he never suffered the slightest indisposition. he also seems to have outgrown the reason he started. once he asked torei, his closest student, why he'd become a monk. to work for the salvation of my fellow beings, torei said. a much better reason than mine, hakuin told him.\nnowhere in what i read does he claim to have beaten the fear of hell. what he says instead, in the autobiography at eighty one, is that what he reached in his forties was bodhicitta, the wish to wake up for the sake of everyone else, which he defines in plain words as doing good by helping others and giving them the teaching. whether that's redemption or just a better job description, he spent his last decades drawing a wandering monk named hotei over and over for people who'd never read a sutra, and he died in the village he was born in.","basically":"a japanese zen monk from the 1700s, the one who asked what the sound of one hand is. zen is the branch of buddhism, the religion that grew from [[buddha|the buddha]]'s teaching, built on sitting still and on koans, the short baffling questions a teacher hands you to break your thinking open, and his is the koan everyone has heard as a joke. a sermon on hell scared him into the monastery as a boy, he trained so hard that in his twenties he came apart in what would now probably be called a nervous breakdown, and after a hermit in a cave taught him to breathe down into his belly he went home to a wrecked village temple and spent fifty years making it the place every serious zen student in japan wanted to be. think of the burnt out overachiever who crashes at twenty five and learns to breathe, then comes back to run the whole program while drawing cartoons for the people who'd never read the books.","born":1686,"died":1769,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 110 250 L 272 62 L 390 250 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 244 95 L 272 62 L 300 95 Q 286 104 272 92 Q 258 104 244 95 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 150 134 C 118 150 92 205 86 250 L 214 250 C 208 205 182 150 150 134 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 104 246 Q 150 222 196 246' stroke-width='2.2' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 138 156 L 120 206 L 134 214'/><path d='M 162 156 L 196 112'/><circle cx='200' cy='104' r='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 194 98 L 190 88 M 198 97 L 197 86 M 203 97 L 205 87 M 206 100 L 212 92 M 193 104 L 185 100' stroke-width='2.4'/><circle cx='150' cy='120' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 141 113 L 148 114 M 152 114 L 159 113' stroke-width='2.6'/><circle cx='145' cy='118' r='1.8' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='155' cy='118' r='1.8' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 145 125 Q 150 128 155 125' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 139 109 C 140 95 160 95 161 109 Q 150 104 139 109 Z' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 141 110 Q 139 118 141 124 M 159 110 Q 161 118 159 126' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='141' cy='125' r='2.5' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='159' cy='127' r='2.5' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 234 230 L 240 250 L 290 250 L 296 230 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 230 230 L 300 230'/><circle cx='265' cy='226' r='8' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='262' cy='225' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='268' cy='225' r='1.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 262 230 Q 265 232 268 230' stroke-width='1.6'/><g transform='translate(284,203) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a boy terrified of hell became the man who asked what one hand sounds like.","key":"hakuin","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hakuin_Ekaku","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"japan society","url":"https://japansociety.org/?p=41008","why":"the 2010 show of his paintings and calligraphy, the first in the west"},{"label":"the eastern buddhist","url":"https://otani.repo.nii.ac.jp/record/8994/files/EB15-2-07.pdf","why":"the first part of wild ivy in norman waddell's translation, free from the journal"},{"label":"the eastern buddhist","url":"https://otani.repo.nii.ac.jp/record/8985/files/EB16-1-08.pdf","why":"the second part, with the zen sickness in his own words"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"he was a hard man to be a rival of. he spent a lot of ink attacking other teachers, and his name for the kind of zen he disliked was do nothing zen. the followers of bankei, an earlier zen teacher with a big following, he said passed their lives in a state of seated sleep. he went after teachers who leaned on silent sitting or on chanting the buddha's name, and after the rival obaku school, which he lumped in with the mixed up chinese buddhism of the day, and his followers purged those things from the temples they ran. none of that is a crime. it did narrow what rinzai zen would be, and every modern rinzai line passes through him.\nthe other thing is his own paperwork. zen runs on transmission, a teacher formally declaring a student his heir, and hakuin, who certified more than eighty heirs on his own say so, never received it from shoju or anyone else. he just said shoju was his teacher. scholars have also noticed that the man counted as his heir in every modern rinzai family tree, gasan jito, probably wasn't one of his real heirs at all, and some have asked whether shoju existed outside hakuin's telling.","moments":["the incense test. a small boy, maybe eleven, praying to the god tenjin: if you can save me from hell without too much trouble, make the smoke go straight up. it did. the next time it didn't, and he switched gods.","iiyama, in his twenties. standing in front of a house on the begging rounds, deep inside a koan, he was knocked cold by the woman of the house and her broom. he came round with his sedge hat in tatters and the koans gone. shoju's whole comment was that he'd really been knocked out back there.","on a late self portrait painted as hotei, the wandering monk who was one of his favorite subjects, he wrote: the 82 year old daddy in this place.","two years before he died he inscribed another self portrait with a verse about how old and ugly he looked."],"name":"hakuin","place":"hara, a village at the foot of mount fuji, japan","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 14 130 C 16 100 38 92 60 92 C 82 92 104 100 106 130 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 46 93 L 60 114 L 74 93 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 94 M 68 84 L 68 94'/><path d='M 32 54 C 23 49 23 69 32 66 M 88 54 C 97 49 97 69 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 47 36 Q 60 32 73 36' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 41 47 Q 49 41 57 47 M 63 47 Q 71 41 79 47' stroke-width='3.4'/><circle cx='50' cy='55' r='2.8' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='55' r='2.8' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 57 L 57 67 L 63 67' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 52 75 Q 60 78 68 75'/><circle cx='86' cy='123' r='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 90 118 L 104 100' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 101 104 L 111 89 L 107 104 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.4'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"if it is in your power to save me from the burning fires of hell without difficulty, please make the smoke from this incense rise up in a straight line.","where":"wild ivy, the prayer he made to tenjin as a boy, in norman waddell's translation, page 85 of the eastern buddhist printing, part one"},{"q":"a buddhist monk, i concluded, has to be the most useless creature on the face of the earth.","where":"wild ivy, on reading at eighteen that yantou had been killed by bandits, norman waddell's translation, page 87 of the eastern buddhist printing, part one"},{"q":"the sound of a distant bell reached my ears. my body and mind dropped completely away.","where":"wild ivy, on the night at eigan-ji in 1707, norman waddell's translation, page 95 of the eastern buddhist printing, part one"}],"start":"wild ivy, his own story of his life, in norman waddell's translation, which also includes idle talk on a night boat, the short book about the hermit and the breathing. he tells the hell, the bell, the broom and the butter himself, and he's funnier than you'd expect. after that, look at the paintings before you read any more of the sermons.","status":"approved","story":"he was born on january 19, 1686, in hara, a village at the foot of mount fuji, and he died in the same village eighty three years later. his mother was a devout follower of nichiren buddhism, a japanese school that puts the lotus sutra, a long scripture which says every living being can become a buddha, above all other teachings. when he was a small child, about eleven by his translator's count, a nichiren priest preached at the temple in the village on the eight burning hells, the hells of fire in buddhist teaching, and the boy came away sure he was going there. he tells it himself, in the story of his life he wrote at eighty one. soon after, in the bath, his mother kept the water boiling, and the sound of it set off a panic so bad he howled. she didn't see what was so frightening about gurgling water. when he asked her how to get out of hell she combed his hair and told him to always revere the god of the kitano shrine, tenjin. so he recited the tenjin sutra and ran a test. he lit incense and asked the god, if it is in your power to save me from the fires of hell without difficulty, please make the smoke rise in a straight line. it rose straight as a string. the next time it scattered, and he moved his prayers to kannon, one of the great bodhisattvas, a bodhisattva being someone who has put off their own final peace to help everyone else get there. then he saw a puppet play about nisshin, a nichiren priest who, in the play, has a red hot plowshare clamped under his arms and a glowing cauldron set on his head and says a true practicer can walk into a blazing fire and come out untouched. i shall become a buddhist priest, the boy decided. i shall become just like him. at fourteen by the japanese count, fifteen by ours, he had his head shaved at shoin-ji, the small zen temple in hara.\nthe fear didn't make him a good monk. it made him a frantic one. he spent three or four years as a novice at another temple, read the lotus sutra his mother loved and found it nothing more than simple tales about cause and effect, and at eighteen or nineteen read that yantou, a famous chinese zen master of long before, had died at the hands of bandits. if awakening couldn't even save you from bandits, what was it for. a buddhist monk, he concluded, had to be the most useless creature on the face of the earth, and he quit and went off to write poetry. what pulled him back was a pile of books in a temple courtyard. he prayed for a sign, pulled one out, and it was a collection of zen stories from china, and he went back to the practice, harder than before. in 1707, at a temple called eigan-ji in the town of takada, he shut himself in a shrine room for seven days. in the middle of the night a distant bell sounded and, as he put it, his body and mind dropped completely away. then came the pride. he was sure nobody in three hundred years had broken through like that. the master there wouldn't confirm it, so he walked off to find a teacher who would, and found the opposite.\nthe teacher was shoju rojin, who lived from 1642 to 1721 and kept a hermitage in iiyama, in a remote part of the country. hakuin handed him a verse about his great awakening. shoju crushed the paper in his left hand, held out his right, and asked what, learning aside, he had actually seen. when hakuin admitted he'd become a monk out of terror of hell, shoju told him he was a self centered rascal. he grabbed hakuin by the nose. he threw him off the veranda, hakuin wrote, as if he were a little kitten. then one day on the begging rounds hakuin stopped in front of a house, lost in a koan, and the old woman of the house came out and knocked him flat with a broom. he came to on the ground with his sedge hat in tatters and the koans he'd been chewing on simply gone. shoju's comment was that he'd really been knocked out back there, and that he hoped hakuin would live to be as old as he was. hakuin stayed eight months and left when a letter came that a teacher of his was ill. he called shoju his true master for the rest of his life. he never received from him the formal handing on of authority that zen teachers are supposed to give their heirs, and why he left after eight months is still, one reviewer of his autobiography says, the biggest puzzle in hakuin scholarship.\nthen came the sickness, which gets its own section below, and the hermit who cured it. in 1716 he was asked to come home to hara, and within two years he was the priest of shoin-ji, the temple where he'd been shaved as a boy. that's when he took the name hakuin, which means concealed in white, for fuji hidden in cloud and snow. the place was a wreck and restoring it took about twenty years. at forty one, by his count, reading the parables chapter of the lotus sutra, the book he'd dismissed as a teenager, he heard a cricket churring at the foundation stones and the whole thing opened for him. the scripture he'd thought was simple tales turned out to be the point. students came, first a few, then hundreds. more than four hundred turned up for one series of lectures. he certified more than eighty of them as teachers in their own right, gave his robe to the closest, torei, and stayed at shoin-ji for about fifty years. every rinzai zen teacher alive today, rinzai being his school of zen, traces a line back to him. the famous question came late. for beginners, in place of the old chinese koan mu, a single syllable a master once answered a question with, he started asking what is the sound of one hand, and he'd hold up one hand in the private interview and wait. the clapping is a later addition. he said the one hand beat mu the way cloud beats mud, and in 1753, at sixty seven, he wrote a short piece about it. the paintings came late too. he only started painting seriously at nearly sixty and then made thousands of them, quick ink pictures of hotei, a wandering monk from the old stories, of blind men feeling their way across a bridge, of a monkey on a branch, dancing foxes, a napping folk god with his boots on, with a line of writing on each. they were sermons for people who couldn't read, and he gave them to the ordinary people who came to him rather than selling them. in his last fifteen years he wrote constantly, much of it in everyday japanese. at eighty one he wrote wild ivy, the story of his life. he died in hara on january 18, 1769, at eighty three.\nand then there's the story everyone tells. a girl in the village, the daughter of a family with a food shop, got pregnant and named hakuin as the father. her parents went to the temple. is that so, he said, and that was all. his good name was gone, and when the baby was born they brought it to him, and he took it and raised it, getting milk from the neighbors. a year later the girl broke and admitted the father was a young man who worked at the fish market. the parents came back in shame and asked for the baby, and he handed it over. is that so, he said. that's the version in 101 zen stories, a small english book put together in the early 1900s by nyogen senzaki and paul reps, an american poet, and reprinted in 1957 in zen flesh, zen bones. the book says its hakuin stories came from japanese books printed around 1900, which is more than a century after he died. the scholarly encyclopedia entry on him doesn't mention it at all, and i can't find anyone who traces it further back. so it comes to you the way it came to me, as a story.","struggle":"the hell never fully left. he says in wild ivy that fear is why he became a monk, and shoju threw it back at him as selfishness. by his own account he went at zen through his twenties as if the fire were at his heels, and after shoju, pushing harder still, he broke. an unnatural heat, he wrote, had taken hold unnoticed in his heart and sent his blood up. his lungs burned and his legs went cold. there was a rushing in his ears like a stream in a valley. his sides under his arms ran with sweat, his eyes watered, he couldn't concentrate, and he became, in his words, extremely fearful and despondent, weak and cowardly in mind and body. the older teachers he went to called it zen sickness and had nothing for it. one told him that if he tried to cure zen sickness he would only make it worse, and to go live in the mountains and be prepared to stay there. his translator says a doctor today might call it tuberculosis, pleurisy or a nervous collapse. he was ill for close to two years.\nthe cure, as he tells it in idle talk on a night boat, the short book he published about it in 1757, came from a hermit. in the first weeks of 1710 he followed a mountain stream into the hills east of kyoto, with directions from a woodcutter, to a cave with a reed blind over its mouth. inside was master hakuyu, said by the villagers to be more than two hundred years old, with black hair flecked with white that reached down over his knees and fingernails close to an inch long. hakuyu gave him two exercises to bring the heat down. one was to imagine a lump of soft butter, pure in color and fragrance and the size and shape of a duck egg, on the crown of his head, melting, running down through his body and carrying the trouble with it until he felt warm from the navel down, as if he were sitting in a hot medicinal bath. the other was to breathe the heat down and keep it in the tanden, a spot below the navel the text calls the elixir field, and stay there. before a month was out, he wrote, his troubles had almost totally vanished, and within three years they were gone.\nhow much of that happened is another question. he gives the date himself, and scholars who've matched his dates against the rest of his life put the sickness later, in his late twenties, and a two hundred year old hermit is a two hundred year old hermit. what nobody doubts is that something broke in him in his twenties and that the breathing is what he handed out afterwards. he published the exercise in 1757 for anyone who wanted it, and wrote that people came to shoin-ji in numbers he couldn't count to thank him. past eighty, he wrote that he never suffered the slightest indisposition, and that in winter he no longer needed socks or a fire to warm his feet.","threads":["doubt","madness","the body","work"],"years":"1686 to 1769"},{"after":"there wasn't a morning after for him. the vizier, the caliph's chief minister, a rich former tax farmer named hamid ibn al-abbas, pushed for his death. the caliph's mother pleaded for him and the caliph cancelled the order once, then signed it. in late march 922 they took him out in public in baghdad. most accounts say he was flogged, a thousand lashes in one, and that his hands and feet were cut off. they disagree on the end: hanged on the gibbet in one telling, beheaded in others. then the body was burned and the ashes thrown in the tigris, the river through baghdad. witnesses reported his last words as all that matters for someone lost in god is that the one god should make him one. the story also has him praying for the crowd to be forgiven.\nwhat came after was other people's. nearly three hundred years later [[attar]], the persian poet, put his life at the very end of his memorial of the saints, his book of sufi lives, as the climax of the whole book, and defended ana al-haqq. [[rumi]] kept coming back to him in the masnavi, his six volume poem, and set his i am the truth as pure light against pharaoh's lying claim to be god. [[hafiz]] wrote that his only crime was giving away the secret. and in 1922, a thousand years after the execution, louis massignon, a french catholic scholar, published four volumes on him, and kept working on him for the rest of his life.","basically":"a muslim mystic from persia, around the year 900, who took the private, careful practice of the sufis and preached it to ordinary people in the street. he is remembered for saying i am the truth, which to a lot of listeners sounded like a man calling himself god. the government of baghdad jailed him for nine years and then put him to death in public in 922. it's like a doctor who's right about something but goes on tv with it before the profession is ready, and the colleagues who half agree with him in private are the ones most angry.","born":858,"died":922,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--teal)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 256 L 380 256'/><path d='M 30 256 L 30 190 M 110 256 L 110 190'/><path d='M 24 190 L 116 190 L 104 152 L 36 152 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 30 214 L 110 214' /><circle cx='46' cy='207' r='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='60' cy='207' r='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='74' cy='207' r='6' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='88' cy='207' r='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='200' cy='118' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 186 112 C 188 98 212 98 214 112' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 200 132 L 200 196'/><path d='M 188 160 L 184 214 L 216 214 L 212 160' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 200 146 L 160 128 M 200 146 L 240 128'/><path d='M 194 214 L 190 254 M 206 214 L 210 254'/><circle cx='136' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 136 164 L 132 212 M 132 212 L 120 254 M 132 212 L 142 254 M 134 180 L 118 198 M 134 180 L 124 204'/><circle cx='268' cy='152' r='14'/><path d='M 268 166 L 272 214 M 272 214 L 262 254 M 272 214 L 284 254 M 270 182 L 286 200 M 270 182 L 282 206'/><circle cx='300' cy='162' r='12'/><path d='M 300 174 L 304 218 M 304 218 L 296 254 M 304 218 L 314 254 M 302 190 L 316 206'/><path d='M 352 256 L 352 196 L 372 196 M 352 204 L 360 196 M 368 196 L 368 206' opacity='.55'/><circle cx='330' cy='60' r='16' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none' opacity='.8'/><g transform='translate(62,125) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he told anyone who would listen that god was inside them, said it too loud, and baghdad killed him for it.","key":"hallaj","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mansur_al-Hallaj","why":"the plain overview of his life and death"},{"label":"encyclopaedia iranica","url":"https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/hallaj/","why":"the careful scholar's version, by jawid mojaddedi"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_Massignon","why":"the frenchman who spent his life on him"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tazkirat_al-Awliya","why":"attar's book of saints that ends with him"}],"lineage":"muslim","mess":"plenty of muslims then and now think he was wrong, and not only the ones who killed him. most sufis of his own day disapproved of him. the charge was that he took something meant for a teacher and a student in a quiet room and shouted it in the street, where people would hear a man calling himself god, which is the worst thing a muslim can say. his own father-in-law and some of his old sufi friends accused him of sorcery and of making a pact with the jinn, spirits made of smokeless fire in muslim belief, because he was said to perform miracles in public. a man who does wonders in the street and asks crowds to kill him is going to look to careful people like a showman or a madman.\nand he did get tangled in politics. he had friends in the palace, he preached in a way that stirred up crowds in a jumpy capital, and his words about the kaaba, the holy cube in mecca that muslims face to pray, were twisted into a plot to destroy it. none of that makes the killing fair. it does mean the people who called him reckless weren't all villains.","moments":["on his first pilgrimage to mecca, the story goes, he vowed to stay a full year in the courtyard of the great mosque, fasting and saying nothing.","on his second pilgrimage he showed up with four hundred followers, and that's when some sufis started saying sorcery.","around 902 he went to mecca one last time dressed in an indian loincloth and a patched robe, and prayed out loud in front of the pilgrims.","massignon was a young french archaeologist in iraq in 1908 when he was arrested as a spy and nearly killed. he came out of it a believing catholic, and a lifelong fixation on a man baghdad had killed a thousand years before."],"name":"al-hallaj","place":"fars in persia, basra, mecca, the roads east, then a prison in baghdad","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 20 130 C 22 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 98 104 100 130' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 48 97 L 60 118 L 72 97' /><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 36 94 48 108 60 110 C 72 108 84 94 84 66 C 76 80 44 80 36 66 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 76 C 56 79 64 79 68 76' stroke='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 50 C 28 26 92 26 90 50 C 80 42 40 42 30 50 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 40 C 50 32 70 32 84 40' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 46 57 L 54 57 M 66 57 L 74 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 88 128 C 118 110 118 84 102 70' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 88 128 L 102 70' stroke-width='1.4'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i am the truth.","where":"attributed. ana al-haqq, as reported by people who heard him; the reports disagree on where he said it, and the line isn't in any reliable manuscript of his own writings"},{"q":"what matters is to walk seven times around the kaaba of your own heart.","where":"attributed and paraphrased; the saying was used against him at his trial, as reported in the accounts of his case"},{"q":"these are your servants. they've gathered to kill me out of zeal for you. forgive them.","where":"reported as his prayer at the gibbet, paraphrased from the accounts gathered by later writers"}],"start":"attar's life of him, the last chapter of the memorial of the saints, for the legend. then the wikipedia and encyclopaedia iranica articles, for what historians can stand behind.","status":"approved","story":"he was born around 858 in or near al-bayda, a town in fars, the province in the south of what's now iran that gave persia its name. his grandfather was a zoroastrian priest, from the old religion persia had before islam. his father carded cotton, which means combing raw cotton clean so it can be spun, and that's what hallaj means: the cotton carder. the boy had the quran, which muslims hold to be god's own speech, memorized before he was twelve.\nat sixteen he became a student of sahl al-tustari, an early sufi teacher in southwest persia. sufis are the people inside islam who want to meet god directly, not only follow the rules. at twenty he went to basra, the old port city in southern iraq, married, and took the sufi robe from a teacher named amr al-makki, who later turned against him. then he went to baghdad to see junayd, the most famous sufi teacher in baghdad. junayd's way was the sober one: you can lose yourself in god, but you come back to your senses and you keep quiet about it in public. the stories people told afterward cast hallaj as the drunk one, and he went off to mecca, the holy city in arabia, against junayd's advice.\nthen he walked. he went to mecca on pilgrimage three times, and in between he traveled east through persia and on to india and central asia, past the edge of the muslim world. he preached in the open to whoever came, telling people to find god inside their own souls, and they gave him a nickname, the carder of innermost souls. back in baghdad he got louder. he talked in public about burning with love for god, and the reports have him saying things like o muslims, save me from god, and god has made my blood lawful to you, so kill me.\nthe line everybody knows is ana al-haqq, i am the truth. al-haqq, the truth or the real, is one of the names muslims use for god, so to many ears he was claiming to be god, the one thing islam forbids above all. his defenders said he meant the opposite: that he had been emptied out, that god had cleared away everything in him but god, so there was no hallaj left to be saying it. nobody's sure when or where he said it. the earliest report puts it in a big mosque in baghdad, later ones in a private talk with junayd, and it isn't in any reliable copy of his own writings.\nhis book, the tawasin, is short and strange, with drawn diagrams for things he said words couldn't hold. one chapter defends iblis, the devil in muslim telling, who refused god's order to bow to adam, the first man. in hallaj's telling iblis is the most stubborn believer in the one god there is, so stubborn he won't bow to anything else, even when god orders it. it's hard not to read the author into it.","struggle":"in 908 some people at court tried to throw out the new caliph, al-muqtadir, a boy of thirteen, and put a rival on the throne. a caliph was the ruler of the whole muslim state, political and religious at once. the coup collapsed fast, the crackdown after it went after the circles hallaj moved in, and he ran for ahvaz, a town in southwest persia. he was caught there around 913 and hauled back to baghdad.\nthe first trial, on charges of claiming to be god, fell apart because a friendly minister stepped in. he was put in the pillory anyway, as an agent of the qarmatians, rebels who had set up their own state in eastern arabia, and then jailed. he stayed in prison for about nine years, mostly inside the palace, where he had powerful friends and powerful enemies. that's the thing he couldn't beat. he was a man who wanted to be taken apart by god, stuck for nine years as a piece in other men's game.","threads":["prison","wandering","love","dying","forgiveness"],"years":"around 858 to 922"},{"after":"after the witch hunts people turned away from him, and he lost the standing he'd had. he spent his last years moving between seneca towns, preaching sobriety and the ceremonies to whoever would listen, less of a big man than he'd been. i know of no record that he took back what he'd done.\nwhat his followers did after him was keep the code and drop the witch hunting. the gaiwiio is still recited in longhouses, over several mornings, by people trained to carry it. the sobriety part held. a lot of haudenosaunee families today will tell you it's why they're still here.","basically":"a seneca man, one of the six nations of the haudenosaunee, the people the english called the iroquois, who spent years drinking while his whole world fell apart after the american revolution. in 1799, flat on his back and near death, he had visions, got sober, and started preaching a code that told his people to quit whiskey, keep the old ceremonies, and hold on to their land. it worked, and it's still practiced two hundred years later. it also set off witch hunts that got people killed. think of the founder of a recovery program that saved thousands, who also had a paranoid streak nobody should copy.","born":1735,"died":1815,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 30 250 L 30 170 L 130 170 L 130 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 20 172 L 80 132 L 140 172'/><path d='M 30 190 L 130 190 M 30 210 L 130 210 M 30 230 L 130 230' opacity='.35'/><path d='M 150 226 L 250 226 M 156 226 L 156 250 M 244 226 L 244 250' /><circle cx='166' cy='214' r='10'/><path d='M 176 216 L 236 218'/><circle cx='200' cy='110' r='14'/><path d='M 200 124 L 200 172'/><path d='M 200 172 L 186 206 M 200 172 L 214 206'/><path d='M 200 138 L 174 140 M 200 138 L 226 140'/><circle cx='250' cy='116' r='14'/><path d='M 250 130 L 250 178'/><path d='M 250 178 L 236 212 M 250 178 L 264 212'/><path d='M 250 144 L 234 168 M 250 144 L 266 168'/><circle cx='300' cy='110' r='14'/><path d='M 300 124 L 300 172'/><path d='M 300 172 L 286 206 M 300 172 L 314 206'/><path d='M 300 138 L 284 162 M 300 138 L 316 162'/><path d='M 150 250 C 150 238 170 236 176 244 L 186 240 L 188 246 L 176 250 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><g transform='translate(80,115.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a dying seneca drunk woke up with a message from the creator, and his people still stand up and recite it in the longhouse.","key":"handsomelake","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Handsome_Lake","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longhouse_Religion","why":"the longhouse religion that came from him, still practiced"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"his first vision told him witchcraft was behind his people's troubles, and he went looking for witches. he demanded confessions from people he accused. some who refused to confess were killed. this wasn't a rumor about him. it's in the record, and people at the time turned against him for it.\na man who'd just found his own way out of the dark decided the dark lived in his neighbors. that's the part nobody should copy.","moments":["quakers, the pacifist christians, had come to cornplanter's town in 1798, invited by cornplanter to teach farming and trades. one of them, henry simmons, wrote down handsome lake's first vision in his journal the summer it happened.","a full recitation of the code takes three days, mornings only, and it's done at the big midwinter ceremony and at a gathering of the six nations in the fall.","cornplanter's town, where the visions happened, is under water now. the kinzua dam flooded it in 1965."],"name":"handsome lake","place":"seneca towns in western new york and on the allegheny river in pennsylvania, then onondaga","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 40 50 C 44 42 54 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 66 44 76 42 80 50' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the creator is sad about four things: whiskey, witchcraft, love charms, and medicine to end a pregnancy.","where":"the code of handsome lake, the section on the four words, as written down by the seneca anthropologist arthur c. parker in 1913, paraphrased"},{"q":"parents shouldn't beat their children. talk to them.","where":"the code of handsome lake, paraphrased"},{"q":"keep the thanksgiving ceremonies. the creator made these for you.","where":"the code of handsome lake, paraphrased"}],"start":"the code of handsome lake, the seneca prophet, arthur c. parker's 1913 version of the text. then anthony wallace's the death and rebirth of the seneca, which tells the whole story plainly, the witch hunts included.","status":"approved","story":"he was born around 1735 at a seneca town on the genesee river in western new york. the seneca are the westernmost of the six nations of the haudenosaunee, a league of peoples in what's now new york, and they called themselves the keepers of the western door. he was a half brother of cornplanter, a seneca war chief. the seneca fought on the british side in the american revolution, and in 1779 american troops burned their towns and crops. after the war they lost most of their land and were squeezed onto small reservations.\nby the 1790s he was living at cornplanter's town on the allegheny river in pennsylvania. whiskey came up the river by the barrel, and the towns were drowning in it. he'd been drinking hard for years. early in 1799 he got sick and stayed in bed for months. in june 1799 he collapsed, and his family thought he'd died.\nhe woke up and said messengers sent by the creator had come to him. they told him the creator was grieved by what the people were doing, whiskey and witchcraft first of all, and that he had to tell them. more visions came that summer, including a journey through the sky to see where the good and the wicked go after they die. he stopped drinking and started preaching.\nwhat he preached came to be called the gaiwiio, the good message, or the code of handsome lake. no drinking. no witchcraft. don't beat your wife, don't abandon your family, don't sell your land. keep the old thanksgiving ceremonies for the corn and the maple and the new year. it pulled a broken people back together. he died at onondaga, the league's central town near what's now syracuse, on august 10, 1815. his followers kept the code, and the longhouse religion that grew from it is still practiced by haudenosaunee people today.","struggle":"years of drinking, while his people lost a war, their towns, and their land, and white traders sold them the thing that finished the job. by 1799 he was a sick old man in bed, and nothing about him suggested anybody would ever listen to him about anything.\nand the visions themselves were frightening. the sky journey showed him punishments for drinkers and wife beaters and witches, in detail. he came back from it believing the danger was everywhere around him, and that belief is where the worst of what he did came from.","threads":["addiction","madness","the land"],"years":"around 1735 to 1815"},{"after":"there isn't a morning after, because the story just has him walk into the cliff. what came after was the poems, copied and recopied, and by the 1000s two book catalogues list the collection. by the 1200s chan painters were drawing him and shide as two ragged little men laughing in the wilderness, hanshan with a scroll for his poems and shide with his kitchen broom, and then as the four sleepers, the two of them with fenggan and the tiger, all asleep in a heap in the open. the first mention of that picture is a poem by a chan abbot who died in 1228. the japanese took the pair up as kanzan and jittoku and have been painting them since the 1300s. the man the official bowed to ran away laughing, and the pictures kept the laugh.\nthen america. in the mid 1950s gary snyder, a young poet studying tang poetry at berkeley, put twenty four of the poems into english. jack kerouac, who shared a cabin with him north of san francisco in 1955 and 1956, wrote him into the dharma bums as japhy ryder, translating han shan at his table, and when the book came out in october 1958 it was dedicated to han shan, at snyder's suggestion. snyder's twenty four ran that autumn in the evergreen review, a literary magazine, with a preface calling him a mountain madman in an old chinese line of ragged hermits. watson's hundred followed in 1962, red pine's complete collection in 1983, and rouzer's, free to read online, after that. rouzer says his modern fame comes largely from snyder. a hermit with no name, and maybe no body, ended up a hero to every kid with a rucksack. the last line of snyder's last poem is try and make it to cold mountain.","basically":"a chinese hermit poet from the tang dynasty, somewhere between the 600s and the 800s, who named himself after the mountain he lived on and is said to have written his three hundred odd poems on cliffs and trees and the walls of people's houses. the only life story we have is a preface by a government official, and scholars think the preface was faked a couple of centuries later, so he may be one man, several, or a voice that monks kept writing in. his poems laugh at monks, at taoist priests chasing eternal life, at the rich and at himself, which is why the taoists and the zen people have both claimed him, and why in the 1950s gary snyder translated him, jack kerouac dedicated the dharma bums to him, and he became a hero to the beats. think of an anonymous account everyone screenshots, whose bio was written by a fan and may cover three different people.","born":null,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--sage)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 30 250 L 150 50 L 200 110 L 250 70 L 380 250 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 134 78 L 150 50 L 166 76 L 158 70 L 150 82 L 142 70 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 236 250 Q 236 198 264 198 Q 292 198 292 250 Z' fill='var(--olive)'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><ellipse cx='60' cy='243' rx='20' ry='8' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 108 170 L 132 170 L 138 212 L 131 206 L 124 214 L 116 206 L 109 214 L 102 212 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 120 166 L 120 214'/><path d='M 120 180 L 96 156 M 120 180 L 144 156'/><path d='M 120 214 L 106 250 M 120 214 L 134 250'/><rect x='139' y='132' width='10' height='26' rx='3' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='120' cy='152' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 108 140 L 132 140 L 128 128 L 112 128 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 106 146 L 92 138 M 134 146 L 148 138 M 110 128 L 104 118 M 130 128 L 136 118' stroke-width='2.4'/><circle cx='115' cy='150' r='1.8' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='125' cy='150' r='1.8' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 112 157 Q 120 167 128 157 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 111 163 L 108 172 M 129 163 L 132 172' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='206' cy='186' r='11' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='202' cy='184' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='210' cy='184' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 202 190 Q 206 194 210 190' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 206 197 L 206 226'/><path d='M 206 208 L 190 220 M 206 208 L 227 214'/><path d='M 206 226 L 196 250 M 206 226 L 216 250'/><path d='M 222 196 L 236 246' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 236 246 L 227 257 M 236 246 L 233 259 M 236 246 L 241 258 M 236 246 L 246 255' stroke-width='2'/><ellipse cx='340' cy='236' rx='30' ry='13' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 328 226 L 326 240 M 339 224 L 337 242 M 350 225 L 348 241 M 360 229 L 358 240' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 368 238 Q 386 236 380 222' stroke-width='2.4'/><circle cx='310' cy='232' r='10' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 304 224 L 302 216 L 309 221 M 316 224 L 318 216 L 311 221' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 305 232 Q 307 234 309 232 M 311 232 Q 313 234 315 232' stroke-width='1.6'/><g transform='translate(48,208) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he wrote his poems on rocks and trees and signed them with his address. nobody knows who he was.","key":"hanshan","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanshan_(poet)","why":"the plain overview, with the fight over his dates"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hanshan_and_Shide","why":"how he and shide have been painted since the 1200s"},{"label":"oapen library","url":"https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/20.500.12657/27418/1/9781501501913.pdf","why":"paul rouzer's complete translation, free, with the scholarly introduction"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Dharma_Bums","why":"the kerouac novel that's dedicated to him"}],"lineage":"tao","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["the story goes that the official found the two of them by the kitchen stove at guoqing temple and bowed, and they laughed at him and ran off. when he sent clothes up the mountain, hanshan shouted thieves, thieves, backed into his cave, and the rock closed.","fenggan, the third of the three, is said to have hulled rice at the temple by day and sung at night, and his room was full of tiger tracks because a tiger went around with him. chan painters later drew the four of them, two laughing men, a monk and the tiger, asleep in a pile.","watson's poem one opens with my father and mother left me a good living, and the wife is at her loom and the son is playing. his poem thirty two says even the wife turned on him. nobody knows which was written first, or by whom.","in the autumn of 1958 the evergreen review ran snyder's twenty four poems on pages 68 to 80, and on october 2 the dharma bums came out with han shan's name on the dedication page."],"name":"hanshan, cold mountain","place":"the tiantai mountains in zhejiang, on china's east coast","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 14 130 C 16 104 36 94 60 94 C 84 94 104 104 106 130 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 28 114 L 40 111 L 43 124 L 31 127 Z' fill='var(--olive)' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 95 M 68 84 L 68 95'/><path d='M 36 44 L 22 34 M 42 34 L 34 18 M 54 29 L 52 14 M 66 29 L 70 14 M 78 34 L 88 18 M 84 44 L 98 34' stroke-width='2.4'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 48 C 40 34 50 30 60 30 C 70 30 80 34 84 48 C 78 42 70 40 60 40 C 50 40 42 42 36 48 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 44 49 Q 50 45 56 48 M 64 48 Q 70 45 76 49' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 59 L 58 66 L 62 66'/><path d='M 48 72 Q 60 86 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 51 73 Q 60 76 69 73' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 40 78 L 36 90 M 47 85 L 45 96 M 73 85 L 75 96 M 80 78 L 84 90' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 78 102 L 106 102 L 106 116 L 78 116 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 78 102 C 72 102 72 116 78 116 M 106 102 C 112 102 112 116 106 116' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"cold mountain? there is no road that goes through.","where":"poem 82 in cold mountain: 100 poems, translated by burton watson, 1962"},{"q":"they laugh at me for being a hick.","where":"poem 26 in cold mountain: 100 poems, translated by burton watson, 1962"},{"q":"try and make it to cold mountain.","where":"the last line of the last of gary snyder's twenty four cold mountain poems, 1958"}],"start":"gary snyder's cold mountain poems, twenty four of them, an hour's reading. then burton watson's cold mountain: 100 poems. red pine's collected songs of cold mountain has all of them, and paul rouzer's complete translation is free to read online.","status":"approved","story":"hanshan means cold mountain, and it isn't a name so much as an address. it's a hill in the tiantai mountains in zhejiang, on china's east coast, and the man who lived there signed his poems with it. that's close to the only thing everyone agrees on. he lived in the tang dynasty, the three centuries from 618 to 907 that the chinese count as the great age of their poetry, and nobody knows which part. the traditional dates are the 630s and 640s. hu shih, a chinese scholar of the last century, put him at 700 to 780. in 1957 a scholar named wu chi-yu decided he was a monk who died in 654. in 1978 edwin pulleyblank, who studied the sounds of old chinese, went through the rhymes and found that about two thirds of the poems rhyme the way people did in the 600s or earlier and the rest sound like the late tang, two centuries on. yu jiaxi, the chinese scholar most of his countrymen follow, said the 700s. burton watson, who put a hundred of the poems into english in 1962, went with the late 700s and early 800s and called it provisional. paul rouzer, the scholar who translated the whole collection, says the most reasonable conclusion is that more than one poet wrote it, maybe many, most of them monks around the temples on tiantai, and that later ones took up the voice on purpose. so this page is about a voice at least as much as a man.\nthe one life story we have is a preface at the front of the old editions, signed by lüqiu yin, who says he was a tang official on his way to govern tai prefecture, the district around the mountains. on the road, he says, he got a terrible headache, and a monk called fenggan from guoqing temple, the big buddhist monastery at the foot of tiantai, sprinkled water on him and cured it, telling him the body is made of the four elements and sickness is an illusion. fenggan told him that two men at the temple, hanshan and shide, were bodhisattvas in disguise, beings who in buddhism have reached enlightenment and stay around to help, by the names of manjushri and samantabhadra. shide ran errands at the kitchen stoves. at guoqing the monks told the official that fenggan hulled rice by day and sang at night and that his room was full of tiger tracks, because a tiger went about with him. hanshan, they said, was a poor crank who lived up in the cliffs and came down to eat the leftovers shide saved for him in a bamboo tube. he wore a cap of birch bark, a torn fur coat and wooden sandals, and he laughed and clapped. the official found the two of them in the kitchen and bowed, and they laughed in his face and ran. he sent clothes up the mountain after them. hanshan shouted thieves, thieves, backed into his cave, and the rock closed behind him. shide's footprints stopped. so the official had the poems gathered from wherever hanshan had written them, on bamboo, on wood, on stones and cliffs and the walls of village houses, and came up with a little over three hundred.\nit's a great story and the people who study it mostly don't believe it. the preface has no date, which a chinese official of that time wouldn't have left off. the name lüqiu yin turns up once in the records of the period, and that's almost certainly another man. the prose is clumsy and wordy, not the work of a senior official. everything else ever written about hanshan comes later and leans on the preface. yu jiaxi showed that its place names belong to a later century and dated it to the late 800s, and most scholars have agreed with him since. watson said flatly that the whole picture may be nothing more than a literary fiction. the oldest surviving printed copy of the poems is from the 1130s or 1140s, five hundred years and more after the earliest dates anyone gives him. so when this page says the story goes, that's what it means. the story goes.\nso the life has to come out of the poems, and the poems don't agree with each other. watson, reading them, saw a gentleman farmer troubled by poverty and family discord, who wandered a long way, maybe held some small government post, and ended up alone on cold mountain. one poem has a wife at her loom and a son playing. another says even his wife turned against him. one says he came to cold mountain at thirty, after a schooling in the books of confucius, the classics every official had to know. another says he's lingered there thirty years. another has him thirty years old, which doesn't fit either. he says they laugh at him for being a hick. he says now he's an old man. rouzer counts thirty nine poems where the speaker names himself or the mountain, and twenty one of the twenty four that gary snyder chose to translate in the 1950s are from that group, which is a big part of why the english hanshan feels like one person. rouzer's own view is that reading the collection as one man's autobiography is largely untenable.\nwhich religion he belonged to is the other fight, and the mountains are half the reason. an old taoist writer called tiantai the perfect place for practicing the arts of immortality, the taoist project of living forever, and guoqing was the home temple of a whole school of buddhism. the poems use both vocabularies. some are warm to the taoist way, some mock taoist priests cooking up elixirs, some are plain buddhist sermons about how nothing lasts, and one says reading cold mountain's poems beats reading the sutras, the buddha's own talks. he's traditionally counted a buddhist, and more exactly a chan poet, chan being the chinese buddhism of sitting meditation that the japanese call zen. in japan, where his name is kanzan, the great zen master [[hakuin]] wrote a commentary on him in the 1740s, and he was honored there as the model of the carefree enlightened layman. taoist-leaning critics have claimed him for their side, and watson says the poems give them something to point to. arthur waley, the english translator who did twenty seven of the poems in 1954, said cold mountain is often the name of a state of mind rather than a place. that's the thing to hold onto. he sits between the tao, the old chinese sense that you stop forcing things and move the way water moves, and zen, which sits still until the mind does, and he laughs at both when they get pompous.","struggle":"there isn't a dated day in his life, so the struggle is whatever the poems carry, and they carry a lot. poverty. the fights at home, and the wife who turned on him. being laughed at as a hick. getting old. watson reads the whole collection not as the report of a man who made it but as a chronicle of spiritual search, which is a polite way of saying he kept writing because he kept not arriving. the poem snyder put first says the path to han-shan's place is laughable, and you can't tell from the poem whether the joke is on the people who can't find it or on the man who lives there.\nthen there's the legend's own version. when an official bows to him he runs. when a gift of clothes comes up the mountain he screams thieves and shuts himself in the rock. you can read that as enlightenment or as a frightened old man who couldn't stand being seen, and the preface lets you pick. the last fight is the one he lost to time. a man put his address where his name should be, and twelve hundred years later nobody can say whether he was one person or a dozen, or which century he was poor in.","threads":["wandering","madness","the land","loneliness","joy"],"year":750,"years":"sometime between the 600s and the 800s. nobody knows."},{"after":"after she got out, she built a life out of the thing that saved her. painting, then poems, then music, and teaching others how. she didn't write about the bad house to get even. she wrote about it to find the way through it, and then kept going.\nafter the laureateship she went back to tulsa, near where she started, and kept writing and playing. being the national poet didn't turn her into a monument. she's still showing up with a saxophone.","basically":"a muscogee poet and musician from tulsa who got out of a hard childhood through art, and in 2019 became the united states poet laureate, the country's official poet, the first native american ever to hold the job. she kept it three terms and used it to put native poets from all over the country on a map. she also plays saxophone in a band. her poems read like prayers you'd say driving at night. think of the kid in the house where it wasn't safe who found the one thing that could carry her out, and then was handed the national microphone and passed it around.","born":1951,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='150' cy='140' r='14'/><path d='M 150 154 L 150 202'/><path d='M 150 202 L 136 236 M 150 202 L 164 236'/><path d='M 150 170 L 172 180 M 150 166 L 172 170'/><path d='M 172 170 L 182 206 C 184 216 196 216 198 206 L 196 196' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 190 212 C 186 222 206 224 204 210' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 138 146 C 130 170 132 190 136 204 M 162 146 C 170 170 168 190 164 204' stroke-width='4'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='60' cy='40' r='3'/><circle cx='120' cy='60' r='2'/><circle cx='200' cy='30' r='3'/><circle cx='260' cy='70' r='2'/><circle cx='330' cy='36' r='3'/><circle cx='370' cy='90' r='2'/><circle cx='40' cy='110' r='2'/><\/g><path d='M 350 120 C 340 110 346 96 358 98 C 348 102 346 114 356 120 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 260 200 C 260 186 320 184 326 198 L 340 172 L 352 178 L 336 202 C 330 212 264 214 260 200 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 268 208 L 266 250 M 280 210 L 282 250 M 318 210 L 316 250 M 328 206 L 330 250'/><path d='M 260 200 C 250 204 248 216 252 224'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 214 M 34 214 L 46 214'/><g transform='translate(40,199.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the first native american poet laureate picked up the saxophone at forty.","key":"harjo","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joy_Harjo","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"her site","url":"https://www.joyharjo.com/","why":"her own site, with her books and music"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/joy-harjo","why":"a life and a lot of poems"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["she started the saxophone at forty, which is late for anyone, and stuck with it. there's something in that for everybody who thinks it's too late to start the thing.","as laureate she built an online map where you can click on a place and hear a native poet from there read."],"name":"joy harjo","place":"tulsa, oklahoma, then santa fe, albuquerque, and back to tulsa","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 92 130 L 98 100 C 100 92 94 88 92 94' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 86 124 C 82 132 100 134 98 122' fill='var(--marigold)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"remember the sky that you were born under,","where":"remember, the first line, in she had some horses, 1983"},{"q":"to pray, you open your whole self to the sky, the earth, the sun, the moon.","where":"eagle poem, the opening, paraphrased"},{"q":"the horses in the poem are all the parts of one woman, the scared ones and the brave ones, and at the end they turn out to be the same horses.","where":"she had some horses, the title poem, paraphrased"}],"start":"she had some horses, the 1983 book, the title poem and remember. then crazy brave, her memoir.","status":"approved","story":"she was born on may 9, 1951, in tulsa, oklahoma. she's a citizen of the muscogee nation, also called the creek, a people whose homeland was alabama and georgia until the government forced them west to what's now oklahoma on the trail of tears in the 1830s. she's descended from monahwee, a muscogee leader who fought against andrew jackson.\nthe house she grew up in wasn't safe, and she's written about it plainly in her memoir crazy brave. at sixteen she left for the institute of american indian arts, a native art school in santa fe, to be a painter. at the university of new mexico she turned to poems. she finished there in 1976 and got a master's in writing at the university of iowa in 1978. her first book, the last song, came out in 1975, and she had some horses in 1983.\nat forty she picked up the alto saxophone. she's played in bands ever since, first one called poetic justice, and put out several albums, reading and singing and playing, because to her the poems were always music.\nin 2019 she was named the twenty third poet laureate of the united states, the first native american to hold the job, and served three terms, until 2022. her project was a map of native poets and poems across the country, called living nations, living words. since then she's won the bollingen prize and the frost medal, two of the biggest prizes in american poetry. she lives in tulsa.","struggle":"the childhood. crazy brave is about growing up in a house where violence was part of the weather, and about a girl who felt everything too much and had nowhere to put it. she got out at sixteen, and the art school was the door.\nand the long middle, which she's also written about, of trying to make a life as a young mother, a poet, and a native woman in a country that didn't expect any of those to amount to anything.","threads":["joy","family","the land"],"years":"born 1951"},{"after":"what he did with the fire was go toward other people's. during the vatican council he pushed the cardinals to drop language hoping for the conversion of the jews, and said he'd rather go to auschwitz than give up his faith. he went to selma. he went against the war. a man who'd watched his world burned for being jewish spent his american years showing up for black americans and vietnamese villagers.\nnear the end, in the interview with carl stern, he said he'd asked for wonder and got it, and that just to be is a blessing, just to live is holy. he died not long after. he never pretended the fire hadn't happened. he just wouldn't give it the last word.","basically":"a rabbi from a long line of hasidic holy men in poland who got out of warsaw six weeks before the nazis came, lost his mother and sisters to them, and ended up in new york writing books about wonder. then he marched in the front row at selma with martin luther king, and came out against the vietnam war, when plenty of people thought a rabbi should stay in his lane. picture an old-world rabbi with a huge white beard at the front of a civil rights march with a hawaiian flower lei around his neck. take from him that prayer can be something you do with your feet.","born":1907,"died":1972,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--olive)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 250 C 60 247 120 253 200 250 S 340 248 400 250'/><g opacity='.55'><circle cx='28' cy='194' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='25' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='35' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 26 200 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 28 208 L 28 224.8'/><path d='M 28 224.8 L 16 250 M 28 224.8 L 40 250'/><path d='M 28.0 216 L 12.0 246'/><path d='M 28.0 216 L 44.0 246'/><circle cx='58' cy='194' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='55' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='65' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 56 200 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 58 208 L 58 224.8'/><path d='M 58 224.8 L 46 250 M 58 224.8 L 70 250'/><path d='M 58.0 216 L 42.0 246'/><path d='M 58.0 216 L 74.0 246'/><circle cx='88' cy='194' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='85' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='95' cy='192' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 86 200 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 88 208 L 88 224.8'/><path d='M 88 224.8 L 76 250 M 88 224.8 L 100 250'/><path d='M 88.0 216 L 72.0 246'/><path d='M 88.0 216 L 104.0 246'/><\/g><circle cx='150' cy='168' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='147' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='157' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 148 172 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 150 182 L 150 215.44'/><path d='M 150 215.44 L 134 250 M 150 215.44 L 166 250'/><path d='M 150.0 190 L 134.0 220'/><path d='M 150.0 190 L 166.0 220'/><circle cx='200' cy='168' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='197' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='207' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 198 172 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 200 182 L 200 215.44'/><path d='M 200 215.44 L 184 250 M 200 215.44 L 216 250'/><path d='M 200.0 190 L 184.0 220'/><path d='M 200.0 190 L 216.0 220'/><path d='M 130 172 q 6 26 20 24 q 14 2 20 -24' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 130 158 q 6 -14 14 -6 q 6 -12 14 -4 q 8 -10 12 0' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 138 184 q 12 14 24 0' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='142' cy='188' r='2.2' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='1.2'/><circle cx='150' cy='189' r='2.2' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='1.2'/><circle cx='158' cy='188' r='2.2' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='1.2'/><path d='M 188 184 q 12 14 24 0' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='192' cy='188' r='2.2' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='1.2'/><circle cx='200' cy='189' r='2.2' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='1.2'/><circle cx='208' cy='188' r='2.2' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='1.2'/><path d='M 330 250 L 330 150' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 302 150 L 358 150 L 358 118 L 302 118 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(318,93.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a hasidic rabbi from warsaw marched at selma next to king and said afterward his legs were praying.","key":"heschel","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Joshua_Heschel","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the king institute","url":"https://kinginstitute.stanford.edu/heschel-abraham-joshua","why":"stanford's page on him and king"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"some jewish leaders thought he went too far with the catholics during the second vatican council, the big meeting in rome in the early sixties where the catholic church rethought a lot of things, including what it said about jews. some thought a rabbi had no business marching or talking about vietnam. he went anyway.","moments":["in january 1963, at a national conference on religion and race in chicago, he opened by saying the first such conference had been between pharaoh, the king of egypt, and moses, the man who told him let my people go, and that it still wasn't over.","at selma the front row wore flower leis, brought by marchers from hawaii, and the photograph of him next to king, white beard and a lei around his neck, is the one the country saw.","his phrase for where faith starts is radical amazement, the plain shock that there's anything at all. it's all through god in search of man."],"name":"abraham joshua heschel","place":"warsaw, berlin, then new york","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 26 62 L 18 40 L 32 46 L 30 28 L 42 40 L 48 24 L 56 38 L 64 22 L 72 38 L 80 26 L 86 42 L 100 30 L 92 48 L 104 52 L 90 62' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 100 40 128 60 130 C 80 128 94 100 90 60 C 82 84 38 84 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 96 C 50 106 70 106 76 96 M 40 110 C 48 118 72 118 80 110' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 44 34 C 48 22 72 22 76 34 C 66 31 54 31 44 34 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i felt my legs were praying.","where":"his notes on the selma march, in moral grandeur and spiritual audacity, a collection of his essays"},{"q":"the sabbaths are our great cathedrals.","where":"the sabbath, his short book about the day of rest, the prologue"},{"q":"i did not ask for success, i asked for wonder, and you gave it to me.","where":"his tv interview with carl stern for nbc, filmed shortly before he died, paraphrased"}],"start":"the sabbath, which is short. then god in search of man, the early chapters on wonder. then moral grandeur and spiritual audacity, his collected essays, for selma and vietnam.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in warsaw in 1907 into a long line of hasidic rebbes, named after one of them, and everyone expected him to become one. hasidism is a joyful, mystical, intensely devout kind of judaism that started in eastern europe in the 1700s. each community gathers around a rebbe, a holy man and teacher, and the job often passes from father to son. instead he went to berlin, wrote a doctorate on the hebrew prophets, the bible's fire-breathing spokesmen for god like isaiah and amos, and kept praying the way he'd been raised.\nin october 1938 the nazis threw him out of germany along with thousands of other jews who held polish papers. he got out of warsaw about six weeks before the germans invaded, went to london, and reached new york in 1940. his mother and his sisters who were still in europe were killed. he called himself a brand plucked from the fire, a line from the prophet zechariah that means a stick yanked out of the flames just before it burns up.\nfrom 1945 until he died he taught at the jewish theological seminary in new york, where conservative rabbis are trained, conservative being the middle road in american judaism between orthodox and reform. the books came fast. man is not alone, the sabbath, god in search of man, the prophets. the sabbath is about the jewish day of rest, friday sundown to saturday sundown, and says the jews built their cathedrals in time, and the seventh day is one of them.\nhe met [[king|martin luther king]] in chicago in 1963 and they became friends. in march 1965 he marched in the front row out of selma, alabama, on the march to montgomery for black americans' right to vote. he spoke against vietnam beside king. ten days before king was killed, heschel introduced him to a room full of rabbis. heschel died in his sleep on the sabbath, december 23, 1972.","struggle":"he mostly didn't write about the murders directly. he wrote about wonder, and a god who comes looking for people, and he wrote it as a man whose mother and sisters had been killed by the country where he'd earned his degree. in 1965, opening a lecture at union theological seminary, a protestant school in new york, called no religion is an island, he said he'd left warsaw six weeks before the disaster, a brand plucked from the fire where his people were burned.\nhis first years in america, at hebrew union college in cincinnati, where reform rabbis are trained, reform being the liberal, modernized branch of judaism that had dropped a lot of the old prayer customs, he was a hasid from warsaw among people who mostly didn't pray the way he did, and he was lonely there. it took him five years to get to new york.","threads":["exile","grief","nonviolence"],"years":"1907 to 1972"},{"after":"the talmud gives the morning after, literally. having just admitted, in front of the men who had made him their leader, that he'd forgotten, he told them to leave it to the people: if they aren't prophets, they're the children of prophets. the next day he watched. whoever had brought a lamb had tucked the knife into its wool, and whoever had brought a goat had stuck it between its horns, so nobody had to carry a knife on the sabbath. he saw it and the law came back to him. this is what i received from shemaya and avtalyon, he said.\nhis students took the lesson further than he had. the talmud says that for three years each house insisted the law went its way, until a voice from heaven announced that both were the words of the living god, but the law follows the house of hillel. the usual dating puts that after the romans destroyed the temple in jerusalem in the year 70, long after both men were dead. the reason the talmud gives isn't that hillel's side was smarter. they were easy to get along with, and they taught shammai's rulings before their own. his descendants went on leading the rabbis in the land of israel for more than four hundred years. when he died, the talmud says, he was mourned with the words alas, the pious man, alas, the humble man.","basically":"a jewish teacher in jerusalem about two thousand years ago, an immigrant from babylonia, which is iraq now, remembered as the most patient man in the room. the best known story about his early days has him too broke to pay the fee at the door of the study hall, so he listens from the roof through the skylight and gets buried in snow, a bit like sitting under a lecture hall window because you can't cover tuition. nearly everything we know about him was written down two hundred to five hundred years after he died, so what we have is how he was remembered, which isn't the same as a record. the jewish student group on more than 800 college campuses today is called hillel, after him.","born":null,"died":10,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--olive)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 262 L 380 262'/><rect x='120' y='142' width='200' height='120' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 106 142 L 334 142'/><rect x='206' y='136' width='30' height='12' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='166' cy='200' r='14'/><path d='M 166 214 L 166 242'/><path d='M 166 242 L 156 262 M 166 242 L 176 262'/><path d='M 166 222 L 190 196 M 166 222 L 152 234'/><circle cx='274' cy='200' r='14'/><path d='M 274 214 L 274 242'/><path d='M 274 242 L 264 262 M 274 242 L 284 262'/><path d='M 274 222 L 254 232 M 274 222 L 290 234'/><path d='M 204 240 L 246 240 M 210 240 L 210 262 M 240 240 L 240 262'/><rect x='214' y='232' width='22' height='8' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='190' cy='106' r='14'/><path d='M 186 119 L 172 140'/><path d='M 182 126 L 204 136'/><path d='M 172 140 L 152 128 L 140 140'/><path d='M 128 141 Q 138 118 156 124 Q 170 120 178 141 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 178 99 Q 190 84 202 99 Q 190 95 178 99 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 246 141 Q 268 128 292 141 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g fill='var(--paper)' stroke='none'><circle cx='48' cy='52' r='2.5'/><circle cx='96' cy='96' r='2.5'/><circle cx='140' cy='40' r='2.5'/><circle cx='232' cy='66' r='2.5'/><circle cx='268' cy='100' r='2.5'/><circle cx='316' cy='48' r='2.5'/><circle cx='356' cy='112' r='2.5'/><circle cx='60' cy='150' r='2.5'/><circle cx='372' cy='60' r='2.5'/><circle cx='212' cy='28' r='2.5'/><circle cx='84' cy='206' r='2.5'/><circle cx='350' cy='190' r='2.5'/><\/g><g transform='translate(304,115) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a stranger dared him to teach the whole torah while standing on one foot, and the story says he did it in a sentence.","key":"hillel","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hillel_the_Elder","why":"the plain overview, with the traditional dates"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Houses_of_Hillel_and_Shammai","why":"the two schools and what they argued about"},{"label":"the jewish encyclopedia, 1906","url":"https://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/7698-hillel","why":"an old scholarly article that says which parts are legend"},{"label":"sefaria","url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Shabbat.31a?lang=en","why":"the talmud page with the bet and the one foot story, in english"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"one thing, and it's the talmud itself that tells it. on the day the sons of beteira put him in charge, he turned on them. what brought it about that i had to come up from babylonia and be made head over you, he said, was your own laziness in not learning from shemaya and avtalyon. then they asked him what to do if someone had forgotten to bring the slaughtering knife before the sabbath, and he had to say, i heard this law once and i have forgotten it. the rabbis drew the lesson themselves: a scholar who gets haughty loses his wisdom, and the example they name is hillel.\nthe prozbul drew fire too. later rabbis in the talmud were uneasy that he had found a way around a rule written in the torah, and one of them said that if he had the power he would cancel it.","moments":["the talmud tells of two men who bet four hundred zuz, silver coins, on whether one of them could make hillel lose his temper. it was a friday and hillel was washing his hair. three times the man came to his door shouting who here is hillel, with questions like why do babylonians have oval heads, and each time hillel called him my son and answered. when the man gave up and cursed him for the lost money, hillel said it was worth losing four hundred zuz, and four hundred more, and hillel still would not get upset.","a rabbinic commentary on leviticus, the third book of the torah, has him leaving his students one day. they ask where he's going and he says, to do a religious duty. which one? to wash at the bathhouse. somebody gets paid to scrub the statues of kings, he tells them, so how much more should he look after a body made in the image of god.","the talmud says he once found a horse to ride, and a servant to run ahead of it, for a poor man from a good family, because that was what the man had been used to. one day no servant could be found, and hillel ran in front of him himself.","at the passover seder, the ritual meal that opens the holiday, jews still put bitter herbs between two pieces of flat unleavened bread and eat them together the way hillel did. in his day, when the temple stood, the lamb went in too."],"name":"hillel","place":"babylonia, then jerusalem","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 104 38 96 60 96 C 82 96 100 104 102 130 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 97 M 68 84 L 68 97'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 31 52 C 28 22 92 22 89 52 C 78 40 42 40 31 52 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 40 35 C 52 29 68 29 80 35' stroke-width='1.8' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 33 64 C 33 96 48 116 60 118 C 72 116 87 96 87 64 C 82 76 72 78 60 78 C 48 78 38 76 33 64 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 94 L 49 104 M 60 98 L 60 110 M 72 94 L 71 104' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 44 50 Q 49 47 54 49 M 66 49 Q 71 47 76 50' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 83 C 57 87 63 87 67 83'/><rect x='80' y='108' width='30' height='12' rx='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 89 112 L 102 112 M 89 116 L 98 116' stroke-width='1.4' opacity='.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"that which is hateful to you do not do to another; that is the entire torah, and the rest is its interpretation. go study.","where":"the talmud, shabbat 31a, in the william davidson edition translation. written down about five hundred years after he lived"},{"q":"if i am not for myself, who is for me? but if i am for my own self only, what am i? and if not now, when?","where":"pirkei avot 1:14, the book of the early teachers' sayings inside the mishnah, in joshua kulp's translation, which marks the word only as the translator's addition"},{"q":"be of the disciples of aaron, loving peace and pursuing peace, loving mankind and drawing them close to the torah.","where":"pirkei avot 1:12, joshua kulp's translation"}],"start":"pirkei avot, the short book of the early teachers' sayings, chapter 1 and then the first part of chapter 2. after that the talmud's page of patience stories, shabbat 31a, in the william davidson edition, which is free to read online.","status":"approved","story":"nobody knows when he was born. the usual guess for his death is around the year 10. he came from babylonia, the land between the two rivers in what's now iraq, where jews had been living since the babylonians hauled them off from jerusalem some five hundred years before, and he went up to jerusalem to study. he lived there in the days of king herod, who ruled the country for rome. everything about him comes from books written long after. the mishnah, the first written collection of the rabbis' teaching, was put together around the year 200. the talmud, the enormous record of later rabbis arguing over the mishnah, was finished in babylonia about five hundred years after he died. one modern scholarly encyclopedia says even the earliest rabbinic sources about him are highly legendary. one old commentary gives him a hundred and twenty years in three neat forties, the same shape as the life of [[moses]], which tells you it's a tribute and not a birth record.\nthe talmud, which calls him hillel the elder, tells his early days this way. every day he worked and earned a small coin. half went to the guard at the door of the study hall where shemaya and avtalyon, the two great teachers of that generation, were teaching, and half fed his family. the usual retelling makes him a woodcutter. the talmud only says he worked. one day he found no work, and the guard wouldn't let him in. so he climbed onto the roof and sat at the edge of the skylight to listen. it was a friday in the dead of winter, and snow came down on him. at dawn shemaya said to avtalyon that the room was usually bright by this hour, and today it was dark. they looked up and saw the shape of a man in the skylight. they found him under three cubits of snow, got him down, washed him and sat him by the fire.\nhow he got to the top is a story too. one year the day before passover, the spring festival that remembers the escape from egypt, fell on the sabbath, the weekly day of rest when work is forbidden. the sons of beteira, the family of scholars then in charge, couldn't remember whether the passover lamb could still be slaughtered that day. somebody told them there was a man who had come up from babylonia, hillel the babylonian, who had studied under shemaya and avtalyon. he argued that the lamb comes first, and they sat him at the head and made him their leader. tradition counts forty years of that, from about 30 bc to about the year 10. whether he ever held an official title is another matter. the jewish encyclopedia of 1906 calls that part hardly historical.\nhis opposite number was shammai, a builder in the usual telling, stern where hillel was easy. the story everyone knows is in the talmud, in the volume on the sabbath, page 31a. a stranger who wasn't jewish came to shammai and said, convert me on condition that you teach me the whole torah while i stand on one foot. the torah is the five books at the start of the hebrew bible, the jewish scriptures. shammai pushed him off with the builder's measuring stick he had in his hand. the man took the same line to hillel, and hillel converted him and said: that which is hateful to you do not do to another; that is the entire torah, and the rest is its interpretation. go study. it's the don't version of what people call the golden rule.\nthe two men themselves are only recorded disagreeing three times, or five by some counts. their students were another matter. they formed two schools, the house of hillel and the house of shammai, and those argued over hundreds of points of law, with hillel's house usually the easier one. he is also credited with the prozbul, a legal paper that lets a loan survive the seventh year, when the torah says all debts are wiped out. the mishnah gives the reason. people had stopped lending to each other as the seventh year got close, and the torah forbids that too. his fix was to hand the debt over to a court, which can still collect.","struggle":"we have stories, not a diary. the sources are thin, and what there is was written by rabbis generations later. so nobody can tell you what he doubted or what kept him awake, and anyone who does is guessing.\nwhat the stories keep is that he was poor and that he was from somewhere else. the roof story is about a man who couldn't pay the door. the talmud turns it into a standard: when a poor person says he was too busy earning a living to study, the answer is, were you any poorer than hillel? and the man who ended up at the head of the table in jerusalem was introduced as a certain man who came up from babylonia. in the story about the bet, one of the questions thrown at him is why babylonians have oval heads, a dig at where he came from.\nand the argument he's famous for wasn't settled in his lifetime. the talmud says the two houses went on disagreeing long after he was gone, and that they once spent two and a half years on whether it would have been better for people never to have been created. they decided it would have been better, and that since we're here, a person should look hard at what he does.","threads":["anger","money","work"],"year":-50,"years":"nobody knows when he was born; died around the year 10"},{"after":"the last line of the diary, from october 1942, is we should be willing to act as a balm for all wounds. then she went and did it. spier was dead and she had offers of hiding places, and she went back into westerbork. with a leather bag over her shoulder she went from barrack to barrack, and she carried letters out and medicine in for as long as her pass worked. a historian who was born in that camp wrote that she became what she had hoped to become. the camp's jewish leaders tried hard to get her taken off the train when her name came up, and failed.\nthere was no rescue. a friend in the camp reported that the news caught her off guard and that her first reaction was shock. a friend who watched her go wrote that she stepped onto the platform talking gaily, with a kind word for everyone she met, perhaps just a touch of sadness. she was dead within three months, at twenty nine. what's left is the notebooks, ten of them by the fullest count, which sat with klaas smelik's family for close to forty years. when a selection was finally printed in 1981 it sold more than two hundred thousand copies in a year.","basically":"a dutch jewish woman in nazi-occupied amsterdam who started a diary at twenty seven because her therapist told her to. it opens like a therapy journal someone her age might keep now, all men and moods and whether she's wasting her life, and within a year and a half it has turned into a running conversation with god, written while the deportation trains were leaving. she had offers to hide, said no, went to work in westerbork, the camp the dutch jews were shipped out through, and died at auschwitz at twenty nine. she had no synagogue and no church behind her, and she came to think god couldn't rescue anyone, so the job was to keep a place for god alive in herself.","born":1914,"died":1943,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--olive)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 46 70 Q 54 60 62 70 Q 70 60 78 70 M 118 44 Q 125 36 132 44 Q 139 36 146 44 M 206 78 Q 213 70 220 78 Q 227 70 234 78 M 282 52 Q 289 44 296 52 Q 303 44 310 52' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 262 250 L 262 140 M 316 250 L 316 140 M 370 250 L 370 140'/><path d='M 244 166 L 390 166 M 244 204 L 390 204' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 285 162 L 293 170 M 293 162 L 285 170 M 339 162 L 347 170 M 347 162 L 339 170 M 285 200 L 293 208 M 293 200 L 285 208 M 339 200 L 347 208 M 347 200 L 339 208' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 56 250 L 56 204 M 82 250 L 82 190 M 106 250 L 106 208'/><path d='M 56 236 L 44 226 M 82 232 L 94 222 M 106 238 L 118 230' stroke-width='2.2'/><g fill='var(--lavender)' stroke-width='2'><circle cx='56' cy='200' r='5'/><circle cx='56' cy='211' r='6'/><circle cx='56' cy='223' r='7'/><circle cx='82' cy='186' r='5'/><circle cx='82' cy='197' r='6'/><circle cx='82' cy='209' r='7'/><circle cx='82' cy='222' r='7'/><circle cx='106' cy='204' r='5'/><circle cx='106' cy='215' r='6'/><circle cx='106' cy='227' r='7'/><\/g><path d='M 150 250 L 150 220 C 150 208 192 208 192 220 L 192 250 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 150 226 L 192 226 M 171 226 L 171 238'/><circle cx='172' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 157 154 C 152 136 162 130 172 132 C 183 130 192 137 187 155'/><path d='M 172 164 L 172 210'/><path d='M 172 211 L 210 213 L 216 250 L 226 250 M 172 211 L 205 219 L 206 250'/><path d='M 172 180 L 196 196 M 172 186 L 190 204'/><rect x='192' y='192' width='20' height='13' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 197 197 L 207 197 M 197 201 L 204 201' stroke-width='1.4'/><g transform='translate(358,113.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"offered a hiding place, she went to the camp instead, and the postcard she threw from the train says we left the camp singing.","key":"hillesum","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Etty_Hillesum","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"jewish women's archive","url":"https://jwa.org/encyclopedia/article/hillesum-etty","why":"a short scholarly life from the encyclopedia of jewish women"},{"label":"etty hillesum centrum","url":"https://ettyhillesumcentrum.nl/etty-hillesum/biografie-van-etty/","why":"the deventer museum's biography, in dutch, with the dates"},{"label":"the second world war in 100 objects","url":"https://www.tweedewereldoorlog.nl/100voorwerpen/en/?p=2211","why":"the postcard itself, kept at the jewish historical museum in amsterdam"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"the love life was a tangle, and she put all of it in the diary. in the same years she was sleeping with her landlord, a widower in his sixties, and with her therapist, who had a fiancée in london. she also took a job with the jewish council, got through her brother's pull, that kept her off the lists for a while when most people had nothing. she knew what the council was. nothing can ever atone, she wrote, for the fact that one section of the jewish population is helping to transport the majority out of the country.\nthe bigger argument is about her acceptance. she wouldn't hide and she wouldn't hate, and she called what was happening a mass fate, and wrote that it didn't much matter whether she went or someone else did. after the war a good many jewish readers took that badly. one scholar reports that survivors among them felt her attitude as a slap in the face to everyone who did resist by going into hiding, and were dismayed by how taken she was with christian texts. tzvetan todorov, a bulgarian-french critic who wrote a book on moral life in the camps, said her fatalism and passivity lent themselves to the murderous project of the nazis. an early reviewer in an american magazine pointed out that you'd never learn from her diary that amsterdam was the one occupied city in western europe where jews fought their persecutors in the streets. her own answer is in the diary. my acceptance is not indifference or helplessness, she wrote. i feel deep moral indignation at a regime that treats human beings in such a way. you can believe her and still think the critics have a point.","moments":["one night, shortly before going to bed, she suddenly went down on her knees in the middle of the big room, between the steel chairs and the matting. she wrote it down the next day. later she wrote that kneeling had become a gesture embedded in her body.","getting ready for the camp, she fretted over which books would fit in with the underwear and the blankets. she settled on the bible, [[rilke]]'s book of hours and letters to a young poet, dostoevsky's novel the idiot, and her russian dictionaries.","on june 8, 1943, two days after she went back to westerbork for good, she wrote that the sky was full of birds, the purple lupins were standing up regally and peacefully, two little old women had sat down for a chat, the sun was on her face, and right before their eyes, mass murder.","two of her long letters describing westerbork were printed illegally in the netherlands in 1943, under a made-up title about a long-dead painter, so the germans wouldn't look twice."],"name":"etty hillesum","place":"deventer and amsterdam, then westerbork","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 106 38 97 60 97 C 82 97 100 106 102 130 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 49 98 L 60 111 L 71 98'/><path d='M 30 118 L 30 130 M 42 112 L 42 130 M 78 112 L 78 130 M 90 118 L 90 130' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 97 M 68 84 L 68 97'/><path d='M 29 60 C 22 34 42 21 60 23 C 80 21 99 35 91 61 C 98 70 95 84 86 88 C 79 90 75 85 77 78 L 43 78 C 45 85 41 90 34 88 C 25 84 22 70 29 60 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 33 51 C 35 35 50 28 64 30 C 77 31 86 39 88 52 C 81 44 72 40 63 40 C 52 41 42 45 33 51 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 44 52 Q 50 48 56 51 M 64 51 Q 70 48 76 52' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='50' cy='58' r='2.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='58' r='2.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 61 L 58 68 L 62 68'/><path d='M 53 76 C 57 80 63 80 67 76'/><path d='M 76 106 L 104 102 L 108 122 L 80 126 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 83 111 L 99 109 M 84 116 L 100 114 M 85 121 L 96 120' stroke-width='1.4'/><path d='M 95 112 L 111 92' stroke-width='2.2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"you cannot help us, but we must help you and defend your dwelling place inside us to the last.","where":"her diary, a prayer written on 12 july 1942, in an interrupted life, translated by arnold pomerans"},{"q":"there is a really deep well inside me. and in it dwells god. sometimes i am there, too. but more often stones and grit block the well, and god is buried beneath. then he must be dug out again.","where":"her diary, 26 august 1941, in etty: the letters and diaries of etty hillesum 1941-1943, translated by arnold pomerans"},{"q":"we left the camp singing.","where":"the postcard she threw from the train on 7 september 1943, printed at the end of an interrupted life, translated by arnold pomerans"}],"start":"an interrupted life and letters from westerbork, in arnold pomerans's translation, sold as one volume. if the early pages about spier wear you out, jump to the summer of 1942 and read on from there, then the long letters from the camp. the complete edition, etty: the letters and diaries of etty hillesum 1941-1943, is for when you want everything.","status":"approved","story":"she was born esther hillesum on january 15, 1914, in middelburg, a town in the southwest corner of the netherlands. the family followed her father's teaching jobs and landed in 1924, when she was ten, in deventer, a small old city on the ijssel river in the east. he taught latin and greek at the gymnasium, the academic high school, and became its headmaster. her mother had come from russia, a refugee from the pogroms, the mob attacks on jews there. etty was the oldest of three. her brother jaap became a doctor. her brother mischa was a pianist who played in public at six. jaap was hospitalized more than once for depression, mischa was diagnosed with schizophrenia, and she called the household a madhouse. one grandfather had been a chief rabbi in the north of the country, but she was given little jewish religious teaching as a child, and in 1937 the family had itself taken off the rolls of the jewish community.\nin 1932 she went to amsterdam to study law, and finished in july 1939. then she started on slavic languages and paid her way giving private russian lessons. in march 1937 she took a room in the house of han wegerif, a widowed accountant in his sixties. she ran the household for him and became his lover. germany invaded the netherlands in may 1940, and that november the occupiers dismissed her father from his school because he was a jew. on february 3, 1941, she met julius spier, a jewish refugee from germany, fifty four years old, who had spent twenty five years at a metal trading firm in frankfurt, studied with carl jung, the psychiatrist in zurich, and now read people's characters from their hands. he called it psychochirology, and his treatment included wrestling with his patients. she became his patient, then his secretary, and then his lover, though he had a fiancée waiting in london and she was still with wegerif. about a month in, most likely on his advice, she started keeping a diary.\nthe early pages are about her body, her moods and the men. then something else comes in. spier put the bible and [[augustine]], the early christian writer, in front of her, and took her deeper into [[rilke]], the poet from prague who wrote in german, and dostoevsky, the russian novelist. she copied lines from all of them into the notebooks. by the bible she meant both halves, the hebrew bible, which is the jewish scriptures, and the new testament, the christian books about [[jesus]]. she started to kneel, and called herself a kneeler in training. on august 26, 1941, she wrote that there was a really deep well inside her, that god dwelt in it, and that more often stones and grit blocked the well and he had to be dug out again. on june 22, 1942, she wrote that she found the word god so primitive at times, that it was only a metaphor after all. her friends accused her of christianity. yes, christianity, she said, and why ever not. the people who study her call her religion non-denominational and hard to put in a box, and warn against turning her into a secret christian.\nin july 1942 the mass deportations of jews from the netherlands began. that month she got an office job at the jewish council, the body the germans had set up to pass their orders down to the jewish community, whose staff were exempt from deportation for the time being. her brother jaap helped her get it. two weeks later, at her own request, she was sent to westerbork, a camp on the heath in the northeast of the country where jews were held until the trains took them east. she started there on july 30, in the council's department of social welfare for people in transit. her pass let her travel, so she went back and forth to amsterdam, carrying letters and messages out of the camp and medicine in. she was in amsterdam, sick, when spier died there on september 15, 1942. friends offered her hiding places. she turned them all down. on june 6, 1943, she went back to westerbork for the last time. her parents and mischa were brought there on june 21, and on july 5 the council's people in the camp lost their special status. she was a prisoner like everyone else.\non tuesday, september 7, 1943, she, her parents and mischa were put on a transport to auschwitz, the biggest of the nazi death camps, in occupied poland. the order came without warning. from the train she threw out a postcard addressed to a friend in deventer, and farmers found it and mailed it. it says she opened the bible at random and found the lord is my high tower, that she was sitting on her rucksack in the middle of a full freight car, and that we left the camp singing. her parents are recorded as dying on september 10, which means on the train or on arrival. the red cross later gave her own date of death as november 30, 1943. nobody knows how she died. mischa was dead by the end of march 1944. jaap was sent to bergen-belsen, a camp in northern germany, in february 1944, and died in 1945, around the time it was freed. before she left amsterdam she had given her notebooks to a housemate to pass on to klaas smelik, a writer she knew, to be published if she didn't come back. he tried for years and no publisher would take them. a selection finally came out in dutch in 1981 as het verstoorde leven, and in english in 1983, translated by arnold pomerans. in america it's called an interrupted life.","struggle":"she went to spier because she was a mess and knew it. the first diary entry, in march 1941, describes her inner chaos and her need for help, and part of what spier did for her was teach her how to handle her depressed and self-absorbed spells. when she found she was pregnant she ended it herself, with twenty quinine pills and hot water, and wrote that she had kept another unhappy being out of the world.\nthe harder fight was with hatred. she was a jew in an occupied city and the anti-jewish rules kept tightening. she wrote of being suddenly beside herself with anger, cursing and swearing at the germans. she also wrote that she saw no other solution than to turn inward and root out all the rottenness there. at westerbork, she wrote, it was brought home to her how every atom of hatred added to the world makes it an even more inhospitable place.\nspier died in september 1942, and the diary as we have it stops a month later, on october 13. after that there are only the letters. in the camp she looked at the faces of the guards one day and was terrified, and wrote that the verse and god made man after his likeness spent a difficult morning with her. she called westerbork hell. she also wrote that her two months behind barbed wire had been the two richest and most intense months of her life.","threads":["war","prison","love","family","dying"],"years":"1914 to 1943"},{"after":"he didn't disown the drug and he didn't cheerlead it. he kept publishing chemistry, retired from sandoz in 1971, and wrote the problem child book to say plainly what he thought. real value in psychiatry and in the kind of looking the mystics do, real danger in careless use, and young people shouldn't be taking it at parties.\nat a hundred he was still giving interviews, and he lived to see the first new lsd therapy study in decades approved in switzerland, in 2007. he said the discovery hadn't been his to choose. it had found him.","basically":"a careful swiss chemist at a drug company who, in 1943, found out by accident that a compound he'd made five years earlier could knock your whole sense of reality loose. he spent the next sixty five years watching it get used for therapy, spy experiments, and the sixties, and he called it his problem child. he lived to a hundred and two. if you've ever made something that got away from you, he's the man who knew exactly how that feels.","born":1906,"died":2008,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 150 250 C 190 250 200 236 230 246 C 260 256 280 232 310 244 C 340 256 360 238 380 246'/><path d='M 30 250 L 30 170 L 110 170 L 110 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 90 170 L 90 140 L 102 140 L 102 170' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='46' y='190' width='18' height='18' fill='var(--blue)'/><rect x='76' y='190' width='18' height='18' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='190' cy='232' r='18'/><circle cx='250' cy='232' r='18'/><path d='M 190 232 L 212 206 L 240 206 L 250 232 M 212 206 L 220 232 L 190 232 M 220 232 L 240 206 M 240 206 L 238 196 L 248 194 M 212 206 L 210 200'/><circle cx='214' cy='160' r='14'/><path d='M 214 174 L 212 206 L 220 232 M 212 206 L 234 214 M 214 186 L 240 196'/><path d='M 240 120 C 250 110 262 124 272 112 M 180 130 C 170 118 188 110 178 100 M 290 150 C 300 140 312 150 322 138' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='330' cy='70' r='16' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 354 70 L 364 70'/><path d='M 306 70 L 296 70'/><path d='M 330 94 L 330 104'/><path d='M 330 46 L 330 36'/><path d='M 347 87 L 354 94'/><path d='M 313 87 L 306 94'/><path d='M 347 53 L 354 46'/><path d='M 313 53 L 306 46'/><g transform='translate(88,118.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a swiss chemist made lsd looking for a heart and lung drug, then rode his bike home through the most famous afternoon in drug history.","key":"hofmann","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_Hofmann","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"his book, free online","url":"https://maps.org/images/pdf/books/lsdmyproblemchild.pdf","why":"lsd: my problem child, his own account, hosted by maps"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"he was a company chemist, and the company sent his drugs to the psychiatrists and researchers who asked, some of whom, it turned out, were working for governments doing ugly things with them. he wasn't one of them.","moments":["in august 1961 [[huxley|aldous huxley]], the novelist who'd written about mescaline, rang him at the lab out of the blue while passing through zurich and took him and his wife to lunch.","in september 1971 he met timothy leary, then a fugitive in switzerland, at a railway station snack bar in lausanne. he found him charming, sincere, and blind to the dangers.","in 1978 he co-wrote the road to eleusis with gordon wasson and a classics professor, carl ruck, arguing that the secret drink at an ancient greek religious festival had ergot in it."],"name":"albert hofmann","place":"basel, switzerland","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 92 96 L 92 106 L 84 124 L 108 124 L 100 106 L 100 96 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 90 96 L 102 96'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"an uninterrupted stream of fantastic pictures, extraordinary shapes with intense, kaleidoscopic play of colors.","where":"lsd: my problem child, chapter 1, quoting his own lab report on the accident of april 16, 1943"},{"q":"wrong and inappropriate use has caused lsd to become my problem child.","where":"lsd: my problem child, his 1979 memoir of the drug"},{"q":"it's medicine for the soul.","where":"interviews around his hundredth birthday in 2006, paraphrased"}],"start":"lsd: my problem child. the first chapter is the bicycle day story in his own words, and chapter 6 is his trip to maría sabina's town.","status":"approved","story":"he was a chemist at sandoz, a big drug company in basel, switzerland, working on ergot, a fungus that grows on rye and other grain. for centuries ergot in bread had poisoned whole villages, and drug companies were pulling useful medicines out of it. in november 1938 he made a new compound from it, number twenty five in a series, lysergic acid diethylamide, lsd-25, hoping for a drug to perk up breathing and circulation. the animal tests were boring. it went on the shelf.\nin april 1943 he had a hunch and made it again. on april 16 some of it got into him by accident, probably through his fingertips, and he had to go home in the middle of the afternoon with, as he wrote later, an uninterrupted stream of fantastic pictures behind his eyes. three days later, on april 19, he took some on purpose to find out. it hit much harder than he'd planned. wartime switzerland had no gas for cars, so he rode his bicycle home with his lab assistant alongside, sure he was losing his mind. people who take lsd now call april 19 bicycle day.\nafter that he went on with his work. in 1958 he took mushrooms from mexico, the ones [[sabina|maría sabina]] used, grown in a lab in paris from samples [[wasson|gordon wasson]] had brought back, and pulled out their active chemical and named it psilocybin. sandoz sold both drugs to researchers for years. in 1962 he went up to her town himself.\nhe watched lsd go from a psychiatry tool to a cia experiment to a street drug to a banned substance. in 1979 he wrote a book about it and called it lsd: my problem child. he kept saying, into his hundreds, that it was medicine for the soul when used with care and a disaster when used carelessly. he died at home in the swiss countryside in april 2008, at a hundred and two.","struggle":"the bicycle ride was not fun. he wrote that he thought a demon had taken him over, that the neighbor lady bringing him milk looked like a witch in a mask, and that he was afraid he'd die and leave his wife and kids without knowing what he'd done to himself. a doctor came and found nothing wrong except big pupils. by the next morning he felt fine, clear, even new.\nthe long struggle was the next thirty years. his discovery got given to people without their knowledge in the cia's secret mind control program, mkultra, and it got handed out at rock concerts. he didn't control any of it and he took it personally. he broke with timothy leary's way of doing things in writing, while still liking the man.","threads":["psychedelics","work"],"years":"1906 to 2008"},{"after":"he wrote island after the fire. it's the book where he tried to build the good version of brave new world, a society organized around attention and kindness and knowing you'll die. it's not a great novel and it's a great answer.\nand on his last afternoon, dying of cancer, too weak to talk, he wrote a note to his second wife laura asking for lsd, a hundred micrograms, as a shot. she gave it to him and read to him from the tibetan book of the dead, the same guide for the dying he'd read to maria, while the news of kennedy's assassination was on every television in the country. he went out the way he'd told maria to.","basically":"an english novelist who wrote brave new world, the 1932 book about a future where everybody's kept happy with a pill and cheap entertainment and nobody wants anything real, which still feels uncomfortably close. then he moved to los angeles, got serious about the mystics, and took a psychedelic in his living room to see what they'd seen. he's the one who first made the case, for regular readers in english, that all the religions are pointing at the same thing underneath. if you've ever said all paths go up the same mountain, he wrote the book on it.","born":1894,"died":1963,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 260 L 20 90 L 380 90 L 380 260' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 150 260 L 150 120 L 250 120 L 250 260' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 165 200 L 175 180 M 190 160 L 205 140 M 220 190 L 235 170' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='210' cy='150' r='12' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 230 150 L 240 150'/><path d='M 190 150 L 180 150'/><path d='M 210 170 L 210 180'/><path d='M 210 130 L 210 120'/><path d='M 224 164 L 231 171'/><path d='M 196 164 L 189 171'/><path d='M 224 136 L 231 129'/><path d='M 196 136 L 189 129'/><circle cx='120' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 120 164 L 120 212'/><path d='M 120 212 L 106 246 M 120 212 L 134 246'/><path d='M 120 178 L 106 202 M 120 178 L 146 172'/><circle cx='113' cy='150' r='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='129' cy='150' r='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 120 150 L 122 150'/><path d='M 20 200 L 150 200 M 20 230 L 150 230 M 250 200 L 380 200 M 250 230 L 380 230' opacity='.35'/><g transform='translate(300,63.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he went almost blind at sixteen, wrote brave new world, and then decided all the mystics were describing the same thing and made a book to prove it.","key":"huxley","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldous_Huxley","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Perennial_Philosophy","why":"the book that lines up all the mystics"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["he taught french at eton, the most famous boys' school in england, for a year around 1918, and one of his students was eric blair, later george orwell, who wrote 1984. brave new world and 1984 had the same schoolteacher.","the perennial philosophy came out in 1945, the same year america dropped the atomic bomb on japan, from a pacifist who'd left england for california partly to get away from the coming war.","he took mescaline for the first time in may 1953 in his living room, with a researcher taking notes, and spent the afternoon looking at the folds of his trousers."],"name":"aldous huxley","place":"england, then los angeles","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 57 56 L 63 56 M 43 55 L 34 53 M 77 55 L 86 53' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the ground of all being is the same whether you get there by the christian door or the buddhist one, and the doorway isn't the room.","where":"the perennial philosophy, paraphrased from its argument"},{"q":"it's a little embarrassing that after forty five years of research the best advice i have is to be a bit kinder to each other.","where":"a late talk, widely quoted, paraphrased"},{"q":"the man who comes back through the door in the wall will never be quite the same as the man who went out.","where":"the doors of perception, his short 1954 book about taking mescaline"}],"start":"the perennial philosophy, chapter 1, which is the whole thesis. the doors of perception is an afternoon. island, his last novel, from 1962, is where he tried to build a good version of brave new world.","status":"approved","story":"an english novelist from a famous family of scientists. his grandfather was darwin's loudest defender. he was nearly blinded by an eye infection as a teenager. brave new world came out in 1932, the novel where babies are grown in bottles and adults are kept content with a happy pill called soma, and he was famous. then in 1937 he moved to california and got quietly religious in a way that fit no church.\nthe perennial philosophy, from 1945, is an anthology with his commentary. perennial as in the plant that keeps coming back every spring. he took [[eckhart|meister eckhart]], a german priest from around 1300, [[rumi]], the persian poet, the [[krishna|gita]], the hindu scripture, [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the chinese sage, the buddhist sutras, which are the buddha's recorded talks, and the christian mystics, and cut them up and laid them side by side to show they're all saying one thing. there's a ground under everything, you're not separate from it, and the whole job is to find that out. mystics, here and everywhere on these pages, are the people who want to experience god directly instead of just believing things about god. it's the book that made the idea respectable in english.\nin may 1953 he took mescaline, the drug in the peyote cactus, in his living room and wrote the doors of perception about it. the title is from william blake, the english poet, who wrote that if those doors were cleaned, we'd see everything as it is, infinite. jim morrison named his band, the doors, after the book. in 1960 he visited timothy leary, the harvard psychologist who became the face of lsd, and the whole psychedelic decade partly runs through that visit. [[ramdass|ram dass]] got to god by a road huxley helped pave.\nhe died on november 22, 1963, the same afternoon kennedy was shot in dallas, after asking his wife laura to inject him with lsd. she did. [[merton|thomas merton]], the american monk, who'd become a monk partly because of one of huxley's early books, had argued with him by letter about whether the drugs got you anywhere real.","struggle":"at sixteen an eye infection left him nearly blind for about eighteen months and he read braille, the raised-dot alphabet, and taught himself to type. his eyes never fully came back. the man who wrote the doors of perception spent his life squinting through them.\nhis first wife maria died of cancer in 1955 and he sat by the bed reading her instructions from the tibetan buddhist tradition for dying, the words you say into a dying person's ear to guide them through, telling her to go toward the light. he wrote it into island, his last novel.\nin 1961 a wildfire in the hills above los angeles took his house and every paper he'd ever kept, letters, manuscripts, forty years. he said afterward it was a clean sweep and he felt light, which is either enlightenment or shock.","threads":["psychedelics","dying"],"years":"1894 to 1963"},{"after":"he didn't take it back and he didn't back down. right there in aleppo in 1215 he sat down and wrote a commentary on his own poems, line by line, showing what each lover's name and empty campsite stood for in his teaching about god. the poems stayed love poems. he just put the key next to them, and when nicholson translated the book in 1911 he printed the poem and the commentary together, so you still read both.\nthere was no making peace with his critics, then or since. he settled in damascus, trained students, finished the first draft of the meccan openings in december 1231, and then went back and did the whole thing again, a second draft finished in 1238, two years before he died. in 1516 selim the first, the turkish emperor who had just taken syria, ordered his tomb restored, and in 1518 built a mosque complex over it.","basically":"a muslim mystic from twelfth century spain who wrote hundreds of books and treatises, one of them about fifteen thousand pages long in modern print. later sufis, the muslims who go after god directly and not only through the rules, call him the greatest master, and for centuries plenty of other muslim scholars have called him a heretic. he wrote the lines people love to quote about a heart that can be a monastery, a temple of idols, the kaaba, the torah and the quran all at once, and he wrote them as a love poem. arguing about him inside islam is like arguing about a film that half the audience calls a masterpiece and half calls blasphemy, except the film is fifteen thousand pages long and hardly anyone has finished it.","born":1165,"died":1240,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--teal)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 262 L 380 262'/><path d='M 250 95 C 250 45 160 35 150 100 C 142 160 215 210 250 262 C 285 210 358 160 350 100 C 340 35 250 45 250 95 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(0,-10)'><path d='M 180 165 L 180 142 L 204 142 L 204 165 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 177 142 L 192 128 L 207 142'/><path d='M 192 128 L 192 118 M 188 122 L 196 122'/><path d='M 189 165 L 189 154 L 195 154 L 195 165'/><path d='M 212 150 L 240 150 L 226 138 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 217 150 L 217 165 M 226 150 L 226 165 M 235 150 L 235 165 M 211 165 L 241 165'/><path d='M 247 143 L 269 143 L 269 165 L 247 165 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 247 150 L 269 150' stroke='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 276 165 L 276 150 C 276 144 285 144 285 150 L 285 165 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 287 165 L 287 150 C 287 144 296 144 296 150 L 296 165 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 302 165 C 308 160 313 162 314 165 C 315 162 320 160 326 165 L 326 155 C 320 150 315 152 314 155 C 313 152 308 150 302 155 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 314 155 L 314 165'/><\/g><ellipse cx='248' cy='192' rx='15' ry='6' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 237 196 L 235 208 M 243 197 L 243 208 M 253 197 L 253 208 M 259 196 L 261 208'/><path d='M 261 189 L 268 179'/><ellipse cx='271' cy='177' rx='5' ry='3' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 270 174 C 269 169 266 166 264 165 M 273 174 C 273 169 271 166 269 165'/><path d='M 233 190 L 229 187'/><circle cx='80' cy='122' r='14'/><path d='M 66 118 C 66 102 94 102 94 118 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 80 136 L 80 200'/><path d='M 80 200 L 66 260 M 80 200 L 94 260'/><path d='M 80 150 L 100 164 L 86 170'/><path d='M 80 150 L 62 188'/><path d='M 108 150 C 118 146 128 146 138 150' opacity='.4'/><g transform='translate(348,235) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"as a teenager he answered a famous philosopher with yes and no, and spent the next sixty years on the gap between them.","key":"ibnarabi","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ibn_Arabi","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"stanford encyclopedia of philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/ibn-arabi/","why":"a careful scholar's account of his ideas, including why unity of being isn't his phrase"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tarjuman_al-Ashwaq","why":"the book of love poems"},{"label":"poem eleven, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/isl/taa/taa14.htm","why":"nicholson's 1911 translation of the religion of love poem, with ibn arabi's own commentary"}],"lineage":"muslim","mess":"for eight hundred years a lot of muslim scholars have called him a heretic. ibn taymiyya, born in 1263, twenty odd years after ibn arabi died, called the unity of being idea worse than unbelief. a story went round that a leading scholar of the day called him a master of evil, though whether that was ever really said is argued over. even his defenders hedged. al-suyuti, the cairo scholar who wrote around a thousand works, defended him before he died in 1505 and still concluded that he was a saint but his books were forbidden to people who don't know sufi language. in 1979 egypt's parliament voted to stop the meccan openings being reprinted, and the sources don't agree on whether the ban ever held.\nthen there's what he said about himself. he claimed to be the seal of the saints, the one who closes the line of saints who follow muhammad, and a verse credited to him says, i am the seal of sainthood without any doubt. that is a very large thing to say about yourself, and his critics said so. his writing is notoriously hard, even for people who give their lives to it, which makes it easy to make him say anything. and there's nizam, the daughter of the man he was studying with. he wrote a book of love poems to her and then insisted they were about god. he called his love for her noble hearted and asked readers not to think anything unbecoming.","moments":["in seville he served fatima of cordoba, a woman past ninety five, for years. he and two friends built her a hut of reeds outside the city. she called herself his divine mother and the light of his earthly mother.","he also sat with shams of marchena, called the mother of the poor, who was over eighty when he met her. he wrote that he had never known anyone with such control over her own soul.","he saw ibn rushd once more, dead. the philosopher died in morocco in december 1198, three years after a court in cordoba had ordered his books burned, and his body was brought home to cordoba. ibn arabi watched them load the coffin on one side of a pack animal and his books on the other to balance it, and wrote two lines: here the master, there his works. would that i knew if his hopes came true."],"name":"ibn arabi","place":"murcia and seville in muslim spain, then mecca, anatolia and damascus","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 36 92 48 108 60 110 C 72 108 84 92 84 66 C 76 80 44 80 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 90 C 54 96 66 96 70 90' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 50 72 C 55 69 58 70 60 72 C 62 70 65 69 70 72'/><path d='M 28 50 C 24 22 44 12 60 12 C 76 12 96 22 92 50 C 80 40 40 40 28 50 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 40 C 48 26 72 26 88 40 M 36 30 C 50 20 72 20 84 30' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 45 57 C 48 60 52 60 55 57 M 65 57 C 68 60 72 60 75 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 84 126 L 106 82'/><path d='M 104 86 L 108 76 L 109 88 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='92' cy='112' r='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"my heart has become capable of every form: it is a pasture for gazelles and a convent for christian monks.","where":"the tarjuman al-ashwaq, poem xi, verse 13, in reynold nicholson's 1911 translation"},{"q":"i follow the religion of love: whatever way love's camels take, that is my religion and my faith.","where":"the tarjuman al-ashwaq, poem xi, verse 15, in reynold nicholson's 1911 translation"},{"q":"yes and no. between the yes and the no, spirits take flight from matter and necks come away from their bodies.","where":"his answer to ibn rushd, as he tells it in the meccan openings; paraphrased from several english versions"}],"start":"the interpreter of desires in nicholson's 1911 translation, which is free online and prints ibn arabi's own commentary under each poem. start with poem eleven. leave the bezels of wisdom for later; it's short and it's brutal.","status":"approved","story":"muhyiddin ibn arabi was born in 1165 in murcia, a city in al-andalus, the part of spain that muslims ruled for almost eight hundred years. his father served the local ruler, and when murcia fell to a rival dynasty in 1172 the family moved to seville, the big city of the south, when the boy was about seven. he got the ordinary education of a cultured young man, nothing especially religious. then in his early teens something hit him. he went on retreat, time alone with god, and later said he had turned to god \"at the hands of jesus\", meaning [[jesus]] as the quran describes him, the quran being what muslims hold to be god's own speech, where jesus is one of the great prophets. in one telling he saw jesus, [[moses]] and the prophet muhammad together in a dream.\naround 1180 his father took him to meet a friend, ibn rushd, known in europe as averroes, a judge and doctor so famous for explaining ancient greek philosophy that latin europe just called him the commentator. ibn arabi tells it himself in the meccan openings, his giant book. he was still a beardless kid. the great man stood up, hugged him and said yes. the boy said yes. ibn rushd looked pleased, the boy saw why, and said no. ibn rushd asked what he had found through mystical unveiling, and whether it matched what reason finds. the boy said yes and no, and that between the yes and the no, spirits take flight from matter and necks come away from their bodies. ibn rushd went pale, shook, and muttered the old prayer, there is no power except with god.\nin seville he studied with a lot of teachers, and two he wrote about most warmly were women in their eighties and nineties. in 1200, after a vision telling him to go east, he left al-andalus for the last time and never came back. he reached mecca for the hajj, the great muslim pilgrimage, in 1202, and started the meccan openings there, the futuhat al-makkiyya, which grew to 560 chapters over the next thirty years. in mecca he studied with a scholar from persia whose daughter, nizam, was a young woman known for her learning, her hard, simple way of living and her preaching. he called her the teacher of the two holy sanctuaries. about twelve years later, back in mecca, he wrote the interpreter of desires, the tarjuman al-ashwaq, sixty one love poems full of lost lovers, empty campsites and camels, and said every name in the book meant her. poem eleven is the one everyone quotes, the heart that has become a pasture for gazelles and a convent for monks, the pilgrim's kaaba and the tablets of the torah.\nthen he kept moving. in 1204 a powerful man from the court in anatolia, the land that is most of turkey today, talked him into coming north, and for the next twenty years he crossed and recrossed anatolia, iraq, syria and egypt and went back to mecca. when that friend died, the story goes, ibn arabi married his widow and raised her son, sadr al-din al-qunawi, who became his closest student and later a friend of [[rumi]] in konya. in 1223, at about fifty eight, he settled in damascus for good. there in 1229 he wrote the fusus al-hikam, the bezels of wisdom, a short, dense book of twenty seven chapters, each on a different prophet. he said the prophet muhammad gave him the book in a dream and he only passed it on. he died in damascus in november 1240, at seventy five.\nthe big idea people pin on him got its name after he was gone: wahdat al-wujud, unity of being, the notion that there is only one real existence, god's, and everything else exists by it. he never used the phrase. ibn taymiyya, the damascus scholar who loathed it, seems to have been the first to say ibn arabi believed it.","struggle":"he left almost nothing about panic or dark nights. what he left is the stretch where people said he was lying. in 1215, while he was in aleppo, a city in northern syria, word reached him that a religious lawyer was saying the preface to the interpreter of desires was false, that the talk of divine knowledge was a cover for plain love poetry. some devout readers called the poems vain and amorous. he had written sixty one poems of longing about a real woman in mecca, and now respectable men were saying he was dressing desire up as religion.\nthe other hard fact is that after 1200 he never went home. murcia, seville and the old women who had trained him were all behind him, and he spent forty years as a foreigner in other people's cities. cordoba fell to the christian kingdoms of the north in 1236, four years before he died, and seville went eight years after. the world he came from was being taken apart while he was still writing about it.","threads":["love","wandering"],"years":"1165 to 1240"},{"after":"the morning after manresa is the exercises. what he did with the year that nearly killed him was write it down as a method, so that nobody else would need the cave. the spiritual exercises are a four week program of about thirty days, done in silence under a guide: a week on your own sins and god's mercy, one on the life of [[jesus]], one on his death, one on his rising. the part people borrow most is the set of rules near the end for telling your good urges from your bad ones. they are the sickbed and the window turned into instructions. one rule defines spiritual consolation as an inward movement that leaves you with more faith, hope and love, and spiritual desolation as the opposite, darkness and restlessness and feeling cut off from god. the next says that in desolation you never change a decision you made in better light, because the one advising you then is the bad spirit. the one after that says the person in desolation should labor to be patient and trust that consolation will come back. a man who spent a week starving himself in 1522 wrote that. the book was printed in 1548 and is still in print in several english translations.\nthe morning after the courts was the order. he did the seven years of school, found six friends, and built a company of educated priests who answered to the pope, so that nobody could jail them for preaching without a degree. the jerusalem plan never happened. he sent francis xavier to india in 1541, and within twenty five years of the pope's approval the jesuits were three thousand. he went home to the basque country once, after paris. and when the men around him asked him, over and over, to tell them how it had all started, he put it off for years and then, in august 1553, dying slowly, walked up and down a room and told a jesuit named gonçalves da câmara the whole thing, the vanity, the leg, the window, in the third person, as if it had happened to someone else.","basically":"a basque soldier and ladies' man whose leg was smashed by a cannonball in 1521. he spent months in bed with nothing to read but a life of [[jesus]] and a book of saints, decided to become one himself, nearly died of the guilt, and in 1540 founded the jesuits, the catholic order he built around schools and foreign missions. his book, the spiritual exercises, is a month long program for sorting out your life, and its rules for telling a good urge from a bad one read like something a decent therapist would hand you today. think of a guy who gets religion during the long rehab after a bad injury, except he then writes the manual people are still using five hundred years on.","born":1491,"died":1556,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='350' cy='52' r='3' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 350 56 L 350 150'/><path d='M 336 76 L 364 76'/><path d='M 346 56 L 354 56 L 354 70 L 346 70 Z' fill='var(--clay)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 82 140 L 92 140 L 92 236 L 82 236 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 318 170 L 328 170 L 328 236 L 318 236 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 92 196 L 318 196 L 318 230 L 92 230 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 100 230 L 100 250 M 310 230 L 310 250'/><ellipse cx='132' cy='186' rx='32' ry='13' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 176 196 C 176 184 200 182 214 184 L 316 184 L 316 230 L 176 230 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 196 188 L 196 230' stroke-width='2' opacity='.5'/><ellipse cx='282' cy='206' rx='28' ry='10' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='142' cy='160' r='14'/><circle cx='138' cy='158' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='146' cy='158' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 138 167 Q 142 165 146 167' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 150 172 L 190 198'/><path d='M 160 178 L 190 166 M 168 184 L 196 174'/><path d='M 184 156 L 202 162 L 220 156 L 220 172 L 202 178 L 184 172 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 202 162 L 202 178' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 190 198 L 282 190'/><path d='M 282 190 L 298 184'/><ellipse cx='252' cy='194' rx='16' ry='9' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 244 187 L 246 202 M 252 185 L 253 203 M 260 187 L 261 201' stroke-width='1.8'/><circle cx='54' cy='238' r='12' fill='var(--teal)'/><g transform='translate(312,143) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a cannonball put him to bed, the only books in the house were about saints, and the rest followed.","key":"ignatius","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ignatius_of_Loyola","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spiritual_Exercises","why":"the book, its four weeks, and the main english translations"},{"label":"christian classics ethereal library","url":"https://ccel.org/ccel/ignatius/autobiography/autobiography","why":"the 1900 english translation of his autobiography, free, chapter by chapter"},{"label":"internet sacred text archive","url":"https://sacred-texts.com/chr/seil/index.htm","why":"elder mullan's 1914 translation of the spiritual exercises, free"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"he told it on himself, and the court records back him up. the autobiography opens by saying that up to his twenty sixth year he was given over to the vanities of the world and wanted a great name for himself, and the biographers fill in the rest: a fancy dresser, an expert dancer, a womanizer, quick to take offense, who fought duels. the part he left out is in the court papers. at carnival in 1515 he, his brother pero, who was a priest, and some relatives were charged with violence against the clergy of the parish at azpeitia. the accusation said the crime was done at night, on purpose, by treachery and in an ambush. a university history of him says the men they jumped belonged to a rival family, that he ran, and that when he was caught he claimed he could only be tried by a church court because he was a cleric, on the strength of the tonsure cut into his hair at about seven, which he had not otherwise honored in years. the case went nowhere. one account says it was dropped, probably with help from above. another says he was let go. nobody says he was innocent, and he did not put it in the autobiography.\nthe man who ran the jesuits for fifteen years was watched closely by friends and enemies, and the worst they left behind is the line in his own rulebook that a jesuit should obey as if he were a dead body.","moments":["on the road to montserrat in 1522 a moor, a muslim of spain, riding the same way, argued with him about the virgin mary. the new convert wanted to kill him. unable to decide, he dropped the reins at a fork and let the animal choose: if it followed the moor's road he would go after him. it kept to the highway. he told that story on himself thirty years later.","in the jail at salamanca he and a companion were chained together so that when one moved the other had to follow. when a young nobleman came to see him there he said that for the love of jesus christ he would gladly wear every handcuff and chain in salamanca.","on april 5, 1541, when the companions elected the first head of the order, every vote was for him. he asked for a second ballot. on april 13 every vote was for him again. he took six more days to say yes, and only after talking it over with a franciscan confessor.","friday, august 4, 1553: he finally told a fellow jesuit that he would tell him his life. he dictated it that september, walking, as far as the manresa years, then stopped for more than a year. the rest came in 1555, and the man writing it down finished the manuscript in genoa that december."],"name":"ignatius of loyola","place":"azpeitia in the basque country, then manresa, paris and rome","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 16 130 C 18 102 36 94 60 94 C 84 94 102 102 104 130 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 50 94 L 60 105 L 70 94 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 95 M 68 84 L 68 95'/><path d='M 33 48 C 36 36 46 30 60 30 C 74 30 84 36 87 48' stroke-width='2' opacity='.45'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 42 33 36 40 34 L 42 54 L 34 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 88 50 C 90 42 87 36 80 34 L 78 54 L 86 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 38 66 C 40 84 50 92 60 92 C 70 92 80 84 82 66 C 78 77 70 82 60 82 C 50 82 42 77 38 66 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 70 Q 60 66 68 70' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 44 49 Q 50 46 55 48 M 65 48 Q 70 46 76 49' stroke-width='2.2'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 58 L 57 66 L 62 66'/><path d='M 55 75 Q 60 77 65 75' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 74 106 L 102 102 L 104 124 L 76 128 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 76 110 L 78 132' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 82 110 L 97 108 M 83 116 L 98 114 M 84 122 L 95 120' stroke-width='1.4'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"up to his twenty-sixth year the heart of ignatius was enthralled by the vanities of the world.","where":"the opening line of his autobiography, in the 1900 english translation edited by j. f. x. o'conor. he was really about thirty."},{"q":"for the love of jesus christ, i gladly would wear all the handcuffs and chains that could be found in salamanca.","where":"his autobiography, chapter six in the 1900 translation, to a young nobleman who visited him in the jail at salamanca"},{"q":"let him who is in desolation labor to be in patience.","where":"the spiritual exercises, rules for the discernment of spirits for the first week, the eighth rule, in elder mullan's 1914 translation"}],"start":"his autobiography, in any modern translation. it is short, he is in the third person throughout, and the manresa chapter alone is worth it. then the spiritual exercises, which are a manual for a guide rather than a book to read in bed, so go straight to the rules for discernment of spirits near the end.","status":"approved","story":"he was born íñigo lópez de loyola in 1491, probably in october, in his family's house at loyola, just outside the town of azpeitia in gipuzkoa, the basque country in the north of spain, then part of the kingdom of castile. the loyolas were small nobility. he was the youngest of thirteen children. his mother died soon after he was born, and the blacksmith's wife took care of him. at about seven he was given the tonsure, the little shaved patch on the head that made a boy a cleric on paper, which mattered later. as a boy he was sent off to be a page in the household of the king's treasurer, and there he learned to dance, fence, gamble and duel. one of his biographers calls him a fancy dresser, an expert dancer, a womanizer, and touchy about insults. by 1509, at eighteen, he was in the service of the duke of nájera, a nobleman who a few years later was running navarre, the small kingdom on the french border, for the spanish crown.\nin may 1521 a french and navarrese army came for the fortress at pamplona, and the other gentlemen inside wanted to surrender. he talked the commander into holding out. on may 20, by the usual dating, a cannonball bounced off a wall and broke one of his legs and badly hurt the other. the castle gave in three or four days later. after twelve or fifteen days in pamplona he was carried home to loyola on a litter. there the doctors decided the bones had been set wrong and broke the leg again. it healed with a lump of bone sticking out and one leg shorter than the other, and because he could not stand how it looked he had the surgeons cut the lump away. there was no anesthetic. he limped for the rest of his life. he was about thirty. his autobiography says twenty six, which is one of the reasons people read it carefully.\nstuck in bed, he asked for romances of chivalry, the knights and ladies stories he liked, and there were none in the house. his brother's wife brought him what there was: a life of [[jesus]] by a monk named ludolph of saxony, and a book of saints' lives called in spanish the flowers of the saints, both in spanish. he read them, and he noticed something. when he daydreamed for hours about a great lady and the feats he would do for her, he was left dry and sad. when he daydreamed about walking to jerusalem and living hard, like [[francis|francis of assisi]] or the founder of the dominicans, the good feeling stayed. he later called that his first piece of reasoning about the things of god. one night he saw, he says, the virgin mary with the child, and after that, he said thirty years later, the old wanting never came back. by the time he could walk he had decided on jerusalem. in march 1522, on the way, he bought a prickly sackcloth robe, a staff and a drinking gourd, spent three days confessing his whole life to a priest at montserrat, a monastery on a mountain in the northeast of spain, gave his fine clothes to a beggar, and stood and knelt all night before the altar of the virgin mary, where he left his sword and dagger hanging. then he walked down to the town of manresa, meaning to stay a few days. he stayed eleven months.\nat manresa he begged his food, did chores at the hospital, let his hair and nails grow, ate no meat and drank no wine, and prayed, his own account says, seven hours a day on his knees, much of it in a cave on the hillside by the river cardoner. that is where the spiritual exercises started. it is also where he came apart. he became sure he had left something out of his confessions. he confessed again, and again, and a priest ordered him to confess nothing from his past that was not clear as day, and it did not help. several times, he says, he was violently tempted to throw himself out of the big window of his room. he went a whole week without food or drink until his confessor ordered him to eat. then it lifted, as if god had woken him from sleep, and he resolved never to speak of his past sins in confession again. after that came the visions. one day by the water about a mile out of town, he says, he understood so much in one go that everything he learned in the rest of his life put together did not equal it. god, he said, was treating him like a teacher treats a pupil. he said all of this, in the third person, to a fellow jesuit in 1553 and 1555, when he was in his sixties and expected to die soon. the spanish title that account goes by means the pilgrim's account, and it is where nearly everything we know about these years comes from.\nin september 1523 he got to jerusalem, meant to stay for good, and lasted three weeks before the franciscans in charge there sent him back to europe. so he decided he needed an education, and at thirty three sat in a free grammar school in barcelona learning latin. he moved on to the university at alcalá, where he and a few companions, who dressed alike, went around talking to people about god, and the inquisition, the church's court for hunting heresy, wanted to know on what authority. he spent more than forty days in jail there and was told to dress like an ordinary student and keep quiet on matters of faith for four more years. at salamanca the dominican friars had him locked up again, chained to a companion so that when one of them moved the other had to follow, for twenty two days. the verdict cleared his life and his teaching and told him, again, four more years of study first. so he went to paris and did the years, seven of them. on august 15, 1534, at montmartre in paris, he and six fellow students, among them francis xavier and peter faber, took vows of poverty and chastity and promised to go to jerusalem, or, if they could not, to put themselves at the pope's disposal. they could not. he was ordained a priest in venice on june 24, 1537, and in rome on september 27, 1540, pope paul iii approved the society of jesus, the jesuits. the following april every one of the companions voted for him as its head. he made them vote again. same result. he ran the order from rome for fifteen years, wrote the jesuit constitutions, its rulebook, and saw the spiritual exercises printed with the pope's approval in 1548. he died in rome on july 31, 1556, probably of malaria, with more than a thousand jesuits in more than a hundred houses. the church declared him a saint on march 12, 1622, the same day as francis xavier and [[teresaavila|teresa of avila]].","struggle":"the dark year is 1522. he walked into manresa in march a convert of ten months and spent much of the year sure that god could not have forgiven him, because he could not be sure he had confessed everything. that is what religious scruples are. the priest's order to stop raking over old sins did not reach him. the window did. he said later that the temptation to throw himself out of it came several times and with force, and that what he shouted back was that he would not do anything to offend god. the week without food or drink was his own idea, a kind of hunger strike against himself, and he only stopped because his confessor told him to. he was about thirty one.\nthe thing he could not beat later was the courts. between 1526 and 1528 he was jailed twice, questioned by the inquisition, and told twice, in effect, to shut up until he had a degree. his answer was to get one, which took seven more years in paris. the autobiography, when it reaches these parts, is nearly proud. what i say is either false or true, he told the judges at salamanca, and if false, condemn it.\nand the leg never stopped. he limped for the rest of his life. in 1553, when he finally agreed to tell his story, the jesuit who wrote it down noted that he was in very feeble health and hoping to last three or four months more to finish it. he lasted three years.","threads":["war","the body","doubt","prison","wandering"],"years":"1491 to 1556"},{"after":"the best what-happens-next story in the book isn't his own, it's what he did with peter's. peter, a fisherman and one of his closest friends, swore he'd die for him. then, the night jesus was arrested, people in a courtyard asked peter three times if he was with jesus, and three times he said he didn't know the man, before the rooster crowed at dawn. after the resurrection, on a beach, over a fire, jesus asked peter three times, do you love me. once for each denial. then he gave him the job. no lecture. that's the whole model.\nand the story about the woman caught in adultery, sleeping with a man who wasn't her husband, which under the old law could get her stoned to death. the religious men dragged her in front of him and asked what to do. he wrote in the dirt, said whoever's never screwed up can throw the first rock, and waited until the crowd left, oldest first. then he said, neither do i condemn you. go, and don't do it again. that's what he did with other people's messes, every time, and it's why the professionals wanted him dead.","basically":"a jewish carpenter from a nothing village in the north of what's now israel who, around the year 30, spent maybe three years walking around telling stories and healing people, eating with exactly the people everybody else avoided, until the roman occupiers executed him as a troublemaker. his friends said they saw him alive afterward, and about a third of the world now calls itself christian after him. strip away two thousand years of churches and he's the one who keeps sitting down with the person everybody else wrote off. if what you got in church felt like rules, go read what he actually said.","born":-4,"died":30,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 250 C 60 247 120 253 200 250 S 340 248 400 250'/><path d='M 30 200 L 176 200 M 44 200 L 44 250 M 162 200 L 162 250' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 68.8 192 C 70.4 204.8 89.6 204.8 91.2 192 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 114.8 192 C 116.4 204.8 135.6 204.8 137.2 192 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 148 200 C 149.1 189.0 156.8 188.0 161.2 191.0 C 167.8 191.0 170 197.0 170 200 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='70' cy='176' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='65' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='75' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 66 180 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 70 188 L 70 200'/><circle cx='104' cy='176' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='99' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='109' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 100 180 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 104 188 L 104 200'/><circle cx='138' cy='176' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='133' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='143' cy='174' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 134 180 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 138 188 L 138 200'/><circle cx='276' cy='168' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='276' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='286' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 277 172 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 276 182 L 262 215.44'/><path d='M 262 215.44 L 226 250 M 262 215.44 L 298 250'/><path d='M 272.5 190 L 238.5 192'/><path d='M 272.5 190 L 306.5 192'/><circle cx='372' cy='188' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='363' cy='186' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='373' cy='186' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 364 194 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 372 202 L 372 222.64'/><path d='M 372 222.64 L 362 250 M 372 222.64 L 382 250'/><path d='M 372.0 210 L 356.0 240'/><path d='M 372.0 210 L 388.0 240'/><g transform='translate(166,177.1) scale(0.85)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a carpenter from nowhere who ate with the wrong people, told stories with no moral, and got executed for it.","key":"jesus","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jesus","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"mark, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/mar.htm","why":"the shortest gospel, in the king james version"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["his first miracle, in john's gospel, was turning water into wine at a wedding in a village called cana because the party was running dry. his mother made him.","he told stories with no moral attached and walked off. a father throwing a party for the son who blew the inheritance, while the good son who stayed home stands outside furious. the story stops there. he never says whether the brother goes in.","he said the sabbath, the weekly day of rest, was made for people, not people for the sabbath, and healed on it to make the point."],"name":"jesus","place":"galilee, then jerusalem","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the kingdom of god is within you.","where":"luke 17:21, which means the gospel of luke, chapter 17, verse 21. the greek can mean within you or among you, depending on the translation; both are true"},{"q":"the sabbath was made for people, not people for the sabbath.","where":"mark 2:27; read chapter 2"},{"q":"don't worry about tomorrow. tomorrow has its own problems. today has enough.","where":"matthew 6:34, paraphrased; read all of the sermon on the mount, matthew chapters 5 through 7"}],"start":"mark, the shortest of the four gospels, in one sitting. then matthew 5 through 7, the sermon on the mount. then luke 15, the three lost things, a lost sheep, a lost coin and a lost son, and the party every time one gets found.","status":"approved","story":"he came from nazareth, a farming village in galilee, the hill country in the north, that nobody had a good word for. in one of the gospels, the four books that tell his life, a man hears where he's from and says, can anything good come out of nazareth? he worked with his hands until he was about thirty. then he walked into the jordan river, where his cousin john, a wild preacher living out in the desert, was dunking people under the water as a sign they were starting over, which is what baptizing means. the story says that when jesus came up, the sky opened and a voice called him son. he came out with a job.\nfor maybe three years he walked around galilee healing people and telling stories, mostly stories. a father who runs down the road to meet the son who took his inheritance early and blew all of it; people call that one the prodigal son. a samaritan, from a people the jews of his day despised, who stops for a man beaten half to death in a ditch after a priest and a temple worker walked right past him; that's the good samaritan. workers hired at five in the afternoon paid the same as the ones who started at dawn. the stories don't explain themselves. that's the method.\nhe ate with tax collectors, locals who collected money for the roman occupiers and skimmed off the top, the most hated guys in town, and with prostitutes. he told the rich to give it away. he said the kingdom of god was inside you. he said the sabbath, the weekly day of rest in jewish law when work was forbidden, was made for people and not the other way around, and he healed people on it. the religious professionals, the temple priests and the strictest teachers, hated him for it. the romans crucified him for it, nailing him to a wooden cross outside jerusalem to die slowly in public, which is what they did to rebels and runaway slaves. his friends said they saw him alive on the third day.\neverybody on these pages who came after has a version of him. [[rohr]]'s christ, bigger than the man, showing up in everything. [[bell]]'s jesus, who's funny. [[tolstoy]]'s teacher, from the gospel tolstoy rewrote himself with the miracles cut out. [[gandhi]]'s sermon on the mount, the three chapters in matthew where jesus says turn the other cheek and love your enemies. [[ramakrishna]]'s vision of him walking up the road. the man himself wrote nothing down.","struggle":"in a garden called gethsemane, the night before he died, he asked god to take the cup away, meaning what was coming for him. sweating, the text says, like drops of blood, with his friends asleep. he asked three times. he didn't get it.\non the cross he quoted psalm 22, an old hebrew prayer-song from the bible's book of psalms, that starts my god, my god, why have you left me. people spend a lot of energy explaining that one away. don't. the man said it.\nhis own family, in mark 3, came to take him home because they thought he was out of his mind. he wept at the grave of his friend lazarus. he was tired enough to sleep through a storm in a boat while the fishermen with him thought they were going to drown.","threads":["dying","love"],"years":"around 4 bc to around 30"},{"after":"after the whirlwind he says, i'd heard about you with my ears. now my eye sees you. and he sits down in the dust. that's not defeat. he asked for god and got god, and that turned out to be the answer, not the explanation he'd been demanding.\nand then the part everybody skips. god tells the friends to go ask job to pray for them, because they got it wrong and he got it right. and job does. he prays for the three men who spent thirty chapters telling him he deserved it. the book says his fortunes turned when he prayed for his friends. not before. read that twice.","basically":"the main character of a book in the bible, probably a story and not a history, about a good man who loses everything, his ten kids included, and then breaks out in sores from head to foot. his friends spend most of the book telling him he must have done something to deserve it. he refuses to buy it and demands that god explain himself, and god shows up and doesn't. if anyone's ever told you everything happens for a reason while you were at the bottom, job is on your side, and so, the book says, is god.","born":-550,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--olive)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='110' cy='180' r='14'/><path d='M 110 194 L 110 236'/><path d='M 110 236 C 90 236 80 252 96 252 L 124 252 C 140 252 130 236 110 236'/><path d='M 110 210 L 96 236 M 110 210 L 124 236'/><path d='M 96 236 L 84 230 L 90 224' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 60 250 C 80 240 100 246 120 250 S 150 240 170 250' opacity='.5'/><g opacity='.8'><circle cx='200' cy='150' r='10'/><path d='M 200 164 L 200 212'/><path d='M 200 212 L 186 246 M 200 212 L 214 246'/><path d='M 200 178 L 186 202 M 200 178 L 226 172'/><circle cx='240' cy='156' r='10'/><path d='M 240 170 L 240 218'/><path d='M 240 218 L 226 252 M 240 218 L 254 252'/><path d='M 240 184 L 226 208 M 240 184 L 266 178'/><circle cx='280' cy='152' r='10'/><path d='M 280 166 L 280 214'/><path d='M 280 214 L 266 248 M 280 214 L 294 248'/><path d='M 280 180 L 266 204 M 280 180 L 306 174'/><\/g><path d='M 205 130 C 200 118 214 112 214 122 M 245 136 C 240 124 254 118 254 128 M 285 132 C 280 120 294 114 294 124' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 340 250 C 320 220 360 200 340 170 C 325 145 355 130 345 100 C 340 85 352 70 350 55' /><path d='M 330 200 C 350 190 360 200 355 215 M 335 130 C 350 120 360 130 356 145' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(60,223.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he lost everything for no reason, his friends spent thirty chapters explaining why, and god showed up and said they were full of it.","key":"job","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Job_(biblical_figure)","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Job","why":"the book itself, and what scholars make of it"},{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/job.htm","why":"the book of job in the king james version"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["the friends sat with him on the ground for seven days and seven nights without saying a word, because they saw his suffering was very great. it's the best thing anybody does in the book. then they opened their mouths.","his wife gets one line: curse god and die. she'd lost ten children too. nobody writes her a chapter.","god's speech from the whirlwind spends a lot of time on the hippopotamus. behemoth, the text calls it. the answer to why do good people suffer includes an extended riff on how great hippos are. sit with that."],"name":"job","place":"the land of uz, wherever that was","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 L 30 100 L 45 106 L 60 94 L 75 106 L 90 100 L 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 26 62 L 18 40 L 32 46 L 30 28 L 42 40 L 48 24 L 56 38 L 64 22 L 72 38 L 80 26 L 86 42 L 100 30 L 92 48 L 104 52 L 90 62' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><g fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none' opacity='.5'><circle cx='34' cy='112' r='1.5'/><circle cx='42' cy='120' r='1.5'/><circle cx='84' cy='116' r='1.5'/><circle cx='90' cy='124' r='1.5'/><circle cx='50' cy='30' r='1.5'/><circle cx='72' cy='27' r='1.5'/><\/g><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"naked i came from my mother's womb and naked i'll go back. the lord gave and the lord took away.","where":"job 1:21, which means the book of job, chapter 1, verse 21; read the first two chapters"},{"q":"i wish i'd never been born.","where":"job 3, the whole chapter, paraphrased; it's the first thing he says after the silence"},{"q":"you're not the ones who spoke rightly about me. job is.","where":"god to the friends, job 42:7, paraphrased"}],"start":"job 1 and 2, then skip to 38 through 42, where god finally talks. then go back and read the friends if you want to hear every bad thing anyone's ever said to you at a funeral.","status":"approved","story":"job was rich and good and had ten kids. up in heaven god pointed him out to the accuser, the satan, which in hebrew just means the accuser, a kind of prosecutor in god's court, not yet the devil of later stories. the accuser said job was only good because he had it good. take it away and he'll curse you to your face. god let him try. in one day raiders took his animals, fire from the sky burned up his sheep, and a wind brought the house down on his kids and killed all ten. then the accuser covered him in sores. his wife told him to curse god and die. he sat down in the ashes and scraped his sores with a broken piece of pot.\nthree friends came and sat with him on the ground in silence for a week, which is the best thing anybody does in the book. then they opened their mouths. for thirty chapters they explain that he must have done something. suffering is punishment. god is fair. look harder. job says no. he never did anything. he wants god to show up and answer for it.\ngod shows up out of a whirlwind and doesn't answer. he asks job where he was when the earth was made. he shows him the ocean, the stars, the war horse that laughs at fear, the hippo, and leviathan, the sea monster nobody can hook. it's the same move as [[krishna]] showing arjuna what he really is in the gita, the hindu scripture. no explanation. just scale.\nthen god turns to the friends and says he's angry with them, because they didn't speak the truth about him the way his servant job did. the guy who yelled at god got it right. the guys who defended god got it wrong. read that twice.","struggle":"the struggle is the whole book. chapter 3 is a man cursing the day he was born, wishing the night he was conceived had been wiped off the calendar. it's the first thing he says after a week of silence and it's not polite.\nthen thirty chapters of his friends explaining that he must have deserved it, and him saying no, over and over, and demanding that god show up and answer for himself. he doesn't get comfort. he gets a whirlwind.","threads":["grief","anger","doubt"],"years":"the book is maybe 2500 years old"},{"after":"what he did with toledo was write it as a love poem. in the dark night a lover slips out of a sleeping house at night to meet the beloved, and the dark is what gets her out the door unseen. he spent years afterward writing commentaries saying the dark is where god works on you while you can't feel a thing.\nwhat he did with úbeda was die there, gently. the story goes that the prior who'd made it hard came around before the end and asked his pardon. john had never given him anything to forgive.","basically":"a tiny, dirt-poor spanish friar in the 1500s who helped [[teresaavila|teresa of avila]] drag their order back to a hard, simple life, and got kidnapped for it by his own brothers, locked in a closet-sized cell, and whipped. in the dark he made up love poems to god in his head, then escaped out a window on a rope of torn blankets. the phrase dark night of the soul comes from him, and he meant something more exact than a bad week: the long stretch where god goes silent and you keep going anyway. if you've ever felt nothing where you used to feel everything, he drew the map.","born":1542,"died":1591,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 272 C 80 262 140 282 200 272 S 320 262 380 272'/><path d='M 150 272 L 150 50 L 250 50 L 250 272' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 150 50 L 150 38 L 164 38 L 164 50 M 186 50 L 186 38 L 200 38 L 200 50 M 236 50 L 236 38 L 250 38 L 250 50'/><rect x='214' y='92' width='14' height='22' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 221 114 C 224 150 218 190 222 232'/><circle cx='222' cy='140' r='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='220' cy='170' r='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='221' cy='205' r='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='234' cy='176' r='8'/><path d='M 234 184 L 234 212 M 234 190 L 222 178 M 234 190 L 223 188 M 234 212 L 226 228 M 234 212 L 242 226'/><circle cx='330' cy='70' r='20' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='322' cy='64' r='17' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><g fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'><circle cx='60' cy='60' r='2'/><circle cx='100' cy='110' r='2'/><circle cx='300' cy='140' r='2'/><circle cx='360' cy='180' r='2'/><\/g><g transform='translate(208,28.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"his own order kidnapped him and locked him in a cell the size of a closet for nine months, and he came out with some of the best poems in spanish.","key":"johncross","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_of_the_Cross","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dark_Night_of_the_Soul","why":"the poem and the phrase"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["the story goes that when teresa first got him she told a friend she now had a friar and a half to start the men's reform. john was the half.","while he was confessor at the convent in avila, the priest the nuns told their sins to, he made a small ink sketch of christ on the cross seen from above, the way god would see it. in 1951 salvador dalí, the surrealist painter with the waxed mustache, painted christ of saint john of the cross from it.","he died just after midnight on december 14, 1591, as the bell rang for matins, the night prayers the friars sang, and is supposed to have said he'd be singing them in heaven."],"name":"john of the cross","place":"castile, a cell in toledo, andalusia","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 33 42 40 38 46 40 L 44 52 Z M 88 50 C 87 42 80 38 74 40 L 76 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 98 98 L 98 124 M 90 106 L 106 106' stroke-width='3'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"in the evening of life we will be judged on love.","where":"sayings of light and love, a little collection of his short sayings, paraphrased"},{"q":"where there is no love, put love, and you will draw love out.","where":"a letter to a carmelite nun, 1591, paraphrased"},{"q":"on a dark night, kindled in love with yearnings, oh happy chance, i went out unseen.","where":"the dark night, the poem, first stanza"}],"start":"the dark night, the poem first, which is eight short stanzas. then the spiritual canticle. the commentaries, his long prose explanations, are for later. kieran kavanaugh's translation.","status":"approved","story":"juan de yepes was born in 1542 in a small town in castile, the heart of old spain. his father died when he was small and the family was desperately poor. he grew up in medina del campo, worked in a hospital, studied with the jesuits, an order of scholarly priests, and at twenty one joined the carmelites, an order of friars and nuns that traced itself back to hermits on mount carmel in the holy land. by his day a lot of their houses had gone comfortable. he was tiny, under five feet by most accounts.\nin 1567, newly ordained and thinking about leaving for the carthusians, the strictest monks around, who live alone in cells in silence, he met [[teresaavila|teresa of avila]]. she was fifty two and trying to take the carmelite nuns back to the poor, hard, prayerful life they'd started with, and she wanted friars to do the same. she talked him out of leaving. in 1568 he and another friar opened the first house of the reform for men, in a farmhouse at duruelo, a hamlet in the middle of nowhere.\nthe reformers were called the discalced, which just means shoeless, because they went in rough sandals as a sign of being poor. the carmelites who hadn't signed on hated the reform. in december 1577 a group of them seized him in avila, took him to their friary in toledo, and locked him in a tiny cell with one slit of light high up. they brought him out to be whipped in front of the other friars. he was there about nine months, and he composed most of the spiritual canticle in his head, a long love poem between a woman and her lover, modeled on the song of songs, the bible's book of love poetry.\nin august 1578 he cut his blankets into strips, tied them together, and went out a window at night. he hid with the discalced nuns in toledo until it was safe. the dark night, the poem that gave us the phrase dark night of the soul, is one of his, and he wrote a long commentary on it later explaining it line by line. he died in 1591 at úbeda, pushed out of every post by his own reformed brothers.","struggle":"toledo was nine months in the dark, through winter into summer, in the same clothes, with lice, on bread and water and scraps of sardine. they whipped him in the dining hall while the friars ate. the friars talked outside his door about how the reform was finished.\nthe end was the same thing in slow motion. in 1591 the reform he'd helped build, now run by a hard man named nicolás doria, stripped him of his posts, and there was talk of sending him off to mexico. he got sick, went to úbeda for treatment, and the prior there, the man in charge of the house, resented him and made his last months hard. he died with his leg covered in sores.","threads":["prison","silence","love"],"years":"1542 to 1591"},{"after":"there isn't one. the book ends on god's question and jonah doesn't answer. no repentance scene, no lesson learned, no line about him walking back into the city. as far as we know he's still sitting outside it in the heat.\nthat's the point. the question's left open because it's aimed at whoever's reading. jewish tradition reads the whole book aloud on the afternoon of yom kippur, the day of atonement, the holiest day of the jewish year, when everyone fasts and asks to be forgiven. it's a good day to be asked whether you want your enemies forgiven too.","basically":"the prophet in a four chapter bible story who gets told to go warn the capital of the empire everyone hates, buys a ticket the other way, gets thrown overboard and swallowed by a huge fish, finally goes and preaches, and the whole city actually listens and god spares them, and he's furious about it. it's a comedy, and the joke is on him. picture being told to go help the country you hate most, and then sulking when it works. he's for anyone who, if they're honest, wants mercy for themselves and fire for everybody else.","born":null,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='70' cy='60' r='18' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 96 60 L 106 60'/><path d='M 44 60 L 34 60'/><path d='M 70 86 L 70 96'/><path d='M 70 34 L 70 24'/><path d='M 88 78 L 95 85'/><path d='M 52 78 L 45 85'/><path d='M 88 42 L 95 35'/><path d='M 52 42 L 45 35'/><path d='M 280 250 L 280 206 L 300 206 L 300 190 L 320 190 L 320 210 L 340 210 L 340 196 L 362 196 L 362 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='140' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 140 192 L 140 234'/><path d='M 140 234 C 120 234 110 250 126 250 L 154 250 C 170 250 160 234 140 234'/><path d='M 140 208 L 126 234 M 140 208 L 154 234'/><path d='M 134 189 Q 140 184 146 189'/><path d='M 190 250 C 200 200 180 140 140 128'/><path d='M 176 150 C 186 150 190 162 184 170 C 178 164 174 158 176 150 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 150 130 C 144 138 146 150 140 156 C 134 148 138 136 150 130 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 192 196 C 204 196 206 210 198 214 C 192 208 190 202 192 196 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 196 250 C 199 244 203 250 206 244 C 209 250 213 244 216 248'/><g transform='translate(343,174.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"god told him to go preach to the enemy, he ran the other way, and when it worked he was furious.","key":"jonah","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Book_of_Jonah","why":"the four chapter book itself, explained"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","why":"the king james bible on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"cowardice first. he ran, and nearly took a ship full of sailors down with him, and they behaved better than he did the whole time. then the sulk. a city of a hundred and twenty thousand people repents and lives, and he's angry enough to want to die about it, and then angrier about a plant. the book isn't hiding him. he's the joke, and the joke is on anybody who's ever wanted god to hit the people they hate.","moments":["the sailors in chapter 1 are pagans, men who worship other gods, and they come off better than the prophet. they pray, they try to save him, and at the end they're making vows to his god.","the hebrew says a great fish. the whale came later. matthew's gospel, written in greek, uses a word for a sea monster, and the king james bible, the english translation from 1611, made it a whale.","when people asked [[jesus]] for a sign he said they'd get none but the sign of jonah, three days in the heart of the earth, and that nineveh repented and they wouldn't. matthew 12.","the king of nineveh put the animals in sackcloth too. the cows repented. it's supposed to be a little funny."],"name":"jonah","place":"israel, a ship, a fish, nineveh","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 L 30 100 L 45 106 L 60 94 L 75 106 L 90 100 L 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 26 62 L 18 40 L 32 46 L 30 28 L 42 40 L 48 24 L 56 38 L 64 22 L 72 38 L 80 26 L 86 42 L 100 30 L 92 48 L 104 52 L 90 62' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 42 30 C 48 18 62 18 60 30 C 54 28 48 28 42 30 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 42 30 L 36 36'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i knew you were gracious and merciful, slow to anger, abounding in steadfast love.","where":"the book of jonah, 4:2, said as a complaint, and the reason he ran"},{"q":"it is better for me to die than to live.","where":"jonah 4:3, because a city was spared"},{"q":"i do well to be angry, angry enough to die.","where":"jonah 4:9, about the plant"}],"start":"the book of jonah, one of the short books near the end of the old testament, all four chapters, which takes about ten minutes. then chapter 4 again.","status":"approved","story":"jonah, son of amittai, a prophet, someone who carries a message from god. god told him to go to nineveh, the great city of assyria, the empire that was the terror of the region, in what's now northern iraq, and cry out against it. he went down to joppa, a port on the coast, and paid for a ship to tarshish, probably in spain, which was about as far the other way as a ship went.\na storm came up. the sailors prayed to their gods and threw the cargo over. jonah was asleep below deck. the sailors cast lots, a kind of drawing straws, to find out whose fault the storm was, and it fell on him. he told them to throw him in, and they tried to row for shore first rather than do it. then they did it, and the sea went quiet, and a great fish swallowed him. three days and three nights. he prayed from inside it and it threw him up on dry land.\nthe second time god said go, he went. he walked into nineveh and said one sentence, forty days and nineveh will be overthrown. the whole city repented, from the king down. the king sat in ashes and put on sackcloth, rough goat-hair cloth people wore to show they were sorry, and put even the cattle in sackcloth. god saw it and didn't destroy them.\nthat's where the book should end and doesn't. jonah went out past the city and sat down to watch, still hoping. god grew a plant to shade him and jonah loved the plant. god sent a worm to kill it and a hot east wind, and jonah said he'd rather die. then god asks, you cared about a plant you didn't even grow, and shouldn't i care about nineveh, with a hundred and twenty thousand people who don't know their right hand from their left, and all those animals. the book ends on the question. jonah never answers.","struggle":"the prayer from inside the fish, in chapter 2, is the bottom. the water closed over him, weeds wrapped around his head, he went down to the roots of the mountains. it's the most sincere he gets in the whole book, and it comes while he's being digested.\nand then the thing he can't beat, which is that god is kinder than he wants god to be. he says it straight out in chapter 4. he ran because he knew nineveh would be forgiven and he didn't want it forgiven. assyria would later wipe out the northern kingdom of israel, ten of the twelve tribes, and carry its people off, in 722 bc. he'd rather die than watch them get mercy.","threads":["anger","forgiveness"],"year":-760,"years":"set in the eighth century bc, written down later"},{"after":"she didn't found anything or go anywhere. she stayed in the room and thought about what she'd seen for twenty years, and the long version is what twenty years of thinking looks like. people came to the street window for advice, and she gave it.\nat the very end of the book she says she'd wanted to know what it all meant, and more than fifteen years after the showings she got an answer. love was his meaning. who showed it to you? love. what did he show you? love. why? for love. it took her fifteen years to get one word, and she thought that was about right.","basically":"an englishwoman in the 1300s who nearly died of an illness at thirty, saw sixteen visions of jesus on what she thought was her deathbed, and then spent the rest of her life shut in a single room on the side of a church, thinking about what she'd seen. she wrote the first book in english we know was written by a woman, and the line everybody knows from it is all shall be well. she lived through the black death, the plague that killed a third or more of europe, so it isn't a greeting card. think of the longest lockdown you can imagine, by choice, for decades, and what came out of it was hope.","born":1343,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--sage)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 70 L 80 70 L 80 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 70 L 44 62 L 50 70 L 56 62 L 62 70 L 68 62 L 74 70 L 80 62'/><path d='M 80 250 L 80 140 L 172 140 L 172 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 76 142 L 126 110 L 176 142'/><path d='M 172 250 L 172 150 L 270 172 L 270 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 168 148 L 278 174'/><rect x='194' y='184' width='44' height='32' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 201 208 C 197 184 227 184 223 208' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='212' cy='200' r='9'/><path d='M 220 210 L 228 204'/><circle cx='231' cy='202' r='3.5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 188 216 L 262 216'/><circle cx='340' cy='70' r='14' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 362 70 L 372 70'/><path d='M 318 70 L 308 70'/><path d='M 340 92 L 340 102'/><path d='M 340 48 L 340 38'/><path d='M 355 85 L 362 92'/><path d='M 325 85 L 318 92'/><path d='M 355 55 L 362 48'/><path d='M 325 55 L 318 48'/><g transform='translate(240,195.8) scale(0.75)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"she spent her life walled into one room beside a church, and came out with all shall be well.","key":"julian","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_of_Norwich","why":"the plain overview of her life and the cell"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/52958","why":"revelations of divine love on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["around 1413 margery kempe came to julian's window for advice. margery was a townswoman from king's lynn, a mother of fourteen, who went on pilgrimage as far as jerusalem and sobbed out loud in church so often that people wanted her gone. julian told her to trust what she'd been given and not be afraid of what people said. margery put it in her own book, which is often called the first autobiography in english.","the ancrene wisse, an old rulebook for anchoresses, allowed them one animal, a cat, for the mice. so every modern picture of julian has a cat. nobody knows if she had one.","the church of st julian was bombed in 1942 and rebuilt, with a room on the side where the cell was. people go and sit in it."],"name":"julian of norwich","place":"norwich, england, in one room","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 45 57 C 48 60 52 60 55 57 M 65 57 C 68 60 72 60 75 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 34 70 C 36 84 44 92 60 92 C 76 92 84 84 86 70 L 88 108 L 32 108 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 24 116 C 18 60 28 22 60 22 C 92 22 102 60 96 116 L 86 116 C 90 72 84 36 60 36 C 36 36 30 72 34 116 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"all shall be well, and all shall be well, and all manner of thing shall be well.","where":"revelations of divine love, her book, the long version, chapter 27"},{"q":"sin is behovely, but all shall be well.","where":"the long version, chapter 27. behovely means something like necessary, or fitting"},{"q":"as truly as god is our father, so truly is god our mother.","where":"the long version, chapter 59, paraphrased into modern english"}],"start":"revelations of divine love, the long version, chapters 5 and 27. start with the hazelnut. a modern translation, like elizabeth spearing's.","status":"approved","story":"we don't know her name. julian comes from the church of st julian in norwich, a city in the east of england, where she lived as an anchoress. an anchoress was a woman enclosed for life in a small room built onto the side of a church, to pray. hers had one window into the church, so she could see the altar, and one onto the street, where people could come and talk to her. she chose it. the service that put anchoresses in their cells borrowed from the service for the dead, because as far as the world was concerned she was gone.\nshe grew up through the black death, the plague that killed somewhere between a third and half of everyone in europe. it hit norwich when she was a small child and came back more than once. in may 1373, when she was thirty and a half, she got so sick that a priest came to be with her at the end and held a crucifix, a cross with jesus's body on it, in front of her face. over the next hours she had sixteen showings, what she called revelations of divine love, visions of jesus on the cross and things shown to her about god.\nin one of them she was shown something small, the size of a hazelnut, lying in the palm of her hand, and understood that it was everything that is made. she wondered how it could last, it was so small. the answer was that it lasts because god loves it.\nshe wrote it down twice. a short version soon after, and a long one twenty years or so later, after thinking about it the whole time. it's the first book we know of in english written by a woman. she was still alive in 1416, when someone left her money in a will. after that, nothing.","struggle":"she asked for it. as a young woman she prayed to see the crucifixion as if she'd been standing there, and for a sickness at thirty so bad she'd think she was dying. she got it, and it nearly killed her.\nand then, the same day, she doubted it. a religious man came by and she told him she'd been raving. he laughed. then she told him the crucifix had seemed to bleed, and he went quiet and serious, and she was ashamed that she'd called it raving to a man who believed her. that night she dreamed the devil had her by the throat. she wrote all of that down too, against herself.","threads":["dying","silence"],"years":"around 1343 to sometime after 1416"},{"after":"he kept weaving. that's the whole of it. there's no conversion and no crisis in the legend. the loom is in the songs because it was what he did with his hands while the songs came, and he never pretended the songs made him better than the loom.\nthe flowers under the sheet that covered his body are the after. both sides got half. he'd have laughed at that too.","basically":"a weaver in fifteenth century india, a guy who ran a loom all day in a holy city packed with priests, who made up songs telling the hindu priests and the muslim clerics they'd both lost the plot. the songs are short and rude, and village people still sing them five hundred years later. think of a guy on a factory line who writes the sharpest lyrics in town and roasts every church and mosque on the block, and then when he dies every one of them wants the body. if you've ever felt closer to god doing the dishes than in a pew, he's with you.","born":1440,"died":1518,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 60 250 L 60 150 L 180 150 L 180 250' /><path d='M 60 170 L 180 170 M 60 190 L 180 190 M 60 210 L 180 210 M 60 230 L 180 230' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 80 150 L 80 250 M 100 150 L 100 250 M 120 150 L 120 250 M 140 150 L 140 250 M 160 150 L 160 250' opacity='.35'/><circle cx='230' cy='160' r='14'/><path d='M 230 174 L 230 216'/><path d='M 230 216 C 210 216 200 232 216 232 L 244 232 C 260 232 250 216 230 216'/><path d='M 230 190 L 202 200 M 230 190 L 258 200'/><path d='M 225 165 Q 230 169 235 165'/><path d='M 180 230 C 240 250 280 220 320 240 S 380 250 400 240'/><path d='M 300 228 C 306 220 320 220 326 228 C 320 236 306 236 300 228 Z M 326 228 L 334 222 M 326 228 L 334 234' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='306' cy='227' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 310 210 C 312 204 316 204 318 210' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(72,123.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a muslim weaver who mocked the hindus and the muslims equally, and both of them claimed his body when he died.","key":"kabir","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kabir","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the songs, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6519","why":"songs of kabir, tagore's 1915 translation, public domain"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["the story of how he got a guru: ramananda, a famous hindu teacher in varanasi, wouldn't take a muslim boy as a student. so kabir lay down before dawn on the ghat, the stone steps down into the ganges where people bathe, and ramananda, coming down in the dark to bathe, stepped on him and cried out ram, ram, a name of god, the way you'd say oh god when you trip over something. kabir got up and said that's my mantra now, the holy word you repeat to pray, and you gave it to me. ramananda kept him.","there are more of his verses in the guru granth sahib, the sikh holy book, than from anyone else who wasn't a sikh guru. the sikhs decided the weaver was theirs too.","five hundred years later his songs are still sung in villages across north india by people who can't read them, the way you know songs by heart you've never once seen written down."],"name":"kabir","place":"varanasi, india","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 50 C 34 30 86 30 90 50 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 50 L 90 50'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 20 118 C 30 108 46 108 56 118 C 46 128 30 128 20 118 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i laugh when i hear that the fish in the water is thirsty.","where":"songs of kabir, the english version by rabindranath tagore, the indian poet who won the nobel prize in 1913, and robert bly's american reworking of it"},{"q":"if god is in the mosque, then who owns the rest of the world?","where":"his songs, paraphrased; the bijak, the book of his songs kept by his own followers, has the sharpest versions"},{"q":"the breath inside the breath. look there.","where":"a phrase that runs through his songs, in robert bly's rendering"}],"start":"the kabir book, robert bly's versions, which is short and loose. then the bijak, the collection his own followers kept, if you want the sharp edges. the hindi songs are still sung across north india and you can find recordings.","status":"approved","story":"he was a weaver in varanasi, the holiest city in hinduism, on the ganges river, where pilgrims come to bathe and the dying come to be burned on the stone steps by the water. he was raised muslim, in a family of weavers near the bottom of the caste system. caste is the old hindu order that ranks every family at birth and keeps them there for life, deciding who you can marry and who you can eat with. the story says he took a hindu guru anyway, a spiritual teacher, and he wrote songs in plain hindi, the everyday language, that peasants could sing.\nhe had no patience for priests of any kind. the brahmin, the hindu priest born into the top caste, wears a sacred thread over his shoulder to show it. kabir asked him, if you're so special, why weren't you born some other way than the rest of us? the mullah, the muslim cleric, climbs the tower of the mosque and shouts the call to prayer. kabir asked whether he thought god was deaf. god wasn't in the building, he said.\nthe songs say the thing you're looking for is already inside you, and every ritual you do to find it is a trip in the wrong direction. he laughs at the fish in the water who's thirsty, which is you, swimming in god and complaining you can't find a drink. you'll find god in the breath inside the breath, he says, not in the temple or the mosque.\nhe's in the guru granth sahib, the holy book of the sikhs, a religion founded around 1500 in punjab, in northern india, by a man named guru nanak. he's in there more than any other writer who wasn't one of the sikh gurus. hindus call him a saint. muslims call him a sufi, one of their mystics. he called himself a weaver.\nthe story goes that when he died the hindus wanted to burn the body, which is how hindus do it, and the muslims wanted to bury it, which is how muslims do it. while they argued, somebody lifted the sheet and found only flowers. they split them. the hindus burned their half and the muslims buried theirs. that's the whole point of him in one story.\nthe dates and half the stories are legend, and people kept writing new songs and putting his name on them for centuries, the way every clever quote online gets pinned on einstein. pretend they're all his; the point holds.","struggle":"he was a low caste weaver, near the bottom of the ladder, in a city run by brahmins, the hindu priests, and mullahs, the muslim clerics, and he told both of them their god wasn't in the building. the stories have him hauled in front of the sultan, the muslim king in delhi, and mocked in the street. he wrote about it with the shrug of a man who'd already decided the argument was beneath him.\nthe songs have loneliness in them too. in some he's a bride sitting up all night for a husband who doesn't come, and the husband is god. in others the soul is a swan, and he keeps asking it where it came from and where it's going to fly. he wasn't only laughing.","threads":["work"],"years":"around 1440 to 1518, nobody's sure"},{"after":"there wasn't a good after. he never got sober. he never made it right with jan. he took care of his mother after her stroke, and married stella sampas, the sister of his boyhood friend, partly so there'd be someone to look after her. that's the whole of the last chapter, and it's not a redemption.\nwhat he left behind is the part of him that was real. the sutras he wrote in notebooks, the life of the buddha, the dharma bums. near the end he told interviewers he was a catholic and always had been. both were true. the tragedy is he couldn't live in either one.","basically":"a working class kid from a french canadian family in a massachusetts mill town who wrote on the road, the book that made a whole generation want to quit everything and drive west. then he couldn't handle being famous and drank himself to death at forty seven. in between he fell hard for buddhism, and never stopped being catholic. he more or less wrote the script for every kid who's backpacked through asia to find themselves, and he couldn't follow it. read him for the hunger, and read what the hunger did to him.","born":1922,"died":1969,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='70' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 70 192 L 70 234'/><path d='M 70 234 C 50 234 40 250 56 250 L 84 250 C 100 250 90 234 70 234'/><path d='M 70 208 L 56 234 M 70 208 L 84 234'/><rect x='112' y='196' width='30' height='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 96 222 L 150 222 L 156 250 L 90 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'><circle cx='104' cy='238' r='2'/><circle cx='114' cy='238' r='2'/><circle cx='124' cy='238' r='2'/><circle cx='134' cy='238' r='2'/><circle cx='144' cy='238' r='2'/><\/g><path d='M 156 244 L 186 244 C 196 244 196 232 188 232 C 180 232 182 244 194 244 L 262 244 L 262 250 L 156 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 250 L 320 130 L 390 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='308' y='112' width='24' height='18' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 302 114 L 320 100 L 338 114'/><path d='M 40 90 C 50 86 60 94 70 90' opacity='.4'/><g transform='translate(320,83.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he typed the book that sent a generation onto the highway, then drank himself to death in his mother's living room at forty seven.","key":"kerouac","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jack_Kerouac","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/jack-kerouac","why":"his life, and the poems people forget he wrote"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Dharma_Bums","why":"the buddhist novel"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"in 1952 his second wife, joan haverty, had a daughter, jan, and he said for years she wasn't his. a court finally made him pay a little support in the early sixties, and he met her twice in his life. she grew up and became a writer and died young, in 1996.\nthe drink killed him. and at the end he'd turned into a bitter, angry man who railed against hippies, the left, and the kids who'd read him, said ugly things about jews, and showed up drunk in 1968 on firing line, the tv show hosted by william buckley, the best known conservative in america. the man who wrote about the holy road ended up shouting at it from the couch.","moments":["the scroll of on the road is about a hundred and twenty feet long. the story goes that a dog ate the last few feet. the scroll itself says so in his handwriting at the end.","in october 1955 he was in the room at the six gallery, a little art space in san francisco, when ginsberg first read howl, the long furious poem that later got its publisher hauled into court for obscenity. kerouac passed around a jug of wine and yelled go.","in the summer of 1956, on snyder's advice, he spent two months alone as a fire lookout on desolation peak in washington. he'd expected to meet god up there. he mostly met himself and got bored."],"name":"jack kerouac","place":"lowell, massachusetts, then new york, the road, and his mother's house","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 96 C 52 104 68 104 72 96' /><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the only people for me are the mad ones.","where":"on the road, part one, chapter one; the sentence goes on a good while after that"},{"q":"beat never meant beaten down. it meant beatific.","where":"his essay the origins of the beat generation, 1959, and interviews, paraphrased. beatific means blissed out, blessed"},{"q":"i see a vision of a great rucksack revolution, thousands of young americans wandering around with packs.","where":"the dharma bums, the speech on the mountain by japhy ryder, the character based on gary snyder, paraphrased"}],"start":"the dharma bums. then some of the dharma, his buddhist notebooks, or wake up, if you want his buddhism raw. then big sur, when you want the truth.","status":"approved","story":"he was born jean-louis kerouac in lowell, massachusetts, in 1922, to french canadian parents, and didn't speak much english until he was about six. his big brother gerard died at nine of rheumatic fever, a heart illness that follows a bad strep infection, when jack was four, and the nuns who sat with the boy said he was a saint. jack believed it his whole life.\nhe went to columbia university in new york on a football scholarship, broke his leg, and drifted out. in new york he fell in with allen ginsberg, a young poet who'd later write howl, and william burroughs, a harvard man and heroin addict who'd later write naked lunch. that circle got called the beat generation, and later just the beats. in the late forties he went back and forth across the country with neal cassady, a fast talking ex car thief from denver. in april 1951 he taped sheets of paper into one long roll so he wouldn't have to stop, and typed on the road, the story of those trips, in about three weeks. it took six years to get it published. when it came out in 1957 he was famous overnight and hated it.\nin 1954 he found a fat collection of buddhist scriptures in english and went at it hard, reading and copying out sutras, the talks the buddha is supposed to have given, and writing his own. the next year, in berkeley, he met the poet gary snyder, a real student of zen, the japanese buddhism of sitting meditation, and the dharma bums, from 1958, is about snyder and climbing a mountain with him. dharma is the buddhist word for the teaching, so the title means something like holy bums. he wrote a life of the buddha called wake up that nobody published until 2008.\nunderneath all of it he was catholic. he'd been raised in it and never really left. by the sixties he was living with his mother, drinking all day, and on october 21, 1969, in st. petersburg, florida, he died bleeding inside from a liver that had given out.","struggle":"fame broke him. he'd spent years writing in poverty and when on the road hit, reporters wanted him to be dean moriarty, the wild young man in the book who was really neal cassady, and he was a shy mama's boy of thirty five who wanted to be left alone to write. he drank to get through the interviews and then he drank all the time.\nbig sur, from 1962, is the book about it. in 1960 he went to a cabin on the california coast that his friend lawrence ferlinghetti, the poet who ran city lights books in san francisco, lent him, to dry out and find the peace he'd written about, and instead cracked up, in detail, on the page. it's the least romantic book about being a famous writer that exists.","threads":["addiction","wandering","fame"],"years":"1922 to 1969"},{"after":"he never fixed it with regine, and he never really tried to take it back. what he did was keep her. in his will he left her everything he had, and wrote that for him an engagement had been as binding as a marriage. she and her husband turned the money down.\nin 1855 her husband was made governor of the danish west indies, the danish islands in the caribbean that are now the u.s. virgin islands, and they were leaving. on the street, the day they sailed, she passed him and said quietly, god bless you, may it go well for you. he stepped back and raised his hat. that was the last time they saw each other. he died that november. she lived until 1904.","basically":"a skinny, brilliant, gloomy danish writer in the 1840s who broke off his engagement to the girl he loved and spent the rest of his short life writing books about faith and dread, most of them under fake names that argue with each other. he's the one who gave us the leap, the idea that faith is a jump you make without proof. picture a guy who dumps the love of his life on purpose, playing the jerk so she'll be the one to walk, and then writes about her in book after book for years. he's for anyone who's ever been frozen in front of a choice.","born":1813,"died":1855,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 300 L 20 230 L 150 230 L 164 300 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 300 L 262 210 L 380 210 L 380 300 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='100' cy='225' r='5'/><path d='M 100 212 L 100 206 M 92 216 L 88 212 M 108 216 L 112 212' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 150 228 C 168 186 180 166 194 154' stroke-dasharray='2 8' opacity='.6'/><circle cx='206' cy='110' r='14'/><path d='M 206 124 L 206 170'/><path d='M 206 170 L 190 206 M 206 170 L 224 202'/><path d='M 206 138 L 192 160 M 206 138 L 226 146'/><path d='M 226 146 L 236 92'/><path d='M 212 92 Q 236 64 260 92 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><g transform='translate(320,183.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he got engaged to the girl he loved, broke it off on purpose a year later, and wrote about her for the rest of his life.","key":"kierkegaard","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%B8ren_Kierkegaard","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the stanford encyclopedia","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/kierkegaard/","why":"a longer, serious guide to his ideas"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"what he did to regine. he asked a girl of eighteen to marry him, took a year, then played a cold, heartless man so she'd be the one to leave, and when she wouldn't, he left anyway. she was in pieces. he told himself he was sparing her, and maybe he was. then he spent years publishing books that circle the engagement, repetition and the part of stages on life's way called guilty? not guilty?, where anyone in copenhagen who knew could read it.\nhe also picked the corsair fight himself, and in his last year he went after bishop mynster, the head of the danish church, his father's pastor and his own, right after the man died.","moments":["in the hospital he refused communion, the bread and wine of the church service, from a priest, because he said priests were officials of the crown. he'd take it from a layman or not at all. he didn't get it.","at his funeral his nephew henrik lund stood up at the grave and protested that the church was burying a man who'd attacked it.","he walked the streets of copenhagen for hours most days, talking to anyone at all, and called it his people bath."],"name":"søren kierkegaard","place":"copenhagen, with a few trips to berlin","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 38 44 C 38 20 70 12 84 34 C 74 28 54 30 38 44 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"life can only be understood backwards, but it must be lived forwards.","where":"his journals, 1843"},{"q":"anxiety is the dizziness of freedom.","where":"the concept of anxiety, his book on dread, paraphrased"},{"q":"purity of heart is to will one thing.","where":"the title of an upbuilding discourse from 1847, one of the religious talks he published under his own name"}],"start":"fear and trembling, the opening sections on abraham. then a good selection of the journals. then works of love if you want to see him warm.","status":"approved","story":"he was the youngest of seven children of a rich copenhagen wool merchant, a gloomy, devout old man who, the story goes, had cursed god on a hillside in jutland, the danish mainland, as a poor shepherd boy. by the time søren was twenty one his mother and five of his brothers and sisters were dead, and his father seems to have taken it as punishment.\nin 1840 he got engaged to regine olsen, who was eighteen and in love with him. within a year he'd decided he couldn't go through with it, and he broke it off in a way designed to make her hate him, so she'd be free. she begged. her father begged. he went to berlin.\nthen he wrote, fast, mostly under pseudonyms, made-up authors who argue with each other. either/or, a huge book that sets a pleasure seeker's life next to a married judge's, and fear and trembling, both in 1843, then the concept of anxiety, then the sickness unto death, which is his name for despair. fear and trembling is about abraham. in genesis, the first book of the bible, god tells abraham to take his son isaac up a mountain and sacrifice him, and abraham goes, three days' walk, knife and all, until an angel stops his hand at the last second. kierkegaard admits he can't understand abraham and can't stop looking at him. the leap comes out of those books. everyone remembers it as the leap of faith, though he mostly just called it the leap.\nat the end of 1845 he picked a fight with the corsair, copenhagen's satire and gossip paper, and it made him a laughingstock for years, cartoons of his thin legs and uneven trousers. in his last year he went after the danish state church itself, the lutheran church the government ran and paid for, in pamphlets he paid for, saying the christianity of the new testament didn't exist in denmark. in october 1855 he collapsed in the street, and he died in hospital that november, at forty two.","struggle":"he called it his melancholy, and it's all over the journals from his twenties on. he believed he carried something from his father that made him unfit to marry, and he never said plainly what it was. scholars still guess.\nregine married johan frederik schlegel, a civil servant, in 1847. kierkegaard kept writing about her for the rest of his life. in 1849 he sent schlegel a letter asking to be allowed to write to her, and schlegel sent it back unopened. and the corsair got under his skin. he'd walked all over copenhagen every day talking to anyone, and after the cartoons kids in the street shouted either/or at him.","threads":["love","doubt","loneliness"],"years":"1813 to 1855"},{"after":"after the adviser told her beauty wasn't science, she did the science, all of it, properly, and then came back to the asters and goldenrod decades later and explained, as a scientist, why they look good together. purple and yellow are opposites on the color wheel, and side by side they pull in more bees than either would alone. so the beauty was doing something all along.\nafter the language was lost, she went and got some of it back. she doesn't pretend she's fluent. she tells what she found.","basically":"a potawatomi scientist who studies moss, the tiny plants almost nobody looks at, and wrote braiding sweetgrass in 2013 for a small press. word of mouth made it one of the best selling nonfiction books in america, millions of copies. it says the land is a gift, and gifts come with obligations, and it says it like a science teacher who also knows the old stories. think of the professor who takes you out to the pond and makes you feel like the whole planet is your family, and doesn't let the science or the stories win the argument alone. take from her that paying attention is a way of saying thank you.","born":1953,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><ellipse cx='300' cy='256' rx='80' ry='16' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='150' cy='170' r='14'/><path d='M 150 184 L 150 222 L 130 250 M 150 222 L 176 238 L 176 250 M 150 196 L 176 214 M 150 196 L 170 218'/><path d='M 170 216 C 186 222 196 232 212 236' stroke-dasharray='3 3' stroke-width='5' stroke='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 40 250 C 40 226 90 222 96 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 236 C 58 226 80 226 92 238' stroke='var(--sage)' stroke-width='5'/><path d='M 230 250 L 232 200 M 244 250 L 248 206 M 258 250 L 254 196'/><g fill='var(--lavender)'><circle cx='232' cy='198' r='6'/><circle cx='254' cy='194' r='6'/><\/g><g fill='var(--marigold)'><circle cx='248' cy='204' r='5'/><circle cx='244' cy='196' r='4'/><\/g><g transform='translate(70,211.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a botanist who got told in her first college interview that beauty wasn't science, and spent forty years proving it was.","key":"kimmerer","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robin_Wall_Kimmerer","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"the publisher","url":"https://milkweed.org/book/braiding-sweetgrass","why":"milkweed editions' page for braiding sweetgrass"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Braiding_Sweetgrass","why":"what's in the book"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["in potawatomi, she writes, there's a word, puhpowee, for the force that pushes mushrooms up out of the ground overnight. english doesn't have one.","she writes that in potawatomi a bay isn't a thing, it's a verb, wiikwegamaa, to be a bay. the water is doing something, it's alive. english makes it a noun, a dead thing.","her newer book, the serviceberry, in 2024, is short and about gift economies, how a berry bush shares, and what it would take for people to."],"name":"robin wall kimmerer","place":"upstate new york, then wisconsin, then syracuse","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 16 128 C 24 114 36 104 46 100' stroke-dasharray='3 3' stroke-width='5' stroke='var(--sage)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"all flourishing is mutual.","where":"braiding sweetgrass, 2013"},{"q":"take only what you need, never the first one or the last, and give something back.","where":"braiding sweetgrass, the chapter on the honorable harvest, paraphrased"},{"q":"the land knows you, even when you are lost.","where":"braiding sweetgrass"}],"start":"braiding sweetgrass, the chapters skywoman falling and learning the grammar of animacy.","status":"approved","story":"she was born on september 13, 1953, in upstate new york. she's a citizen of the potawatomi nation, a great lakes people who were forced west in the 1830s, many of them on a march in 1838 called the trail of death, and ended up in kansas and oklahoma. her grandfather was sent to carlisle, the government boarding school built to erase native kids' languages and ways. she grew up without the potawatomi language, and as an adult she set out to learn it.\nthe story she tells in braiding sweetgrass is that when she started college, the adviser asked why she wanted to study botany, and she said she wanted to know why asters and goldenrod, a purple flower and a yellow one, look so beautiful together. he told her that wasn't science. she studied botany anyway, at the state university of new york college of environmental science and forestry in syracuse, then got a master's and a doctorate at the university of wisconsin, by 1983.\nshe went back to that college in syracuse to teach, and directed its center for native peoples and the environment. her first book, gathering moss, in 2003, is about mosses, and won the john burroughs medal, the big prize for nature writing.\nbraiding sweetgrass came out in 2013 from milkweed editions, a small nonprofit press. it's built around sweetgrass, a plant sacred to many native nations, and it braids three strands together, the science, the native stories, and her own life as a mother and teacher. it opens with skywoman, a creation story of a woman falling from the sky onto a turtle's back, which is why so many native people call this continent turtle island, and goes through pecans, strawberries, and the honorable harvest, the old rules for taking from the land. it sold over two million copies in ten years. she got a macarthur fellowship, the so-called genius grant, in 2022.","struggle":"the language. her grandfather lost it at carlisle, and the family grew up without it. she started learning potawatomi as a grown woman, one of a handful of students with a few elderly speakers, and she's written honestly about how slow and humbling it was.\nand science itself, the version that told her at eighteen that what she loved about plants didn't count. she spent a career inside that world, publishing, teaching, getting tenure, while carrying the other way of knowing that it had told her to leave at the door.","threads":["the land","joy","family"],"years":"born 1953"},{"after":"he never got a next chapter to make right what he'd done wrong. what he did instead, the night before he died, was stand up in memphis and say he'd been to the mountaintop. that's moses in the bible, who led his people forty years through the desert and got to see the promised land from a mountain but died before he could go in. king said he might not get there with them and wasn't worried about anything. thirty nine years old, with the fbi's tapes in a drawer somewhere, and he said it like a man who'd already set the weight down.\nthe after was left to everyone else. that's the honest version.","basically":"the baptist preacher from atlanta who led the civil rights movement, the fight in the fifties and sixties to end the laws that kept black americans segregated and kept them from voting. you know him from the i have a dream speech in washington in 1963, but he was twenty six when it started, and in the thirteen years he had he got stabbed, jailed, wiretapped by his own government, dropped by his allies, and shot dead at thirty nine. take from him that you can refuse to hate the people hurting you and still refuse to let them do it.","born":1929,"died":1968,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 20 250 L 20 140 L 130 140 L 130 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 55 165 L 95 165 L 95 200 L 55 200 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 65 165 L 65 200 M 75 165 L 75 200 M 85 165 L 85 200'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><path d='M 110 150 L 128 146 L 130 160 L 112 164 Z'/><path d='M 150 120 L 170 114 L 173 130 L 153 136 Z'/><path d='M 200 100 L 222 96 L 224 112 L 202 116 Z'/><\/g><circle cx='280' cy='140' r='14'/><path d='M 280 154 L 280 200'/><path d='M 280 200 L 264 236 M 280 200 L 298 232'/><path d='M 280 168 L 266 190 M 280 168 L 300 176'/><path d='M 275 145 Q 280 149 285 145'/><g opacity='.6'><circle cx='330' cy='156' r='10'/><path d='M 330 170 L 330 216'/><path d='M 330 216 L 314 252 M 330 216 L 348 248'/><path d='M 330 184 L 316 206 M 330 184 L 350 192'/><circle cx='365' cy='166' r='9'/><path d='M 365 180 L 365 226'/><path d='M 365 226 L 349 262 M 365 226 L 383 258'/><path d='M 365 194 L 351 216 M 365 194 L 385 202'/><\/g><g transform='translate(105,113.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a baptist preacher who got his method from a hindu who got it from a russian novelist who got it from a jewish carpenter.","key":"king","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Martin_Luther_King_Jr.","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the king institute","url":"https://kinginstitute.stanford.edu/","why":"stanford's archive of his letters, sermons, and speeches"},{"label":"the nobel site","url":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/1964/king/facts/","why":"his 1964 peace prize"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"the fbi, under j. edgar hoover, wiretapped him for years and recorded extramarital affairs, and he had them. boston university, where he got his doctorate, found in 1991 that parts of his dissertation, the long research paper you write for a phd, were copied from other people's work without credit. both true, and both his own to carry.","moments":["in 1958 a woman stabbed him in the chest with a letter opener at a book signing in harlem. the blade sat against his aorta, the main artery out of the heart. the surgeons told him if he'd sneezed he'd have died. he put that in his last speech.","the letter from birmingham jail was written on the margins of a newspaper and scraps his lawyers smuggled out. it's addressed to eight white clergymen who'd asked him to be patient.","in 1959 he went to india and slept in a room where gandhi had stayed. he said afterward he was more convinced than ever that nonviolent resistance was the most potent weapon available to oppressed people."],"name":"martin luther king jr","place":"atlanta, montgomery, memphis","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 46 72 C 52 66 58 70 60 72 C 62 70 68 66 74 72 C 68 76 62 74 60 74 C 58 74 52 76 46 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i come to india as a pilgrim, not a tourist.","where":"his 1959 trip, said on arrival"},{"q":"injustice anywhere is a threat to justice everywhere.","where":"letter from birmingham jail, 1963"},{"q":"i have decided to stick with love. hate is too great a burden to bear.","where":"where do we go from here, a 1967 speech to the civil rights group he led, paraphrased"}],"start":"letter from birmingham jail, which takes twenty minutes. then the riverside speech, beyond vietnam. then strength to love, a book of his sermons.","status":"approved","story":"he was a twenty six year old pastor in montgomery, alabama in 1955 when rosa parks, a black seamstress who'd been organizing for years, refused to give up her bus seat to a white man. the boycott that followed, black montgomery walking to work for a year rather than ride segregated buses, needed a face. he didn't ask for it. the night his house got bombed, with his wife and baby daughter inside, he stood on the porch and told the angry crowd outside, some of them armed, to put their guns away and go home.\nthe method was [[gandhi]]'s, and he said so. in 1959 he went to india and said he was going as a pilgrim, not a tourist. he walked where gandhi walked. what he brought back was the certainty that you could break a system without becoming it.\nin 1966 a vietnamese buddhist monk named [[thay|thich nhat hanh]] sat with him in chicago and asked him to speak against the vietnam war. in 1967 king nominated him for the nobel peace prize, and on april 4 at riverside church in new york came out against the war in a speech called beyond vietnam. it cost him most of his allies in washington, the president included. exactly one year later he was shot on a motel balcony in memphis, where he'd gone to stand with striking garbage workers. he was thirty nine.\nthe letter from birmingham jail, written on scraps in a cell in 1963 after he was arrested for marching without a permit, is the best thing he wrote. it's addressed to the moderate white clergy, eight local religious leaders, a rabbi among them, who'd put out a statement in the paper telling him to wait. it doesn't ask them for anything.","struggle":"january 27, 1956, midnight, montgomery. a phone call telling him to get out of town or die. he sat at his kitchen table over a cup of coffee and, in his own words, told god he was at the end of his powers, he had nothing left, he couldn't face it alone. he says he heard an inner voice tell him to stand up for righteousness and he'd never be alone. three days later his house was bombed.\nby 1967 he was exhausted in a way that scared the people around him. the movement had split, the white liberals had left over vietnam, the young black power activists thought he was finished. he checked into hospitals for exhaustion more than once and kept going anyway.","threads":["nonviolence","prison","dying"],"years":"1929 to 1968"},{"after":"he forgave the hunter. that's the last thing he does in the story. the man is horrified and krishna tells him it's fine, this was owed, go in peace. then he leaves the body.\nthe gita's own answer to what you do after is chapter 18, the last one. he lays it all out and then says, i've told you everything, reflect on it fully, then do what you want. the god gives the man the choice back. arjuna picks up the bow.","basically":"one of the most loved gods in hinduism, the blue-skinned one with the flute, and the voice of the bhagavad gita, a seven hundred verse poem where he talks a terrified soldier through the morning of a battle. picture a guy frozen in the passenger seat, and the driver turns around, and the driver is god, and instead of telling him it'll be fine he says get up, do your job, and let go of how it turns out. gandhi read it every day. if you're stuck in front of something you have to do, that's the whole book.","born":-200,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 250 C 60 247 120 253 200 250 S 340 248 400 250'/><ellipse cx='63.0' cy='219.4' rx='30.6' ry='14.4' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 88.2 210.4 L 106.2 185.2'/><ellipse cx='111.6' cy='181.6' rx='11.700000000000001' ry='7.2' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='117.0' cy='179.8' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 104.4 176.2 l -1.8 -8.1 M 111.6 175.3 l 0.9 -8.1'/><path d='M 43.199999999999996 228.4 L 43.199999999999996 250'/><path d='M 54.0 228.4 L 54.0 250'/><path d='M 77.4 228.4 L 77.4 250'/><path d='M 88.2 228.4 L 88.2 250'/><path d='M 32.4 215.8 q -12.6 9.0 -9.0 27.0'/><path d='M 196 234 L 196 194 L 296 194 L 296 234 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='214' cy='236' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='278' cy='236' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 196 220 L 150 214'/><g opacity='.35'><circle cx='198' cy='167' r='12' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='1.4'/><circle cx='190' cy='164' r='12' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='1.4'/><\/g><circle cx='206' cy='170' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='204' cy='168' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='214' cy='168' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 205 174 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 206 184 L 206 205.92000000000002'/><path d='M 206 205.92000000000002 L 194 234 M 206 205.92000000000002 L 218 234'/><path d='M 206.0 192 L 232.0 184'/><path d='M 206.0 192 L 240.0 190'/><circle cx='262' cy='200' r='13' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='255' cy='198' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='265' cy='198' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 256 207 q 4 -4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 262 213 C 258 226 246 226 244 240' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 250 214 q 12 -8 24 0' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 232 188 C 180 196 150 196 118 176' stroke-width='2' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 238 186 C 190 190 160 188 120 170' stroke-width='2' opacity='.7'/><g transform='translate(286,169.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a god disguised as a chariot driver, who spends eighteen chapters telling a soldier to get up.","key":"krishna","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krishna","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"the gita, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/hin/gita/index.htm","why":"edwin arnold's old english verse version, public domain"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"he's a god in a story. the stories about him outside the gita have him as a kid stealing butter from every kitchen in the village, and stealing the clothes of the village milkmaids while they bathed in the river and climbing a tree with them. the tradition reads all of it as lila, divine play, god enjoying himself in the world. the gita itself is a god telling a man to go fight his own family, and people have used it for war and for nonviolence both.","moments":["the childhood stories have him stealing butter from every kitchen in the village and getting caught with it all over his face, a mother scolding a god. the tradition calls it lila, divine play. every indian grandmother has a painting of it.","in chapter 11 arjuna asks to see him as he really is, and gets every face, every mouth, all of time, everything being born and eaten at once, and begs him to please put the friendly face back on.","oppenheimer, who ran the atomic bomb project, remembered a line from chapter 11 at the first test in the new mexico desert. thoreau read the gita in his cabin by walden pond. the book gets around."],"name":"krishna","place":"the battlefield of kurukshetra","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 34 86 34 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 60 36 C 62 26 66 16 74 10' /><ellipse cx='76' cy='9' rx='5' ry='7' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='76' cy='9' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 40 40 L 60 32 L 80 40' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 20 118 L 78 92' stroke-width='5'/><path d='M 30 113 L 30 113 M 40 109 L 40 109 M 50 104 L 50 104' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"you have a right to your work, never to its fruit.","where":"bhagavad gita 2:47, which means chapter 2, verse 47; read all of chapter 2"},{"q":"however people come to me, that's how i meet them. every road is mine.","where":"bhagavad gita 4:11, paraphrased"},{"q":"i've told you everything. now think it over, and do as you choose.","where":"bhagavad gita 18:63, paraphrased; the last thing he says before the end"}],"start":"the bhagavad gita, chapter 2, then 11, then 18. eknath easwaran's translation is warm; he was an indian english professor who ended up teaching meditation in california. barbara stoler miller's is clean; she was an american scholar of sanskrit, the old language it's written in. ram dass's paths to god is the gita as he taught it.","status":"approved","story":"the bhagavad gita, which means the song of god, is one conversation from the middle of the mahabharata, india's giant old epic, one of the longest poems ever written. it's set on a battlefield called kurukshetra just before a war between two halves of the same royal family, cousins fighting over a kingdom. the greatest archer alive, a prince named arjuna, looks across at the other army, sees his cousins and his old teachers and the great-uncle he loved, drops his bow, sits down in his chariot, and says he won't fight.\nhis chariot driver is krishna. in that world a chariot was a two-wheeled war cart, and the driver handled the horses while the warrior fought from the back, so the two of them were in it together, close enough to talk. krishna is also god, though arjuna half forgets that. they're old friends, and in-laws too.\nthe first thing krishna says is get up. don't be a coward, it doesn't suit you. then the philosophy. you have a right to your work but never to its fruit, meaning the results. do the thing in front of you and let go of how it comes out. the self doesn't die when the body does. whatever road people take to me, i meet them on it.\nin chapter 11 arjuna asks to see krishna as he really is, and krishna shows him every face, every mouth, all of time, everything being born and dying at once. arjuna begs him to put the disguise back on. it's the same move as god answering [[job]], the man in the bible who lost everything, out of a whirlwind. no explanation, just scale.\n[[gandhi]] read it every day. [[ramdass|ram dass]] called it his bible. henry david thoreau, the american who lived alone in a cabin by walden pond and wrote the book about it, read it there. robert oppenheimer, the physicist who ran the project that built the atomic bomb, said a line from chapter 11 came into his head at the first bomb test in 1945: now i am become death, the destroyer of worlds. it's seven hundred verses and the whole of it is get up, do your work, don't grip.","struggle":"outside the gita, in the rest of the mahabharata, the giant epic it comes from, krishna's own people destroy themselves. after the war, gandhari, the mother of the hundred brothers who died on the other side, cursed him for letting it happen and said his own clan would die the same way. he accepted it. thirty six years later his clan, the yadavas, got drunk at a festival at prabhasa, by the sea, and beat each other to death with reeds that the story says turned to iron clubs in their hands, and he watched it.\nthen he went into the forest to sit, and a hunter named jara mistook his foot for a deer and put an arrow through it. the god who told arjuna the self can't be killed died of an arrow in the heel.","threads":["war","work"],"years":"the gita is roughly two thousand years old"},{"after":"the morning after ommen he did the thing he said he'd do. he went on talking, for fifty seven years, and he wouldn't be anybody's guru. he also let foundations and schools be set up in his name, and he fought over his copyrights, so the man with no organization died with several. about the affair there's no turn to report. he never confirmed it, the court fight outlived him, and other people settled it.\non february 7, 1986, ten days before the end, he had a tape recorder turned on. an immense energy and intelligence went through this body, he said, and nobody has done it. nobody. he had asked for no memorial service and there was none. his ashes were divided between ojai, england and india, and some of them went to the beach at adyar where a man had picked him out of the sand seventy seven years before.","basically":"an indian boy picked off a beach in 1909 by an englishman who said he could see auras, and raised by a movement called theosophy to be the world teacher, the messiah the whole planet was supposedly waiting for. in 1929 he dissolved the order built around him, gave back the castle and the land, said truth is a pathless land and that he wanted no followers, and then spent fifty seven more years giving talks to people who followed him anyway. if you've heard someone say just watch your mind, no method, no teacher, that's him, more or less. think of a child star groomed from twelve by a management company for the biggest role in the world, who cancels the act on opening night and then keeps touring the same halls, telling the crowd every night not to listen to him.","born":1895,"died":1986,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 226 L 200 226 L 200 250 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><circle cx='120' cy='104' r='14'/><path d='M 115 102 h 0.5 M 125 102 h 0.5' stroke-width='3.4'/><path d='M 116 110 L 124 110' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 120 118 L 120 184'/><path d='M 120 184 L 104 226 M 120 184 L 136 226'/><path d='M 120 136 L 166 118'/><path d='M 166 118 L 172 112 M 166 118 L 174 118 M 166 118 L 172 124' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 120 136 L 96 170'/><path d='M 176 226 L 176 150 M 166 226 L 186 226'/><rect x='169' y='134' width='14' height='18' rx='6' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 75.4 173.2 L 76.1 190.1 L 92.0 196.0 L 76.1 201.9 L 75.4 218.8 L 64.9 205.5 L 48.6 210.1 L 58.0 196.0 L 48.6 181.9 L 64.9 186.5 Z' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='2.4'/><circle cx='238.0' cy='216' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 234.6 215 h 0.3 M 241.4 215 h 0.3' stroke-width='2.0'/><path d='M 225.4 231 Q 238.0 223 250.6 231' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='266.4' cy='216' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 263.0 215 h 0.3 M 269.8 215 h 0.3' stroke-width='2.0'/><path d='M 253.8 231 Q 266.4 223 279.0 231' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='294.8' cy='216' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 291.4 215 h 0.3 M 298.2 215 h 0.3' stroke-width='2.0'/><path d='M 282.2 231 Q 294.8 223 307.4 231' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='323.2' cy='216' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 319.8 215 h 0.3 M 326.6 215 h 0.3' stroke-width='2.0'/><path d='M 310.6 231 Q 323.2 223 335.8 231' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='351.6' cy='216' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 348.2 215 h 0.3 M 355.0 215 h 0.3' stroke-width='2.0'/><path d='M 339.0 231 Q 351.6 223 364.2 231' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='380.0' cy='216' r='9' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 376.6 215 h 0.3 M 383.4 215 h 0.3' stroke-width='2.0'/><path d='M 367.4 231 Q 380.0 223 392.6 231' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='232.0' cy='192' r='7.5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 229.2 191 h 0.3 M 234.8 191 h 0.3' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 221.5 205.5 Q 232.0 197.5 242.5 205.5' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='269.1' cy='192' r='7.5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 266.2 191 h 0.3 M 272.0 191 h 0.3' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 258.6 205.5 Q 269.1 197.5 279.6 205.5' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='292.8' cy='192' r='7.5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 289.9 191 h 0.3 M 295.7 191 h 0.3' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 282.3 205.5 Q 292.8 197.5 303.3 205.5' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='329.9' cy='192' r='7.5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 327.0 191 h 0.3 M 332.8 191 h 0.3' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 319.4 205.5 Q 329.9 197.5 340.4 205.5' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='353.6' cy='192' r='7.5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 350.8 191 h 0.3 M 356.5 191 h 0.3' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 343.1 205.5 Q 353.6 197.5 364.1 205.5' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='390.8' cy='192' r='7.5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 387.9 191 h 0.3 M 393.7 191 h 0.3' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 380.3 205.5 Q 390.8 197.5 401.3 205.5' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='240.0' cy='172' r='6.5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 237.5 171 h 0.3 M 242.5 171 h 0.3' stroke-width='1.4'/><path d='M 230.9 184.5 Q 240.0 176.5 249.1 184.5' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='267.6' cy='172' r='6.5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 265.1 171 h 0.3 M 270.1 171 h 0.3' stroke-width='1.4'/><path d='M 258.5 184.5 Q 267.6 176.5 276.7 184.5' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='295.2' cy='172' r='6.5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 292.7 171 h 0.3 M 297.7 171 h 0.3' stroke-width='1.4'/><path d='M 286.1 184.5 Q 295.2 176.5 304.3 184.5' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='322.8' cy='172' r='6.5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 320.3 171 h 0.3 M 325.3 171 h 0.3' stroke-width='1.4'/><path d='M 313.7 184.5 Q 322.8 176.5 331.9 184.5' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='350.4' cy='172' r='6.5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 347.9 171 h 0.3 M 352.9 171 h 0.3' stroke-width='1.4'/><path d='M 341.3 184.5 Q 350.4 176.5 359.5 184.5' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='378.0' cy='172' r='6.5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 375.5 171 h 0.3 M 380.5 171 h 0.3' stroke-width='1.4'/><path d='M 368.9 184.5 Q 378.0 176.5 387.1 184.5' stroke-width='2'/><g transform='translate(42,199) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"raised from fourteen to be the world teacher, he stood up in front of three thousand believers at thirty four and shut the whole thing down.","key":"krishnamurti","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jiddu_Krishnamurti","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_of_the_Star_in_the_East","why":"the order they built around him, and the day he dissolved it"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_First_and_Last_Freedom","why":"the book with huxley's foreword, and how it came about"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lives_in_the_Shadow_with_J._Krishnamurti","why":"radha sloss's book, and what people made of it"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"the affair. rosalind williams met the brothers at ojai in 1922, not yet twenty. nitya was in love with her, in a high minded way. in 1927 she married d. rajagopal, the indian theosophist who ran krishnamurti's business, edited his books and organized the camps, and their daughter radha was born in 1931. according to radha's book, lives in the shadow with j. krishnamurti, published in 1991, five years after his death, the affair between her mother and krishnamurti began in 1932 and ran about twenty five years, into the late 1950s. as a small girl she called him krinsh and watched him climb the stairs to her mother's room with a flower. by her account her mother got pregnant several times and went off to have abortions, believing he was taking precautions because he said he was, and his support went as far as getting her on and off the bus. in his theosophical years he had told the young people around him not to marry.\nwhat came of it. the friendship with the rajagopals went sour for good and ended in a long and vicious court fight over property and money. he sued rajagopal to get back property, funds and the rights to his own talks, manuscripts and letters. the case formally began in 1971. a lot came back while he was alive, and the rest was settled in 1986, after he was dead. he never confirmed the affair, and nothing he said in public touches it that i can find. the people around him kept it quiet. in 1995 the krishnamurti foundation of america put out a pamphlet saying its trustees had known since the lawsuits of the 1970s and early 1980s. mary lutyens, his authorized biographer, answered radha's book in 1996 with a book of her own that admits the relationship happened. a man who told three thousand people he wanted no followers spent his last fifteen years in court over who owned his words.","moments":["in january 1927 annie besant told the associated press, the world teacher is here. two and a half years later the world teacher told the order built for him to go home.","the speech of august 3, 1929 went out on dutch radio. besant was in the crowd listening. leadbeater, at the society's convention in adyar later that year, said the coming had gone wrong.","in 1939 aldous huxley put his first american novel, after many a summer, out with a dedication to krishnamurti, in friendship. the two of them went walking in the mojave desert.","at saanen in the summer of 1985, ninety years old and giving the last of twenty five summers of talks there, he was told people were sad it was ending. if one is sad, he said, it's about time that we left."],"name":"jiddu krishnamurti","place":"madanapalle and adyar in south india, then england, then ojai, california","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 104 38 95 60 95 C 82 95 100 104 102 130 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 50 95 L 60 110 L 70 95 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 57 99 L 60 104 L 63 99 L 61 118 L 59 118 Z' fill='var(--clay)' stroke-width='1.8'/><path d='M 50 95 L 40 122 M 70 95 L 80 122' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 32 56 C 25 52 25 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 95 52 95 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 32 30 46 24 60 25 C 76 25 90 32 89 54 C 84 42 74 38 66 40 C 56 34 44 40 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 66 40 C 70 36 76 35 82 40' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='1.4' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 43 50 Q 49 46 56 49 M 64 49 Q 71 46 77 50' stroke-width='2.4'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='2.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='2.3' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 68 L 62 68'/><path d='M 53 76 Q 60 79 67 76'/><path d='M 104 128 L 104 96 M 97 128 L 111 128' stroke-width='2.2'/><rect x='99' y='82' width='10' height='15' rx='5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i maintain that truth is a pathless land, and you cannot approach it by any path whatsoever, by any religion, by any sect.","where":"the speech dissolving the order of the star, ommen, 3 august 1929, as the krishnamurti foundations print it"},{"q":"why did they have to pick on me?","where":"to emily lutyens, as a young man in england, in mary lutyens's krishnamurti: the years of awakening, 1975"},{"q":"freedom is pure observation without direction, without fear of punishment and reward.","where":"the core of the teaching, the one page statement he wrote in 1980 at mary lutyens's request"}],"start":"the first and last freedom, with huxley's foreword, which is his talks from 1947 to 1952 cut into chapters you can read in any order. if that's too much, freedom from the known, from 1969, which is short. for the life, mary lutyens's the years of awakening, and then radha sloss's lives in the shadow for the part lutyens left out.","status":"approved","story":"jiddu krishnamurti was born on may 11, 1895, in madanapalle, a small town about 150 miles north of madras in the south of india, which was then ruled by britain. that's the date his biographer mary lutyens settled on. the records of the time were loose and other dates get argued. he was the eighth child of telugu speaking brahmin parents, brahmins being the priestly caste at the top of the hindu order. his father collected rents for the british. his mother, whom he loved, died when he was ten. the father had belonged to the theosophical society since 1882. theosophy was a movement started in new york in 1875 that mixed hindu and buddhist ideas with seances and a belief in hidden masters, perfected teachers who were said to guide humanity from behind the scenes. when he retired, the father got a clerk's job at the society's headquarters at adyar, on the river at the edge of madras, and in january 1909 he moved the children there.\ncharles leadbeater was a former anglican priest in his fifties, one of the society's big names, who said he could see the aura, the colored light around a person. he had left the society in 1906 after two boys in chicago, one of them fourteen, accused him of misconduct. he admitted he had told a few boys approaching puberty that masturbation would ease their obsessive thoughts, resigned before a committee could meet, and was voted back in at the end of 1908. charges beyond what he admitted were never proven, and annie besant, the englishwoman who ran the society from 1907, called the proceedings a travesty of justice. he got back to adyar in february 1909. that year, on the society's beach by the river, he noticed the clerk's thin son. lutyens describes the boy as scraggy, dirty, ill nourished, with crooked teeth, and the man who helped him with his homework thought him dim witted. leadbeater said his aura was the most wonderful he had ever seen, without a particle of selfishness in it. the theosophists were waiting for the world teacher, a great being they called maitreya, to take a human body and teach the world. leadbeater decided this was the body. the sources don't agree on the month, and krishnamurti himself said later that without that discovery he would have died.\nbesant took the boys over. in march 1910 she became their legal guardian, and he came to treat her as a mother. in april 1911, at benares, she founded the order of the star in the east to get the world ready for the coming, with krishnamurti, not yet sixteen, as its head. by late 1913 it had around 15,000 members and by 1926 more than 43,000. that spring she took him and his younger brother nitya to england, to private tutors and a daily program of sports, lessons, yoga and meditation. he tried more than once to get into a university and never did. their father changed his mind and sued in 1912 to get his sons back, and his case made accusations against leadbeater, the kind that follow a man around, which the court never settled. in 1913 the high court in madras ruled for the father. besant appealed, and in may 1914 the empire's highest court, in london, overturned it, partly on the ground that the boys had lived in england since 1911 and had not objected to her care. by then he was of age anyway. a scholar who went through all 1,205 pages of the case file found almost nothing in it in the boys' own voices, and no record anywhere of krishnamurti speaking about what his father had alleged. what he did say, about seventy five years later, talking about himself as the boy, was that the boy was vague, uncertain, woolly, and didn't seem to care what was happening.\nin 1922 the brothers went to ojai, a valley town in southern california, partly for the climate, because nitya had tuberculosis. on august 17 that year krishnamurti started complaining of a sharp pain in his neck. two days later he reported what he called the first most extraordinary experience, of being at one with everything around him, and the day after that he described leaving his body and a profound calm. for weeks it came back almost every night, and it went on at intervals for the rest of his life. the people around him called it the process, and nobody has settled what it was. on november 13, 1925, nitya died at ojai, twenty seven years old, of influenza on top of the tuberculosis. krishnamurti was on a ship to india for the society's fiftieth anniversary and the telegram caught up with him in egypt. the leaders, besant and leadbeater among them, had assured him nitya would recover. people who saw him said the news broke him completely. twelve days later, by the same accounts, he was quiet, radiant and free of all emotion, and whatever faith he had left in theosophy and the people running it had been shaken to the root.\nthe movement had kept growing. a castle at eerde in the netherlands and about 5,000 acres around it had been given to the order in the early 1920s, and thousands came every summer to camps at ommen nearby. in january 1927 besant told the associated press that the world teacher is here. on august 3, 1929, at the ommen camp, in front of besant and about 3,000 members, with dutch radio carrying it, he dissolved the order. truth is a pathless land, he said, and you cannot approach it by any path whatsoever, by any religion, by any sect. no organization should be formed to lead people along a path, and he did not want followers. he gave back the castle and the land, resigned from the trusts, and soon after cut his ties to theosophy. leadbeater's verdict, at the society's convention at adyar later that year, was that the coming had gone wrong. krishnamurti was thirty four, and he kept talking for fifty seven more years, in india, europe and america, calling himself the speaker from the stage, a word he said gave him no authority. in 1938 he met [[huxley|aldous huxley]], the english novelist who had moved to los angeles, and they became close. huxley pushed him to write, took him to his own publisher, and the first and last freedom came out in 1954 with a ten page foreword by huxley and was in its sixth printing by the end of the year. from 1961 to 1985 he spoke every summer at saanen in switzerland, first in a town hall that held 350, then in a tent for 900, then to thousands. in the 1960s foundations were set up in his name in india, america and england, and there are schools, rishi valley in india, ten miles from where he was born, started in 1928, and brockwood park in england, from 1969. he died at home in ojai just after midnight on february 17, 1986, of pancreatic cancer, at ninety.","struggle":"the english years first. he was a boy from a small town in south india with a famous aura and the hopes of a whole movement on him, living among tutors. why did they have to pick on me, he asked emily lutyens, the theosophist who mothered him in london. he wrote to leadbeater once that he had not been what is called happy for many years.\nthen the body. from august 1922 the thing at ojai came back night after night, the neck pain, the leaving of the body, and it never fully stopped. doctors and writers have called it a yogic awakening, or epilepsy, or malaria, or grief for his mother, or meditation. lutyens, who remembered him from the first world war on, rejected the medical explanations and said he regarded it as spiritually necessary. around then he called himself god intoxicated.\nthen nitya. the two of them shared everything and were as close as twins, lutyens says, though they looked nothing alike. when the telegram came in november 1925 he said he had never believed it could happen, and that he had now suffered and knew what death was. years later, telling it, he put it flatter: when he died i said, what a joke this is. the people who had promised him nitya would live were the same people who had promised him he was the world teacher. four years later he dissolved the order.","threads":["fame","grief","doubt","family","the body"],"years":"1895 to 1986"},{"after":"after the wandering he came back to the hill, more or less. he settled into being what the vision had said, a wičháša wakȟáŋ, a holy man, running ceremonies, doctoring people, doing the heyoka's backwards work. his son archie fire lame deer became a holy man too.\nhe didn't pretend the wild years were a mistake he'd outgrown. his whole point was that they were part of the training. you can't help a drunk if you've never been one. you can't talk to a man in jail if you think you're better than him.","basically":"a lakota holy man from the rosebud reservation who spent his twenties and thirties doing about every job and every dumb thing a man can do, and then told all of it, laughing, in a book he made with a white writer in 1972. he was a heyoka, one of the sacred clowns who do everything backwards so people can see themselves. think of the uncle who's been to jail and back, tells the filthiest stories at the table, and is also the one the family calls when somebody's dying. take from him that the holy man doesn't have to be the cleanest person in the room. he has to have been in all the rooms.","born":1903,"died":1976,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 250 C 60 247 120 253 200 250 S 340 248 400 250'/><path d='M 250 250 C 295.0 159.0 355.0 159.0 400 250' fill='var(--sage)'/><ellipse cx='332' cy='176' rx='10' ry='4' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 96 250 L 96 196 L 146 196 L 146 250 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><ellipse cx='121' cy='196' rx='25' ry='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 96 214 L 146 214 M 96 232 L 146 232' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='206' cy='232' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='201' cy='230' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='211' cy='230' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 202 236 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 206 218 L 206 176'/><path d='M 206 176 L 190 134 L 196 112 M 206 176 L 224 136 L 236 118'/><path d='M 206 210 L 184 250 M 206 210 L 228 250'/><path d='M 34 250 L 34 206' stroke-width='4'/><g transform='translate(22,181.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a lakota holy man who'd been a rodeo clown, a sheepherder, a sign painter and a jailbird, and figured all of it counted.","key":"lamedeer","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Fire_Lame_Deer","why":"the plain overview of his life"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"the women, mostly. he tells about one night a policeman came home, found him with his wife, and shot at him. he ran. he tells it like a man who knows exactly how that sounds and isn't going to clean it up for you. a lot of them, a lot of it stupid.\nand the booze and the jail, which he also doesn't clean up. the book is sloppy the way a good long night at the kitchen table is sloppy.","moments":["the name he came down the hill with at sixteen, tahca ushte, lame deer, was his great-grandfather's, a chief.","he learned most of his english on the road in the wandering years, not in school, which you can hear in the book.","erdoes went on to write lakota woman with mary crow dog, a young lakota woman who'd been inside the wounded knee occupation of 1973."],"name":"john fire lame deer","place":"the rosebud reservation in south dakota, and a lot of road","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 18 42 C 28 35 92 35 102 42 C 92 47 28 47 18 42 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 38 40 C 38 16 82 16 82 40 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"a clown's work is sacred. it's a medicine man's job.","where":"lame deer, seeker of visions, the chapter on the heyoka"},{"q":"a medicine man ought to have lived all of it, the good and the rotten, drunk and sober, so he knows what the people he's helping are going through.","where":"lame deer, seeker of visions, paraphrased"},{"q":"i had a thirst for women. i loved many girls, more than a hundred.","where":"lame deer, seeker of visions, the chapters on his wandering years"}],"start":"lame deer, seeker of visions, the first chapter, alone on the hilltop, then the chapter on the clowns. remember erdoes wrote the english.","status":"approved","story":"he was born on march 17, 1903, on the rosebud reservation in south dakota, home of the sicangu lakota. his father was hunkpapa and his mother miniconjou, two of the other lakota bands. at sixteen he went alone up a hill for his hanbleceya, the lakota vision quest, fasting and praying for days in a pit dug into the hilltop and waiting for a vision. he came down with one, and took his great-grandfather's name, tahca ushte, lame deer.\nthen came what he called his wandering years. he rode in rodeos and worked as a rodeo clown, the guy in the barrel who distracts the bull. he was a soldier, a sign painter, a potato picker, a tribal policeman, a sheepherder, a singer, and, as he put it, a jail prisoner. he drank. he learned english on the road. he chased women all over the west.\nand he was a heyoka, a sacred clown. among the lakota, a person who dreams of the wakinyan, the thunder beings, has to become a heyoka, and heyokas do everything backwards. they shiver in a heat wave and complain of being full when food runs out. it's funny, and it's supposed to be. the laughing opens people up. he said a clown's work is sacred.\nin 1967 richard erdoes, an austrian-born illustrator, came to the pine ridge reservation on assignment for life magazine and stayed in the lakota world. he and lame deer made lame deer, seeker of visions, published in 1972. lame deer talked and erdoes wrote the english and put it in order, the same kind of partnership john neihardt had with [[blackelk|black elk]]. so the voice in the book is lame deer's, through erdoes's hands. he took part in the protests of the early seventies, and died on december 14, 1976, in south dakota.","struggle":"he had the vision at sixteen and then spent about twenty years running from it. rodeos, bars, jail, other men's wives. he knew what he was supposed to be and he went and was everything else first.\nthe drinking was real. the book doesn't make a sermon of it and doesn't make it cute either. it's in there next to the rodeo stories like it was just the weather in those years.","threads":["addiction","love","wandering","joy"],"years":"1903 to 1976"},{"after":"in the story he wrote it down for the gatekeeper, the border guard, and left. that's the after. the story has no school, no crowd of students, no coming back to check on the book. it's the only scripture in this forest where the author's response to writing it was to disappear.\nthe book's own answer to failure is chapter 76. the stiff and the hard are companions of death, the soft and yielding are companions of life. the strong stiff tree is the one that gets cut down. he'd already said what to do next before you asked.","basically":"the name given to an old chinese sage who may never have existed, credited with the tao te ching, a thin book of eighty one short poems about living like water. it's one of the most translated books in the world. the whole idea is that forcing things makes them worse, and the soft thing wins in the end. if you've ever tried so hard at something that it fell apart in your hands, this book was written for that exact morning.","born":-500,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--sage)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 150 250 C 150 200 250 200 250 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 160 220 C 140 210 140 195 155 200 M 240 220 C 260 210 260 195 245 200'/><path d='M 165 250 L 165 268 M 185 250 L 185 268 M 215 250 L 215 268 M 235 250 L 235 268'/><circle cx='200' cy='160' r='14'/><path d='M 195 165 Q 200 169 205 165'/><path d='M 200 174 L 200 210 M 200 188 L 180 202 M 200 188 L 224 200'/><path d='M 190 148 C 195 138 205 138 210 148' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 120 M 370 250 L 370 120 M 290 120 L 380 120 M 296 108 L 374 108'/><circle cx='335' cy='170' r='11'/><path d='M 335 184 L 335 232'/><path d='M 335 232 L 321 266 M 335 232 L 349 266'/><path d='M 335 198 L 349 222 M 335 198 L 309 192'/><path d='M 306 180 L 322 178' /><rect x='296' y='172' width='14' height='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(376,81.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he opens his book by telling you the book can't do it, then writes eighty more chapters anyway.","key":"laotzu","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laozi","why":"the plain overview of the legend"},{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/216","why":"the tao te ching in an old public domain translation"}],"lineage":"tao","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["the old story, told by sima qian, china's first great historian, has confucius, the famous teacher of duty and good manners whose sayings shaped china for two thousand years, going to ask him about ritual and coming away shaken. he told his students he'd met a dragon. in china a dragon isn't a monster. it's a thing that rides the wind and clouds up to heaven and can't be caught or pinned down. confucius meant he had no idea what he'd just met.","the tao te ching is eighty one chapters and about five thousand chinese characters, which makes it shorter than most of the introductions written about it.","chapter 11 is the one about empty spaces. thirty spokes meet at the hub, and it's the hole in the middle that makes the wheel work. same with the inside of a pot, and the doors and windows cut into a room."],"name":"lao tzu","place":"china, maybe","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 100 40 128 60 130 C 80 128 94 100 90 60 C 82 84 38 84 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 96 C 50 106 70 106 76 96 M 40 110 C 48 118 72 118 80 110' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 34 86 34 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='60' cy='28' r='7' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 40 50 C 44 42 54 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 66 44 76 42 80 50' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 45 57 C 48 60 52 60 55 57 M 65 57 C 68 60 72 60 75 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the way that can be spoken of is not the constant way.","where":"tao te ching, chapter 1, first line, in d.c. lau's translation"},{"q":"we shape clay into a pot, but it's the emptiness inside that holds what we want.","where":"tao te ching, chapter 11, paraphrased"},{"q":"nothing is softer than water, and nothing beats it at wearing down the hard.","where":"tao te ching, chapter 78, paraphrased"}],"start":"the tao te ching, chapters 1 through 4, then 8, 11, 76 and 78. each chapter is a few lines; you can read the whole book in an evening. any translation. stephen mitchell's is loose and pretty, d.c. lau's is a scholar's and plainer.","status":"approved","story":"the story is that he was keeper of the archives, the royal library, for the zhou, the family of kings who ruled china back then. he got sick of the whole thing and headed west. old paintings show him riding an ox. at the last gate out of the country the guard recognized him and wouldn't let him leave until he wrote down what he knew. he wrote a short book in two parts, a little over five thousand characters, and went on, and nobody knows where he ended up.\nthere may never have been a lao tzu. scholars are split on it. the name just means old master. the tao te ching, roughly the book of the way and its power, is most likely a collection that grew over time and settled into shape around the fourth and third centuries bc. it doesn't matter. the book is the teacher.\nthe first line is the tao that can be spoken isn't the eternal tao. tao means the way, the thing underneath everything that makes it all go, and the book says right up front that once you've put it in words, you've already missed it. every scripture on these pages should start that way and this is the only one that does.\nthe rest is about water, which is soft and beats everything hard, the way a river cuts a canyon. about the empty space in a pot, which is the part you actually use. about doing by not doing, wu wei in chinese, which isn't laziness. it's not forcing, like floating in the ocean, which only works once you stop thrashing. the more you read it the less there is, on purpose.","struggle":"chapter 20 of the tao te ching is the loneliest thing in scripture. everyone else is bright, i alone am dim. everyone has a purpose, i'm like a fool, drifting. everyone else has plenty, i alone seem to have nothing. whoever wrote that, and it wasn't nobody, was not having a good year.\nthe legend has him leaving after a long time at court because he saw the kingdom rotting. that's a man who tried and quit, not a man who floated above it.","threads":["silence"],"years":"sixth century bc, or fourth, or never"},{"after":"no redemption, exactly. he got out in 1976, moved to los angeles, and reinvented himself as a futurist and a talk show guest. in the early eighties he went on a paid debate tour with g. gordon liddy, the former prosecutor who'd raided millbrook and later went to prison for watergate. they made money off it and became friends.\nwhen he got his cancer diagnosis in 1995 he treated his own dying as a show too, and a kind of teaching. he talked openly about it, invited people over, and planned his ashes into space. you can call that brave or you can call it more circus. it was probably both.","basically":"a harvard psychologist who ate magic mushrooms in mexico in 1960, came home a true believer, and became the most famous drug preacher in america. turn on, tune in, drop out was his. he was fired, raided, jailed, broke out of prison with the help of a radical underground group, ran to algeria and switzerland, got caught, and ended up talking to the fbi. picture a tenured scientist turning into a cult influencer in about four years. he's why psychedelic research got shut down for thirty years, and he's worth reading so you know what not to do.","born":1920,"died":1996,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 40 250 L 200 250 L 200 230 L 40 230 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='120' cy='114' r='14'/><path d='M 120 128 L 120 176'/><path d='M 120 176 L 106 210 M 120 176 L 134 210'/><path d='M 120 142 L 100 118 M 120 142 L 140 118'/><path d='M 115 119 Q 120 123 125 119'/><path d='M 150 230 L 150 176 M 144 170 C 144 162 156 162 156 170 C 156 178 144 178 144 170' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 300 150 L 318 90 L 336 150 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 304 150 L 296 168 L 312 156 M 332 150 L 340 168 L 324 156'/><path d='M 318 160 C 312 190 326 210 316 240' stroke-dasharray='3 7' opacity='.6'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='330' cy='40' r='2'/><circle cx='260' cy='60' r='2'/><circle cx='370' cy='90' r='2'/><\/g><g transform='translate(46,208.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he had the science in his hands at harvard and turned it into a circus, and he knew it.","key":"leary","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timothy_Leary","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harvard_Psilocybin_Project","why":"the harvard experiments that got him fired"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"he turned a science into a circus, and he knew it. the harvard research could have been careful, and he made it a movement, then a slogan, then a brand. the backlash he helped cause is a big reason lsd and psilocybin were banned and serious research stopped for about thirty years.\nafter his capture he talked to the fbi to get his sentence cut, and the informant question has followed him ever since. his defenders say he warned people and gave the fbi little that hurt anyone. others who knew him never forgave it. what's clear is he went into the program under a code name. the rest of it, drink, five marriages, bad fathering, was all his own. the risks of the drugs he was selling as salvation were real then and are now. psychotic breaks in some people, a bad trip that doesn't end when you want it to, rarely hallucinogen persisting perception disorder, where the visuals never fully go away, and prison.","moments":["in the fall of 1960 [[huxley|aldous huxley]] was a visiting professor at mit, just down the road, and met with him that season. huxley's advice, roughly, was go slow and keep it quiet. he did the opposite.","he made the phrase set and setting famous, meaning your mindset going in and the place you're in matter as much as the drug. in 1964 he, alpert, and ralph metzner, a grad student in their circle, wrote the psychedelic experience, a trip manual built on the tibetan book of the dead, the old buddhist guide read to the dying.","in september 1971, a fugitive in switzerland, he met [[hofmann|albert hofmann]], the man who made lsd, at a railway station snack bar in lausanne. hofmann thought he was charming and blind to the dangers."],"name":"timothy leary","place":"harvard, millbrook, prison, algeria, prison, los angeles","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 96 C 52 104 68 104 72 96' /><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"turn on, tune in, drop out.","where":"the human be-in, golden gate park, san francisco, january 14, 1967. he said the phrasing came partly from marshall mcluhan, the media thinker"},{"q":"i learned more in the five hours after taking these mushrooms than in the preceding fifteen years of studying psychology.","where":"his account of cuernavaca, mexico, august 1960, paraphrased"},{"q":"why not?","where":"his last words, according to his son zach, who was there, may 1996"}],"start":"flashbacks, his 1983 autobiography, which is funny and unreliable. then, for the honest version, jay stevens's storming heaven, a history of lsd in america.","status":"approved","story":"he was a clinical psychologist, a good one, with a well known book on personality. in 1959 he got a lecturer's job at harvard, the most famous university in america. in august 1960, on vacation in cuernavaca, mexico, he ate psilocybin mushrooms by a swimming pool and said later he'd learned more in five hours than in fifteen years of psychology.\nback at harvard he started the harvard psilocybin project with his colleague richard alpert, later [[ramdass|ram dass]]. they gave psilocybin, made in switzerland by [[hofmann|albert hofmann]]'s company, to grad students, to prisoners at a massachusetts jail to see if it kept them from reoffending, and in 1962 to divinity students on good friday. [[huxley|aldous huxley]] was an early adviser. the work got loose and sloppy fast. there were parties. the promise to keep it away from undergraduates got broken. harvard fired him in may 1963, officially for skipping his classes.\na rich young family, the hitchcocks, lent him and alpert a sixty four room mansion in millbrook, new york, and it became a psychedelic commune for five years. in january 1967 at the human be-in, a huge hippie gathering in golden gate park in san francisco, he told the crowd to turn on, tune in, drop out. richard nixon, the president, is said to have called him the most dangerous man in america.\narrested for small amounts of marijuana, he got ten years in 1970. that september he climbed out of a california prison along a telephone wire, and the weather underground, a radical group that set off bombs against government buildings, smuggled him out of the country for a fee paid by a hippie drug ring. he went to algeria, fell out with the black panthers in exile there, moved to switzerland, and was caught in kabul, afghanistan, in early 1973. back in prison he cooperated with the fbi. he got out in 1976.\nafter that he was a showman, touring with the watergate conspirator who'd once raided him, selling the idea that computers were the new lsd, making software, talking. he died of prostate cancer in beverly hills in may 1996, and the next year a few grams of his ashes went into orbit on a rocket.","struggle":"in 1955, on his thirty fifth birthday, his first wife marianne killed herself. he was left with two young kids. that's the man who went to mexico five years later.\nthe kids paid for his fame. his daughter susan, who'd been dragged through millbrook, raids, and exile, struggled her whole life and died in jail in 1990. he was all over the news for thirty years, and the people nearest him got the least of him.","threads":["psychedelics","fame","prison","exile"],"years":"1920 to 1996"},{"after":"she went and told them. that's the after in the book, and then the record goes quiet. she isn't named in acts, the book about what the first christians did next. whatever she did with the rest of her life, nobody wrote it down anywhere we can trust.\nthe legends fill it in. an eastern tradition says she went to ephesus, a big greek city in what's now turkey. a french one says she landed in provence, in the south of france, and lived out her life in a cave at sainte-baume. an old story says she went to the emperor tiberius, who ruled rome when jesus died, with an egg to tell him about the resurrection, and that's why orthodox icons, the painted holy images of the eastern church, show her holding a red one. none of it is history. the history is a woman told to go and tell, who went.","basically":"one of the women who followed jesus around galilee and paid for the trip out of her own pocket, who stayed at the cross when the men ran, and who, in john's gospel, is the first person to see him alive again. then in 591 a pope mixed her up with a different woman in a sermon, and for fourteen hundred years she got painted and preached as a reformed prostitute, which the bible never says. think of somebody whose whole reputation got hijacked by one bad take that went viral and stayed that way for centuries. she's for anyone who stayed when everyone else left, and anyone who's been remembered wrong.","born":null,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--sage)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 22 250 C 22 224 62 224 62 250' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 42 216 L 42 206 M 16 228 L 8 222 M 68 228 L 76 222' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 262 250 C 264 170 372 160 380 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 306 250 C 306 214 338 214 338 250 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='250' cy='228' r='22' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='100' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 100 168 L 100 216'/><path d='M 100 216 L 86 250 M 100 216 L 114 250'/><path d='M 100 182 L 86 206 M 100 182 L 126 176'/><path d='M 132 140 L 112 248 L 100 246'/><path d='M 146 250 L 146 236 M 156 250 L 156 240'/><circle cx='146' cy='232' r='4' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='156' cy='236' r='4' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 177 150 C 172 166 174 178 180 186 M 203 150 C 208 166 206 178 200 186'/><circle cx='190' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 190 168 L 190 216'/><path d='M 190 216 L 176 250 M 190 216 L 204 250'/><path d='M 190 182 L 204 206 M 190 182 L 164 176'/><path d='M 185 159 Q 190 163 195 159'/><g transform='translate(240,181.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the church spent almost fourteen hundred years calling her a prostitute, and nothing in the book says so.","key":"magdalene","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Magdalene","why":"the plain overview, and how her name got dragged"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","why":"the king james bible on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["a second century text, written a hundred years or more after jesus, called the gospel of mary, which survives only in pieces, has peter asking the others whether jesus really spoke privately with a woman and not openly to them. it isn't in the bible. the argument in it sounds real.","in john 20:17 he tells her not to hold on to him. in latin, the old language of the western church, that's noli me tangere, and it's on the wall of half the museums in europe.","the alabaster jar painters give her, a pale stone jar of perfumed oil, belongs to the woman in luke 7 and the mary of bethany in john 12. every picture of her with a jar is a picture of the mix-up."],"name":"mary magdalene","place":"magdala on the sea of galilee, then jerusalem","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><ellipse cx='96' cy='112' rx='8' ry='10' fill='var(--peach)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"they have taken away my lord, and i don't know where they have laid him.","where":"the gospel of john, 20:13"},{"q":"rabboni.","where":"john 20:16, which john translates for you as teacher"},{"q":"i have seen the lord.","where":"john 20:18, what she told the disciples"}],"start":"john 20:1 to 18, near the end of john's gospel in any bible. then luke 8:1 to 3, the three verses about the money. then luke 24:1 to 11.","status":"approved","story":"mary from magdala, a fishing town on the sea of galilee, the big lake in the north of what's now israel. luke 8, in the gospel of luke, one of the four books that tell jesus's life, says seven demons had gone out of her. nobody knows what that meant, illness, madness, something else, and the text doesn't say.\nthe same passage says she and joanna, whose husband ran the household of herod antipas, the ruler of galilee, and susanna and many other women traveled with [[jesus]] and the twelve, his inner circle, and paid for it out of their own money. the ministry ran on women's money.\nwhen he was arrested, mark says, the disciples all ran. she didn't. she stood near the cross with the other women and watched him die, and then watched where they laid him. john's gospel puts one of the men at the cross too. the rest were gone.\nearly on sunday morning, still dark, john says, she went to the tomb and found the stone rolled away. she ran for [[peter]] and the other disciple, the one john calls the disciple jesus loved. they came, looked, and went home. she stayed outside, crying. a man she took for the gardener asked why, and she said if you carried him off, tell me where. he said mary. she said rabboni, my teacher. he sent her to tell the others, and she went and said i have seen the lord. that's why her old title is apostle to the apostles. an apostle is someone sent out with a message, so she's the one who was sent to the ones who were sent. thomas aquinas, the great medieval catholic thinker, used the title, and the vatican used it again in 2016 when it raised her day on the church calendar to a feast, the same rank the male apostles get.","struggle":"seven demons is the struggle, and it's all we get. whatever it was, it was bad enough to count to seven, and it was over before the story starts. she's in the book as someone who had already been through it.\nthen good friday, the day of the crucifixion. she watched him die slowly and watched them close the tomb, and on sunday even the body was gone. the grief in john 20 is so raw she can't see who's standing in front of her. and when she and the other women told the apostles, luke says it seemed to them like nonsense, and they didn't believe them.\nthe rest of what came at her came after she died. in a sermon in 591, pope gregory the great, one of the most powerful popes of the early middle ages, merged her with the unnamed sinful woman who washes jesus's feet with her tears and anoints them in luke 7, and with mary of bethany, the sister of martha and lazarus. the sinful woman became a prostitute in the preaching, and the prostitute became mary magdalene, in sermons and paintings for fourteen centuries. none of it is in the text. the catholic church quietly separated them again in 1969, when it revised its calendar. the orthodox churches of the east never mixed them up in the first place.","threads":["grief","love"],"years":"the first century"},{"after":"after the year in bed he got up and took on the household, david's widow and daughter included. he wouldn't live off teaching torah, so he made himself a doctor. both of his major books were finished after the drowning, the mishneh torah around 1180 and the guide around 1190. in the mishneh torah he ranks eight ways of giving to the poor, and at the top, above every kind of handout, he puts the gift, the loan, the partnership or the job that means a person never has to ask again.\nthe same pen that wrote the hard lines could go the other way. a man known as obadiah the proselyte, who had come to judaism from islam, asked whether he was allowed to say our god and god of our fathers in the prayers, when his fathers hadn't been jews. maimonides told him to say every word, that converts are children of abraham, and that where a born jew can point to his ancestors, a convert can point to god as his parent and protector. he kept at the doctoring, and by one standard account he worked himself into exhaustion. when he died his son abraham maimonides was eighteen. he took over both jobs, leading egypt's jews and doctoring at court, and when he heard his father's books had been burned in france he wrote a book in their defense.","basically":"a rabbi and a doctor from the 1100s, born in muslim spain, driven out as a boy, and settled in egypt, where he ended up a physician at the sultan's court. he wrote the mishneh torah, a code of all of jewish law with the arguments taken out, and the guide for the perplexed, a hard book for people who love both their religion and science and can't see how to keep both. picture a refugee kid who grows up to be the president's doctor and the country's top legal mind at the same time, and writes the century's toughest philosophy book on the side. he lived his whole life among muslims and wrote his philosophy in arabic.","born":1138,"died":1204,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--olive)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='338' cy='54' r='13' fill='var(--marigold)'/><g opacity='.75'><path d='M 62 78 L 104 78 L 96 90 L 70 90 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 83 78 L 83 46'/><path d='M 83 50 L 102 72 L 83 72 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 97 q 9 -8 18 0 t 18 0 t 18 0 t 18 0 t 18 0 t 18 0'/><\/g><path d='M 120 250 L 120 180'/><rect x='120' y='216' width='142' height='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 256 230 L 256 250'/><path d='M 123 216 L 126 194 L 137 197 L 136 216 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><circle cx='152' cy='198' r='14'/><path d='M 164 206 L 200 211'/><path d='M 196 204 L 262 208 L 262 216 L 196 216 Z' fill='var(--teal)'/><path d='M 178 208 L 226 188'/><path d='M 172 207 L 186 215'/><rect x='224' y='168' width='15' height='19' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 228 174 L 235 174 M 228 179 L 235 179' stroke-width='1.5'/><circle cx='292' cy='160' r='14'/><path d='M 292 174 L 292 216'/><path d='M 292 216 L 280 250 M 292 216 L 304 250'/><path d='M 292 188 L 250 180 M 292 188 L 306 206'/><g opacity='.6'><circle cx='336' cy='178' r='9'/><circle cx='362' cy='182' r='9'/><path d='M 336 187 L 336 250 M 362 191 L 362 250'/><\/g><g transform='translate(108,153) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"his little brother drowned with the family's money. he stayed in bed for a year, then got up and became the sultan's doctor.","key":"maimonides","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maimonides","why":"the plain overview of his life and books"},{"label":"stanford encyclopedia of philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/maimonides/","why":"the careful scholarly version of what he thought"},{"label":"cambridge university library","url":"https://exhibitions.lib.cam.ac.uk/discardedhistory/?p=105","why":"his brother david's last letter, with a picture of it"},{"label":"stanford encyclopedia of philosophy","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/maimonides-islamic/","why":"what he took from the muslim philosophers"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"he could be hard, and some of what he wrote is ugly to read now. in the mishneh torah he rules that a wife who won't do the housework she owes can be made to do it, even with a rod. abraham ben david, another great rabbi of his own time, in the south of france, disputed that ruling. he also says a husband shouldn't let his wife out more than once or twice a month, though he adds that a home isn't a jail, and that fathers shouldn't teach their daughters torah because most women can't keep their minds on it. near the end of the guide he pictures god as a king in a palace and sorts people by how near they get. the ones outside the country altogether, the turks who wander the far north, the kushites of the south, which is the bible's word for the black peoples south of egypt, and anyone like them nearer home, he calls irrational beings, below mankind but above monkeys. and in the epistle to yemen, a letter from around 1172 to jews there who were being forced to convert, he calls [[muhammad|the prophet muhammad]] the madman. he wrote that as a man whose own family had been driven out by muslim rulers, to people being squeezed by another one. he also wrote it from inside the muslim world, which he never left. i can't find that he took any of it back.\nthe other mess is the fight over his books, and it started while he was alive. the mishneh torah gave rulings without sources, and abraham ben david wrote that he'd abandoned the method of everyone before him. when maimonides wrote that anyone who thinks god has a body has left the faith, the same critic answered that greater and better men than he had thought so. others said he didn't really believe the dead will rise, and around 1191 he had to write a whole essay to say that he did. after he died it got worse. at the start of 1232 a rabbi in montpellier, in the south of france, and two of his students put a ban on his books and on anyone who studied them, and got the rabbis of northern france to ban the study of philosophy. then dominican friars burned his books in public. most sources put it at montpellier in 1232 or 1233, and one puts a burning in paris. his defenders believed fellow jews had denounced the books to the friars, and historians still argue over whether that's what happened. his tomb in tiberias was desecrated. in the 1240s the talmud itself was burned in paris, and the fight went quiet for a few decades.","moments":["not long after he reached egypt, a crusader king besieged a town in the nile delta and took jews captive. maimonides sent five letters to the jewish communities of lower egypt asking them to pool their money for the ransom.","david's last letter, written around 1170 from a port in what's now sudan, is in the cambridge university library. it's in judeo-arabic, on paper, and it tells his brother not to worry.","in the 1199 letter he says the sabbath is the one day his own community can see him. after the morning service they came to him and he taught them until noon, and some came back in the afternoon.","he called the books of al-farabi, a muslim philosopher who died around 950, finer than fine flour."],"name":"maimonides","place":"cordoba in spain, then fez in morocco, then fustat, next to cairo, in egypt","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 104 38 96 60 96 C 82 96 100 104 102 130 Z' fill='var(--olive)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 97 M 68 84 L 68 97'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 33 64 C 33 92 46 110 60 112 C 74 110 87 92 87 64 C 80 76 70 78 60 78 C 50 78 40 76 33 64 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 47 88 L 48 96 M 60 90 L 60 102 M 73 88 L 72 96' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 30 47 C 22 22 44 8 62 9 C 84 9 99 26 90 47 C 76 37 46 37 30 47 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 35 35 C 50 25 72 25 86 35 M 43 21 C 56 16 70 16 80 22' stroke-width='1.8' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 60 12 C 56 20 56 28 60 36' stroke-width='1.8' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 89 44 C 97 52 97 66 93 76'/><path d='M 45 52 Q 49.5 49 54 51 M 66 51 Q 70.5 49 75 52' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='50' cy='58' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='58' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 54 75 C 57 78 63 78 66 75'/><path d='M 80 108 L 104 104 L 106 124 L 82 128 Z' fill='var(--teal)'/><path d='M 93 106 L 95 126' stroke-width='1.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"one should accept the truth from whatever source it proceeds.","where":"eight chapters, his short book on ethics, the opening pages, in joseph i. gorfinkle's translation"},{"q":"he grew up on my knees, he was my brother, he was my student.","where":"his letter about his brother david's death, written about eight years after it, in s.d. goitein's translation in letters of medieval jewish traders"},{"q":"by each additional negative attribute you advance toward the knowledge of god.","where":"the guide for the perplexed, part one, chapter 59, in m. friedländer's translation. a negative attribute is something you say god is not"}],"start":"his letters first. the one about david and the one to samuel ibn tibbon about his working day are a page or two each, and you'll know him afterward. then eight chapters, which is short. then the guide for the perplexed, part one, chapters 51 to 59, on what can and can't be said about god. in the mishneh torah, the tenth chapter of the laws of gifts to the poor has the eight levels of giving.","status":"approved","story":"moses ben maimon was born in cordoba, in muslim spain. the newer reference books say 1138, because he wrote that he finished one of his books in 1168 at the age of thirty. britannica and the older books say 1135. his father, maimon, was a rabbinic judge, and jews know the son as the rambam, short for our rabbi moses son of maimon. in 1148 the almohads, a berber muslim dynasty from north africa, took the city. the muslim rulers before them had let jews and christians keep their religions. the almohads didn't. the choice was islam, leaving, or death, and the family left. he was ten, or thirteen by the older date.\nfor about ten years they moved around southern spain and north africa, and around 1160 they settled in fez, in morocco, which was almohad country too. how they lived there is an old argument. two medieval muslim writers say he went through a conversion to islam, and one of his modern biographers thinks the family did pretend to. other scholars say there's no credible evidence of that, and that at most he dressed and acted like a muslim in public and kept on as a practicing jew. there are also stories, in more than one version, that he was later accused of having abandoned islam and got off, in one telling because a muslim friend spoke up for him, in another because a conversion made under force doesn't count. what's certain is what he wrote. a rabbi had ruled that jews who'd said the muslim creed to stay alive were no longer jews and that their secret prayers were worthless. maimonides, still a young man, answered with the letter on forced conversion. say the words and live, he argued, keep what you can in private, and get out as fast as you can to somewhere you can be a jew in the open.\nin 1165 the family took that advice and sailed east. they landed in the land of israel, which crusaders held then, visited jerusalem, and went on to egypt. they settled in fustat, the old city next to cairo, and he lived there the rest of his life. his younger brother david ben maimon traded in precious stones and supported everyone while moses studied and wrote. then david drowned, and the money went with him. what that did to him is below. afterward he went to work as a doctor, because he thought it was a sin to make a living from religion. he was appointed physician to the chief secretary of saladin, the sultan who ruled egypt and took jerusalem back from the crusaders in 1187. many accounts say he treated saladin himself, though the older encyclopedias name only the secretary. he stayed a court doctor under the sultans who came after, and for stretches he was also the official head of egypt's jewish community.\nthe books came out of those same years. first a commentary on the mishnah, the oldest written collection of jewish oral law, from around the year 200. he started it at twenty three and finished it in egypt in 1168, at thirty. inside it are his thirteen principles, a list of what a jew has to believe. it starts with god existing, being one, and having no body. then the mishneh torah, written in hebrew between about 1170 and 1180. it's fourteen books that set out all of jewish law, everything in the torah, the first five books of the hebrew bible, and in the talmud, the huge record of the rabbis arguing over the mishnah. he gives the ruling and leaves out the argument and the sources. someone who'd read the torah and then his book, he wrote, wouldn't need any other. then, around 1190, the guide for the perplexed, written in judeo-arabic, which is arabic in hebrew letters. it's set up as a long letter to a former student, joseph ben judah, who couldn't see how to keep both the torah and the science of aristotle, the ancient greek philosopher muslim scholars called the first teacher. its most famous move is about god. you can't say what god is, he argues, only what god is not, and every no gets you a little closer.\nhe died in fustat in december 1204. one old encyclopedia says jews and muslims there mourned in public for three days. the medieval sources say his body was carried to tiberias, a town on the sea of galilee, though nothing from the time itself records the move. the tomb there carries the line people still use for him: from moses to moses there arose none like moses. the first one is [[moses]] of the bible. christians read him too. thomas aquinas cites him as rabbi moses, and his way of talking about god by saying what god isn't fed into [[eckhart|meister eckhart]].","struggle":"david was the younger brother, and moses had been his teacher. david did the trading so moses could write. around 1170 he crossed the desert to a port in what's now sudan, didn't like the goods on offer, and decided to sail on to india, which moses had told him not to do. his last letter home survived in the cairo geniza, the storeroom of a synagogue in fustat where old papers piled up for centuries. it tells moses not to worry, because he who saved him from the desert will save him on the sea. the ship went down in the indian ocean. nobody's sure of the year, and the dates given run from 1169 to 1177. the family's savings went down with him, along with money other merchants had trusted him with.\nabout eight years later moses wrote to a judge he'd known in the land of israel, who had never sent a word of sympathy. he called it the greatest misfortune of his entire life. david had left him a little daughter and a widow to look after. on the day the news came, he wrote, he fell ill and stayed in bed for about a year, with a sore boil, fever and depression, and was almost given up. eight years on he was still mourning and unable to accept consolation. he grew up on my knees, he wrote. he was my brother, he was my student.\nthe other thing he never got on top of was the work. in september 1199, in his sixties, he wrote to samuel ibn tibbon, who was translating the guide into hebrew in the south of france and wanted to sail over and talk it through. don't risk the voyage, he said, you'd see me and get little else out of it. every morning he rode from fustat to the sultan in cairo. he came home in the afternoon almost dying with hunger and found the waiting rooms full, jews and non-jews, nobles and common people, judges and bailiffs, friends and foes. he ate the only meal he took in twenty four hours, then saw patients until nightfall and past it, talking to them and writing prescriptions while lying down from sheer fatigue. by night he could scarcely speak. the only day his own community could get to him was the sabbath.","threads":["grief","exile","work","family"],"years":"1138 to 1204, though older books say he was born in 1135"},{"after":"mecca was the after. in april 1964 he made the hajj and wrote a letter home saying he'd eaten from the same plate and prayed beside muslims whose eyes were the bluest of blue and whose hair was the blondest of blond, and it had made him rethink race. he still thought america was racist to the bone. he stopped saying white people were devils, and he said he'd let himself be used to make sweeping indictments of all white people and wouldn't do it again.\nhe had less than a year left. he started two organizations, traveled through africa, talked about taking america to the united nations over human rights, went to selma, and dictated the rest of his life to alex haley, the writer who'd later write roots. he knew they were coming for him. he kept going to the meetings.","basically":"a kid whose father died under a streetcar in what the family always believed was a murder, and whose mother was locked in a state hospital, who became a street hustler in boston and harlem, went to prison at twenty, taught himself words by copying the dictionary, and came out the fiercest voice of black anger in america. for twelve years he preached that white people were devils. then he went on the pilgrimage to mecca, prayed next to blue-eyed muslims, publicly changed his mind, and was shot dead less than a year later. he's for anyone who's had to admit in public that they were wrong about something big, with enemies watching.","born":1925,"died":1965,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--teal)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><rect x='20' y='70' width='170' height='180' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='90' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 90 192 L 90 234'/><path d='M 90 234 C 70 234 60 250 76 250 L 104 250 C 120 250 110 234 90 234'/><path d='M 90 208 L 76 234 M 90 208 L 104 234'/><path d='M 64 228 L 90 234 L 116 228 L 116 244 L 90 250 L 64 244 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 90 234 L 90 250'/><rect x='134' y='244' width='34' height='6' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='136' y='238' width='30' height='6' fill='var(--blue)'/><rect x='134' y='232' width='34' height='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 20 70 L 190 70 M 50 70 L 50 250 M 80 70 L 80 250 M 110 70 L 110 250 M 140 70 L 140 250 M 170 70 L 170 250' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 196 150 C 232 110 272 124 300 176' stroke-dasharray='2 9' opacity='.5'/><rect x='290' y='190' width='50' height='60' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><rect x='290' y='204' width='50' height='6' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='256' cy='232' r='5'/><circle cx='272' cy='236' r='5'/><circle cx='358' cy='232' r='5'/><circle cx='374' cy='236' r='5'/><path d='M 256 237 L 256 250 M 272 241 L 272 250 M 358 237 L 358 250 M 374 241 L 374 250'/><g transform='translate(140,210.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he preached for twelve years that white people were devils, went to mecca, and wrote home that he'd had to throw out some of his conclusions.","key":"malcolmx","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malcolm_X","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Autobiography_of_Malcolm_X","why":"the book he told to alex haley"},{"label":"the king institute","url":"https://kinginstitute.stanford.edu/malcolm-x","why":"stanford's page on him and king"}],"lineage":"muslim","mess":"he was a thief and a hustler and a drug dealer and he robbed houses, and he told all of it himself in the autobiography. then for twelve years he preached the nation's teaching that white people were a race of devils, and meant it. in 1962, when a plane full of white art patrons from atlanta crashed in paris, he called it a beautiful thing and said god had answered their prayers.\nhe said ugly things about jews in those years too, in speeches and interviews. he called king an uncle tom, the old insult for a black man who bows and scrapes to white people.","moments":["in prison he started at the first page of the dictionary and copied every word. he said later that months passed without his even thinking about being in prison, and that he'd never been so free in his life.","on march 26, 1964, he and [[king|martin luther king]] crossed paths in the capitol during the senate fight over the civil rights act, the law that outlawed segregation in public places. they shook hands, a photographer caught it, and that was the only time they met.","in 2021, fifty six years after the killing, two of the three men convicted of it were exonerated. they'd spent decades in prison for something they didn't do."],"name":"malcolm x","place":"omaha, lansing, boston, harlem, mecca","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><rect x='41' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><rect x='62' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><path d='M 58 55 L 62 55 M 41 54 L 34 52 M 79 54 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 46 72 C 52 66 58 70 60 72 C 62 70 68 66 74 72 C 68 76 62 74 60 74 C 58 74 52 76 46 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i have been forced to rearrange much of my thought patterns and to toss aside some of my previous conclusions.","where":"his letter home from mecca, april 1964, paraphrased slightly"},{"q":"by any means necessary.","where":"the founding rally of the organization of afro-american unity, the group he started after he left the nation, june 1964, and a phrase he used often"},{"q":"i'm for truth, no matter who tells it.","where":"the autobiography of malcolm x, the book he told to the writer alex haley, the last chapter"}],"start":"the autobiography of malcolm x, as told to alex haley, the chapter called saved and the chapter called mecca. then manning marable's malcolm x: a life of reinvention, the big 2011 biography, for the corrections.","status":"approved","story":"malcolm little was born in omaha in 1925. his father, earl, was a baptist preacher who organized for marcus garvey, the jamaican-born leader who told black people to be proud of africa and build their own businesses and institutions, and the family was burned out of a house in lansing, michigan. in 1931 his father died under a streetcar. the police called it an accident. the family believed white supremacists killed him. his mother held on for years, then broke down, and in 1939 she was committed to a state mental hospital. the kids went to foster homes.\nhe was smart. when he told a teacher he wanted to be a lawyer, the teacher told him that was no realistic goal for a black kid and he should be a carpenter. by his late teens he was in boston and then harlem, where they called him detroit red, hustling, running numbers, an illegal street lottery, selling drugs, robbing houses. in 1946 he got eight to ten years for burglary.\nin prison his brothers and sisters wrote to him about the nation of islam and its leader, elijah muhammad. the nation was a black american religious movement that used the name of islam but taught its own things, including that white people were a race of devils, and that black people should live separately and build their own world. elijah muhammad said he was god's messenger. malcolm wanted to write to him and couldn't write well enough, so he copied the dictionary out by hand, page by page. he came out in 1952 and took the x for the african family name he'd never know, since little was a name from slavery.\nhe built the nation from a few hundred members into tens of thousands and became the most feared voice in america. in 1964 he broke with elijah muhammad, made the hajj, the pilgrimage to mecca in saudi arabia every muslim who can is supposed to make once in their life, and came back as el-hajj malik el-shabazz. on february 21, 1965, he was shot to death on the stage of the audubon ballroom in upper manhattan, in front of his wife betty and their daughters. he was thirty nine.","struggle":"prison was the floor. twenty years old, in charlestown state prison, in a cell with no plumbing, so angry and so profane that the other inmates called him satan. he tells it himself.\nthen the break. in 1963 he learned that elijah muhammad, the man he'd called god's messenger, had fathered children with women who worked for him as secretaries, and that the nation meant to keep it quiet. after kennedy was killed malcolm said the chickens had come home to roost, meaning america's violence had come back around on it, and elijah banned him from speaking for ninety days. by 1964 he was out, and he knew the nation wanted him dead. his house was firebombed a week before he was killed.","threads":["prison","anger","doubt"],"years":"1925 to 1965"},{"after":"the after is the memoir. old, sick, needing a co-writer to get it on paper, he told the whole thing, the drinking, the lies, the marriage, and called it all is grace. there's no clean ending. he didn't get sober for good and ride off. he just stopped hiding.\nwhich is the ragamuffin gospel with the lights on. he'd spent twenty years telling people god loved them in the gutter, and at the end he let everyone see the gutter he'd been writing from the whole time.","basically":"a funny, charming ex-marine who became a catholic priest, fought the bottle his whole life, left the priesthood to get married, and in 1990 wrote the book that told a generation of church kids that god loved them as screwups, not after they got it together. then, old and sick, he wrote a memoir admitting he'd been drinking far more than anyone knew the whole time he was on the road preaching that, and lying to cover it. if you've ever sat in church feeling like the only fraud in the room, he was the guy on the stage, and at the end he said so.","born":1934,"died":2013,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 250 250 L 250 150 L 370 150 L 370 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 240 152 L 310 110 L 380 152'/><path d='M 270 250 L 270 196 L 300 196 L 300 250' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='294' cy='224' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><rect x='320' y='175' width='30' height='26' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 335 175 L 335 201 M 320 188 L 350 188'/><circle cx='285' cy='184' r='5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='170' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 170 192 L 170 234'/><path d='M 170 234 C 150 234 140 250 156 250 L 184 250 C 200 250 190 234 170 234'/><path d='M 170 208 L 156 234 M 170 208 L 184 234'/><path d='M 198 243 L 220 243 L 226 245.5 L 234 245.5 L 234 248.5 L 226 248.5 L 220 250 L 198 250 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><g opacity='.5'><path d='M 60 40 L 54 56 M 110 30 L 104 46 M 160 50 L 154 66 M 210 34 L 204 50 M 90 90 L 84 106 M 190 100 L 184 116 M 140 120 L 134 136 M 60 140 L 54 156'/><\/g><g transform='translate(90,232.0) scale(1.2)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he wrote the book on grace for failures, kept failing, and admitted at the end that he'd lied about it.","key":"manning","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brennan_Manning","why":"the plain overview of his life and the ragamuffin gospel"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"drunk, over and over. left the priesthood and broke his vows, including the promise never to marry, and then the marriage broke too. and he lied, to friends and to readers, about how sober he was. he put the lying in his own memoir, which is how anybody knows.","moments":["in the late sixties he spent time with the little brothers of jesus, a small religious order that follows charles de foucauld, a french soldier who became a hermit in the sahara. they work ordinary jobs and pray among the poor. he writes about months alone in a cave in the spanish desert.","the words all is grace are also how the diary of a country priest ends, a 1936 french novel by georges bernanos about a sick young priest in a village that doesn't want him. it's the priest's last words as he dies.","rich mullins loved the ragamuffin gospel so much he called his group a ragamuffin band. manning had once led him on a three-day silent retreat, and they stayed friends for life."],"name":"brennan manning","place":"new york, then new orleans, then the road","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 57 56 L 63 56 M 43 55 L 34 53 M 77 55 L 86 53' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"my deepest awareness of myself is that i am deeply loved by jesus christ and i have done nothing to earn it or deserve it.","where":"the ragamuffin gospel, his 1990 book"},{"q":"the gospel of grace is for the bedraggled, beat-up, and burnt-out.","where":"the ragamuffin gospel, his 1990 book, the opening pages, paraphrased"},{"q":"god loves you as you are, not as you should be, because you're never going to be as you should be.","where":"from a filmed talk, lightly paraphrased. there he says it as jesus speaking: i'll love you as you are, not as you should be"}],"start":"the ragamuffin gospel, the first chapter. then all is grace, which is the same man telling you what was going on while he wrote the first one.","status":"approved","story":"born richard manning in new york in 1934. a marine at the tail end of the korean war, who by his own memoir spent it writing for a military newspaper in japan, then a franciscan priest, ordained in 1963. the franciscans are the order of friars that goes back to [[francis]] of assisi, men who promise to own nothing. he took the name brennan along the way. he was funny, charming, and a very good preacher.\nhe was also an alcoholic. he went into treatment in the seventies, got sober, relapsed, got sober, relapsed. in 1982 he left the franciscans and the priesthood to marry a woman named roslyn, which a catholic priest isn't allowed to do. the marriage ended in divorce years later.\nthe ragamuffin gospel came out in 1990 and found everybody who'd been told they weren't good enough for church. a ragamuffin is a dirty, ragged kid. its whole argument is that grace, god's love that you can't earn and can't lose, is for the bedraggled, the beat up, and the burnt out, first. it was huge among evangelicals, the born again, bible-first protestants, a lot of whom had grown up being told to try harder. the singer rich mullins, who wrote the worship song awesome god, named his band after it.\nhe spent the next twenty years on the road preaching that to crowds. in 2011, old and failing, he put out a memoir called all is grace, which says he'd been drinking more than anyone knew and lying to cover it. he died in new jersey in 2013.","struggle":"the drinking never let go of him. he'd preach grace to a room of thousands and go back to the hotel and drink alone. all is grace, the memoir, is frank about relapses he hid for years while the books kept selling.\nand under that, the shame about the shame. the man famous for telling everyone god loved them as they were kept hiding how he really was, and the memoir says so.","threads":["addiction","forgiveness"],"years":"1934 to 2013"},{"after":"when cassius was killed, cassius dio, a roman historian who lived through those years, says marcus was sorry he hadn't had the chance to pardon him, and burned the man's letters without reading them so he wouldn't have to know who else had been involved. he forgave the cities that had backed the revolt.\nand he kept writing, and he kept going back to the front. there's no turn in his story. he got up every morning and did the job, which is what the notebook keeps telling him to do, and he died doing it. the notebook doesn't pretend he was good at being who he wanted to be. that's why it's still useful.","basically":"a roman emperor who spent the last ten years of his life in cold army camps running a war he never wanted, and at night wrote in a private notebook telling himself to be patient, be decent, and remember he'd be dead soon. he never meant anyone to read it, and now it's on every nightstand from locker rooms to silicon valley. picture the most powerful person on earth keeping a notes app full of pep talks to himself, and the pep talks turn out to be good. take from him that the people running the world have to talk themselves out of bed too.","born":121,"died":180,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 268 C 60 258 100 278 140 268 S 220 258 260 268 S 340 278 380 268'/><path d='M 20 242 L 380 242'/><path d='M 50 242 L 110 130 L 170 242 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 110 130 L 110 242' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 96 242 L 110 200 L 124 242' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='230' cy='170' r='14'/><path d='M 230 184 L 230 226'/><path d='M 230 226 C 210 226 200 242 216 242 L 244 242 C 260 242 250 226 230 226'/><path d='M 230 200 L 216 226 M 230 200 L 244 226'/><path d='M 214 222 L 246 214 L 250 226 L 218 234 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 280 242 C 280 232 300 232 300 242 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 290 232 C 286 226 290 220 290 216 C 294 222 296 226 290 232 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='330' cy='60' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='250' cy='40' r='2'/><circle cx='200' cy='80' r='2'/><circle cx='370' cy='110' r='2'/><\/g><g transform='translate(110,108.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the most powerful man in the world kept a notebook telling himself to get out of bed.","key":"marcus","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marcus_Aurelius","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the meditations, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/2680","why":"an old translation of the notebook at project gutenberg"},{"label":"stanford encyclopedia","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/stoicism/","why":"the stoic ideas he was living by, laid out properly"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"christians were killed under him. in 177 at lyon, in gaul, which is roughly france, a mob and then the authorities turned on the christian community. a slave girl named blandina and an old bishop named pothinus were among the dead. blandina was tortured from morning to night, and in the end thrown to a wild bull in the arena. the governor wrote to the emperor asking what to do with the ones who were roman citizens, and the answer, as the church historian eusebius preserves it about a hundred and fifty years later, was that the ones who denied the faith should go free and the ones who confessed should be executed. that's his answer, not somebody else's.\nand commodus. marcus made his teenage son co-emperor in 177 and left him everything. the four emperors before marcus had each picked the best man they could find and adopted him. marcus had a son, and picked him. commodus was a disaster, vain and cruel, who fought in the arena as a gladiator and was strangled in 192. he's the villain in the movie gladiator, loosely.","moments":["the first book of the meditations is just thanks. he goes through his grandfather, his mother, his teachers, one at a time, and says what each of them taught him. he thanks his teacher rusticus for lending him his copy of the lectures of [[epictetus]], the old slave turned teacher.","the bronze statue of him on horseback in rome survived the middle ages because people thought it was constantine, the first christian emperor, and didn't melt it down.","a line in book 11 sneers at people who die for a cause out of stubbornness, like the christians. some scholars think a later copyist added the word christians. either way, he didn't think much of them."],"name":"marcus aurelius","place":"rome, then army camps on the danube","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 32 50 C 36 36 48 30 60 30 C 72 30 84 36 88 50' opacity='.7'/><g fill='var(--sage)'><ellipse cx='36' cy='42' rx='3' ry='6' transform='rotate(-40 36 42)'/><ellipse cx='44' cy='35' rx='3' ry='6' transform='rotate(-60 44 35)'/><ellipse cx='53' cy='31' rx='3' ry='6' transform='rotate(-80 53 31)'/><ellipse cx='84' cy='42' rx='3' ry='6' transform='rotate(40 84 42)'/><ellipse cx='76' cy='35' rx='3' ry='6' transform='rotate(60 76 35)'/><ellipse cx='67' cy='31' rx='3' ry='6' transform='rotate(80 67 31)'/><\/g><path d='M 16 112 L 46 104 M 16 112 C 12 116 20 120 20 114 M 46 104 C 50 100 44 98 44 104' /><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"at dawn, when you don't want to get up, tell yourself, i'm getting up to do the work of a human being.","where":"the meditations, his private notebook, book 5, section 1, paraphrased"},{"q":"first thing in the morning, tell yourself you'll meet meddling, ungrateful, arrogant people today, and none of them can actually hurt you.","where":"meditations, book 2, section 1, paraphrased"},{"q":"see that you don't turn into a caesar, that you don't get dyed purple.","where":"meditations, book 6, section 30, paraphrased. purple was the emperor's color, and he's warning himself not to let the job soak in"}],"start":"meditations, book 2, section 1, then all of book 5. gregory hays's translation reads like it was written this year.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in rome in 121 to a rich family and noticed young by the emperor hadrian, the one who built the wall across northern england. roman emperors often picked their heirs by adoption instead of blood, and hadrian arranged it so marcus would be emperor one day. he wanted to be a philosopher. he got the empire instead, in 161, and for the first eight years he shared it with his adoptive brother lucius verus.\nnothing went right. a war with parthia, the rival empire in what's now iran. the soldiers came home carrying a plague, probably smallpox, that killed people across the empire for years. then the germanic tribes came over the danube, the river that was rome's northern border, and he spent most of the last decade of his life in army camps along it, a sick, tired man in his fifties running a war he never wanted.\nin those camps he wrote in greek, the language educated romans did their thinking in, a notebook for himself that we call the meditations. he never meant anyone to read it. book two is headed among the quadi, one of the tribes he was fighting, on the river gran, in what's now slovakia. book three, at carnuntum, a legion base near what's now vienna. it's mostly him talking himself down. be kind to the people who'll annoy you today. you're going to die soon. do the next thing well.\nhe died on campaign in 180 and left the empire to his son commodus. the notebook survived by luck and wasn't printed until the sixteenth century.","struggle":"his family died around him. his wife faustina bore him at least thirteen children and most of them died young, before him. faustina herself died in 175 at a village in cappadocia, in the middle of what's now turkey, while traveling with him in the east. he renamed the place after her.\nthe same year his best general in the east, avidius cassius, heard a false rumor that marcus was dead and declared himself emperor. his own officers killed him three months later. marcus spent the last years of his life sick, at war, and grieving, and the notebook is what that sounds like from inside.","threads":["war","grief","dying","work"],"years":"121 to 180"},{"after":"there wasn't time for much of an after. he got sick, and he spent the last year doing what he'd always done, talking, a little more quietly.\nhis brother dennis wrote the brotherhood of the screaming abyss, in 2012, and told the whole story, the genius and the con, with love. that book is the honest after. terence never wrote it himself.","basically":"a funny, brilliant, fast talking guy from colorado who went up the amazon with his brother in 1971, came back with wild ideas, and became the voice of psychedelics in the nineties. his talks were passed around on cassettes and now fill youtube. he said magic mushrooms made us human and that time would end in 2012. neither is true. picture the most charismatic podcaster you've ever heard, mixing real knowledge with things he made up, and never letting you see the seam. enjoy him, and check everything.","born":1946,"died":2000,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 C 100 240 180 260 260 250 S 360 244 380 250 L 380 290 L 20 290 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 60 240 L 60 115'/><ellipse cx='60' cy='103' rx='35' ry='26' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 340 236 L 340 126'/><ellipse cx='340' cy='116' rx='30' ry='23' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='170' cy='120' r='14'/><path d='M 170 134 L 170 182'/><path d='M 170 182 L 156 216 M 170 182 L 184 216'/><path d='M 170 148 L 144 150 M 170 148 L 196 150'/><path d='M 165 125 Q 170 129 175 125'/><path d='M 200 110 C 214 100 222 114 236 104 C 250 94 258 110 272 100 M 204 126 C 222 120 232 132 250 124' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 230 268 C 250 280 300 280 320 268 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(46,128.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the best talker psychedelics ever had spent the nineties telling people to take huge doses alone in the dark, while privately too scared to take them himself.","key":"mckenna","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terence_McKenna","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stoned_ape_theory","why":"his idea about human evolution, and why scientists don't accept it"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"he's sloppy with the truth, and that's his part in it. he mixed real botany and history with things that were just wrong, like the stoned ape idea and the 2012 prediction, and delivered all of it in the same confident voice. people took his word for it and still do.\nworse, all through the nineties, while he told crowds to take heroic doses alone in the dark, he'd stopped taking mushrooms himself after a trip that scared him. he didn't tell them that. his brother did, twelve years after he died. the risks he talked around are real. terror, psychotic breaks in people prone to them, and the danger of doing it alone with no one there.","moments":["in 1985 he and his partner kathleen harrison started botanical dimensions, a garden in hawaii to save medicinal plants from the rainforest.","people recorded his talks on cassette and traded them, and a lot of them are still circulating, sometimes set to space footage."],"name":"terence mckenna","place":"colorado, berkeley, the amazon, hawaii","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 26 62 L 18 40 L 32 46 L 30 28 L 42 40 L 48 24 L 56 38 L 64 22 L 72 38 L 80 26 L 86 42 L 100 30 L 92 48 L 104 52 L 90 62' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><rect x='41' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><rect x='62' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><path d='M 58 55 L 62 55 M 41 54 L 34 52 M 79 54 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"nature loves courage.","where":"his talks in the nineties, attributed; it's the line people quote most"},{"q":"culture is not your friend.","where":"his talks, attributed"},{"q":"the mushroom was the catalyst that made us human.","where":"food of the gods, 1992, paraphrased. this is the stoned ape idea, and it's not accepted science"}],"start":"the brotherhood of the screaming abyss, his brother dennis's memoir, before anything terence wrote. then food of the gods if you want to see how he argued, with the warning that the big idea in it is wrong.","status":"approved","story":"he grew up in a small town in colorado, got into science fiction and plants, and studied ecology and shamanism at berkeley in the late sixties. shamanism is the old practice, in many cultures, of a healer who goes into altered states for the community.\nin 1971 he and his younger brother dennis, with a few friends, went to la chorrera in the colombian amazon looking for a plant drug. what happened there, by their account, was mostly mushrooms and a strange experiment built around mushrooms and sound, after which dennis was in a kind of breakdown for days. the brothers wrote a book about it, the invisible landscape, in 1975. dennis became a respected ethnopharmacologist, a scientist who studies plant drugs. terence became a talker.\nby the late eighties he was the star of the lecture circuit, at esalen, the retreat center on the big sur coast, at raves, on radio. he pushed what he called the heroic dose, his phrase for taking a lot, alone, in silent darkness. his book food of the gods, from 1992, put forward the stoned ape idea, that early humans eating mushrooms on the african plains gave us language and consciousness. it's not accepted science. there's no evidence for it. he also built a theory that time itself would climax in december 2012. it didn't.\nin 1999 he had a seizure, and it was a brain tumor, glioblastoma, the worst kind. he died in california in april 2000, at fifty three, talking about death to the end.","struggle":"at the end of the eighties, by his brother's account, he had a bad trip on mushrooms, a terrifying vision of total meaninglessness, and he was badly shaken. dennis wrote in 2012 that terence never took mushrooms again after it, and only rarely and nervously took ayahuasca or dmt.\nthen the tumor in 1999. he'd spent his life talking about death as the great adventure, and he got about a year to find out. in his last interviews he was honest that he was frightened and also curious.","threads":["psychedelics","fame","dying","wandering"],"years":"1946 to 2000"},{"after":"he ended it. in september 1966 he made a formal, permanent commitment to live as a hermit, alone in a cinder block cabin in the woods up the hill from the monastery, and kept it. he didn't burn the journals. he ordered them published twenty five years after his death, all of it, so nobody could turn him into a plaster saint.\nand then he went east, which was the answer to the other doubt. the last big thing he wrote in his journal came at polonnaruwa, a ruined old city in sri lanka with enormous buddhas carved out of the rock. he wrote that he'd finally seen what he'd been looking for and the whole thing was clear. a week later he was dead.","basically":"a wild young guy in the 1930s, drinking and chasing women through two universities, who walked into a silent monastery in the kentucky woods at twenty six and wrote a memoir about it that became a bestseller. he spent the rest of his life as the most famous monk in america, writing against war and racism from a place he wasn't supposed to leave, and falling in love once when he wasn't supposed to. he's the catholic who went looking for god in buddhism without leaving his own church. if you want to know whether you can go all the way into one tradition and still learn from the others, he's the proof.","born":1915,"died":1968,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 60 250 L 60 170 L 150 170 L 150 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 172 L 105 130 L 160 172'/><path d='M 92 250 L 92 200 L 120 200 L 120 250' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='106' cy='200' r='9'/><path d='M 106 214 L 106 256'/><path d='M 106 256 C 86 256 76 272 92 272 L 120 272 C 136 272 126 256 106 256'/><path d='M 106 230 L 92 256 M 106 230 L 120 256'/><path d='M 150 250 C 200 245 240 255 290 250 S 360 245 390 250' stroke-dasharray='3 9' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 250 120 L 290 108 L 296 132 L 256 144 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 120 L 273 132 L 290 108'/><path d='M 200 150 C 220 130 235 125 250 122' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='340' cy='70' r='20' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 368 70 L 378 70'/><path d='M 312 70 L 302 70'/><path d='M 340 98 L 340 108'/><path d='M 340 42 L 340 32'/><path d='M 360 90 L 367 97'/><path d='M 320 90 L 313 97'/><path d='M 360 50 L 367 43'/><path d='M 320 50 L 313 43'/><g transform='translate(35,220.3) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the most famous monk in america wrote down everything, including the parts a monk isn't supposed to have.","key":"merton","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Merton","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the merton center","url":"https://merton.org/","why":"the thomas merton center at bellarmine university in louisville, which keeps his papers"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Seven_Storey_Mountain","why":"the memoir that made him famous"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"in 1966, at fifty one and a monk for twenty five years, he fell in love with a student nurse half his age while in the hospital for back surgery. he saw her secretly for months and wrote it all in his journal, which he ordered published after his death. he ended it, and he told on himself. the child he fathered in england and never acknowledged is the part he never fixed.","moments":["in 1937 he bought a book on medieval philosophy, saw the imprimatur inside the cover, the catholic church's official stamp saying a book is approved, and nearly threw it out the train window. he read it instead. it was the beginning.","he entered the monastery on december 10, 1941, three days after pearl harbor, the japanese attack that pulled america into world war two, and died on december 10, 1968. he'd have noticed.","joan baez, the folk singer, drove out to see him at the hermitage in december 1966 and they talked for an afternoon. he was in the middle of the worst year of his life and didn't tell her."],"name":"thomas merton","place":"france, new york, a monastery in kentucky","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i have no idea where i am going. i do not see the road ahead of me. but i believe the desire to please you does in fact please you.","where":"thoughts in solitude, his short 1958 book of reflections, the famous prayer, paraphrased"},{"q":"at the corner of fourth and walnut i suddenly realized i loved all these people, that they were mine and i theirs.","where":"conjectures of a guilty bystander, his 1966 book of notebook entries, paraphrased. fourth and walnut is a busy street corner in downtown louisville"},{"q":"nhat hanh is my brother. he is more my brother than many who are nearer to me by race and nationality.","where":"his 1966 essay, nhat hanh is my brother"}],"start":"the seven storey mountain if you want the story. new seeds of contemplation, his short book on what praying quietly actually is, if you want the teaching. the asian journal, his notebooks from the last trip, if you want the end.","status":"approved","story":"orphaned young, his mother dead when he was six and his father when he was sixteen. he drank and slept around through cambridge in england, where he fathered a child he never met, and then columbia in new york. then in 1941 he walked into a trappist monastery in kentucky, the abbey of gethsemani, and stayed twenty seven years. trappists are the strictest kind of catholic monk, up in the middle of the night to pray, mostly silent, farming, owning nothing. the seven storey mountain, his memoir of getting there, named for the mountain souls climb to get clean in dante's old poem, came out in 1948 and became a bestseller, and a lot of young men joined monasteries because of it.\nhe never stopped writing, which trappists mostly don't do. seventy books, thousands of letters. by the early sixties he was writing against the bomb, against racism, against the war, and his order kept telling him to knock it off.\nthen he turned east. he read the zen masters, the chinese and japanese buddhist teachers famous for sitting still and saying short strange things. he wrote letters to d.t. suzuki, the japanese scholar who more or less introduced zen to the west, and met him in new york in 1964. he met [[thay|thich nhat hanh]], a vietnamese buddhist monk, at his monastery in 1966 and called him his brother. in 1968 he finally got permission to go to asia. he met the dalai lama, the head of tibetan buddhism, living in exile in india, three times in dharamsala that november. on december 10 in bangkok, after giving a talk, he was found dead in his room, apparently electrocuted by a faulty fan. same day of the year he'd entered the monastery.\nhe's the bridge. almost every christian on these pages who talks to the east walked across him.","struggle":"he spent years wanting out. not out of the church, out of gethsemani, his monastery, to a stricter and lonelier order, the carthusians, monks who live alone in separate cells and barely see each other. his abbot, the head of the monastery, kept saying no. he wrote about it in his journal like a man scratching at a locked door.\nthe affair in 1966 wasn't a slip. it was months. he wrote that he'd never been loved like that and didn't know what to do with it. he was fifty one and he'd been a monk for twenty five years and he was, by his own account, a fool in love.\nunder all of it was a doubt he never fully put down. was the famous monk, the bestseller, the letters from everywhere, anything to do with god at all, or had he just found a more interesting way to be thomas merton.","threads":["silence","love","fame"],"years":"1915 to 1968"},{"after":"the after is the whole rest of his life. he didn't build a monastery or write a rulebook. he sat in caves and sang, and people came up the mountain to hear a murderer explain how it feels to put it down.\nat the end, the story goes, a jealous geshe, a tibetan monk with the equivalent of a doctorate in religion, who couldn't stand a cave singer getting all the devotion, had him poisoned. milarepa knew, took the poison anyway, and the man, once he saw what he'd done, took milarepa as his teacher. that's the last song.","basically":"a tibetan kid in the eleventh century whose uncle robbed his family blind after his father died, so his mother sent him off to learn black magic, and, the story goes, he used it to kill thirty five people. then the guilt ate him alive. he spent years hauling rocks for a brutal teacher and decades alone in mountain caves, half starved, singing what he'd learned. he's tibet's favorite saint. if you think you've done something too bad to come back from, he's the one they point to.","born":1040,"died":1123,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 200 L 90 120 L 150 180 L 210 100 L 280 190 L 340 130 L 380 180' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 120 260 C 110 190 190 170 230 200 C 260 220 250 260 250 260 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='185' cy='202' r='12' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 185 216 L 185 258'/><path d='M 185 258 C 165 258 155 274 171 274 L 199 274 C 215 274 205 258 185 258'/><path d='M 185 232 L 171 258 M 185 232 L 199 258'/><path d='M 20 260 L 380 260'/><path d='M 300 260 L 300 200 L 340 200 L 340 260 M 300 220 L 340 220 M 300 240 L 340 240 M 320 200 L 320 220 M 310 220 L 310 240 M 330 220 L 330 240'/><path d='M 306 200 L 306 186 L 322 186' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(60,239.0) scale(1.4)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the story says he killed thirty five people with black magic. what he did next made him tibet's favorite saint.","key":"milarepa","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milarepa","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marpa_Lotsawa","why":"the teacher who made him build the towers"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"thirty five people, the story says, killed on purpose, as a young man. it doesn't get worse than that, and he spent the rest of his life answering for it. the story is the point. nobody's too far gone, and the proof is a murderer.","moments":["most paintings of him have his right hand cupped at his ear. some say he's listening to the sounds of nature. some say it's just how a singer holds his hand.","his two great students were rechungpa and gampopa, a doctor who became a monk after his wife and child died, and who turned the cave songs into an actual lineage, a line of teachers passed hand to hand. it's called the kagyu, one of the four big schools of tibetan buddhism, and mingyur rinpoche comes out of it.","he wore a single cotton robe through the himalayan winter, kept warm by a meditation practice for making heat inside the body. the tibetan word for someone who lives like that in cotton is repa. that's the second half of his name. mila was his family's."],"name":"milarepa","place":"the mountains of western tibet","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 92 60 C 104 52 110 62 106 72 C 102 80 92 78 90 70' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"my religion is to live and die without regret.","where":"attributed to him everywhere; i couldn't find it in any translation of his songs"},{"q":"in the beginning nothing comes. in the middle nothing stays. in the end nothing goes.","where":"attributed to him by modern meditation writers; i couldn't find it in any translation of his songs"}],"start":"the life of milarepa, written down around 1488, about three hundred and fifty years after he died, by tsangnyön heruka, a wandering tibetan yogi, somebody who meditates full time out in the wild, known as the madman of tsang. read it in lobsang lhalungpa's or andrew quintman's translation. then dip into the hundred thousand songs.","status":"approved","story":"nearly all of this comes from a life story written about three hundred and fifty years after he died, and very little is known about the real man. in the story, his father died when he was a boy, and an uncle and aunt took the family's land and everything in it and worked his mother and sister like servants. his mother never forgave it. she sent him off to learn black magic for revenge, the kind of curse-for-hire sorcery tibetans took completely seriously then. he learned it. the story says he brought a house down on his uncle's family during a wedding feast and killed thirty five people, then wrecked the village's harvest with a hailstorm.\nthe guilt got him. he went looking for a teacher who'd take a murderer and found marpa, a married man with a temper who'd made the dangerous trek over the himalayas to india more than once to bring buddhist teachings back to tibet and translate them. marpa refused to teach him anything. instead he made him build a stone tower by hand, alone, then tear it down and carry the stones back where they came from, then build another somewhere else, again and again. milarepa's back was one open sore. he nearly gave up. marpa finally gave him the teachings, the meditation instructions he'd carried back from india, and told him what the towers had been for. all that hauling had been working off his karma, the weight of what he'd done coming back to him.\nhe went into the caves in the mountains and stayed. there was no food but the nettles growing outside, and he ate so many that his skin turned green. he sang everything he understood, and the songs are still sung. hunters and villagers came across him and he sang to them, and some of them stayed.\nwhen the songs talk about how good it feels to sit alone in a cave with nothing, they're coming from a man who had a mass killing to work off. that's the whole reason tibet loves him. nobody's too far gone. imagine the most beloved preacher in your town being a man who'd killed people and told you so every time he opened his mouth.","struggle":"the guilt was the engine. after the killings he couldn't sleep, and the story has him going first to another teacher, who sent him on to marpa.\nmarpa took him and broke him. build the tower, tear it down, build it over there, tear it down, his back an open sore, and every time he asked for the teachings marpa cursed him and threw him out. marpa's wife, dagmema, felt sorry for him and forged a letter in marpa's name sending him to another of marpa's students, a teacher named ngokpa. milarepa meditated there and nothing happened, because it wasn't the teachings that needed fixing.\nthen years alone in caves with nothing. his sister found him once, green and skeletal from living on nettles, and wept.","threads":["anger","forgiveness","work"],"years":"around 1040 to 1123, or 1052 to 1135 in the old life story"},{"after":"what he did with the panic was stop running from it. he let it in, looked at it, and it came apart, because it had needed the running. that's the whole teaching and it came from a scared kid, not a sage.\nwhat he did with the shame at the train station was hold the bowl out anyway. what he did with the dying at kushinagar, where the buddha died, was let go of the last thing, being a rinpoche, being somebody, and he says the joy that showed up was the point of the whole trip. then he walked back into the monastery four and a half years later, sat down, and went back to work.","basically":"a tibetan monk born in 1975 who had panic attacks as a kid and figured out how to stop them by sitting still and letting them in. now he teaches that all over the world with a big grin. in his thirties he walked out on his own monasteries in the night to live as a beggar, and nearly died doing it. if you've ever had your chest lock up at 3am, he's the one on these pages who actually knows what that's like.","born":1975,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='200' cy='120' r='14'/><path d='M 200 134 L 200 176'/><path d='M 200 176 C 180 176 170 192 186 192 L 214 192 C 230 192 220 176 200 176'/><path d='M 200 150 L 186 176 M 200 150 L 214 176'/><path d='M 195 125 Q 200 129 205 125'/><path d='M 165 60 C 150 40 190 30 200 50 C 215 25 250 40 235 60 C 255 70 230 90 215 75 C 200 95 170 85 180 68 C 160 75 150 70 165 60' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 45 L 270 32 M 262 62 L 285 60 M 245 25 L 258 12' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 300 250 L 380 250 M 300 262 L 380 262 M 315 250 L 315 262 M 335 250 L 335 262 M 355 250 L 355 262 M 375 250 L 375 262'/><g transform='translate(56,220.3) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he had panic attacks as a kid, got sent to a three year retreat at thirteen, and it got worse before it got better.","key":"mingyur","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yongey_Mingyur_Rinpoche","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his community","url":"https://tergar.org/","why":"tergar, the meditation network he runs, with short guided meditations"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"he abandoned a lot of responsibilities and a lot of people to go on the wandering retreat, and he's honest that it scared everyone who worked for him.","moments":["his father, tulku urgyen rinpoche, taught him to meditate at nine by pointing at his own mind and saying, roughly, look, that's it, you're done.","in 2002 he let scientists at the university of wisconsin, in richard davidson's lab, put him in a brain scanner while he meditated, and the results helped kick off the whole science-of-meditation era you see in the headlines now. he thinks it's funny that people needed the machine.","he tells the story about his own panic in almost every talk, with a grin, like a guy showing you a scar."],"name":"yongey mingyur rinpoche","place":"nubri, nepal, then everywhere","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 100 C 52 108 74 108 90 98 L 98 130 L 44 130 Z' fill='var(--marigold)' opacity='.9'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the harder you chase calm, the faster it runs.","where":"the joy of living, his first book, paraphrased"},{"q":"you can't stop the waves but you can learn to surf. actually you already know how, you just forgot.","where":"his talks, paraphrased"},{"q":"i was lying in the dirt dying and i'd never been happier.","where":"in love with the world, his book about the wandering years, paraphrased"}],"start":"the joy of living, his first book, which is about the panic and the brain science. then in love with the world, which is the wandering retreat and the food poisoning. tergar, the network of meditation groups he runs, has guided meditations that are short and plain.","status":"approved","story":"his father was tulku urgyen rinpoche, one of the great tibetan meditation teachers of the last century. rinpoche is a title tibetans give their important teachers, it means precious one, and mingyur was named one as a small boy, recognized as the rebirth of a teacher who'd died long before. he grew up in the himalayas with panic attacks so bad he thought he was dying. real ones. the kind that put you on the floor.\nat thirteen he entered a three year retreat, which is what it sounds like. three years in a closed building with a small group, meditating from before dawn into the night, not coming out. the first year the panic got worse. then he stopped fighting it and let it in and watched it, and it fell apart in a few days. it had needed the fight to stay alive.\nin 2011, running a network of monasteries and a teaching schedule on several continents, he walked out of his monastery in the middle of the night with almost no money and no plan, to do the old wandering retreat. that's an ancient practice in tibet and india where a monk leaves everything and lives on the road, sleeping wherever and eating whatever people give him. think of a ceo leaving a note on the desk and walking out with a toothbrush. not long after, at kushinagar, the town in india where [[buddha|the buddha]] died, he got food poisoning so bad he lay on the ground for days and nearly died. he says that was the best thing that ever happened to him. he came back four and a half years later.\nhe teaches that awareness, the plain part of you that notices things, is already there under whatever you're feeling, and you don't have to fix the feeling to find it. panic was his teacher. he says that without irony.","struggle":"the panic attacks started around age six. he describes lying in bed in the mountains certain he was dying, night after night, and nobody around him with a name for it. this was nepal in the early eighties. nobody was saying panic disorder.\nthe first year of the three year retreat was worse, not better. he was thirteen and shut in a building with the thing he was most afraid of and no way out but through. he says he sat there and hated it.\non the wandering retreat, the first night, sleeping on the floor of the train station in varanasi, a big crowded city on the ganges, he was so ashamed of begging that he couldn't do it. a famous teacher with monasteries on three continents, unable to hold out a bowl for food the way monks have for twenty five hundred years. he went hungry rather than ask.","threads":["madness","wandering","joy"],"years":"born 1975"},{"after":"she left. that's the after. no reconciliation with the palace, no triumphant return. the story goes that the family later sent brahmin priests to dwarka to bring her back, and she went into the temple and didn't come out.\nand the songs outlived the kingdom. five centuries on they're sung by women at weddings and by beggars on trains. m.s. subbulakshmi, the great singer from south india, played her in a film in 1945 and sang her songs for the rest of her life. [[gandhi]]'s ashram sang them at prayers, and when a british admiral's daughter, madeleine slade, came to live there, he named her mirabehn, sister mira.","basically":"a sixteenth century indian princess who decided as a little girl that the god krishna was her husband, and never backed off it, not for her in-laws, not for the palace, not when, the stories say, they tried to poison her. when her husband died she wouldn't play the royal widow and went off singing and dancing in the temples with beggars instead. she's the patron saint of every woman who was told to be quiet and wasn't. her songs are still sung all over india, at weddings and on trains.","born":1498,"died":1546,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 20 250 L 20 150 L 130 150 L 130 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 20 150 L 20 140 L 32 140 L 32 150 M 48 150 L 48 140 L 60 140 L 60 150 M 76 150 L 76 140 L 88 140 L 88 150 M 104 150 L 104 140 L 116 140 L 116 150'/><path d='M 50 140 C 50 118 90 118 90 140' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 70 120 L 70 110'/><path d='M 60 250 L 60 214 C 60 200 80 200 80 214 L 80 250' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 140 256 C 200 262 300 254 390 258' stroke-dasharray='3 8' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 222 172 C 196 196 190 226 196 244 L 262 244 C 268 226 262 196 236 172 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='229' cy='118' r='14'/><path d='M 224 123 Q 229 127 234 123'/><path d='M 213 112 C 214 96 244 96 245 112 C 248 124 246 136 244 144' fill='none'/><path d='M 229 132 L 229 172 M 229 146 L 206 120 M 229 146 L 252 120'/><path d='M 214 244 L 210 250 M 244 244 L 250 250'/><g fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'><circle cx='206' cy='250' r='2'/><circle cx='212' cy='252' r='2'/><circle cx='248' cy='252' r='2'/><circle cx='254' cy='250' r='2'/><\/g><path d='M 174 234 C 192 236 194 252 176 254 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 175 244 L 160 245 M 160 238 L 160 252'/><g transform='translate(104,118.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a rajput princess said krishna was her husband, and when her in-laws sent poison she drank it and kept singing.","key":"mirabai","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirabai","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/mirabai","why":"a short life and some of her songs in english"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["the story goes that a great scholar monk in vrindavan wouldn't see her, because he didn't meet women. she sent back word that she'd thought the only man in vrindavan was krishna, and everyone else was one of his girls. he came out.","the tradition says her guru was ravidas, a cobbler, from the people at the bottom of the hindu caste ranking, the ones higher castes called untouchable and wouldn't eat with. some of her songs name him. historians aren't sure the dates line up.","she's usually shown with an ektara, a one stringed instrument wandering singers carry, and bells on her ankles."],"name":"mirabai","place":"merta and chittor in rajasthan, then the road to vrindavan and dwarka","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 45 57 C 48 60 52 60 55 57 M 65 57 C 68 60 72 60 75 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 38 46 C 44 34 76 34 82 46 C 74 41 46 41 38 46 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 24 116 C 18 60 28 22 60 22 C 92 22 102 60 96 116 L 86 116 C 90 72 84 36 60 36 C 36 36 30 72 34 116 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 29 110 C 24 64 32 30 60 28 C 88 30 96 64 91 110' opacity='.4' stroke-dasharray='1 5'/><path d='M 106 130 L 110 72' stroke-width='3'/><ellipse cx='104' cy='120' rx='8' ry='10' fill='var(--marigold)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"mine is the mountain lifter, gopal, and nobody else.","where":"the song mere to giridhar gopal, paraphrased. giridhar, mountain lifter, is krishna, from the story where he holds a whole hill up on one finger to keep a storm off his village. gopal means cowherd"},{"q":"mira tied bells on her ankles and danced.","where":"the song pag ghunghroo bandh mira nachi, the first line"},{"q":"the rana sent a cup of poison, and mira laughed as she drank it.","where":"the same song, a later verse, paraphrased; the verses change from singer to singer"}],"start":"for love of the dark one, andrew schelling's translations, which are short and alive. then find the songs sung, m.s. subbulakshmi's first.","status":"approved","story":"mira was born around 1498 into the rathore royal family of merta, in rajasthan, the desert country of northwest india. the rathores were rajputs, the warrior clans who ruled that country from hill forts. the story goes that as a little girl she saw a wedding procession go by and asked her mother who her husband would be, and her mother, to shut her up, pointed at a small image of [[krishna]], the blue-skinned cowherd god hindus love like a sweetheart. she took it seriously for the rest of her life.\naround 1516 she was married to bhoj raj, crown prince of mewar, the proudest of the rajput houses, at chittor, their hill fortress. within a few years he was dead. a royal widow in that world could be expected to commit sati, to climb onto her husband's funeral fire and burn with him. the story goes she refused, and said her husband was alive, and it was krishna.\na widow of the royal house singing and dancing in temples in front of wandering holy men was a scandal, and the new rana, the new king, wanted it stopped. the stories say they tried to kill her. a cup of poison sent to her as holy water, and she drank it and nothing happened. a snake in a flower basket, and when she opened it, a garland.\nshe left the palace and walked. to vrindavan, the town where krishna grew up, and then to dwarka on the western coast, the city he ruled as a grown king. the story goes that there, in the temple, she went into the image of krishna and never came out. how many of the songs sung as hers are really hers nobody knows. they're sung all over india today.","struggle":"a young widow in a court that wanted her dead or quiet. the songs are full of it. the rana is angry, the family calls her shameless, the town talks. lok laj, what people will say, runs all through her songs, and she keeps saying she's thrown it off.\nand the songs aren't all ecstasy. a lot of them are a woman waiting for a lover who doesn't come. she can't sleep, she can't eat, she asks the rain clouds for news of him. the ache of separation is half of what she sang.","threads":["love","family","wandering"],"years":"around 1498 to 1546"},{"after":"after the pulitzer he could have written the same book again. he didn't. he taught at berkeley, stanford and the university of arizona, painted, wrote poems and plays and memoirs, and kept going back to the same few things, the rock tree, his grandmother, the bear, the land.\nin his old age he talked about the land as sacred in the plainest way, in lectures and interviews, like it wasn't a controversial thing to say. he'd spent sixty years making it sound obvious.","basically":"a kiowa writer with a huge voice and a huge mustache, whose first novel won the pulitzer prize in 1969, the first time the prize went to a native american. it cracked the door open for every native writer since, louise erdrich, sherman alexie, tommy orange, all of them. then he wrote a small book about his grandmother and his people's long walk from montana to oklahoma that's one of the most beautiful things in american prose. think of the first indigenous name on the bestseller shelf, and the one the others still thank. take from him that your people's story is also your own, and you're allowed to tell it slowly.","born":1934,"died":2024,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 260 L 380 260'/><path d='M 180 260 L 196 110 C 200 96 244 96 248 110 L 264 260 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 206 260 L 212 108 M 222 260 L 222 102 M 238 260 L 232 108' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='100' cy='164' r='14'/><path d='M 100 178 L 100 226'/><path d='M 100 226 L 86 260 M 100 226 L 114 260'/><path d='M 100 192 L 84 216 M 100 192 L 116 216'/><path d='M 92 172 C 96 168 104 168 108 172' stroke-width='3'/><g fill='var(--marigold)'><circle cx='290' cy='40' r='3'/><circle cx='310' cy='46' r='3'/><circle cx='330' cy='44' r='3'/><circle cx='346' cy='54' r='3'/><circle cx='350' cy='74' r='3'/><circle cx='372' cy='76' r='3'/><circle cx='372' cy='56' r='3'/><\/g><path d='M 300 260 C 310 246 330 246 340 260' fill='var(--sage)'/><g transform='translate(222,83.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"as a baby they took him to a giant rock tower in wyoming and named him after it, and he spent his life writing his way back.","key":"momaday","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N._Scott_Momaday","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/n-scott-momaday","why":"a life and some poems"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Way_to_Rainy_Mountain","why":"the small book most people should start with"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["the kiowa story says devils tower was a tree that grew up into the sky to save seven sisters from a bear, and they became the stars of the big dipper. he tells it in the way to rainy mountain.","the delight song of tsoai-talee, his most loved poem, is a long list of all the things he is, a feather, a fish, a shadow, and it ends by saying he is alive."],"name":"n. scott momaday","place":"lawton, oklahoma, then reservations in arizona and new mexico, then stanford, arizona and santa fe","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 46 72 C 52 66 58 70 60 72 C 62 70 68 66 74 72 C 68 76 62 74 60 74 C 58 74 52 76 46 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"an indian is an idea which a given man has of himself.","where":"his essay the man made of words, 1970"},{"q":"a single knoll rises out of the plain in oklahoma, north and west of the wichita range.","where":"the way to rainy mountain, the first line of the introduction"},{"q":"we are what we imagine.","where":"the man made of words, 1970"}],"start":"the way to rainy mountain, the introduction, which is short and might be the best thing he wrote. then house made of dawn.","status":"approved","story":"he was born on february 27, 1934, in lawton, oklahoma. his father, al momaday, was a kiowa painter, and his mother, natachee scott, was a writer of mostly white and some cherokee descent. the kiowa are a plains people who came down from montana to the southern plains, and were forced onto a reservation in oklahoma in 1867. as a baby he was taken to devils tower in wyoming, a huge rock tower the kiowa call tsoai, the rock tree, and given the name tsoai-talee, rock tree boy.\nhis parents taught school on reservations across arizona and new mexico, among navajo, apache and pueblo people, and from twelve he grew up at jemez pueblo in new mexico. he went on to stanford and finished a doctorate in english in 1963.\nhis first novel, house made of dawn, came out in 1968. it follows abel, a young man from a new mexico pueblo who comes home from world war ii and can't fit back in. he kills a man, goes to prison, drifts in los angeles and drinks, and finally goes home, where his grandfather is dying, and runs at dawn with the runners of the old ceremony. it won the pulitzer prize for fiction in 1969, and critics started calling what came after it the native american renaissance.\nthe same year came the way to rainy mountain. it follows the kiowa migration from montana to oklahoma, told three ways at once on facing pages, the old story, the history, and his own memory, and it's built around the death of his grandmother aho and his trip back to her grave at rainy mountain, a hill in oklahoma. he taught for decades, painted, wrote poems, and got the national medal of arts in 2007. he died in santa fe on january 24, 2024, at eighty nine.","struggle":"he didn't put his private troubles on display, and i don't know much about them. what he wrote about was loss. the kiowa had lost the buffalo, the land, and the sun dance, and in the way to rainy mountain he says the last kiowa sun dance was held in 1887, and his grandmother was there as a child.\nand house made of dawn is about a veteran who can't find his way back into his own life, and drinks, and ends up in prison. it's not a book about him. it's a book by somebody who'd watched that happen to people he knew.","threads":["the land","grief","family"],"years":"1934 to 2024"},{"after":"after he killed the egyptian, he ran, and spent forty years as a shepherd for his father in law. the bush found him there, an eighty year old man who said he couldn't talk, tending someone else's sheep. the after was four decades of nothing, and then everything.\nafter the rock, when he was told he'd never enter the land, he didn't argue. deuteronomy, the fifth book of the bible, is him at the border, retelling the whole story to the kids who'd make it in, giving them the law one more time, and then walking up the mountain alone to look at what he wouldn't get. nobody knows where he's buried. the book says god did it himself.","basically":"the central figure of the hebrew bible, the jewish scriptures that christians call the old testament, the man who, as the story goes, led a nation of slaves out of egypt and came down a mountain with the ten commandments. he was also a fugitive who'd killed a man, and a bad talker who tried five different excuses to get out of the job. after forty years walking his people through the desert, he was told he couldn't go into the land he'd been walking them to. if you've ever been handed something way too big and said please, pick someone else, he said it first, to god's face.","born":-1300,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--olive)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='120' cy='140' r='14'/><path d='M 120 154 L 120 202'/><path d='M 120 202 L 106 236 M 120 202 L 134 236'/><path d='M 120 168 L 106 192 M 120 168 L 146 162'/><path d='M 96 250 C 97 244 107 244 108 250 M 82 250 C 83 244 93 244 94 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 200 250 C 180 250 170 220 190 210 C 180 190 210 180 215 195 C 225 175 250 185 240 205 C 260 210 250 240 230 250 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 200 235 C 195 220 210 215 206 200 M 220 240 C 228 225 216 215 226 200 M 212 250 C 208 238 220 232 214 220' stroke='var(--marigold)' opacity='.9'/><path d='M 300 250 L 350 140 L 400 250' opacity='.7'/><g opacity='.6'><circle cx='350' cy='122' r='7'/><path d='M 350 136 L 350 150'/><path d='M 350 184 L 336 218 M 350 184 L 364 218'/><path d='M 350 150 L 336 174 M 350 150 L 376 144'/><\/g><g transform='translate(216,168.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a stuttering fugitive who argued with a burning bush, asked god for a name, and got told i am who i am.","key":"moses","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moses","why":"the plain overview"},{"label":"exodus, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/exo.htm","why":"the book of exodus in the king james version"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"he killed a man in egypt and hid the body in the sand. later the book has him ordering a lot of killing in god's name. while he was up the mountain the people made a golden calf to worship, and when he came down, about three thousand of them were killed in a day on his orders. the book doesn't flinch. neither should you.","moments":["his father in law jethro, a priest of midian, a desert people, not an israelite, watched him judging everybody's disputes from morning to night and told him he'd wear himself out, delegate. moses did. the first management consultant was an outsider who worshipped other gods.","he asked to see god's face and was told nobody sees that and lives, but he could stand in a crack in the rock and see god's back going by. he came down the mountain glowing and had to wear a veil.","in numbers 12 his own brother and sister, aaron and miriam, complained about his wife, a woman from cush, the kingdom south of egypt. god took moses's side and struck miriam with a skin disease for a week. the text calls him the most humble man on the face of the earth, which is a strange thing to find in a book tradition says he wrote himself."],"name":"moses","place":"egypt, then the desert for forty years","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 100 40 128 60 130 C 80 128 94 100 90 60 C 82 84 38 84 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 96 C 50 106 70 106 76 96 M 40 110 C 48 118 72 118 80 110' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 104 130 L 108 40' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 60 18 L 60 8 M 42 24 L 36 16 M 78 24 L 84 16' opacity='.5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"who am i to go to pharaoh?","where":"exodus 3:11, which means the book of exodus, chapter 3, verse 11. exodus is the second book of the bible, the escape from egypt. read the whole of chapters 3 and 4, the argument"},{"q":"show me your glory.","where":"exodus 33:18; read the whole chapter, he only gets the back"},{"q":"please, lord, send someone else.","where":"exodus 4:13"}],"start":"exodus 3 and 4 for the bush and the argument. exodus 33 for the back of god. deuteronomy 34 for the ending, which is short and it's the best thing in the torah, the first five books of the bible, which tradition says he wrote himself.","status":"approved","story":"the story goes that the israelites, the hebrew tribes who became the jewish people, were slaves in egypt, and pharaoh, the king, ordered every newborn israelite boy thrown in the nile. moses's mother hid him in a basket in the reeds by the river, and pharaoh's own daughter found him and raised him in the palace. as a young man he saw an egyptian overseer beating a hebrew slave, killed the overseer, hid the body in the sand, and ran for the desert. years later he was tending his father in law's sheep on a mountain when a bush caught fire and didn't burn up, and a voice told him to go back and get his people out.\nhe tried to get out of it five times. i'm nobody. what's your name. they won't believe me. i can't talk. send someone else. god got annoyed and sent his brother aaron along to do the speaking. when moses asked for a name, he got i am who i am, which isn't a name. it's a refusal to be one.\nhe led them out, and the story says the sea split and they walked across on dry ground with the egyptian army behind them. then into the desert and up mount sinai, where he got the law, the ten commandments on stone tablets and hundreds of other rules for how to live. he asked to see god's face and was told nobody sees that and lives, but he could see god's back going by. he came down glowing and had to wear a veil because it scared people.\nhe wandered forty years with people who complained the entire time. once they were dying of thirst and god told him to speak to a rock and water would come out. moses was so fed up he yelled at them and hit the rock with his staff instead, twice. the water came anyway. but for that, he was shown the promised land, canaan, roughly where israel is now, from a mountaintop, and told he couldn't go in. he died up there. nobody knows where he's buried.","struggle":"he didn't want the job and said so five times. i'm nobody. what's your name. they won't believe me. i can't talk. please send someone else. god got angry and sent him anyway, with his brother aaron to do the speaking.\nthen forty years in the desert with people who complained about everything, and in numbers 11, the book of the bible about the desert years, when they were all crying for meat, he finally breaks. why did you lay the burden of all these people on me? did i give birth to them? if this is how you're going to treat me, kill me now. that's in the bible. a prophet asking god to kill him because he's tired of everybody.","threads":["exile","wandering"],"years":"the story is set maybe 3300 years ago"},{"after":"she kept going. every day for about fifty years she got up before dawn, prayed into a dark she couldn't feel anything in, and went and washed people. she didn't tell the crowds. she told her confessors, in writing, in detail, and let them see all of it.\nin 1961 joseph neuner, the jesuit she finally told the whole thing to, told her the darkness might be part of the work, the same abandonment the people on the street felt, the feeling of being wanted by nobody. she wrote back that for the first time in eleven years she'd come to love it. it didn't lift. she just stopped reading it as proof she'd failed. she never made the other things right, the keating money or the pain in the homes. both halves of her stand.","basically":"an albanian nun who walked out of a comfortable convent school in calcutta in 1948 to live in the slums and pick dying people up off the street, and became one of the most famous women on earth doing it. after she died her private letters showed that for almost fifty years she felt nothing when she prayed, no god, no comfort, nothing, and she kept going anyway. she also took money from crooks and a dictator and ran homes where the dying got very little for their pain, so she's not a poster. if you've ever kept showing up to something long after the feeling left, she did it longer than anybody.","born":1910,"died":1997,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 200 222 L 280 222 L 280 232 L 200 232 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 204 232 L 204 250 M 276 232 L 276 250'/><circle cx='214' cy='213' r='8'/><path d='M 222 222 C 230 208 270 208 278 222' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 300 222 L 380 222 L 380 232 L 300 232 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 304 232 L 304 250 M 376 232 L 376 250'/><circle cx='314' cy='213' r='8'/><path d='M 322 222 C 328 210 342 210 346 222' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 282 250 C 282 242 298 242 298 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 290 242 C 286 236 290 230 290 228 C 292 232 296 236 290 242 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 180 250 C 180 242 196 242 196 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 188 242 C 184 236 188 230 188 228 C 190 232 194 236 188 242 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 92 186 C 84 120 136 120 128 186 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='110' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 110 168 L 110 216'/><path d='M 110 216 L 96 250 M 110 216 L 124 250'/><path d='M 110 184 L 132 194 M 110 180 L 132 184'/><path d='M 130 182 C 130 192 150 192 150 182 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 142 178 C 138 170 146 166 142 158' stroke-dasharray='2 5' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(350,197.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the most famous saint of the century felt god had left her for almost fifty years, and went to work every morning anyway.","key":"motherteresa","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mother_Teresa","why":"the plain overview of her life, the letters and the criticism included"},{"label":"the nobel prize","url":"https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/peace/1979/teresa/facts/","why":"her 1979 nobel peace prize page, with her lecture"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Missionary_Position:_Mother_Teresa_in_Theory_and_Practice","why":"hitchens's book against her, for the other side"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"in 1981 she flew to haiti, accepted an honor from the duvaliers, the father and son dictators who ran the country for almost thirty years with a private militia that tortured and killed people, praised the family, and took their money. she took over a million dollars from charles keating, an american banker whose savings and loan, a kind of savings bank, collapsed and wiped out the savings of thousands of ordinary people, a lot of them retirees. when he was tried for fraud she wrote to the judge asking for mercy. a prosecutor wrote back asking her to return the money to the people keating had robbed. she never answered him and never gave it back.\nchristopher hitchens, the british journalist and famous atheist, and others, including the editor of the lancet, one of the big medical journals, in 1994, reported what went on in the homes for the dying. little real pain relief, needles reused, suffering treated as something to offer up to christ. that's the old catholic idea that your pain, handed over to god, gets joined to jesus's pain on the cross and does some good, and it's a hard thing to hear when it's somebody else's pain and there's medicine for it. she spoke hard against contraception and divorce, and in her nobel lecture called abortion the greatest destroyer of peace. her part in it is money, pain, and being very sure.","moments":["before she started, in 1948, she went to patna, a city in northern india, for a few months to learn basic nursing from the medical mission sisters, an american order of nuns who were doctors and nurses. a teacher of twenty years, back to being a student.","when she won the nobel in 1979 she asked them to cancel the banquet and give her the money for the poor. they did.","she died on september 5, 1997, five days after princess diana. at the state funeral in calcutta her body rode through the streets on the same gun carriage, a wagon built for hauling a cannon, that had carried [[gandhi]]'s."],"name":"mother teresa","place":"skopje, then calcutta for the rest of it","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 24 116 C 18 60 28 22 60 22 C 92 22 102 60 96 116 L 86 116 C 90 72 84 36 60 36 C 36 36 30 72 34 116 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 27 116 C 22 62 31 26 60 26 C 89 26 98 62 93 116 L 89 116 C 94 64 86 31 60 31 C 34 31 26 64 31 116 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"if i ever become a saint, i will surely be one of darkness.","where":"a letter from 1962, in come be my light, the book of her private letters"},{"q":"for the first time in eleven years i have come to love the darkness.","where":"a letter to joseph neuner, a jesuit priest she confided in, 1961, in come be my light, paraphrased. the jesuits are an order of catholic priests known for being scholars"},{"q":"we can't all do great things, but we can do small things with great love.","where":"attributed to her everywhere; the idea runs through her talks"}],"start":"come be my light, her private letters, edited by brian kolodiejchuk, the priest who argued her case for sainthood. start with the letters from the fifties on. then the missionary position, christopher hitchens's short, furious book against her, for the other side. read both.","status":"approved","story":"she was born anjezë gonxhe bojaxhiu to an albanian family in skopje, which is now the capital of north macedonia. at eighteen she joined the sisters of loreto, an order of nuns based in ireland that ran schools, and they sent her to india. for almost twenty years she taught at a girls' school the order ran in calcutta, behind a wall, and by her own account she was happy there.\non september 10, 1946, on a train up to darjeeling, a hill town in the himalayas, she heard what she called a call within a call. the first call had been to become a nun. this one said leave the convent and go live with the poorest of the poor, and serve them right where they were. it took two years to get permission from the church. in 1948 she walked out in a cheap white cotton sari with a blue border and started an open air school in a slum.\nrome approved her new order of nuns, the missionaries of charity, in 1950. in 1952 she opened a home for the dying in an old pilgrims' hostel next to the temple of kali, the hindu goddess, at kalighat, so people picked up off the street wouldn't die on it. think of a hospice with almost no money, for people nobody was coming to look for. the nobel peace prize came in 1979. by the time she died there were homes in over a hundred countries.\nthen in 2007 her private letters came out, in a book called come be my light, and everyone found out what her confessors had known for decades. confessors are the priests a catholic tells her sins and her inner life to, and she told hers everything. almost from the moment the work began she felt nothing when she prayed. no presence, no voice, no comfort, for close to fifty years. she'd asked for the letters to be destroyed. the church kept them, and in 2016 it made her a saint.","struggle":"the letters are specific. she writes of feeling unwanted by god, of god not being god, of god maybe not existing. she writes that when she tries to lift her thoughts to heaven there's such emptiness that the thoughts come back like sharp knives. she smiled for the cameras through all of it and called the smile a cloak.\nit started around 1949, right after the work began, the same work she'd been so sure god had asked for. the voice on the train went quiet and never really came back. she'd been certain she was called and then she was certain of nothing, and she kept the daily routine of her order anyway, up before dawn, prayer, then the streets, every single day.","threads":["doubt","money","dying"],"years":"1910 to 1997"},{"after":"the morning he finally won is the one to look at. when mecca fell he was standing in the city that had mocked him, tortured his followers and driven him out, with ten thousand armed men behind him. he declared an amnesty. the accounts have him telling the people of mecca what joseph, in the quran, told the brothers who had sold him: no blame on you today. a handful of people were excluded by name, and four of them were killed. everyone else went home. bilal, the man they had staked out on the sand, gave the call to prayer from the kaaba, and the idols in it, three hundred and sixty by the traditional count, came down.\nand there's the morning after khadija. in one year he lost the person who had believed him before anyone else did and the uncle who had kept him alive, and then he was stoned out of ta'if. he kept going, and three years later he got his people out to medina. none of that cancels the section above. both are on the record, and the reader gets to hold both.","basically":"the man islam starts with. a merchant from mecca, in arabia, who at about forty said an angel had given him words from god, and spent the next twenty two years reciting them, getting run out of town for them, and then leading a community that took over most of arabia. about two billion muslims hold that he was the last prophet, and that the quran is god's speech, not his. picture a well liked businessman who starts telling his hometown that the gods in its main shrine are nothing, and won't stop. this page doesn't show him, because most muslims don't picture him, and it tells the hard parts straight, because every page here does.","born":570,"died":632,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--teal)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 352 38 A 22 22 0 1 0 366 76 A 17 17 0 1 1 352 38 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g fill='var(--paper)' stroke='none'><circle cx='250' cy='40' r='2.2'/><circle cx='290' cy='78' r='2'/><circle cx='60' cy='46' r='2.2'/><circle cx='96' cy='92' r='1.8'/><circle cx='214' cy='70' r='1.8'/><circle cx='318' cy='120' r='2'/><\/g><path d='M 26 270 L 150 58 L 198 150 L 224 118 L 292 270 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 132 142 Q 150 106 168 142 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 150 98 L 150 86 M 126 112 L 117 104 M 174 112 L 183 104'/><path d='M 152 146 C 176 172 122 192 150 216 S 214 246 252 264' stroke-dasharray='1 10'/><path d='M 10 270 L 390 270'/><rect x='262' y='238' width='28' height='32' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 262 248 L 290 248'/><rect x='302' y='248' width='20' height='22' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='326' y='238' width='20' height='32' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='352' y='252' width='24' height='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 310 270 L 310 260 M 334 270 L 334 258 M 362 270 L 362 262'/><g transform='translate(324,211) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"an orphan from mecca went up a mountain to be alone and came down shaking.","key":"muhammad","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muhammad","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the first revelation, in the source","url":"https://sunnah.com/bukhari:3","why":"aisha's account of the cave, in sahih al-bukhari"},{"label":"the morning hours","url":"https://quran.com/93","why":"the short chapter of the quran that speaks to him as an orphan"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Depictions_of_Muhammad","why":"why this page has no picture of him"}],"lineage":"muslim","mess":"after khadija died he married many times, thirteen wives by the usual count. one was aisha, the daughter of his closest companion. the collections of reports that muslims trust most have her saying, in her own voice, that she was six when she was married to him and nine when the marriage was consummated. he was about fifty. it was within the customs of the time, and for centuries the critics of islam went after his many marriages and barely mentioned her age. some modern muslim writers argue she must have been older. by any standard a reader would use today, a man of fifty married a child. another wife had been married to the freed slave he'd raised as a son, which looked like incest to people at the time, until a verse of the quran declared it allowed. two more came to him as captives of war.\nhe was also a commander, and some of it is hard reading. in 627, after the siege of medina, the banu qurayza, a jewish tribe of the town, were accused of dealing with the enemy. the earliest biography says an arbiter ruled that the men be killed and the women and children enslaved, and that it was done, somewhere between four hundred and nine hundred men. a few modern historians doubt the numbers, or the story. the reports also say he called for the killing of ka'b ibn al-ashraf, a poet who had mocked him and stirred up his enemies. and he owned slaves, bought and was given them, as the people around him did. the quran pushes hard toward freeing slaves and never bans owning them.","moments":["khadija was his employer before she was his wife. she sent a friend to ask whether he'd consider marrying a woman who had the means to provide for herself.","when he came home from the cave in 610 he said cover me, cover me. they wrapped him up and waited until the fear had passed, and only then did he tell khadija what had happened.","bilal had been born a slave and tortured for following him. muhammad chose him to give the call to prayer, the first person ever to do it.","he died on june 8, 632, in aisha's room in medina, in her arms as the reports have it."],"name":"muhammad","place":"mecca and medina, in western arabia","said":[{"q":"the strong one isn't the one who overpowers people. the strong one is the one who controls himself when he's angry.","where":"sahih al-bukhari 6114, from the collection of his sayings most muslims trust first; closely paraphrased"},{"q":"none of you has faith until he wants for his brother what he wants for himself.","where":"sahih al-bukhari 13; closely paraphrased"},{"q":"what a deed is worth depends on the intention behind it, and everyone gets what they intended.","where":"sahih al-bukhari 1, the first saying in the collection; closely paraphrased"}],"start":"the short chapters at the back of the quran, which muslims hold to be god's speech and not his, starting with chapter 93, the morning hours. then the first pages of sahih al-bukhari, where aisha tells the story of the cave.","status":"approved","story":"he was born around 570 in mecca, a trading town in western arabia. his father died about six months before he was born and his mother when he was six. a grandfather took him in and died two years later, and an uncle, abu talib, raised him after that. he grew up to lead trading caravans. a wealthy merchant named khadija hired him to take her goods to syria, liked how he handled it, and proposed to him through a friend. he was about twenty five. tradition says she was forty, though some sources say closer to twenty eight. they were married about twenty five years, and he took no other wife while she lived.\nin 610, at about forty, he was alone in a cave on mount hira outside the city, where he went to pray. the account muslims trust most, passed down from his wife aisha, says an angel seized him and said read. he said he didn't know how to read. it happened three times, and then came the first words of the quran: read, in the name of your lord who created. he went home shaking and told khadija, cover me, cover me. she told him god would never disgrace him, because he was good to his family, helped the poor and looked after his guests. her cousin waraqa, a christian, said this was the same messenger that had come to [[moses]], and warned him that anyone who brings such a thing gets treated as an enemy. muslims hold that the angel was gabriel and that the words are god's own, not muhammad's.\nfor about twelve years he preached in mecca that there is one god and that the idols in the kaaba, the cube shaped shrine at the center of town, were nothing. some of his early followers were slaves, and they paid for it. bilal, a man born into slavery in mecca, was staked out in the sun with a rock on his chest and kept saying one, one. in 622 muhammad and his people left for medina, a town to the north. that move is the hijra, and the muslim calendar counts from it.\nin medina he was no longer only a preacher. he was the head of a community, and mecca came after it. they fought at badr in 624 and at uhud in 625, and in 627 the meccans laid siege to medina for two weeks and went home. then, around the turn of the year 630, he marched on mecca with about ten thousand men, and the city fell with almost no fighting.\nhe stayed nineteen days and went back to medina. in march 632 he made the pilgrimage to mecca and gave what's remembered as the farewell sermon, telling the crowd that their lives and their property were sacred to one another and that the old interest debts were wiped out. three months later, on june 8, 632, he died in medina, in aisha's room. a word on the sources. the oldest life of him, by ibn ishaq, was written around 767, more than a hundred and thirty years after he died, and the big collections of his sayings, the hadith, were put together generations after him. muslim scholars built careful methods for sorting solid reports from weak ones, and historians still argue about how much can be known. what's on this page is the story as those early sources tell it.","struggle":"he never knew his father, lost his mother at six and his grandfather at eight. the quran brings it up itself. a short chapter near the end, the morning hours, asks him: did he not find you an orphan and shelter you.\nthe first revelation didn't feel like a gift. by the early accounts he came down the mountain afraid he was possessed. then the words stopped coming for a while, and the early accounts say he was in such despair that he climbed up meaning to throw himself off, until the angel appeared again.\nin 619 khadija died, and so did abu talib, the uncle whose standing had protected him. it's remembered as the year of sorrow. he went to a nearby town called ta'if looking for a new home for his people, and they drove him out with stones.","threads":["grief","exile","war","family","forgiveness"],"years":"around 570 to 632"},{"after":"the summer his son died, he went out on the road and began telling the tales. after sashia died and he got sick, he kept teaching and nathan kept writing. in the spring of 1810, knowing he didn't have long, he moved to uman. in 1768 jews and poles had been massacred there, somewhere between two thousand and twenty thousand of them, the estimates are that far apart, and he wanted to be buried among those dead. he took a house that had belonged to one of the town's maskilim, the jews of the jewish enlightenment, a movement that was at odds with the hasidim, and he sat with them and played chess. in those last months he told his followers one sabbath, the jewish day of rest, that he knew nothing at all anymore, that he was only a simple man. then he cried out in yiddish: never give up. he died on october 16, 1810.\nhe never named a successor, and he had told them he would go on leading them after he died. so they never took another rebbe. other hasidim called them the dead hasidim, and they wore it as a badge of honor. nathan printed the books, and at rosh hashanah in 1811 he brought the followers to the grave in uman. he led that gathering every year until he died in 1844. under soviet rule only a dozen or so risked the trip. now tens of thousands come every autumn. there's no cure in this story. in his last months he was still saying he knew nothing, and still shouting at a room full of people not to quit.","basically":"a jewish holy man in ukraine around 1800, a rebbe, which is what hasidic jews call the teacher they gather around. hasidism is a joyful, mystical kind of judaism, and his great-grandfather started it. nachman swung between high spirits and black gloom, and built a whole way of living out of not giving in to the gloom. his main advice was to go off alone for an hour a day, a field or the woods if you can, and talk to god out loud in your own language about whatever is actually going on. it's close to what people now do in a therapist's office or a journal, except the listener is god and you're standing in a field. he died of tuberculosis at thirty eight, his followers never replaced him, and tens of thousands of people still travel to his grave every year.","born":1772,"died":1810,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--olive)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 195.0'/><path d='M 2.5999999999999943 195.0 C -15.0 162.0 18.0 134.5 40 145.5 C 67.5 131.2 97.2 162.0 77.4 195.0 C 62.0 206.0 18.0 206.0 2.5999999999999943 195.0 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 96 250 L 96 208.0'/><path d='M 67.44 208.0 C 54.0 182.8 79.2 161.8 96 170.2 C 117.0 159.28 139.68 182.8 124.56 208.0 C 112.8 216.4 79.2 216.4 67.44 208.0 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 14 250 L 14 215.0'/><path d='M -9.8 215.0 C -21.0 194.0 0.0 176.5 14 183.5 C 31.5 174.39999999999998 50.4 194.0 37.8 215.0 C 28.0 222.0 0.0 222.0 -9.8 215.0 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 0 250 L 150 250 L 150 300 M 260 300 L 260 250 L 400 250' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 150 250 L 148 300 M 260 250 L 262 300' stroke-width='0.1' opacity='0'/><path d='M 150 250 L 260 250 L 260 300 L 150 300 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.12'/><path d='M 146 250 L 264 250' stroke-width='5'/><path d='M 196 284 L 232 284' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='206' cy='168' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='204' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='214' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='209' cy='174' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 206 182 L 206 215.44'/><path d='M 206 215.44 L 188 250 M 206 215.44 L 224 250'/><path d='M 206.0 190 L 172.0 192'/><path d='M 206.0 190 L 240.0 192'/><path d='M 320.0 250 L 352.0 243.6 M 352.0 250 L 320.0 243.6'/><path d='M 326.4 243.6 C 320.0 226.0 334.4 222.8 336 208.4 C 340.8 221.2 352.0 227.6 345.6 243.6 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 326.4 224.0 L 345.6 224.0 L 345.6 236.0 L 326.4 236.0 Z M 336 224.0 L 336 236.0' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 350 40 C 328.0 48.0 328.0 72.0 350 80 C 340.0 70.0 340.0 50.0 350 40 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(208,261.1) scale(0.85)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he fought despair his whole short life and told everyone else it was forbidden to give up.","key":"nachman","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nachman_of_Breslov","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"encyclopedia.com","url":"https://www.encyclopedia.com/people/philosophy-and-religion/judaism-biographies/nahman-bratslav","why":"three encyclopedia entries on one page, arthur green's and zvi mark's among them"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosh_Hashana_kibbutz","why":"the yearly gathering at his grave, with the numbers"},{"label":"sefaria","url":"https://www.sefaria.org/Likutei_Moharan,_Part_II.48?lang=en","why":"the narrow bridge teaching itself, in hebrew and english"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"he fought with nearly everyone in his own world. he told other rebbes bluntly that the revelations they were so proud of were false, and he kept attacking what he called the falsely famous. in zlatopol it started, by one account, on yom kippur, the fast day when jews ask forgiveness, when he criticized the man leading the prayers. the shpoler zeide condemned him and accused him of heretical leanings, and there was an attempt to have him excommunicated, thrown out of the community. his own uncle baruch joined the other side. historians still argue about what the fight was really about. green reads it as a young man's new path against an old man's established one. in bratslav, one reference work says, he came into conflict with every rebbe in the area. and he put himself above all of them. a tzaddik, a righteous one, is the hasidic word for the holy man who stands between ordinary people and god, and mark writes that nachman considered his own rank incomparably higher than the other rebbes of his day and than the tzaddikim before them, the baal shem tov included. green hears an air of megalomania in some of what he said, and in the rest the exact opposite, a man who felt empty and unworthy of the job.\nthen the messiah business. when his first son was born in 1805, his circle caught fire with the idea that the messiah, the rescuer jews have been waiting for, was close, and nachman pinned those hopes on the baby. the boy died the next summer and the hopes went with him. soon afterward he gave out a secret teaching on the order in which the redeemer would come, the scroll of secrets, set down only in hints and abbreviations and kept hidden by his followers. he also had no use for philosophy in religion, and named [[maimonides]], the jewish philosopher, as an example of the error. and the gathering at his grave today is for men only, and in recent years it has come with reports of heavy drinking and fights with the people who live there.","moments":["istanbul, 1798, as nathan tells it. on his way to the holy land the young rebbe went through the streets barefoot, playing war games with the neighborhood children. one side was called france.","the first tale, the lost princess, is about a king's right-hand man who spends years looking for the king's daughter and keeps failing at the last moment. it ends with one line saying that in the end he got her out. how, nachman never said.","the thirteenth and longest tale, the seven beggars, has no ending either. six beggars come to a wedding, one a day, and tell their stories. the seventh, the one with no feet, never gets his turn. nachman said he would tell no more, and that it would not be heard until the messiah comes.","in uman in 1810, dying, he played chess with the town's maskilim. one of them later moved to england and, by one encyclopedia's account, taught at cambridge university."],"name":"nachman of breslov","place":"medzhybizh, bratslav and uman, in what's now ukraine","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 104 38 96 60 96 C 82 96 100 104 102 130 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 97 M 68 84 L 68 97'/><path d='M 50 97 L 60 110 L 70 97'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 66 C 36 92 50 106 60 106 C 70 106 84 92 86 66 C 80 76 70 78 60 78 C 50 78 40 76 34 66 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 45 49 Q 49.5 46 54 49 M 66 49 Q 70.5 46 75 49' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 54 85 C 57 88 63 88 66 85'/><path d='M 37 42 C 40 22 80 22 83 42 C 70 35 50 35 37 42 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 33 50 C 27 56 32 63 28 70 C 26 75 30 79 28 84'/><path d='M 87 50 C 93 56 88 63 92 70 C 94 75 90 79 92 84'/><path d='M 104 128 L 104 112'/><circle cx='104' cy='104' r='9' fill='var(--sage)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"it is a great mitzvah to always be happy.","where":"likutey moharan, part two, teaching 24, in moshe mykoff's translation for the breslov research institute. the sentence goes on: and to make every effort to determinedly keep depression and gloom at bay."},{"q":"know, too! a person must cross a very, very narrow bridge. the main rule is: do not be frightened at all!","where":"likutey moharan, part two, teaching 48, moshe mykoff's translation. the version everyone knows, the whole entire world is a very narrow bridge, is the wording of a modern hebrew song by baruch chait, kol ha'olam kulo. the book says a person has to cross one."},{"q":"never give up! there is absolutely no basis for despair!","where":"two cries in yiddish to his followers in uman in the last months of his life, recorded by nathan in sichot haran, section 153, and given here in aryeh kaplan's english from rabbi nachman's wisdom."}],"start":"the seven beggars, the longest of his tales, even without its ending. then rabbi nachman's wisdom, aryeh kaplan's translation of nathan's notes on his life and his talk. arthur green's tormented master if you want the whole life from a historian.","status":"approved","story":"he was born on april 4, 1772, in medzhybizh, a small town in what's now western ukraine. it was the town of [[baalshemtov|the baal shem tov]], the man who started hasidism, who had died there twelve years earlier and is buried there. nachman's mother was the baal shem tov's granddaughter. his father's father had been one of the old man's disciples, and nachman was named for him. his mother's brother, baruch of medzhybizh, kept a rich court in the same town. so he grew up inside the family business. he was married at thirteen or fourteen, the sources differ, to a girl named sashia, and moved in with her father. there he took to wandering off by himself into the fields and the woods.\nby about twenty he was gathering disciples of his own. then in 1798 he announced he was going to the land of israel. his wife was distraught. by nathan's account he told one daughter she could go to her future in-laws, said someone would take in her sisters, that their mother could find work as a cook, and that he would sell everything in the house to pay for the trip. what we know of the trip comes from nathan of nemirov, the follower who later wrote everything down, and nathan only met him three years after he got back, so this is the story as nachman's own circle told it. he left in may with one companion. in istanbul he ran through the streets barefoot, playing war games with the local children. he landed on september 10, 1798, the day before rosh hashanah, the jewish new year, and said that the moment he had walked four steps in the holy land he had what he came for. he wanted to turn around and go home. he stayed about six months instead, among the hasidim of the galilee, in the north of the country. then napoleon, the french general who had invaded egypt, came marching up the coast in march 1799, and nachman got out on a ship he took for a merchant boat. it was a turkish warship. the jews of the island of rhodes paid the captain two hundred thaler to let him off.\nin 1800 he moved to zlatopol, a town near shpola, where an old and well-loved rebbe known as the shpoler zeide, the grandfather of shpola, already had the district. the old man was about seventy five and nachman was twenty eight. the zeide took the move as trespassing, and a quarrel broke out that ran for two years and pulled in other rebbes. in 1802 nachman gave up and moved to bratslav, the town his followers are still named for. breslov is the same name the way they say it. a twenty two year old scholar from a town nine miles off, nathan of nemirov, came to hear him and found his teacher. from then on nathan wrote down the lessons and the conversations, the stories and the life. nearly everything we have of nachman came through nathan's pen, starting with likutey moharan, the collected teachings, first printed in 1808.\nwhat he taught was mostly how to keep going. the practice he cared about most he called hitbodedut, which means making yourself be alone. set aside an hour a day, in a room or out in a field, and say your piece to god out loud in the language you actually talk in. for his people that was yiddish, the everyday language of eastern european jews, and not the hebrew of the prayer book. the other half was joy, and he meant it as an order. it is a great mitzvah to always be happy, he taught, a mitzvah being a commandment, a thing god requires of you. he knew it doesn't come naturally. he said to get there by acting silly if you have to, and to keep the broken heart to one short stretch of the day.\nand he told stories. in the summer of 1806 he began telling long, strange fairy tales in yiddish, about a lost princess and about seven beggars at a wedding. there are thirteen main ones, and nathan printed them after he died. by then nachman was sick. his wife died of tuberculosis in 1807 and he caught it soon after. in the spring of 1810 he moved to uman, another town in ukraine, and he died there on october 16, 1810. he was thirty eight.","struggle":"the thing he couldn't beat was the gloom. nathan's account of his boyhood has him starting over and over. he would throw himself into serving god, fall, begin again, and stumble again. he hid in a small attic in his father's house and begged god in yiddish. as a grown man and a rebbe he still talked openly about his times of weakness, which one modern scholar, zvi mark, describes as bordering on despair and depression. arthur green, whose biography of him is called tormented master, says his swings between elation and melancholy are well documented, and that today they would probably be diagnosed as manic depression. that is a scholar's guess about a man two hundred years dead. nobody examined him. his own followers read the same falls as steps, each drop the start of a higher climb.\nthen the deaths. he and sashia had eight children, six girls and two boys. two of the girls died as babies. his first son was born in 1805 and died in the summer of 1806, and neither boy lived to grow up. in june 1807 sashia died of tuberculosis. he got engaged again the next month, and right after that he came down with it himself. he went to lemberg, the city now called lviv, and spent about eight months with the doctors there.\nin 1808 there was the book. he had dictated it to nathan word by word, and nathan admitted he never understood it. sick as he was, nachman came to believe the illness was a punishment from above for having written a book, and he had the copies burned. nathan could remember only that it spoke of the greatness of welcoming a guest and making up a bed for him. his followers call it the burned book. two years later, in 1810, a fire took his house in bratslav.","threads":["joy","grief","doubt","loneliness"],"years":"1772 to 1810"},{"after":"he stopped walking. after all those years on the road he settled at kartarpur, took off the traveling clothes, the story goes, put on ordinary ones, and farmed. the man who'd been to mecca went back to the plow and ran the kitchen.\nand when it came to who would carry it on, he passed over his own sons and picked a disciple named lehna, who'd done the dirty work, and renamed him angad. his son sri chand went his own way and founded an order of wandering holy men who gave up home and family. nanak died in 1539.","basically":"the founder of sikhism, a religion that now counts around twenty five million people, most of them from the punjab. he was a village accountant's son who walked out of a river saying there's no hindu and no muslim, spent years walking across asia with a muslim musician, singing, and ended up a farmer running a free kitchen where rich and poor sat on the same floor. every sikh temple on earth still feeds anyone who walks in, no questions, which is a pretty good test of whether an idea worked. take from him that what you believe shows up where you eat and who you eat with.","born":1469,"died":1539,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 262 L 380 262'/><g transform='translate(40 212) scale(.7)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 56'/><path d='M 0 56 C -20 56 -30 72 -14 72 L 14 72 C 30 72 20 56 0 56'/><path d='M 0 30 L -14 56 M 0 30 L 14 56'/><\/g><ellipse cx='52' cy='270' rx='11' ry='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(90 212) scale(.7)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 56'/><path d='M 0 56 C -20 56 -30 72 -14 72 L 14 72 C 30 72 20 56 0 56'/><path d='M 0 30 L -14 56 M 0 30 L 14 56'/><\/g><ellipse cx='102' cy='270' rx='11' ry='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(140 212) scale(.7)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 56'/><path d='M 0 56 C -20 56 -30 72 -14 72 L 14 72 C 30 72 20 56 0 56'/><path d='M 0 30 L -14 56 M 0 30 L 14 56'/><\/g><ellipse cx='152' cy='270' rx='11' ry='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='202' cy='270' rx='11' ry='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(240 212) scale(.7)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 56'/><path d='M 0 56 C -20 56 -30 72 -14 72 L 14 72 C 30 72 20 56 0 56'/><path d='M 0 30 L -14 56 M 0 30 L 14 56'/><\/g><ellipse cx='252' cy='270' rx='11' ry='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='300' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 300 164 L 300 212'/><path d='M 300 212 L 286 246 M 300 212 L 314 246'/><path d='M 300 180 L 322 190 M 300 176 L 322 180'/><path d='M 295 155 Q 300 159 305 155'/><path d='M 322 190 L 348 222'/><path d='M 326 222 L 378 222 C 380 250 368 262 352 262 C 336 262 324 250 326 222 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 322 222 L 382 222'/><path d='M 338 212 C 334 202 342 196 338 186 M 364 212 C 368 202 360 196 364 186' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(190,237.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he went into the river to bathe, didn't come out for three days, and when he did he said there is no hindu and there is no muslim.","key":"nanak","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guru_Nanak","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the scriptures, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/skh/","why":"sikh texts in english at the internet sacred text archive"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["when he died, the story goes, hindus wanted to cremate him and muslims wanted to bury him. he'd told them to lay flowers on either side of him, and whoever's flowers were still fresh in the morning could have the body. in the morning both sets were fresh and the body was gone. it's the same story they tell about kabir, almost exactly. two men who said the same thing got the same ending.","nearly everything he taught, he sang. the sikh scripture is arranged by raga, the scale and mood a piece of indian music is built on, not by author or subject.","the langar at the golden temple in amritsar, the holiest place sikhs have, feeds tens of thousands of people a day, for free, sitting on the floor."],"name":"guru nanak","place":"the punjab, and on foot across half of asia","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 28 54 C 26 24 94 24 92 54 C 84 40 36 40 28 54 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 44 C 50 34 70 34 84 44 M 32 50 C 46 38 74 38 88 50' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 44 98 C 48 116 72 116 76 98' stroke-dasharray='0.1 5' stroke-width='5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"there is no hindu, there is no muslim.","where":"the janamsakhis, the collections of stories of his life written down after he died, what he said coming out of the river"},{"q":"ik onkar. there is one.","where":"the first words of the guru granth sahib, the sikh holy book, at the start of the mul mantar, the short creed it opens with"},{"q":"truth is high, but higher still is truthful living.","where":"sri rag, one of the musical sections of the guru granth sahib, paraphrased"}],"start":"japji sahib, his long morning hymn, which opens the scripture. read it in any translation, then find it sung.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in 1469 in talwandi, a village in the punjab, the land of five rivers that's now split between india and pakistan. the village is on the pakistan side now and called nankana sahib. his family was hindu. his father was the village accountant and wanted him in business. the boy kept giving things away.\nas a young man he worked at the government storehouse in sultanpur lodhi. one morning, the story goes, he went to bathe in the river bein and didn't come back. they searched the river. on the third day he walked out, and the first thing he said was na koi hindu, na koi musalman. there is no hindu, there is no muslim.\nthen he walked. four long journeys over many years, with a muslim musician named mardana playing the rabab, a kind of lute, while he sang. the accounts put him in the himalayas and tibet, in sri lanka, in baghdad and mecca. at mecca, the story goes, he lay down to sleep with his feet toward the kaaba, the black shrine at the center of mecca that every muslim on earth faces to pray. an angry man told him to move them, and he said, then turn my feet toward where god isn't.\nin his last years he settled at kartarpur on the ravi river, farmed, and built the community around a kitchen. the langar. free food, cooked by volunteers, and everyone sits on the floor in rows to eat it, brahmin priest next to the man who sweeps the street, rich next to poor. every gurdwara, every sikh temple in the world, still does it. [[kabir]], a weaver poet from varanasi who'd been saying a lot of the same things, ended up with his songs in the same holy book as nanak's, the guru granth sahib, which sikhs treat as their living teacher.\nthe stories about him were written down generations later and they grew in the telling. the hymns are his.","struggle":"his father thought he was useless. the story goes that he gave the boy twenty rupees to go make a good deal, and nanak met a group of hungry holy men on the road and spent all of it feeding them. he came home and said he'd made the true bargain. the stories say his father hit him.\naround 1520 babur, a warlord from central asia who went on to found the mughal empire, the dynasty that ruled most of india for the next two hundred years, came down into the punjab and sacked the town of saidpur. the tradition says nanak was there and was taken prisoner. what's certain is the hymns he wrote about it. he asks god straight out, so much beating, so much screaming, and you felt no pain? it's in the scripture. a guru calling god to account.","threads":["wandering","work"],"years":"1469 to 1539"},{"after":"the after is what his people did. [[ramdass|ram dass]] spent fifty years on the four instructions. larry brilliant, the young doctor he kept pushing toward the world health organization, the united nations' health agency, worked on the campaign that ended smallpox. krishna das, a kid from long island who sat at his feet, went home lost after he died and spent years falling apart. he came back by leading kirtan, call and response singing of the names of god, and he's been filling halls with strangers singing along for decades now.\nhe left no book, no system, and no successor. the temples at kainchi and vrindavan are still there, and the crowds still come to kainchi every june for the anniversary of the temple.","basically":"an old man in a plaid blanket in the hills of north india who fed everybody, gave almost no talks, and seemed to know what you were thinking before you did. a fired harvard professor, a hippie doctor who helped wipe out smallpox, and later a kid named steve jobs all went looking for him. picture the grandpa the whole neighborhood goes to, except the neighborhood turned out to be half the world. take from him the short list he gave out. love everyone, serve everyone, remember god, tell the truth.","born":null,"died":1973,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 236 L 380 236 M 20 246 L 380 246'/><path d='M 40 236 L 40 246 M 70 236 L 70 246 M 100 236 L 100 246 M 130 236 L 130 246 M 160 236 L 160 246 M 190 236 L 190 246 M 220 236 L 220 246 M 250 236 L 250 246 M 280 236 L 280 246 M 310 236 L 310 246 M 340 236 L 340 246 M 370 236 L 370 246' opacity='.5'/><rect x='40' y='170' width='130' height='50' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='130' y='140' width='40' height='30' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 64 170 L 64 146 L 80 146 L 80 170'/><path d='M 64 140 C 60 130 70 124 64 114' opacity='.4' stroke-dasharray='2 6'/><circle cx='70' cy='228' r='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='110' cy='228' r='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='150' cy='228' r='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 250 290 C 244 236 264 222 280 222 C 296 222 316 236 310 290 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 262 232 L 262 290 M 280 222 L 280 290 M 298 232 L 298 290 M 254 250 L 306 250 M 250 270 L 310 270' opacity='.45'/><circle cx='280' cy='206' r='16'/><path d='M 275 211 Q 280 215 285 211'/><g transform='translate(110,115.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a fat old man in a plaid blanket who read ram dass's mind and told a young doctor to go end smallpox.","key":"neemkaroli","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neem_Karoli_Baba","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his ashram in america","url":"https://www.nkbashram.org/","why":"the temple his western devotees built in taos, new mexico"},{"label":"ram dass's foundation","url":"https://www.ramdass.org/","why":"where ram dass's stories of him live"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"the miracles, the mind reading, being in two places at once, all come from people who loved him, and none of it can be checked. read them that way.","moments":["steve jobs went to india in 1974 with his friend dan kottke, hoping to meet him, and found out he'd died months before. jobs stayed on in india for months anyway and came home changed.","the first thing people remember about his temples is the food. whoever you were, you got fed before you got anything else.","he called larry brilliant doctor america, and kept telling him, as brilliant tells it, that he'd go to work for the united nations and help get rid of smallpox."],"name":"neem karoli baba","place":"the hills of uttarakhand and the plains of north india","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 32 56 C 22 50 22 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 98 50 98 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 70 C 46 84 74 84 80 70' stroke-dasharray='1 4' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 18 130 C 18 102 34 90 60 94 C 86 90 102 102 102 130 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 32 102 L 32 130 M 46 95 L 46 130 M 74 95 L 74 130 M 88 102 L 88 130 M 22 112 L 98 112 M 19 122 L 101 122' opacity='.45'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"love everyone, serve everyone, remember god, tell the truth.","where":"the instructions ram dass brought home; in be here now, ram dass's 1971 book, and miracle of love, the collection of stories about the old man"},{"q":"sub ek. it's all one.","where":"one of his most repeated phrases, in miracle of love"},{"q":"love is the strongest medicine.","where":"attributed, in miracle of love; ram dass quoted it for years"}],"start":"miracle of love, compiled by ram dass, the stories of him from people who knew him. then be here now, the first section, which is ram dass's own story of meeting him.","status":"approved","story":"almost nothing about his early life is certain. he went by different names in different towns, came and went without warning, and didn't talk about himself. the story goes that a conductor threw him off a train for having no ticket, near a village called neeb karori, and the train wouldn't start again until they let him back on. the village name stuck.\nby the sixties he was maharaj-ji, which just means great king and is what people call a holy man they love, to thousands. a heavy old man wrapped in a plaid blanket on a wooden bench, joking, feeding people, and mostly refusing to give talks. he built temples to hanuman, the monkey god from the ramayana, india's great epic, who's the devoted servant of the god-king ram. he built ashrams, communities that grow up around a teacher, at kainchi in the himalayan foothills near the hill town of nainital, and at vrindavan, the town where the god krishna is said to have grown up.\nin 1967 [[ramdass|richard alpert]], a harvard psychologist who'd been fired for his drug experiments, was brought to him. the old man told alpert he'd been out under the stars the night before thinking about his mother, and that she'd died of her spleen. both true. alpert hadn't told anyone. he cried for two days and went home as ram dass. the westerners who followed him got a short list of instructions and a lot of food.\nhe died in the early hours of september 11, 1973, in a hospital in vrindavan, after leaving kainchi on a night train. the next year a nineteen year old named steve jobs, who'd go on to start apple, went to india to find him and got there too late.","struggle":"we don't have his struggle. he wrote nothing, gave almost no talks, and the people around him recorded what he did to them, not what went on in him. whatever it cost him, he kept it under the blanket.\nwhat the stories do say is that he wouldn't stay put. he'd vanish from an ashram without a word, turn up in some other town days later, and leave again. the devotees spent a lot of time looking for him.","threads":["love","wandering"],"years":"died in 1973; nobody's sure when he was born"},{"after":"he came back. that's the after. he'd tried the romantic version, the robe and the road, and turned around and went home to the shop, the family, the smoke. the rest of his life happened in a small room up a steep stair over a street in bombay.\nand he didn't make the dying into a performance. visitors asked him about it and he answered with the same flat impatience as everything else. the body was going. he'd never thought he was the body. he kept smoking.","basically":"a guy who ran a cigarette stand in bombay, got one instruction from a teacher, and practiced it for three years until he was done. then he kept the stand, and talked to visitors in a tiny room upstairs, chain smoking, losing his temper, and throwing people out. imagine the most awake person in your city is the guy at the corner store, and he's rude to you. take from him that you don't need a mountain. you need to notice that you exist, and stay there.","born":1897,"died":1981,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><rect x='70' y='70' width='150' height='180' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 70 170 L 220 170 M 60 70 L 230 70'/><path d='M 64 176 L 226 176 L 214 190 L 76 190 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 84 216 L 206 216'/><g stroke-width='2'><path d='M 150 206 L 150 216 M 156 206 L 156 216 M 162 206 L 162 216 M 180 206 L 180 216 M 186 206 L 186 216 M 192 206 L 192 216'/><\/g><circle cx='130' cy='96' r='12'/><path d='M 130 110 L 130 152'/><path d='M 130 152 C 110 152 100 168 116 168 L 144 168 C 160 168 150 152 130 152'/><path d='M 130 126 L 116 152 M 130 126 L 144 152'/><path d='M 140 100 L 154 104' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 157 100 C 162 92 154 88 160 80 C 164 74 158 70 162 62' stroke-dasharray='2 5' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 222 250 L 222 170 M 244 250 L 244 170 M 222 234 L 244 234 M 222 218 L 244 218 M 222 202 L 244 202 M 222 186 L 244 186'/><circle cx='320' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 320 168 L 320 214'/><path d='M 320 214 L 336 250 M 320 214 L 302 246'/><path d='M 320 182 L 334 204 M 320 182 L 300 190'/><g transform='translate(106,194.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a cigarette seller in bombay was told he wasn't the body, believed it, and went back to selling cigarettes.","key":"nisargadatta","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nisargadatta_Maharaj","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I_Am_That","why":"the book of his talks"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"a temper, and he didn't hide it. he'd shout at visitors, mock their questions, and throw people out of the room, people who'd flown halfway around the world included. he smoked his own product to the end. and he left his family in 1937 to go wandering, briefly.","moments":["the talks went on between rounds of devotional singing he kept up every day, which surprises people who know him only from the book.","maurice frydman, who translated i am that from marathi, the language of the bombay region, died in 1976, three years after it came out.","his teacher gave him the instruction and he said he gave it three years, and that it was enough. he never claimed it was complicated."],"name":"nisargadatta maharaj","place":"a small shop and the loft above it, bombay","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 66 76 L 82 81' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 85 78 C 90 72 83 68 88 60 C 92 54 86 50 90 44' stroke-dasharray='2 4' opacity='.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"wisdom tells me i am nothing. love tells me i am everything.","where":"i am that, the book of his talks; the next line is that between the two his life flows"},{"q":"stay with the i am, and let go of everything else.","where":"his teacher's instruction, repeated all through i am that, paraphrased"},{"q":"you are not what you take yourself to be.","where":"i am that, paraphrased; he says versions of it on nearly every page"}],"start":"i am that, in frydman's translation. one conversation a morning, anywhere in the book.","status":"approved","story":"maruti kambli was born in 1897 and grew up in a farming family. as a young man he came to bombay, clerked for a while, and opened small shops. the one that did best sold beedis, tobacco rolled up in a leaf, the cheapest smoke in india. he married and had children.\nin 1933 a friend took him to siddharameshwar maharaj, a teacher in the navnath line, an old chain of teachers said to go back to nine legendary yogis. the teacher told him he wasn't the body and he wasn't the mind. he was only the bare sense i am, the plain feeling of existing that's there before you think anything about who you are, and he should stay with that and nothing else. nisargadatta did exactly that, every spare minute, for three years. in 1936 the teacher died.\nthe next year he walked out of bombay to be a wandering monk and headed for the himalayas. on the road a fellow disciple talked him out of it and told him the giving up was supposed to happen inside. he came back. most of the shops had gone under by then. he kept one.\nhe talked to whoever climbed the steep stair to a tiny loft room in his house in khetwadi, a crowded neighborhood of bombay, and for decades that wasn't many people. then maurice frydman, a polish engineer who'd lived in india for decades, taped the talks and translated them, and i am that, the big book of those conversations, came out in 1973. after that people came from everywhere, and he chain smoked and answered them. he died of throat cancer in 1981.","struggle":"in 1937 he walked away from a wife, children, and a business to go be holy on the road. it lasted months. he came back to a family that had managed without him and a business that mostly hadn't.\nthe cancer came in his early eighties, in the throat, from the cigarettes he'd sold all his life. he kept the room open and kept talking to visitors as long as his voice would carry.","threads":["work","family","dying"],"years":"1897 to 1981"},{"after":"in winnipeg, nearly every day, he wrote himself a short note, what he called a spiritual imperative, an order to himself, from the part of him that still knew something to the part that didn't. he kept them private for eight years and let them out as the inner voice of love in 1996, the year he died. it's the most naked thing he wrote.\nthen he went back to daybreak and back to the bathroom with adam in the morning, running the water. the friendship with nathan survived. he didn't come out of it cured. he came out of it back at work, with the same wound and a better idea of where it went.","basically":"a dutch catholic priest who taught at yale and harvard and wrote small, warm books that sold millions, while privately he was lonely, depressed, and gay, and kept that last part mostly to himself as a priest sworn to celibacy. at fifty four he quit harvard to be the pastor of a group home for people with intellectual disabilities outside toronto, and spent his mornings bathing and shaving a man who couldn't speak. picture a famous professor walking off the lecture circuit to work the early shift at a group home, and saying it was the best thing that ever happened to him. he's for anyone who's done plenty and still isn't sure anybody's glad they exist.","born":1932,"died":1996,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 90 M 24 90 L 64 90' stroke-width='4'/><path d='M 70 0 L 76 110 M 104 0 L 98 110 M 76 110 L 98 110' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 300 0 L 304 100 M 340 0 L 336 100 M 304 100 L 336 100' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 20 262 C 120 290 280 290 380 262' stroke-dasharray='4 6' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='222' cy='126' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='221' cy='124' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='231' cy='124' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 222 130 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 208 130 L 160 142 L 118 126 M 160 142 L 122 162'/><path d='M 214 138 L 254 128 M 214 138 L 256 140'/><path d='M 316 100 L 320 134 L 326 176' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='326' cy='190' r='13' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='317' cy='188' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='327' cy='188' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 318 196 h 8' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 304 100 L 316 100 M 336 100 L 328 112' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 322 150 L 282 146 M 322 150 L 284 160'/><g transform='translate(44,65.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a harvard professor quit to go live with people who couldn't read his books, and one of them became his teacher.","key":"nouwen","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henri_Nouwen","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his foundation","url":"https://henrinouwen.org/","why":"the henri nouwen society, with his writings and a free daily meditation"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Return_of_the_Prodigal_Son_(Rembrandt)","why":"the painting his best book is about"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"he was exhausting to his friends, needy, phoning at all hours, and he'd have told you so himself.","moments":["in 1974 he spent seven months living as a monk at the abbey of the genesee in upstate new york, a monastery of trappists, the strict, mostly silent order [[merton|thomas merton]] belonged to. he baked bread and pulled rocks out of fields, and wrote the genesee diary about how bad he was at it.","in 1991 he got to know a trapeze troupe, the flying rodleighs, at a circus in germany, and traveled with them. their leader told him the flyer never catches the catcher. you let go, stretch out your arms, and wait for the catcher to grab you. he meant to write a book about it and died first.","adam died in february 1996. nouwen wrote a short book about him, adam, god's beloved, and died that september before it came out."],"name":"henri nouwen","place":"the netherlands, then yale and harvard, then a house outside toronto","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><rect x='41' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><rect x='62' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><path d='M 58 55 L 62 55 M 41 54 L 34 52 M 79 54 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i am the prodigal son every time i search for unconditional love where it cannot be found.","where":"the return of the prodigal son, his book about the rembrandt painting"},{"q":"being the beloved is the core truth of who you are, and the other voices are trying to talk you out of it.","where":"life of the beloved, a short book he wrote for a friend who didn't share his faith, paraphrased"},{"q":"the minister's wounds, looked at honestly, become a source of healing for other people.","where":"the wounded healer, his 1972 book about people who help people, paraphrased"}],"start":"the return of the prodigal son. then the inner voice of love, the notes he wrote to himself in winnipeg, if it's a bad month. the wounded healer is short.","status":"approved","story":"a dutch catholic priest, ordained in 1957, who came to america to study psychology and stayed to teach. notre dame, then yale divinity school, where they train ministers, for ten years, then harvard. his books were short and warm and sold by the million. the wounded healer, from 1972, says the thing a minister really has to give people is his own wounds, the places he's been hurt, looked at honestly, because that's where he can meet them.\nhe was lonely, restless, and never at home in the competition of university life. in 1985 he spent a year in france at l'arche, which is french for the ark. it's a network of homes started in 1964 by jean vanier, a canadian philosopher, where people with intellectual disabilities and the assistants who help them share a house and a life, cooking, eating, and praying together. in 1986 he left harvard for good and moved to l'arche daybreak, the one outside toronto, as its pastor.\nthey gave him adam arnett to care for. adam couldn't speak, needed help with everything, and had seizures. every morning nouwen bathed him, shaved him, dressed him, and fed him breakfast, slowly, and badly at first. he said later that adam was the one taking care of him.\nthe return of the prodigal son, from 1992, came out of a painting. in the gospel of luke jesus tells a story about a younger son who takes his share of the inheritance early, blows all of it, and comes home broke hoping to be hired on as a servant, and his father runs down the road to hug him. the older son, the one who stayed home and did everything right, sulks outside the party. rembrandt, the dutch master, painted the homecoming near the end of his own life. nouwen sat in front of it for hours in 1986 at the hermitage, the enormous art museum in what was then leningrad. he found himself in the younger son, the one who ran off, then in the older one, the resentful good boy, and then a friend at daybreak told him the job was to become the father. he died of a heart attack in the netherlands in 1996. he was sixty four.","struggle":"in late 1987 he fell apart. a close friendship at daybreak with a younger man, nathan ball, had become the center of his emotional life, and when nathan pulled back nouwen couldn't function. he left daybreak and spent about six months with therapists in winnipeg, in what he called anguish and what most people now would call a breakdown.\nunder that was the longer thing. he was gay and a celibate priest, a man who'd promised never to have sex or marry, and he kept it mostly private his whole life; the people close to him knew, and the biographers said it after he died. he fought depression for years, and a need to be wanted that he described as bottomless.","threads":["loneliness","love","work"],"years":"1932 to 1996"},{"after":"after the childhood she went to the woods and didn't come back. that's close to literal. she built her whole life around the walk, the notebook, and the one person she loved, and she kept that shape for over half a century. it looks simple. it was a choice she made every morning.\nafter molly, she kept walking. new woods, new ponds, florida instead of the cape. the late books are plainer and more openly about god and grief than the early ones. she didn't pretend to be done grieving and she didn't stop going out in the morning.","basically":"a poet who walked the same woods and ponds and beaches at the tip of cape cod nearly every morning for decades with a little notebook, writing down what she saw, and turned it into some of the best loved poems in america. she's the one people read at funerals and graduations, the one wild and precious life poet. she was also tougher and more private than the tote bags make her look. take the walk from her. go outside, look at one thing until you actually see it, and write it down.","born":1935,"died":2019,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 70 250 L 70 135'/><ellipse cx='70' cy='123' rx='35' ry='26' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 330 250 L 330 120'/><ellipse cx='330' cy='106' rx='40' ry='30' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 250 250 L 250 158'/><ellipse cx='250' cy='148' rx='28' ry='21' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 250 196 L 262 190' stroke-width='3.5'/><circle cx='160' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 160 168 L 160 214'/><path d='M 160 214 L 144 250 M 160 214 L 178 246'/><path d='M 160 182 L 146 204 M 160 182 L 180 190'/><rect x='176' y='186' width='14' height='18' rx='2' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 120 60 L 130 66 L 140 60 M 150 50 L 160 56 L 170 50 M 180 64 L 190 70 L 200 64 M 210 54 L 220 60 L 230 54'/><path d='M 20 270 C 100 262 200 278 380 266' stroke-dasharray='3 8' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 104 250 L 104 232 L 128 232 L 128 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='116' cy='232' rx='12' ry='3' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(116,212.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"she walked into the woods every morning with a notebook for fifty years and came out with poems about paying attention.","key":"oliver","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mary_Oliver","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"her site","url":"https://maryoliver.beacon.org/","why":"the authorized site, with recordings of her reading"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/mary-oliver","why":"a life and some poems"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["she said that when she was walking and found she'd come out without a pencil, it was a problem, so she hid pencils in the trees along the trails.","she'd get a line down in the notebook on the walk and work it up later at the desk. the walk came first, every day.","she loved dogs, and late in life put out a whole book of poems about them, dog songs, in 2013."],"name":"mary oliver","place":"ohio, then provincetown on the tip of cape cod, then florida","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><rect x='86' y='100' width='18' height='24' rx='2' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 90 106 L 100 106 M 90 111 L 100 111 M 90 116 L 97 116' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 108 96 L 100 126' stroke-width='2.5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"tell me, what is it you plan to do with your one wild and precious life?","where":"the summer day, the last lines, in new and selected poems, volume one"},{"q":"you do not have to be good.","where":"wild geese, the first line, in dream work, 1986"},{"q":"her instructions for living a life come to three things. pay attention. be astonished. tell somebody.","where":"the poem sometimes, in red bird, 2008, paraphrased"}],"start":"new and selected poems, volume one: the summer day, wild geese, when death comes. then upstream, her essays, for how she lived.","status":"approved","story":"she was born in 1935 in maple heights, ohio, outside cleveland. she spent her childhood in the woods to get away from a house she didn't feel safe in. she wrote poems out there from about fourteen.\nas a teenager she wrote to the sister of edna st. vincent millay, one of the most famous american poets of the twenties, who had died a few years before. she ended up living for years at millay's old place, steepletop, in upstate new york, helping sort the papers. she went to ohio state and then vassar and didn't finish at either.\nat steepletop she met the photographer molly malone cook. they moved to provincetown, the old fishing town and artists' colony at the tip of cape cod, and stayed together over forty years, until molly died in 2005. molly handled the world so mary could write. mary walked. every morning, in the woods and the dunes and along the ponds, with a small notebook, writing down what she saw.\namerican primitive won the pulitzer prize, the big american prize for poetry, in 1984. her books sold like almost no poet's in america. she didn't do much publicity and didn't care much for the fuss. after molly died she moved to florida, and died there in january 2019, at eighty three.","struggle":"the childhood is the thing under everything, and she wrote about it only late, and only a little. she said the woods saved her. she said the poems started there as a way to get out.\nmolly's death in 2005 flattened her. she'd been with her since her twenties. she wrote about the grief in thirst and our world, and then left provincetown, where every path had molly on it. in her late seventies she was treated for lung cancer, and kept writing.","threads":["the land","grief","love"],"years":"1935 to 2019"},{"after":"he never scrubbed the record. in his letters he keeps saying it. i persecuted the church. i'm the least of the apostles, the first messengers sent out to spread the word, and i don't deserve the name. he didn't hide it and he didn't let it stop him either. he worked harder than all of them, he said, and then caught himself. not me, the grace in me.\nwhat he did with the thorn was the whole method. weakness was the point. beaten, shipwrecked, jailed, and writing joy from a cell. philippians is that, a short letter from prison that says rejoice twice in one line like he knew you'd argue.","basically":"a strict religious jew in the first century who went after the first followers of jesus, dragging them off to jail, until something knocked him flat on a road and he switched sides completely. then he spent thirty years walking and sailing around the roman empire, starting little churches in people's houses and writing them letters, and those letters make up a big part of the new testament, the christian half of the bible. imagine the loudest guy against something becoming its best organizer overnight, and never once hiding what he'd been. if you've ever done harm and wondered whether you still get to do good, he spent his whole life asking that out loud.","born":5,"died":64,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 20 250 C 100 245 300 255 380 250'/><path d='M 210 30 L 200 110 M 230 40 L 210 112 M 190 40 L 192 110' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='210' cy='30' r='14' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='150' cy='225' r='14'/><path d='M 164 228 L 220 236 M 220 236 L 250 250 M 220 236 L 240 224 M 185 231 L 180 210 M 195 232 L 205 214'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 200 L 350 200 L 350 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 310 218 L 340 218 M 310 232 L 340 232' opacity='.35'/><g transform='translate(305,173.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he was hunting christians when the light knocked him off the road, and then his name ended up on almost half the books in the new testament.","key":"paul","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paul_the_Apostle","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"acts, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/act.htm","why":"the book of acts, his road story, in the king james version"},{"label":"romans, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bib/kjv/rom.htm","why":"his letter to the romans in the king james version"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"he wrote a few lines about women keeping quiet in church and about slaves obeying their masters that have been used for two thousand years to hurt people, including by american slaveholders quoting him from the pulpit. scholars argue over which of those he actually wrote, because some of the letters with his name on them were probably written by his followers after he died. before the road he was hunting people down and dragging them to jail. he never pretended otherwise.","moments":["he and peter, the fisherman who'd been one of jesus's closest friends and was a leader of the first church, had a public fight in antioch, a big city in what's now southern turkey. peter had been eating with christians who weren't jewish, then stopped when some strict men came up from jerusalem, because a lot of jews then wouldn't share a table with non-jews. paul says he called him a hypocrite to his face, in front of everyone. the two founders of the church, going at it over dinner.","acts says he made tents for a living. in his own letters he says he worked night and day and preached free of charge, so he wouldn't be a burden on anyone.","on the way to rome as a prisoner, to be tried before the emperor, he was shipwrecked on malta, got bitten by a snake on the beach, and shook it off into the fire. the islanders had been waiting for him to swell up and drop dead, and when he didn't, they decided he was a god. acts doesn't say what he told them. but years earlier in a town called lystra, when a crowd tried to worship him, he tore his clothes and shouted that he was just a man like them."],"name":"paul","place":"tarsus, damascus, everywhere the roads went","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 70 C 44 86 52 104 60 110 C 68 104 76 86 80 70 C 72 80 48 80 40 70 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 16 112 L 46 104 M 16 112 C 12 116 20 120 20 114 M 46 104 C 50 100 44 98 44 104' /><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"in him we live and move and have our being, as some of your own poets have said.","where":"acts 17:28, which means the book of acts, chapter 17, verse 28; bible references work that way everywhere on these pages. the poets line points at we are his offspring, which is from aratus. in him we live is usually credited to epimenides, though scholars aren't sure. read all of chapter 17"},{"q":"love is patient. love is kind. it doesn't keep score.","where":"1 corinthians 13, from his first letter to the church in corinth, a rowdy port city in greece, paraphrased; read the whole chapter"},{"q":"i don't do the good i want. i do the thing i hate.","where":"romans 7:15 and 7:19, from his letter to the christians in rome, paraphrased; read all of chapter 7"}],"start":"acts 17 for the athens speech. 1 corinthians 13 for love. then philippians, his letter to the church in philippi, a town in northern greece, the whole thing. he wrote it from prison and it's short.","status":"approved","story":"saul of tarsus, a city near the south coast of what's now turkey, was a pharisee, one of the strictest groups of jewish teachers, the ones who carried every detail of god's law into daily life. acts says he was also a roman citizen, a legal status most people in the empire didn't have. he was on his way to damascus, in syria, to arrest followers of jesus, a new group of jews who said their executed teacher had come back from the dead. on the road a light hit him, he fell to the ground, and a voice asked, saul, why are you persecuting me. he got up blind. he spent three days blind in the city, not eating or drinking. when he could see again he was theirs. that's how the book of acts tells it. in his own letters he says only that he persecuted the church and that god revealed his son to him. he's better known by his roman name, paul.\nhe never met [[jesus]] in the flesh. he spent the next thirty years walking and sailing around the mediterranean starting churches, getting beaten, getting shipwrecked, and writing letters back to the churches when they screwed up, which was constantly. those letters are the oldest christian writing there is, older than the gospels, the four accounts of jesus's life that open the new testament.\nacts is the book of the bible about what the first christians did after jesus, and in chapter 17 paul, who's been arguing in the marketplace in athens, gets taken to the areopagus, the city's old high court, and stands up in front of the philosophers and quotes their own poets at them. the athenians had an altar in the city carved to an unknown god. paul told them that unknown god was the one he was talking about, and quoted greek poets from centuries before. in him we live and move and have our being, a line usually credited to epimenides, though scholars aren't sure. we are his offspring, which is from aratus. that's the move: the old texts talking to each other, and a preacher admitting the other guys had it first.\nhe was a hard man, sure of himself, and he wrote the best thing about love anybody ever has, the love is patient, love is kind passage that gets read at almost every church wedding. tradition says rome cut his head off under nero, the emperor who blamed the christians for the great fire of rome in 64 and had them killed in public, some of them burned alive as torches.","struggle":"he had something wrong with him that he never names. a thorn in the flesh, he calls it, a messenger of satan to keep him from getting too proud. people have been guessing what it was for two thousand years. he says he begged god three times to take it away and got told, my grace is enough for you, my power works best in weakness. he stopped asking.\nand romans 7, in his letter to the christians in rome, is the most honest paragraph in the book. i don't understand what i do. what i want to do, i don't do. what i hate, i do. the man whose name is on almost half the books in the new testament said he couldn't get his own hands to obey him. anybody who's sworn off something at midnight and done it again by lunch knows exactly what he means.","threads":["forgiveness","wandering"],"years":"around 5 to around 64"},{"after":"what she did with the rock was turn around and look at the arm that threw it. her whole teaching is that move. when the feeling comes, don't throw it and don't swallow it, stay with it a little longer than you can stand. the practice she gives out most is tonglen, tibetan for sending and taking. you breathe in the thing you don't want, the fear or the grief or the rage, for everybody who has it, and breathe out some relief for them. it's backwards from the meditation apps, where you breathe in calm and breathe out stress. she says start with your own pain, since it's right there.\nwhat she did with shambhala was leave without a speech and keep teaching, mostly on her own, into her eighties. as far as i know she hasn't written the full reckoning with trungpa.","basically":"an american schoolteacher with two kids and two marriages behind her who hit the worst rage of her life in her thirties, found tibetan buddhism in the middle of it, and became a nun. now she's the one whose book gets handed around hospital waiting rooms and passed between friends going through a divorce. she's the friend who doesn't tell you it'll be fine and just sits down in it with you. the one move to take from her is this. when the bad feeling comes, stay with it a little longer than you can stand.","born":1936,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='90' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 90 168 L 90 216'/><path d='M 90 216 L 76 250 M 90 216 L 104 250'/><path d='M 90 182 L 76 206 M 90 182 L 116 176'/><path d='M 118 172 C 170 90 250 80 300 240' stroke-dasharray='2 8' opacity='.55'/><path d='M 214 104 C 212 94 226 90 232 98 C 238 106 228 114 220 112 C 214 112 214 108 214 104 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 196 110 L 206 106 M 198 120 L 208 114' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 320 250 L 320 128 L 380 128 L 380 250'/><path d='M 380 130 L 352 138 L 352 244 L 380 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='338' cy='160' r='10'/><circle cx='334' cy='158' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='340' cy='158' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><g transform='translate(268,225.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"her husband came home and told her he was having an affair, she threw a rock at him, and everything she teaches starts there.","key":"pema","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pema_Ch%C3%B6dr%C3%B6n","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"her foundation","url":"https://pemachodronfoundation.org/","why":"the pema chödrön foundation, with her talks and books"},{"label":"the abbey","url":"https://gampoabbey.org/","why":"gampo abbey, the monastery on the cliff she directed"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"she stayed loyal for decades to her teacher, chögyam trungpa, who drank himself to death at forty seven and did real harm to people around him, and she defended him. when his son, who had inherited his organization, was reported in 2018 to have abused students, she stepped back, and in early 2020, when it looked like he'd return to teaching, she resigned as a senior teacher.","moments":["she says she read working with negativity and felt like the man was talking straight to her, about her, in the middle of the worst of it.","the tibetan word she's made famous is shenpa, the hook, the itch before you scratch it, the half second where you could still not send the text. taking the leap, from 2009, is a short book about nothing else.","she took her novice vows in 1974 from the sixteenth karmapa. the karmapa is the head of trungpa's branch of tibetan buddhism, and tibetans believe it's the same teacher reborn over and over, sixteen times by then, going back to the twelfth century."],"name":"pema chödrön","place":"new york, california, then a monastery on a cliff in nova scotia","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 100 C 52 108 74 108 90 98 L 98 130 L 44 130 Z' fill='var(--marigold)' opacity='.9'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"things don't really get solved. they come together and they fall apart.","where":"when things fall apart, her 1997 book, early on, paraphrased"},{"q":"only to the extent that we expose ourselves over and over to annihilation can that which is indestructible be found in us.","where":"when things fall apart, the opening chapters"},{"q":"you are the sky. everything else is just the weather.","where":"attributed to her everywhere; the idea runs through her talks, the sentence is hard to pin down"}],"start":"when things fall apart, the first three chapters. then the places that scare you, from 2001. no time to lose when you want her going through shantideva's handbook line by line.","status":"approved","story":"she was born deirdre blomfield-brown in new york in 1936. she married young, had two kids, divorced, taught elementary school in california, and married again.\none day the second husband came home and told her he was having an affair. she picked up a rock and threw it at him. she's told that story for decades as the moment the floor went out from under her, and the reason she went looking for anything that would hold.\nwhat held was a magazine article called working with negativity, by chögyam trungpa, a tibetan monk who'd fled when china took over tibet, ended up in the west, given up his robes, and was teaching americans in colorado. the article was about exactly the rage she was in. she met him in 1972, and in 1974 took novice vows, the first step of becoming a nun, head shaved and robes on. in 1981 she went to hong kong for full ordination, the complete set of vows a fully ordained monk takes. tibetan buddhism had never kept that line going for women, so she took it from the chinese tradition, which had. she was one of the first american women to do it.\nin the mid eighties she became director of gampo abbey, a monastery trungpa had founded on a cliff over the gulf of st lawrence on cape breton island, way up the nova scotia coast in canada. when things fall apart came out in 1997, edited from talks she'd given there, and it's the book people hand each other in hospital hallways. in 2005 she put out no time to lose, a line by line walk through the way of the bodhisattva, [[shantideva]]'s eighth century handbook on how to live for other people.","struggle":"the rage came first. she's described the anger after the second marriage ended as the most intense thing she'd ever felt, and she had no idea what to do with it. she was in her mid thirties with two kids.\nthen the monastery. she's said gampo abbey was the first place she couldn't run from herself, and that every habit she'd used to hold herself together came apart there. when things fall apart is her report from inside that, not a lecture from after it.","threads":["anger","family"],"years":"1936 to now"},{"after":"john 21, the last chapter of john's gospel, is the after, and it's the best one in the book. seven of them go back to fishing and catch nothing all night. at dawn a man on the shore tells them to throw the net on the right side, and it fills, a hundred and fifty three fish. when someone says it's the lord, peter puts his coat on, which is backwards, jumps into the water, and swims for it. on the beach there's a charcoal fire going with fish on it. the last time peter stood at a charcoal fire he said he didn't know him.\nafter breakfast jesus asks him three times, do you love me. three, for three. the third time peter's hurt, and says you know everything, you know i love you. jesus never says i forgive you. he says feed my sheep, meaning take care of my people, and tells him that when he's old someone else will dress him and lead him where he doesn't want to go. no speech about the denial. the question three times, and a job.","basically":"a fisherman from a lake in northern israel who dropped his nets to follow jesus and became his loudest, most eager friend, and then, the night jesus was arrested, swore three times he'd never met the man. after the execution, jesus found him back at his boat and asked him three times if he loved him, and gave him a job instead of a lecture. he went on to lead the first christians, and the catholic church counts him as the first pope. if you've ever sold out someone you loved right when it counted, he's the one who got asked back.","born":null,"died":64,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 250 C 39 247 78 253 130 250 S 221 248 260 250'/><path d='M 250 232 C 290 224 330 240 400 232 L 400 300 L 250 300 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 270 262 q 16 -8 32 0 q 16 -8 32 0 M 330 282 q 14 -8 28 0' stroke-width='2' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='330' cy='90' r='26' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 364 90 L 376 90'/><path d='M 354 114 L 363 123'/><path d='M 330 124 L 330 136'/><path d='M 306 114 L 297 123'/><path d='M 296 90 L 284 90'/><path d='M 306 66 L 297 57'/><path d='M 330 56 L 330 44'/><path d='M 354 66 L 363 57'/><ellipse cx='44' cy='238' rx='14' ry='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='58' cy='224' r='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 56 216 l -2 -6 l 5 3 l 2 -6 l 3 7' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 65 225 l 8 2 l -8 2' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 30 236 q -14 -16 -4 -26 q -4 10 8 18' stroke-width='2.4'/><path d='M 40 248 L 38 258 M 48 248 L 50 258' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='140' cy='168' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='138' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='148' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 139 172 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 140 182 L 140 215.44'/><path d='M 140 215.44 L 128 250 M 140 215.44 L 152 250'/><path d='M 140.0 190 L 106.0 192'/><path d='M 140.0 190 L 174.0 192'/><path d='M 191.0 250 L 221.0 244.0 M 221.0 250 L 191.0 244.0'/><path d='M 197.0 244.0 C 191.0 227.5 204.5 224.5 206 211.0 C 210.5 223.0 221.0 229.0 215.0 244.0 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='316' cy='230' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='308' cy='228' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='318' cy='228' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='313' cy='236' r='2.5' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 304 236 l -16 -14 M 328 234 l 14 -12' stroke-width='2.6'/><path d='M 296 240 l 4 -4' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 338 240 l 4 -4' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 346 222 L 352 236 L 390 236 L 398 222 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 372 222 L 372 190' stroke-width='2.4'/><g transform='translate(364,200.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he swore he'd die with him, said he'd never met him before the rooster crowed, and then got asked three times on a beach if he loved him.","key":"peter","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Peter","why":"the plain overview, the gospels and the traditions"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10","why":"the king james bible on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"cowardice, the plain kind, and a temper to go with it. the sword in the garden, which jesus told him to put away. the rebuke that got him called satan. in acts, the book right after the gospels about what the first christians did next, a couple named ananias and sapphira sell some land, lie about how much of the money they're giving the church, and drop dead at his feet, and he doesn't look sorry.\nand years later at antioch he'd been eating with gentile christians, which strict jews wouldn't do, until some men came from james, jesus's brother, who ran the church in jerusalem and was strict about jewish law. then peter quietly stopped and ate apart. paul says he did it out of fear, and that the other jewish christians followed him, even barnabas, paul's own partner.","moments":["fifty days after the crucifixion, at the jewish harvest festival of pentecost, in acts 2, the man who'd folded in front of a servant girl stood up in jerusalem and preached to a crowd. in acts 4 the council, the jewish high court that had handed jesus over, looks at him and john, sees unschooled, ordinary men, and notices they'd been with jesus.","in acts 10 he sees a sheet let down from the sky full of animals jewish law said never to eat, and a voice tells him to eat, three times. then the men of a roman centurion, an army officer, knock on the door, and he goes into a gentile's house, which he'd been raised never to do.","in acts 12 an angel walks him out of the prison of herod agrippa, the local king under rome, at night. he knocks at the gate of the house where the church is praying for him, and the servant girl, rhoda, is so glad she runs in to tell everyone and leaves him standing outside, still knocking.","the story goes, from a second century book called the acts of peter, that he was slipping out of rome ahead of nero and met jesus on the road going the other way. he asked where are you going, in latin quo vadis. to rome, to be crucified again. peter turned around."],"name":"peter","place":"capernaum on the sea of galilee, then jerusalem, antioch, rome","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><circle cx='94' cy='106' r='5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 97 110 L 108 124 M 104 119 L 108 116 M 107 123 L 111 120'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"go away from me, lord, i'm a sinful man.","where":"the gospel of luke, 5:8, after the net nearly broke with fish"},{"q":"i don't know the man.","where":"the gospel of matthew, 26:72, the second time he denied him"},{"q":"lord, you know everything, you know that i love you.","where":"the gospel of john, 21:17, the third time he was asked"}],"start":"john 21, the whole chapter, the last one in the gospel of john, near the start of the new testament in any bible. then mark 14 from verse 27 to the end, the promise, the fire, and the rooster. then galatians 2, one of paul's letters, for antioch.","status":"approved","story":"simon was a fisherman on the sea of galilee, a big freshwater lake in the north of what's now israel, with his brother andrew. he had a wife, and a mother-in-law who gets healed of a fever in the first chapter of mark, the shortest of the gospels, the four books that tell jesus's life. jesus called him cephas, which is aramaic, the language they spoke, for rock. in greek it comes out petros, peter. the nickname was rocky, more or less. the gospels spend the next three years showing you how little like a rock he was.\none night on the lake he saw [[jesus]] walking on the water and asked to come out to him. he got a few steps, looked at the wind, and sank. later he was the first to say out loud that jesus was the messiah, the king the jews had been promised who'd come and set everything right, and a few verses after that he took him aside to tell him he'd got it wrong about having to die. jesus said get behind me, satan, the name for the enemy. matthew, another of the gospels, fits all of it into chapters 14 through 16.\nat the last supper, the final meal jesus ate with his friends the night before he died, peter swore that even if he had to die with him he'd never deny him. that night, in the olive grove outside jerusalem where the soldiers came for jesus, he pulled a sword and cut off the ear of the high priest's servant, a man john names as malchus. the high priest was the top religious official in jerusalem, and the one who'd had jesus arrested. hours later, warming himself at a charcoal fire in the high priest's courtyard, peter told a servant girl and then others that he didn't know the man. the rooster crowed. luke says jesus turned and looked at him. he went out and wept bitterly.\nafter the crucifixion he went back to fishing, and that's where the best morning in the book happens, down in the after. then he preached in jerusalem, took the message into the house of a gentile, a non-jew, a line jews of his day didn't cross, and got into a public fight with [[paul]], the former persecutor who became christianity's great letter writer, at antioch, a big city in what's now turkey. tradition says he was crucified in rome under nero, the emperor who blamed the christians for the great fire of rome, upside down, at his own request, because he didn't think he deserved to die the way jesus had.","struggle":"the denial is the struggle, and it's specific. he'd said out loud, in front of all of them, that the others might fall away but he wouldn't. then a servant girl at a fire asked him a question and he folded. mark says he started cursing and swearing he didn't know him. all four gospels tell it and none of them clean it up.\nthen the three days between the execution and the empty tomb. the man he'd sworn to die with was dead, and the last thing peter had said about him in public was that he'd never known him. nobody wrote down what those days were like for him. you can guess.","threads":["forgiveness","love"],"years":"the first century, died in rome in the sixties by tradition"},{"after":"after the book he kept the tone careful. he pushed for research instead of rushing to legalize, talked about the risks in almost every interview, and wrote this is your mind on plants, from 2021, about caffeine, opium, and mescaline, which is as much about the law as the drugs.\nhe's the one on these pages who found this late and handled it like a grown up. no conversion, no robes, just a man saying it changed how he sees some things and here's exactly how.","basically":"the journalist who told america how to eat, famous for eat food, not too much, mostly plants, who in his late fifties decided to find out what the new psychedelic research was about and ended up trying it himself. how to change your mind, from 2018, is the book most people read first, and it's why a lot of middle aged people who never touched drugs in college started asking about psilocybin. he's a skeptic by temperament and it shows. if you want the careful, grown up tour, start here.","born":1955,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 200 L 170 200 L 170 250 M 40 208 L 170 208' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='100' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 100 164 L 100 200 M 100 176 L 130 196 M 100 176 L 76 196'/><rect x='118' y='192' width='28' height='8' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 220 250 L 220 60 L 380 60 L 380 250' fill='var(--sage)' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 300 60 L 300 250'/><path d='M 340 250 L 340 125'/><ellipse cx='340' cy='116' rx='25' ry='19' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 260 250 L 260 234 M 246 234 C 246 216 274 216 274 234 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><g transform='translate(236,231.1) scale(0.7)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a food writer in his sixties who'd never tripped went and did it, carefully, and wrote the book that made your aunt curious about mushrooms.","key":"pollan","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Pollan","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/How_to_Change_Your_Mind","why":"the 2018 book, and the netflix series made from it"},{"label":"the publisher","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/529343/how-to-change-your-mind-by-michael-pollan/","why":"penguin's page for how to change your mind"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"the fair criticism is that the book is one comfortable man's view, and that describing his own underground trips in a bestseller is a kind of advertising whether he means it or not. he's said versions of that himself.","moments":["for how to change your mind he interviewed [[griffiths|roland griffiths]] at length and sat in on the world of the hopkins studies.","he tried psilocybin, lsd, and the venom of a desert toad, which has a very strong short acting psychedelic in it. the toad was the one that scared him."],"name":"michael pollan","place":"long island, then connecticut, then berkeley, california","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 32 56 C 22 50 22 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 98 50 98 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><rect x='41' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><rect x='62' y='49' width='17' height='13' rx='2'/><path d='M 58 55 L 62 55 M 41 54 L 34 52 M 79 54 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"eat food. not too much. mostly plants.","where":"in defense of food, 2008, the first line"},{"q":"i came to it as a skeptic and a journalist, not a seeker.","where":"how to change your mind, 2018, paraphrased from the prologue"},{"q":"spiritual might just mean the opposite of egotistical.","where":"how to change your mind, the chapters on his own trips, paraphrased"}],"start":"how to change your mind. the prologue and chapter 1, then his own trips in chapter 4. or the netflix series if you'd rather watch.","status":"approved","story":"he's a journalist and professor who made his name writing about plants and food. the omnivore's dilemma, from 2006, followed four meals back to where they came from, a fast food meal, organic, and a meal he hunted and gathered himself. in defense of food, from 2008, boiled it down to seven words. he's taught at berkeley and harvard.\nin the early 2010s he got curious about the new research at johns hopkins and nyu, where people with cancer were being given psilocybin and coming out less afraid of dying. he wrote about it for the new yorker, the magazine, in 2015. then he spent years on a book.\nhow to change your mind came out in 2018. it's three things at once. a history of psychedelics, from [[hofmann|albert hofmann]]'s bicycle ride and [[wasson|gordon wasson]]'s trip to [[sabina|maría sabina]]'s town through [[leary|timothy leary]]'s crash. a report on the new science, with [[griffiths|roland griffiths]] at the center, and the brain scans that show the default mode network, the part of the brain that keeps the running story of me going, quieting down on psilocybin. and his own trips, taken around the age of sixty with underground guides, told plainly, including the scary parts. it was a bestseller, and in 2022 netflix made it into a four part series.\nin 2020 he helped start the uc berkeley center for the science of psychedelics. he's careful to say he's not an evangelist, and that these drugs can do real harm.","struggle":"he said he was scared going in. a lifelong worrier in his late fifties, with a family, deciding to take drugs he'd avoided all his life. one of his trips was frightening, and he wrote that the fear was real and that the guides were the reason it didn't turn into something worse.\nhis bigger worry since the book is the hype. he's said plainly that psychedelics are being oversold, and he knows his own book is part of how people got excited.","threads":["psychedelics","doubt"],"years":"born 1955"},{"after":"after the surrender he decided to be useful in the new world instead of dying in the old one. he went to washington again and again for his people, dealt with the ranchers, and opened the star house to anybody who came, white or comanche. whatever he took, he also gave away a lot.\nand he went looking for his mother. he found where she was buried in texas, and in 1910 he had her body brought to oklahoma and reburied near him. a year later he died and was buried next to her. they're both at fort sill now.","basically":"the last war chief of the comanche, the horse nation that ruled the southern plains, and the son of a white woman who'd been kidnapped by them as a child and never wanted to go back. he fought the army until 1875, surrendered, and then turned into a businessman who made deals with texas cattlemen, rode in a president's inaugural parade, and spent nights in a tipi praying with peyote. think of a guerrilla commander who becomes a ceo and a religious founder, and never quite stops being any of the three. take from him that surviving can mean becoming someone nobody expected, on purpose.","born":1852,"died":1911,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 160 L 170 160 L 170 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 162 L 105 118 L 180 162' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 205 L 170 205' opacity='.4'/><rect x='60' y='174' width='18' height='20' fill='var(--blue)'/><rect x='130' y='174' width='18' height='20' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 96 250 L 96 218 L 116 218 L 116 250' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 80 146 L 83 138 L 86 146 L 78 141 L 88 141 Z M 120 146 L 123 138 L 126 146 L 118 141 L 128 141 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 235 250 L 290 150 L 345 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 284 140 L 290 150 L 296 140'/><path d='M 282 250 L 290 226 L 298 250' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.6'/><circle cx='220' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 220 168 L 220 216'/><path d='M 220 216 L 206 250 M 220 216 L 234 250'/><path d='M 220 182 L 204 206 M 220 182 L 236 206'/><path d='M 200 144 C 206 140 234 140 240 144 M 208 142 C 208 128 232 128 232 142 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 300 170 C 296 160 306 152 302 142 M 306 170 C 312 160 304 152 310 140' opacity='.4'/><g transform='translate(290,133.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the son of a stolen white girl and a comanche war leader, he fought the army to the end, then got rich on cattle and led all-night peyote prayers.","key":"quanahparker","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quanah_Parker","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Native_American_Church","why":"the peyote church he helped start"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"he had eight wives over his life and several at once, which was ordinary for a comanche leader and scandalous to the government, which ordered him to give up all but one. the story goes he told the agent he would, as soon as the agent picked which ones to send away and went and told them himself. the agent didn't.\nthe money is the real mess. texas cattlemen who leased comanche land paid him personally on the side, starting in the 1880s, and a lot of comanches believed he was selling out the tribe's grass for his own house. he got richer than anybody around him, and not all of it was clean.","moments":["in march 1905, at theodore roosevelt's inauguration in washington, he rode in the parade with other native leaders, geronimo among them.","at adobe walls a hunter named billy dixon shot a warrior off a horse nearly a mile away. he said himself it was luck."],"name":"quanah parker","place":"the staked plains of texas, then fort sill and cache, in what's now oklahoma","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 35 70 C 33 86 35 100 37 114 M 85 70 C 87 86 85 100 83 114' stroke-width='6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the white man goes into his church and talks about jesus. the indian goes into his tipi and talks to jesus.","where":"attributed to him, and quoted by peyote people ever since; there's no dated source"},{"q":"no kill my father. he not there.","where":"what he said about the rangers' claim they'd killed peta nocona at the pease river; he said his father died a few years later, sick"},{"q":"my mother loved the indians and the wild life so much, she never wanted to go back to the white people.","where":"attributed, from when he had her body moved to be buried near him in 1910, paraphrased"}],"start":"s. c. gwynne's empire of the summer moon, from 2010, the big readable book about quanah and the comanches. it's by a white journalist, so read it knowing whose telling it is.","status":"approved","story":"in 1836 comanches raided fort parker, a family settlement in texas, and carried off a nine year old girl named cynthia ann parker. she grew up comanche, forgot english, married a war leader named peta nocona, and had three children. quanah was the oldest, born probably around 1852. in december 1860 texas rangers attacked the family's camp on the pease river and took cynthia ann and her baby daughter back to her white relatives. she didn't want to go. she never adjusted, the baby died in 1864, and cynthia ann died in 1871. quanah was a boy when she was taken, and he never saw her again.\nhe grew up with the kwahadi, the most remote comanche band, out on the staked plains of west texas, and became a war leader young. on june 27, 1874, he led hundreds of warriors against a post of white buffalo hunters at a place called adobe walls, in the texas panhandle. the hunters had long range rifles and held them off, his horse was shot out from under him, and he was hit. the army answered with the red river war, and in june 1875 he brought his band into fort sill, in indian territory, now oklahoma. they were among the last comanches to surrender.\nthen he changed completely. he took his mother's last name. he learned to deal with white cattlemen and leased comanche grazing land to texas ranchers, and got rich doing it. he built a big two story house near cache, oklahoma, with white stars painted on the roof, which everybody called the star house. in 1905 he rode in theodore roosevelt's inaugural parade, and that april roosevelt came to hunt wolves with him.\nand he found peyote, a small cactus with a mind-altering drug in it, mescaline. the story goes that after a bull gored him in texas, a mexican healer gave him peyote tea, and he got well. he became a leader of the peyote road, the all-night prayer meetings in a tipi led by a road man, where people eat peyote, sing, and pray until morning. he died of heart failure at the star house on february 23, 1911. the native american church, the legal home of that way of praying, was incorporated in oklahoma in 1918, and he's counted as one of its founders.","struggle":"he lost his mother to the rangers when he was a boy, and his father a few years later, from old wounds and grief, by quanah's account. by his teens he was on his own with a band that was being hunted.\nthen surrender. he went from the freest life on the plains to standing in line at an army post for rations. everything he'd been trained for was over, while he was still a young man, and he had to figure out what a comanche man was for now.","threads":["psychedelics","war","money","family"],"years":"probably 1852, some say earlier, to 1911"},{"after":"the morning after the lamp, the way the story goes, she walked out of that house free and went into the desert, and when she came back she found a small room and prayed in it. she never married anyone and never took the money. there's no redemption arc here because the slavery wasn't her mess. it was done to her. what she did with the freedom was give all of it to god.\nthen came her long afterlife in other people's books. the big sufi writers of the next few centuries quoted her verses about two kinds of love for god and wrote pages explaining them. by the time attar got to her she was already a legend, and the legend kept growing. the woman herself mostly disappeared into it, but the idea she's credited with never did.","basically":"a woman in eighth century iraq who, the stories say, was sold as a slave when she was a kid, got freed, and then stayed poor and single on purpose her whole life. she's the one credited with bringing the idea of loving god for god's own sake, not for a reward and not out of fear, into sufism, the mystical side of islam. almost everything about her was written down centuries after she died, so she's part person and part legend. it's the difference between the job you do for the paycheck and the thing you'd do for free, and she said god should be the second one.","born":717,"died":801,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--teal)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='330' cy='60' r='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 196 142 L 176 226 L 220 226 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 186 226 L 184 248 M 210 226 L 212 248'/><path d='M 184 128 C 180 104 212 104 208 128 C 210 140 206 148 198 146 C 196 148 184 146 184 128 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='196' cy='124' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 182 122 C 182 104 210 104 210 122' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 194 160 L 162 176 M 198 160 L 228 180'/><path d='M 160 190 L 164 150'/><path d='M 164 150 C 150 138 160 124 166 114 C 168 126 180 132 170 148 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 228 180 C 222 188 244 188 238 180' /><path d='M 220 190 L 246 190 L 242 214 L 224 214 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 226 222 L 226 228 M 238 224 L 238 232' stroke='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 110 70 C 120 60 132 64 140 58 M 262 90 C 272 82 284 86 292 80' opacity='.4'/><g transform='translate(34,223) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"she wanted to burn down heaven and put out hell so people would love god for nothing.","key":"rabia","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rabia_of_Basra","why":"the plain overview, and honest about what's legend"},{"label":"rabi'a the mystic, free online","url":"https://archive.org/details/margart_smith_rabia","why":"margaret smith's 1928 book, the first big english life of her"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tazkirat_al-Awliya","why":"the book almost all her stories come from"}],"lineage":"muslim","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["on the road to mecca, the holy city every muslim hopes to make the pilgrimage to, she's supposed to have stopped in the desert and said she needed the lord of the house, not the house. the kaaba, the cube shaped shrine at the center of mecca, then came out to meet her. ibrahim ibn adham, a prince who'd given up his throne to live poor, took fourteen years to get to mecca and found the shrine missing because it had gone to her.","the governor of basra offered her a hundred thousand gold coins as a dowry and ten thousand a month on top. she told him it didn't please her that he should be her slave and everything he owned should be hers, or that he should pull her away from god for a single moment.","another ascetic, a man famous for giving up the world, proposed too. in margaret smith's translation she told him, o sensual one, seek another sensual like thyself. hast thou seen any sign of desire in me?","in one telling hasan asks to marry her and she asks him four questions about death and judgment that nobody alive can answer. then she says that since she has those four to think about, how would she have time for a husband."],"name":"rabia of basra","place":"basra, in what's now southern iraq","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 20 130 C 22 104 38 94 60 94 C 82 94 98 104 100 130' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 26 60 C 22 22 98 22 94 60 C 96 80 92 96 84 104 L 36 104 C 28 96 24 80 26 60 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 54 C 32 26 88 26 90 54 C 80 38 40 38 30 54 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 48 58 C 50 60 54 60 56 58 M 64 58 C 66 60 70 60 72 58'/><path d='M 60 62 L 58 68 L 62 68'/><path d='M 54 76 C 57 78 63 78 66 76'/><path d='M 102 124 L 106 96'/><path d='M 106 96 C 98 88 102 80 106 74 C 108 82 114 86 110 94 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"if i worship you out of fear of hell, burn me in it. if i worship you hoping for paradise, shut me out of it. if i worship you for yourself, don't keep yourself from me.","where":"attributed to her and paraphrased. the prayer comes down through later sufi writers, not from anything she wrote"},{"q":"it is the lord of the house whom i need, what have i to do with the house?","where":"attributed; said on the road to mecca in attar's memorial of the saints, as margaret smith translates it in rabi'a the mystic, 1928, page 9"},{"q":"my peace, o my brothers, is in solitude, and my beloved is with me alway.","where":"attributed; verses from the stories of her turning down marriage, as margaret smith translates them in rabi'a the mystic, 1928, in the chapter on her life"}],"start":"margaret smith's rabi'a the mystic and her fellow saints in islam, from 1928, which is free online and says plainly which stories are legend. then the rabia chapter of attar's memorial of the saints in any english version, for the legend in full.","status":"approved","story":"rabia was a muslim holy woman in basra, a port city in the south of what's now iraq that was one of the big centers of learning and trade in the islamic world. as best anyone can tell she lived from around 717 to 801, and even those dates come from much later and nobody's sure where they started. here's the honest problem with everything else. she didn't leave a book. no writing has ever been tied to her for certain. a few writers in the century after her mention her, and most of the stories people tell come from the memorial of the saints, a persian book of the lives and miracles of muslim holy people, written by [[attar]], a persian poet and mystic who died around 1221. that's about four hundred years after her. so read the rest as the story goes, not as a court record.\nthe story goes that she was the fourth daughter of a poor family, which is what rabia means, the fourth. her parents died, a famine hit basra, the sisters got scattered, and a man grabbed her and sold her as a slave for six small coins. she spent her nights praying. one night her master saw a lamp hanging over her head with no chain holding it up, lighting the whole house. it scared him, and he set her free.\nafter that she went out into the desert for a while, then got herself a small room and gave her life to prayer. she stayed poor and she stayed single, and men kept asking. the famous suitor in the stories is hasan al-basri, the great preacher of basra, a man famous for poverty and the fear of god. it can't be true the way it's told. hasan died in 728, when she'd have been about eleven. margaret smith, the english scholar who wrote the first big book on her in 1928, said it's chronologically almost impossible, and there's no evidence the two of them ever met.\nwhat she's remembered for is one idea. the holy men of basra before her, hasan included, preached poverty and a trembling fear of god. rabia is credited with saying you should love god for god's own sake. not to get into paradise, not to stay out of hell. the encyclopedias call her the first to put it that way in sufism, the mystical side of islam, the people who want to meet god directly and not only follow the rules. in the decades after her, that kind of mysticism spread all over the islamic world.\nthe story that carries it goes like this. someone saw her with a burning torch in one hand and a bucket of water in the other and asked where she was going. to heaven, she said, to throw fire on paradise and water on hell, so both of them disappear and people look at god with no hope and no fear. it's a late story. the version with her name on it was written down by aflaki, a biographer of rumi, in the 1300s, more than five hundred years after she died. around the same time joinville, a french knight writing the life of his king, had heard the same legend, which makes her the first sufi to turn up in a european book.","struggle":"the stories give her about the hardest start there is. orphaned, a famine, sold as a child, praying through the nights while she belonged to someone else. some tellings add that after she was freed she spent a while as a musician, following a flute player, before she turned all the way to god. a french scholar of sufism points out that the sinful woman who turns holy is a very common plot in christian saints' lives too, which is a good reason to doubt that part.\nthe harder thing is what she chose once she was free. no husband, no money, no protector, in a city that ran on trade. she said her peace was in solitude and her beloved was with her always. whatever you make of that, she kept choosing it every time somebody offered her a way out, and the offers were real money.","threads":["love","money","loneliness"],"years":"around 717 to 801"},{"after":"after the visions he didn't become a saint on a hill. he stayed at the temple by the river and talked to whoever came, in bengali, his own language, with jokes. the gospel of sri ramakrishna is just a diary of those conversations, and it's the one book in this whole forest that's mostly laughing.\nthe last year, dying, he taught from a bed in a rented garden house at cossipore, on the edge of calcutta, and made vivekananda promise to keep the boys together, the handful of young men who'd become his first monks. vivekananda did, and seven years later stood up in chicago and told america that all religions are true. that's the after. a barely literate priest's disciples built the ramakrishna mission, monks and schools and hospitals, on five continents.","basically":"a village kid from bengal, in eastern india, born in 1836, who could barely read, became the priest at a big temple outside calcutta, and spent years so overwhelmed by god that his family thought he'd lost his mind. then he did something almost nobody does. he lived as a muslim for a while, then as a christian, to see where they went, and came back saying they all get you to the same place. his student carried that to america in 1893, and a lot of the all-paths-up-the-same-mountain talk you hear today runs back through him. if you've ever wanted to test a religion instead of just believing it, he's the one who actually did.","born":1836,"died":1886,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 196 C 60 188 120 204 180 196 S 300 188 400 196 L 400 300 L 0 300 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='110' cy='126' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='107' cy='124' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='117' cy='124' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 108 130 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 110 140 L 106 174 L 132 196 L 94 196'/><path d='M 109 148 L 137 158'/><path d='M 109 148 L 149 174'/><circle cx='160' cy='226' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 152 222 l 4 -2 M 164 222 l 4 -2' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='176' cy='214' r='3' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='184' cy='204' r='4' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='190' cy='192' r='5' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 300 196 L 300 150 L 356 150 L 356 196 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 294 150 L 328 118 L 362 150 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 322 196 L 322 172 L 334 172 L 334 196' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 0 196 L 110 196 M 300 196 L 400 196' stroke-width='2.6'/><g transform='translate(318,108.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a temple priest who tried being a muslim, then a christian, to check, and came back saying it's all the same water.","key":"ramakrishna","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramakrishna","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Gospel_of_Sri_Ramakrishna","why":"the record of his conversations"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["his first real teacher was a woman, the bhairavi brahmani, a wandering tantric, which means a follower of the hidden side of hinduism that works through the body and ritual. she showed up at the temple in 1861 and told him his madness was the highest state, that the old texts described it, and that she'd seen it before. nobody had said that to him.","then a naked wandering monk named totapuri taught him advaita vedanta, the hindu teaching that you and god and everything are one thing and the separate self is a mistake. ramakrishna went into a state so deep he didn't move for three days. totapuri had taken forty years to get there.","the story goes that his hand would curl up and refuse if anyone put money in it. he tested it. it kept happening."],"name":"ramakrishna","place":"dakshineswar, outside calcutta","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 70 C 46 84 74 84 80 70' stroke-dasharray='1 4' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"as many faiths, so many paths.","where":"the gospel of sri ramakrishna, the record of his conversations kept by a schoolteacher named mahendranath gupta, who signed it only m; it's the refrain of the whole book"},{"q":"when you want god the way a drowning man wants air, you'll find him.","where":"the gospel of sri ramakrishna, the story of the teacher holding a student's head under the water, paraphrased"},{"q":"the winds of god's grace are always blowing. you have to raise the sail.","where":"his sayings, as collected by his disciples, paraphrased"}],"start":"the gospel of sri ramakrishna, in swami nikhilananda's translation, done in new york in the 1940s by a monk of his order. it's a diary of his conversations and it runs over a thousand pages. open it anywhere.","status":"approved","story":"he was a barely literate village boy who became the priest of a kali temple on the ganges at dakshineswar, just north of calcutta. kali is the hindu mother goddess, drawn black with her tongue out and a necklace of heads, terrifying to look at and, to the people who love her, a mother. a temple priest in india takes care of the god every day. he wakes the statue, bathes it, dresses it, feeds it and waves lamps in front of it, the way you'd care for someone you loved. ramakrishna did that and then couldn't stop. he spent years in states of god-drunk ecstasy that his relatives took for insanity. he'd fall down in the road. he'd weep for the goddess like a kid crying for his mother. people came to see him the way they'd come to see a fire.\nthen he did something nobody does. in 1866 he lived as a muslim for a stretch under a muslim teacher, saying the muslim prayers and eating the food, and had a vision. in 1874 he had someone read him the bible, did the same with christianity, and had a vision of [[jesus]] walking toward him. he came back and said what he'd say for the rest of his life: as many faiths, so many paths. his picture for it was a lake with steps down to the water on every side. hindus come down one side and call what they drink jal. muslims come down another and call it pani. the english come down a third and call it water. it's all the same water.\nthe best story about him is one he told, about a teacher and a student, not about himself. a young man asked his teacher how to see god. the teacher took him to the water and held his head under until he fought for air. when you want god the way you just wanted air, the teacher said, you'll see him.\nhis star disciple was vivekananda, a sharp, skeptical college kid from calcutta who'd walked in demanding to know if ramakrishna had actually seen god, and got told yes, more clearly than i see you. vivekananda became a monk and took the message to chicago in 1893, to the parliament of the world's religions, a big gathering held alongside the world's fair. he opened with sisters and brothers of america, and the crowd, thousands of people, applauded for minutes before he could go on. everything on these pages that assumes the traditions are talking about one thing runs partly through a barely literate priest who checked.","struggle":"for years his family thought he was insane. he'd fall down in the road. he'd stop in the middle of the temple worship and stare. he stopped sleeping. they married him off at twenty three to a five year old girl to settle him, which tells you what the eighteen hundreds thought a cure looked like. she stayed with her own family until she was grown, and she became a teacher in her own right, sarada devi, whom his followers call the holy mother.\nthe ecstasy wasn't gentle. he describes the longing for the goddess as a physical pain that made him rub his face in the dirt and weep, and it went on for years.\nhe died of throat cancer at fifty, and in the last months his disciples begged him to ask the divine mother, his name for kali, to cure him. he said he couldn't. she'd put it there.","threads":["madness","joy"],"years":"1836 to 1886"},{"after":"his mother came back years later to live with him on the hill, and he looked after her until she died in 1922. they buried her at the foot of arunachala and he moved down to the grave, and the ashram grew up around it. the boy who wouldn't go home ended up building a home around his mother.\nat the end, with the arm eaten through, people begged him not to leave them. he said they gave too much importance to the body. on the evening of april 14, 1950, the people outside his room started singing arunachala shiva, a hymn to the hill, and he died while they were singing.","basically":"a teenager from south india who was hit out of nowhere by a terror of dying, lay down on the floor and went all the way into it, and never got scared again. he spent the next fifty four years on one hill, mostly sitting quietly, and people came from all over the world just to be in the room with him. picture the calmest person you've ever met, then imagine that's all they did, for half a century. take one question from him, who am i, and don't take anybody's word for the answer, his included.","born":1879,"died":1950,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 30 250 C 90 210 150 70 210 60 C 262 70 300 180 380 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 210 58 C 204 48 210 40 210 34 C 214 40 218 48 210 58 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='96' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 96 192 L 96 234'/><path d='M 96 234 C 76 234 66 250 82 250 L 110 250 C 126 250 116 234 96 234'/><path d='M 96 208 L 82 234 M 96 208 L 110 234'/><path d='M 90 176 C 92 178 94 178 96 176 M 98 176 C 100 178 102 178 104 176'/><ellipse cx='300' cy='224' rx='34' ry='15' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 280 236 L 278 250 M 292 238 L 292 250 M 310 238 L 310 250 M 322 236 L 324 250 M 334 220 C 342 230 340 240 336 244'/><ellipse cx='262' cy='212' rx='10' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 256 205 L 250 198 M 266 204 L 270 197'/><g transform='translate(252,78.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"at sixteen he lay down on the floor and practiced being dead, and got up knowing the thing that dies isn't him.","key":"ramana","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ramana_Maharshi","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"britannica","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Ramana-Maharshi","why":"a short, sober encyclopedia entry"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Self-enquiry_(Ramana_Maharshi)","why":"his one method, the question who am i"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["a cow named lakshmi came to the ashram in the twenties and more or less joined. he treated her like one of the devotees. when she died in 1948 she was buried with full rites in the ashram grounds, and he said she'd been liberated, meaning free of the round of being born and dying again.","the story goes that at the moment he died a big shooting star crossed the sky toward the hill. henri cartier-bresson, the french photographer, one of the most famous of the century, was at the ashram and saw it.","the english novelist somerset maugham visited in 1938. his 1944 novel the razor's edge is about a young american who goes to india looking for something, and the holy man he finds there is at least partly ramana."],"name":"ramana maharshi","place":"madurai, then the hill of arunachala at tiruvannamalai, for fifty four years","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 70 C 46 84 74 84 80 70' stroke-dasharray='1 4' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 104 130 L 108 40' stroke-width='4'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the question who am i will burn up every other thought, and in the end it burns up too, like the stick used to stir a funeral pyre.","where":"who am i?, a short booklet of his answers to a follower named sivaprakasam pillai, paraphrased. the stick that stirs a cremation fire gets thrown in at the end"},{"q":"they say i'm dying, but i'm not going anywhere. where would i go?","where":"said to his attendants in his last days, 1950, as they remembered it; paraphrased"},{"q":"happiness is your own nature, and it's not wrong to want it. the mistake is looking for it outside.","where":"talks with sri ramana maharshi, the notes of conversations in his hall, paraphrased"}],"start":"who am i?, which is about twenty pages. then be as you are, the collection of his talks edited by david godman.","status":"approved","story":"venkataraman was an ordinary schoolboy from the tamil-speaking south of india, living at his uncle's house in madurai, a big temple city down there. in the summer of 1896, when he was sixteen, alone in an upstairs room and perfectly healthy, a terror of death came over him out of nowhere.\nhe didn't run from it. he lay down, stiffened his body like a corpse, held his breath, and asked what dies. the body would be carried off and burned. something was still there, watching the whole thing. he said the fear left that day and never came back.\nsix weeks later he left a note saying he'd gone in search of his father, which meant god, and not to spend money looking for him, and took the train toward arunachala. that's a hill at the town of tiruvannamalai that hindus have treated for centuries as the god shiva himself, in the form of a mountain. he got there on september 1, 1896, and never left. for years he barely spoke. he sat in a dark vault under the big temple in town, then in caves on the hill.\nhis whole method was one question. who am i? whatever thought comes up, ask who it's coming to, and follow the i back to where it starts. mostly he taught it by sitting in a room and saying nothing, and people said that's where it happened. in the thirties the westerners started coming. he died in 1950 at the ashram at the foot of the hill, ashram being the hindu word for a community that grows up around a teacher, part retreat house and part commune.","struggle":"the struggle in his story is the body, and what other people wanted from it. in the vault under the temple, an underground shrine room called the patala lingam, insects ate sores into his thighs and he didn't notice. local boys threw stones down at him. an older holy man, seshadri swamigal, chased them off, and in the end people carried him out.\nin 1898 his mother found him and begged him to come home. he wouldn't speak. finally he wrote on a scrap of paper that what's meant to happen will happen however hard you try to stop it, and what isn't won't, and the best thing is to stay quiet. she went home without him.\nin 1949 a growth came up on his left elbow. it was a sarcoma, a cancer of the flesh and bone. there were operations, and his people begged him to try every other cure too, and it kept coming back. he let them try. it didn't trouble him, and that troubled them.","threads":["dying","the body","silence"],"years":"1879 to 1950"},{"after":"in 1976 he wrote a piece in yoga journal, the main magazine of the american yoga world, called egg on my beard. he said in print that he'd been conned, that he'd conned others along with him, and that the guru who'd told him to tell the truth had apparently meant it. no spin, no wisdom-of-the-lesson, just i was full of shit, and here's how.\nthen he went and did the least glamorous things a famous guru could do. in 1978 he co-founded seva, a charity that pays for cataract surgeries so poor people in nepal and india who've gone blind can see again. he spent years with stephen levine, a poet and meditation teacher, sitting with dying people. he backed a project that mailed meditation books to people in prison. he said the stroke finally made him what he'd been pretending to be, somebody who had to be helped.","basically":"a harvard psychology professor with a mercedes and a drinking problem who took magic mushrooms with a colleague in 1961, got fired for it, went to india, and came back with a beard and a new name. picture a tenured guy with the tesla and the anxiety who throws it all over for an old man in a blanket. the part that matters is what came after. he spent fifty years getting it wrong in public and telling you exactly how, right up through a stroke that took his speech.","born":1931,"died":2019,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 240 L 380 240'/><path d='M 90 150 C 60 150 50 240 90 240 L 150 240 C 190 240 180 150 150 150 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='120' cy='138' r='14'/><path d='M 115 143 Q 120 147 125 143'/><path d='M 178 190 C 210 140 250 130 285 150' stroke-dasharray='2 9'/><circle cx='292' cy='152' r='9' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 292 143 L 295 137'/><circle cx='320' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 320 164 L 320 212'/><path d='M 320 212 L 306 246 M 320 212 L 334 246'/><path d='M 320 178 L 300 154 M 320 178 L 340 154'/><path d='M 315 155 Q 320 159 325 155'/><path d='M 285 60 Q 285 42 308 44 Q 319 26 341 38 Q 360 30 365 48 Q 375 58 360 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 300 78 L 292 100 M 340 78 L 350 100' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(40,210.3) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"favorite":"my favorite person in the whole forest. he gets flowers.","hit":"he was a harvard psychologist named richard alpert until a man in a blanket knew what he was thinking.","key":"ramdass","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ram_Dass","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his foundation","url":"https://www.ramdass.org/","why":"the love serve remember foundation, with his talks, podcasts, and books"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Be_Here_Now_(book)","why":"the 1971 book that made him"},{"label":"the trees answer","url":"https://www.ramdass.org/judging-less-harshly/","why":"his answer on judging yourself less harshly, from his foundation"},{"label":"loving awareness","url":"https://www.ramdass.org/?p=6214","why":"the practice in his own words, with a video, from his foundation"},{"label":"little shmoos","url":"https://beherenownetwork.com/ram-dass-here-and-now-ep-16-little-shmoos/","why":"episode 16 of the here and now podcast"},{"label":"sara davidson","url":"https://saradavidson.com/?p=64","why":"ram dass has a son, by the writer who was there when he found out"}],"lineage":"hindu","love":[{"d":"every teacher in here has a mess. he's the one who went and printed his. the fame going to his head is something we mostly know because he said so. when he got conned, he wrote the article about getting conned. show the mess, then show what they did with it. this whole place runs on that, and it's his move.","t":"he told on himself first."},{"d":"he said that after all those years of practice he still had every one of his neuroses. what changed is they used to be huge and frightening and they'd take him over. now they're little shmoos, friendly little things, and he invites them in for tea. a shmoo is a friendly blob from an old comic strip. that's a whole method, and you can use it by lunch.","t":"he's funny about it."},{"d":"he grew up jewish in boston and remembered his bar mitzvah as an empty ritual. a hindu holy man gave him his name. then at sixty he went back and wrote that he wasn't born into judaism by accident, and that he needed to find ways to honor that. he never asked anybody to sign up for anything. he kept pointing at the thing they're all pointing at.","t":"he never picked a team."},{"d":"mushrooms opened the door for him and he never pretended otherwise. he also never pretended they were the house. you can visit, he'd say, and you can't stay. that's about as honest as anybody famous has been on the subject.","t":"he was straight about the drugs."},{"d":"it would have been easy to stay on the stage. he helped start a charity for blind people, sat with the dying, and got books into prison cells. then he wrote how can i help? with paul gorman, a whole book about what helping is, and how it goes sideways when you need to be the helper.","t":"he went and worked."},{"d":"he'd made his living talking, and the stroke took most of his words. he said it took his faith for a while too. then he taught for another twenty two years, slower, from a wheelchair. if you've built a life on what your body can do, this is the part to read twice.","t":"his body quit and he didn't."},{"d":"somebody asked him how to quit judging yourself so hard. he said, go out in the woods and look at the trees. some are bent and some are straight, and you don't hold it against them. you figure each one grew that way for a reason, and you let it be a tree. then you get near people and it's all too this and too that. so he practiced turning people into trees. if this forest has a house rule, that's it.","t":"he turned people into trees."}],"mess":"he got fired from harvard partly for giving psilocybin, the mushroom drug, to an undergraduate. the one promise he and leary had made the university was that they'd keep it away from undergraduates, and he broke it. he was honest about it later. the drugs were the door, he'd say for decades, but they weren't the room. they could show you the place and they couldn't let you live there.","moments":["his father george alpert was a boston lawyer and president of the new haven railroad, one of the big east coast rail lines, and called him rum dum for years. eventually the old man came around. ram dass took care of him at the end.","he gave the royalties from be here now to the hanuman foundation, the nonprofit he set up, named for the monkey god in hindu stories who's the perfect servant, and never took them back. that's why a man with a two million copy bestseller lived pretty small until friends bought him the house in maui.","in his last years on maui he ended nearly every talk the same way. i'm loving awareness, and so are you, and we're just walking each other home.","in 1967 he gave maharaj-ji 915 micrograms of lsd, about nine times a normal dose, to see what the old man would make of it. as he tells it in be here now, nothing happened. not everybody buys that. skeptics, [[mckenna|terence mckenna]] among them, figured the old man palmed the pills. what ram dass took from it stuck either way. the old man told him you could get to the same place without it.","at seventy eight he found out he had a son. a letter came from a man in maine that said, i believe you may be the father of my older brother. his first thought was that somebody was trying to hustle him. then a dna test said it was true. the son, peter, was a banker in north carolina, from something brief at stanford when ram dass was twenty four.","in the nineties he started talking in public about being bisexual, about having been with men as well as women. he was a famous spiritual teacher in his sixties by then. nobody made him."],"name":"ram dass","place":"boston, then india, then maui","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='8'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='8'/><path d='M 58 56 L 62 56 M 42 55 L 34 52 M 78 55 L 86 52'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 44 98 C 48 116 72 116 76 98' stroke-dasharray='0.1 5' stroke-width='5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"we're all just walking each other home.","where":"a line he said in talks so often nobody's sure of the first time"},{"q":"i can do nothing for you but work on myself. you can do nothing for me but work on yourself.","where":"be here now, his 1971 book"},{"q":"the stroke did what forty years of meditation couldn't.","where":"still here, the book he finished after the stroke, and fierce grace, the 2001 documentary about him, paraphrased"},{"q":"now they're sort of like little shmoos.","where":"on his neuroses. the here and now podcast, episode 16, which got named for it"},{"q":"you appreciate the tree.","where":"his answer to how to judge yourself less harshly, posted by his foundation"},{"q":"if you think you're enlightened, go spend a week with your family.","where":"attributed to him. his own foundation passes it around"}],"start":"be here now, the square brown book with the hand-drawn middle; read the first part, which is his own story. then still here, which he wrote after the stroke, about getting old. then how can i help?, if you take care of people, for a living or at home. walking each other home, from 2018, written with mirabai bush, is the one about dying. to hear him, the here and now podcast is his old talks. to see him, fierce grace, the 2001 documentary, then becoming nobody, from 2019.","status":"approved","story":"richard alpert had the whole thing. a psychology professorship at harvard, his own small plane, a mercedes, a sailboat, an apartment full of antiques. he was miserable and drank too much and knew it.\nthen in 1961 timothy leary, a harvard colleague who'd go on to become the most famous drug evangelist in america, handed him psilocybin, the stuff in magic mushrooms, and the floor fell out. alpert watched every part he played, professor, pilot, the whole list, peel away one after another, until there was nothing left but somebody watching, and that somebody was fine. harvard fired them both in 1963. alpert spent the next few years trying to get the drug to say something it would keep saying, and it wouldn't. he'd go up, see everything, and come back down, every time.\nso he went to india in 1967 with no plan. a young american drifter drove him up into the hills to meet his own teacher, a fat old man wrapped in a blanket named neem karoli baba, maharaj-ji to the people around him. the old man told alpert he'd been out under the stars the night before thinking about his mother, and that she'd died of her spleen. both true. he hadn't told anyone. nobody in india could have known. alpert cried for two days. the old man gave him a new name, ram dass, servant of god, and told him to love everyone and tell the truth.\nbe here now, his account of all that, came out in 1971. it's a strange square brown book, part memoir and part hand-drawn pages you turn sideways, and it sold a couple million copies. for a lot of kids in the seventies it was the first spiritual book that sounded like them. in 1997 a stroke took his right side and most of his speech. he called it fierce grace, eventually, meaning a gift that nearly kills you. he spent his last fifteen years on maui, slower, kinder, and by his own account further along than he'd ever been when he could talk.","struggle":"the fame after be here now went to his head and he knew it. he'd stand up in front of a thousand people talking about surrender, about letting the self go, and go back to the hotel and be a guy who'd just given a great talk about letting the self go.\nin 1974 a brooklyn housewife named joyce green, who went by joya, convinced him she was channeling maharaj-ji, his guru, who'd died the year before. he fell for it completely, moved his whole practice around her, and told everyone she was the real thing. it took him two years to see it. it's the spiritual version of getting pulled into a con you'd have spotted in a second if it happened to a friend.\nthe stroke in 1997 put him in a wheelchair, took his words, and for a while took his faith. he said he'd spent forty years talking about the soul and when the body broke he found out he'd been mostly body.","threads":["psychedelics","the body","fame","love","dying"],"try":[{"d":"this is the one he did himself. put your attention in the middle of your chest. take a few deep breaths, down low. breathe in love and breathe out love. say it to yourself, i am loving awareness. thoughts will show up. you don't have to climb on. watch them go by, and love whatever you can be aware of. love neutralizes fear, he said.\nit works sitting down. it also travels. try it on a trail.","t":"i am loving awareness."},{"d":"next time somebody is driving you nuts, and that includes you, try it for one minute. that one grew that way. you don't have to like the tree.","t":"turn somebody into a tree."}],"years":"1931 to 2019"},{"after":"there's no after with the family. he didn't go back. what he did with the ten dry years was keep sitting at the desk, and when the elegies finally came he wrote to his friends in a kind of shock, saying it had come through him like a storm, and got it down.\nand at the end, he wrote his own epitaph and chose his own grave, in the churchyard at raron in the swiss mountains, under a line that starts rose, oh pure contradiction.","basically":"a thin, charming, usually broke poet from prague who lived off rich admirers and wrote the letters every writing student gets handed sooner or later, the ones that say be patient with what you don't understand yet and live the questions. he also walked out on his wife and baby to do it, and never really went back. he's the guy whose advice about art is perfect and whose life you shouldn't look at too closely. take the advice anyway. it's good.","born":1875,"died":1926,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 300 L 0 150 L 200 150 C 206 200 214 250 224 300 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='30' y='100' width='60' height='50' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='52' y='70' width='22' height='30' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 100 L 30 94 L 38 94 L 38 100 M 82 100 L 82 94 L 90 94 L 90 100'/><circle cx='160' cy='54' r='14'/><path d='M 160 68 L 160 114'/><path d='M 160 114 L 144 150 M 160 114 L 178 146'/><path d='M 160 82 L 146 104 M 160 82 L 180 90'/><path d='M 150 76 C 138 84 132 92 124 104' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 240 70 L 300 62 M 250 96 L 330 90 M 236 124 L 290 120' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 230 250 C 260 240 290 260 320 250 S 360 240 400 250 M 240 280 C 270 270 300 290 330 280 S 370 270 400 280'/><circle cx='190' cy='144' r='5' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 190 149 L 190 150'/><g transform='translate(63,48.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he wrote the most famous advice ever given to a young poet, and he'd left his own baby to go write it.","key":"rilke","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rainer_Maria_Rilke","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/rainer-maria-rilke","why":"a life and some poems in english"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Letters_to_a_Young_Poet","why":"the letters, where most people start"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"he walked out on his wife and his baby daughter within about a year of the wedding. clara went to paris to work too, and ruth was left with clara's parents. they never divorced and they wrote to each other, but he chose the work over the family and kept choosing it. there was a long line of women after that.\nhe lived most of his life on other people's money. princesses, countesses, rich admirers. muzot was bought by a swiss businessman, werner reinhart, so rilke could live in it. he was very good at being taken care of, and not much good at taking care of anyone.","moments":["the story goes that in the autumn of 1926 he pricked his hand on a rose thorn cutting flowers for a visitor, and the wound wouldn't heal. that part's legend. the leukemia was real and was diagnosed only weeks before he died.","the first letter to kappus says the only question that matters is whether you'd die if you weren't allowed to write. if the answer is yes, build your life around it. he did.","at duino, the story comes from the castle's owner, his patron princess marie von thurn und taxis. he was walking on the cliffs in a hard wind and the first line of the first elegy came to him out of the air. he wrote it down in his notebook and went back inside."],"name":"rainer maria rilke","place":"prague, munich, paris, a castle on the adriatic, a tower in the swiss valais","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 80 C 52 92 68 92 70 80 C 66 84 54 84 50 80 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 46 72 C 52 66 58 70 60 72 C 62 70 68 66 74 72 C 68 76 62 74 60 74 C 58 74 52 76 46 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"live the questions now.","where":"letters to a young poet, the fourth letter, july 1903"},{"q":"you must change your life.","where":"the last line of archaic torso of apollo, a poem about a broken ancient statue with no head, in new poems, 1908"},{"q":"who, if i cried out, would hear me among the angels?","where":"the duino elegies, the first line of the first elegy, paraphrased"}],"start":"letters to a young poet, the first and fourth letters. then the first duino elegy. stephen mitchell's translations are the ones most people read.","status":"approved","story":"he was born rené rilke in prague in 1875, when it belonged to the austrian empire. his mother had lost a baby daughter before he was born, and for his first years she dressed him as a girl and kept his hair long. then his father sent him to military school, where he was miserable for five years.\nin his early twenties he fell in with lou andreas-salomé, a russian-born writer fourteen years older than him, married, brilliant, who'd turned down the philosopher nietzsche and would later train with freud. she got him to change rené to rainer and took him to russia twice. in 1901 he married the sculptor clara westhoff, and their daughter ruth was born that december. within a year he'd gone to paris to write a book on rodin, the most famous sculptor alive, the man who made the thinker. for the rest of his life he mostly lived apart from them.\nfrom 1903 to 1908 he wrote ten letters to a young military cadet named franz kappus who'd sent him some poems and asked if they were any good. they were published after rilke died, as letters to a young poet, and they're the thing most people know him for. be patient with everything unresolved in your heart. live the questions now.\nin january 1912, staying as a guest at duino castle, on the adriatic coast near trieste, he started a set of long poems he called elegies, which means poems of mourning, here mourning and praise at once. then he stalled for ten years. in february 1922, alone in a little stone tower called muzot in switzerland, it all came at once. he finished the duino elegies and wrote the sonnets to orpheus, fifty five of them, in a few weeks. orpheus is the singer in greek myth who went down to the land of the dead to bring his wife back. rilke died of leukemia in december 1926, at fifty one.","struggle":"the ten years between the first elegies and the last were a wall. he wrote bits and fragments and letters, hundreds of letters, and couldn't finish the thing he knew he was supposed to write. he said he was stuck and scared that he'd never get it back.\nthe first world war made it worse. in 1916 the austrian army drafted him, and after a few weeks of training he was put at a desk in the war archive in vienna, doing paperwork until friends got him out that summer. he'd lived his whole life for the inner life, and the world had no use for it.","threads":["family","work"],"years":"1875 to 1926"},{"after":"the morning after the boyhood was the rest of his life. the fat boy who cried through his fingers became the man who told small children, at the end of every episode, i like you just the way you are. he didn't do it from a pulpit. he rarely talked about his faith on air, and he said teaching by example was as powerful as preaching. he called the space between the child and the television set holy ground. walking into the studio each day he said, dear god, let some word that is heard be yours. the adults had told the boy to let on that he didn't care. he spent his working life telling children the opposite, that the mad they felt was real and that they could do something with it.\nwith clemmons the turn is real and it's partial. rogers changed his advice, kept him on the show for twenty five years, and had his friends on the set. i can't find that he ever said in public he'd been wrong. clemmons forgave him anyway, and understood, he said, which is more. the last thing rogers did, with stomach cancer in him and the surgery waiting, was lead the rose parade at the start of 2003. he died about seven weeks after the operation, at home, with a catholic archabbot's last rites on him and his wife in the room.","basically":"the soft spoken man who hosted mister rogers' neighborhood, a public television show for small children in america, for more than thirty years. he was also an ordained minister in the presbyterian church, one of the protestant churches, and the church ordained him to the show, not to a congregation. he rarely talked about his faith on air. someone with that reach today would have a podcast and a product line. his show's budget was six thousand dollars in 1969, and he told the senate that paid for less than two minutes of cartoons.","born":1928,"died":2003,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='318' cy='116' r='12'/><path d='M 318 128 L 318 150'/><path d='M 318 136 L 298 150 M 318 136 L 338 150'/><path d='M 310 108 Q 314 104 318 108 M 318 108 Q 322 104 326 108' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='314' cy='114' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='322' cy='114' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='318' cy='121' r='2' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 252 250 L 252 150 L 380 150 L 380 250 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><rect x='296' y='162' width='40' height='10' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 262 146 L 278 140'/><rect x='274' y='134' width='10' height='6' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 128 132 L 172 132 L 178 186 L 122 186 Z' fill='var(--teal)'/><path d='M 150 130 L 150 186'/><circle cx='150' cy='112' r='14'/><path d='M 138 104 Q 150 94 162 104'/><circle cx='145' cy='110' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='155' cy='110' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 145 118 Q 150 122 155 118' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 150 144 L 124 176'/><path d='M 150 144 L 184 150 L 194 128'/><path d='M 194 128 L 191 122 M 194 128 L 197 121 M 194 128 L 199 125' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 150 186 L 140 250 M 150 186 L 164 250'/><rect x='92' y='186' width='124' height='9' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 100 195 L 100 250 M 208 195 L 208 250'/><rect x='122' y='176' width='34' height='10' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 128 181 L 150 181' stroke-width='1.4'/><path d='M 200 186 L 200 166'/><ellipse cx='200' cy='160' rx='5' ry='7' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='104' cy='174' r='10' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 97 166 L 95 159 L 101 163 M 111 166 L 113 159 L 107 163' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 98 178 L 101 181 M 108 178 L 111 181' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='101' cy='173' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='107' cy='173' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><g transform='translate(352,123) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a minister whose church was a children's show. his weight stayed at 143 for thirty years, and he'd tell you the number spells i love you.","key":"rogers","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fred_Rogers","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fred_Rogers%27s_1969_United_States_Senate_testimony","why":"the hearing, with the numbers"},{"label":"american rhetoric","url":"https://www.americanrhetoric.com/speeches/PDFFiles/Fred%20Rogers%20-%20Testimony%20to%20Congress%20on%20PBS%20Funding.pdf","why":"the transcript of the six minutes"},{"label":"pittsburgh theological seminary","url":"https://www.pts.edu/Fred_Rogers","why":"the seminary's own page on its lunch hour student"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["may 1, 1969, washington. he'd talked for about six minutes. senator john pastore said: i'm supposed to be a pretty tough guy, and this is the first time i've had goose bumps for the last two days.","henri nouwen, the dutch priest, used to phone him from wherever in the world he happened to be, to ask how he was. and he really wanted to know, rogers wrote after nouwen died.","in the nineties he went to meet koko, the gorilla. she folded him in her long arms as though he were a child and took off his shoes. she took my shoes off, tom, he told the reporter.","february 2003. he was in a coma at his house in pittsburgh. a catholic archabbot, the head of a monastery, gave him the last rites, the catholic prayers for the dying. he was a presbyterian minister who'd read the catholic mystics for years, and that's how he went out.","in 1968 he hired françois clemmons, a black singer, to play officer clemmons, and in 1969, when towns were still fighting over whether black and white people could share a swimming pool, had him cool his feet in the same little wading pool on air and dried them with his own towel. that same year, by clemmons's own account, rogers told him he couldn't be openly gay and stay on the show, and suggested he marry. he did. the marriage ended in 1974 and he lived openly after that, and stayed on the show until 1993. he says rogers changed his advice, told him to find a steady partner, welcomed his gay friends on the set, and that he forgives him and, more than that, understands."],"name":"fred rogers","place":"latrobe and pittsburgh, pennsylvania","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 104 38 95 60 95 C 82 95 100 104 102 130 Z' fill='var(--teal)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 50 95 L 60 108 L 70 95 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 57 99 L 60 106 L 63 99 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 60 108 L 60 130' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 32 56 C 25 52 25 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 95 52 95 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 33 50 C 34 34 46 28 60 29 C 74 28 86 34 87 50 C 80 42 70 38 58 40 C 48 41 40 45 33 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 44 50 Q 50 47 56 50 M 64 50 Q 70 47 76 50' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 59 L 58 66 L 62 66'/><path d='M 50 73 C 55 79 65 79 70 73'/><circle cx='98' cy='112' r='10' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 91 104 L 89 97 L 95 101 M 105 104 L 107 97 L 101 101' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 93 118 L 95 120 M 101 118 L 103 120' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='95' cy='111' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='101' cy='111' r='1.4' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i give an expression of care every day to each child, to help him realize that he is unique.","where":"to the senate subcommittee on communications, may 1, 1969, from the transcript of the hearing"},{"q":"when i was a boy and i would see scary things in the news, my mother would say to me, look for the helpers. you will always find people who are helping.","where":"his own telling of what his mother said, which he put in print in 1986 and repeated for the rest of his life; wording as pbs gives it"},{"q":"it takes one letter to say i and four letters to say love and three letters to say you.","where":"to tom junod, in esquire magazine, november 1998, explaining what the number 143 meant to him"}],"start":"the six minutes in front of the senate, from may 1969. it's online, and it's the whole man. then can you say hero, tom junod's profile in esquire from november 1998. then the good neighbor, maxwell king's biography, for the long version.","status":"approved","story":"fred rogers was born on march 20, 1928, in latrobe, a small town in western pennsylvania. his father ran a brick company. his mother knitted sweaters for soldiers and volunteered at the town hospital, and had wanted to be a doctor. he was named for her father, fred mcfeely, and spent a lot of his boyhood with him. he was shy, overweight and asthmatic, kept home a lot, and he was bullied. in a speech to graduating students in 1997 he told it plainly. boys chased him home from school shouting freddy, hey, fat freddy, we're going to get you, freddy, and he'd run for the house of a widow who lived nearby. when he told the adults, they said to just let on that he didn't care and then nobody would bother him. he did care. he resented it and cried whenever he was alone, and, he said, i cried through my fingers as i made up songs on the piano. he'd been playing since he was five. he had a ventriloquist's dummy and stuffed animals and made up whole worlds in his bedroom. when he was eleven his parents adopted a girl, and he had a sister. by high school he'd come out of his shell enough to be president of the student council and editor of the yearbook.\nhe went to dartmouth college for a year, then to rollins college in florida, where he studied music and met joanne byrd, a pianist from jacksonville. they married in 1952 and had two sons. he graduated in 1951 and went to nbc in new york, one of the big commercial networks, as a floor director. in 1953 he went home to pittsburgh to help start wqed, the first community supported public television station in the country. he helped build a children's show there, the children's corner, which josie carey hosted. he stayed off camera. the station manager gave him a tiger puppet, and it became daniel striped tiger. from the fall of 1954 he went to pittsburgh theological seminary, a school where ministers train, on his lunch breaks and at night. it's fairly unorthodox to go to seminary on your lunch hour, he said later, which is what i literally did. it took eight years. he graduated in 1962 with honors and a prize for preaching, and in 1963 the pittsburgh presbytery, the regional body of the church, ordained him with a charge to go on working with children and families through the media. one church account puts the ordination in 1962. he never ran a congregation, and he went back before church officials regularly to keep his standing as a minister. around then he also began working with margaret mcfarland, a child psychologist in pittsburgh, who was his adviser on children for about thirty years.\nin 1963 the canadian broadcasting corporation, canada's public broadcaster, hired him in toronto to make a fifteen minute show in black and white called misterogers, and the cbc insisted he go in front of the camera for the first time. in 1967 he bought the rights, packed up the sets, and moved back to pittsburgh with his wife and sons. mister rogers' neighborhood went out nationally on public television in 1968. he wrote and edited every episode, wrote about two hundred songs and thirteen operas, made up the characters and hosted. he talked to small children about divorce, about a pet dying, about brothers and sisters, and on june 7, 1968, in the week robert kennedy was assassinated, he made a segment about that.\nthe next year the money nearly went. lyndon johnson, on his way out of the white house, had proposed twenty million dollars for public television, and richard nixon's new administration wanted it cut to ten. on may 1, 1969, rogers sat down in front of the senate subcommittee on communications, chaired by john pastore of rhode island. he said he had a written statement, that it was philosophical and would take about ten minutes, so he wouldn't read it. he said his show's budget was six thousand dollars, which pays for less than two minutes of cartoons. he said: i give an expression of care every day to each child, to help him realize that he is unique. then he recited the words of a song he'd written after a boy asked him what you do with the mad that you feel. it took about six minutes. pastore said he was supposed to be a pretty tough guy and this was the first time he'd had goose bumps in two days. i think it's wonderful, he said. looks like you just earned the twenty million dollars. public broadcasting got the full twenty million.\nhe stopped making the show in 1975 to make programs for adults, and came back in 1979. its best season for viewers was 1985 to 1986, when about one home in fifty had it on. it ran to 895 episodes. production ended in december 2000 and the last new episode aired on august 31, 2001. he was diagnosed with stomach cancer in october 2002, put off surgery so he could serve as grand marshal of the rose parade at the start of 2003, had the operation on january 6, and died at home in pittsburgh on february 27, 2003, with his wife beside him. he was seventy four. he's buried in latrobe.","struggle":"the loneliness came first and he never pretended it hadn't. the reporter who wrote the long esquire profile of him in 1998 described the puppets that were the comfort of his lonely boyhood. in the 1997 speech he called himself a perfect target for ridicule, and said he'd resented the treatment and cried whenever he was alone. the book that quotes the speech says that's where his lifelong hunt for what is essential in other people came from.\nthen the body, where you can see the discipline. the fat boy became a man who swam nearly every morning of his life, at a club pool in pittsburgh, and whose weight stayed at 143 pounds for as long as he was mister rogers, about thirty one years when the esquire reporter asked. he didn't smoke or drink. after his father died in 1970 he gave up meat, kept fish for a while, and by the early 1980s was a vegetarian. he said he couldn't eat anything that had a mother. by 1998 he was in bed at half past nine and up at five thirty, some say half past four, to read the bible and pray for the people who had written asking him to. he napped every day. a boy once asked him to pray for him. rogers asked the boy to pray for him instead, and said later he'd asked because he wanted the boy's intercession. it's a monk's schedule run out of a tv studio.\nand there was the mad, which he admitted to. the song he recited to the senate came from a boy's question, what do you do with the mad that you feel, and he said the point was that a child can control what he feels. in 1975 he stopped the show, which caught the people who worked with him off guard, and it was four years before he came back.","threads":["loneliness","the body","work","silence"],"years":"1928 to 2003"},{"after":"he didn't leave. that's the thing about rohr. every reason to walk out of the church and he stayed in it, built a center at the edge of it, and kept the door propped open so people who were halfway out could come and go.\nwhen the letters come he answers them, politely, and goes back to work. he calls his position an alternative orthodoxy. orthodoxy is the official version of what the church believes, and his point is that franciscans have always lived inside it, doing it their own way. i'm inside the house, he's saying. i just use the side door.","basically":"a catholic priest in new mexico, in his eighties now, who's spent fifty years telling people their faith isn't dead just because the version they grew up with stopped working. his big idea is that life has two halves, and the second one only starts when something you built falls apart. what most people call a midlife crisis, he calls the door. if you grew up in church and quietly walked out, he's the one saying you might be closer now than you were in the pew.","born":1943,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 250 C 60 247 120 253 200 250 S 340 248 400 250'/><circle cx='170' cy='168' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='168' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='178' cy='166' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 169 172 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 170 182 L 170 215.44'/><path d='M 170 215.44 L 158 250 M 170 215.44 L 182 250'/><path d='M 170.0 190 L 192.0 192'/><path d='M 170.0 190 L 206.0 200'/><path d='M 216 166 C 210 196 218 214 238 216 C 258 214 266 196 260 166 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><ellipse cx='238' cy='166' rx='22' ry='6' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 240 172 l -5 12 l 7 10 l -4 14' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 234 176 l -22 -10' stroke-width='2' opacity='.75'/><path d='M 242 186 l 26 -4' stroke-width='2' opacity='.75'/><path d='M 238 198 l -24 8' stroke-width='2' opacity='.75'/><path d='M 244 170 l 18 -16' stroke-width='2' opacity='.75'/><ellipse cx='336' cy='242' rx='10' ry='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='346' cy='234' r='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 351 234 l 7 2 l -7 2 M 332 249 l -1 6 M 340 249 l 1 6 M 326 240 q -10 -6 -6 -14' stroke-width='2'/><g transform='translate(40,225.7) scale(0.9)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a franciscan priest who says the first half of your life builds the box and the second half starts when something breaks it.","key":"rohr","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Rohr","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his center","url":"https://cac.org/","why":"the center for action and contemplation, where you can sign up for the free daily meditation"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"plenty of conservative catholics think he's a heretic, meaning somebody teaching what the church says is false, and he's fine with that.","moments":["in 1971 in cincinnati he founded the new jerusalem community, a lay community, meaning ordinary people, not priests or nuns, that at its height had hundreds of people living close and sharing money. it's where he learned that community is hard and worth it.","the center for action and contemplation started in 1987 in albuquerque. the name was a fight he wanted to end. the activists thought sitting in silent prayer was navel gazing, and the people who did the silent prayer thought activism was ego. he said do both or you're doing neither.","he keeps a dog, and the dogs show up in the books. venus, then opie. he says a dog will teach you more about unconditional presence than most theologians."],"name":"richard rohr","place":"kansas, then albuquerque","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 57 56 L 63 56 M 43 55 L 34 53 M 77 55 L 86 53' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"we don't think ourselves into a new way of living. we live ourselves into a new way of thinking.","where":"a line he uses constantly, in books and talks"},{"q":"if you don't transform your pain, you'll transmit it.","where":"falling upward, his 2011 book about the second half of life, and elsewhere, paraphrased"},{"q":"the first half of life is discovering the script. the second half is writing it and owning it.","where":"falling upward, paraphrased"}],"start":"falling upward. then the universal christ if you want the big argument. his daily meditations, a short email the center for action and contemplation sends out every morning, are short and free.","status":"approved","story":"he's a franciscan, a priest ordained in 1970. franciscans are the catholic order that follows francis of assisi, a rich merchant's kid from italy in the 1200s who gave away everything he owned, preached to birds, and, the story goes, got off his horse and kissed a leper, a man with the disease everybody was terrified to touch. rohr took his vows to a guy like that.\nin 1987 he founded the center for action and contemplation in albuquerque, a teaching and retreat center. action means working for justice out in the world. contemplation means sitting quiet with god. the name is the whole program. you do both, or neither one works.\nfalling upward, from 2011, is his best known book and it's one point. the first half of life is about building a container, identity, career, rules, a self. the second half only begins when that container gets cracked by something you didn't sign up for. a divorce, say, or a diagnosis. nobody chooses to fall. you get thrown.\nhe reads the bible the way the mystics read it. mystics are the people in every religion who want to experience god directly instead of just believing things about god, and they read scripture looking for what it's pointing at instead of what it's saying. the universal christ, from 2019, argues that christ was never [[jesus]]'s last name. it's a title, it means the anointed one, and rohr says it's the name for god showing up in everything, rocks and dogs and strangers, with jesus as the clearest example.\nhe doesn't get everybody all the way there, and that's fine. he's the guy holding the door open between the church a lot of us grew up in and everything outside it.","struggle":"he's spent fifty years as a priest in a church that keeps writing letters about him. complaints to rome, meaning the vatican, the pope's headquarters. cardinals, the church's top officials under the pope, asking questions. bishops uninviting him from their dioceses. he says he's had to learn to hold the institution loosely and the vows tight.\nin his own telling the harder struggle was quieter. somewhere in midlife he found that the first half of life's answers had run out on him too, and that the book he'd write about falling was going to be about himself.","threads":["doubt","silence"],"years":"born 1943"},{"after":"he stopped looking. he said, roughly, why should i seek? i am the same as he. his essence speaks through me. that's not a man who got over it. that's a man who decided the missing person was inside him now, and wrote sixty thousand lines to prove it.\nthen he found two more friends, a goldsmith named salah al-din and then his favorite student, husam al-din, and dictated the masnavi, his six volume poem, to husam over the last twelve to fifteen years of his life. husam wrote it down and read it back to him. the wound never closed. it became the mouth.","basically":"a muslim scholar in thirteenth century turkey, a respected preacher with a following, whose whole life got blown open at thirty seven by a scruffy wandering holy man he met in the street. the two of them were inseparable, his students got jealous, and then the friend disappeared. some say he was murdered, some say he just walked away. rumi spent the rest of his life turning that loss into poems, tens of thousands of lines of them. today he's all over wedding programs and instagram, usually in soft american versions with the islam taken out. if you've ever lost somebody and found them still talking inside you, that's his whole subject.","born":1207,"died":1273,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--teal)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 260 L 380 260'/><path d='M 230 150 C 170 260 290 260 230 150 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='230' cy='108' r='14'/><path d='M 230 122 L 230 150 M 230 132 L 196 112 M 230 132 L 264 114'/><ellipse cx='230' cy='258' rx='58' ry='7' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 150 200 C 140 170 170 160 180 190 M 290 200 C 300 170 270 160 262 190' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='70' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 70 164 L 70 210'/><path d='M 70 210 L 54 246 M 70 210 L 88 242'/><path d='M 70 178 L 56 200 M 70 178 L 90 186'/><circle cx='330' cy='60' r='20' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 300 90 C 320 80 340 100 360 90' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(345,230.3) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a respectable professor of religion until a wild man walked into his life, and then he couldn't stop singing.","key":"rumi","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumi","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the masnavi, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/isl/masnavi/index.htm","why":"whinfield's old abridged english version of his big poem, public domain"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mevlevi_Order","why":"the whirling order his followers started"}],"lineage":"muslim","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["his family fled the mongols, the armies from the east that were flattening whole cities, out of the country around balkh, in what's now afghanistan and tajikistan, when he was a boy. the story says the old poet attar, the great persian mystic who wrote the conference of the birds, met the family on the road, gave the boy a book, and said of the father and the son walking behind him, here comes a sea followed by an ocean. not every rumi scholar thinks it happened.","the whirling comes from him. the story is that he heard the goldsmiths hammering in the bazaar, the market street, and started turning right there. his followers made it a practice, a slow spin with one palm up to take from heaven and one palm down to give it to the earth. the dervishes still do it in konya every december, on the anniversary of his death.","when he died in december 1273, the story goes, jews and christians walked in the funeral with the muslims, and when someone asked why, they said he'd taught them more about their own holy books."],"name":"rumi","place":"the country around balkh, then konya, in what's now turkey","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 28 54 C 26 24 94 24 92 54 C 84 40 36 40 28 54 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 44 C 50 34 70 34 84 44 M 32 50 C 46 38 74 38 88 50' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 45 57 C 48 60 52 60 55 57 M 65 57 C 68 60 72 60 75 57'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"out beyond ideas of wrongdoing and rightdoing, there is a field. i'll meet you there.","where":"one of his four line poems, number 395 in the standard persian edition, as coleman barks renders it in the essential rumi. barks is an american poet who doesn't read persian and reworks older scholars' english translations into loose free verse. the persian says beyond islam and unbelief, not wrongdoing and rightdoing"},{"q":"the wound is the place where the light enters you.","where":"attributed to him everywhere; it's a tidied up version of a line in coleman barks's loose english, keep looking at the bandaged place, that is where the light enters you"},{"q":"you were born with wings. why crawl through life?","where":"attributed to him everywhere; i couldn't find it in any named translation of his poems"}],"start":"the essential rumi, coleman barks's collection, for the doorway, knowing it's a doorway. then the masnavi, book one, in jawid mojaddedi's translation, which goes straight from the persian and keeps the rhymes, for the real thing.","status":"approved","story":"jalal ad-din rumi was a muslim scholar and preacher in konya, a city in the middle of what's now turkey. he was a serious man with students, the kind of religious professor people came to for rulings on how to live. then in 1244 a wandering dervish named shams of tabriz stopped him in the street. a dervish was a muslim holy man who'd given up everything, owned nothing, and drifted from town to town living on what people gave him. shams asked rumi a question, and nobody agrees what it was.\none version goes like this. shams asked who was greater, the prophet muhammad or bayazid, an earlier muslim mystic, somebody who wants to meet god directly and not only follow the rules, famous for saying wild things like glory be to me. rumi said muhammad, obviously. then why, shams asked, did muhammad say he'd never known god as well as he should, while bayazid talked like he'd swallowed god whole? rumi answered that bayazid's thirst was small and one gulp filled him, and muhammad's thirst never ended. in some tellings rumi fainted. whatever really happened, the scholar fell apart and came back a poet.\nshams and rumi were inseparable for a couple of years, shut away together talking for days on end, and rumi's students hated it. their teacher had stopped teaching them. then shams vanished for good. a later story says people close to rumi murdered him. others say he just walked away. rumi wrote thousands of poems to him, in his voice, as him, and signed them with shams's name. the divan, rumi's giant collection of short love poems, is named for shams, not for rumi.\nthe masnavi, his big book, is six volumes of stories inside stories in rhyming couplets. it opens with a reed flute crying because it was cut from the reed bed, and every sad song it plays is that one ache. you're the flute. all of it points at one thing: you and god are lovers who've been apart too long. sufis, the people inside islam who go after god directly like that, call it the quran in persian, the quran being islam's holy book.\nthe whirling dervishes, the men in tall felt hats and white skirts who spin in slow circles as a way of praying, come from him. his son organized them into an order after he died. and he's the best selling poet in america, which would have made him laugh.\nwhen he died, the story goes, christians and jews came to his funeral alongside the muslims, and when asked why, they said he'd taught them more about their own holy books.\none thing to know before you read him. the popular english versions by coleman barks are loose and strip out most of the islam. in the originals the god he's in love with is allah, and the lines are full of the quran and the prophet muhammad. the american versions make him sound like he came from nowhere in particular, which is worth knowing when you read him.","struggle":"losing shams broke him open twice. the first time shams left, in 1246, rumi sent his own son, sultan walad, all the way to damascus, in syria, to bring him back, and he came. the second time, about a year later, shams vanished for good, and the rumor was that rumi's other son and some jealous students had killed him. rumi went looking for him anyway, as far as damascus.\nthe poems that came out of that are grief pretending to be love poems, or love poems that had to go through grief to get here. he wrote in shams's name because he couldn't stand to write in his own. it's like losing your best friend and still talking to him out loud in the car, for years, and meaning every word.","threads":["love","grief"],"years":"1207 to 1273"},{"after":"there wasn't a comeback. she kept healing the people in her own town who still came to her, for as long as she could, and she kept saying plainly that the foreigners had ruined it. she worked with álvaro estrada, a man from huautla, to get her own life down in her own words, and that book came out in 1977. it's the only reason we have her side at all.\nafter she died she became a symbol, her face on murals and souvenirs in mexico and far beyond, including in huautla. the town that burned her house sells her picture now. that's not a redemption. it's just what happened.","basically":"a mazatec healer in a mountain town in southern mexico who couldn't read or write and spent her life using mushrooms at night to heal sick people. in 1955 she let an american banker watch, he wrote it up, and within ten years her town was full of hippies and rock fans looking for a trip. her neighbors blamed her, her house got burned, and she died poor. every psilocybin study at johns hopkins, every microdosing podcast, runs back through her kitchen. take from her what it costs when the thing you hold sacred becomes somebody else's weekend.","born":1894,"died":1985,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 270 C 80 250 120 200 170 190 C 230 180 300 220 380 210 L 380 290 L 20 290 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 120 206 L 120 166 L 190 166 L 190 196' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 112 168 L 155 136 L 198 168'/><rect x='140' y='174' width='18' height='16' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 149 186 L 149 180'/><path d='M 149 178 C 146 176 149 172 149 172 C 149 172 152 176 149 178' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='330' cy='60' r='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='322' cy='55' r='15' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='262' cy='180' r='5'/><path d='M 262 185 L 262 202 M 262 202 L 257 212 M 262 202 L 267 212'/><circle cx='284' cy='186' r='5'/><path d='M 284 191 L 284 208 M 284 208 L 279 218 M 284 208 L 289 218'/><circle cx='306' cy='190' r='5'/><path d='M 306 195 L 306 212 M 306 212 L 301 222 M 306 212 L 311 222'/><circle cx='328' cy='190' r='5'/><path d='M 328 195 L 328 212 M 328 212 L 323 222 M 328 212 L 333 222'/><circle cx='350' cy='188' r='5'/><path d='M 350 193 L 350 210 M 350 210 L 345 220 M 350 210 L 355 220'/><circle cx='372' cy='186' r='5'/><path d='M 372 191 L 372 208 M 372 208 L 367 218 M 372 208 L 377 218'/><g transform='translate(160,128.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"she let one american banker sit in on a healing night, and the whole world followed him up the mountain.","key":"sabina","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mar%C3%ADa_Sabina","why":"the plain overview of the life"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["wasson recorded her chanting through a velada in 1956, and the record came out in 1957. the chants go on for pages, her naming who she is over and over. poets later translated them and printed them as poetry. she never read them.","in 1962 the swiss chemist [[hofmann|albert hofmann]] came up the mountain with pills of the drug he'd made from her mushrooms in his lab. she took them and said the pills had the same power as the mushrooms.","she said she first ate the mushrooms as a small girl, out in the hills, long before anyone taught her what they were for."],"name":"maría sabina","place":"huautla de jiménez, in the mountains of oaxaca, southern mexico","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 36 70 C 34 86 36 100 40 112 M 84 70 C 86 86 84 100 80 112' stroke-width='5'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 40 104 L 80 104 M 44 110 L 48 116 L 52 110 L 56 116 L 60 110 L 64 116 L 68 110 L 72 116 L 76 110' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"from the moment the foreigners arrived, the saint children lost their purity.","where":"maría sabina: her life and chants, the book of her own story as told to álvaro estrada, a mazatec writer from her town, 1977; paraphrased from the english translation"},{"q":"before wasson, nobody took the mushrooms just to find god. they were always for the sick, to get well.","where":"the same book, paraphrased"},{"q":"the language belongs to the saint children. they speak, and i have the power to translate.","where":"the same book, paraphrased"}],"start":"maría sabina: her life and chants, the book she told to álvaro estrada. read her before anyone who wrote about her.","status":"approved","story":"she was a mazatec, one of the indigenous peoples of the mountains in oaxaca, a state in the far south of mexico, and she lived her whole life in and around huautla de jiménez, a town on a steep ridge. she never learned to read. she said she'd forgotten what year she was born, only that her mother told her it was on the feast of mary magdalene. she was widowed twice and raised her kids on what she could grow and sell.\nshe was a curandera, the spanish word for a healer, and her work was the velada, which means a night vigil. somebody sick, or somebody in trouble, came to her, and she sat up with them through the night with the mushrooms, which the mazatecs called the little saints or the saint children. the velada was for healing and for finding out what was wrong. she said the mushrooms did the talking and she only translated. what happened inside those nights belonged to her people, and this page leaves it there.\nin june 1955 an american named gordon wasson, a vice president at the bank j.p. morgan and an amateur mushroom scholar, came up the mountain and asked to sit in. she let him. in may 1957 he published the story in life magazine, the biggest picture magazine in america, under the title seeking the magic mushroom. in the magazine he changed her name. in his private two volume book that same year, and in what he wrote after, he used her real name and the name of her town. imagine a stranger posting your grandmother's address on a travel site as the best place in the world to get high.\nthe seekers came. by the early sixties young americans and europeans were climbing to huautla, then hippies by the hundreds, plus scientists and writers and people who were famous for other things. most of them didn't want healing. they wanted a trip. the mexican government eventually sent in police and soldiers and deported foreigners. her neighbors blamed her for bringing all of it. her house was burned. she was jailed for a short time. one of her sons was murdered. she said the foreigners had spoiled the little saints and they'd lost their purity.\nshe died on november 22, 1985, poor, in the town she'd never left. she didn't get rich off any of what came after.","struggle":"she'd already had a hard life before anybody from outside knew her name. two husbands dead, kids to feed, and a town that went to her when it was sick and gossiped about her the rest of the time. that was just the job.\nwhat broke her was the sixties. her own practice, the thing she'd done since she was a girl, became the reason people drove twenty hours from mexico city and flew in from california. the veladas stopped being about sick people. she said the saint children didn't work the same anymore, and she said it as a fact about the world, the way you'd say a well went bad.","threads":["psychedelics","fame","grief"],"years":"about 1894 to 1985"},{"after":"after he rose into the air and vanished, so the story goes, the monks later found him far to the south, living as a layman, an ordinary guy out of robes, and he told them where he'd hidden two other books in his room. then he stayed gone. he didn't come back to be congratulated.\nthe book he left is still the one tibetan teachers hand you when you ask how to actually live. the dalai lama, the exiled leader of tibetan buddhism, has taught it more than any other text. the slob's homework became the syllabus.","basically":"an indian monk around the year 700 whose fellow monks had him down as a total slacker, and who turned out to have written the most practical guide to being a decent person in all of buddhism. tibetan teachers still hand it to you when you ask how to actually live, and the dalai lama has taught from it more than any other book. think of the guy at work everybody's written off who turns in the best thing the place ever made, then quits. if anger's your problem, his chapter on patience is the one that talks you down.","born":685,"died":763,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 170 250 L 170 220 L 230 220 L 230 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='200' cy='110' r='14'/><path d='M 200 124 L 200 166'/><path d='M 200 166 C 180 166 170 182 186 182 L 214 182 C 230 182 220 166 200 166'/><path d='M 200 140 L 172 150 M 200 140 L 228 150'/><path d='M 195 115 Q 200 119 205 115'/><path d='M 175 190 C 190 200 210 200 225 190' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 180 205 C 195 212 205 212 220 205' opacity='.3'/><g opacity='.7'><circle cx='70' cy='210' r='10'/><path d='M 70 224 L 70 266'/><path d='M 70 266 C 50 266 40 282 56 282 L 84 282 C 100 282 90 266 70 266'/><path d='M 70 240 L 56 266 M 70 240 L 84 266'/><circle cx='120' cy='216' r='9'/><path d='M 120 230 L 120 272'/><path d='M 120 272 C 100 272 90 288 106 288 L 134 288 C 150 288 140 272 120 272'/><path d='M 120 246 L 106 272 M 120 246 L 134 272'/><circle cx='300' cy='212' r='10'/><path d='M 300 226 L 300 268'/><path d='M 300 268 C 280 268 270 284 286 284 L 314 284 C 330 284 320 268 300 268'/><path d='M 300 242 L 286 268 M 300 242 L 314 268'/><circle cx='350' cy='218' r='9'/><path d='M 350 232 L 350 274'/><path d='M 350 274 C 330 274 320 290 336 290 L 364 290 C 380 290 370 274 350 274'/><path d='M 350 248 L 336 274 M 350 248 L 364 274'/><\/g><path d='M 235 250 C 236 244 246 244 247 250 M 252 250 C 253 244 263 244 264 250' fill='var(--peach)'/><g transform='translate(200,193.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the other monks thought he was a lazy slob, so they set him up to recite scripture in public and he recited a masterpiece.","key":"shantideva","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shantideva","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bodhicaryavatara","why":"his one big book"},{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.sacred-texts.com/bud/tpol/index.htm","why":"the path of light, an old public domain english version of the way of the bodhisattva"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["the nickname the monks gave him, bhusuku, is a squashed-together word for eat, sleep, and shit. that's who wrote the way of the bodhisattva.","the exchange of self and other in chapter 8 is the hardest practice in the book. you take the other person's happiness as if it were yours and your own as if it were theirs, and then you actually want theirs more. he says it's the only thing that's ever worked.","pema chödrön, the american buddhist nun, wrote a whole book walking through it, called no time to lose. she says she teaches it because it's the book that made her laugh at herself."],"name":"shantideva","place":"nalanda, india","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 100 C 52 108 74 108 90 98 L 98 130 L 44 130 Z' fill='var(--marigold)' opacity='.9'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"where would i find enough leather to cover the earth? with leather on my feet, the earth is covered.","where":"the way of the bodhisattva, his one big book, chapter 5, verses 13 and 14"},{"q":"if there's a fix, why be upset? if there's no fix, what's the use of being upset?","where":"the way of the bodhisattva, chapter 6, the one on patience, verse 10"},{"q":"all the happiness in the world comes from wanting others to be happy. all the misery comes from wanting it for yourself.","where":"the way of the bodhisattva, chapter 8, paraphrased"}],"start":"the way of the bodhisattva in the padmakara translation, done by a group of western translators working with tibetan teachers in france. read chapter 6 first, then 8. pema chödrön, an american buddhist nun whose book when things fall apart gets passed around by people having the worst year of their lives, walks through the whole thing in no time to lose.","status":"approved","story":"he was a monk at nalanda, in northeast india, for centuries the biggest buddhist university in the world, a sprawling campus where thousands of monks lived and studied, the closest thing that age had to harvard. the other monks thought he did nothing but eat, sleep, and go to the toilet. they gave him a nickname that meant exactly that.\nso they set him up. they asked him to recite scripture in front of the whole assembly, figuring he'd stand there with nothing and be humiliated. he asked whether they wanted something old or something new. they said new. he recited the way of the bodhisattva, ten chapters of verse nobody had ever heard, from memory. the legend says partway through the chapter on wisdom he rose into the air and vanished, and his voice kept going.\nthe book is about how to become a bodhisattva. in buddhism that's someone who could take their own final peace and walk away from the whole mess of life, and won't, until everybody else gets out too. like a lifeguard who won't leave the beach while anyone's still in the water. chapter 6, on patience, is the hardest thing in buddhist writing. he says one flash of anger burns up the good you've done over a thousand eons, ages so long they're lifetimes stacked on lifetimes. and he says the person hurting you is a treasure, because without them you'd have nowhere to practice patience.\nthe famous image is from chapter 5. you'll never find enough leather to cover the whole earth so it stops cutting your feet. put leather on the soles of your feet and it's the same thing. you don't fix the world. you fix where you stand on it.","struggle":"the legend says he practiced at night, alone, and let the whole monastery think he was a slob during the day. if that's true, the struggle was years of being the guy everyone had written off, and choosing not to correct them.\nthe text itself is a struggle written down. the chapter on patience isn't a calm man's chapter. it's somebody talking himself out of anger line by line, out loud, because he needed to hear it.","threads":["anger"],"years":"the eighth century"},{"after":"after the raid he didn't hide. he kept teaching, kept writing, gave talks, and in 2010 was the subject of a documentary, dirty pictures. friends and fans called him the godfather of ecstasy, which he disliked.\nann spent her last years making the case for mdma therapy, which by then was in serious clinical trials paid for by maps, the multidisciplinary association for psychedelic studies, and speaking about marriage and the shadow, the parts of yourself you don't want to see. she outlived him by eight years and kept the hill going.","basically":"sasha was a chemist who worked for dow chemical, then built a lab behind his house and spent forty years making new psychedelic compounds and trying them himself, carefully, with a small group of friends. he's the one who got mdma, now known as ecstasy or molly, into the hands of therapists in the seventies. ann was his wife, a lay therapist who used those drugs with clients before they were banned. together they wrote two huge books that are half memoir and half chemistry. think of the nerdiest couple you know, with a license from the government to do the one thing it hates most.","born":1925,"died":2022,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 260 C 120 230 260 230 380 260 L 380 290 L 20 290 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 280 240 L 280 180 L 360 180 L 360 240' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 272 182 L 320 150 L 368 182'/><path d='M 120 236 L 120 206 L 230 206 L 230 236 M 110 206 L 240 206'/><path d='M 150 206 L 150 190 L 142 180 L 164 180 L 156 190 L 156 206' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 190 206 L 190 176 M 200 206 L 200 186' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='90' cy='132' r='14'/><path d='M 90 146 L 90 194'/><path d='M 90 194 L 76 228 M 90 194 L 104 228'/><path d='M 90 160 L 76 184 M 90 160 L 116 154'/><circle cx='260' cy='138' r='14'/><path d='M 260 152 L 260 200'/><path d='M 260 200 L 246 234 M 260 200 L 274 234'/><path d='M 260 166 L 274 190 M 260 166 L 234 160'/><path d='M 74 124 L 106 124 M 76 118 C 80 110 100 110 104 118' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(208,194.0) scale(0.8)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a chemist tested hundreds of new mind drugs on himself in a shed on his hill, and his wife helped him write it all down as a love story.","key":"shulgin","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexander_Shulgin","why":"the plain overview of the life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ann_Shulgin","why":"ann's life, the therapist half of the pair"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PiHKAL","why":"their first book together"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"the argument is about the work. publishing detailed methods for making new drugs put them in the hands of people who were never going to be careful, and some of the compounds from his notebooks showed up years later in the street drug trade. he believed in information being free. the cost of that was paid by strangers.\nand mdma is not harmless. people have died from overheating and from drinking too much water at raves, and the questions about what heavy use does to the brain aren't settled. in 2024 the us food and drug administration turned down an application to approve mdma therapy for ptsd, asking for more study.","moments":["they tested new compounds with a small circle of old friends, the same people for decades, who met at the house and wrote careful notes.","the shulgin rating scale, a simple plus and minus system he invented for how strong an effect was, is still used by researchers.","pihkal stands for phenethylamines i have known and loved, a family of chemicals that includes mescaline. tihkal is the same joke for tryptamines, the family with psilocybin and dmt in it."],"name":"sasha and ann shulgin","place":"a hilltop farm in lafayette, california","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 26 62 L 18 40 L 32 46 L 30 28 L 42 40 L 48 24 L 56 38 L 64 22 L 72 38 L 80 26 L 86 42 L 100 30 L 92 48 L 104 52 L 90 62' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 100 40 128 60 130 C 80 128 94 100 90 60 C 82 84 38 84 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 96 C 50 106 70 106 76 96 M 40 110 C 48 118 72 118 80 110' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 92 96 L 92 106 L 84 124 L 108 124 L 100 106 L 100 96 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 90 96 L 102 96'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i learned there was a great deal inside me.","where":"sasha, on his first mescaline experience in 1960, told in pihkal and in interviews"},{"q":"it's my low-calorie martini.","where":"sasha, describing mdma in his notes from the seventies, as later quoted"},{"q":"i publish so the information is out there and can't be taken away.","where":"sasha, in interviews about why they printed the chemistry, paraphrased"}],"start":"the first half of pihkal, the love story, which is book one. skip book two.","status":"approved","story":"alexander shulgin, sasha to everyone, was born in berkeley in 1925, served in the navy, and got a phd in biochemistry. at dow chemical in the fifties he invented a profitable pesticide, and the company let him do more or less what he wanted. in 1960 he took mescaline, the psychedelic in the peyote cactus, and said he learned there was a great deal inside him. he spent the rest of his life on it. he left dow in 1966 and built a lab behind his house on a hill in lafayette, east of san francisco.\nhe held a license from the dea, the us drug enforcement administration, to work with banned substances, consulted for them, and testified as an expert. meanwhile he designed and made hundreds of new compounds and tested them on himself first, then with a small circle of friends, and rated what happened. in 1976 he made mdma, a drug first made by the german company merck in 1912 and forgotten, and gave it to a psychotherapist friend, leo zeff, who was about to retire and instead spent his last years teaching it to hundreds of other therapists. they said it helped people talk about things they'd never been able to say.\nann, born in new zealand in 1931, married him in 1981. she was a lay therapist, trained by doing rather than by a degree, and worked with clients using mdma and other drugs while that was still legal. together they wrote pihkal: a chemical love story, in 1991, and tihkal: the continuation, in 1997. the first half of each is their life, a love story told in turns. the second half is the chemistry. this page stays out of that half.\nin 1994, two years after pihkal came out, the dea raided the lab, fined him twenty five thousand dollars over samples people had sent him anonymously, and took the license back. he kept working and teaching anyway. sasha had a stroke in 2010 and died of liver cancer in 2014 at eighty eight. ann died in 2022.","struggle":"sasha's first wife, nina, died in 1977, after a stroke. he met ann not long after, and the love story in pihkal starts from that grief.\nthe government that licensed him turned on him. the pihkal book, with its recipes in the back, embarrassed the dea, and the 1994 raid ended the legal side of his lab. in his last years a stroke took most of his speech and some of his memory. ann looked after him on the hill, with friends raising money for his care.","threads":["psychedelics","love","work"],"years":"sasha 1925 to 2014, ann 1931 to 2022"},{"after":"after the surrender he didn't pretend it hadn't happened and he didn't pretend to be converted either. he went home to a cabin on the grand river, near where he was born, raised horses and kids, and argued with the agent. in 1889, when a government commission came to break up the great sioux reservation and buy the pieces, he spoke against it. they got the signatures anyway.\nafter the wild west show he came home too. he'd seen the cities and the crowds. the story goes that he gave most of his show money away to hungry kids in the streets, and couldn't understand how a country that rich let its own children beg. he never went back on tour.","basically":"the hunkpapa lakota holy man who became the face of the plains nations saying no. he wasn't a general. he was the one people trusted to pray, to dream, and to decide, and in 1876 he saw the biggest native victory over the us army in a vision before it happened. then he spent four hungry years in canada, four months in a wild west show, and was shot by his own people's police on his own doorstep. think of the one elder in town everybody, the government included, knows will not sign. take from him that you can lose everything and still be the person who didn't sell.","born":1831,"died":1890,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 30 250 L 80 160 L 130 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 74 150 L 80 160 L 86 150'/><path d='M 72 250 L 80 226 L 88 250' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 292 250 L 330 180 L 368 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 324 170 L 330 180 L 336 170'/><path d='M 322 250 L 330 226 L 338 250' fill='var(--page-ink)' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 200 250 L 200 110 M 200 140 L 184 118 M 200 132 L 218 112'/><path d='M 184 118 L 180 132 M 218 112 L 224 126' stroke='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='236' cy='178' r='14'/><path d='M 236 192 L 236 234'/><path d='M 236 234 C 216 234 206 250 222 250 L 250 250 C 266 250 256 234 236 234'/><path d='M 236 208 L 222 234 M 236 208 L 250 234'/><g transform='translate(110,40) rotate(180) scale(.28)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -16 52 M 0 28 L 16 52'/><\/g><g transform='translate(160,70) rotate(170) scale(.28)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -16 52 M 0 28 L 16 52'/><\/g><g transform='translate(270,50) rotate(190) scale(.28)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -16 52 M 0 28 L 16 52'/><\/g><path d='M 104 50 L 100 60 M 154 80 L 150 90 M 264 60 L 262 70' opacity='.4'/><g transform='translate(330,163.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he danced until he dropped, saw soldiers falling into camp like grasshoppers, and a few weeks later custer rode in.","key":"sittingbull","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sitting_Bull","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the battlefield","url":"https://www.nps.gov/libi/index.htm","why":"the national park service site at the little bighorn, which now honors the lakota and cheyenne who fought there"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"he was proud and stubborn, and the agent at standing rock called it arrogance in every report. that was the job.","moments":["in 1884, at a show in st. paul, minnesota, he saw a young sharpshooter named annie oakley hit everything she aimed at. he adopted her as a daughter, the lakota way, named her little sure shot, and offered sixty five dollars for a photograph of the two of them. she used the name the rest of her life.","the story goes that when the shooting started at his arrest, the gray horse buffalo bill had given him, trained for the show, heard the gunfire and sat down and started doing its tricks.","his son crow foot, the boy who handed over the rifle at fort buford, was killed with him at the cabin in 1890.","in 2016 thousands of people camped at standing rock, where he lived and died, against the dakota access pipeline, an oil line under the missouri river upstream of the reservation's water."],"name":"sitting bull","place":"the grand river and the yellowstone country on the northern plains, then canada, then the standing rock reservation on the border of the dakotas","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 35 70 C 33 86 35 100 37 114 M 85 70 C 87 86 85 100 83 114' stroke-width='6'/><path d='M 80 34 C 86 22 92 12 98 4 C 98 16 92 28 84 36 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 82 35 L 97 7' opacity='.5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"let us put our minds together and see what life we can make for our children.","where":"attributed; widely quoted and hard to pin to a date or place"},{"q":"i wish it to be remembered that i was the last man of my tribe to give up my rifle.","where":"reported from his surrender at fort buford, july 1881, paraphrased"},{"q":"the voice in the vision said, i give you these because they have no ears.","where":"the sun dance vision, june 1876, as his relatives told it later, paraphrased. no ears meant the soldiers wouldn't listen"}],"start":"ernie lapointe's sitting bull: his life and legacy, written by his great-grandson from the family's own stories. then robert utley's the lance and the shield, a careful life by a white historian.","status":"approved","story":"he was born around 1831 on the grand river in what's now south dakota, into the hunkpapa, one of the seven bands of the lakota, the plains nation white americans called the sioux. hunkpapa means head of the circle, because when the bands camped together, their lodges stood at the entrance. as a boy they called him slow, because he took his time with everything. at fourteen he rode into a fight with the crow, a rival nation, and counted coup, which means he touched an enemy with his hand or a stick, a braver thing than killing him. his father gave him his own name for it. tatanka iyotake. sitting bull.\nhe became a wičháša wakȟáŋ, a holy man, which is the lakota word for someone who prays and dreams on behalf of the people. outsiders say medicine man. by the 1870s, the lakota who refused to go live on reservations looked to him as their leader. in early june 1876, in a huge camp on rosebud creek in montana, he held a sun dance, the great summer ceremony where people fast and dance for days around a sacred tree to pray for everyone. the story his relatives told is that he had fifty small pieces of skin cut from each arm as an offering, then danced until he passed out. when he came to, he said he'd seen soldiers falling into the camp upside down, like grasshoppers, and heard a voice say they were being given to the people.\non june 25, 1876, george custer and the seventh cavalry attacked the big camp on the little bighorn river. custer and every man with him died. sitting bull was in his forties and didn't lead the charge. he was the holy man, and people said the vision had come true. the army's answer was a war that didn't stop, and in may 1877 he led his people north across the border into canada, grandmother's land, the lakota called it, after queen victoria. the buffalo ran out there too. on july 19, 1881, at fort buford, an army post in dakota territory, he surrendered, and had his young son crow foot hand over his rifle.\nthe army held him as a prisoner of war at fort randall, on the missouri, for about twenty months, then sent him to the standing rock reservation. in 1885 he toured for four months with buffalo bill's wild west, the traveling show of cowboys and real indians, for about fifty dollars a week, and sold signed photographs of himself. in 1890 the ghost dance, a new religion from nevada promising the dead would come back, reached standing rock. the government agent there, james mclaughlin, had hated him for years and was sure he'd lead an uprising. before dawn on december 15, 1890, lakota police came to arrest him at his cabin. a crowd gathered, somebody fired, and lieutenant bull head shot him in the chest and sergeant red tomahawk shot him in the head. two weeks later came wounded knee.","struggle":"canada was four years of watching his people get hungrier. the buffalo herds that had fed the lakota for generations were being shot out by white hunters for their hides, and there was nothing left to follow. the canadian government wanted him gone and wouldn't give him land. by 1881 families were slipping back south to surrender one after another, and he went last, with the ones who were left.\nthen twenty months as a prisoner, and then a reservation where an agent's job was to make his kind of leader stop existing. he was a man whose whole life had been deciding things for his people, and now a clerk decided when they ate.","threads":["war","exile","fame","the land"],"years":"around 1831 to 1890"},{"after":"the morning after the bishop's letter she didn't obey and she didn't go quiet. about three months later she sent the reply. she kept going after that, too. in early 1692 she was buying a two story cell inside the convent, and a second volume of her work was printed in seville with first dream in it.\nthen the quiet, and what it means is the fight. octavio paz, in the traps of faith, his 1982 book, tells it as a woman caught between churchmen and made to give in. catholic scholars in mexico read the same papers as a nun who decided for herself and gave her things away willingly. the papers can carry either reading. the renewal of vows doesn't mention books. under it she left a space for the date of her death and asked her sisters to pray for her, who had been and was the worst there ever was. she signed it yo, la peor del mundo, i, the worst in the world. the phrase most people know, i, the worst of all, is the title of a 1990 film about her, and some accounts give her signature that way. one historian has reported a list of what was in her cell when she died, 180 books and fifteen bundles of writing, which would mean she never fully stopped. other scholars doubt the list. nobody disputes how it ended. she died nursing her sisters. whether the last two years were surrender or peace, the record doesn't say.","basically":"a poet and nun in mexico city in the late 1600s, when mexico was a spanish colony called new spain, who taught herself everything because girls weren't let into the university. she chose the convent because she didn't want a husband and did want a library, and from her cell she wrote poems that got printed in spain. when a bishop told her in print, under a woman's name, to turn away from worldly learning, she answered with a long letter saying women have every right to study, and people now call it the first feminist manifesto. think of the sharpest person in the building being told to stay in her lane by a boss on a fake account, hitting reply all, and then going quiet two years later for reasons scholars still fight about.","born":1648,"died":1695,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 206 44 L 252 44 L 252 98 L 206 98 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><circle cx='232' cy='66' r='9' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 214 56 L 214 60 M 212 58 L 216 58 M 244 84 L 244 88 M 242 86 L 246 86' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 196 L 112 196 L 112 250 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 62 250 L 62 230 Q 76 216 90 230 L 90 250 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><ellipse cx='78' cy='188' rx='24' ry='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='78' cy='187' r='3.5' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 102 188 L 126 182'/><path d='M 68 172 Q 62 164 68 156 Q 74 148 68 140 M 88 172 Q 82 164 88 156' stroke-width='2' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 142 176 C 136 116 184 116 178 176 L 172 176 C 176 132 144 132 148 176 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='160' cy='150' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 155 155 Q 160 159 165 155' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 160 164 L 160 212'/><path d='M 160 212 L 146 248 M 160 212 L 174 248'/><path d='M 160 180 L 126 182'/><path d='M 160 182 L 188 172'/><path d='M 184 158 L 200 163 L 216 158 L 216 174 L 200 179 L 184 174 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 200 163 L 200 179' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 290 250 L 290 100 L 366 100 L 366 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 290 150 L 366 150 M 290 200 L 366 200'/><rect x='296' y='116' width='10' height='34' fill='var(--teal)' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='308' y='110' width='9' height='40' fill='var(--clay)' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='319' y='122' width='11' height='28' fill='var(--sage)' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='348' cy='132' r='11' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 337 132 L 359 132 M 348 143 L 348 150 M 342 150 L 354 150' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='296' y='164' width='9' height='36' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='307' y='170' width='12' height='30' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='321' y='160' width='8' height='40' fill='var(--blue)' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='296' y='214' width='12' height='36' fill='var(--olive)' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='310' y='208' width='9' height='42' fill='var(--clay)' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='321' y='218' width='10' height='32' fill='var(--teal)' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='339' y='212' width='9' height='38' fill='var(--sage)' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='350' y='220' width='10' height='30' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke-width='2'/><g transform='translate(334,173) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"she became a nun so she could be left alone with her books, and at the end the books went too.","key":"sorjuana","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juana_In%C3%A9s_de_la_Cruz","why":"the plain overview of her life and work"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La_Respuesta_a_Sor_Filotea_de_la_Cruz","why":"the reply, her letter defending a woman's right to learn"},{"label":"academy of american poets","url":"https://poets.org/poet/sor-juana-ines-de-la-cruz","why":"a short life and her poems in english"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://poetryfoundation.org/poets/sor-juana","why":"another short life, with poems"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"she owned a person. when she took her vows in 1669 her mother gave her an enslaved woman named juana de san josé, who lived in the convent cell as her servant. the sources call her a mulata, the colonial word for someone of mixed african and european ancestry, and one account has her four years younger than the nun. on june 6, 1684, sor juana sold her to her own sister, josefa maría, for two hundred and fifty gold pesos. the bill of sale between the two sisters is where that comes from. octavio paz, the mexican poet who wrote the big book on her, puts the sale in 1683 and says it included the woman's nursing baby. almost nothing else about juana de san josé was written down.\nit was ordinary in her world, where a nun with money kept servants in her cell. it's still the case that the great letter about a woman's right to her own mind was written by a woman who had sold another one. nothing i've read has a line from her about it.","moments":["before she turned three, by her own account, she got a teacher to give her reading lessons by saying her mother had ordered it. the teacher didn't believe her and taught her anyway.","as a child she stopped eating cheese because she'd heard it made people slow. wanting to know things, she says, was stronger in her than wanting to eat. later, learning latin, she would cut off four or six finger widths of her hair and give herself until it grew back to learn a thing. if she hadn't, she cut it again.","in february 1669, in the convent's book of professions, she wrote herself down as the legitimate daughter of pedro de asuaje and isabel ramírez. her parents weren't married.","on february 8, 1694, in the same book, she renewed her vows and signed with her blood. the scholar who went through the whole book says she's the only nun in it who wrote a renewal and the only one who used blood for ink."],"name":"sor juana inés de la cruz","place":"nepantla and mexico city, in new spain, now mexico","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 16 130 C 18 102 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 102 102 104 130 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 31 62 C 31 88 44 98 60 98 C 76 98 89 88 89 62 L 90 110 L 30 110 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 43 49 Q 48 44 54 47 M 66 47 Q 72 44 77 49' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 C 57 78 63 78 68 74'/><path d='M 22 118 C 16 60 28 22 60 22 C 92 22 104 60 98 118 L 87 118 C 91 72 84 36 60 36 C 36 36 29 72 33 118 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='60' cy='110' r='14' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='60' cy='110' r='9.5' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='56' cy='107' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='64' cy='107' r='1.5' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 56 109 L 56 115 M 64 109 L 64 115' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 4 112 L 15 115 L 26 112 L 26 125 L 15 128 L 4 125 Z' fill='var(--teal)'/><path d='M 15 115 L 15 128' stroke-width='1.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"you foolish men who lay the guilt on women, not seeing you're the cause of the very thing you blame.","where":"the poem that begins hombres necios, translated by michael smith, as printed by the academy of american poets"},{"q":"had aristotle prepared victuals, he would have written more.","where":"the reply to sor filotea, 1691, translated by margaret sayers peden in a woman of genius"},{"q":"my purpose in studying is not to write, much less to teach (this would be overbearing pride in my case), but simply to see whether studying makes me less ignorant.","where":"the reply to sor filotea, 1691, translated by alan s. trueblood in a sor juana anthology"}],"start":"the reply to sor filotea. it's a letter, it's short, and she tells her own life in it. margaret sayers peden's translation is called a woman of genius, and alan trueblood's a sor juana anthology has the reply and the poems together. then hombres necios, which is one page. save first dream for last.","status":"approved","story":"she was born juana inés ramírez in san miguel nepantla, a village near mexico city, in new spain, which is what spain called its colony in mexico and the lands around it. the date handed down is november 12, 1651. a baptism record from 1648 that looks like hers says otherwise, and scholars who've been through the parish books point out that her sister maría was baptized in 1651, so nobody's sure. her mother, isabel ramírez, was born in mexico to spanish parents, her father was a spaniard, and the two weren't married. she grew up on a farm her grandfather leased, with his books. almost everything about her childhood comes from a letter she wrote at around forty, so it's her own telling. before she was three, she says, she tagged along behind an older sister to a woman who taught reading, told the teacher her mother wanted her taught too, and could read before her mother found out. at six or seven she heard there was a university in mexico city and begged her mother to change her clothes and send her there. the spanish only says change my clothes, and translators give it as dress me as a boy. her mother said no, so she read her grandfather's books, and no punishment could stop her.\nas a girl she was sent to relatives in mexico city, and in 1664, at about sixteen, she went to live at the court of the viceroy, the man who ran new spain for the king, as a lady in waiting to his wife, leonor carreto. when she was seventeen the viceroy gathered a panel of scholars to test her, and she impressed them. in august 1667 she tried the discalced carmelites, the strict order started by [[teresaavila|teresa of avila]], and left after three months. in february 1669 she took her vows, the promises that make a woman a nun for life, at san jerónimo, a convent of hieronymite nuns where the rules were easier. she was blunt about why. she had no wish at all to marry, she says, and the convent was the least unsuitable and most decent choice open to her. what she'd wanted was to live alone, with no duties to get in the way of study and no community noise to break the quiet of her books. she didn't get alone. she got a cell of her own with servants, musical and scientific instruments, visitors who included two vicereines, and a library. the number that gets repeated is four thousand books. nobody has a catalog, and a list said to be of her cell when she died, which scholars argue over, counts 180.\nfrom that cell she wrote love poems, plays, church songs, one of them partly in nahuatl, the language of the aztecs, and arguments about god. her best known poem starts hombres necios, foolish men, and tells men they blame women for the very thing they talk them into. first dream, close to a thousand lines, is about knowledge, the universe, and how far a human mind can reach. in her letter she calls it the only thing she remembers writing for her own pleasure. in 1689 the vicereine maría luisa, countess of paredes, had her first book printed in madrid. admirers called her the phoenix of america.\nin 1690 she wrote out her objections to a famous sermon preached forty years earlier by antónio vieira, a portuguese jesuit. vieira had claimed he could top anything the saints had said about the greatest proof of love [[jesus|christ]] ever gave. she took his argument apart, defending [[augustine]], thomas aquinas and john chrysostom against him, and added an idea of her own, that god's greatest favors are the ones he holds back. the bishop of puebla, another city in new spain, printed it without asking her, under the title carta atenagórica, and put a letter of his own in front, dated november 25, 1690, and signed sor filotea de la cruz, sor meaning sister. it's a pity, the letter says, that so great a mind stoops to the low news of the earth. he didn't want her to give up books, he wrote, only to better them by sometimes reading the book of jesus christ. she answered on march 1, 1691. the reply to sor filotea tells her childhood, says she studies only to be less ignorant, lists learned women from the bible down to her own day, and takes on [[paul]]'s line that women should keep silent in the churches. it means pulpits, she says, not study and not writing.\nafter that, in most tellings, the archbishop of mexico, francisco de aguiar y seijas, came down on her and wanted the worldly study gone. by 1693 she seems to have stopped writing. her books and instruments went. most accounts say she sold them and the money went to the poor, and some say they were taken. on february 8, 1694, twenty five years after her vows, she renewed them in the convent's book of professions, the register where each nun wrote out her promises, and signed, the entry says, with her blood. in april 1695 a sickness the sources call plague went through the convent. she nursed her sisters, caught it, and died on april 17, 1695, in her middle forties.","struggle":"people told her to stop her whole life, and she wrote it down. in the reply she says a superior at the convent, very holy and very simple, once decided that study was a matter for the inquisition, the church's court for hunting heresy, and ordered her not to read. she obeyed for the three months the woman was in charge. she didn't touch a book. but not studying at all wasn't in her power, she says, so she studied whatever god had made and used the whole world as her book. even the kitchen, where she watched how an egg holds together and fries in fat.\nher confessor, the priest who heard her sins and guided her, was antonio núñez de miranda, a jesuit with weight in the inquisition, and he ran her down in public. around 1681 or 1682 she wrote him a letter that stayed lost until 1980. in it she lets him go. i have this nature, she tells him, i was born with it and i'll die with it.\nand she was careful, because she had to be. in the same reply that defends a woman's right to learn she says flat out that she wants no trouble with the inquisition, that she's ignorant and trembles at saying something that sounds wrong. reading it, you can't always tell where the humility stops and the irony starts.","threads":["silence","work","fame"],"years":"1648 or 1651 to 1695"},{"after":"after the curse he didn't write back. no answer to the synagogue survives, no attack on the people who threw him out. he went to work at the grinding wheel, kept his friends, answered letters from all over europe, and spent years on a book that ends in joy, what he called the intellectual love of god, the happiness of seeing how everything fits.\nthe story goes that when his father died his sister fought him over the inheritance, and he took her to court and won, and then gave her all of it except one bed. he died in a rented room in the hague on a sunday afternoon while his landlord's family was at church. he'd already arranged for his desk, with the ethics in it, to be sent to his publisher in amsterdam.","basically":"a jewish kid in 1600s amsterdam who got thrown out of his community at twenty three with a curse so thorough nobody was allowed to talk to him or stand within six feet of him, and who spent the rest of his short life alone in rented rooms grinding lenses and working out that god and nature are the same thing. einstein said spinoza's god was the only one he believed in. picture someone cut off from his whole family and world before he's twenty four who never publicly hits back, and ends his big book on joy. he's for anyone who got kicked out for thinking and kept thinking.","born":1632,"died":1677,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--olive)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><rect x='290' y='50' width='70' height='70' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 325 50 L 325 120 M 290 85 L 360 85'/><circle cx='343' cy='67' r='9' fill='var(--marigold)'/><rect x='170' y='190' width='130' height='10' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 180 200 L 180 250 M 290 200 L 290 250'/><circle cx='220' cy='172' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='220' cy='172' r='3' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 220 188 L 220 190'/><ellipse cx='262' cy='184' rx='10' ry='6' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 310 120 L 264 178' stroke-dasharray='2 6' opacity='.6'/><g fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none' opacity='.4'><circle cx='204' cy='160' r='1.2'/><circle cx='198' cy='150' r='1.2'/><circle cx='210' cy='146' r='1.2'/><circle cx='194' cy='164' r='1.2'/><\/g><path d='M 117 150 C 112 170 114 182 120 186 M 143 150 C 148 170 146 182 140 186'/><circle cx='130' cy='154' r='14'/><path d='M 130 168 L 130 216'/><path d='M 130 216 L 116 250 M 130 216 L 144 250'/><path d='M 130 182 L 116 206 M 130 182 L 156 176'/><g transform='translate(222,233.5) scale(1.1)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"his own synagogue cursed him at twenty three and told everyone to stay four cubits away, and he went off and ground lenses.","key":"spinoza","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baruch_Spinoza","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the stanford encyclopedia","url":"https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/spinoza/","why":"a longer, serious guide to his ideas"},{"label":"the text, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/3800","why":"the ethics on project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"jewish","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["leibniz came to see him in the hague in november 1676, a few months before he died, and they talked more than once. afterward leibniz played it down.","in 1929 a rabbi in new york cabled einstein to ask whether he believed in god. einstein cabled back that he believed in spinoza's god, who shows himself in the harmony of what exists, and not in a god who concerns himself with what people do.","the story goes that around the time of the herem someone came at him with a knife, and he kept the torn coat afterward as a reminder."],"name":"baruch spinoza","place":"amsterdam, then rented rooms near leiden and the hague","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><circle cx='98' cy='110' r='9' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 93 106 C 95 103 98 102 101 102' opacity='.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i have taken care not to mock human actions, or mourn them, or hate them, only to understand them.","where":"his political treatise, an unfinished book on government, chapter 1, paraphrased"},{"q":"a free man thinks of nothing less than of death, and his wisdom is a meditation on life.","where":"the ethics, his big book, part 4, proposition 67, paraphrased. he numbered everything like geometry"},{"q":"all things excellent are as difficult as they are rare.","where":"the ethics, the last line of part 5"}],"start":"the ethics, the appendix to part 1, where he takes apart the idea of a god who acts like a person. then part 5. steven nadler's spinoza: a life, the standard biography, for the man.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in amsterdam in 1632 into a family of portuguese jews who had been forced to live as christians back in portugal, where the inquisition, the church's heresy court, hunted down converts who kept jewish ways in secret. he went to the community's schools, was very good at them, and started asking the questions he'd later put in print, like whether moses really wrote the books with his name on them, and whether god is the kind of being who makes choices at all.\non july 27, 1656, the community put him under herem, the hebrew word for a ban, the jewish version of excommunication, being cut off completely. it was one of the harshest curses the community ever issued, read out in the synagogue. cursed by day and cursed by night, lying down and rising up. no one was to speak with him, write to him, stay under a roof with him, come within four cubits of him, or read anything he wrote. a cubit is about a forearm, so four is about six feet, the same distance we all learned in 2020. he was twenty three. the document says abominable heresies and doesn't say which.\nhe left, went by benedictus, the latin form of his name, both meaning blessed, and made his living grinding glass lenses for microscopes and telescopes, first in rijnsburg near leiden, then outside the hague, then in it. he was good at it. in 1673 he turned down a professorship at heidelberg, the big university in germany, because he wanted to stay free to think.\nthe ethics took him years and he didn't dare publish it. it's laid out like a geometry textbook, definitions and axioms and proofs, and it says there's one substance, one basic stuff that everything is, which you can call god or nature, deus sive natura in latin, and everything is in it. no god outside the world making choices. it came out after he died in 1677, of a lung disease, at forty four, probably made worse by years of breathing glass dust.","struggle":"he opens an early unfinished book, on the improvement of the understanding, by saying experience had taught him that the things people usually chase, money and honor and pleasure, were empty, and that he went looking for something that would give lasting joy. he says he felt like a sick man with a deadly illness looking for a remedy, however uncertain.\nthen being alone. cut off from his family and his people at twenty three, he lived in rented rooms for the rest of his life. in 1672 a mob in the hague tore apart the de witt brothers, johan and cornelis. johan had run the dutch republic for nearly twenty years, and spinoza admired him. the story goes, from leibniz, the german philosopher and mathematician, that he wrote a sign calling them the lowest of barbarians and meant to post it at the scene, and his landlord locked him in the house to keep him from getting killed.","threads":["exile","joy","work"],"years":"1632 to 1677"},{"after":"after carlisle he spent the rest of his life slowly taking it back. he'd been the model student, and in land of the spotted eagle he said the schools had it backwards, that native children should be taught by their own people, their own language and their own ways, and that white america had plenty to learn from them.\nafter the wreck he went home, tried being a chief, and found reservation politics under a government agent weren't a chief's life anymore. so he left for los angeles and made a living playing the thing he was, badly written by others, while he pushed to change who did the writing. then he wrote it himself.","basically":"an oglala lakota kid who got on a train in 1879 to go to carlisle, the first big government boarding school for native children, where they cut his hair and had him pick a new name off a list on the wall. he came out the model student, toured with buffalo bill's wild west, got smashed up in a train wreck, became a chief, quit, and went to hollywood to play indians in the movies and fight to get real ones hired. then in his sixties he wrote the books that said, calmly, that his people had been right about the land all along. think of someone who went through the program designed to erase him and came out with the clearest account of what it tried to take.","born":1868,"died":1939,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 300 250 L 320 176 L 340 250 M 320 176 L 320 250'/><rect x='296' y='146' width='50' height='30' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='308' cy='136' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='334' cy='136' r='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 296 162 L 280 158 L 280 170 L 296 166'/><circle cx='160' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 160 164 L 160 212'/><path d='M 160 212 L 146 246 M 160 212 L 174 246'/><path d='M 160 178 L 174 202 M 160 178 L 134 172'/><ellipse cx='139' cy='140' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(285 139 140)'/><ellipse cx='143' cy='133' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(308 143 133)'/><ellipse cx='149' cy='127' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(331 149 127)'/><ellipse cx='160' cy='124' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(359 160 124)'/><ellipse cx='170' cy='127' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(388 170 127)'/><ellipse cx='177' cy='132' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(411 177 132)'/><ellipse cx='181' cy='140' rx='3' ry='8' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(434 181 140)'/><path d='M 60 250 L 60 216 L 100 216 L 100 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 60 216 L 98 204 L 100 212' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><g transform='translate(321,109.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he was in the first class at the school built to kill the indian in him, and fifty years later he wrote that the white man was the one who'd made the land wild.","key":"standingbear","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luther_Standing_Bear","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carlisle_Indian_Industrial_School","why":"the school he was in the first class of"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["on may 24, 1883, the day the brooklyn bridge opened in new york, a lakota kid who'd been on the plains four years earlier led the carlisle band across it.","he picked the name luther by pointing at squiggles on a wall he couldn't read. he kept it his whole life."],"name":"luther standing bear","place":"the lakota country in dakota territory, carlisle in pennsylvania, the wild west show, then los angeles","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><ellipse cx='24' cy='43' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(290 24 43)'/><ellipse cx='30' cy='33' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(308 30 33)'/><ellipse cx='38' cy='25' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(325 38 25)'/><ellipse cx='49' cy='20' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(342 49 20)'/><ellipse cx='60' cy='18' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(360 60 18)'/><ellipse cx='71' cy='20' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(378 71 20)'/><ellipse cx='82' cy='25' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(395 82 25)'/><ellipse cx='90' cy='33' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(412 90 33)'/><ellipse cx='96' cy='43' rx='4' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)' transform='rotate(430 96 43)'/><path d='M 35 70 C 33 86 35 100 37 114 M 85 70 C 87 86 85 100 83 114' stroke-width='6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"only to the white man was nature a wilderness.","where":"land of the spotted eagle, 1933, the introduction; the lakota, he says, never thought of the plains and hills as wild"},{"q":"the old lakota knew that a man's heart away from nature becomes hard.","where":"land of the spotted eagle, paraphrased"},{"q":"i went east to show my father i was brave, and i thought i was going to my death.","where":"my people the sioux, 1928, the chapters on going to carlisle, paraphrased"}],"start":"land of the spotted eagle, the introduction and the chapters on nature and on schooling. then my people the sioux for the carlisle years.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in december 1868 into the oglala lakota, and named ota kte, plenty kill. in october 1879, at about ten, he was one of the first children at the carlisle indian industrial school in pennsylvania, run by an army officer named richard henry pratt whose motto was kill the indian, save the man. the school meant to make native kids into white americans, no language, no hair, no ceremonies. he went, he said later, to show his father he was brave, sure he'd never come back.\nat carlisle they cut his hair, put him in a uniform, and told him to choose a name from a list of english names written on the wall. he pointed at one without being able to read it. luther. he became pratt's model student, and in may 1883 he marched with the carlisle band across the brooklyn bridge on the day it opened.\nback on the reservation he taught school and clerked. in 1902 and 1903 he toured with buffalo bill's wild west, the traveling show of cowboys and real indians, including in england. in april 1903 the show's train wrecked outside chicago, three native performers died, and he was broken nearly everywhere a person can be, hips, leg, arm, ribs, collarbone, nose. in 1905 the oglala chose him as a chief, and he left that same year.\nin 1912 he moved to california and went into the movies, playing indians in westerns, and pushing hollywood to hire native actors for native parts instead of white men in makeup. in his sixties he wrote my people the sioux, in 1928, and land of the spotted eagle, in 1933. he died of the flu on february 20, 1939, while working on a movie called union pacific.","struggle":"carlisle took his language, his hair, and his name in the first week, and he was ten. more than a hundred and eighty children who went there are buried in the school cemetery. he was one of the kids who lived, and he did it by becoming exactly what they wanted, for years.\nthen the wreck in 1903. he was in his thirties and a performer, and his body was wrecked in a single night. he spent months healing and never toured again.","threads":["exile","family","the land","fame"],"years":"1868 to 1939"},{"after":"what he did after japan, as far as anyone can tell, was start over. he went to a country that had been at war with his fifteen years earlier, at an age when most priests are settling in, and sat down every morning with whoever came in the door.\nand he died the way he'd taught. he told his students that if he suffered at the end, that was all right, that would be a suffering buddha, buddha being just the word for someone who's woken up, and there'd be nothing wrong in it. thin and sick, he got through the ceremony handing over the seat, and two weeks later he was gone. he was sixty seven.","basically":"a small, cheerful japanese priest who came to san francisco at fifty five to do funerals and weddings for japanese american families, and ended up teaching a generation of hippies how to sit still. a lot of american zen runs back through him, and so does the phrase beginner's mind, which you've probably seen on a slide in some office mindfulness session. take from him that you never have to become an expert at your own life. you get to start fresh every morning.","born":1904,"died":1971,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 300 44 A 40 40 0 1 0 322 52' stroke-width='7' opacity='.8'/><ellipse cx='80' cy='248' rx='26' ry='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='80' cy='176' r='14'/><path d='M 80 190 L 80 232'/><path d='M 80 232 C 60 232 50 248 66 248 L 94 248 C 110 248 100 232 80 232'/><path d='M 80 206 L 66 232 M 80 206 L 94 232'/><path d='M 74 174 C 76 176 78 176 80 174 M 82 174 C 84 176 86 176 88 174'/><ellipse cx='180' cy='248' rx='24' ry='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='180' cy='180' r='12'/><path d='M 180 194 L 180 236'/><path d='M 180 236 C 160 236 150 252 166 252 L 194 252 C 210 252 200 236 180 236'/><path d='M 180 210 L 166 236 M 180 210 L 194 236'/><path d='M 170 172 C 164 184 166 196 170 200 M 190 172 C 196 184 194 196 190 200' opacity='.7'/><ellipse cx='250' cy='248' rx='24' ry='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='250' cy='182' r='12'/><path d='M 250 196 L 250 238'/><path d='M 250 238 C 230 238 220 254 236 254 L 264 254 C 280 254 270 238 250 238'/><path d='M 250 212 L 236 238 M 250 212 L 264 238'/><ellipse cx='320' cy='248' rx='24' ry='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='320' cy='180' r='12'/><path d='M 320 194 L 320 236'/><path d='M 320 236 C 300 236 290 252 306 252 L 334 252 C 350 252 340 236 320 236'/><path d='M 320 210 L 306 236 M 320 210 L 334 236'/><ellipse cx='372' cy='248' rx='20' ry='5' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(372,221.1) scale(0.85)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a small japanese priest came to san francisco to do funerals for a congregation, and the hippies started showing up at dawn to sit.","key":"suzuki","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shunry%C5%AB_Suzuki","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the zen center","url":"https://www.sfzc.org/","why":"san francisco zen center, the community his students started"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zen_Mind,_Beginner%27s_Mind","why":"his one book, where to start"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["trudy dixon, the student who did most of the editing on zen mind, beginner's mind, died of cancer in 1969, before the book came out. she was thirty. her introduction is still in it.","at tassajara he'd spend afternoons moving big stones around for the garden, a little man in robes with a pry bar, students hauling on the other end.","his teacher's nickname for him became the title of the biography, crooked cucumber, by his student david chadwick, which is the best way into the man."],"name":"shunryu suzuki","place":"japan, then san francisco and a canyon in the mountains inland from big sur","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 32 56 C 22 50 22 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 98 50 98 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 48 98 L 60 114 L 72 98' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='49' y='114' width='22' height='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"in the beginner's mind there are many possibilities, but in the expert's there are few.","where":"zen mind, beginner's mind, his one book, the prologue"},{"q":"when you do something, you should burn yourself completely, like a good bonfire, leaving no trace of yourself.","where":"zen mind, beginner's mind, the talk called no trace"},{"q":"each of you is perfect the way you are, and you can use a little improvement.","where":"attributed; his students tell it, it isn't in the book"}],"start":"zen mind, beginner's mind, the prologue and then any talk that grabs you. crooked cucumber, david chadwick, for the man.","status":"approved","story":"he was the son of a zen priest and became one himself as a boy. zen is the japanese branch of buddhism that cares less about scripture and more about sitting still and seeing straight. his family's school was soto, the quieter of zen's two big schools, the one that says just sit, no riddles to solve, no goal. he spent the first half of his life running country temples in japan. his teacher called him crooked cucumber, a cucumber too bent to be any use, and he never argued.\nin 1959, at fifty five, he came to san francisco to look after sokoji, a japanese american soto temple in japantown. the job was funerals, weddings, and a congregation of families. then a few americans turned up asking about zazen, which is soto meditation, sitting facing a wall with your back straight and your eyes half open, doing nothing on purpose. he told them he sat every morning and they could sit with him. more came.\nthe students became san francisco zen center in 1962. in 1967 they bought tassajara, an old hot springs resort at the end of a dirt road in the mountains inland from big sur, and made it the first zen monastery outside asia. he was a small, quiet man who laughed a lot and moved rocks for the gardens with his students.\nzen mind, beginner's mind came out in 1970, talks his students taped and edited, and it's still the book people start with. in 1971 he had cancer. in late november he handed the zen center over to his american student richard baker in a ceremony, and he died on december 4. he's no relation to d.t. suzuki, the japanese scholar who spent decades writing the books that first explained zen to westerners. people mix them up all the time.","struggle":"in 1952, back in japan, his wife chie was killed at his temple by a mentally ill man who'd been staying there. suzuki was away. he'd let the man stay. that's the kind of thing a person carries, and he didn't talk about it much.\nin san francisco he had two congregations under one roof and they didn't get along. the japanese american board at sokoji wanted a priest for their families, and got a man whose mornings belonged to a room full of long haired americans. he tried to serve both for ten years. in 1969 he left sokoji and moved in with the students.","threads":["grief","dying"],"years":"1904 to 1971"},{"after":"after the statue she didn't become serene. she became busy. convent after convent over twenty years, with no money and bad health, arguing with men who could have had her locked up, and writing books in between letters about leaky roofs and debts.\nshe wrote the interior castle in a few months in 1577, in the middle of the worst fights over the reform, the same year her friend and fellow reformer [[johncross|john of the cross]] was kidnapped by his own brothers. the book says you get to god by going in, one room at a time, and that humility is the floor of every room. she wrote it while the building around her was on fire.","basically":"a spanish nun in the 1500s, funny, chatty, and vain as a girl, who coasted through convent life for almost twenty years, then broke down in front of a statue at thirty nine and turned into a force of nature. she started seventeen convents on terrible roads from the back of a covered cart, fought bishops and her own order, had her book seized by the church's heresy court, and wrote a book about prayer that's still one of the best maps of the inside of a person. think of someone who starts a chain of tiny shelters with no money, bad health, and every official in the country against her, and wins. she's for anyone who figures they already missed their chance.","born":1515,"died":1582,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 C 80 244 140 256 200 250 S 320 244 380 250'/><path d='M 50 252 C 60 240 140 240 150 252 C 130 262 70 262 50 252 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 82 176 C 76 116 124 116 118 176 L 112 176 C 116 132 84 132 88 176 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='100' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 100 164 L 100 212'/><path d='M 100 212 L 86 246 M 100 212 L 114 246'/><path d='M 100 178 L 80 154 M 100 178 L 120 154'/><path d='M 80 70 Q 80 52 100 54 Q 110 36 130 48 Q 147 40 151 58 Q 160 68 147 70 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='200' cy='205' rx='24' ry='13' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 186 216 L 184 244 M 196 218 L 196 246 M 206 218 L 206 246 M 216 216 L 218 244 M 180 200 L 170 188 M 224 202 L 232 214 M 224 206 L 244 212'/><ellipse cx='164' cy='186' rx='9' ry='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 166 180 L 164 166 M 170 181 L 172 168'/><g transform='rotate(-12 290 220)'><path d='M 240 222 L 340 222 L 340 190 L 240 190 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 240 190 C 240 150 340 150 340 190' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='262' cy='232' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='318' cy='232' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><g transform='translate(281,139.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"she was a lukewarm nun for almost twenty years, woke up at thirty nine, and then founded convents all over spain from the back of a cart.","key":"teresaavila","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Teresa_of_%C3%81vila","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Interior_Castle","why":"her book on prayer"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"she was vain as a girl, loved romances and perfume and her own looks, and she tells you so. as a founder she could be ruthless in a negotiation, and got her way with bishops by being charming and not letting go.","moments":["in 1576 a friar painted her portrait, the only one done from life. she looked at it and said, the story goes, god forgive you, brother juan, for painting me ugly and bleary eyed.","she met [[johncross|john of the cross]] in 1567, when he was twenty five and about to leave for the carthusians, the strictest monks around, and talked him into her reform instead.","the river story. crossing a flooded stream, her cart tipped and dumped her in the mud, and she heard god say this is how he treats his friends. she said that's why he has so few. it's attributed, not documented, and it's the most teresa thing she may never have said."],"name":"teresa of avila","place":"ávila, and every bad road in castile","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 100 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 100 100 102 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 104 C 40 96 50 94 60 94 C 70 94 80 96 90 104' opacity='.7'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 34 70 C 36 84 44 92 60 92 C 76 92 84 84 86 70 L 88 108 L 32 108 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 24 116 C 18 60 28 22 60 22 C 92 22 102 60 96 116 L 86 116 C 90 72 84 36 60 36 C 36 36 30 72 34 116 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 96 128 C 98 112 104 98 114 88 C 112 102 106 116 98 126' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"god walks among the pots and pans.","where":"the book of her foundations, her account of starting the convents, chapter 5, paraphrased"},{"q":"let nothing disturb you, let nothing frighten you, all things pass, god does not change.","where":"a short poem found in her breviary, the prayer book nuns carried, after she died"},{"q":"if this is how you treat your friends, no wonder you have so few.","where":"attributed; the story is she said it to god after her cart dumped her in a river"}],"start":"the book of her life, her autobiography, chapters 8 and 9, the lukewarm years and the statue. then the interior castle, the first dwelling places, which is what she calls the rooms.","status":"approved","story":"teresa de ahumada was born in avila, a walled city in castile, in 1515. her grandfather was a converso, a jew whose family had converted to christianity, and the spanish inquisition, the church's court for hunting down heresy, including people it suspected of secretly still being jewish, made him do public penance in toledo. the family worked hard afterward to pass as old christians, spaniards with no jewish blood. at seven she and her brother ran away from home to get martyred, killed for their faith, by the moors, the muslims of north africa, so they could go straight to heaven. an uncle caught them on the road.\nat twenty she entered the convent of the incarnation in avila, a house of carmelite nuns, an order that traced itself back to hermits on mount carmel in the holy land. it was big, comfortable, and chatty, with visitors in the parlor, the visiting room, all day, and she liked the chatting. then she got so sick she lay in a coma for days and they dug her grave. she was paralyzed for about three years after.\nfor almost twenty years she was, by her own account, a middling nun. in 1554, at about thirty nine, she walked past a statue of christ covered in wounds and broke down in front of it. she counted her real life from there.\nin 1562 she founded a small, poor, strict convent, st joseph's in avila, where the nuns went in rough sandals instead of shoes, discalced, the old word for shoeless, as a sign they were poor. the town fought it. then she founded more, seventeen in all, traveling in covered carts on terrible roads, sick most of the time, fighting bishops and her own order. she wrote the interior castle in 1577, a book about the soul as a castle with god in the innermost room. she died in 1582 at alba de tormes, on the road.","struggle":"the twenty years were the struggle. she describes being pulled between god and the parlor, wanting both, doing neither well. she gave up mental prayer, which means quiet prayer in your own words, just talking to god as a friend, for about a year because she thought she was too bad to pray, and she calls it one of the worst things she ever did.\nthen, once the visions came, nobody believed her. her confessors, the priests who heard her sins and guided her, told her it was the devil. one told her to make an obscene gesture at the visions to drive them off, the fig, a rude hand sign about like giving the finger, and she did, and apologized to christ while she did it. the inquisition took her autobiography and kept it for years.","threads":["work","wandering"],"years":"1515 to 1582"},{"after":"he built plum village out of a farm in southwest france in 1982, with a handful of vietnamese refugees, and turned exile into a home that took in everybody. tens of thousands of people came through. he taught them to wash the dishes.\nwhen his own country finally let him back in 2005, he went, taught, and left again. in 2018, after the stroke, he went home to the temple in hue where he'd been a teenage novice and stayed until he died. nobody had to explain the ending to him.","basically":"a vietnamese buddhist monk who spent the vietnam war telling both sides to stop, got locked out of his own country for it, and spent the next forty years in france teaching people to wash the dishes like it mattered. a lot of what's in the meditation app on your phone traces back to him, usually with the hard parts sanded off. if you've ever tried to be present and felt like it was one more thing to fail at, he's the one who makes it small enough to actually do.","born":1926,"died":2022,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--blue)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='220' cy='140' r='14'/><path d='M 220 154 L 220 200'/><path d='M 220 200 L 204 236 M 220 200 L 238 232'/><path d='M 220 168 L 206 190 M 220 168 L 240 176'/><path d='M 215 145 Q 220 149 225 145'/><g fill='var(--peach)'><circle cx='150' cy='250' r='5'/><circle cx='120' cy='250' r='5'/><circle cx='90' cy='250' r='5'/><circle cx='60' cy='250' r='5'/><\/g><path d='M 150 245 L 150 238 M 120 245 L 120 238 M 90 245 L 90 238 M 60 245 L 60 238'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 205 L 370 205 L 370 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='335' cy='205' rx='35' ry='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='335' cy='200' rx='16' ry='4' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 330 190 C 328 180 334 176 332 168 M 340 190 C 342 178 336 174 340 164' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(378,178.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he got banned from his own country for saying the war should stop, and spent forty years teaching people to wash dishes.","key":"thay","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thich_Nhat_Hanh","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"his community","url":"https://plumvillage.org/","why":"plum village, with his talks, guided meditations, and retreats"}],"lineage":"buddhist","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["he wrote a poem in 1978 called please call me by my true names, after hearing about a twelve year old girl on one of those refugee boats who'd been raped by a pirate and drowned herself. in the poem he is the girl and he is the pirate, because if he'd been born in the pirate's village he might have been the pirate. that's the whole of his teaching in one poem.","martin luther king called him an apostle of peace and nonviolence in the nobel nomination letter. they'd met once.","at plum village a bell rings every so often and everybody stops, mid-sentence, mid-step, for three breaths. visitors find it annoying for about a day."],"name":"thich nhat hanh","place":"hue, vietnam, then exile, then home","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 40 100 C 52 108 74 108 90 98 L 98 130 L 44 130 Z' fill='var(--marigold)' opacity='.9'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 48 C 40 36 80 36 86 48' opacity='.35'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 30 52 C 32 28 88 28 90 52 C 80 44 40 44 30 52 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 30 52 L 90 52' /><path d='M 40 44 L 40 34 M 50 40 L 50 30 M 60 38 L 60 28 M 70 40 L 70 30 M 80 44 L 80 34' opacity='.35'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"wash the dishes to wash the dishes. not to get them done.","where":"the miracle of mindfulness, his small 1975 book on paying attention, paraphrased"},{"q":"there's no way to peace. peace is the way.","where":"a line he used often. a.j. muste, an american pacifist minister, said it first and he knew that"},{"q":"people say walking on water is a miracle. walking on the earth is the miracle.","where":"the miracle of mindfulness, paraphrased"}],"start":"the miracle of mindfulness. then peace is every step, which is even shorter. any of his short talks on breathing will do more than a long book.","status":"approved","story":"a vietnamese zen monk. zen is the branch of buddhism built around sitting meditation and paying close attention to right now. he became a novice monk at sixteen. in the sixties, with the war tearing his country apart, the communist north against the american-backed south, he refused to take a side and organized monks and nuns to rebuild bombed villages instead. he called it engaged buddhism, buddhism that gets its hands dirty out in the world instead of staying in the temple. both sides hated him for it.\nin 1966 he came to america to ask for peace. he met [[merton|thomas merton]], the most famous monk in america, who wrote an essay calling him his brother. he met [[king|martin luther king]], who nominated him for the nobel peace prize the next year and then came out against the war himself, partly because of him. vietnam responded by refusing to let him come home. that lasted almost forty years.\nhe settled in france and built plum village, a monastery and retreat center on old farmland in the southwest. his books are short, plain, and mostly about breathing, walking, and doing the dishes with your whole attention. the miracle of mindfulness, from 1975, is where half the mindfulness industry started, the apps and the corporate workshops, and he'd be the first to say the industry missed the point.\na stroke in 2014 took his speech. in 2018 he went home to the temple in hue, the old imperial city in central vietnam, where he'd started as a teenager, and died there in january 2022, at ninety five.","struggle":"exile was the struggle. in his journal from the early sixties, published as fragrant palm leaves, he writes about a loneliness so total he'd sit and watch a single leaf for hours because it was the only thing that made sense.\nthe young workers he'd trained to rebuild bombed villages kept getting killed, by one side or the other. he had to write letters to their families from another country.\nin 1976 he chartered a ship to pull boat people out of the south china sea. boat people were the vietnamese who fled after the war in small overcrowded boats, and huge numbers of them drowned or were attacked by pirates. singapore expelled him for the rescue and it collapsed. he called it one of the hardest failures of his life.","threads":["war","exile","nonviolence"],"years":"1926 to 2022"},{"after":"after the fire, he became the man in concord who knew the woods better than anyone alive. he spent the rest of his life surveying them, measuring them, and writing down which plant flowered on which day, year after year, pages and pages of it. scientists use his records now to track how the climate's changed.\nafter john, he wrote the book, even if it flopped. and after walden he didn't stay a hermit. he said he left for as good a reason as he went, that he had several more lives to live. he went back to the house in town and lived there until he died, looking after the pencil business and his mother.","basically":"a stubborn new england oddball who built a tiny house by a pond, lived in it for two years to find out what he actually needed, and wrote the book everybody who's ever fantasized about quitting and moving to the woods is quoting, whether they know it or not. he also spent a night in jail rather than pay a tax that went to a government backing slavery, and the essay he wrote about it got read by gandhi and martin luther king. take from him that you find out what you need by doing without the rest for a while.","born":1817,"died":1862,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 240 L 220 240'/><path d='M 60 240 L 60 170 L 150 170 L 150 240' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 172 L 105 134 L 160 172'/><path d='M 130 150 L 130 128 L 142 128 L 142 158'/><path d='M 94 240 L 94 196 L 118 196 L 118 240' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='106' cy='196' r='9'/><path d='M 106 210 L 106 252'/><path d='M 106 252 C 86 252 76 268 92 268 L 120 268 C 136 268 126 252 106 252'/><path d='M 106 226 L 92 252 M 106 226 L 120 252'/><ellipse cx='300' cy='250' rx='90' ry='24' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 290 246 C 296 238 310 238 314 246 C 308 250 296 250 290 246 Z M 314 246 L 322 240' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 20 270 C 60 262 100 276 150 268' stroke-dasharray='3 8' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 30 262 L 48 262 L 46 272 L 32 272 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><g fill='var(--paper)'><circle cx='176' cy='234' r='6'/><circle cx='190' cy='234' r='6'/><circle cx='183' cy='222' r='6'/><\/g><path d='M 360 200 L 360 105'/><ellipse cx='360' cy='96' rx='25' ry='19' fill='var(--sage)'/><g transform='translate(183,199.8) scale(0.6)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he spent two years in a cabin by a pond reading the gita, and one night in jail that changed the twentieth century.","key":"thoreau","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_David_Thoreau","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the books, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/205","why":"walden and civil disobedience at project gutenberg"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walden_Pond","why":"the pond itself, a state park now"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"in april 1844 he and a friend were cooking fish in the woods near concord in a dry spring, and the fire got away from them. it burned about three hundred acres. for years people in town called him a woods burner. he wrote about it in his journal six years later, and admitted that while it burned he'd gone up a hill and watched it.\nthe story that his mother did his laundry while he was playing hermit gets told as a gotcha. it's partly true. walden was a mile and a half from town, he walked in every day or two, and he ate at his family's table and emerson's. he never claimed otherwise. the book says so, if you read it.","moments":["in 1859 he stood up in the concord town hall and defended john brown, the abolitionist who'd led an armed raid on the federal armory at harpers ferry, virginia, to start a slave uprising, and was about to be hanged for it. almost nobody in the north would say his name. the town officials wouldn't ring the bell for the meeting, so he rang it himself.","near the end, a friend tried to get him talking about the next world, and the story goes he said, one world at a time."],"name":"henry david thoreau","place":"concord, massachusetts, and a cabin a mile and a half out of town","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 38 84 48 90 60 90 C 72 90 82 84 84 66' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"i went to the woods because i wished to live deliberately.","where":"walden, the chapter where i lived, and what i lived for"},{"q":"in the morning i bathe my intellect in the stupendous and cosmogonal philosophy of the bhagvat geeta.","where":"walden, the chapter the pond in winter"},{"q":"i did not know we had ever quarreled.","where":"attributed; said on his deathbed to an aunt who asked if he'd made his peace with god"}],"start":"walden, the chapters where i lived, and what i lived for, and the pond in winter. then civil disobedience, which is short.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in concord, massachusetts, in 1817, went to harvard, and came home. he taught school, quit after two weeks rather than whip the children, worked in the family pencil business and made better pencils, surveyed land, and walked. every day, for hours. ralph waldo emerson lived down the road and took him in as a sort of younger brother. emerson was the most famous writer in america, the center of a circle called the transcendentalists, who believed god shows up in nature and in your own gut more than in any church.\non july 4, 1845, he moved into a one room cabin he'd built himself on emerson's land at walden pond. he stayed two years, two months, and two days. he planted beans, read, wrote, watched ice, and took notes on everything. walden came out of it in 1854. he said he went to the woods to live deliberately.\nhe read the [[krishna|bhagavad gita]] out there, the hindu scripture where the god krishna talks a prince through his terror on the morning of a battle, and said so. in the morning, he wrote, he bathed his intellect in its stupendous and cosmogonal philosophy, cosmogonal meaning about how the universe got made. then he went down to the pond for water and found men cutting the ice into blocks to ship south, and thought the pure walden water was mingling with the sacred water of the ganges, india's holy river.\nin july 1846 the tax collector stopped him on his way to the cobbler and asked for six years of back poll tax, a flat tax every man paid just for being there. he refused, because the government backed slavery and was fighting a war with mexico that he and plenty of others saw as a land grab to spread slavery. he spent a night in the concord jail until somebody, probably his aunt, paid it. he turned the night into an essay, resistance to civil government, in 1849. it's usually called civil disobedience now. [[gandhi]] read it. he died of tuberculosis in 1862, at forty four.","struggle":"in january 1842 his older brother john cut his finger shaving, got lockjaw, which is tetanus, and died in henry's arms eleven days later. henry then came down with the symptoms of lockjaw himself, with no wound at all, and was sick for weeks. he could barely say john's name for years afterward.\nhis first book, a week on the concord and merrimack rivers, was about a boat trip he'd taken with john. it came out in 1849 and flopped. he'd paid for the printing. in 1853 the publisher sent back the unsold copies and he wrote in his journal that he now had a library of nearly nine hundred volumes, over seven hundred of which he'd written himself.","threads":["the land","prison","grief"],"years":"1817 to 1862"},{"after":"the boy who swore off the church at seven was baptized in the halifax river at daytona in 1913 and ordained a baptist minister in 1925. decades later, preaching at boston university, he told the chapel about his father's funeral and what he'd said to his mother as they left the cemetery. a student who was there remembers that he paused, smiled, looked down from the pulpit and said, and here i am.\nabout the marches there's no turn to report. he never joined one, and i can't find that he ever said he should have. what he did was what he'd always done. when king was stabbed in new york in 1958, thurman went to see him in the hospital and told him to ask his doctors for two more weeks, time away from the pressure of the movement to look again at himself and the cause. king wrote back that he was following the advice. thurman spent his last sixteen years in san francisco, running his trust and writing. he died there on april 10, 1981.","basically":"a black baptist preacher from florida, raised by a grandmother who had been enslaved, who became the quiet teacher behind the american civil rights movement. in 1949 he wrote a short book saying [[jesus]] was a poor jew living under roman rule, and that what he taught was meant first for people with their backs against the wall. [[king|martin luther king]] read it, and so did plenty of the people around him. thurman himself stayed out of the marches. think of the person who never posts and never takes the stage, and who turns out to be the one the organizers all go to see when they're coming apart.","born":1899,"died":1981,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 62 250 L 70 150 L 98 150 L 106 250 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 40 152 C 8 146 10 100 44 98 C 46 62 104 56 120 88 C 154 82 170 126 140 146 C 128 162 62 164 40 152 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 70 125 L 114 125'/><circle cx='124' cy='196' r='14'/><path d='M 120 210 L 110 246'/><path d='M 110 246 L 144 228 L 160 250'/><path d='M 118 222 L 138 230'/><path d='M 134 222 L 145 227 L 156 222 L 156 233 L 145 238 L 134 233 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 145 227 L 145 238'/><g opacity='.6'><circle cx='250' cy='170' r='10'/><path d='M 250 180 L 250 222 M 250 222 L 240 250 M 250 222 L 262 250 M 250 194 L 264 186'/><path d='M 264 192 L 264 146'/><rect x='252' y='124' width='24' height='22' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='302' cy='174' r='9'/><path d='M 302 183 L 302 224 M 302 224 L 293 250 M 302 224 L 313 250 M 302 196 L 314 189'/><path d='M 314 194 L 314 152'/><rect x='303' y='132' width='22' height='20' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='348' cy='178' r='8'/><path d='M 348 186 L 348 226 M 348 226 L 340 250 M 348 226 L 358 250 M 348 198 L 359 192'/><path d='M 359 196 L 359 158'/><rect x='349' y='140' width='20' height='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><\/g><g transform='translate(78,98) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he wrote the book the civil rights movement read before it marched. then he didn't march.","key":"thurman","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Howard_Thurman","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the king institute","url":"https://kinginstitute.stanford.edu/thurman-howard","why":"stanford's page on him and king, with the letters"},{"label":"the howard thurman papers project","url":"https://www.bu.edu/htpp/howard-thurman-chronology/","why":"boston university's year by year chronology of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jesus_and_the_Disinherited","why":"the book, chapter by chapter"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"his part in it is what he didn't do. he took no part in any of the big civil rights campaigns. his own biographer says so, and says there are no photographs of him marching, arms locked, with king. he didn't walk from selma to montgomery. some activists criticized him for it. the scholar who edited his papers remembers the complaint this way: we thought he would be the [[moses]] of our people, but now he's gone into this mystical stuff. moses being the man in the bible who led the slaves out of egypt. some of his own congregation in san francisco wanted the church to be a center for protest, more than he ever did. and when his autobiography came out in 1979, his biographer says, the reviews in the new york times and the nation, a liberal weekly, were very negative, and both criticized him for his absence from civil rights activism.\nhis answer stayed the same. i am not a movement man, he told the journalist lerone bennett in 1978. it's not my way. i work at giving witness in the external aspect of my life to my experience of the truth. in 1973 he wrote to jesse jackson, one of king's young organizers, that all of my life i have shunned publicity and the limelight. his friends answered for him too. otis moss jr., a baptist pastor who did march at selma, said thurman took part at the level that shapes the philosophy that creates the march. even that defense starts by granting the charge. his biographer lists the possible reasons, and then writes, we do not know.","moments":["the day he left daytona for school, the railroad agent wouldn't check his trunk, because the tag had to go on a handle and his trunk was tied with rope, and he had no money to send it any other way. he was crying when a black man in work clothes asked him, boy, what in hell are you crying about. the man paid the charge, handed him the receipt and went off down the track without another word.","as a boy he'd sit with his back against the big oak in the backyard. in storms, he wrote, the branches snapped and fell, but the topmost ones swayed just enough to keep from breaking loose.","near bardoli in february 1936, gandhi asked the thurmans to sing him a spiritual, one of the songs enslaved black americans made. the one he asked for was were you there when they crucified my lord.","by 1949 the church in san francisco had about 285 members, and about six in ten of them were white."],"name":"howard thurman","place":"daytona, florida, then atlanta, washington, san francisco and boston","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 20 130 C 22 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 98 104 100 130 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 97 M 68 84 L 68 97'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 57 100 L 60 108 L 63 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 25 52 25 68 32 66 M 88 56 C 95 52 95 68 88 66'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 44 C 40 32 52 29 60 29 C 68 29 80 32 84 44 C 78 37 68 34 60 34 C 52 34 42 37 36 44 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 44 50 Q 49 47 54 49 M 66 49 Q 71 47 76 50' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 45 54 Q 50 52 55 54 M 65 54 Q 70 52 75 54' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.6'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 58 L 57 65 L 62 65' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 52 72 Q 60 69 68 72'/><path d='M 54 78 C 57 81 63 81 66 78' stroke-width='2.2'/><path d='M 38 112 L 60 118 L 82 112 L 82 123 L 60 128 L 38 123 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 60 118 L 60 128'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"the masses of men live with their backs constantly against the wall.","where":"jesus and the disinherited, 1949, chapter one"},{"q":"i am not a movement man. it's not my way.","where":"to lerone bennett jr., the editor at ebony magazine, in a 1978 interview"},{"q":"the test of life is often found in the amount of pain we can absorb without spoiling our joy.","where":"what he wrote inside the copy of his book deep river that he sent martin and coretta king in 1955"}],"start":"jesus and the disinherited, which is a little over a hundred pages. then with head and heart, his autobiography, for the stories. then meditations of the heart, his book of meditations, for the quiet side of him.","status":"approved","story":"howard thurman was born on november 18, 1899, in florida. most books say daytona, on the state's east coast. the scholars who edited his papers say it was probably west palm beach, with the family back in daytona soon after. either way daytona is where he grew up. his father died when he was seven, and the person who raised him most was his grandmother, nancy ambrose, who had been born into slavery on a plantation in madison county, florida, in 1842 or 1843. she could neither read nor write, so two or three times a week he read the bible to her. she wanted the psalms, the bible's old songs and prayers, some of isaiah, one of the hebrew prophets, and the gospels, the four books about jesus, again and again. she never wanted the letters of [[paul]], the early missionary whose letters fill a good part of the new testament, except now and then one chapter of first corinthians. when he was halfway through college he finally asked why. she told him that in slavery days the master's minister would hold services for the slaves, and three or four times a year his text was from paul: slaves, be obedient to them that are your masters. she had promised her maker that if she ever learned to read and if freedom ever came, she would not read that part of the bible.\ndaytona had no high school for black children. after seventh grade he studied on his own with his school principal, passed the eighth grade exam, the first in his community to do it, and went off to a baptist boarding school in jacksonville, one of only three high schools for black students in florida. he finished first in his class at morehouse college in atlanta in 1923 and graduated in 1926 from rochester theological seminary, a baptist school for ministers in rochester, new york. he was ordained, made a minister, in 1925. in the spring of 1929 he went to haverford college, outside philadelphia, to study with rufus jones, a quaker and pacifist who taught philosophy there and wrote about mystics, people who say they have met god directly. in 1932 he went to howard university, the black university in washington, to teach religion and run its chapel.\nin september 1935 he sailed for south asia with his wife, sue bailey thurman, and another couple, four black americans invited by a christian student movement there to tour india, burma, now myanmar, and ceylon, the island now called sri lanka. all of it was under british rule. in ceylon, after he gave a talk at the law college in colombo, the principal asked him to coffee and went straight at him. the slave traders were christians. thurman lived in a christian nation where his people were segregated, lynched and burned. i think you are a traitor to all the darker peoples of the earth, the man said, and asked what an intelligent man could say in defense of his position. they talked for more than five hours. in february 1936 he spent three hours with [[gandhi]] near bardoli, in the west of india, and gandhi wanted to know the same thing, why the slaves had taken the religion of their masters.\non that same trip, at the khyber pass, the old mountain road near peshawar, he wrote that they saw clearly what they must do when they got back to america. in 1944 he gave up a tenured job at howard, a job for life, and moved to san francisco to start the church for the fellowship of all peoples with alfred fisk, a white presbyterian minister. it's usually described as the first church in the country set up on purpose to be interracial. in 1949 he answered the man in colombo in print. jesus and the disinherited runs a little over a hundred pages. it says jesus was a poor jew, one of a minority living under a bigger power that controlled them, and that his teaching began as a way for people like that to survive without being eaten alive by fear, by the lying you do to stay safe, or by hate. it also says christianity has too often ended up on the side of the strong against the weak.\nin 1953 boston university made him dean of marsh chapel, its campus church, the first black dean of chapel at a mostly white university in the country. king was finishing his doctorate there at the time. thurman retired in 1965, went back to san francisco, ran the howard thurman educational trust and kept writing. his autobiography, with head and heart, came out in 1979. he died in san francisco on april 10, 1981, at eighty one.","struggle":"the first thing that went wrong was a funeral. saul thurman died of pneumonia in 1907, after five days sick. howard was seven. saul didn't belong to the church and wasn't counted a christian, so the minister wouldn't bury him from it. nancy ambrose took it to the board of deacons, the men who ran the congregation, and won. the minister still wouldn't preach, so a travelling evangelist did it and, in thurman's words, preached my father into hell. the boy said that when he grew up and became a man he would never have anything to do with the church. his biographer calls saul his stepfather. thurman always wrote my father.\nthe second was his first wife. he married katie kelley in june 1926, and their daughter olive was born the next year. katie died of tuberculosis on december 21, 1930. he was thirty one, with a three year old. the next summer he went to london, scotland, paris and geneva, for rest and renewal, as the chronology of his papers puts it. in 1932 he married sue bailey.\nand then there's the question from colombo, which he couldn't answer in an afternoon. it was put to him late in 1935. the book that answers it came out in april 1949. in between he kept giving the argument as talks, again and again, until a week of lectures at a college in austin, texas, in april 1948 turned into the book.","threads":["nonviolence","family","silence"],"years":"1899 to 1981"},{"after":"he did the things a rich man could do. he set up soup kitchens during the famine of 1891 and fed thousands. he gave up the copyright on everything he wrote after 1881 so anyone could print it. he made his own boots, badly. he used the money from resurrection, his last big novel, to ship a whole persecuted sect to canada, the doukhobors, peasant christians the government was punishing for refusing to serve in the army.\nand at eighty two, in the night, he finally walked out to go live as he'd preached. he got as far as a railway station and died there in the stationmaster's bed with reporters outside. it's not a clean ending. it's an honest one.","basically":"a russian count, an aristocrat with a big country estate and peasants working his land, who wrote two of the greatest novels ever written and then fell into a depression so deep he hid the rope from himself. what got him out was reading jesus literally, especially the part about never meeting violence with violence, and that one idea went from him to gandhi to martin luther king. picture the most famous writer alive walking away from his own career at fifty to find out what any of it was for. he's for anyone who knows exactly how they should live and keeps not doing it.","born":1828,"died":1910,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 20 250 L 20 150 L 140 150 L 140 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 10 152 L 80 105 L 150 152'/><path d='M 40 180 L 60 180 L 60 200 L 40 200 Z M 100 180 L 120 180 L 120 200 L 100 200 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='240' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 240 164 L 240 210'/><path d='M 240 210 L 224 246 M 240 210 L 258 242'/><path d='M 240 178 L 226 200 M 240 178 L 260 186'/><path d='M 260 164 L 300 130'/><path d='M 292 122 C 300 112 316 118 312 130 C 316 138 298 140 292 132 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='340' cy='60' r='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='332' cy='55' r='15' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 330 250 L 330 226 L 390 226 L 390 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='345' cy='250' r='6'/><circle cx='375' cy='250' r='6'/><g transform='translate(60,123.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he wrote war and peace, decided none of it mattered, and spent thirty years trying to live like a peasant with a house full of servants.","key":"tolstoy","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leo_Tolstoy","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/43302","why":"the kingdom of god is within you, the book gandhi read, public domain"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"he was hell to be married to. he made his wife sophia read his diaries of his sexual past the week before their wedding, then spent decades fighting her over money, the children, and his desire to give everything away. she bore thirteen children and copied war and peace out by hand seven times. it's cruelty to his wife, told straight.","moments":["in 1901 the russian orthodox church, the country's official church, excommunicated him. crowds turned out to cheer him in the street the next day.","he ran a school for peasant children on his estate in the 1860s with no grades, no punishment, and kids who could leave whenever they wanted. it was a hundred years early.","in his last year he put out a little book of sayings by lao tzu, the ancient chinese sage behind the tao te ching, for russian readers. the count had stopped caring which country god came from."],"name":"leo tolstoy","place":"yasnaya polyana, russia","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 100 40 128 60 130 C 80 128 94 100 90 60 C 82 84 38 84 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 96 C 50 106 70 106 76 96 M 40 110 C 48 118 72 118 80 110' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"everyone thinks of changing the world. no one thinks of changing himself.","where":"a line attributed to him everywhere. the idea is all through his late diaries"},{"q":"the kingdom of god is within you. it's not in a building.","where":"the kingdom of god is within you, 1894, paraphrased from luke 17:21, where jesus says it"},{"q":"if you want to be happy, be.","where":"attributed to him in a hundred places, likely a compression of his diaries"}],"start":"a confession, which is short. then the kingdom of god is within you. skip the novels unless you have a winter.","status":"approved","story":"count leo tolstoy had written the two biggest novels in the russian language by fifty. war and peace follows a handful of families through napoleon's invasion of russia. anna karenina is about a married woman's affair, and it ends with her under a train. and he was so depressed he hid the rope so he wouldn't hang himself. a confession, from around 1882, is him trying to explain why a man with everything wanted out.\nwhat pulled him back was the gospels, the four books in the bible that tell [[jesus]]'s life, stripped of everything the church had added. he wrote his own version that stitched the four into one, cut out the miracles, and kept the sermon on the mount, the part where jesus says don't resist evil with violence and love your enemies. he decided that was the whole thing and everything else was furniture.\nthe kingdom of god is within you, from 1894, is that argument at book length. jesus meant it, so no armies and no violence from anybody, for any reason, ever. the russian orthodox church, the country's official church, excommunicated him in 1901, which means it formally threw him out. a young indian lawyer in south africa read the book and said it overwhelmed him. they wrote to each other the last year of tolstoy's life, and the lawyer, [[gandhi]], named his commune after him. tolstoy also put together a little book of sayings by [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the ancient chinese sage behind the tao te ching, and wrote about [[buddha|the buddha]] with respect. he'd stopped caring which door the truth came through.\nat eighty two he finally walked out of his house in the night to go live as he preached, got as far as a little railway station at astapovo, and died there of pneumonia while reporters waited outside.","struggle":"in 1869, in a hotel room in arzamas, a small town east of moscow, he woke up in the night with a terror of death so complete that he described it forty years later like it had just happened. no reason, no illness. just you're going to die and none of this means anything.\nby fifty, with war and peace and anna karenina behind him, he was hiding the rope from himself and not going hunting alone with his gun. a confession is that. it's short and it's the most honest thing a famous man has ever written about wanting to quit.\nthen, having found his answer, he spent thirty years unable to live it. he preached poverty from a manor house. he preached chastity and kept getting his wife pregnant. he knew it, everyone knew it, and it ate him.","threads":["money","family","doubt"],"years":"1828 to 1910"},{"after":"the morning after the war she had two hundred dollars to show for it and a mortgage on the farm. she opened the door. her parents were there already, and her brothers' families, and then came the boarders, and orphans, and people who couldn't work, and anyone too old to earn, as the national park service, which keeps her house now, puts it. she raised pigs and sold pork by the thousand pounds and butter by the hundred, went out and told her story for money, and had bradford write it down twice. when the house burned in 1880 her family and friends built a brick one on the spot, and made some of the bricks there.\nin 1896, at seventy four, she bid twelve hundred and fifteen dollars at auction for the twenty five acres next door, to make a home for old and poor black people, and named one building john brown hall. she couldn't run it alone, so in 1903 she gave the land to her church, the ame zion church, a black methodist denomination, on condition that it open the place. it opened in june 1908, with a hundred dollar entrance fee she'd argued against and lost. in 1911 she moved in as a patient. she died there on march 10, 1913, and her last words were a line of [[jesus]]'s from the gospel of john, one of the four accounts of his life, where he tells his friends he is leaving: i go away to prepare a place for you. they buried her with semi-military honors. the pension never did call her a scout.","basically":"a woman born into slavery on maryland's eastern shore who walked out in 1849, about ninety miles alone at night, and then went back thirteen times to bring out around seventy people, most of them her own family. she couldn't read, a head injury from her teens dropped her into sleep without warning, and she said flat out that she didn't plan any of it, she asked god and he told her. in the civil war she went up a south carolina river with the union army and more than seven hundred people came off the plantations in one night. think of someone today who walks her whole extended family out of a war zone, one trip at a time, no papers and no phone, and tells the reporters afterward that it wasn't her.","born":1822,"died":1913,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 330 34 L 336 56 L 358 62 L 336 68 L 330 90 L 324 68 L 302 62 L 324 56 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 150 48 L 150 58 M 145 53 L 155 53 M 230 70 L 230 78 M 226 74 L 234 74 M 380 120 L 380 128 M 376 124 L 384 124 M 110 90 L 110 98 M 106 94 L 114 94' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 38 250 L 42 160 L 56 160 L 60 250 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 20 160 C 0 150 6 110 34 108 C 36 80 76 76 86 100 C 110 96 118 130 96 148 C 90 162 36 166 20 160 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 56 176 L 96 166'/><g opacity='.65'><circle cx='180' cy='178' r='11'/><rect x='160' y='186' width='16' height='16' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 180 189 L 180 224 M 180 224 L 170 250 M 180 224 L 190 250 M 180 200 L 196 208 M 180 200 L 166 196'/><circle cx='140' cy='184' r='10'/><path d='M 140 194 L 140 226 M 140 226 L 131 250 M 140 226 L 149 250 M 140 204 L 154 210 M 140 204 L 128 212'/><circle cx='106' cy='190' r='9'/><path d='M 106 199 L 106 228 M 106 228 L 98 250 M 106 228 L 114 250 M 106 208 L 118 214 M 106 208 L 96 216'/><\/g><circle cx='222' cy='200' r='8'/><circle cx='219' cy='199' r='1.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='225' cy='199' r='1.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 222 208 L 222 232 M 222 232 L 215 250 M 222 232 L 229 250 M 222 214 L 232 202 M 222 216 L 212 224'/><path d='M 236 196 L 264 196 L 272 244 L 228 244 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 250 172 L 250 198'/><path d='M 234 184 L 250 178 L 266 184 L 260 200 L 240 200 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><circle cx='250' cy='158' r='14'/><path d='M 237 152 Q 250 134 263 152 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><circle cx='246' cy='157' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='254' cy='157' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 246 164 L 254 164' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 250 186 L 270 200 M 250 186 L 232 202'/><path d='M 274 186 L 274 250'/><path d='M 242 244 L 238 250 M 258 244 L 262 250'/><g transform='translate(74,141) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"god told her where to go, she said, and in thirteen trips back into slave country she never lost anyone.","key":"tubman","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harriet_Tubman","why":"the plain overview of her life, with larson's dates"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raid_on_Combahee_Ferry","why":"the 1863 river raid, boat by boat"},{"label":"national park service","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/harriet-tubman-and-the-underground-railroad.htm","why":"the trips, the numbers, and the people who helped her"},{"label":"project gutenberg","url":"https://gutenberg.org/cache/epub/57821/pg57821-images.html","why":"bradford's 1869 book, the whole thing, free"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"the gun, mostly. bradford's second book says she carried a revolver on the road, and that when men gave out and wanted to turn back she put it to them and said, you go on or die. the line bradford prints is longer and has a slur in it. the reasoning was that a man who went back would be beaten until he gave up the route and everyone on it, and that a dead man tells no tales. the people she pointed it at were the people in her care, worn out and scared, and she was the one who'd walked them into that spot. no one i've read records her firing it. bradford is the only source, and bradford was building a saint, so the story reaches us as a point in her favor, which tells you something about the people around her as well.\nshe wasn't a pacifist about any of it. she prayed for brodess to die, and when he did she said she'd have given the world full of gold to bring that poor soul back. she found john brown his recruits, and bradford writes of her reverence for him. on the combahee she went up the river with a raid that burned the plantations and a rice mill as it went. none of this was hidden. she'd tell you herself, and she'd tell you the lord was in it.","moments":["september 17, 1849. she set out with her brothers ben and henry. the brothers lost their nerve and turned around, and she turned around with them. a few weeks later she went alone.","she told bradford she used to dream of flying over fields and towns and rivers and mountains, looking down on them like a bird, and that at the line there would be ladies all dressed in white who put out their arms and pulled her across.","june 1863, on the combahee. one woman brought two pigs, a white one and a black one, she said, and we took them all on board. the only thing that went wrong that night, by her account, was that her dress got torn when the crowd came up the side of the boat.","a white woman once asked her whether she really thought women ought to vote. i suffered enough to believe it, she said."],"name":"harriet tubman","place":"dorchester county, maryland, then philadelphia, st. catharines in canada, and auburn, new york","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 16 130 C 18 102 36 94 60 94 C 84 94 102 102 104 130 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 16 130 C 20 108 30 100 44 98 L 40 130 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 104 130 C 100 108 90 100 76 98 L 80 130 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 50 95 L 60 108 L 70 95 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 86 50 C 97 47 99 63 90 67' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 54 C 33 32 46 26 60 26 C 74 26 87 32 88 54 C 82 44 72 38 60 38 C 48 38 38 44 32 54 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 60 27 L 60 38' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 44 51 Q 50 48 55 50 M 65 50 Q 70 48 76 51' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 59 L 57 66 L 62 66'/><path d='M 53 75 Q 60 77 67 75'/><path d='M 102 12 L 104.5 20 L 112 22.5 L 104.5 25 L 102 33 L 99.5 25 L 92 22.5 L 99.5 20 Z' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='1.6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"when i found i had crossed that line, i looked at my hands to see if i was the same person. there was such a glory over everything.","where":"scenes in the life of harriet tubman, sarah bradford's 1869 book, pages 19 and 20. bradford wrote her words down in dialect spelling, made plain here"},{"q":"it wasn't me, it was the lord. i always told him, i trust to you. i don't know where to go or what to do, but i expect you to lead me. and he always did.","where":"to bradford, in scenes in the life of harriet tubman, 1869, page 35, spelling made plain"},{"q":"oh lord, if you aren't ever going to change that man's heart, kill him, lord, and take him out of the way.","where":"her prayer for edward brodess, as she told it to bradford. scenes in the life of harriet tubman, 1869, pages 14 and 15, spelling made plain"}],"start":"sarah bradford's scenes in the life of harriet tubman, 1869. it's short, it's free online, and it's the closest thing there is to her own voice, with two warnings: bradford wrote her speech in dialect, and the numbers are inflated. then kate clifford larson's bound for the promised land, from 2004, which is where the dates on this page come from.","status":"approved","story":"she was born araminta ross, called minty, on a plantation in dorchester county, on maryland's eastern shore, the flat farm and marsh country across the chesapeake bay from baltimore. the year is most likely 1822. the historian kate clifford larson found a payment to a midwife in march of that year, and that's the best record there is. harriet herself said 1825, and her gravestone says 1820. her mother, rit green, and her father, ben ross, were enslaved by different owners, and she was the fifth of their nine children. two of her older sisters were sold to traders and taken out of the state. at about six she was hired out to a man who was meant to teach her weaving and sent her into the marshes to check muskrat traps instead, and she got sick. then to a woman who whipped her, and after she tried to take a lump of sugar she ran and hid in a neighbor's pigpen for four days. she never learned to read. her mother told her the bible stories. the family was methodist, one of the protestant churches, and she had no use for the white preachers who told enslaved people to be meek. the stories she kept were the old ones about god getting people out, [[moses]] walking the hebrew slaves out of egypt among them.\nin her early teens, in the mid 1830s, she was hired to a neighboring farm, and one evening she went with the cook to the store. an enslaved man from another farm had walked off without leave and was chased in there. the overseer, the man who ran the field hands, told her to help hold him. she wouldn't. the man bolted, the overseer grabbed a two pound weight off the counter and threw it after him, and it hit her in the head. it broke my skull, she said later, and cut a piece of that shawl clean off and drove it into my head. nobody sent for a doctor. by her own account she lay sick from christmas until march, and then they put her back in the field. for the rest of her life she had headaches and spells where she dropped into sleep in the middle of whatever she was doing and couldn't be woken, though she sometimes knew what was going on around her. larson calls them epileptic seizures. and she had dreams and visions, which she took as god talking to her, a gift she said came down from her father. around 1844 she married john tubman, a free black man, and started going by harriet, which was her mother's name too.\nin march 1849 edward brodess, who owned her, died. he'd tried to sell her before, and the spells had put the buyers off. on september 17 she set off with her brothers ben and henry. they lost their nerve and turned around, and she went back with them. an ad went out offering a hundred dollars for each of them. a few weeks later, in october or november, she went alone, at night, steering by the north star, helped near home by a white woman and, most likely, by quakers along the way, a christian group that had turned against slavery early. nobody wrote the route down. it was about ninety miles, up through delaware into pennsylvania, where she was free. she said afterward that when she found she had crossed the line she looked at her hands to see if she was the same person, that there was such a glory over everything, and that she was a stranger in a strange land, and her home, after all, was down in maryland. she found work as a domestic in philadelphia and saved.\nthen she went back, on what people were calling the underground railroad, the loose chain of helpers and hiding places that moved runaways north. the first trip, in 1850, was for a niece and the niece's two children, who were about to be sold at auction. in 1851 she went for john tubman and found he had married another woman and wouldn't come. she went for her brothers, their wives, nieces and nephews, and in 1857 for her parents, both old, after her father was warned he'd be arrested for hiding runaways. the fugitive slave act of 1850 let owners have runaways arrested anywhere in the country, so the free states weren't safe any more and she took people all the way to st. catharines, a town in canada. the numbers got inflated early. sarah bradford, who wrote her life down in 1869, said nineteen trips and about three hundred people, and that stuck for a century. larson, who went through the records, counts about thirteen trips and about seventy people, nearly all of them family and friends from the same corner of maryland, plus directions and contacts for fifty or sixty more who made it out on their own. harriet herself, talking to audiences in 1858 and 1859, said fifty or sixty people in eight or nine trips. she carried a small pistol. near a hiding place she sang go down moses, or bound for the promised land, two of the spirituals, the songs enslaved black americans made, and changed the tempo to say whether it was safe to come out. people called her moses. thomas garrett, a quaker in delaware who passed runaways north, wrote that she talked with god, and he talked with her every day of her life. at a women's vote meeting in 1896 she said she never ran her train off the track and never lost a passenger.\nin 1859 she bought seven acres on the edge of auburn, new york, from frances seward, wife of william seward, the senator from new york who would be lincoln's secretary of state, for twelve hundred dollars with twenty five down. she was legally a runaway and the sale was illegal, and seward backed it. the year before she had met john brown, the white abolitionist planning an armed rising against slavery. he called her general tubman and she found him recruits in canada. when he made his raid at harpers ferry in october 1859 she was sick in massachusetts and had her doubts about his chances. when the civil war came, in 1861, she had a vision that it would end slavery. in january 1862 she went to port royal, on the south carolina coast, where the union army was, and nursed people who'd fled the plantations, and soldiers. by early 1863 she was running scouts. on the night of june 1, 1863, two union gunboats went up the combahee river with colonel james montgomery and a few hundred black soldiers, on her intelligence; larson says she guided them past the mines in the river. by morning the plantations along the banks were burning and more than seven hundred and fifty people had run down to the water and crowded onto the boats, women with pots of rice on their heads and babies hung round their necks. a boston paper said eight hundred. a charleston paper said six or seven hundred. the army's own reports have never been found. nearly all the able bodied men joined up. for three years of this she was paid two hundred dollars. she went home to auburn, married nelson davis in 1869, a veteran twenty two years younger who'd been boarding with her, and adopted a baby girl, gertie, with him in 1874. she fought the government for a pension for twenty five years, got eight dollars a month as a soldier's widow in the 1890s and twenty in 1899, for the nursing, and never a cent as a scout. she died in auburn, of pneumonia, on march 10, 1913, in the home she'd founded.","struggle":"the head came first and never left. the spells could take her anywhere. bradford tells of her dropping into a dead sleep in a new york office while she waited for a man to bring her money, and the maryland state archives has her collapsing on the road while bringing four people out, with one of the men talking the others out of leaving her there. the spells scared off buyers when brodess tried to sell her, which is the one good turn they ever did her. she carried that head for close to eighty years.\nthen the ones she couldn't get. in 1851 she walked back into maryland for her husband, and he had another wife and wouldn't come. the state archives calls it one of her few personal defeats, and says she threw herself into the rescues after that. in november 1860 she went for one of her sisters' children and couldn't raise the thirty dollar bribe it would have taken to get them out, and nobody knows what became of them. garrett's count was that she got out every relative she had but one sister and that sister's three children.\nand money, the whole way. three years with the army came to two hundred dollars. in october 1865 a train conductor forced her, violently, into the smoking car. in october 1873 swindlers with a story about gold knocked her out with chloroform, took her purse and left her a trunk full of rocks. in 1880 a boarder set the wooden house on fire by accident and it burned to the ground. bradford, in 1886, describes her with her little place about to be sold to satisfy a mortgage, and herself without the means to redeem it.","threads":["family","the body","war","money"],"years":"around 1822 to 1913"},{"after":"after the overdose he turned his life around, married, and went to school, all the way to a doctorate. after the churches told him to drop the drum, he picked it back up and brought it into their buildings.\nhe didn't end the anger by forgetting it. he turned it into an argument, a long polite one, with the churches, and kept it up until he died. his last book, rescuing the gospel from the cowboys, was finished by others and came out in 2015.","basically":"a sicangu lakota activist who hated christianity for what it had done to his people, found jesus anyway during an overdose on a hawaiian beach in 1974, and then spent forty years insisting you could follow jesus without giving up the drum, the dance, and being lakota. the churches that had told native converts their ceremonies were the devil's didn't love hearing it. think of the guy who gets saved and comes back to church and tells them their missionaries owe his grandmother an apology. take from him that you don't have to pick between where you came from and what saved you.","born":1954,"died":2013,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 230 C 60 220 120 240 200 230 S 340 222 400 232 L 400 300 L 0 300 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 0 214 L 400 214' opacity='.3'/><path d='M 320 214 C 316 170 318 130 328 100'/><path d='M 328 100 C 310 88 290 92 282 104 M 328 100 C 344 84 366 88 374 100 M 328 100 C 322 80 330 66 340 62 M 328 100 C 340 96 356 110 360 124' stroke='var(--sage)' stroke-width='5'/><circle cx='170' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 170 164 L 170 198 L 150 214 M 170 198 L 190 214 M 170 176 L 150 146 M 170 176 L 190 146'/><ellipse cx='80' cy='200' rx='26' ry='8' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 54 200 L 54 212 C 60 220 100 220 106 212 L 106 200'/><g transform='translate(318,85.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a lakota kid who'd helped take over the bureau of indian affairs overdosed on a beach in maui, yelled at jesus, and spent the rest of his life telling churches they'd done it wrong.","key":"twiss","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Twiss","why":"the plain overview of his life"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["he'd been inside the occupation of the bureau of indian affairs in 1972. forty years later he died in the same city.","he led christian worship in powwow regalia, dancing, which some of the churches he spoke at thought was the devil. he kept doing it."],"name":"richard twiss","place":"the rosebud reservation in south dakota, then maui, then the portland area in the pacific northwest","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 96 C 52 104 68 104 72 96' /><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 57 56 L 63 56 M 43 55 L 34 53 M 77 55 L 86 53' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"jesus, if you are real, would you forgive me, would you come into my life?","where":"his account of the night on maui in 1974, in interviews"},{"q":"god loves you, but he doesn't like you. no more drumming, no more powwows, no more ceremonies.","where":"his summary of what the missionaries had told native people, in interviews"},{"q":"you don't have to stop being lakota to follow jesus.","where":"the message of one church many tribes and his last book, paraphrased"}],"start":"rescuing the gospel from the cowboys, his last book, finished after he died. then one church many tribes.","status":"approved","story":"he was born in 1954 on the rosebud reservation in south dakota, among his mother's people, the sicangu lakota. sicangu means burnt thighs, a name that goes back, the story goes, to a band running through a prairie fire.\nin 1972, at eighteen, he was part of the american indian movement's trail of broken treaties, a caravan to washington, d.c., that ended with activists occupying the headquarters of the bureau of indian affairs, the federal agency that runs a lot of native life. he came out of it hating white people, he said later, and hating their religion.\nby 1974 he was a beach bum on maui, doing drugs, partying, sleeping on the sand and living on food stamps, by his own description. he overdosed. he said that at the bottom of it he yelled at the top of his lungs, jesus, if you're real, would you forgive me and come into my life, and felt more peace than he ever had.\nin 1976 he married katherine kroshus, and in 1997 they started wiconi international, a native christian ministry, wiconi being the lakota word for life. he led worship with a powwow drum and danced in full regalia, and wrote one church many tribes, about native christians keeping their cultures. he finished a doctorate at asbury theological seminary in 2011. on february 6, 2013, in washington, d.c., he had a heart attack, and he died three days later with his family around him.","struggle":"the anger first. he'd grown up on a reservation that was one of the poorest places in america and learned the history, and the only thing that made sense to him at eighteen was fury. then the drugs, and a beach, and an overdose, at twenty.\nthen the churches. after he converted, a lot of the christians around him told him his drum and his dances and his lakota ways were pagan and had to go. he spent years believing them before he decided they were wrong.","threads":["addiction","anger","forgiveness"],"years":"1954 to 2013"},{"after":"the morning after his father died, with the house full of creditors, he didn't get religion. he got a temper and a job hunt and a year of doubt, and at the end of it he walked into the temple to ask for money and couldn't. he took that as the answer, and it shaped everything. when ramakrishna died two years later and the boys had nothing, he didn't scatter them. he kept them in the haunted house, and then he walked away from them for five years, which looked like abandoning them and turned out to be the plan. the one who'd asked for proof spent the rest of his life on the part of religion that doesn't need any, feeding people. his phrase for it was daridra narayana, god as the poor man.\nthe fame didn't fix him and it didn't ruin him either. he came home from chicago in 1897 a celebrity and used it to buy land on the ganges for a monastery and a mission whose stated work was hospitals, schools, and famine relief, and that's what it still does. then he went west again, came back sick, handed the work to the others, and spent his last months at belur teaching the young monks and feeding the animals. on the last day he meditated, walked, taught a sanskrit class, and died that evening. no redemption story needed. he'd already done the thing.","basically":"a sharp calcutta college kid who walked into a temple in 1881 and asked the priest, [[ramakrishna]], have you seen god. the priest said yes, more clearly than i see you, and the kid spent the next twenty years turning that yes into monks and hospitals and the first hindu teaching in america. think of a young lawyer with a philosophy degree who meets a barely literate saint, can't shake him, ends up in a borrowed orange turban in chicago in 1893 telling a hall full of americans that every religion is true, and gets a two minute standing ovation for calling them sisters and brothers. yoga in america, and most of the idea that the traditions are talking about one thing, runs through him.","born":1863,"died":1902,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 118 C 60 112 120 116 180 114 C 230 112 300 116 400 112'/><path d='M 330 112 L 330 92 L 340 80 L 350 92 L 350 112'/><circle cx='70' cy='62' r='18'/><path d='M 120 250 C 110 224 128 206 150 200 C 190 190 240 188 268 196 C 290 202 296 226 284 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 150 214 C 170 210 200 208 230 210'/><circle cx='200' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 200 164 L 200 194'/><path d='M 200 194 C 184 194 170 200 168 204 L 232 204 C 230 200 216 194 200 194'/><path d='M 200 176 L 178 198 M 200 176 L 222 198'/><path d='M 0 236 C 20 228 40 244 60 236 S 100 228 118 236'/><path d='M 290 240 C 310 232 330 248 350 240 S 385 232 400 238'/><path d='M 0 270 C 24 262 48 278 72 270 S 120 262 144 270 S 192 278 216 270 S 264 262 288 270 S 336 278 360 270 S 390 264 400 268'/><path d='M 30 202 C 50 194 70 210 90 202'/><path d='M 320 196 C 340 188 360 204 380 196'/><path d='M 330 154 L 338 164 L 372 164 L 380 154 Z'/><path d='M 355 154 L 355 132'/><g transform='translate(250,169) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"the college kid who asked a priest if he'd seen god, and got told yes.","key":"vivekananda","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swami_Vivekananda","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"belur math","url":"https://belurmath.org/","why":"the monastery he built on the ganges, still the home of the ramakrishna mission"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parliament_of_the_World%27s_Religions","why":"the chicago gathering where he stood up"}],"lineage":"hindu","mess":"he smoked, cigars and a pipe, and he ate meat, and he said so in public in a country where a monk doing either was a scandal, and he defended it with a temper. the temper was real. his brother monks got scorched regularly, and so did his american hosts.\nhe said a lot of things, fast, to a lot of rooms, and they don't all agree. he praised islam in one place and said things about hindu greatness in another that hindu nationalists have been waving around ever since, and they didn't have to bend them much. he took money from rajas and rich american widows to build what he built. and he worked himself to death at thirty nine, which he'd have called a choice and the people who loved him called something else.","moments":["1881, in an english literature class at his college, the scottish principal was explaining a wordsworth poem about a trance and said if you want to know what that is, go see ramakrishna at dakshineswar. so he went.","august 1893, at a farmhouse in massachusetts, a harvard professor named john henry wright listened to him for a few hours and then wrote the parliament that asking this man for credentials was like asking the sun to state its right to shine, and bought him a train ticket to chicago.","1896, new york. nikola tesla, the inventor, met him at a party and got interested in the hindu ideas of prana and akasha, energy and matter, and told him he thought he could show they were the same thing mathematically. he couldn't, then. somebody else did a few years later.","december 1892, kanyakumari. no boat fare, so he swam out to the rock through water the fishermen warned him had sharks, sat there three days, and came back with a plan."],"name":"swami vivekananda","place":"calcutta, then all of india on foot, then america and england, then belur","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 16 130 C 18 102 36 92 60 92 C 84 92 102 102 104 130 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 30 116 C 44 106 76 106 92 118'/><path d='M 36 122 L 60 110 M 84 122 L 60 110'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 50 C 28 24 46 16 60 16 C 76 16 94 24 90 50 C 80 40 66 36 60 36 C 52 36 40 40 30 50 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 38 30 C 52 26 70 26 84 32'/><path d='M 34 44 C 30 36 24 34 20 40 C 22 46 28 48 34 44 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='50' cy='58' r='2.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='58' r='2.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 44 50 C 48 47 53 47 56 50 M 64 50 C 67 47 72 47 76 50'/><path d='M 52 72 L 68 72'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"each soul is potentially divine. the goal is to bring that out.","where":"raja yoga, 1896, the opening lines of the preface, paraphrased"},{"q":"arise, awake, and stop not till the goal is reached.","where":"his line, lifted from the katha upanishad, which he used in lectures and letters for the rest of his life"},{"q":"help and not fight. assimilation and not destruction. harmony and peace and not dissension.","where":"the last words of his closing address at the parliament of the world's religions, chicago, september 27, 1893"}],"start":"raja yoga, the first chapter, and then the chicago addresses, which are short and can be read in an hour.","status":"approved","story":"he was born narendranath datta on january 12, 1863, in calcutta, the son of a lawyer, in a house with money and books. he went to college, read the english philosophers, boxed, sang, and joined the brahmo samaj, a modern hindu reform group that had cut out the idols and the miracles. he wanted proof of god, not a story, and he went around asking religious men if they'd seen him. nobody said yes.\nin 1881 he went up the river to the kali temple at dakshineswar and asked [[ramakrishna]], the temple priest everybody said was either a saint or insane. ramakrishna said yes, i see him more clearly than i see you, and i can show you. narendra thought the man was mad, and kept coming back for five years. in 1884 his father died suddenly and the family went from comfortable to hungry. he asked ramakrishna to pray to kali for money for them. ramakrishna told him to go in and ask her himself. he went in three times and each time all he could ask for was knowledge and love of god. that's the story he told about how he lost the argument.\nramakrishna died of throat cancer in august 1886, in a rented house at cossipore, and left a dozen young men with no money and a promise to stay together. they moved into a broken down house at baranagar that was supposed to be haunted, took the vows of monks, and went hungry a lot. then narendra left and walked india for five years, mostly alone, carrying a water pot and a couple of books, sleeping wherever, seeing how poor the country actually was. at the end of 1892 he got to kanyakumari, the rock at the southern tip where three seas meet, swam out to it, sat for three days, and decided the poor needed food and schools before they needed philosophy, and that he'd go to america to get the money.\na raja in rajasthan, ajit singh of khetri, backed him and, the story goes, gave him the name vivekananda. his madras students raised the fare. he sailed in may 1893 and got to chicago for the parliament of the world's religions, a big gathering of religions held with the world's fair, and found out he was early and had no credentials. a harvard professor he'd met by chance, john henry wright, wrote a letter saying that asking this man for credentials was like asking the sun for its right to shine. he still lost the address in chicago, slept in a boxcar in a freight yard, and got taken in the next morning by a woman who saw him from her window. on september 11, 1893, he stood up and said sisters and brothers of america, and the hall applauded for minutes before he could go on. he closed the parliament two weeks later with help and not fight, assimilation and not destruction.\nhe stayed in the west almost four years. he lectured everywhere, got fleeced by a lecture bureau, started the vedanta society in new york in 1894, and dictated raja yoga, the first book that explained hindu meditation to americans in their own words. william james read it and quoted him in the varieties of religious experience. harvard offered him a chair in eastern philosophy in 1896 and he said no, he was a monk. he went home in january 1897 to crowds at every station, founded the ramakrishna mission that may, built belur math on the ganges as its home, and put an irish schoolteacher named margaret noble to work as sister nivedita running a school for girls. he went west once more, came home sick, and died at belur on july 4, 1902. he was thirty nine. he'd said he wouldn't see forty.","struggle":"1884. his father dropped dead and it turned out there was nothing left, and creditors. he was twenty one, the oldest son, walking calcutta barefoot looking for a job, lying to his mother about having eaten so his brothers and sisters could. he wrote later that he doubted god existed that year, and told ramakrishna so to his face. that's the year of the three trips into the temple, where he couldn't get the word money out of his mouth.\n1886 to 1893. ramakrishna dead, a dozen boys in a haunted house with no income, and then five years on foot across india with nothing, watching a whole country starve. he said afterward that he'd wept at what he saw. in chicago in the summer of 1893 he was a monk with no address in a city he didn't know, sleeping in a freight car, being turned away from doors because of his color and his clothes.\nthe body. diabetes and asthma, bad insomnia, by his mid thirties. he worked through all of it and he knew what it was doing. he told people he wouldn't live to forty, and he didn't.","threads":["wandering","doubt","work","money"],"years":"1863 to 1902"},{"after":"he admitted it, at least partly. in his later writing he said he was held responsible for ending a practice that went back thousands of years, and that he was afraid she was right about him. he also stood by publishing. he didn't give back what he'd taken, because there wasn't a way to.\nhe spent the rest of his life on scholarship, the soma book and the eleusis book, and died in connecticut in december 1986, a year after her. there's no redemption arc here. there's a man who said sorry in a book and meant it about halfway.","basically":"a wall street banker, head of public relations at j.p. morgan, who spent his vacations chasing the history of mushrooms. in 1955 he became one of the first outsiders to take part in a mazatec mushroom ceremony, and in 1957 he told the whole country about it in the biggest magazine in america. that article is where the sixties' magic mushroom craze starts. he meant it as scholarship. the woman who trusted him paid for it. think of the travel writer whose viral post about a hidden village ends with the village overrun.","born":1898,"died":1986,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 250 L 40 170 L 130 170 L 130 250' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 172 L 85 132 L 140 172'/><path d='M 72 250 L 72 200 L 98 200 L 98 250' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='230' cy='140' r='14'/><path d='M 230 154 L 230 202'/><path d='M 230 202 L 216 236 M 230 202 L 244 236'/><path d='M 230 168 L 244 192 M 230 168 L 204 162'/><rect x='190' y='160' width='20' height='14' rx='2' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='196' cy='167' r='4'/><path d='M 280 250 L 360 250 L 370 226 L 290 226 Z' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='300' y='232' width='24' height='12' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 180 162 L 140 196' stroke-dasharray='2 6' opacity='.6'/><g transform='translate(250,233.5) scale(1.1)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a j.p. morgan vice president ate the mushrooms with maría sabina, went home, and put her town in life magazine.","key":"wasson","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/R._Gordon_Wasson","why":"the plain overview of the life"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"this is his part in it, and it's not small. a woman who couldn't read, in a town with no road, let him into the most sacred thing she did because he asked, and he went home and published it in a magazine read by millions. the false name in life magazine didn't last the year. his own book named her and her town. then came the hippies, the rock tourists, the soldiers, the fire.\nand one of his trips, in 1956, was partly paid for in secret by the cia's mind control program, mkultra, through a front. the documents say he didn't know. he was a banker who liked mushrooms and he became the door the whole mess came through.","moments":["the life magazine story ran with a photo spread and made his name. it made hers a target.","in 1956 he brought back recordings of her chanting through a velada, and they came out as a record the next year.","in 1962 he went back up the mountain with [[hofmann|albert hofmann]], who brought her the psilocybin he'd made from her mushrooms in pill form."],"name":"gordon wasson","place":"montana, then new york, and the mountains of oaxaca","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='7' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 57 56 L 63 56 M 43 55 L 34 53 M 77 55 L 86 53' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><rect x='82' y='104' width='24' height='16' rx='2' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='94' cy='112' r='5'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"we were the first white men in recorded history to eat the divine mushrooms.","where":"seeking the magic mushroom, his life magazine article, may 13, 1957, paraphrased"},{"q":"i am held responsible for the end of a religious practice in mesoamerica that goes back for millennia.","where":"his later writing on maría sabina, in the 1970s, paraphrased"},{"q":"my only intention was to add to the sum of what people know.","where":"his defense of himself when asked about her, paraphrased"}],"start":"read maría sabina's own book first, the one she told to álvaro estrada. then, if you want his side, the life magazine article, seeking the magic mushroom, from may 1957.","status":"approved","story":"he grew up in montana, became a newspaper reporter, and ended up at j.p. morgan, one of the most powerful banks in the world, rising to vice president for public relations. in 1927 on his honeymoon in the catskills, the mountains north of new york city, his russian wife valentina, a children's doctor, ran into the woods and came back with an armful of wild mushrooms to cook. he was horrified. she was delighted. the two of them spent the next thirty years studying why some cultures love mushrooms and some fear them.\nthat trail took him to mexico. in june 1955, in huautla de jiménez, a town high in the mountains of oaxaca, [[sabina|maría sabina]], a mazatec healer, let him and a photographer friend, allan richardson, sit through one of her all-night healing ceremonies, the velada, and eat the mushrooms. he wrote that it was the first time white men in recorded history had done it.\non may 13, 1957, life magazine, the giant weekly picture magazine that was in half the living rooms in america, ran his story, seeking the magic mushroom, with photographs. he gave her a false name in the magazine. the same year, in a two volume book called mushrooms, russia and history, which he and valentina printed in a small expensive edition, he used her real name and the name of her town. after that, anyone who wanted to could find her.\nhe kept at it. soma, from 1968, argued that the holy drink in the rig veda, the oldest hindu scriptures, was made from a red and white mushroom, fly agaric. the road to eleusis, from 1978, with the swiss chemist [[hofmann|albert hofmann]] and a classics professor, carl ruck, argued that an ancient greek religious festival used a drink made with ergot, a fungus. scholars still argue about both.","struggle":"valentina died of cancer in 1958, the year after the life article. the whole mushroom project had been the two of them, and he carried on alone.\nby the seventies he knew what had happened in huautla. the hippies, the army checkpoints, a burned house, a woman blamed by her own town. he wrote about it with regret, and he also kept saying knowledge was worth it. both of those lived in him at once until he died.","threads":["psychedelics","fame"],"years":"1898 to 1986"},{"after":"there isn't a redemptive after. he kept teaching, kept drinking, and died in his sleep at fifty eight in a cabin on mount tamalpais, just north of san francisco, in november 1973, with the talks still pouring out.\nwhat he leaves is the honesty of the gap, which is its own kind of teaching. he said the ego, the separate little me we all think we are, is a hoax, and he couldn't get out from under his. both true. the talks still land because he never pretended the second part wasn't.","basically":"an englishman with a great voice who moved to california and explained zen and taoism, the big eastern traditions about letting go, to americans on the radio in the fifties and sixties, and made it sound like the best joke you'd ever heard. his old lectures are all over youtube now, cut up and set to ocean footage. he taught letting go better than almost anybody and couldn't let go of the bottle, and it killed him at fifty eight. listen to him for the teaching, and keep the second part in mind while you do.","born":1915,"died":1973,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 300 L 0 230 C 60 226 120 200 170 150 C 210 110 250 90 290 96 C 320 100 330 130 300 140 C 280 146 270 128 288 118 C 300 112 316 124 306 134 L 400 240 L 400 300 Z' fill='var(--blue)'/><path d='M 20 262 q 12 -6 24 0 q 12 -6 24 0 M 60 282 q 10 -6 20 0' stroke-width='2' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 128 212 L 206 160' stroke-width='6'/><path d='M 122 216 L 212 156' stroke-width='2' opacity='.5'/><circle cx='176' cy='110' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='173' cy='108' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='183' cy='108' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 174 114 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 176 124 L 172 160'/><path d='M 172 160 L 150 196 M 172 160 L 198 172'/><path d='M 174 136 L 140 120 M 174 136 L 212 128'/><path d='M 30 256 l 0 -16 l 4 -6 l 0 -8 l 8 0 l 0 8 l 4 6 l 0 16 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><g transform='translate(282,69.0) scale(0.85)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"an episcopal priest who quit, became the voice of zen on the radio, called himself a spiritual entertainer, and drank himself to death at fifty eight.","key":"watts","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alan_Watts","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Way_of_Zen","why":"his 1957 book on zen"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"alcoholic, badly, for his last decade. three marriages, seven children, most of whom he left. sloppy and sad, and he knew it.","moments":["he published his first book, the spirit of zen, at twenty one, in 1936, in london, having never been to japan.","he lived for years on a converted ferryboat, the vallejo, moored in sausalito, the houseboat town across the bay from san francisco, with a rotating cast of artists and drunks and a view of the water. the book was mostly written there.","his 1959 series on kqed, san francisco's public tv station, called eastern wisdom and modern life, put a smiling englishman explaining the tao into american living rooms nearly a decade before the beatles went to india and made it fashionable."],"name":"alan watts","place":"england, then san francisco","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 80 C 52 92 68 92 70 80 C 66 84 54 84 50 80 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 50 C 36 36 50 32 60 34 C 70 32 84 36 86 50 C 80 46 74 44 70 46 C 68 40 52 40 50 46 C 46 44 40 46 34 50 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"you didn't come into this world. you came out of it, like a wave from the ocean.","where":"the book, his 1966 book about the self, paraphrased"},{"q":"trying to define yourself is like trying to bite your own teeth.","where":"a line he used in talks"},{"q":"a myth is an image in terms of which we try to make sense of the world.","where":"the way of zen, his 1957 history of zen"}],"start":"the book. then any of his talks, which are all over the internet and better than the books. the way of zen if you want the history.","status":"approved","story":"an english kid who read every buddhist book in london by his teens, moved to america in 1938, became an episcopal priest for about five years, and left the church in 1950. episcopalians are the american branch of the church of england. he left over his marriage falling apart and his growing sense that he didn't believe the furniture anymore, the creeds and the rules the church had built around the thing itself.\nhe landed in san francisco and became the guy who explained zen and taoism to america in plain english. zen is the branch of buddhism built around sitting still and noticing right now. taoism is the old chinese way of going with the grain of things instead of forcing them, from [[laotzu|lao tzu]]. he did it on kpfa, a listener-funded radio station in berkeley, in lectures, and in the way of zen, his 1957 history of it. he had a voice like a friendly uncle and a laugh you could hear from the next room. the serious zen teachers and scholars were not amused. he called himself a spiritual entertainer, which was honest.\nhe ran with the psychedelic crowd. the joyous cosmology, from 1962, is his book about lsd, and timothy leary and richard alpert, the two harvard psychologists running drug experiments, alpert later [[ramdass|ram dass]], wrote the foreword. the book, from 1966, is his best. its full title is the book on the taboo against knowing who you are, and the thing we're not supposed to know is that you're not a separate ego in a bag of skin. you're the whole thing, pretending.\nhe was a wonderful teacher of not gripping who couldn't stop gripping a bottle. he died in his sleep in 1973 in a cabin on mount tamalpais, the big mountain just north of san francisco.","struggle":"he was a beautiful explainer of letting go and couldn't. the drinking got serious in his fifties, a bottle of vodka a day by the end according to people who loved him. he was supporting two families on lectures and getting on planes hungover to give talks about the present moment.\nin his autobiography, in my own way, from 1972, he's frank about the gap. he said he was a spiritual entertainer and not a saint and never claimed to be. that's true and it's also the sound of a man letting himself off the hook.","threads":["addiction","fame","family"],"years":"1915 to 1973"},{"after":"after he got fired he got another government job within days, in the attorney general's office, and kept going to the hospitals. after the stroke he moved into his brother's house in camden and later bought a small house on mickle street, and people came from england and all over to sit with the old man in the cluttered front room.\nhe never stopped working on the one book. there were several editions in his lifetime. he put the last one together in his final months, in 1891 and 1892, and it's the one called the deathbed edition. the book grew from twelve poems to more than four hundred.","basically":"a brooklyn newspaperman who paid to print a book of poems in 1855 that sounded like nothing before it, loose and huge and sexy and in love with everybody, and then wrote rave reviews of it himself under no name. then during the civil war he spent about three years sitting with wounded and dying soldiers in the army hospitals in washington. he's the grandfather of every poet who writes the way people talk, and of everybody who ever made a fake account to hype their own work. take from him that you can be full of contradictions and still show up for people every single day.","born":1819,"died":1892,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--lavender)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 250 C 60 247 120 253 200 250 S 340 248 400 250'/><path d='M 20.0 250 l -4.8 -19.2'/><path d='M 30.799999999999997 250 l 0.0 -19.2'/><path d='M 41.599999999999994 250 l 4.8 -19.2'/><path d='M 52.4 250 l 9.6 -19.2'/><path d='M 220.0 250 l -4.4 -17.6'/><path d='M 229.9 250 l 0.0 -17.6'/><path d='M 239.8 250 l 4.4 -17.6'/><path d='M 360.0 250 l -5.2 -20.8'/><path d='M 371.7 250 l 0.0 -20.8'/><path d='M 383.4 250 l 5.2 -20.8'/><path d='M 395.1 250 l 10.4 -20.8'/><circle cx='96' cy='234' r='15' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='91' cy='232' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='101' cy='232' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 92 238 q 4 4 8 0' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 84 242 q 12 16 26 0' fill='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><path d='M 111 238 L 200 236' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 200 236 L 246 222 L 282 250 M 200 236 L 262 248' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 128 238 L 158 178' stroke-width='3'/><path d='M 132 240 L 176 248' stroke-width='2.4'/><ellipse cx='152' cy='172' rx='7' ry='4' fill='var(--marigold)'/><ellipse cx='164' cy='172' rx='7' ry='4' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 158 166 l 0 12 M 158 166 l -4 -6 M 158 166 l 4 -6' stroke-width='1.6'/><path d='M 311.8 232 C 314.4 252.8 345.6 252.8 348.2 232 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='322' cy='228' r='5' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='334' cy='226' r='5' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='344' cy='230' r='5' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='1.6'/><circle cx='328' cy='236' r='5' fill='var(--marigold)' stroke-width='1.6'/><g transform='translate(372,227.1) scale(0.85)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he published his own book, reviewed it himself, and then spent the war holding the hands of dying boys.","key":"whitman","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Walt_Whitman","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"poetry foundation","url":"https://www.poetryfoundation.org/poets/walt-whitman","why":"a life and a lot of the poems"},{"label":"the book, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1322","why":"leaves of grass at project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"other","mess":"when leaves of grass came out he wrote at least three glowing reviews of it himself and got them printed without his name on them. that's pretty funny. less funny, in 1890 the english writer john addington symonds, who was gay himself, asked him straight out whether the poems about love between comrades meant what they seemed to mean, and whitman wrote back that he had six illegitimate children. there's no evidence any of them existed.\nsome of what he wrote about black americans is ugly. he didn't think free black men should vote, and he compared black voters to baboons. the poems are bigger than the man was on some days.","moments":["when lincoln was shot in april 1865 whitman wrote when lilacs last in the dooryard bloom'd, a long poem of grief, and o captain! my captain!, the one schoolkids still recite. he'd watched lincoln ride past in the streets of washington and by whitman's account they'd nodded to each other.","there's a photograph of him as an old man with a butterfly perched on his finger. the butterfly was cardboard. it turned up in his papers in the library of congress.","the last lines of song of myself tell you that if you want him again, look for him under your boot soles."],"name":"walt whitman","place":"long island, brooklyn, the army hospitals in washington, camden, new jersey","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 100 40 128 60 130 C 80 128 94 100 90 60 C 82 84 38 84 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 44 96 C 50 106 70 106 76 96 M 40 110 C 48 118 72 118 80 110' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 50 C 30 60 30 66 34 70 L 36 52 Z M 88 50 C 90 60 90 66 86 70 L 84 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 42 48 C 46 44 52 44 56 48 M 64 48 C 68 44 74 44 78 48' stroke-width='4'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 52 74 C 56 79 64 79 68 74'/><path d='M 22 44 C 30 38 90 38 98 44 C 90 48 30 48 22 44 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 38 42 C 38 20 82 20 82 42 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"do i contradict myself? very well then i contradict myself.","where":"song of myself, the long poem at the heart of leaves of grass, section 51; the next line is the one about multitudes"},{"q":"i am large, i contain multitudes.","where":"song of myself, section 51"},{"q":"go over everything you've been told at school or church or in any book, and throw out whatever insults your own soul.","where":"the preface to the 1855 leaves of grass, paraphrased"}],"start":"song of myself, sections 1, 6 and 52. then specimen days, his prose memoir, for the war hospitals.","status":"approved","story":"he was born on long island in 1819 and grew up in brooklyn. he left school at eleven and worked as a printer's boy, a teacher, a newspaperman, and an editor. at thirty six he paid to print a thin book of poems with no author's name on the title page, just a picture of himself in work clothes and a jaunty hat. leaves of grass, july 1855.\nalmost nobody noticed. he sent a copy to ralph waldo emerson, the most respected writer in america, who wrote back that it was the most extraordinary piece of wit and wisdom america had yet contributed, and greeted him at the beginning of a great career. whitman printed the letter in the next edition without asking.\nin december 1862 his brother george was wounded at fredericksburg, virginia, one of the worst union defeats of the civil war, and walt went south to find him. george was fine. walt stayed in washington for the rest of the war, apart from about six months sick at home in brooklyn in 1864, and went to the army hospitals most days, sitting with wounded and dying soldiers, bringing oranges and tobacco and paper, and writing letters home for the men who couldn't. by his own count he saw tens of thousands of them.\nhe was gay or bisexual, most biographers say, as surely as anyone can say that about a man in the nineteenth century. his great love was most likely peter doyle, a young streetcar conductor he met in washington in 1865. he had a stroke in 1873, moved to camden, new jersey, and kept revising the same book until he died there in 1892.","struggle":"in 1865 he had a clerk's job at the department of the interior. the new secretary, james harlan, fired him, most likely because he'd come across whitman's copy of leaves of grass and was offended by it. a friend wrote an angry pamphlet calling him the good gray poet, and the name stuck.\nthe hospitals wore him down. most days he'd sit with the wounded for four or five hours, and then go back and do it again. his health went in those years and never came back. the stroke in 1873 left him partly paralyzed, and his mother died that may. he called it the great dark cloud of his life.","threads":["war","dying","love"],"years":"1819 to 1892"},{"after":"after wounded knee he didn't go on tour, didn't start a church, and didn't denounce the lakota or the dance. he went on living in mason valley and yerington, doing ranch work, sometimes working as a healer for people who asked, and receiving the visitors who kept coming to see him for years.\nhe lived another forty years. he died in yerington on september 20, 1932, and was buried at schurz, on the walker river paiute reservation. the round dance he taught still gets danced. the world didn't end. he'd have told you that was never the point.","basically":"a northern paiute man in nevada who worked on a white family's ranch, took their name, jack wilson, and heard their bible stories at the table. on new year's day 1889, during a solar eclipse, he had a vision. work, don't lie, don't fight, dance the round dance, and the dead will come back and the world will be made new. the ghost dance spread across the west like wildfire, the lakota heard it their own way, and the government's panic over it ended in the massacre at wounded knee. think of someone whose message of peace goes viral and gets turned into something he never said, with a body count. take from him how little control you have over a thing once it leaves your hands.","born":1856,"died":1932,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><circle cx='300' cy='70' r='30' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='300' cy='70' r='38' stroke-dasharray='2 6'/><g transform='translate(90,150) scale(.55)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -26 30 M 0 28 L 26 30'/><\/g><g transform='translate(125,140) scale(.55)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -26 30 M 0 28 L 26 30'/><\/g><g transform='translate(160,146) scale(.55)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -26 30 M 0 28 L 26 30'/><\/g><g transform='translate(195,140) scale(.55)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -26 30 M 0 28 L 26 30'/><\/g><g transform='translate(230,150) scale(.55)'><circle cx='0' cy='0' r='14'/><path d='M 0 14 L 0 62'/><path d='M 0 62 L -14 96 M 0 62 L 14 96'/><path d='M 0 28 L -26 30 M 0 28 L 26 30'/><\/g><path d='M 330 250 C 322 236 320 226 330 220 C 336 226 346 224 346 234 C 352 236 352 246 344 250 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><g transform='translate(338,207.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a paiute ranch hand saw god during an eclipse and told everyone to dance and be kind, and the army heard war.","key":"wovoka","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wovoka","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ghost_Dance","why":"the movement he started, and what happened to it"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"he let people believe he could control the weather, and his neighbors mostly did believe it. it was the job his father had too.","moments":["the story goes that he could make ice fall out of a clear summer sky, and that he ended a drought. his authority among the paiute rested on it as much as on the vision.","twenty years before him, in 1869, another paiute holy man named wodziwob had a similar vision and started a dance, and wovoka's father helped spread it. the 1889 dance was the second one.","in january 1892 james mooney, the smithsonian researcher, came out to nevada to find him, and got an interview. that report is still the main account in his own words."],"name":"wovoka","place":"smith valley and mason valley, near yerington, nevada","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 50 96 L 60 112 L 70 96 M 60 112 L 60 130' /><path d='M 56 100 L 60 108 L 64 100 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 30 86 30 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 18 42 C 28 35 92 35 102 42 C 92 47 28 47 18 42 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 38 40 C 38 16 82 16 82 40 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"do not tell lies. do not hurt anybody. do right always.","where":"the messiah letter, the message he gave to cheyenne and arapaho visitors in 1891, written down by one of them and printed by james mooney, a smithsonian researcher, paraphrased"},{"q":"when the earth shakes, do not be afraid. it will not hurt you.","where":"the messiah letter, 1891, paraphrased"},{"q":"i went up to the other world and saw god, and all the people who had died long ago, happy and young.","where":"his account of the vision to james mooney, paraphrased"}],"start":"james mooney's the ghost-dance religion and the sioux outbreak of 1890, the smithsonian report from 1896, the chapters on wovoka. then michael hittman's wovoka and the ghost dance.","status":"approved","story":"he was born around 1856 in smith valley, nevada, into the northern paiute, a people of the great basin, the dry country of lakes and sagebrush between the rockies and the sierra. his father was a weather doctor, a man people believed could bring rain.\nfrom about eight years old until nearly thirty he worked for a white rancher near yerington named david wilson. the family called him jack wilson, and he heard their bible stories and prayers. he knew both worlds before he was grown.\nhe got sick with a fever, and on january 1, 1889, the day of a total eclipse of the sun over nevada, he fell into something like a coma. when he came out of it he said he'd been taken up to heaven and seen god, and the dead living happily there. he was told to tell the people to love one another, not to fight, not to lie or steal, to work, and to dance a traditional round dance. if they did, the dead would come back and the earth would be made new. he never left nevada to spread it. people came to him.\nthey came by train from nations all over the west. the lakota sent men, among them kicking bear and short bull, in 1889 and 1890. back home the message grew harder edges. dancers wore ghost shirts they believed would stop bullets, and some said the whites would be swept away. the government saw an uprising coming. on december 15, 1890, police killed sitting bull while arresting him, partly over the dance, and on december 29 the army killed hundreds of lakota at wounded knee creek. he hadn't wanted any of it.","struggle":"wounded knee gutted him. he'd preached a dance and a way of living that said don't hurt anybody, and it came back to him as a mass grave in the snow in south dakota. and people who'd believed in him had to reckon with what the dance had led to.\nand the dead didn't come back. the new world he'd promised didn't arrive in 1890 or 1891 or ever. he had to go on living in the old one, in the same valley, as the man whose prophecy hadn't happened.","threads":["nonviolence","grief","fame"],"years":"around 1856 to 1932"},{"after":"the montreal year didn't end his work. it fed it. six years later, putting together a course on jesus, he found that the things he'd understood most deeply about jesus and the crucifixion were all dated from the depression. he had gone home to oxford in 1986, and the first volume of the big series came out in 1992. if today i manage to function as a pastor, he wrote, it is not least because i know something about pain.\nthe arguments he answers, and then he keeps going. i can't find that he has taken anything back on marriage or gender, or that piper has on paul. but in 2008, with piper's book against him a few months old, he called piper a good, beloved brother in christ and said he would just prefer that he read paul differently. and in 2025 a listener who had transitioned ten years earlier asked on his podcast whether living as a man might be a sin. he said the whole conversation is very new, that the old manuals of theology didn't prepare anyone for it, and that he had no experience of it as a pastor himself. there's no retraction in that, and no victory lap. he's in his late seventies and still taking questions.","basically":"an english historian of the first century who was also a bishop, in his late seventies now, with more than seventy books behind him. his big claim is that most christians have the ending wrong: the first ones weren't hoping to leave for heaven, they were waiting for god to come and repair this world, bodies included, and they believed the repair started the morning [[jesus]] walked out of the tomb. it's like finding out the company was never going to transfer you somewhere nicer. the owner is coming back to fix the building, and you're on the crew.","born":1948,"died":null,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--peach)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 40 250 C 40 180 150 180 150 250 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 74 250 L 74 224 C 74 206 108 206 108 224 L 108 250 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='136' cy='231' r='19' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 282 62 C 272 46 296 36 306 48 C 316 32 346 38 344 56 C 362 56 362 80 344 80 L 290 80 C 272 82 268 66 282 62 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 296 92 L 292 134 M 316 92 L 316 146 M 336 92 L 340 134' stroke-dasharray='2 8' opacity='.6'/><circle cx='226' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 226 164 L 226 212'/><path d='M 226 212 L 212 250 M 226 212 L 240 250'/><path d='M 226 180 L 250 190 M 226 186 L 248 208'/><path d='M 221 155 Q 226 159 231 155'/><path d='M 254 172 L 244 236'/><path d='M 237 234 L 251 238 L 247 250 L 235 247 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 300 250 L 300 222'/><circle cx='300' cy='212' r='12' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 284 250 Q 300 238 316 250'/><g transform='translate(83,170.5) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"a bishop and historian who keeps telling christians that going to heaven when you die was never the point.","key":"wright","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/N._T._Wright","why":"the plain overview of his life and books"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Perspective_on_Paul","why":"the argument about paul he's in the middle of"},{"label":"time magazine","url":"https://time.com/archive/6942509/christians-wrong-about-heaven-says-bishop/","why":"his 2008 interview on heaven, short and clear"}],"lineage":"christian","mess":"his part in it is argument, and he gets it from both directions. the justification fight first. in 2007 john piper, a baptist pastor in minneapolis, published a whole book against him, the future of justification: a response to n.t. wright. for piper the good news is that christ's perfect record gets counted as yours through faith alone, and wright, by making justification mostly about who is in god's people, blurs the one thing a guilty person needs to hear. piper sent him the draft first, and wright sent back eleven thousand words. wright's public answer was a book of his own in 2009, justification: god's plan and paul's vision. neither man moved.\nthen sex and gender, where he holds the traditional line and says it sharply. he sat on the commission that wrote the windsor report in 2004, set up after the episcopal church chose gene robinson, an openly gay priest, as a bishop. the report asked for a halt to more such bishops and to church blessings of same sex couples. in december 2005, the month england's first civil partnerships, legal unions for same sex couples, took place, he wrote to his clergy that they must not hold blessing services and that he'd be very sorry if they forced him to make disciplinary enquiries. a vicar in his diocese went ahead that same month with his partner of nineteen years, and told the bbc it was about time the state recognised couples like them. we're all in new territory, wright said afterward. in 2014 he argued that the word marriage has meant man and woman for thousands of years, and that when governments suddenly change the meaning of key words you really should watch out, and his examples were nazi germany and soviet russia. in august 2017 he wrote to the times of london that confusion about gender identity is a modern, and now internet-fuelled, form of the ancient philosophy of gnosticism, an old family of movements that treated the physical world as a mistake, and warned that vulnerable young people would pay for it. candida moss, a historian of early christianity, answered in the daily beast that this was cheap slander, and quoted specialists who said the gnostics were a mixed bunch whose ideas about gender don't line up with ours. conservatives have had their turn too. in 2025 he said on his podcast that abortion is wrong, but that after rape or incest the best thing is to end the pregnancy as soon as possible, and a writer in the magazine first things said he'd handled a grave question carelessly.","moments":["in 2001 he described his mornings. very early, in the study armchair, with a pot of tea and three books: the hebrew old testament for a psalm, the old greek translation of it for a chapter, and the greek new testament for a paragraph, more or less.","by his own account he sang and played guitar in a folk club in vancouver in the late 1960s.","on november 23, 2012, he argued in the times for women bishops, and said the reason was the bible, not progress. the first person sent to tell anyone that jesus had risen, he pointed out, was [[magdalene|mary magdalene]].","he spent 2003 to 2010 as one of the five bishops who sit in the house of lords automatically. when he left durham he said he had loved the people, the place, the heritage and the work."],"name":"n.t. wright","place":"the north of england, then oxford, montreal, durham and st andrews","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 18 130 C 20 104 38 96 60 96 C 82 96 100 104 102 130 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 97 M 68 84 L 68 97'/><path d='M 46 95 Q 60 104 74 95 L 74 102 Q 60 111 46 102 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 34 45 C 25 48 24 61 29 69 C 30 62 32 56 35 51 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 86 45 C 95 48 96 61 91 69 C 90 62 88 56 85 51 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 35 66 C 37 86 49 93 60 93 C 71 93 83 86 85 66 C 79 72 70 73 60 73 C 50 73 41 72 35 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 46 80 L 47 85 M 60 84 L 60 89 M 74 80 L 73 85' stroke-width='1.6' opacity='.5'/><path d='M 45 49 Q 49.5 46 54 48 M 66 48 Q 70.5 46 75 49' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 59 L 58 66 L 62 66'/><path d='M 53 78 C 57 81 63 81 67 78'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"our culture is very interested in life after death, but the new testament is much more interested in what i've called the life after life after death.","where":"interview in time magazine, february 7, 2008"},{"q":"if today i manage to function as a pastor, it is not least because i know something about pain.","where":"my pilgrimage in theology, a short essay in the journal themelios, january 1993"},{"q":"what we are doing at the moment is building for the kingdom.","where":"to a reporter for religion news service, 2012, explaining why what people do now matters"}],"start":"surprised by hope, for the heaven argument in plain words. simply christian if you want his whole case for the faith. paul: a biography for paul as a person. the resurrection of the son of god only if you want all eight hundred pages.","status":"approved","story":"nicholas thomas wright, tom to everyone, was born on december 1, 1948, in morpeth, a market town in northumberland, in the north of england. he grew up in what he calls a middle-anglican home, meaning plain church of england, and says he was about seven when he began to realise he was called to be ordained, to be made a priest. he went to sedbergh, a boarding school in the hills of northwest england, and then to oxford, where he took a first, the top grade, in classics in 1971 and trained for the priesthood at wycliffe hall. he was ordained in 1975 and finished a doctorate in 1981 on [[paul]]'s letter to the romans.\nthe jobs zigzag between the library and the pulpit. a junior fellow and chaplain at merton college, oxford, in 1975. chaplain at a college in cambridge from 1978 to 1981. five years teaching the new testament at mcgill university in montreal, 1981 to 1986. back to oxford as a college chaplain and tutor until 1993. then the church took him. he was dean of lichfield, the priest who runs the cathedral there, from 1994 to 1999, then canon theologian, one of the senior priests, at westminster abbey, the london church where kings and queens are crowned, from 2000 to 2003, and then bishop of durham from 2003 to 2010, a job that comes with a seat in the house of lords, the upper house of britain's parliament. in 2010 he went back to teaching, at the university of st andrews in scotland, and since october 2019 he's been a senior research fellow at wycliffe hall, where he started.\nhis field is the first century, and especially second temple judaism, which is what scholars call the jewish world that jesus and paul lived in, before the romans destroyed the temple in jerusalem in the year 70. all the way through he's been writing a history of how christianity began, a series called christian origins and the question of god. the third volume, the resurrection of the son of god, from 2003, runs past eight hundred pages. it goes through what greeks, romans and jews believed happened after death, and argues that when a jew of that time said resurrection he meant a body, a person physically alive again, not a soul floating free. his conclusion is that the empty tomb and the meetings with jesus afterward should be read as two things that actually happened, and that no historian has come up with a better explanation for why the first christians talked the way they did.\nsurprised by hope, his 2007 book for ordinary readers, draws the consequence. when you die, he says, you're with god, resting, something like being asleep. but that's a stop on the way. what the new testament cares about is what he calls life after life after death, the day the dead are raised into a remade world, the new heavens and the new earth. new creation is his short name for it. christ comes here, he told time magazine in 2008, to join the heavens and the earth together. he blames the escape version on greek speaking christians who had soaked up plato, the athenian philosopher who taught that behind the world we can touch there's a truer, unseen one. he reads the kingdom of god the same way. in how god became king, from 2012, he says the four gospels are the story of god becoming king on earth as in heaven, through jesus, and that the creeds jump from his birth straight to his death and skip everything in between. this is how he sums up what the gospels and paul say about easter: jesus is raised, therefore the new creation has begun, and we have a job to do.\nthe other half of his work is paul. in 1977 an american scholar, e.p. sanders, published paul and palestinian judaism, which argued that the jews of paul's day were not trying to earn their way to god. they believed they were already in, through the covenant, god's standing agreement with abraham's family, and they kept the law of [[moses]] to stay in. a year later wright gave a lecture offering what he called a new way of looking at paul. the british scholar james dunn named the whole movement the new perspective on paul in a lecture in 1982, and the name stuck. wright's version says that justification, paul's word for god declaring someone in the right, is mostly about who belongs to god's people. his answer is anyone who trusts the messiah, jew or gentile, and the old badges like circumcision, the mark on the body that showed a man was a jew, don't settle it. the older protestant reading, the one that goes back to martin luther, the german monk who split the western church in the 1500s, says paul was telling guilty individuals how to get right with god. wright says the church has been on the wrong foot about paul since [[augustine]].","struggle":"the one dark stretch he has put in print is montreal. he got to mcgill in 1981, in his early thirties, with a new doctorate and a teaching job. during my second year at mcgill, he wrote later, i plunged into the deepest depression i've ever known. he says he wrestled in prayer, searched the scriptures, examined his conscience, and fell apart.\none evening he told his wife. the next morning a letter arrived from a christian psychotherapist who, in wright's words, had felt an inexplicable but irresistible urge to write. i still have that letter, he says. over the following year he learned a great deal about himself and his own emotions. he told all this in a few sentences in a 1993 essay. he doesn't name a cause, and nobody else should guess one for him.\nthe other hard one he has named came in 2010. he had been bishop of durham for seven years and was trying to write books at the same time. on april 27 he announced he was leaving for a university job. this has been the hardest decision of my life, he said. he was sixty one.","threads":["dying","the body","work"],"years":"born 1948"},{"after":"his answer to huizi about the pot was that at first he grieved like anybody. then he looked back to before she was born, before she had a body, before she even had breath, and saw that she'd changed into life and now changed out of it, the way the seasons go round. she's asleep in the big room now, he said, meaning the whole world, and if i sat here bawling it would mean i didn't understand. so i stopped.\nand when huizi died, he walked past his grave and told a story. a plasterer got a speck of mud on the tip of his nose and a carpenter named shi took it off with a whirling axe, without touching skin. shi said he could only do it with that one plasterer. since huizi died, zhuangzi said, i've had no one to work with. no one to talk to. the man who drummed at his wife's funeral missed his friend.","basically":"a chinese thinker from about twenty three hundred years ago who wrote the funniest serious book there is, full of talking trees, happy fish, a cook who never sharpens his knife, and a man who turns down a kingdom because he'd rather be a turtle in the mud. he's the patron saint of everyone who ever turned down the promotion and never regretted it. take from him that being useless to the machine might be how you get to have a life.","born":-369,"died":-286,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--sage)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 86 250 C 98 214 70 196 94 164 C 108 146 84 128 100 104 M 112 250 C 118 220 100 200 118 172 C 128 156 112 134 120 110'/><path d='M 104 132 C 128 126 146 130 168 120'/><path d='M 40 100 C 30 70 60 40 96 46 C 120 26 170 40 166 74 C 190 90 170 120 146 112 C 130 124 100 120 92 110 C 70 120 40 116 40 100 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='66' cy='78' r='4' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='130' cy='66' r='5' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='104' cy='92' r='3' opacity='.4'/><circle cx='200' cy='234' r='12'/><path d='M 194 233 C 196 235 198 235 200 233 M 202 233 C 204 235 206 235 208 233'/><path d='M 212 238 L 270 240 M 270 240 L 306 246 M 270 240 L 302 232 M 230 239 L 214 226'/><path d='M 218 214 C 222 210 226 210 228 214 M 226 204 C 230 200 234 200 236 204' opacity='.4'/><ellipse cx='262' cy='150' rx='9' ry='14' fill='var(--marigold)' transform='rotate(-30 262 150)'/><ellipse cx='280' cy='150' rx='9' ry='14' fill='var(--lavender)' transform='rotate(30 280 150)'/><path d='M 271 142 L 271 162 M 271 142 L 266 132 M 271 142 L 276 132'/><path d='M 240 200 C 250 186 246 176 258 168' stroke-dasharray='2 7' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(150,104.4) scale(0.8)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"he dreamed he was a butterfly and woke up not sure which one he was, and thought that was funny.","key":"zhuangzi","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhuang_Zhou","why":"the plain overview of his life"},{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zhuangzi_(book)","why":"the book with his name on it, and the butterfly dream"}],"lineage":"tao","mess":"nothing i know of.","moments":["when he was dying, his disciples wanted to give him a big funeral. he said heaven and earth were his coffin and the stars were his jewels, what more did they want. they said they were afraid the crows would eat him. he said above ground the crows get me, below ground the ants. why rob one to feed the other?","if an empty boat drifts into yours on the river, you don't get angry, even if you've got a temper. if there's a man in it, you shout. make yourself the empty boat. that's chapter 20.","chapter 26 ends with him asking where he can find a man who's forgotten words, so he can have a word with him."],"name":"zhuangzi","place":"the state of song, in north china","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 36 66 C 34 96 46 120 60 122 C 74 120 86 96 84 66 C 76 82 44 82 36 66 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 48 92 C 52 100 68 100 72 92' opacity='.4'/><path d='M 32 52 C 34 34 86 34 88 52 C 80 44 40 44 32 52 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='60' cy='28' r='7' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 45 55 C 48 58 52 58 55 55 M 65 55 C 68 58 72 58 75 55'/><circle cx='50' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='57' r='1.6' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 48 72 C 54 82 66 82 72 72 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M 51 74 C 56 76 64 76 69 74' stroke='var(--paper)' stroke-width='2'/><ellipse cx='94' cy='102' rx='6' ry='9' fill='var(--marigold)' transform='rotate(-25 94 102)'/><ellipse cx='106' cy='102' rx='6' ry='9' fill='var(--lavender)' transform='rotate(25 106 102)'/><path d='M 100 95 L 100 110 M 100 95 L 97 89 M 100 95 L 103 89'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"was it zhuang zhou dreaming he was a butterfly, or a butterfly dreaming it was zhuang zhou?","where":"the zhuangzi, the book named after him, chapter 2, the end, paraphrased"},{"q":"everyone knows the use of the useful. nobody knows the use of the useless.","where":"zhuangzi, chapter 4, the last line"},{"q":"the fish trap is for catching fish; once you've got the fish you can forget the trap.","where":"zhuangzi, chapter 26, where it goes on to say words are for catching meaning"}],"start":"the inner chapters, burton watson's translation, chapters 1 through 7. then the way of chuang tzu, thomas merton's loose versions, which are short.","status":"approved","story":"zhuang zhou lived in the fourth century bc in song, one of the small warring kingdoms china was broken into back then, before it was one country. sima qian, china's first great historian, writing about two hundred years later, says he was once a minor official in charge of a lacquer garden, a grove of the trees whose sap makes varnish. that's about all we know. the book with his name on it has thirty three chapters, and scholars think he wrote the first seven, the inner chapters, and his followers wrote the rest.\nthe book is jokes. talking trees and skulls. a cook named ding who's used the same knife for nineteen years without sharpening it, because he doesn't hack at the ox. he feels for the gaps between the joints and lets the blade slide through where the animal is already open. hunchbacks and cripples who turn out to be the wise ones. confucius, china's great teacher of duty and good manners, who'd died about a century before, gets made to say things he'd never say, about the tao, the way, the natural grain of things that life goes better when you stop fighting. and the best tree in the forest is the useless one, so knotted nobody bothers to cut it down, so it gets old.\nthe king of chu, the biggest kingdom in the south, sent two officials to offer him the job of running the state. he was fishing and didn't look up. he asked them whether a sacred tortoise, dead three thousand years and wrapped in silk in the king's temple, would rather be dead and honored or alive and dragging its tail in the mud. alive, they said. go away, he said. i'll drag my tail in the mud.\nhis best friend was huizi, a logician who argued with him about everything. on a bridge over the hao river zhuangzi said look how happy the fish are. huizi said you're not a fish, how do you know. zhuangzi said you're not me, how do you know i don't know. it goes around once more, and nobody wins, and that's the point. the tradition puts him right next to [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the legendary old master whose tao te ching, a short strange book of eighty one little poems, is where the whole tradition starts.\nwe barely know he existed, and he'd have found the question funny.","struggle":"he was poor. chapter 20 has him going to see the king of wei in patched clothes, shoes tied on with string. when the king asks why he's so wretched, he says he's poor, not wretched. chapter 26 has him going to borrow grain from an official who says sure, as soon as the taxes come in, and zhuangzi tells him a story about a fish flopping in the puddle of a wheel rut, who needs a bucket of water today, and gets promised a whole river someday.\nand his wife died. huizi came to pay his respects and found him sitting on the floor, legs sprawled, drumming on a pot and singing. huizi said she lived with you, raised your children, grew old with you, and you won't even cry. this is too much.","threads":["grief","money","joy"],"years":"the fourth century bc"},{"after":"after pratt threw her out, she didn't stop writing, and she didn't go quiet. she went to the reservation in utah and turned her anger into work, an opera, organizing, letters. and when she got to washington she stayed twenty years, testifying, writing reports, and pushing congress on citizenship and land and money stolen from native families.\nshe never got the credit. in 1938, the year she died, hanson rewrote the sun dance opera for broadway and left her name off it. she's buried at arlington, under her married name, and the name she gave herself is on the books.","basically":"a yankton dakota girl who went east at eight because missionaries promised her red apples and a train ride, and got her hair cut off at a boarding school in indiana. she grew up to teach at carlisle, the flagship government indian school, and then wrote essays in the atlantic monthly, the most respected magazine in the country, telling everybody what the schools did to children. the school's founder fired her. she wrote an opera, then spent the rest of her life in washington lobbying congress. think of the whistleblower who writes the exposé while she's still on the payroll. take from her that you can use the tools they trained you with to tell the truth about them.","born":1876,"died":1938,"doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 400 300' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='300' fill='var(--clay)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 20 250 L 380 250'/><path d='M 150 200 L 290 200 M 160 200 L 160 250 M 280 200 L 280 250'/><circle cx='120' cy='120' r='14'/><path d='M 120 134 L 120 182'/><path d='M 120 182 L 106 216 M 120 182 L 134 216'/><path d='M 120 148 L 106 172 M 120 148 L 146 142'/><path d='M 110 128 C 104 146 106 160 108 170 M 130 128 C 136 146 134 160 132 170' stroke-width='5'/><rect x='176' y='186' width='40' height='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 200 178 L 216 190' stroke-width='2'/><rect x='240' y='184' width='30' height='6' fill='var(--blue)'/><rect x='240' y='190' width='30' height='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><rect x='240' y='178' width='30' height='6' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 320 246 C 330 240 344 242 356 246' stroke-width='6'/><circle cx='318' cy='232' r='5'/><circle cx='330' cy='232' r='5'/><path d='M 321 236 L 340 222 M 327 236 L 308 222'/><g transform='translate(255,163.2) scale(0.55)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"hit":"they cut off her braids at eight, she taught at their flagship school, and then she told the whole country what those schools did.","key":"zitkalasa","learn":[{"label":"wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zitkala-%C5%A0a","why":"the plain overview of her life"},{"label":"american indian stories, free online","url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10376","why":"her childhood and school essays, at project gutenberg"}],"lineage":"native","mess":"she campaigned hard against peyote, the cactus used as a sacrament in the native american church, the church [[quanahparker|quanah parker]] helped build. in 1918 she testified to congress for banning it, alongside white reformers she otherwise fought. plenty of native people then and now think she was flat wrong and helped the government go after a native religion. that's the one thing on her record that people still argue about.","moments":["zitkala-ša, red bird, is a name she gave herself as a young woman. the government knew her as gertrude simmons bonnin, and that's the name on her grave.","the osage report she co-wrote in 1923 is about the same murders that became the book and the movie killers of the flower moon."],"name":"zitkala-ša","place":"the yankton reservation in dakota territory, indiana, carlisle, the uintah reservation in utah, then washington, d.c.","portrait":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 120 130' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 22 130 C 24 104 40 96 60 96 C 80 96 96 104 98 130' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 30 108 C 45 100 75 100 90 108' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 52 84 L 52 96 M 68 84 L 68 96'/><path d='M 30 60 C 26 80 28 100 30 108 L 90 108 C 92 100 94 80 90 60 C 88 36 32 36 30 60 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='60' cy='58' r='28' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 32 56 C 30 32 90 32 88 56 C 80 42 40 42 32 56 Z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='50' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='70' cy='56' r='2.2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 60 60 L 58 67 L 62 67'/><path d='M 53 75 L 67 75'/><path d='M 35 70 C 33 86 35 100 37 114 M 85 70 C 87 86 85 100 83 114' stroke-width='6'/><\/svg>","said":[{"q":"now my long hair was shingled like a coward's!","where":"the school days of an indian girl, 1900, in the atlantic monthly and later in american indian stories"},{"q":"if this is paganism, then at present, at least, i am a pagan.","where":"why i am a pagan, the atlantic monthly, december 1902, the last line"},{"q":"my indian nature is a moaning wind, but it comes out like the low voice of a seashell, only for those who lean in to hear it.","where":"an indian teacher among indians, 1900, paraphrased"}],"start":"american indian stories, which is free online. the three pieces about her childhood and school days, then why i am a pagan, which is short.","status":"approved","story":"she was born on february 22, 1876, on the yankton reservation in dakota territory, now south dakota. yankton dakota are one of the seven council fires, the seven nations of the people called the sioux. her mother raised her. her name means red bird. in 1884, at eight, she went off with missionaries to white's manual labor institute, a quaker boarding school in wabash, indiana, because they'd told her about red apples in the east and the train ride sounded exciting. her mother didn't want her to go.\nthe school cut her long hair. among the dakota, she'd been taught, only mourners and cowards had their hair cut short. she hid under a bed, and they dragged her out. she went home and came back, went to earlham college in indiana, and in 1897 started teaching music at carlisle, the big government indian school in pennsylvania run by richard henry pratt.\nin 1900, while she was still connected to carlisle, the atlantic monthly printed three of her essays, impressions of an indian childhood, the school days of an indian girl, and an indian teacher among indians. they told what it was like to be the kid on the other end of the program. pratt called one of her stories trash, and in 1901 she was out. in 1902 she married raymond bonnin, who worked for the indian service, and they spent fourteen years on the uintah and ouray reservation in utah.\nthere, with a white composer named william hanson, she wrote the sun dance opera, which opened in vernal, utah, in february 1913, the first american indian opera, about the sun dance, a ceremony the government had outlawed. in 1916 she became secretary of the society of american indians and moved to washington, d.c. in 1924 she co-wrote oklahoma's poor rich indians, a report on how white guardians were robbing and killing osage people for their oil money. in 1926 she started the national council of american indians and ran it until she died, on january 26, 1938. she's buried at arlington national cemetery, under the name gertrude simmons bonnin.","struggle":"the school took her hair and her language, and when she went home to her mother she couldn't be all the way there either. she wrote that she was neither a wild indian nor a tame one. she spent her life in between, and wrote about it without pretending it had resolved.\nand her mother. she'd gone against her mother's wishes to go east at eight, and she wrote about the distance that opened between them, and never closed.","threads":["exile","family","anger","work"],"years":"1876 to 1938"}],"meetings":[{"also":["the negus of abyssinia","ja'far ibn abi talib","umm salama"],"key":"abyssinia","lines":"a few years after [[muhammad]] began preaching in mecca, his followers there were being persecuted by the quraysh, the tribe that ran the town, which was his own tribe. so around 615 he told them to cross the sea to abyssinia, a christian kingdom in what's now ethiopia and eritrea, whose king, he said, would not put up with injustice. the king's title was the negus. muhammad stayed in mecca. about a dozen men and four women went first, and the accounts count around a hundred people in a second, bigger group.\nwhat happened there comes from ibn ishaq, whose life of muhammad is the earliest there is, put together well over a century later. he tells it in the voice of umm salama, one of the women who went, who later married muhammad. the quraysh sent two men with presents of fine leather to ask the king to send the runaways home. the negus wanted to hear the muslims first. their speaker was ja'far ibn abi talib, muhammad's cousin. he said, more or less: we were a rough people who worshipped idols, ate carrion and treated guests badly, until god sent us a messenger who told us to speak the truth, keep our word and look after our kin.\nthe king asked to hear what this messenger had brought. ja'far recited from the quran, which muslims hold to be god's own speech, from the chapter named for mary, the mother of [[jesus]]. the story says the negus wept until his beard was wet, and said these words and the ones his own faith rested on came out of the same lamp. the next day the men from mecca tried again: ask them what they say about jesus. ja'far said jesus was god's servant and messenger, his spirit and his word. the negus picked a stick up off the ground and said jesus was no more than that, not by the length of the stick. he gave the presents back and told the muslims they were safe in his country.\nthat's one report, written down long after, and historians argue over details like whether there were really two separate waves. ja'far stayed until 628. muslim accounts say the negus died in 630, and that muhammad prayed the funeral prayer for him from far away, with no body in front of him.","status":"approved","title":"a christian king takes in the first muslims.","when":"around 615, mecca and the christian kingdom of abyssinia, in what's now ethiopia","where":"ibn ishaq's life of muhammad, in alfred guillaume's 1955 english translation, the part on the emigration to abyssinia. umm salama's report, passed down through ibn ishaq, is also in the musnad of ahmad ibn hanbal, a big collection of reports about muhammad, number 1740.","who":["muhammad"],"year":615},{"also":["barnabas","james, the brother of jesus"],"key":"antioch","lines":"three years after he was knocked flat on the road to damascus and went from hunting jesus's followers to being one, [[paul]] went up to jerusalem to see [[peter|cephas]], which is peter's aramaic name, and stayed with him fifteen days. paul says so himself in galatians 1, one of his letters. they were on the same side of the big question, whether gentiles, non-jews, had to become jews to follow jesus.\nthen at antioch, a big city in what's now turkey, peter had been eating with gentile christians, which strict jews wouldn't do, until men came from james, jesus's brother, who led the church in jerusalem. then peter quietly pulled back and ate apart, and the others followed him, even barnabas, paul's partner on the road. paul says he opposed him to his face in front of everyone and called it hypocrisy. we only have paul's side. we don't know what peter said back.\nin acts 15, at a meeting of the church leaders in jerusalem, peter stands up and says god made no distinction between us and them, which is the thing paul was shouting about. a later letter under peter's name, which most scholars doubt he wrote, calls paul our dear brother and admits some of his letters are hard to understand. somebody wanted the two of them at peace.","status":"approved","title":"paul calls peter a hypocrite to his face.","when":"around the year 49, antioch","where":"galatians 1:18 and 2:11 to 14, in paul's letters, in the new testament. acts 15. 2 peter 3:15 and 16.","who":["peter","paul"],"year":49},{"also":["the dalai lama"],"key":"asia","lines":"after twenty seven years in a kentucky monastery, [[merton]] got permission to go to asia. in november, in dharamsala, the town in the indian himalayas where the tibetans live in exile, he met the dalai lama, the head of tibetan buddhism, three times. they talked about meditation, marxism, and the life of a monk, and each came away saying the other was the real thing.\nat polonnaruwa, a ruined old city in sri lanka, merton stood barefoot in front of the giant buddhas carved out of the rock and wrote that he'd finally seen what he'd been looking for, and that the whole thing was clear. a week later, in bangkok, on december 10, after giving a talk, he was found dead in his room, apparently electrocuted by a faulty fan.\ndecember 10 was the day he'd entered the monastery in 1941. twenty seven years to the day.","status":"approved","title":"the monk goes east and doesn't come back.","when":"november and december 1968","where":"the asian journal of thomas merton, his notebooks from the trip, the last thirty pages.","who":["merton"],"year":1968},{"also":["epimenides","aratus"],"key":"athens","lines":"in athens, standing in front of the philosophers on the areopagus, a rocky hill where the city's council met, [[paul]] didn't quote scripture. they'd never heard of it. he quoted their own poets. in him we live and move and have our being, that's epimenides, an old poet from crete. we are his offspring, that's aratus, a greek poet who wrote about the stars.\nhe pointed at an altar they'd built to an unknown god, a sort of just-in-case altar, and said, that one, i'll tell you about that one.\nit's the oldest example in the bible of somebody saying you already know this. you already wrote it down. you just called it something else.","status":"approved","title":"paul quotes the pagans to the pagans.","when":"around the year 50","where":"acts 17:16 through 34. read the whole chapter.","who":["paul"],"year":50},{"also":["baha ud-din walad"],"key":"attar-rumi","lines":"the story goes that when [[rumi|rumi's]] father, baha ud-din walad, was moving his family and his students west, they came through nishapur, and the old poet [[attar]] saw them. he said, here comes a sea followed by an ocean, and gave the boy a copy of his asrarnama, a long poem about the soul caught in the material world. some versions have him bouncing the child on his knee.\nrumi never mentions it. neither does his son sultan walad, who wrote about the family's journey west. franklin lewis, rumi's modern biographer, says it didn't happen, and another scholar files it with the succession myths, the stories people tell so one great teacher can hand the torch to the next. even the ages don't agree. one common telling makes rumi eighteen, which would put the meeting after attar was dead.\nwhat's real is the reading. rumi loved [[attar|attar's]] poems, and lines attributed to him put attar far ahead of himself, through all seven cities of love while rumi was still at the bend of the first street.","status":"approved","title":"the old pharmacist and the boy, a story nobody can prove.","when":"around 1216, if it happened at all, nishapur","where":"franklin lewis's rumi: past and present, east and west, the scholar's biography, which weighs the story and turns it down.","who":["attar","rumi"],"year":1216},{"also":["menachem mendel of kotzk","the apter rav"],"key":"baalshemtov-heschel","lines":"[[heschel|abraham joshua heschel]] was born in warsaw in 1907, but he wrote that his cradle stood in mezbizh. that's his spelling of medzhybizh, the small town in ukraine where [[baalshemtov|the baal shem tov]], the healer and teacher hasidism traces itself back to, spent his last twenty years and died in 1760. heschel's father came from there. heschel was named after an ancestor, the apter rav, a hasidic rebbe who settled in the town in 1813 and is buried close to the baal shem tov. the earliest fascination he could recall, he said, was the baal shem, whose parables gave him some of his first insights as a child.\nthen, in his ninth year, a second presence entered his life: menachem mendel of kotzk, the kotzker, a hasidic rebbe in poland who had no patience for false piety and spent his last twenty years in seclusion. he died in 1859. heschel's book a passion for truth sets the two against each other, and sets the kotzker beside [[kierkegaard|søren kierkegaard]], the danish christian thinker. the baal shem, he writes, keeps reminding us how close god is to man. the kotzker keeps recalling how far man is from truth, and from god.\nhe doesn't pick one. in a very strange way, he wrote, i found my soul at home with the baal shem but driven by the kotzker. and then: i had no choice: my heart was in mezbizh, my mind in kotzk. heschel died on december 23, 1972. the book came out the next year.","status":"approved","title":"heschel says his heart is in the baal shem tov's town and his mind in kotzk.","when":"1973, in a book that came out the year after heschel died","where":"a passion for truth, by heschel, 1973. the introduction, called why i had to write this book.","who":["baalshemtov","heschel"],"year":1973},{"also":["joshu sasaki"],"key":"baldy","lines":"[[cohen|leonard cohen]] met joshu sasaki, a japanese teacher of rinzai zen, the strict school known for koans, the riddles with no logical answer that teachers give students, in los angeles in the seventies, and kept sitting with him for decades. in the mid nineties he moved up to the zen center on mount baldy, in the mountains above los angeles, cooked for him, drove him around, and in 1996 was ordained a monk.\nthe years on the mountain were his own, and so was whatever he made of them. he came down in 1999 and said he hadn't been looking for a new religion. he was a jew who sat.","status":"approved","title":"a songwriter cooks for an old zen master.","when":"the seventies to the nineties, mount baldy","where":"book of longing, cohen's poems from the mountain years. sylvie simmons's i'm your man, the big biography, the chapters on mount baldy.","who":["cohen"],"year":1996},{"also":["sue bailey thurman","edward carroll","mahadev desai"],"key":"bardoli","lines":"[[thurman|howard thurman]] was thirty six, a baptist minister who ran the chapel at howard university, the black university in washington. he, his wife sue and the reverend edward carroll were three of four black americans sent by christian student groups on a speaking tour of india, burma and ceylon, the island now called sri lanka. the fourth, carroll's wife phenola, was sick that day. they took a train about two hundred miles north from bombay, and at dawn mahadev desai, the secretary who had been at [[gandhi]]'s side for nearly twenty years, drove them the last twenty miles in a battered model t ford. gandhi came out of his tent to meet them. desai told thurman he had never seen him greet visitors so warmly.\nthey had three hours, and for a good part of them gandhi asked the questions. could black americans vote. what about lynching, the schools, the churches. was the prejudice against color growing or dying out. and why had the slaves taken the religion of their masters. in islam, he said, the moment a slave accepts the faith he is his master's equal. thurman had been getting that question all across the tour, and he spent the next thirteen years working out his answer, a short book called jesus and the disinherited. gandhi, for his part, said he didn't care for the word nonviolence, because it's a negative word. ahimsa, his own word for it, was the greatest and the activest force in nature.\nsue thurman asked him to come to america. we want you not for white america, she said, but for the negroes. he said he couldn't. he had to make good the message here before he brought it to them. he asked them to sing a spiritual, were you there when they crucified my lord. and saying goodbye he told them that if it comes true, it may be through the negroes that the unadulterated message of nonviolence will be delivered to the world. desai printed his account of it in harijan, gandhi's weekly paper, the next month. [[king|martin luther king]] was seven years old. he went to india himself in 1959, eleven years after gandhi was shot.","status":"approved","title":"gandhi asks three black americans why they are christians.","when":"february 21, 1936, near bardoli in gujarat, western india","where":"mahadev desai's account, with our negro guests, in harijan, march 1936. thurman's own telling is in his autobiography, with head and heart.","who":["thurman","gandhi"],"year":1936},{"also":[],"key":"beach","lines":"[[peter]] had gone back to fishing on the sea of galilee, the lake where jesus first found him. all night, nothing. at dawn a man on the shore told them to throw the net on the right side, and it filled so full they couldn't haul it in. peter put his coat on and jumped into the water.\n[[jesus]] had a charcoal fire going on the beach with fish on it, and bread. peter had last stood at a charcoal fire in the courtyard of the high priest, the night jesus was arrested, telling people three times he'd never known him. after breakfast jesus asked him three times, do you love me, and three times gave him a job. he never brought up the courtyard.\nno lecture. the same fire, the same number, and a different answer.","status":"approved","title":"three times, for three times.","when":"a morning after the crucifixion, the sea of galilee","where":"john 21, the last chapter of the gospel of john, in any bible. then john 18:15 to 27 for the other fire.","who":["peter","jesus"],"year":30},{"also":["gary snyder","allen ginsberg"],"key":"berkeley-1955","lines":"in the fall of 1955 [[kerouac]], who'd written on the road but couldn't get it published yet, met gary snyder, a young poet who'd studied chinese and japanese and was about to go to kyoto to train in zen, the japanese buddhism of sitting meditation. that october they were both at a reading at the six gallery, a little art space in san francisco, the night their friend allen ginsberg first read howl, his long furious poem. a few weeks later snyder took kerouac and a friend up matterhorn peak in the sierra nevada.\nkerouac wrote it all up as the dharma bums, with snyder as japhy ryder. snyder didn't love being turned into a character. but the book sent a lot of young americans off with a rucksack and a copy of the sutras, the buddha's talks, and the next summer, on snyder's advice, kerouac went up to the fire lookout on desolation peak alone.","status":"approved","title":"a drinker follows a zen student up a mountain.","when":"october 1955, berkeley and the high sierra","where":"the dharma bums. snyder's side of it is in his interviews.","who":["kerouac"],"year":1955},{"also":["bill wilson","gerald heard"],"key":"billw-lsd","lines":"bill wilson, who'd started alcoholics anonymous after a flash of white light in a detox bed in 1934, took lsd in los angeles starting in 1956 with researchers there. gerald heard, an english writer on mysticism who'd come to california with [[huxley|aldous huxley]] in 1937, and huxley himself were among the people he did it with.\nhe said it brought back something like the light of 1934, and he hoped it could help drinkers who couldn't reach a spiritual experience on their own. most of aa was appalled that the founder was taking a mind altering drug. he never told members to try it.","status":"approved","title":"the founder of aa tries lsd.","when":"1956 into the late fifties, los angeles","where":"pass it on, aa's biography of bill wilson, the chapter on lsd.","who":["huxley"],"year":1956},{"also":["neem karoli baba"],"key":"blanket","lines":"richard alpert, the harvard psychologist who'd been fired for giving students psychedelics, came to india with a bag of lsd and no plan. a young american drifter drove him up into the hills to see his own guru, a fat old man wrapped in a plaid blanket called neem karoli baba, maharaj-ji to his people.\nthe old man told alpert he'd been thinking about his mother the night before, and that she'd died of her spleen. she had. nobody in india could have known that. alpert cried for two days.\nhe was given the name [[ramdass|ram dass]], servant of god. the instruction was to love everyone, serve everyone, remember god, and tell the truth. he spent fifty years on it.","status":"approved","title":"the man in the blanket knows about his mother.","when":"1967, the himalayan foothills","where":"be here now, the first section. miracle of love, ram dass's collection of stories about maharaj-ji.","who":["ramdass"],"year":1967},{"also":[],"key":"bonhoeffer-gandhi","lines":"[[bonhoeffer]], the german pastor who'd later die fighting hitler, wrote to friends that he wanted to go to india and sit at [[gandhi]]'s feet and learn how nonviolent resistance actually worked, because he thought the german church was going to need it. gandhi wrote back and said come.\nhe never went. the situation in germany got worse and he stayed. within a decade the pacifist was in a conspiracy to kill hitler.\nthe trip that didn't happen is one of the great what-ifs on these pages. the german lutheran and the hindu, in the same room, in 1935.","status":"approved","title":"bonhoeffer wants to go learn nonviolence from gandhi, and never gets there.","when":"1934","where":"bonhoeffer's letters from 1934, in the english edition of his collected works, volume 13.","who":["bonhoeffer","gandhi"],"year":1934},{"also":["bronson alcott"],"key":"brooklyn-1856","lines":"in november 1856 [[thoreau]] and bronson alcott, a teacher and dreamer from their concord circle and the father of louisa may alcott, who wrote little women, went to brooklyn to meet [[whitman]] in the house he shared with his mother. thoreau came away shaken. he wrote to a friend that whitman was the greatest democrat the world had seen, meaning a man of the common people, nothing to do with the party, and that some of the poems were very brave and a few made him uncomfortable.\nthoreau told him the poems sounded like the old hindu scriptures and asked if he'd read them. whitman said no, tell me about them.\ntwo men who'd never read each other's minds and ended up in the same place, one from a pond and one from a crowded street.","status":"approved","title":"thoreau asks whitman about the gita, and whitman says tell me.","when":"november 1856, brooklyn","where":"thoreau's letter to his friend harrison blake, december 7, 1856.","who":["thoreau","whitman"],"year":1856},{"also":["paul brunton"],"key":"brunton","lines":"paul brunton was an english journalist touring india in 1930 and 1931, hunting holy men, skeptical and a bit of a showman. at tiruvannamalai, the temple town in south india at the foot of the holy hill arunachala, he came into the hall where [[ramana]] sat with visitors and meant to fire off his questions. by his own account he couldn't. he sat there and the questions went quiet.\na search in secret india came out in london in 1934 and sold. it's the book that sent the westerners to the hill. the man who hadn't left arunachala in thirty five years started getting visitors from everywhere, because a journalist wrote a few chapters about sitting in a room.","status":"approved","title":"a london journalist sits in the hall and his questions go quiet.","when":"1931, tiruvannamalai, then london, 1934","where":"a search in secret india, paul brunton, the chapters on the hill.","who":["ramana"],"year":1931},{"also":["judah magnes"],"key":"buber-gandhi","lines":"on november 26, 1938, a little over two weeks after kristallnacht, the night the nazis burned synagogues across germany, [[gandhi]] published an article called the jews in harijan, his weekly paper. he said his sympathies were all with the jews and that the german persecution seemed to have no parallel in history. then came the advice. if he were a jew born in germany, he wrote, he would claim germany as his home, refuse to be expelled or treated as less, and dare them to shoot him or throw him in a dungeon. this was satyagraha, the method he had worked out in south africa, where he said the indians had stood in precisely the same place. on palestine he was just as plain. it belongs to the arabs, he wrote, in the same sense that england belongs to the english, and it was wrong to enter it under the shadow of the british gun.\n[[buber|martin buber]] had left germany for jerusalem that year, after five years under the nazis. gandhi's was a voice he said he had long known and honoured. he took weeks over the answer, with days passing between paragraphs, and dated it february 24, 1939. do you know what a concentration camp is like, he asked, and what goes on there. he wrote that he had seen jews in germany do exactly what gandhi was asking, and that it changed nothing. testimony without acknowledgment, he called it, a martyrdom cast to the winds. about palestine he said two peoples had real claims on the same land and he would not wave either one away. we have no desire to dispossess them; we want to live with them. and he said something gandhi would not have. he did not want force, but if there was no other way to keep evil from destroying good, he trusted he would use it.\nthe letter was sent to gandhi's home in india and printed that year as a pamphlet, together with one from buber's friend judah magnes, the american rabbi who ran the hebrew university. no answer came. there is no record that gandhi replied, and it isn't clear he ever read it. one account says he was away from home when it arrived.","status":"approved","title":"gandhi tells the jews of germany to resist without violence, and buber writes back.","when":"november 1938 to february 1939, a newspaper in india and a letter from jerusalem","where":"buber's letter is in a land of two peoples, the collection of his writings on jews and arabs. gandhi's article the jews is in harijan, november 26, 1938, and in his collected works.","who":["buber","gandhi"],"year":1939},{"also":[],"key":"buber-heschel","lines":"by 1937 [[buber|martin buber]] had spent four years keeping jewish learning for adults going in nazi germany. he had reopened the lehrhaus in frankfurt, the free school of jewish study his friend franz rosenzweig started in 1920, and he ran a central office that organized jewish adult education around the country, now that jews were shut out of german schools. the standard reference books say that in 1937 he named [[heschel|abraham joshua heschel]] his successor at both. heschel was about thirty, descended from hasidic masters, with a doctorate from the university of berlin.\nthe details shift a little depending on who tells it. heschel's daughter, susannah heschel, writes that buber invited him to direct the lehrhaus and that he moved from berlin in 1936. others say he took a teaching post there in 1937. what's clear is that the two became friends. on one of heschel's first visits for tea at buber's house in heppenheim the talk turned to how to teach prayer, and heschel said what we need to teach is not the prayerbook, but how to pray. in the months before buber left, heschel helped him learn to speak modern hebrew, the language he was going to need in jerusalem.\nbuber left in 1938. heschel didn't get long. late that october the gestapo arrested him in the rented room where he was living in frankfurt and deported him to poland with the other polish jews in germany. buber's books on hasidism were later picked apart by scholars. heschel, who came from the world those books describe, said that if you want to know hasidism as it was, begin with buber.","status":"approved","title":"buber hands his school in frankfurt to a thirty year old named heschel.","when":"1937 and 1938, frankfurt, germany","where":"edward kaplan and samuel dresner's biography abraham joshua heschel: prophetic witness. susannah heschel's foreword to in this hour, his writings from nazi germany and london.","who":["buber","heschel"],"year":1937},{"also":[],"key":"buddha-laotzu","lines":"tradition puts [[buddha|the buddha]], the indian prince who walked out of his palace and founded buddhism, and [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the chinese sage said to have written the tao te ching, a short book of poems about going with the grain of things, in the same century. one in india, one in china, neither aware of the other. that's probably tidy nonsense. it doesn't matter.\na thousand years later buddhism walked into china and met taoism, lao tzu's way, and what came out of that marriage was chan, which the japanese call zen. zen is the branch of buddhism that's mostly sitting still and paying attention. the sitting is the buddha's. the not-forcing, the water, the laughing at rules, that's lao tzu.\nwhen [[watts|alan watts]], the englishman who explained zen on california radio in the fifties, explained zen to america, half of what he was explaining was the tao te ching with a shaved head.","status":"approved","title":"they never met, and their kid is zen.","when":"the sixth century bc, by tradition","where":"the tao te ching, chapter 1. the dhammapada, a short early collection of the buddha's sayings. then the way of zen, alan watts's 1957 book, chapter 1, for how the two met.","who":["buddha","laotzu"],"year":-480},{"also":["mahavira"],"fork":{"at":"buddha","from":"hindu","kind":"fork","label":"the buddha walks out","to":"buddhist"},"key":"buddha-walks-out","lines":"[[buddha|the buddha]] grew up about twenty five hundred years ago below the himalayas, on the outer edge of the religious world of north india. that world ran on the vedas, the sacred hymns and ritual texts that the brahmins, the priestly class, kept in memory and chanted. the brahmins ran the sacrifices, offerings into a fire and sometimes animals, and they held that a brahmin is born, not made. nobody called any of this hinduism. hindu began as a word from old persia for the land along the indus river.\nhe wasn't the first to walk away. north india in his day was full of shramanas, wanderers who had left home to work out how to get free. the jains, whose teacher mahavira lived around the same time, were one such group. many of these wanderers rejected the vedas and the sacrifices. when the buddha left his father's house he became one of them. he starved himself until his bones showed, then gave that up too. so the life he chose was already there to choose.\nwhat he threw out was the authority of the vedas, the sacrifices, above all the killing of animals, and the idea that birth makes anyone holy. in one old talk a brahmin tending his fire offering sees him coming to beg his food and shouts at him to stay back, you outcast. the buddha answers that nobody is an outcast by birth and nobody is a brahmin by birth. what you do makes you one or the other. his order took in people of every caste. he also dropped the atman, the permanent self or soul. what he kept was karma, the idea that what you do comes back to you, and rebirth, being born again and again until you get free. both ideas were older than him.\nafter him the two sides tell it differently. historians say the brahmins' religion kept changing, took in ideas from the wanderers and the buddhists, and over the next several centuries became what we now call hinduism. the great epics, including the one [[krishna]] speaks in, come from that stretch. hindus often call their tradition sanatana dharma, the eternal order, with no founder, and from around the fifth century ad many came to count the buddha as an avatar of vishnu, god come down in a body. buddhists traditionally don't accept that. historians still argue over how fixed the ranking by birth was in his day. in india itself buddhism thinned out. by the time nalanda, its great university in the north, was destroyed around 1200, it had taken root in sri lanka, tibet and china.","status":"approved","title":"the buddha walks out on the priests and keeps karma.","when":"around 450 bc, give or take some decades. nobody can name the year, and scholars still argue over his dates","where":"the vasala sutta, the talk with the brahmin at his fire, is in the sutta nipata, an old collection of the buddha's talks.","who":["buddha"],"year":-450},{"also":["dwight goddard"],"key":"buddhist-bible","lines":"in 1954 [[kerouac]] found a buddhist bible, a fat anthology of sutras, the talks the buddha is supposed to have given, put into english by an american named dwight goddard in 1932. it took him over. he read it over and over, carried it around, and started the notebooks that became some of the dharma, hundreds of pages of notes on [[buddha|the buddha]] and his own head.\nin 1955 he wrote wake up, a life of the buddha, retelling the story straight out of goddard's pages. it sat unpublished until 2008. he never stopped crossing himself either.","status":"approved","title":"a catholic finds the sutras in a library.","when":"1954","where":"wake up: a life of the buddha. some of the dharma. goddard's a buddhist bible.","who":["kerouac","buddha"],"year":1954},{"also":[],"key":"camus-dostoevsky","lines":"[[camus|albert camus]] never met [[dostoevsky|fyodor dostoevsky]], who was long dead before camus was born. he read him instead, and kept reading him. the myth of sisyphus, camus's 1942 essay on whether a life with no given meaning is worth living, takes up the possessed, dostoevsky's 1872 novel, later called demons in english, as a book about what camus called philosophical suicide, the move of killing the question by jumping to an answer.\nin 1959, the year before he died, he turned the possessed into a play. he cut the novel into three parts, twenty four scenes on seven sets, with thirty three actors and a running time of about four hours, put it on at the théâtre antoine in paris with walls that moved and a stage that turned, and directed it himself. he paid for part of it with his nobel money. he said dostoevsky's book was a prophecy of what nihilism, the belief that nothing is worth anything, does to people. the critics liked it. that spring his old primary school teacher, louis germain, wrote wishing him luck with it.","status":"approved","title":"the unbeliever puts the russian's devils on stage.","when":"1942 to 1959, paris","where":"camus's the possessed is in print in english as a play. the myth of sisyphus has the pages on dostoevsky.","who":["camus","dostoevsky"],"year":1959},{"also":["coretta scott king"],"key":"capitol","lines":"[[malcolmx|malcolm x]] and [[king|martin luther king]] both came to the capitol to watch the senate fight over the civil rights act, the law that outlawed segregation in public places and discrimination in hiring. after king's press conference malcolm walked up, they shook hands and smiled, the cameras went off, and it was over in about a minute. it's the only time they met.\nin february 1965 malcolm went to selma while king was in jail there, and he told coretta scott king, king's wife, he hadn't come to make things harder for her husband. he said that if white people knew what the alternative was, meaning him, they might be more willing to hear king. seventeen days later malcolm was dead. three years after that, king was.","status":"approved","title":"one handshake, one photograph.","when":"march 26, 1964, washington, then february 1965, selma","where":"the photographs from that day. coretta scott king's my life with martin luther king jr. peniel joseph's the sword and the shield, which is about the two of them.","who":["malcolmx","king"],"year":1964},{"also":["richard henry pratt"],"key":"carlisle-three","lines":"the carlisle indian industrial school opened in 1879, founded by an army officer, richard henry pratt, to kill the indian and save the man, meaning erase the native child and keep the body. more than ten thousand children went through it before it closed in 1918.\n[[standingbear|luther standing bear]] was in the first class, in 1879, and picked his name off a wall. [[zitkalasa|zitkala-ša]] taught music there from 1897, then wrote about schools like it in the atlantic, and pratt threw her out. [[kimmerer|robin wall kimmerer]]'s grandfather was sent there, and she grew up without the potawatomi language because of it, and went back to learn it as a grown woman.\na student who lived through it, a teacher who told on it, and a granddaughter who went looking for what it took. that's what one school does across a hundred years.","status":"approved","title":"one school, three lives.","when":"1879 to the present, carlisle, pennsylvania","where":"luther standing bear's my people the sioux. zitkala-ša's american indian stories. robin wall kimmerer's braiding sweetgrass.","who":["standingbear","zitkalasa","kimmerer"],"year":1879},{"also":[],"key":"civil-disobedience","lines":"[[thoreau]] spent one night in the concord jail in 1846 for refusing to pay a tax to a government that backed slavery, and wrote an essay about it. in 1907 [[gandhi]], then a lawyer in south africa, was leading indians there against a new law that made them register with the police and give their fingerprints. he read the essay and printed extracts in his newspaper, indian opinion. he used the phrase civil disobedience for the rest of his life. he said different things about it at different times. once he said he'd taken the name from thoreau. later he said the resistance in south africa had already started before he ever read the essay.\nin the forties, a student at morehouse college, a black men's college in atlanta, named [[king|martin luther king jr.]] read the same essay. in stride toward freedom, his book about the montgomery bus boycott, he says it was his first contact with the idea of refusing to cooperate with an evil system.\na night in jail over a small tax. it traveled to durban and back to alabama.","status":"approved","title":"one night in a small town jail goes a long way.","when":"1907, south africa; then 1944, atlanta","where":"civil disobedience, all of it. gandhi's satyagraha in south africa. king's stride toward freedom, the chapter pilgrimage to nonviolence.","who":["thoreau","gandhi","king"],"year":1907},{"also":[],"key":"concord","lines":"the rabbinical assembly, the association of conservative rabbis in america, met at the concord hotel, a big resort in the catskill mountains north of new york city, and invited [[king|martin luther king]]. [[heschel]] introduced him. he asked where in america you could hear a voice like the voice of the prophets of israel, and said king was a sign that god hadn't forsaken the country.\nking spoke and took questions from the rabbis. ten days later he was shot and killed on a motel balcony in memphis. heschel lived four more years.","status":"approved","title":"ten days before memphis.","when":"march 25, 1968, the catskills","where":"the transcript was printed as conversation with martin luther king in the journal conservative judaism, spring 1968.","who":["heschel","king"],"year":1968},{"also":["john neihardt"],"key":"crazy-horse-cousin","lines":"[[blackelk|black elk]] was [[crazyhorse|crazy horse]]'s second cousin, and grew up in the same camps. in black elk speaks, the book john neihardt made from his interviews in 1931, he talks about crazy horse the way a boy talks about the family hero. he says crazy horse was a strange man who went about by himself and didn't talk much.\nblack elk also tells of the killing of crazy horse at fort robinson in september 1877, and of how the people mourned. the chapter is one of the main native accounts of it that we have, filtered through neihardt's english.\nblack elk himself had a vision at nine that he told almost nobody about for years. he knew what it was to carry something you couldn't explain.","status":"approved","title":"the boy watched his cousin.","when":"the 1870s, the lakota camps and fort robinson, nebraska","where":"black elk speaks, the chapters on crazy horse.","who":["crazyhorse","blackelk"],"year":1877},{"also":["surendranath mitra"],"key":"dakshineswar-1881","lines":"narendranath datta was eighteen, a calcutta college student who read the english philosophers and belonged to the brahmo samaj, a reform group that had thrown out the idols and the miracles. in november 1881 a neighbor, surendranath mitra, needed a singer for a gathering at his house, and the kid next door could sing. the guest of honor was [[ramakrishna]], the priest from the kali temple at dakshineswar, a man who could barely read. he heard the boy sing and asked him to come up the river. narendra went, and asked what he'd been asking every religious man in the city: have you seen god. yes, ramakrishna said, more clearly than i see you.\nhe didn't buy it, and he kept coming back for almost five years to check. the story goes he once hid a coin under the old man's mattress, because ramakrishna said he couldn't bear the touch of money, and watched him sit down and jump back up like he'd been stung. he laughed out loud at advaita, the teaching that everything is god, this jug, this cup, us. ramakrishna didn't argue. he told him to test him the way a money changer tests a coin, and not to take his word until he had.\nthe testing ended in august 1886 in a rented house at cossipore, with the old man dying of throat cancer and handing the other boys over to the one who'd doubted him hardest. narendra became [[vivekananda|swami vivekananda]]. seven years later he stood up at the parliament of the world's religions in chicago and said what the priest had taught him.","status":"approved","title":"a college skeptic spends five years testing a priest.","when":"november 1881 to august 1886, calcutta and dakshineswar","where":"swami nikhilananda's vivekananda: a biography, the first chapters. the gospel of sri ramakrishna has the two of them talking, from the diary of a man who was in the room.","who":["vivekananda","ramakrishna"],"year":1881},{"also":[],"key":"damascus","lines":"[[paul]] was a strict, brilliant jewish religious scholar hunting down the first followers of [[jesus]], a few years after the romans had executed him. he was on the road to damascus, in syria, to arrest more of them when a light put him on the ground and a voice asked why he was persecuting it. he'd never met the man. he'd only hunted his friends.\nhe spent the rest of his life saying he'd met him anyway, and writing letters to the little churches he started that are older than any of the four gospels, the four accounts of jesus's life in the bible. every account of jesus we have was written after paul had already told everyone what it meant.\nthe guy who never met him shaped how everyone who reads about him meets him. that's worth sitting with.","status":"approved","title":"they never met, and then they did.","when":"a few years after the crucifixion","where":"acts 9, in the book right after the gospels. then galatians 1, one of paul's own letters, where he tells it himself.","who":["paul","jesus"],"year":34},{"also":[],"key":"day-merton","lines":"[[merton|thomas merton]] and [[dorothyday|dorothy day]] wrote to each other for years. he was a trappist, a monk in a strict, mostly silent order, at a monastery in the kentucky woods. she was running soup lines on the lower east side of new york.\nwhen merton started writing against nuclear weapons and the war, the catholic worker, her penny newspaper, printed him. his essay the root of war is fear ran in the paper in october 1961, and the heads of his order were not pleased. she gave him a place to say it when his own order wanted him quiet.\ntwo catholics who'd both lived hard before they converted, and both ended up as the church's bad conscience.","status":"approved","title":"the monk sends his war essays to the soup kitchen.","when":"the late fifties and sixties, by mail","where":"merton's letters to her are in the hidden ground of love, a collection of his letters. hers are in all the way to heaven, her collected letters.","who":["dorothyday","merton"],"year":1961},{"also":["vine deloria sr.","philip deloria"],"key":"deloria-and-the-churches","lines":"[[deloria|vine deloria jr.]] came from a church family. his grandfather philip deloria, a yankton dakota leader, became an episcopal priest, the american branch of the church of england. his father, vine deloria sr., was an episcopal archdeacon who spent his life as a missionary on the dakota and lakota reservations. deloria went to seminary himself.\nthen he wrote custer died for your sins in 1969, with a whole chapter telling the churches that their missions had done damage and that they should stop converting indians and start helping the tribes. in 1973 god is red went further, and argued that native religion wasn't an early draft of christianity but a different way of being religious, rooted in land instead of history.\nit's an argument with his own family's life's work, carried out in public, by a man who knew that work from the inside. he never said it was easy.","status":"approved","title":"the priest's son answers the church.","when":"1969 to 1973","where":"custer died for your sins, the chapter on missionaries. god is red.","who":["deloria"],"year":1969},{"also":["john neihardt"],"key":"deloria-on-black-elk","lines":"[[deloria|vine deloria jr.]] spent his career suspicious of white writers telling native stories. he'd written a whole chapter mocking anthropologists. but in 1979 he wrote the introduction to a new edition of [[blackelk|black elk speaks]], the book john neihardt made in 1932 from black elk's account of his vision and his life.\nhe knew neihardt had shaped it. he called it a religious classic anyway, and said it had become a kind of bible for a younger generation of native people who were trying to find their way back to their own religion.","status":"approved","title":"the skeptic blesses the book.","when":"1979","where":"vine deloria jr.'s introduction to the 1979 edition of black elk speaks.","who":["deloria","blackelk"],"year":1979},{"also":[],"key":"desert","lines":"before he starts teaching, [[jesus]] goes into the desert alone for forty days, and the devil shows up with offers. turn these stones into bread. jump off the top of the temple and let the angels catch you. every time he answers with a line from deuteronomy, one of the five books that carry the name of [[moses]], the old lawgiver. he never says anything of his own out there. he just quotes.\nlater he takes three friends up a mountain and they see him lit up, blazing, and moses and elijah, the great fire-and-thunder prophet of the old stories, standing there with him, talking. peter, one of the friends, wants to build tents and make it permanent. a cloud shuts him up.\nthe new testament, the christian half of the bible, keeps reaching back into the old testament, the jewish scriptures that came first. that's how these books work. nobody starts from scratch.","status":"approved","title":"moses shows up on the mountain.","when":"around the year 29","where":"matthew 4 for the desert. matthew 17 for the mountain. deuteronomy 6 and 8 for what he's quoting.","who":["jesus","moses"],"year":29},{"also":["larry brilliant","girija brilliant"],"key":"doctor-america","lines":"larry brilliant was a young american doctor who'd come to india in the early seventies, by his own telling on a bus trip with the hog farm, a traveling hippie commune, and ended up at the feet of [[neemkaroli|neem karoli baba]], maharaj-ji to his people, at kainchi, his ashram in the himalayan foothills, with his wife girija. the old man called him doctor america and kept telling him he'd go to work for the united nations and help rid the world of smallpox, a disease that killed hundreds of millions of people in the twentieth century alone.\nhe got a job, in the world health organization's smallpox program in india, going village to village hunting cases. the world health organization is the united nations' health agency. the last natural case of the deadly form of smallpox anywhere on earth was a little girl in bangladesh in 1975. the world was declared free of it in 1980.\nin 1978 the brilliants, [[ramdass|ram dass]], and a few others started seva, which means service, a charity that has paid for millions of sight saving eye operations since. the man in the blanket never gave a talk on public health. he just kept telling a doctor where to go.","status":"approved","title":"the old man tells a hippie doctor to go end smallpox.","when":"the early seventies, kainchi, then the villages of india","where":"larry brilliant's memoir, sometimes brilliant. miracle of love, the collection of stories about the old man, has stories from him too.","who":["neemkaroli","ramdass"],"year":1973},{"also":["vladimir solovyov","nikolai strakhov"],"key":"dostoevsky-tolstoy","lines":"[[dostoevsky|fyodor dostoevsky]] and [[tolstoy|leo tolstoy]] were the two biggest writers in russia for twenty years, read each other closely, and never once shook hands. the nearest they came was a lecture. in the early months of 1878 vladimir solovyov, a philosopher of twenty five who had become dostoevsky's friend, gave a series of public talks in st petersburg on what he called godmanhood, his word for the joining of god and humanity. tolstoy came to one of them in march with nikolai strakhov, a writer who was close to both men. dostoevsky was in the same hall. strakhov introduced nobody. the story goes that tolstoy had asked not to be introduced to anyone, and that both men were sorry about it afterwards. one account dates the lecture to 1877. nobody disputes that they were in the room together, once, and left separately.\nwhat they had instead was the page. in 1877 dostoevsky gave several chapters of his column, a writer's diary, to tolstoy's new novel anna karenina, which he read very carefully. in private he filed tolstoy's work under gentry-landowner literature, the novels of people who owned land. tolstoy, for his part, cared for one dostoevsky book, the house of the dead, the account of the prison camp in siberia. on september 26, 1880, he wrote to strakhov that he knew no better book in all of modern russian writing, pushkin included, and added, if you see dostoevsky, tell him that i love him. strakhov carried the letter over. dostoevsky was embarrassed to be put above pushkin.\nthat winter he was dead. tolstoy wrote to strakhov in february 1881 that he had never seen the man and never had any direct dealings with him, and that he had been the very closest, dearest and most necessary man to him. tolstoy visited optina too, the monastery dostoevsky had gone to after his son died, and didn't think much of the elders there.","status":"approved","title":"the two of them sit in the same hall and nobody makes the introduction.","when":"march 1878, a lecture hall in st petersburg","where":"tolstoy's letters to nikolai strakhov from september 1880 and february 1881, which every life of either man quotes. the lecture itself is in print as solovyov's lectures on divine humanity.","who":["dostoevsky","tolstoy"],"year":1878},{"also":["pope leo ix","cardinal humbert","michael cerularius","pope innocent iii","pope paul vi","patriarch athenagoras"],"fork":{"from":"christian","kind":"fork","label":"1054, east and west","to":"christian","year":1054},"key":"east-and-west-1054","lines":"in 1054 the christian church had two centers. the latin west looked to the pope, the bishop of rome. the greek east looked to the patriarch of constantinople, the chief bishop of the city now called istanbul. that year pope leo ix sent a cardinal named humbert east to negotiate with the patriarch, michael cerularius. on july 16 humbert walked into hagia sophia, the cathedral of constantinople, during a service and laid on the altar a paper putting the patriarch out of the church. the patriarch did the same to humbert and the men with him. that day became the textbook date for the split between the catholic church in the west and the orthodox church in the east.\ntwo things make the day smaller than it sounds. pope leo had died that april, so humbert was acting for a dead man. and the sentences named persons, not churches. in the east, dealings with rome stayed fairly friendly for a good while after. the date many give for the real break is april 1204, when an army of western crusaders and venetians took constantinople, looted it for three days and set up a western emperor there. the pope of the day, innocent iii, had forbidden the attack. the orthodox church in america's own history says that after 1204 reunion had virtually no chance.\nunderneath were two arguments. one is the pope. catholics hold that he has full and supreme power over the whole church, and point to [[jesus]] telling [[peter]], on this rock i will build my church. the orthodox hold that the bishop of rome is first among equals, with no power to govern the other churches. the other is one latin word, filioque, which means and the son. the creed, the statement of belief settled in 381, says the holy spirit proceeds from the father. churches in the west added and the son, first in spain in 589, and rome took it up in 1014. the orthodox object to the idea and to the creed being changed at all. catholics say it is what the church has always taught.\non december 7, 1965, pope paul vi and patriarch athenagoras put out a joint statement. they regretted the offensive words on both sides and removed the sentences of 1054 from memory and from the midst of the church. they also said this was not enough to end the differences, and it wasn't. the two churches still do not share communion. in 2004 pope john paul ii wrote of the pain and disgust of 1204, eight centuries on, and patriarch bartholomew accepted the apology.","status":"approved","title":"1054 gets the blame for a split that took centuries.","when":"16 july 1054 in constantinople, now istanbul, is the famous day. nobody can name the real one, and scholars have proposed 1009, 1204, 1277 and 1484 as well","where":"the joint catholic and orthodox declaration of december 7, 1965, is short and on the vatican's website. the orthodox church in america's church history, the chapter on the eleventh century, tells 1054 from the eastern side.","who":["peter"],"year":1054},{"also":["carl ruck"],"key":"eleusis","lines":"[[wasson|gordon wasson]], the banker who'd taken the mushrooms with maría sabina, and [[hofmann|albert hofmann]], the chemist who'd made lsd, wrote the road to eleusis with carl ruck, a classics professor at boston university. eleusis was a town near athens where for about two thousand years greeks went through a secret initiation, the mysteries, and came out saying they no longer feared death.\nthe book argues that the drink everyone took there had ergot in it, the grain fungus lsd came from. it's a guess. scholars still argue about it. but it's the two men who'd done the most to bring mushrooms and lsd into the modern world, trying to prove it had all happened before.","status":"approved","title":"a banker and a chemist go looking for an ancient drink.","when":"1978","where":"the road to eleusis, 1978.","who":["wasson","hofmann"],"year":1978},{"also":["ralph waldo emerson"],"key":"emerson-letter","lines":"[[whitman]] sent a copy of his self published book, leaves of grass, to ralph waldo emerson, the most respected writer in america. emerson wrote back on july 21, 1855, that he found it the most extraordinary piece of wit and wisdom america had yet contributed, and greeted him at the beginning of a great career.\nwhitman had the letter reprinted in a newspaper without asking, and put a line of it in gold on the spine of the second edition, like a blurb on a movie poster. emerson was not delighted. he kept up with him anyway, and later tried to get him to cut the sex poems, walking with him on boston common, the big park in the middle of the city. whitman listened and said no.","status":"approved","title":"a famous man writes back.","when":"july 1855, concord to brooklyn","where":"emerson's letter to whitman, july 21, 1855. whitman tells the boston common story in specimen days, his prose memoir.","who":["whitman"],"year":1855},{"also":[],"key":"flowers","lines":"[[kabir]] was a weaver and poet in varanasi, india, who spent his life telling hindus and muslims that god wasn't in the temple or the mosque. when he died, the story goes, the hindus wanted to burn the body, which is how hindus handle their dead, and the muslims wanted to bury it, which is how muslims do, and they argued over the shroud.\nsomeone lifted it and there was no body. just flowers. they split the flowers and each did their own rite.\nit's a legend. it's also the best answer anyone's given to the question of who owns a saint.","status":"approved","title":"both sides claim the body and find flowers.","when":"1518, or thereabouts","where":"any life of kabir tells it. the kabir book, robert bly's english versions of the poems, has it in the introduction.","who":["kabir"],"year":1518},{"also":[],"key":"fools-crow-black-elk","lines":"[[foolscrow|frank fools crow]] was [[blackelk|black elk]]'s nephew. both were oglala lakota holy men on the pine ridge reservation, both toured with buffalo bill's wild west as young men, and both carried the old ceremonies through the decades when the government had banned them.\nblack elk rode into wounded knee creek on the day of the massacre in 1890. fools crow was born around then, near the same creek. eighty three years later, in 1973, when activists occupied wounded knee, fools crow was the elder at the center of it, and helped end it without another massacre.","status":"approved","title":"the uncle and the nephew, both holy men, both at wounded knee.","when":"the twentieth century, pine ridge","where":"thomas mails's fools crow. black elk speaks.","who":["foolscrow","blackelk"],"year":1930},{"also":[],"key":"francis-rohr","lines":"[[rohr|richard rohr]], the franciscan priest in new mexico who writes about the second half of life, was ordained in 1970. that means he took vows in the order [[francis]] started around 1209 with a handful of men and nothing in their pockets, promising, like them, to own nothing.\nrohr's whole program is francis's life with the labels on. he named his place the center for action and contemplation, meaning praying and working for the poor as one thing, not two. in 2014 he wrote eager to love, a book about francis as an alternative way, a man who stayed inside the church and lived at its edge, which is also a fair description of rohr.\nthe bird on the ground in rohr's drawing is there for a reason.","status":"approved","title":"a friar in albuquerque takes vows to a man who kissed lepers.","when":"eight centuries apart","where":"rohr's eager to love: the alternative way of francis of assisi.","who":["francis","rohr"],"year":1970},{"also":["sultan al-kamil"],"key":"francis-sultan","lines":"in 1219 the fifth crusade, one of the wars european christians fought to take the holy land from its muslim rulers, was bogged down in egypt. a crusader army had been camped for over a year outside damietta, a walled port at the mouth of the nile. the man they were fighting, al-kamil, the sultan, the muslim ruler of egypt, was camped upstream. some time that september, most likely, [[francis|francis of assisi]] and one other brother walked out of the christian camp and across the lines, hoping to convert the sultan or be killed trying.\nthe nearest thing to a witness is jacques de vitry, a bishop who was there with the crusader army. early in 1220 he wrote in a letter that francis wasn't afraid to cross to the enemy's army, and that he preached to the muslims for several days. a few years later he added that the sultan listened closely. a crusader chronicle from those years tells of two unnamed clerics who did the same. in it the sultan's advisers say the law calls for their heads, and the sultan says he won't kill men who risked death for the sake of his soul. he offers them land, gold and silk, they refuse it, and he feeds them and has them escorted back. nobody wrote down what was actually said. no known arab source mentions the visit at all.\nthe famous part came later. around 1260, forty years on, bonaventure, the head of the franciscans, the order of brothers francis started, wrote the order's official life of him. in his telling francis offers to walk into a great fire with the sultan's priests to show whose faith is true. the priests want no part of it, francis offers to go in alone, and the sultan says no. it's the scene the painters picked. it isn't in the earlier accounts, and historians don't take it as history.\ntwo months after the visit the crusaders took damietta, and in 1221 they lost it again and left egypt. the sultan had offered them jerusalem if they would just go, and the pope's man in the camp turned it down. in 1229 al-kamil handed jerusalem to the christians anyway, by treaty. in the basilica at assisi there's an ivory horn on display that's said to be his gift to francis.","status":"approved","title":"francis crosses the battle lines to preach to the sultan.","when":"probably september 1219, outside damietta, in egypt","where":"john tolan's saint francis and the sultan: the curious history of a christian-muslim encounter, which goes through every telling from 1220 on. jacques de vitry's letter of 1220 is the earliest account. the trial by fire is in bonaventure's major legend, his life of francis.","who":["francis"],"year":1219},{"also":["maurice frydman"],"key":"frydman","lines":"maurice frydman was a polish jewish engineer who came to india in the thirties, took a hindu monk's vows for a while, worked with [[gandhi]]'s people on getting indian villages to make their own cloth and tools, and sat with [[ramana]] at his hill in the south.\ndecades later, an old man himself, he found [[nisargadatta]], the cigarette seller, teaching in the loft over his house in bombay. frydman taped the talks, translated them out of marathi, the local language, and made them into i am that, in 1973. he died three years later.\nwithout him the cigarette seller stays a neighborhood teacher in khetwadi, his crowded corner of bombay. frydman's the reason the loft filled up.","status":"approved","title":"one polish engineer, two rooms.","when":"the thirties to 1973, the hill and the loft","where":"i am that, the translator's note at the front.","who":["ramana","nisargadatta","gandhi"],"year":1973},{"also":[],"key":"gandhi-gita","lines":"two theosophists, members of a society of westerners who were into eastern religion, asked [[gandhi]], a twenty year old indian law student in london, to read the [[krishna|gita]] with them, in edwin arnold's english translation. he was embarrassed he'd never read his own scripture in the original. it became the book he read every day for the rest of his life.\naround the same time somebody handed him a bible. he couldn't get through the old testament. then he hit the sermon on the mount, the three chapters where jesus says turn the other cheek and love your enemies, and said it went straight to his heart, and that the [[jesus|sermon]] and the gita were saying the same thing.\na hindu who found his own scripture through the english, and his method through a jewish carpenter. the empire that educated him should have seen it coming.","status":"approved","title":"a shy law student meets the gita in english.","when":"1889, london","where":"his autobiography, the story of my experiments with truth, part 1, chapter 20. the gita, chapter 2. the sermon on the mount is matthew 5 through 7.","who":["gandhi","krishna","jesus"],"year":1889},{"also":[],"key":"garden-milan","lines":"[[augustine]] was thirty one, a star teacher of public speaking in milan with a fiancée lined up and a habit he couldn't quit. he was in a garden, sick of himself, crying under a fig tree, when a child's voice from the next house started chanting take up and read. he went back to where he'd left a copy of the letters of [[paul]], the early christian whose letters make up a big part of the new testament, and opened it at random.\nit fell open at paul's letter to the christians in rome, chapter 13. not in partying and drunkenness, not in sleeping around, put on the lord jesus christ and stop making plans for the flesh. he says he didn't want to read any further, and didn't need to.\na letter a tentmaker wrote to rome three hundred years earlier, landing on exactly the one man it was written for.","status":"approved","title":"a child sings, and augustine opens paul.","when":"august 386, milan","where":"the confessions, augustine's memoir, book 8, chapter 12. then romans 13:13 and 14, in the new testament, the christian second half of the bible.","who":["augustine","paul"],"year":386},{"also":[],"key":"ghaffarkhan-gandhi","lines":"in 1938 [[gandhi|gandhi]] toured the north-west frontier province twice with [[ghaffarkhan|abdul ghaffar khan]], once in may and again from october into november. he went to utmanzai, khan's home village, looked over ranks of khudai khidmatgar, the servants of god, went up to the khyber pass, and spoke at a public meeting in peshawar with khan beside him. at one meeting in october khan translated his speech for the crowd.\nthese were pashtuns, men from a culture of guns and blood feuds, and [[gandhi|gandhi]] said that such men laying down their arms and taking nonviolence as the stronger weapon sounded almost like a fairy tale.\nnine years later both men lost the argument over partition. [[ghaffarkhan|khan]] is reported to have told the congress leaders they had thrown his people to the wolves.","status":"approved","title":"gandhi goes to the frontier to see the nonviolent army for himself.","when":"may 1938, and again october to november 1938","where":"the gandhi heritage portal's photographs of the 1938 frontier tour, and the chronology of gandhi's life on mkgandhi.org. easwaran's nonviolent soldier of islam tells it at length.","who":["ghaffarkhan","gandhi"],"year":1938},{"also":["ibn rushd"],"key":"ghazali-ibnrushd","lines":"[[ghazali|al-ghazali]] was a muslim scholar teaching at a college in baghdad when, around 1095, he put out the incoherence of the philosophers. it went after the muslim thinkers who followed the ancient greek philosophers, avicenna above all, and took on twenty of their teachings. on three he said they had left islam: that the world never had a beginning, that god knows only kinds of things and not each single thing, and that souls will never return to bodies after death. anyone who taught those three in public, he ruled, was an unbeliever who could be killed. he died in december 1111.\nfifteen years later, in 1126, ibn rushd was born in cordoba, in muslim spain. europe knows him as averroes. he became a chief judge and then chief doctor to the ruler, and he explained the greek philosophers so well that latin europe just called him the commentator. around 1180 he answered al-ghazali with a book called the incoherence of the incoherence. he quotes al-ghazali a passage at a time and replies to each one.\nal-ghazali's best known move was about cause and effect. when fire touches cotton, he argued, nothing makes it necessary that the fire is what does the burning. god puts the two side by side, and god could do otherwise. ibn rushd had no patience for that. it's self-evident, he wrote, that things have natures of their own that decide what each thing does. he didn't defend everything al-ghazali had attacked, and he criticized avicenna too. but on the three charges he gave no ground. the world has always existed, god knows it in his own way, and he refused to sign on to the rising of bodies.\nit didn't go well for him at home. in 1195 a tribunal in cordoba condemned his teaching, ordered his books burned and sent him off to a nearby town, and he died in 1198. how much the muslim world read him after that is argued over. jewish and christian scholars read him hard, and the incoherence of the incoherence was put into latin twice, in 1328 and 1526. al-ghazali never got to answer. by the time the reply was written he'd been dead for sixty nine years.","status":"approved","title":"ibn rushd answers a dead man, passage by passage.","when":"1180, answering a book from around 1095. the two men were never alive at the same time","where":"both books are in english: michael marmura's translation of the incoherence of the philosophers, and simon van den bergh's 1954 translation of the incoherence of the incoherence. the stanford encyclopedia of philosophy's articles on al-ghazali and on ibn rushd, free online, lay out both sides.","who":["ghazali"],"year":1180},{"also":["kicking bear","short bull"],"key":"ghost-dance-reaches-lakota","lines":"after [[wovoka]], a paiute holy man in nevada, had his vision on new year's day 1889, people came from all over the west to hear him. the lakota sent men, among them kicking bear and short bull, who brought the ghost dance home to the reservations in south dakota. it promised that if people danced and lived right, the dead would come back and the old world would return.\non the lakota reservations it took on harder edges, like the ghost shirts, painted shirts some believed would stop bullets. [[blackelk|black elk]] danced it, had visions in it, and says in black elk speaks that he made some of the first ghost shirts from what he saw. kicking bear brought it to [[sittingbull|sitting bull]]'s camp at standing rock in october 1890, and sitting bull let the dancers gather there.\nthe government read it as a coming war. police were sent to arrest sitting bull on december 15, 1890, and killed him. two weeks later came wounded knee. wovoka, who'd told people not to hurt anybody, was heartsick.","status":"approved","title":"a peace dance from nevada came east and turned into a reason to kill people.","when":"1889 and 1890, nevada and the lakota reservations","where":"james mooney's the ghost-dance religion and the sioux outbreak of 1890. black elk speaks, the chapters on the messiah and the ghost dance.","who":["wovoka","sittingbull","blackelk"],"year":1890},{"also":["walter pahnke","huston smith"],"key":"good-friday","lines":"on good friday 1962 walter pahnke, a harvard grad student working with [[leary|timothy leary]]'s group, gave psilocybin to half of twenty divinity students, and a placebo to the rest, during a service at marsh chapel at boston university. most who got the drug reported a mystical experience. to measure that, pahnke built a questionnaire from the philosopher w. t. stace's list of what mystics in every religion report, the same idea [[huxley|aldous huxley]] had made popular in the perennial philosophy, and william james, the harvard psychologist, had laid out in 1902.\nin 2006 [[griffiths|roland griffiths]] at johns hopkins ran the careful version, with a proper comparison drug and follow up, and used a descendant of pahnke's questionnaire, now called the mystical experience questionnaire. his results looked a lot like the chapel's. what leary's circle did loosely, he did slowly, and it held.","status":"approved","title":"a chapel full of divinity students, and the study that answered it forty years later.","when":"april 1962, boston; then 2006, baltimore","where":"griffiths's 2006 paper in psychopharmacology. rick doblin's 1991 follow up of the good friday volunteers.","who":["leary","griffiths"],"year":1962},{"also":["levi","andrew"],"key":"gospel-of-mary","lines":"there's an old book called the gospel of mary. it isn't in the bible and it isn't by anyone who was there. scholars put it in the second century, a hundred years or more after [[jesus]] died. the text only calls her mary, and most scholars take her to be [[magdalene|mary magdalene]], though a few have argued for another mary.\nin the part that survives, jesus has just left, and his disciples, his closest followers, are in tears, asking how they're supposed to go out and preach. mary gets up and tells them not to cry, his grace will be with them. [[peter]] says, sister, we know the savior loved you more than the other women, tell us his words. she says she'll tell them what's been hidden from them, and describes a vision of a soul rising past four powers that try to stop it.\nthen it turns. andrew says he doesn't believe the savior said any of this. peter asks whether he really spoke privately with a woman and not openly to them, and whether they're all supposed to turn around and listen to her. mary cries and asks peter if he thinks she made it up. a disciple named levi answers for her. he says peter has always been hot tempered, and now he's going at the woman the way their enemies would. if the savior made her worthy, who is peter to reject her. then they go out to preach.\nnobody reads this as a record of a real night. karen king, a harvard historian of early christianity who wrote a book about it, reads it as second century christians arguing, through peter and mary, about whether a woman could teach and whether a private vision counts. the only long copy is a papyrus book from the fifth century that a german diplomat bought in egypt in 1896. it wasn't published until 1955, and ten of its pages are missing, six at the start and four in the middle.","status":"approved","title":"in a gospel that isn't in the bible, peter doubts her and levi takes her side.","when":"written in the second century, found in egypt in 1896","where":"the gospel of mary itself is a few pages long and easy to find in translation. karen king's the gospel of mary of magdala: jesus and the first woman apostle, from 2003, gives the text and the arguments about it.","who":["magdalene","peter"],"year":150},{"also":["guru arjan"],"key":"granth","lines":"[[nanak]], the founder of the sikhs, was born in 1469, when [[kabir]], a weaver poet who mocked hindu priests and muslim clerics alike, was probably still alive a few hundred miles away in varanasi, the holy city on the ganges. there's no good evidence they met, though later stories say they did. what they share is the message. one god, no caste, no ranking of people by birth, the temple and the mosque both beside the point.\nin 1604 guru arjan, the fifth sikh guru, fifth in the line of teachers after nanak, compiled the book that became the guru granth sahib, the sikh holy book, and put hundreds of kabir's verses in it next to nanak's hymns. so a weaver from a muslim family has his songs read out every day in every gurdwara, every sikh temple, on earth.\nand they share a death. both stories end with hindus and muslims arguing over the body, one side wanting to cremate it and the other to bury it, then a sheet lifted, and flowers where the man had been. it's the same legend told twice, which tells you what everybody wanted to be true about both of them.","status":"approved","title":"a weaver's songs go into the sikh book, and both men get the same ending.","when":"1604, amritsar, and two old stories about flowers","where":"the guru granth sahib, the section of songs by saints who weren't sikhs, where kabir's verses are. any life of nanak for the flowers.","who":["nanak","kabir"],"year":1604},{"also":[],"key":"grof-hofmann","lines":"in 1954 a package came to the psychiatry department of the university medical school in prague, in what was then czechoslovakia. it was from sandoz, the swiss drug company where [[hofmann|albert hofmann]] had discovered lsd, and it held samples of the drug and a letter telling how he'd found it. sandoz was sending the same thing to clinics and therapists in many places, asking whether it had any real use in psychiatry. [[grof|stanislav grof]] was a medical student volunteering in that department. that, he says, is when he first heard hofmann's name.\nhe wanted to try it right away, and the faculty board ruled that students couldn't be test subjects. so he waited, and volunteered as soon as he graduated. his first session was in 1956. he has said his life was never the same after it.\nthey didn't meet for more than ten years. grof dates it to the late 1960s, when hofmann came to see a newly built psychiatric research center in maryland, where grof and his colleagues were studying therapy with psychedelics, the family of drugs lsd belongs to. grof showed him around washington while the cherry trees were in bloom. in 1984 he interviewed him at esalen, the retreat center on the california coast, and hofmann told him that lsd in the drug scene was the profaning of a sacred substance.\nhofmann put grof in his own book, lsd: my problem child. he lists him among the doctors who first used lsd as an aid in therapy, calls him the czech psychiatrist who emigrated to the united states, and points readers to his 1975 book. grof goes further. he calls hofmann his spiritual father. he last saw him in 2008, at hofmann's house in a village on the swiss border with france, two years after the old man's hundredth birthday. hofmann died of a heart attack four weeks later.","status":"approved","title":"a box from hofmann's company reaches a medical student in prague.","when":"1954 to 2008, prague, maryland and switzerland","where":"grof's foreword to albert hofmann und sein lsd, the 2011 biography by dieter hagenbach and lucius werthmüller, which came out in english as mystic chemist. grof's 1984 interview with hofmann at esalen, printed in the bulletin of the multidisciplinary association for psychedelic studies. hofmann's lsd: my problem child, the chapter on lsd in psychiatry.","who":["grof","hofmann"],"year":1954},{"also":["goethe","marianne von willemer"],"key":"hafiz-goethe","lines":"[[hafiz]] was a persian poet who was born, lived and died in shiraz, in what's now iran. he died in 1390. his poems are about love and wine and showing up hypocrites, and iranians still keep his book in the house, know lines of it by heart, and open it at random for advice. in the spring of 1814, more than four hundred years after he died, a publisher sent goethe, germany's national poet, a new german translation of hafiz's divan, which means his collected poems, in two volumes. the translator was joseph von hammer. goethe was sixty four.\nit hit him hard. by july he was writing what he called poems to hafiz, and by the next may he had a hundred of them. he called hafiz his twin. in one poem he says that even if the whole world went under, hafiz is the one he wants to compete with, and that the two of them should share pleasure and pain like twins. in those same two years he met marianne von willemer, a woman of about thirty, and fell for her. in the book she is suleika, the beloved, and he is hatem. several of the poems in it are hers, which she only revealed later.\nthe west-eastern divan came out in 1819, twelve books of poems, one of them named for hafiz, with goethe's own notes explaining the people and places to his german readers. a bigger edition followed in 1827. in 2000 weimar, the town where goethe lived and died, put up a monument to the two of them. it's two chairs cut from one block of granite, facing each other.","status":"approved","title":"goethe, at sixty four, finds a twin who died in 1390.","when":"1814 to 1819, weimar in germany, answering shiraz four hundred years earlier","where":"the west-eastern divan in the complete annotated english translation published by gingko, which includes goethe's notes and essays. the encyclopaedia iranica's article on goethe, free online, for how he came to hafiz.","who":["hafiz"],"year":1819},{"also":["cornplanter","henry simmons"],"key":"handsome-lake-quakers","lines":"in 1798 cornplanter, a seneca war chief, invited quakers, the pacifist christians from philadelphia, to his town on the allegheny river to teach his people farming and trades. a few young quakers came and lived there. one of them, henry simmons, kept a journal.\nin june 1799 cornplanter's half brother [[handsomelake|handsome lake]], a sick old drinker, collapsed, and woke up with his first vision. simmons wrote it down that summer. that's part of why we know as much as we do about how the code of handsome lake began.\nthe quakers had been preaching sobriety, hard work, and farming, and so did handsome lake. historians have argued ever since about how much he took from them. what's clear is that the code he preached was his own, and seneca, and that it kept the old seneca ceremonies the quakers didn't care about. he may have borrowed their broom. he swept his own house.","status":"approved","title":"the quakers wrote down the vision.","when":"1798 and 1799, cornplanter's town on the allegheny river, pennsylvania","where":"anthony wallace's the death and rebirth of the seneca. the code of handsome lake, as written down by arthur c. parker.","who":["handsomelake"],"year":1799},{"also":["gary snyder"],"key":"hanshan-kerouac","lines":"[[kerouac|jack kerouac]] met gary snyder in berkeley in the fall of 1955, when snyder was a graduate student in tang poetry who'd been turning [[hanshan|han shan]]'s poems into english, and in 1955 and 1956 the two of them shared a cabin in the hills north of san francisco. kerouac wrote it all up as the dharma bums, with snyder as japhy ryder, and when ray smith, kerouac's stand-in, drops by japhy's little house in berkeley, japhy is at his table translating han shan. the old hermit is one of japhy's chief models for how to live as a buddhist, which in the book means a rucksack and a mountain.\nthe book came out on october 2, 1958, dedicated to han shan, at snyder's suggestion. the last pages are a vision of han shan on the mountain. that same autumn snyder's twenty four cold mountain poems came out in the evergreen review with a preface calling han shan a mountain madman in an old chinese line of ragged hermits, and a chinese poet nobody can date became a hero of the beats. paul rouzer, who later translated the whole collection, says han shan's modern fame largely comes from snyder, and that kerouac's novel fictionalized snyder's enthusiasm.","status":"approved","title":"a beatnik dedicates his novel to a hermit who may never have existed.","when":"1955 to 1958, berkeley and new york","where":"the dharma bums, the dedication page and the last pages. snyder's cold mountain poems, with his preface. paul rouzer's introduction to the complete translation.","who":["hanshan","kerouac"],"year":1958},{"also":["abyssinian baptist church","adam clayton powell sr"],"key":"harlem","lines":"[[bonhoeffer]], a young german theologian, came to union seminary, a big school for ministers in new york, on a fellowship and found the classes shallow. what he found instead was abyssinian baptist church in harlem, one of the biggest black churches in the country, where he taught sunday school for a year and heard the spirituals, the songs enslaved people made, and preaching that came out of grief and got to joy without skipping anything.\nhe took recordings of the spirituals back to germany and played them for his students. it's the same black church world that raised [[king|martin luther king]], though king was a baby in atlanta at the time.\na lot of what he later said about costly grace, faith that actually costs you something, he first heard sung by people who'd paid for it.","status":"approved","title":"a german theologian finds god in harlem.","when":"1930 to 1931, new york","where":"any biography. eric metaxas's bonhoeffer and charles marsh's strange glory both tell it. his own letters from that year.","who":["bonhoeffer"],"year":1930},{"also":["timothy leary","ralph metzner"],"key":"harvard","lines":"[[huxley]], the novelist who'd written the doors of perception about his mescaline trip, visited timothy leary, a harvard psychology professor, in 1960 and pointed him at the mystics. in march 1961 leary gave his colleague richard alpert psilocybin, the drug in magic mushrooms, in his kitchen, and alpert, the future [[ramdass|ram dass]], watched his whole identity come apart and something else stay. harvard fired them both in 1963.\n[[watts|alan watts]], the voice of zen on california radio, was in it too. his lsd book, the joyous cosmology, came out in 1962 with a foreword by leary and alpert. in 1964 leary, alpert, and ralph metzner, a grad student in their circle, published the psychedelic experience, a trip manual built on the tibetan book of the dead, the old buddhist guide read to the dying.\nthe drugs were the door, for all three of them. huxley died on lsd. watts drank himself to death. alpert went to india and found the room behind the door, and spent fifty years saying the door was never the point.","status":"approved","title":"a novelist, a psychologist, and a radio host find the same door.","when":"1960 to 1964, cambridge and beyond","where":"the doors of perception. the joyous cosmology. be here now, the first section, which is alpert telling this himself.","who":["huxley","ramdass","watts"],"year":1961},{"also":[],"key":"hillel-jesus","lines":"[[hillel]] taught in jerusalem and died around the year 10, if the usual guess is right. [[jesus]] was a boy then, up north in galilee, and started teaching around the year 30. so they shared a country, and their lives overlapped by a dozen years or so, if the dates hold. no source i've found puts them in the same room. people have speculated that jesus heard him, or picked things up from his school, and speculation is all it is.\nwhat ties them together is one sentence. the talmud, the great record of the rabbis' arguments, finished about five hundred years after hillel, tells of a stranger who asked to be taught the whole torah, the jewish scripture and law, while standing on one foot. hillel said: that which is hateful to you do not do to another; that is the entire torah, and the rest is its interpretation. go study. in the gospel of matthew, in the sermon on the mount, jesus says: in everything do to others as you would have them do to you, for this is the law and the prophets. one says don't and the other says do. both say this one line is the whole of scripture. people call it the golden rule.\nscholars have gone back and forth on whether one got it from the other. the jewish encyclopedia of 1906 took it for granted that hillel's answer was well known in the country and was, in its words, not without its effect on the founder of christianity. later scholars are more careful. geza vermes, an oxford scholar of ancient judaism, pointed out that the do and the don't versions keep turning up side by side in jewish writing before and after the first century. the jesus seminar, a group of scholars who voted on which gospel sayings go back to jesus himself, marked this one gray, their color for words that fit his ideas but probably weren't his, because it's plain common sense and lacks his usual edge. one of them wrote that the closing tag about the law and the prophets sounds like something the writer of matthew learned from hillel.\nthe hillel story has its own catch. it sits in a book finished about five hundred years after he died, so nobody can prove he said it on the day. and the rule was older than both men. leviticus, the third book of the torah, already had love your neighbor as yourself. the book of tobit, a jewish story written around 200 bc, has it in hillel's form: what you hate, do not do to anyone.","status":"approved","title":"two jewish teachers, one rule, and no record they met.","when":"around the year 10 and around the year 30, jerusalem and galilee","where":"shabbat 31a in the talmud, william davidson edition. matthew 7:12, new revised standard version. tobit 4:15. the hillel article in the jewish encyclopedia of 1906.","who":["hillel","jesus"],"year":30},{"also":["julius spier"],"key":"hillesum-rilke","lines":"[[rilke|rainer maria rilke]] died in december 1926. [[hillesum|etty hillesum]] was twelve, a schoolgirl in deventer, a small city in the east of the netherlands. they never met. fifteen years later, in german-occupied amsterdam, she was a jewish woman of twenty seven keeping a diary, and she read him harder than she read anyone. she was fluent in german. people who have gone through her notebooks say he is the writer she quotes most. julius spier, her therapist and then her lover, took her deeper into him. on september 26, 1942, eleven days after spier died, she wrote in the diary that she noticed more and more how rilke had been one of her great teachers of the past year.\nwhen she was getting ready for westerbork, the transit camp the dutch jews were deported through, she fretted over which books would fit in her pack. she settled on the bible, dostoevsky's novel the idiot, her russian dictionaries, and two by rilke. one was letters to a young poet, his advice to a cadet who had sent him poems. the other was the book of hours, poems from his twenties that speak straight to god.\non september 7, 1943, she was put on a train to auschwitz, and she died there that november. whether the rilke was still with her nobody wrote down. the account of her leaving puts the bible and [[tolstoy]] in her rucksack, and the postcard she threw from the train says she was sitting on it.","status":"approved","title":"she picks a dead poet to take to the camp.","when":"1941 to 1943, amsterdam and westerbork, fifteen years after he died","where":"her diaries and letters, complete in etty: the letters and diaries of etty hillesum 1941-1943. the chapter on her and rilke in reading etty hillesum in context, a 2018 collection of studies. letters to a young poet open next to it.","who":["hillesum","rilke"],"year":1942},{"also":["allan richardson"],"key":"huautla-1955","lines":"[[wasson|gordon wasson]], a j.p. morgan vice president who studied mushrooms on the side, and allan richardson, a photographer friend, made it up to huautla de jiménez, a mazatec town high in the mountains of southern mexico. [[sabina|maría sabina]], a curandera, a healer, agreed to let them sit in on a velada, an all-night healing vigil, and eat the mushrooms with her.\nfor her it was the work she'd always done. for him it was the find of his life. two years later he put it in life magazine, the biggest picture magazine in america, and the next ten years of her town went the way you'd expect.","status":"approved","title":"a banker sits up all night in a healer's house.","when":"june 1955, huautla de jiménez, oaxaca","where":"maría sabina: her life and chants, her own account as told to álvaro estrada. then wasson's life magazine article, seeking the magic mushroom, may 13, 1957.","who":["wasson","sabina"],"year":1955},{"also":[],"key":"huxley-leary","lines":"[[huxley|aldous huxley]], who'd written the doors of perception about his mescaline afternoon, spent the fall of 1960 as a visiting professor at mit, down the road from harvard. [[leary|timothy leary]] had just come back from mexico on fire about mushrooms, and the two of them met and talked about the new project that season.\nhuxley's idea was to go quietly, starting with artists, writers, and thinkers, and let it spread slowly. leary went the other way. huxley died three years later, the same year harvard fired leary.","status":"approved","title":"the old novelist tells the young professor to go slow.","when":"the fall of 1960, cambridge, massachusetts","where":"flashbacks, leary's autobiography, which tells it his way. huxley's letters from 1960 to 1962.","who":["leary","huxley"],"year":1960},{"also":["sadr al-din qunawi"],"key":"ibnarabi-rumi","lines":"[[ibnarabi|ibn arabi]], the muslim mystic and writer, settled in damascus in 1223 and died there in 1240. [[rumi]], who became the great persian poet of konya, in what's now turkey, went to damascus to study while the old master was living there, and is said to have stayed four years. so they could have passed in the street. there's a story that they did: ibn arabi sees the young rumi walking behind his father and says, there goes an ocean walking behind a lake. it comes from the saints' lives, not from anyone who was there, and historians say there's no information that the two ever met. almost the same line is given to [[attar]], the poet, in another story about the boy rumi.\nthe link that's on the record is a third man. sadr al-din al-qunawi was the son of a friend of ibn arabi's at the court in anatolia, the land that is most of turkey today. when the friend died ibn arabi took the boy on. most accounts say he married the widow, which would make qunawi his stepson, though that isn't proven. qunawi was trained in ibn arabi's house in damascus and was left the final draft of the meccan openings, his master's biggest book. then he lived and taught in konya, rumi's city.\nwhat passed between qunawi and rumi comes mostly from aflaki, who began writing the lives of rumi and his circle in 1318, forty five years after rumi died, as a devoted member of rumi's order. he says qunawi was against rumi at first and came round after a dream. he says that when rumi was dying he took his medicine from nobody's hand but qunawi's, and that, asked who should lead the prayer at his funeral, he said sadr al-din is the worthiest. neither man mentions the other by name in anything he wrote himself.\nas for the ideas, william chittick, a scholar of both men, says he can't find a single idea in rumi's writing that belongs specifically to ibn arabi. rumi died on december 17, 1273. qunawi died the next year.","status":"approved","title":"two oceans, one city, and no proof they ever met.","when":"damascus before 1240, if it happened at all, and konya up to 1273","where":"william chittick's essay on ibn arabi and rumi. masataka takeshita's paper sadr al-din al-qunawi as seen from a mevlevi point of view, which goes through everything aflaki says about the two of them. mustafa tahrali's article on ibn arabi's influence in the ottoman era, on the ibn arabi society's site, for the damascus legend.","who":["ibnarabi","rumi"],"year":1273},{"also":["abraham","john of damascus"],"fork":{"at":"muhammad","from":["jewish","christian"],"kind":"fork","label":"one more prophet","to":"muslim"},"key":"islam-begins","lines":"in muslim terms, [[muhammad]] did not start a religion. the word islam means submission to god, and muslims hold that this is the one faith god sent again and again, through adam, abraham, [[moses]], [[jesus]] and others, and that muhammad, starting in 610, brought it whole and for the last time. the quran, which muslims hold to be god's own speech, calls it the religion of abraham. it says abraham was neither a jew nor a christian, only a man who gave himself to the one god. muslims believe abraham built the kaaba, the shrine in mecca, as a house of worship.\nso the quran speaks to jews and christians as people god had already spoken to. it calls them people of the book, communities that were given scripture before, the torah through moses and the gospel through jesus. it honors jesus as the messiah, born of the virgin mary, a prophet who worked miracles. and in medina the first muslims prayed facing jerusalem. then, sixteen or seventeen months after the move there, by the report muslims trust most, verses came telling them to turn toward the kaaba. muslims have faced mecca ever since. the reports disagree about which way he faced before medina.\nhere is where the others parted. most of the jews of medina did not accept him as a prophet. by their teaching prophecy had ended centuries earlier, with the last prophets of the hebrew bible, and parts of the quran did not match their scriptures. christians could not accept what the quran says about jesus, that he was not god or the son of god, and that he was not crucified, though it appeared so. for christians those are the center of the faith. a christian text from around 634 already calls him a deceiving prophet, and in the 700s a christian writer named john of damascus listed islam among the heresies, wrong versions of the christian faith. muslims, for their part, hold that the older scriptures had become distorted and that the quran is the final, unaltered word.\nthe quran also calls muhammad the seal of the prophets, which muslims take to mean the last. jews and christians would not accept a prophet after their own, and muslims would not accept that the line had stopped before him. nobody moved. under muslim rule jews and christians kept their religions and paid a special tax for it. the place in medina where the turn is said to have happened is still called the mosque of the two directions.","status":"approved","title":"muslims say muhammad brought back the oldest religion, not a new one.","when":"610 to 632, in mecca and medina, in western arabia. the turn of the prayer direction came sixteen or seventeen months after the move to medina in 622","where":"the quran, chapter 3, verse 67, for abraham. chapter 2, verses 144 to 150, for the turn toward mecca. chapter 33, verse 40, for the seal of the prophets. the count of months is in sahih al-bukhari.","who":["muhammad"],"year":610},{"also":["james, the brother of jesus","rabbi akiva","john chrysostom"],"fork":{"at":"jesus","from":"jewish","kind":"fork","label":"the long split","to":"christian"},"key":"jews-christians","lines":"[[jesus]] was a jew. so were [[peter]], [[paul]] and [[magdalene|mary magdalene]], and they died jews. nobody in the first years thought they had started a new religion. they were jews who believed the messiah, the king the jewish scriptures promised, had come. so when did the two part? a historian named shaye cohen puts it in one line: it was a process, not an event.\nthe first crack was about who could join. around the year 50 the leaders met in jerusalem to decide whether gentiles, non-jews, had to be circumcised and keep the law of [[moses]] to follow jesus. peter, paul and james, the brother of jesus, all spoke, and the answer was no. gentiles only had to stay away from food offered to idols, from blood, from the meat of strangled animals, and from sexual immorality. it sounds like a small ruling. it meant the movement could fill up with people who had never been jews, and it did.\nthen the ground moved under both of them. in 70 the romans destroyed the temple in jerusalem, and each group had to work out how to go on without it. rome put a special tax on jews afterward, and in 96 the emperor nerva changed how that tax worked, which historians say pushed jews and christians further apart in roman eyes. in 132 a jewish commander named bar kokhba led a revolt against rome, and rabbi akiva, the most influential teacher of his day, backed him as the messiah. the christians already had one. early christian writers say his fighters punished christians who wouldn't join.\neven then ordinary people kept crossing back and forth. in 325 the council of nicaea, a meeting of bishops called by the emperor constantine, ruled that easter would be dated by the church's own reckoning and no longer by the jewish calendar. and in 386 and 387 a preacher in antioch named john chrysostom was still shouting at his own congregation for going to synagogue on the sabbath and keeping the jewish feasts. his sermons are hateful about jews, and during the second world war the nazis reprinted them. so it took three hundred years and more, and the last stretch of it was ugly.","status":"approved","title":"the split that took three hundred years.","when":"around 30 to 325, and nobody can name the day","where":"acts 15 and galatians 2, in the new testament, for the meeting in jerusalem. the historian quoted is shaye j. d. cohen.","who":["jesus","peter","paul"],"year":50},{"also":["paramahansa yogananda"],"key":"joy-permeated","lines":"paramahansa yogananda was an indian monk who'd been teaching yoga and meditation in america since 1920, out of a headquarters in los angeles. in 1935 and 1936 he went home to india, and in bengal he met [[anandamayi|anandamayi ma]], the village girl whose trances had made her a saint. he asked her about her life. she told him her awareness had never tied itself to the body, and she'd been the same before she was born, the same as a little girl, the same as a wife, and the same now.\nhe wrote it up as chapter 45 of autobiography of a yogi, his 1946 memoir, and called her the joy-permeated mother. it's the book steve jobs arranged to have handed out at his own memorial. for a lot of americans that chapter was the first time they heard her name.","status":"approved","title":"a yogi from los angeles meets the mother full of joy.","when":"1936, bengal","where":"autobiography of a yogi, chapter 45.","who":["anandamayi"],"year":1936},{"also":["margery kempe"],"key":"julian-margery","lines":"around 1413 a woman named margery kempe came to norwich, a city in england, to see [[julian|julian of norwich]]. julian was an anchoress, a woman who'd had herself shut into a room on the side of a church for life, and she was about seventy. margery was about forty, a townswoman with a husband and at least fourteen children. she had visions of jesus, and she cried so hard and so often that her whole town knew her for it. she wanted somebody who knew the territory to tell her whether any of it was real.\nmargery's book says jesus told her to go. she told the old woman everything, the tears and the revelations, to find out if there was any deceit in them. julian's answer, as margery gives it, was a way to check for herself. the holy ghost, the christian name for god's spirit at work in a person, never moves anything against charity, which means love. someone who's always doubting is like the sea, pushed around by the wind. as for the crying, she passed on a line from an old saint, that tears torment the devil more than the pains of hell. and don't be afraid of what people say. the more spite and shame you get from the world, the more it counts with god.\nthey were together many days, margery says, talking. we only have her side of it. she said she couldn't read or write, so about twenty years later she spoke her whole life aloud to two scribes, calling herself this creature all the way through. then the book dropped out of sight for centuries, known only from a few excerpts, until the one surviving copy turned up in an english family's country house in the early 1930s. it was identified as hers in 1934.","status":"approved","title":"margery kempe asks the anchoress whether her visions are real.","when":"around 1413, norwich, england","where":"the book of margery kempe, book 1, chapter 18. the wording followed here is the modern english version of the passage printed with georgia ronan crampton's 1994 edition of the shewings of julian of norwich.","who":["julian"],"year":1413},{"also":["maria von wedemeyer","adam clayton powell sr."],"key":"kierkegaard-bonhoeffer","lines":"[[kierkegaard|søren kierkegaard]], the danish writer, died in 1855. [[bonhoeffer|dietrich bonhoeffer]], the german pastor, was born in 1906. they never met. one read the other. kierkegaard spent his last year attacking the danish state church in newspaper articles and pamphlets he published himself. his complaint was that it had made being a christian so easy that anyone could be one without knowing what it meant. in 1937 bonhoeffer published the cost of discipleship, which goes after what he calls cheap grace, grace without the cross, and names it the mortal enemy of the church.\nhere's what's established. bonhoeffer owned a german collection of kierkegaard's journals, the notebooks he filled with thousands of pages, that an editor had put together in 1934, and he marked it up. a scholar who went through bonhoeffer's own copy wrote that it's now certain he used it as a direct source for the book. an earlier study had found the book and the collection sharing phrases, cheap grace among them.\nhere's what isn't. nobody can say how much of the book is kierkegaard's. in his own writing bonhoeffer cites only three of kierkegaard's books, and the scholar who wrote a whole book on the two of them warns that tracing influence always stays partly guesswork. there's also a different account of the phrase, that it was coined by adam clayton powell sr., the pastor of abyssinian baptist church in harlem, whom bonhoeffer heard preach when he was studying in new york.\nthe plainest evidence is a letter. in february 1944, from prison, he wrote to maria von wedemeyer, the young woman he was engaged to, about a book she'd been reading that he couldn't stand. he told her he'd welcome it if she took a strong dose of kierkegaard as an antidote, and he named fear and trembling, practice in christianity and the sickness unto death.","status":"approved","title":"bonhoeffer marks up kierkegaard's journals.","when":"1937, germany, eighty two years after kierkegaard died","where":"the letter is in love letters from cell 92, the letters between bonhoeffer and maria von wedemeyer. the scholarship is geffrey kelly's 2008 essay kierkegaard as antidote and as impact on dietrich bonhoeffer's concept of christian discipleship, and matthew kirkpatrick's 2011 book attacks on christendom in a world come of age. the cost of discipleship, chapter 1, for cheap grace itself.","who":["kierkegaard","bonhoeffer"],"year":1937},{"also":[],"key":"krishnamurti-huxley","lines":"[[huxley|aldous huxley]] had moved to california in 1937 and was turning from novels toward the mystics. in 1938 he met [[krishnamurti]], who had been living in ojai, a valley town in southern california, since the 1920s, and the two became close friends, worried together about nationalism and the war coming in europe. they went walking in the mojave desert. in 1939 huxley published his first american novel, after many a summer, with a dedication to krishnamurti, in friendship, and he wrote to a friend that better than books had been some recent conversations with krishnamurti.\nkrishnamurti talked and didn't write. huxley told him to, suggested the shape of a book made from talks with the questions people asked afterward, and took him to harper, his own publisher. the first and last freedom came out in may 1954 with a ten page foreword by huxley and was in its sixth printing by the end of the year. it's still the book of his that has sold the most. huxley had once disagreed with him about the worth of the intellect. in the foreword he sounds like he's come round.\nhuxley died in november 1963. by then he had been to saanen, in switzerland, to hear krishnamurti talk, one of the famous people who turned up there, and he had written island, his 1962 novel, which some readers think owes something to the first and last freedom.","status":"approved","title":"the novelist gets the man who wouldn't write a publisher.","when":"1938 to 1963, los angeles, ojai and the mojave desert","where":"the wikipedia page on the first and last freedom, which tells how the book came about. huxley's letters, including one to christopher isherwood. the aldous huxley society in switzerland has a page on the dedication of after many a summer.","who":["krishnamurti","huxley"],"year":1954},{"also":[],"key":"kushinagar","lines":"[[mingyur|mingyur rinpoche]], a tibetan meditation teacher with monasteries on three continents, walked out of his monastery in june 2011 with almost no money to do the old wandering retreat, leaving everything and living on the road as a beggar monk. not long after he was at kushinagar, the town in india where [[buddha|the buddha]] died, and he got food poisoning from a cheap meal and lay on the ground for days, sure he was dying.\nthe buddha died at kushinagar after a bad meal, twenty five centuries earlier. mingyur knew it. he says that lying there, letting go of everything, was the happiest he'd ever been, and it's the reason the four and a half years that followed worked.\nthe story rhymes on purpose. he'd tell you he didn't plan it.","status":"approved","title":"he nearly dies where the buddha died, of the same thing.","when":"2011, kushinagar","where":"in love with the world, his book about the wandering years, the first hundred pages.","who":["mingyur","buddha"],"year":2011},{"also":["qin shi"],"key":"lao-dan","lines":"in the book of [[zhuangzi]], the chinese joker philosopher, [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the old master behind the tao te ching, shows up as a character called lao dan. in the later chapters he lectures confucius, china's great teacher of duty and manners, more than once. putting the country's most respected teacher in the student's chair was a taoist joke at confucius's expense.\nat the end of chapter 3, lao dan dies. a friend named qin shi comes to mourn, cries three times, and leaves. a disciple asks if that's any way to mourn a friend. qin shi says the old man came when it was his time and went when it was his time, and if you're at peace with that, grief can't get in. the ancients, he says, called it being cut down from the hanging, as if life strings you up and death lets you down.\nthe chinese say lao-zhuang, the two names run together like one. one wrote the short strange book. the other wrote the one with the jokes.","status":"approved","title":"lao tzu dies in zhuangzi's book, and a friend cries three times and leaves.","when":"the fourth century bc, in a book","where":"zhuangzi, chapter 3, the end. the tao te ching next to it.","who":["zhuangzi","laotzu"],"year":-300},{"also":[],"key":"lausanne-1971","lines":"[[leary|timothy leary]] had broken out of a california prison the year before and was living in switzerland. his lawyer set up a meeting with [[hofmann|albert hofmann]], and they met at the snack bar in the lausanne railway station.\nhofmann liked him. he found him charming and sure of his mission, and also blind to practical dangers and unpleasant facts. he'd already written that leary's publicity had pushed lsd onto young people. he said all of it in his book, kindly, and didn't take any of it back.","status":"approved","title":"the father of lsd meets its loudest salesman.","when":"september 3, 1971, lausanne, switzerland","where":"lsd: my problem child, chapter 5, meeting with timothy leary.","who":["hofmann","leary"],"year":1971},{"also":["george custer"],"key":"little-bighorn-1876","lines":"in june 1876 thousands of lakota and cheyenne were camped together in montana. early that month, at a sun dance on rosebud creek, [[sittingbull|sitting bull]], the hunkpapa holy man, saw soldiers falling into the camp upside down. on june 25 george custer's seventh cavalry attacked the camp on the little bighorn river, and custer's command was wiped out.\n[[crazyhorse|crazy horse]], the oglala war leader, fought there. so, at twelve, did his second cousin [[blackelk|black elk]], who'd later tell john neihardt about it for black elk speaks, including scalping a soldier who wasn't quite dead.\nfor the lakota it was the high point and the beginning of the end. within a year crazy horse had surrendered and sitting bull was in canada.","status":"approved","title":"the holy man, the fighter, and a twelve year old boy, in the same camp.","when":"june 1876, montana","where":"black elk speaks, the chapters on the battle. robert utley's the lance and the shield. the national park service site at the battlefield.","who":["sittingbull","crazyhorse","blackelk"],"year":1876},{"also":["martin luther","johann tetzel","philip melanchthon","charles v","zwingli","henry viii"],"fork":{"from":"christian","kind":"fork","label":"1517, luther","to":"christian","year":1517},"key":"luther-1517","lines":"on october 31, 1517, martin luther, the german monk who taught at the university of wittenberg, sent his archbishop the ninety five theses, a list of points for debate. they were about indulgences. in catholic teaching an indulgence takes away punishment still owed for sins already forgiven. it is not forgiveness itself and not a guarantee of heaven. but that year a preacher named johann tetzel was offering them in germany for money, to help build st. peter's basilica in rome, and a saying went around: as soon as the coin in the coffer rings, the soul from purgatory springs. purgatory, in catholic belief, is where a soul is made clean after death. luther went after that saying.\nthe famous picture is luther nailing the list to the door of all saints' church in wittenberg. it may never have happened. the first to tell it was a man named philip melanchthon, and it conflicts with things luther said himself. he wanted a debate among scholars. what he got was printers. before the year was out several hundred copies had been printed in germany.\nrome told him to take it back. in 1520 the pope condemned forty one of his statements and gave him sixty days. luther burned the pope's letter in public, and on january 3, 1521, the pope excommunicated him, put him out of the church. in april he stood before the emperor charles v in the city of worms and said he could not and would not recant. luther's account is that a person is put right with god by faith, as a gift, and that the bible is the only authority. the catholic account is that the pardon sellers had gone past what the church taught and the teaching itself was sound. in 1567 pope pius v cancelled every indulgence that involved money. the church still grants indulgences, and it has never lifted the excommunication.\nit did not stay one movement. a preacher in zurich named zwingli was making his own reform, and the two fought a bitter war of pamphlets over communion. in january 1525 another group began baptising adults again and were named anabaptists, rebaptisers. in 1529 six princes protested a ruling of the empire, which is where the word protestant comes from. in 1534 henry viii ended the pope's authority in england. in 1543, three years before he died, luther wrote on the jews and their lies, which calls for setting synagogues on fire and smashing jewish homes. in 1994 the evangelical lutheran church in america rejected what it called his violent invective. and on october 31, 1999, in augsburg, germany, lutherans and catholics signed a statement that they share a common understanding of how a person is put right with god.","status":"approved","title":"a professor objects to pardons for money, and the western church comes apart.","when":"31 october 1517 in wittenberg, germany, for the ninety five theses. the break with rome ran from then to 1521, and the splitting into many churches went on for decades","where":"the ninety five theses themselves, which are short. the 1994 declaration of the evangelical lutheran church in america to the jewish community. the 1999 statement is the joint declaration on the doctrine of justification.","who":[],"year":1517},{"also":[],"key":"maimonides-ghazali","lines":"[[ghazali|al-ghazali]], the great muslim teacher, died in persia in 1111. [[maimonides]], the rabbi and doctor, was born in cordoba, in muslim spain, a generation later, so they never met. but maimonides grew up, studied and wrote inside the muslim world, in arabic, and one leading scholar says he must have known al-ghazali's work just from where and when he lived.\naround 1190 maimonides finished his book of philosophy and called it, in arabic, dalalat al-ha'irin, the guide for the perplexed. in 1979 a scholar pointed out that al-ghazali had described god as the guide of the perplexed, dalil al-mutahayyirin, and asked whether the title came from there. it's a suggestion, not a settled fact. others have found traces elsewhere. scholars point to a picture of al-ghazali's that maimonides uses too, a boy who'd rather have his toys than sex, to explain why people in bodies can't imagine the joys of the next world. one scholar finds a reference to a book of al-ghazali's in the epistle to yemen, a letter maimonides wrote to jews under pressure there, and argues that al-ghazali's ideas also reached him through the almohads, the dynasty that drove his family out of spain.\nhow much he actually read is harder to pin down. the encyclopaedia judaica says he most probably read the incoherence of the philosophers, al-ghazali's attack on the thinkers who followed aristotle, the ancient greek philosopher, and that it can't be shown for certain. what anyone can check is the table of contents. the ihya, al-ghazali's forty book guide to living islam from the inside, opens with a book of knowledge. the mishneh torah, maimonides' fourteen book code of jewish law, opens with a book of knowledge.","status":"approved","title":"the rabbi's hardest book may carry a muslim teacher's name for god.","when":"around 1190, fustat, in egypt, about eighty years after al-ghazali died","where":"sarah pessin's article the influence of islamic thought on maimonides, in the stanford encyclopedia of philosophy, lists who has argued what. steven harvey's essay alghazali and maimonides and their books of knowledge is the close comparison.","who":["maimonides","ghazali"],"year":1190},{"also":["ibn rushd"],"key":"maimonides-ibn-rushd","lines":"ibn rushd, the muslim judge and philosopher europe calls averroes, was born in cordoba in 1126, the grandson of the city's chief judge. [[maimonides]] was born there in 1138, or 1135 by the older count, the son of a rabbinic judge. in 1148 the almohads, a berber muslim dynasty from north africa, took the city. ibn rushd was in his early twenties. maimonides was ten, or thirteen. none of the sources i could find has the two of them meeting, then or later.\nthe conquest sent them opposite ways. the almohads gave jews the choice of islam, leaving, or death, and the boy's family left, for morocco and then egypt. ibn rushd rose under them. in 1169 he was presented to the almohad caliph and made a judge in seville, then in cordoba, and in 1182 he became the caliph's doctor. maimonides ended up a court doctor in egypt. each was an expert in his own religion's law, and each spent his life on aristotle, the ancient greek philosopher, and on how to read scripture next to him.\nmaimonides did find him, on paper. around 1190, in egypt, he got hold of ibn rushd's commentaries on aristotle. he wrote that he had lately received everything ibn rushd had written on aristotle's works, and that the man was extremely right. scholars disagree on whether he'd read any of it early enough to shape the guide for the perplexed, his own great work of philosophy, finished about then. both had their books burned. in 1195 a tribunal in cordoba condemned ibn rushd's teaching, ordered his works burned and banished him. in the 1230s, friars burned maimonides' books in montpellier, in the south of france. and maimonides' praise sent jewish philosophers to ibn rushd for the next four hundred years. by 1340 nearly all of his commentaries had been put into hebrew.","status":"approved","title":"two boys from cordoba, and one conquest sends them opposite ways.","when":"1148, cordoba, the year the city changed hands","where":"peter adamson's review of the guide in the london review of books sets the two men side by side. the stanford encyclopedia of philosophy's articles on ibn rushd and on the influence of islamic thought on maimonides have the details.","who":["maimonides"],"year":1148},{"also":["john c.h. wu"],"key":"merton-chuang","lines":"[[merton|thomas merton]] was a trappist monk, the strictest kind of catholic monk, known for keeping silence, at an abbey in kentucky, and the most famous monk in america after his memoir became a bestseller. he couldn't read chinese. he worked from several translations of [[zhuangzi]] side by side, with help from john wu, a chinese catholic scholar, over years, and in 1965 published the way of chuang tzu, his own loose versions of the stories.\nin the note at the front he says, roughly, that he enjoyed writing it more than any other book he could remember. the empty boat is in it, where you don't get mad at a boat with nobody in it. so is the useless tree that lived because nobody wanted its wood. that same year he moved out of the monastery into a cinderblock hermitage in the woods.\na monk in kentucky, translating a man who turned down a kingdom to drag his tail in the mud. he knew exactly why he liked him.","status":"approved","title":"a trappist spends years with a chinese joker.","when":"the early sixties to 1965, kentucky","where":"the way of chuang tzu, thomas merton, the note to the reader first.","who":["merton","zhuangzi"],"year":1965},{"also":["louis massignon","abdul aziz"],"key":"merton-hallaj","lines":"[[hallaj|al-hallaj]] was a muslim mystic, someone who goes after god directly, who was put to death in baghdad in 922. he's remembered for saying i am the truth, which a lot of people heard as a man calling himself god. a thousand years later a french catholic scholar, louis massignon, published four volumes on him. in 1959 a mutual friend put massignon in touch with [[merton|thomas merton]], the writer who lived as a trappist, the strictest kind of catholic monk, at the abbey of gethsemani in kentucky. the two never met. it was all letters, and the books massignon sent.\nin the spring of 1960 merton was reading a book of hallaj's prayers and sayings in massignon's french. on march 18 he wrote in his journal: hallaj is right, and our piety is so safe as to be impious. the same day he wrote to massignon that to read hallaj makes one lament and beat his breast.\nhallaj has a line about our hearts being a virgin that only god's truth opens. massignon had a french name for that innermost place, le point vierge, the virgin point, the secret spot in a person where god shows himself. merton took it. in conjectures of a guilty bystander, his 1966 book made from his notebooks, he tells how he stood on a street corner in louisville, kentucky, and suddenly loved everyone walking past. he reaches for le point vierge, says he can't translate it, and describes a point of pure truth at the center of each person that belongs entirely to god. sidney griffith, the scholar who traced the phrase, says it goes back through massignon to hallaj.\nmassignon also gave merton's name to abdul aziz of karachi, a pakistani customs official who studied sufism, the mystical side of islam. he wrote in november 1960, merton answered the same month, and they kept writing until 1968. merton hardly ever told anyone how he himself prayed. he told him. in april 1968 merton wrote that for more than a year he had been giving weekly talks on sufism to the monks at gethsemani. he died that december.","status":"approved","title":"a monk in kentucky reads a man baghdad killed in 922.","when":"march 1960, a monastery in kentucky, and baghdad a thousand years earlier","where":"sidney griffith's essay merton, massignon, and the challenge of islam, in the merton annual, volume 3. the journal line is in a search for solitude, merton's journals, volume 3, page 380, and the letter to massignon is in witness to freedom, his letters, page 276. his letters to abdul aziz are in the hidden ground of love.","who":["merton","hallaj"],"year":1960},{"also":[],"key":"merton-huxley","lines":"in 1937 [[merton]], then a hard-drinking columbia student and not yet a christian, read ends and means, a book of essays by [[huxley]], the english novelist who'd written brave new world. it was the first book that told him mysticism, experiencing god directly, was real and worth wanting. he says so in the seven storey mountain, his memoir. four years later he was a trappist, the strictest kind of catholic monk.\ntwenty years on, huxley had taken mescaline, a psychedelic from the peyote cactus, and written the doors of perception about it. merton wrote to him and said, politely, that he didn't think a drug got you to the same place as prayer. huxley wrote back. neither convinced the other.\nthe book that made the monk, and the monk telling the author he'd taken a wrong turn. they stayed on good terms.","status":"approved","title":"a novelist's book makes a monk, then they argue about drugs.","when":"1937, then 1958","where":"the seven storey mountain, part 2. merton's letters to huxley are in the hidden ground of love, a collection of his letters.","who":["merton","huxley"],"year":1937},{"also":["marpa"],"key":"milarepa-marpa","lines":"[[milarepa]] came to marpa as a man who had killed people with sorcery and now wanted to be taught. marpa was a translator who had spent twelve years studying with teachers in india, and he lived as a married man with his wife and sons in the south of tibet. before he'd teach anything, he put milarepa through abuse and trials. he had him build three towers, one after another, and pull each one down. then he set him to one last tower, several stories high. when he finally accepted him, he said the trials had been the way to clean off what milarepa had done.\nhow much of it happened, nobody can say. the telling everyone knows is the life of milarepa, finished in 1488 by tsangnyön heruka, a wandering yogi called the madman of tsang, roughly three hundred and fifty years after milarepa died. sources differ on milarepa's own dates by a decade or two. tsangnyön worked from older biographies, and his book was one of the first in tibet printed from carved woodblocks, so it spread fast. one scholar says it shows buddhism as tibetans understood it in the fifteenth century, projected back in time. very little is known about milarepa as a historical person at all. in 1505 the same man put together a life of marpa too.\nso the towers come to us from a storyteller, three and a half centuries on. a tower said to be the last one still stands in southern tibet.","status":"approved","title":"marpa makes him build the towers, in a book written centuries later.","when":"the eleventh century, southern tibet, as told in a book from 1488","where":"the life of milarepa, tsangnyön heruka's book, in andrew quintman's translation. quintman's own 2014 study of the biographies is called the yogin and the madman.","who":["milarepa"],"year":1090},{"also":["billy hitchcock","g. gordon liddy"],"key":"millbrook","lines":"after harvard fired them in 1963, [[leary|timothy leary]] and richard alpert, later [[ramdass|ram dass]], moved into a sixty four room mansion on a big estate in millbrook, new york, lent to them by billy hitchcock and his siblings, heirs to a banking fortune. it became a psychedelic commune, part research center, part party, part religious experiment.\nthe local assistant district attorney, g. gordon liddy, later famous for the watergate burglary, raided it in 1966. alpert went to india in 1967 and came back ram dass. leary kept going. the two of them stayed friends and disagreed for the rest of their lives about what the drugs were for.","status":"approved","title":"after harvard, a mansion.","when":"1963 to 1967, millbrook, new york","where":"be here now, the first section, alpert's own story. storming heaven, jay stevens's history of lsd in america, on millbrook.","who":["leary","ramdass"],"year":1963},{"also":["madeleine slade"],"key":"mirabehn","lines":"madeleine slade was the daughter of a british admiral. she read a book about [[gandhi]] by romain rolland, a french novelist who'd won the nobel prize, and in 1925 came to live at his ashram at sabarmati, a community on a riverbank outside the city of ahmedabad. he named her mirabehn, sister mira, after [[mirabai]], the sixteenth century princess who walked out of the palace for the god krishna.\nit was the right name. she'd left an admiral's house for a mud floor, learned to spin cotton on a wheel, which was gandhi's way of getting india to make its own cloth instead of buying british, and went to prison with the movement more than once. and mira's songs were in the ashram's hymnbook, sung at the prayers she knelt through every day.","status":"approved","title":"gandhi names an admiral's daughter after a princess who left the palace.","when":"1925, the sabarmati ashram","where":"mirabehn's autobiography, the spirit's pilgrimage. bapu's letters to mira, for gandhi's side. bapu, father, is what his people called him.","who":["mirabai","gandhi"],"year":1925},{"also":["roger heim"],"key":"mushrooms-basel","lines":"mushrooms from the mazatec country, where [[sabina|maría sabina]] worked, were brought out by [[wasson|gordon wasson]] and the french mushroom expert roger heim, who grew them in his lab in paris. heim sent a batch to [[hofmann|albert hofmann]] at sandoz, the drug company in basel, switzerland.\nin 1958 hofmann pulled out the active chemical, named it psilocybin, and then made it from scratch in the lab. within a couple of years sandoz was shipping it to researchers, including timothy leary's group at harvard. the thing a healer used at night for sick neighbors became a white pill in a bottle.","status":"approved","title":"her mushrooms go into a swiss lab.","when":"1957 to 1958, paris and basel","where":"lsd: my problem child, chapter 6, the sacred mushroom teonanácatl.","who":["hofmann","wasson","sabina"],"year":1958},{"also":[],"key":"my-friend-walt","lines":"as a girl in ohio, getting out of a house she didn't feel safe in, [[oliver|mary oliver]] took [[whitman]], the poet of leaves of grass, into the woods with her. she wrote an essay about it much later, called my friend walt whitman, about him being the friend she needed then.\nthe two of them have more in common than you'd expect, a lot of grass and a lot of walking.","status":"approved","title":"a kid in the woods with whitman in her pocket.","when":"the late forties and fifties, ohio","where":"her essay my friend walt whitman, in her book blue pastures and again in upstream.","who":["oliver","whitman"],"year":1950},{"also":[],"key":"nachman-baalshemtov","lines":"[[baalshemtov|the baal shem tov]] died in medzhybizh, his town in what's now western ukraine, in 1760. twelve years later his granddaughter had a son there, [[nachman]]. so they never met. what the boy got was the town, the grave and the name. nathan of nemirov, nachman's scribe, tells it this way: as a small child, no more than six, he kept going to the grave at night, even in the hard winter frosts, to talk to his great-grandfather and ask for help getting close to god. that is a devoted follower writing years later, so hold the age loosely.\nthe name opened doors and he didn't trust it. his uncle baruch, another grandson, held court in medzhybizh and saw himself as the heir. nachman wanted no part of being an heir. people think i am worthy of this great level because i am a great-grandson of the holy baal shem tov, nathan records him saying. nothing could be further from the truth. he said he had worked for it, with fasts and a great deal of struggle, and that any person could reach what he had reached.\nthen he went further. the scholar zvi mark writes that nachman ranked himself above the holy men who came before him, the baal shem tov included. and louis jacobs, reviewing arthur green's biography tormented master, says he set up a new branch of hasidism in opposition, to some extent at least, to his great-grandfather's teachings. the baal shem tov is buried in medzhybizh. nachman chose to be buried in uman.","status":"approved","title":"the founder's great-grandson says the family name isn't why.","when":"1772 onward, medzhybizh. two men who never met","where":"sichot haran 165 and 166 and shivchei haran 19, nathan's notes, in english as rabbi nachman's wisdom, translated by aryeh kaplan. arthur green's tormented master for the historian's view.","who":["nachman","baalshemtov"],"year":1772},{"also":["guru arjan","guru tegh bahadur","guru gobind singh"],"key":"neither-hindu-nor-muslim","lines":"by 1500 north india had lived under muslim kings in delhi for almost three hundred years, and two kinds of devotion had grown up side by side. one was bhakti, the hindu way of loving god directly, in songs in the everyday languages, open to every caste, the ranks families are born into. the other was sufism, the mystical side of islam. sufi teachers had settled across the north by the 1200s, and their houses drew hindus of every caste as well as muslims. scholars say the two were alike enough to blur, and they still argue about which one shaped the other.\n[[kabir]] came out of that overlap. he was a weaver in varanasi, the hindu holy city, and nearly everything else about him is legend, down to his dates, which historians put at either 1398 to 1448 or 1440 to 1518. the stories say a muslim weaver couple raised him and a hindu teacher taught him. his songs went after what he saw as empty practice in both religions, and in his lifetime hindus and muslims both threatened him for it. after he died both claimed him. a community that follows him, the kabir panth, formed in the 1600s and 1700s and counts about nine and a half million people. many songs under his name were added later by others.\n[[nanak|guru nanak]] was born in 1469 in the punjab. the old accounts of his life, written by followers after he died and full of legend, say he vanished into a river for days and came back saying there is no hindu, there is no muslim. sikhs read that as a statement that people are equal whatever they are called. they do not read it as a plan to merge two religions. historians place nanak in the same stream of devotion as kabir. sikhs hold that his teaching was revealed by god and is not derived from anything. the sikh coalition, a sikh organisation in new york, says it flatly: not a blend of any two or three religions.\nwhat grew from nanak was its own road. nine gurus followed him. two were killed under the mughal emperors, guru arjan in 1606 and guru tegh bahadur in 1675. in 1699 the tenth, guru gobind singh, created the khalsa, the order of initiated sikhs, and before he died in 1708 he named the scripture itself as the guru after him. that book also holds songs by hindu and muslim saints, the sufi saint farid among them. nobody can show that kabir and nanak ever met. the word sikh means student, and there are twenty five to thirty million of them now.","status":"approved","title":"a weaver and a guru say neither label fits.","when":"around 1500, in varanasi and the punjab, in north india. kabir's dates are argued over by forty years, and the sikh path took two more centuries to take its settled shape","where":"the sikh coalition's questions and answers about sikhism, for the sikh account in sikh words. the janamsakhis, the old lives of nanak, for the river.","who":["kabir","nanak"],"year":1500},{"also":["white bird"],"key":"nez-perce-to-canada","lines":"when the nez perce fled in 1877, the place they were running to was canada, because [[sittingbull|sitting bull]] and his lakota had crossed there that spring and the army couldn't follow. [[chiefjoseph|chief joseph]] and his people got to within about forty miles.\njoseph surrendered at the bear paw mountains on october 5, 1877. that night about two hundred and thirty nez perce, joseph's daughter among them, slipped through the army lines with a leader named white bird and made it across. sitting bull's people took them in.\ntwo nations that had often been enemies, sheltering together in grandmother's land because it was the last place left.","status":"approved","title":"the nez perce were running to sitting bull.","when":"october 1877, the bear paw mountains in montana and the canadian border","where":"the national park service pages on the nez perce flight. kent nerburn's chief joseph and the flight of the nez perce.","who":["chiefjoseph","sittingbull"],"year":1877},{"also":[],"key":"nobel","lines":"in january [[king]] wrote to the nobel committee in norway, the people who pick the peace prize, nominating [[thay|thich nhat hanh]] and saying he knew of nobody more worthy. nominations are supposed to stay secret. he said it publicly anyway. no prize was given that year.\non april 4 at riverside church in new york, king came out against the vietnam war in full, in a speech called beyond vietnam. the newspapers, the white house, and most of his own allies turned on him. exactly a year later, to the day, he was shot in memphis.\nnhat hanh's government answered by not letting him come home for thirty nine years. they'd both paid. neither of them took it back.","status":"approved","title":"king nominates a buddhist monk for the peace prize, then pays for it.","when":"january and april 1967","where":"king's nomination letter is published by the king institute at stanford. the riverside speech, beyond vietnam, is the thing to read.","who":["king","thay"],"year":1967},{"also":[],"key":"nouwen-merton","lines":"[[nouwen]] came to america in the sixties and read [[merton]], the kentucky monk whose memoir made him the most famous monk in america, the way a lot of young priests did, as the man who'd shown them a way to pray without leaving the world's trouble outside the door.\na few years after merton died in 1968, nouwen wrote a short book about him, pray to live, later reissued as thomas merton: contemplative critic. nouwen's own later path runs straight through merton's. the genesee diary, about his seven months living as a trappist monk. the writing that told on himself.","status":"approved","title":"the young dutch priest writes a book about the monk.","when":"the early seventies","where":"nouwen's pray to live, or thomas merton: contemplative critic.","who":["nouwen","merton"],"year":1972},{"also":["john f. kennedy"],"key":"november22","lines":"on the afternoon of november 22, 1963, [[cslewis|c.s. lewis]], who wrote the narnia books, died at home outside oxford, a week before his sixty fifth birthday. that same day in los angeles [[huxley|aldous huxley]], who wrote brave new world, died of cancer, with lsd in his veins at his own request, while his wife read to him. in dallas, president john kennedy was shot.\nthe papers had room for one of them. lewis spent his life as an apologist, which means a professional defender of the christian faith. huxley spent his arguing that all the great religions point at the same thing underneath, what he called the perennial philosophy. they'd come at nearly the same question from opposite ends, and they went out quietly on the same afternoon.\npeter kreeft, a catholic philosophy professor, later wrote a little book imagining the three of them arguing somewhere on the other side.","status":"approved","title":"three men die on the same day, and the news only has room for one.","when":"november 22, 1963, oxford and los angeles","where":"peter kreeft's between heaven and hell. then a grief observed, lewis's notebook after his wife died, and island, huxley's last novel, side by side.","who":["cslewis","huxley"],"year":1963},{"also":[],"key":"pema-shantideva","lines":"[[shantideva]] was a monk at nalanda, the great buddhist university in northern india, around the eighth century. the story goes the other monks thought he was a slob who did nothing but eat, sleep and take a shit, and set him up to give a public talk so he'd embarrass himself. he got up and recited a long poem nobody had heard before, the way of the bodhisattva, a handbook for becoming a bodhisattva, someone who puts off their own peace until everybody else gets there too. thirteen hundred years later [[pema|pema chödrön]], an american nun, taught it for years, and in 2005 put out no time to lose, her line by line walk through it.\nchapter 6 is about patience. it says the person who hurts you is where you get to practice, and that one flash of anger burns up a lot of good you've done. she'd thrown a rock at a husband who told her he was cheating. you can see why she kept coming back to it.\nthe lazy monk and the furious divorcee, and the book in between that told both of them to stay put.","status":"approved","title":"a nun who threw a rock teaches the book on patience.","when":"the eighth century and 2005, nalanda and nova scotia","where":"no time to lose, pema chödrön. the way of the bodhisattva, the padmakara translation, open next to it.","who":["pema","shantideva"],"year":2005},{"also":[],"key":"perennial","lines":"the perennial philosophy is an anthology. [[huxley]] took [[eckhart]], the german priest from around 1300, [[rumi]], the persian poet, the [[krishna|gita]], the hindu scripture, [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the chinese sage, the [[buddha|buddhist]] sutras, the buddha's recorded talks, and the christian mystics, and cut them into pieces and laid the pieces next to each other under headings, so you could see them saying the same thing in different accents.\nthe argument is that there's a ground under everything, you're not separate from it, and every tradition at its deepest is about finding that out. the doorway isn't the room.\nit's the book this whole forest is quietly a copy of. huxley did it first, in 1945, with better sources.","status":"approved","title":"huxley puts everyone on the same page.","when":"1945","where":"the perennial philosophy, chapter 1, and then whichever chapter title grabs you.","who":["huxley","eckhart","rumi","laotzu","krishna","buddha"],"year":1945},{"also":[],"key":"pilgrim","lines":"[[gandhi]] had been dead eleven years. [[king]] went anyway, for a month, with his wife coretta, and said on landing that other countries he might visit as a tourist but to india he came as a pilgrim.\nhe met nehru, india's first prime minister, sat with gandhi's people, and saw the places gandhi had lived. what he brought home was the certainty that the method worked at scale, and a discipline about not hating the people you're fighting that he kept until memphis, where he was killed nine years later.\nhe never met gandhi. gandhi never met tolstoy. tolstoy never met jesus. the thread doesn't need them in the room.","status":"approved","title":"king goes to india as a pilgrim.","when":"february 1959","where":"king's essay my trip to the land of gandhi, in ebony, the big black-owned magazine, july 1959. it's in the published king papers.","who":["king","gandhi"],"year":1959},{"also":["anita hofmann"],"key":"pills-huautla","lines":"[[hofmann|albert hofmann]] and his wife anita went to mexico with [[wasson|gordon wasson]] in 1962, and in huautla he gave [[sabina|maría sabina]] synthetic psilocybin, the chemical he'd made from her mushrooms, as pills, for a velada, her all-night healing vigil.\nafterward she told him the pills had the same power as the mushrooms, that there was no difference. he took that as the highest compliment his chemistry ever got. hofmann tells it himself in his book. it's also the moment the little saints could be made in a factory.","status":"approved","title":"the chemist brings the mushroom back in a pill.","when":"the fall of 1962, huautla de jiménez","where":"lsd: my problem child, chapter 6, a mushroom ceremony.","who":["hofmann","sabina","wasson"],"year":1962},{"also":[],"key":"pollan-hopkins","lines":"when [[pollan|michael pollan]], the journalist who'd written about food and plants, started looking into psychedelics, the place he went first was [[griffiths|roland griffiths]]'s lab at johns hopkins. he interviewed griffiths and the volunteers from the cancer studies.\nhow to change your mind, from 2018, opens on that work. griffiths is the steady center of the book, the careful scientist who makes the rest of it credible, and pollan's book is how a lot of people first heard his name.","status":"approved","title":"the food writer sits down with the scientist.","when":"the mid 2010s, baltimore","where":"how to change your mind, chapter 1, a renaissance.","who":["pollan","griffiths"],"year":2015},{"also":[],"key":"psalm22","lines":"on the cross, mark's gospel says, [[jesus]] cried out in aramaic, the everyday language of his time and place, eloi, eloi, lema sabachthani, my god, my god, why have you forsaken me. it's the first line of psalm 22, one of the old hebrew prayer songs, which is headed as a psalm of [[david]], the shepherd king. the people standing there thought he was calling elijah, the old fire-and-thunder prophet people expected to come back.\nthe psalm goes on to people mocking, bones out of joint, and clothes divided up by casting lots, a kind of rolling dice, and john's gospel says the soldiers did exactly that. it also ends somewhere else entirely, in praise, and people have argued for two thousand years about whether quoting the first line meant the whole thing.\nluke, another of the gospels, gives him a different last line, into your hands i commit my spirit. that's psalm 31, also headed as david's. a man dying in the words of a king who'd been at the bottom more than once.","status":"approved","title":"the last thing he said was a psalm.","when":"a psalm from around 1000 bc, a cross around the year 30","where":"mark 15:33 to 37. psalm 22, all of it, in the middle of any bible. then luke 23:46 and psalm 31:5.","who":["david","jesus"],"year":30},{"also":["hasan of basra"],"key":"rabia-hasan","lines":"in the stories, [[rabia|rabia of basra]], the muslim saint who said god should be loved for nothing, has a regular sparring partner. he's hasan of basra, the great preacher of the city, a man known for poverty and the fear of god. in one story he throws his prayer rug onto the water and calls her to come out and pray on it with him. she throws hers into the air, flies up onto it, and tells him to come up there, where people can see. he can't. then she tells him, what you did a fish can do, and what i did a fly can do. the real work is something other than both.\nthere are more. in one he asks her to marry him. she asks him four questions about what happens after death that he can't answer, and says that with those four on her mind, what would she want with a husband. in another he says he spent a whole night and day talking with her, and it never once crossed his mind that he was a man or hers that she was a woman, and that at the end he saw himself as bankrupt and her as the sincere one.\nnearly all of this comes from the memorial of the saints, a persian book of the lives of muslim saints by [[attar]], the poet, written about four hundred years after she died. and the dates don't work. hasan was born in 642 and died in basra in 728. rabia, by the usual dates, was born around 717, which makes her about eleven when he died. margaret smith, the english scholar who wrote a whole book on her in 1928, called hasan as her suitor chronologically almost impossible. there's no evidence on record that the two ever met.\nin every one of these stories she gets the last word. the man she gets it over had been in his grave since she was a girl.","status":"approved","title":"rabia gets the last word on a preacher who died when she was a child.","when":"basra, in what's now southern iraq, in the 700s, as told four hundred years later","where":"margaret smith's rabi'a the mystic and her fellow saints in islam, 1928, free online, which tells the stories and then does the arithmetic. the rabia chapter of attar's memorial of the saints, in any english version, for the legend itself.","who":["rabia"],"year":728},{"also":[],"key":"ramakrishna-doors","lines":"[[ramakrishna]] was a priest of kali, the fierce hindu mother goddess, at a temple on the river outside calcutta. in 1866 he lived as a muslim for a while, under a muslim teacher, prayed the prayers, and had a vision. in 1874 he had someone read him the bible, got obsessed with a painting of mary holding the baby [[jesus]], and had a vision of jesus walking toward him across the temple garden.\nhe came back and said the thing he'd say until he died. as many faiths, so many paths. water is water, whatever word you use.\nhe didn't argue it. he tested it. that's why it landed.","status":"approved","title":"a hindu priest tries the other doors to check.","when":"1866 and 1874, dakshineswar","where":"the gospel of sri ramakrishna, the huge record of his conversations a follower kept. swami nikhilananda's introduction to the english version tells both episodes.","who":["ramakrishna","jesus"],"year":1874},{"also":["auguste rodin","clara westhoff"],"key":"rodin-paris","lines":"in 1902 [[rilke]] went to paris to write a short book on auguste rodin, the most famous sculptor alive, the man who made the thinker. clara, rilke's wife, had studied with rodin. rilke stood in the old man's studio and watched him work every day, whether he felt like it or not, and decided that was the secret.\nthe story goes that rodin told him to go to the zoo at the jardin des plantes, the botanical garden in paris, and look at one animal until he really saw it. rilke went and wrote the panther, the poem about a panther pacing behind bars until the bars are all it sees.\nin 1905 he became rodin's secretary and moved into a cottage at meudon, rodin's place outside paris. in may 1906 rodin fired him, for answering letters on his own. they made it up later. the new poems, from those years, are the ones about things, looked at the way rodin looked at them.","status":"approved","title":"the poet goes to learn how to work.","when":"1902 to 1906, paris and meudon","where":"rilke's book auguste rodin. the panther, in new poems.","who":["rilke"],"year":1902},{"also":["christopher de vinck","george wirth"],"key":"rogers-nouwen","lines":"[[rogers|fred rogers]] and [[nouwen|henri nouwen]] were both ordained men who did their real work somewhere other than a church, one on a children's television show, the other at l'arche daybreak, a home outside toronto where people with intellectual disabilities and their helpers live together. they were friends for the last years of nouwen's life. nobody has written down how they met, as far as i can find. the nouwen archive at the university of toronto keeps rogers's letters to him, photographs of the two of them, and a file on a visit rogers made to daybreak with two companions from july 23 to 25, 1992. rogers remembered nouwen teaching him a short latin song of praise, jubilate deo, at the table there.\nthe letter that survives in print is from april 25, 1989. rogers had been hurt by an article about his work and sent it to nouwen. nouwen wrote back that he could well understand how much it must have hurt, that it made no sense to argue with the writer, and that he felt a solidarity with rogers because his own books got the same kind of reviews. let us pray for each other, he wrote, that we remain faithful and not become bitter. it's in love, henri, the 2016 collection of his letters.\nnouwen died of a heart attack in the netherlands in september 1996. rogers wrote a short piece for a book of memories of him gathered by christopher de vinck. nouwen used to phone him from wherever in the world he was to ask how he was, rogers wrote, and he really wanted to know. henri's death, he wrote, has confirmed for me the enormous power of silence.","status":"approved","title":"the children's host and the priest tell each other not to get bitter.","when":"1989 to 1996, by letter and phone, and at daybreak near toronto in july 1992","where":"love, henri, the collection of nouwen's letters, for the 1989 letter. nouwen then, gathered by christopher de vinck, for rogers's piece, in the journey, we need friends.","who":["rogers","nouwen"],"year":1992},{"also":[],"key":"rohr-bell","lines":"[[rohr]], the catholic priest in albuquerque, built his whole approach on [[merton]], the trappist monk, and says so. the quiet, looking-underneath way of reading the bible, the refusal to pick between working for justice and sitting in prayer, the willingness to say the east got there too. merton's the ghost in rohr's books.\n[[bell]], who'd built a church of ten thousand in michigan, got pushed out of evangelicalism in 2011 for doubting hell, the same year rohr published falling upward, his book about the second half of life. bell has had rohr on his podcast, and the two of them are the main reason a guy raised in church can read ram dass without feeling like a traitor.\na trappist monk, a franciscan priest, and a former megachurch pastor with a podcast. that's the christian side's chain of custody, if you grew up in church and drifted.","status":"approved","title":"the franciscan and the ex-megachurch pastor hold the door open.","when":"the 2010s","where":"falling upward. velvet elvis, bell's first book. the robcast episodes with richard rohr.","who":["rohr","bell","merton"],"year":2011},{"also":["junius rusticus","arrian"],"key":"rusticus-notes","lines":"when [[marcus|marcus aurelius]] was a young man being trained to be emperor, his teacher junius rusticus, a stoic philosopher and senator, lent him his own copy of the notes of [[epictetus]]'s lectures, the ones his student arrian wrote down. epictetus was a former slave with a lame leg who taught that you control what you think about things and nothing else. marcus thanks rusticus for it in the first book of the meditations, his private notebook, right there in the list of the people who made him.\nthen he quotes epictetus all through the notebook, by name. you're a little soul carrying around a corpse, as epictetus used to say. that's book four.\na man who'd been owned, lame, exiled to a provincial town, and the most powerful man in the world reading him in a tent on the danube, rome's northern frontier. neither of them would've found that surprising. that was the point of what epictetus taught.","status":"approved","title":"the emperor learned from a slave.","when":"the 140s, rome","where":"meditations, book 1, section 7, and book 4, section 41.","who":["marcus","epictetus"],"year":145},{"also":["ralph abernathy","ralph bunche"],"key":"selma","lines":"[[heschel]] and [[king|martin luther king]] met at the national conference on religion and race in chicago in january 1963, where heschel opened by saying the first such conference was between pharaoh, the king of egypt, and moses, who told him let my people go. they became friends.\non march 21, 1965, heschel flew to alabama and marched out of selma in the front row, next to king, on the march to montgomery for black americans' right to vote, both of them wearing flower leis. afterward he wrote that even without words the march was worship, and that he felt his legs were praying.\ntwo years later, in april 1967, they both spoke at riverside church in new york against the war in vietnam, at a night sponsored by clergy and laymen concerned about vietnam, an antiwar group heschel had helped start. the hasid from warsaw, the devout mystical jew, and the baptist preacher from atlanta ended up saying the same thing about the same hebrew prophets, the ones like amos who said god wants justice more than worship.","status":"approved","title":"a rabbi in the front row.","when":"january 1963, chicago, then march 1965, selma","where":"moral grandeur and spiritual audacity, heschel's essays, edited by his daughter susannah heschel. taylor branch's at canaan's edge, the third volume of his history of king's years, for selma.","who":["heschel","king"],"year":1965},{"also":["shams of tabriz"],"key":"shams","lines":"[[rumi]], who'd become the best loved poet in the persian language, was then a respected islamic scholar in konya, in what's now turkey, with students and a reputation. shams of tabriz was a wandering dervish, a muslim mystic who lived on the road, and nobody could stand him. shams stopped him in the street and asked him a question, and the versions of the question disagree, and rumi fell off his mule or fainted or just stood there, depending who tells it.\nfor about two years they were inseparable and everyone around rumi hated it. then shams vanished. rumi's students may have killed him. rumi spent the rest of his life writing to him.\nevery poem you've read on a mug is a love letter to a missing friend who might have been murdered by the people who loved the poet. that's the mess under the beauty and it's why the beauty holds.","status":"approved","title":"a wild man stops a professor in the street.","when":"1244, konya","where":"the divan-e shams, the huge book of rumi's poems, which he signed with shams's name. franklin lewis's rumi: past and present, east and west, a scholar's biography, for what actually happened.","who":["rumi"],"year":1244},{"also":[],"key":"sign-of-jonah","lines":"people kept asking [[jesus]] to prove himself. in matthew's gospel, one of the four accounts of his life in the bible, some pharisees, a jewish religious group of his day, and some experts in the law come and say, teacher, we want to see a sign from you. a miracle on demand. he says no. the only sign this generation gets is the sign of the prophet [[jonah]].\njonah is the man in a four chapter book of the hebrew bible who's told to go warn nineveh, a great city god calls wicked. he sails the other way, spends three days and three nights in the belly of a great fish, finally goes, and the city repents. the story is set in the 700s bc. most scholars think it was written long after that, some say the 300s bc or later, and as a story, not as history. either way it was a book centuries old when jesus reached for it.\nthe gospels don't explain the sign the same way. in matthew it's the fish. as jonah was three days and three nights in the belly of the sea monster, he says, so he'll be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth, and christians read that as his time in the tomb. in luke's gospel there's no fish. jonah himself was the sign to the people of nineveh, and jesus will be one to this generation. in mark's gospel, another of the four, he just refuses. no sign, and no jonah.\nmatthew and luke end it on the same line. the people of nineveh will stand up at the judgment and condemn this generation, because they changed when jonah preached, and something greater than jonah is here. in his own book jonah was displeased when that city was spared. jesus holds it up as the one that listened.","status":"approved","title":"asked for a miracle, jesus points to jonah.","when":"around the year 30, as told in matthew's and luke's gospels","where":"matthew 12:38 to 41, and again at 16:1 to 4. luke 11:29 to 32. mark 8:11 and 12 for the version with no jonah. then the book of jonah, all four chapters.","who":["jonah","jesus"],"year":30},{"also":["carlos montezuma"],"key":"society-of-american-indians","lines":"in 1911 [[eastman|charles eastman]] helped found the society of american indians, the first national organization run by native people themselves, mostly doctors, lawyers, teachers, and writers who'd been through the white schools. [[zitkalasa|zitkala-ša]] joined, and in 1916 became its secretary and moved to washington.\nthe thing that tore the society apart was peyote, the cactus used as a sacrament by the peyote road that [[quanahparker|quanah parker]] had helped spread. eastman and zitkala-ša both wanted it suppressed. other members said it was a native religion and deserved the same protection as any church. in 1918 zitkala-ša testified to congress for a ban. the society broke up in 1923.\ntwo of the best native writers of their time, on the side that lost the argument, as it turned out. the native american church is legal and has hundreds of thousands of members.","status":"approved","title":"the doctor and the writer in the first native national group, and the fight over peyote.","when":"1911 to 1923, the society of american indians","where":"the society of american indians' history. zitkala-ša's american indian stories for her side of the school years.","who":["eastman","zitkalasa"],"year":1911},{"also":["manuel fernández de santa cruz, the bishop of puebla"],"key":"sorjuana-teresa","lines":"[[teresaavila|teresa of avila]] died in spain in 1582. [[sorjuana|juana inés]] was born in new spain, spain's colony in mexico, in 1648 or 1651. they never met. but in august 1667, still in her teens and fresh from the viceroy's court, juana tried teresa's order first. she entered the convent of san josé in mexico city, a house of the discalced carmelites, the strict barefoot nuns teresa had started, whose first house in avila was named for saint joseph too. she left in november, after three months, probably because it was too hard, and took her vows with the easier hieronymite nuns instead.\ntwenty four years later she needed her. in 1690 the bishop of puebla had printed one of juana's arguments without asking and told her, under a nun's name, to turn from worldly study. the line she had to get past was from [[paul]], in his first letter to the corinthians, that women should keep silent in the churches. in her reply to sor filotea, dated march 1, 1691, juana asks the obvious question. if that line means women may not write, how is it the church has let teresa write, and three other women she names beside her, and many more? and it can't be because they're saints. teresa wasn't a saint yet when she wrote. she was made one in 1622, forty years after she died. so paul, juana says, was talking about preaching from pulpits and nothing else.\nshe quotes her too, and calls her the holy mother and my mother teresa. after teresa saw the beauty of christ, juana writes, nothing else could pull at her, because everything looked ugly next to it. teresa's reforms had drawn the interest of the inquisition in her day, though no charges were ever laid. in the same reply juana says she wants no trouble with the inquisition and trembles at putting a word wrong. within three years her books were gone.","status":"approved","title":"a nun told to put down her pen calls teresa as her witness.","when":"1691, a convent cell in mexico city, and a saint who had died in spain in 1582","where":"the reply to sor filotea, the pages near the end where she argues about paul. margaret sayers peden's translation is a woman of genius, and alan trueblood's is in a sor juana anthology.","who":["sorjuana","teresaavila"],"year":1691},{"also":["gottfried leibniz"],"key":"spinoza-leibniz","lines":"in november 1676 a thirty year old german named gottfried leibniz came to see [[spinoza|baruch spinoza]] in the hague, the dutch city where spinoza lived. leibniz had spent four years in paris and was on his way, by way of london, to a new job as librarian to a duke in hanover, in germany. he's remembered now as one of the inventors of calculus. spinoza was forty three and had three months to live.\nleibniz already knew a good deal about the ethics, spinoza's big book, which was finished and not yet published, through two friends they had in common. he came ready, with notes for his own proof that god exists. by his own notes they talked about the ethics, about physics, and about that proof. they talked politics too. leibniz is the one who wrote down what spinoza told him about the murder of the de witt brothers, that he'd been moved to go out at night and put up a paper near the spot calling the killers the lowest of barbarians.\nnobody's sure how long it went on. the usual account has them talking over several days. one reference work dates it november 18 to 21, and a modern writer who studied it guesses three to seven days.\nafterward leibniz made it smaller. in 1683 he told a new patron in a letter that he'd spent a couple of hours with spinoza while passing through the hague, and he presented himself there as a fierce critic of the man's thinking. he'd also been carrying a letter for spinoza that a man in london had trusted him with. he deliberately never handed it over, and it's lost.","status":"approved","title":"leibniz spends days with spinoza, then calls it a couple of hours.","when":"november 1676, the hague, in the netherlands","where":"matthew stewart's the courtier and the heretic, a book about the two men and this meeting. the spinoza web, a scholarly site on spinoza's life, quotes leibniz's 1683 letter.","who":["spinoza"],"year":1676},{"also":[],"key":"spring66","lines":"[[thay|thich nhat hanh]], a vietnamese buddhist monk, came to america to ask for an end to the war in his country. in late may he drove to the abbey of gethsemani, the monastery in kentucky, and spent two days with [[merton]], the most famous monk in america, who wrote afterward that nhat hanh was his brother, more his brother than many nearer by race and nationality.\non may 31 he sat down in chicago with [[king|martin luther king]] and told him what the war looked like from a village. king came out against the war publicly the next spring.\nthree men from three traditions, none of them in charge of anything, and the most important conversation in american religion that year happened in a guest room and a hotel.","status":"approved","title":"a vietnamese monk meets a trappist and a baptist in the same month.","when":"may 1966, kentucky and chicago","where":"merton's essay nhat hanh is my brother. nhat hanh's own account in at home in the world, a late book of his stories.","who":["thay","merton","king"],"year":1966},{"also":["abu bakr","ali ibn abi talib","umar","uthman","aisha","muawiya","husayn"],"fork":{"from":"muslim","kind":"fork","label":"sunni and shia","to":"muslim","year":656},"key":"sunni-and-shia","lines":"[[muhammad]] died in medina in 632, and the argument started that day. while his close family was preparing his body for burial, some of the muslims of medina met at a place called the saqifa to pick a leader. abu bakr, his closest friend and his father in law, hurried there with umar, another close companion. umar swore loyalty to abu bakr and the rest followed. that made abu bakr the first caliph, the leader of the muslim community after the prophet. ali ibn abi talib, the prophet's cousin and son in law, was with the family at the body and not in the room. he most likely held back his own pledge for about six months.\nthe sunni account, held by about eighty five in a hundred muslims today, is that muhammad named no successor and left the choice to the community, and that abu bakr rightly succeeded him. sunnis honor the first four caliphs as the rightly guided: abu bakr, then umar, then uthman, then ali. ali is on that list. sunnis did not reject him. they say his turn came fourth.\nthe shia account is that the prophet did choose. in march 632, on the way home from his last pilgrimage, he stopped the caravan at a pond called ghadir khumm and said, he whose mawla i am, ali is his mawla. shia muslims read that arabic word as master, and hold that leadership belonged to ali and then to his descendants, the imams. sunnis accept that he said it. they read it as a statement of love and support for ali and not an appointment. the name shia comes from shiat ali, the party of ali.\nthen it came to blood. in 656 rebels killed uthman and ali was made caliph. that december, near basra in iraq, he fought an army that aisha, the prophet's widow, had joined, and the next year he fought muawiya, the governor of syria, who would not recognise him. in 661 ali was killed while praying in the mosque at kufa, in iraq, and muawiya became caliph. in 680 ali's son husayn, the prophet's grandson, refused to pledge loyalty to the new caliph yazid. an army of thousands cut his small band off from water at karbala, in iraq, and killed him and most of those with him on the tenth of october. both branches count husayn a martyr. shia muslims mourn him every year on ashura, the tenth day of the month of muharram, and historians say karbala is what turned a party into a separate tradition.","status":"approved","title":"muhammad dies and his people disagree about who leads.","when":"632 to 680, in medina, iraq and syria. the argument began the day he died, and it took generations more to harden into two named branches","where":"both branches accept that the words at ghadir khumm were spoken, and they differ on what the words mean. the early muslim histories of the first war among muslims and of karbala carry the rest.","who":["muhammad"],"year":656},{"also":["d.t. suzuki"],"key":"suzuki-eckhart","lines":"d.t. suzuki, the japanese scholar who taught the west what zen was, wrote a book in 1957 called mysticism: christian and buddhist, and the christian he used was [[eckhart]]. a dominican friar, a member of a catholic preaching order, from around 1300 was the closest thing in europe to what suzuki was trying to describe.\n[[merton]], the american trappist monk, read it and started writing to suzuki. they met in new york in 1964, the monk's one trip out of the monastery in years. [[watts|alan watts]], the englishman who'd go on to explain zen on american radio, had been reading suzuki since the thirties, met him in london in 1936, and built most of his zen on him.\nso the line runs from a german mystic, some of whose teachings the pope condemned, to a japanese scholar, and on to a trappist in kentucky and a radio host in california. the church buried eckhart. zen dug him up.","status":"approved","title":"eckhart, seven hundred years dead, explains zen to christians.","when":"1957 to 1964","where":"suzuki's mysticism: christian and buddhist. merton's zen and the birds of appetite, his 1968 book of essays on zen, has the suzuki dialogue.","who":["eckhart","merton","watts"],"year":1957},{"also":[],"key":"teresa-john","lines":"in 1567 [[teresaavila|teresa]] was looking for friars to start a men's branch of her reform, her push to take the carmelites, an order of nuns and friars, back to a poor, strict, prayerful life. in medina del campo she met [[johncross|john]], twenty five, newly ordained, and about to leave for the carthusians, the strictest monks around. she talked him out of it. the next year he opened the first house, a farmhouse at duruelo.\nfrom 1572 he was confessor at her convent of the incarnation in avila, the priest the nuns went to with their sins and their prayer lives, for about five years. the story goes that once, talking through the iron grille that separated the nuns from visitors about the trinity, the christian idea that god is one god and also father, son, and spirit, they were both caught up in ecstasy at the same moment. when john was kidnapped by his own order in december 1577 she wrote to philip ii, the king of spain, begging him to do something.\nshe was the talker and the fixer. he was the poet in the cell. the reform needed both, and so did the two of them.","status":"approved","title":"a fifty two year old nun recruits a tiny friar.","when":"1567 to 1582, medina del campo and avila","where":"teresa's letters, including the one to philip ii in december 1577. the book of her foundations, her account of starting the convents, the chapters on duruelo.","who":["teresaavila","johncross"],"year":1567},{"also":["john wilson","frank eagle","reuben snake","george tinker"],"key":"the-peyote-road","lines":"peyote is a small cactus that native peoples have prayed with for thousands of years. the ceremony came north over many generations from peoples along the mexican border, and in the late 1800s it took hold on reservations in what is now oklahoma, among the kiowa and comanche first. [[quanahparker|quanah parker]], the comanche leader, is the person most closely tied to those early years. the story goes that he came to it after a bull gored him in texas and a mexican healer treated him with it. he passed the ceremony on to john wilson, a caddo and delaware man, and the two ways it is still held go back to the two of them.\nmembers call peyote medicine and a sacrament, a holy thing taken in worship. the all-night meeting is for prayer and for healing. what happens inside it is theirs, and this page leaves it there. how much christianity is in it depends on who holds the meeting. wilson's way brings in jesus and the bible openly. in quanah's way christianity is present but not pressed. a line passed down as quanah's says the white man goes into his church house and talks about jesus, but the indian goes into his tipi and talks to jesus. george tinker, an osage scholar of religion, reads wilson's jesus his own way: not yet christian, but an attempt to take hold of the colonizer's spiritual power inside what was still a traditional indian ritual.\nthe religion was banned in places, and its people answered on paper. on october 10, 1918, a coalition of peyote people from several tribes incorporated the native american church of oklahoma, which gave their religion a legal definition. its first president was a man named frank eagle. quanah had died in 1911. he is counted among the church's first leaders. today it reaches more than fifty tribes, and counts of its members run from a quarter million to four hundred thousand.\nthe law took far longer. in 1978 congress declared it the policy of the united states to protect native religions, with nothing to enforce it. in 1990 the supreme court ruled, in a case from oregon, that a state could prohibit peyote even in ceremony. reuben snake, a ho-chunk leader and a road man of the church, organized the campaign to get a law passed against that ruling. he died in june 1993. on october 6, 1994, president clinton signed amendments to the american indian religious freedom act. they say that the use, possession or transportation of peyote by an indian, for ceremonial purposes in a traditional indian religion, is lawful and shall not be prohibited by the united states or by any state.","status":"approved","title":"the peyote road becomes a church on paper.","when":"the 1880s to 1994, in oklahoma and then across north america. the paperwork has a date, october 10, 1918. the religion's beginning does not","where":"george tinker's note on john wilson's rattle, at the national museum of the american indian. one nation under god, the book on the church by huston smith and reuben snake. the 1994 law is public law 103-344.","who":["quanahparker"],"year":1918},{"also":["sue bailey thurman"],"key":"thurman-king","lines":"[[thurman|howard thurman]] was thirty years older than [[king|martin luther king]], and his wife sue had been at college in atlanta in the same years as king's mother. in 1953 thurman came to boston university as dean of marsh chapel while king was finishing his doctorate there. king heard him preach. they watched the world series on television at the thurmans' house, and sue and king talked seriously about his coming to fellowship church, the interracial church the thurmans had just left in san francisco. that, thurman wrote, was when she discovered his commitment to montgomery, alabama.\nthe famous part is the book. people say king carried a worn copy of jesus and the disinherited, thurman's short book about what jesus has to say to people with their backs against the wall, all through the montgomery bus boycott, the year black montgomery stayed off the segregated buses. it gets repeated everywhere, and it's softer than it sounds. the firmest version is from king's friend and biographer lerone bennett, who wrote only that king read or reread the book during the boycott. jesse jackson said that he, king and other leaders carried it along with the bible. the foreword to the current edition says if it is true, as some accounts indicate. what's solid is that king read it within months of its coming out in 1949 and quoted it, without credit, in a student paper. and in march 1956, three months into the boycott, he wrote to thurman that he'd been told thurman could give them real advice.\n1958 is solid too. a woman stabbed king with a letter opener at a book signing in new york, and thurman came to see him in the hospital. he told king to ask his doctors for two more weeks than they'd planned. it would give him, thurman wrote later, time away from the immediate pressure of the movement, and let him rest his body and mind with healing detachment. king wrote back: i am following your advice on the question.","status":"approved","title":"the old preacher tells king to take two more weeks.","when":"1953 to 1958, boston, montgomery and a hospital in new york","where":"with head and heart, thurman's autobiography, the pages on boston and on 1958. the king institute at stanford has their letters online.","who":["thurman","king"],"year":1958},{"also":[],"key":"tolstoy-gandhi","lines":"[[gandhi]], then a lawyer fighting for indians' rights in south africa, read the kingdom of god is within you by [[tolstoy]], the russian novelist, and said it overwhelmed him. tolstoy had cut the miracles out of the gospels and kept one thing. don't resist evil with violence. love your enemies. [[jesus]] with the furniture removed.\nin 1908 tolstoy wrote a letter to a hindu, a long reply to an indian editor who'd written him, arguing that india could end british rule without firing a shot. gandhi got hold of it, printed it, and wrote to tolstoy. they wrote back and forth until tolstoy died in 1910. gandhi named his commune in south africa, a farm where protesters and their families lived, tolstoy farm.\na jewish carpenter, read by a russian novelist, read by a hindu lawyer, who then got read by [[king|a black baptist preacher in atlanta]]. the thread doesn't care about the labels.","status":"approved","title":"a russian count and an indian lawyer write letters.","when":"1909 to 1910","where":"tolstoy's a letter to a hindu, which is short. the kingdom of god is within you if you have the patience.","who":["tolstoy","gandhi","jesus"],"year":1909},{"also":[],"key":"tolstoy-laotzu","lines":"in his last year [[tolstoy]] put out a little book of sayings by [[laotzu|lao tzu]], the ancient chinese sage behind the tao te ching, chosen and arranged by him. he'd also written about the buddha with respect. by then the man who'd been thrown out of the russian orthodox church didn't much care which door the truth came in through.\nlao tzu's big idea is not forcing things, the soft thing outlasting the hard, water wearing down stone. it's the same idea as tolstoy's refusal to meet violence with violence. he knew it.","status":"approved","title":"the count, at the end, publishes lao tzu.","when":"1910","where":"tolstoy's calendar of wisdom, a book he put together with a reading for every day of the year, has lao tzu all through it.","who":["tolstoy","laotzu"],"year":1910},{"also":["charles wilkins"],"key":"walden-gita","lines":"[[thoreau]] had the [[krishna|bhagavad gita]] with him at walden, the hindu scripture where the god krishna talks a prince through his terror on the morning of a battle. his copy was charles wilkins's translation from 1785, the first one into english, and he read it in the mornings. he said so in walden, in the chapter on the pond in winter.\nthat winter men came to cut the ice off the pond in blocks to ship it south. thoreau heard it went as far as india, and he imagined the water of walden melting into the ganges, india's holy river, where a hindu priest was sitting under a tree with the same book he was reading.\na yankee in a cabin and a two thousand year old conversation on a battlefield. the book gets around.","status":"approved","title":"the ice from walden goes to india.","when":"the winter of 1846 and 1847, walden pond","where":"walden, the chapter the pond in winter. the gita, chapter 2.","who":["thoreau","krishna"],"year":1846},{"also":[],"key":"whirlwind","lines":"[[job]] is the man in the old hebrew book who loses his children, his money, and his health in about a week, for no reason he's ever given. he spends thirty chapters sitting in the ashes demanding that god answer for it. god finally speaks out of a whirlwind and answers nothing. he shows job the ocean, the stars, the war horse, the sea monster. scale instead of reasons.\nin the [[krishna|gita]], the hindu scripture that's one long conversation on a battlefield, the warrior prince arjuna asks his chariot driver, who turns out to be the god krishna, to show himself as he really is. krishna does. every face, every mouth, all of time eating itself. arjuna begs him to put the disguise back on.\ntwo books that never heard of each other, from opposite ends of asia, and the same answer to the same question. the question was why. the answer was look.","status":"approved","title":"god shows up and doesn't explain.","when":"two old books, one move","where":"job 38 through 42, near the middle of any bible. bhagavad gita chapter 11.","who":["job","krishna"],"year":-300},{"also":["buffalo bill","annie oakley"],"key":"wild-west-show","lines":"buffalo bill's wild west was william cody's traveling show of cowboys, sharpshooters, stagecoach robberies and real native people acting out battles, and it played to huge crowds in america and europe. a lot of lakota men took the job. it paid, and it got you off the reservation.\n[[sittingbull|sitting bull]] toured with it for four months in 1885, for about fifty dollars a week, and sold signed photographs of himself. [[blackelk|black elk]] joined in 1886 to see the white world, performed for queen victoria in london in 1887, got stranded in europe, and made it home in 1889. [[standingbear|luther standing bear]] toured in 1902 and 1903, including in england, until the show's train wrecked outside chicago and nearly killed him. [[foolscrow|frank fools crow]], black elk's nephew, traveled with it as a young man.\nnone of them were in the show at the same time, as far as i know. but it's the same show, and it's strange to think of the white world getting its picture of the lakota from men who were also, on the side, some of the most serious holy men and writers their people had.","status":"approved","title":"four lakota men worked for buffalo bill, in different years, and each came home.","when":"1885 to 1903, the united states and europe","where":"black elk speaks, the chapters on the show and grandmother england. luther standing bear's my people the sioux. thomas mails's fools crow.","who":["sittingbull","blackelk","standingbear","foolscrow"],"year":1885},{"also":["russell means"],"key":"wounded-knee-1973","lines":"on february 27, 1973, the american indian movement and local oglala took over the village of wounded knee on the pine ridge reservation, where the massacre had happened in 1890, and federal agents surrounded them for seventy one days.\n[[foolscrow|frank fools crow]], the oglala ceremonial chief, was the senior elder behind the protest, took their case to the united nations, and helped negotiate the end. [[lamedeer|john fire lame deer]], the sicangu holy man and sacred clown, took part in the protests of those years too, wounded knee among them.\ntwo men born before the first world war, who'd both grown up in the shadow of the 1890 massacre, standing with kids a third their age at the same creek.","status":"approved","title":"two old holy men at the second wounded knee.","when":"february to may 1973, wounded knee, south dakota","where":"the wikipedia pages on the wounded knee occupation. thomas mails's fools crow.","who":["foolscrow","lamedeer"],"year":1973},{"also":["elaine goodale"],"key":"wounded-knee-chapel","lines":"on december 29, 1890, soldiers killed hundreds of lakota at wounded knee creek on the pine ridge reservation, many of them women and children. [[blackelk|black elk]], then twenty seven, heard the shooting and rode toward it, and saw the dead in the gulch. he was wounded in the fighting that followed.\n[[eastman|charles eastman]], a santee dakota doctor fresh out of medical school, was the government physician at pine ridge. the wounded were brought to the episcopal mission chapel, where the christmas tree was still up, and he and the missionaries pulled out the pews, laid down hay, and worked. when the blizzard cleared he rode out with the search party and found a few still alive under the snow, a baby girl among them.\nelaine goodale, the white teacher eastman married the next year, worked beside him. i don't know that black elk and eastman ever met. they were two dakota and lakota men of the same generation standing in the same snow, one a holy man who'd stay on the reservation all his life, the other a christian doctor who'd spend his explaining that holy man's world to white america.","status":"approved","title":"one of them rode into it, and the other took the wounded in.","when":"december 29, 1890, to january 1891, pine ridge, south dakota","where":"charles eastman's from the deep woods to civilization, the chapters on pine ridge. black elk speaks, the chapters on wounded knee.","who":["eastman","blackelk"],"year":1890},{"also":["marcus borg"],"key":"wright-borg","lines":"[[wright|tom wright]] and marcus borg had been friends since their oxford days. borg was a professor in oregon and a leading liberal scholar of jesus. wright was the conservative englishman. in 1999 they published the meaning of jesus: two visions, taking turns chapter by chapter on the birth, the death, whether he was god, and whether he rose.\nearly that year they argued it out in public at the national cathedral in washington, and a television crew caught the heart of it. borg: i do believe in the resurrection of jesus. i'm just skeptical that it involved anything happening to his corpse. wright: was jesus bodily raised from the dead or not? and i say yes, and marcus says no. neither one budged. wright went on recommending borg's books anyway, and sometimes lectured beside him.\nborg died in january 2015, at seventy two. wright told the new york times there had been a deep and rich mutual affection and friendship between them. two men who disagreed about the biggest claim in their religion and wouldn't let it cost them each other.","status":"approved","title":"two friends argue about whether the tomb was empty, and stay friends.","when":"1999, a book and a cathedral in washington","where":"the meaning of jesus: two visions, by marcus borg and n.t. wright, 1999.","who":["wright"],"year":1999},{"also":[],"key":"wright-lewis","lines":"[[cslewis|c.s. lewis]] died in november 1963, nine days before [[wright|tom wright]] turned fifteen. nothing on record says they ever met. but wright says that in his late teens and early twenties he read everything of lewis's he could get his hands on, some of it several times over, while his oxford tutors looked down their noses if you so much as mentioned the man. i owe lewis a great debt, he wrote.\nin 2007, in an essay called simply lewis, he went back through mere christianity, lewis's plain explanation of the faith, and graded it. the part on how christians should behave he called the finest. then the marks came off. lewis's famous argument that jesus must have been bad or mad or god drastically short-circuits the argument, wright said, because it skips the jewish world jesus lived in, where people already believed god had come to live among them, in the temple in jerusalem. he found lewis frustratingly fuzzy on heaven and immortality, too close to plato, and he pointed to the astonishing absence of the resurrection from lewis's pages on jesus.\nthen he ended it the way a student writes to a teacher. as another imperfect apologist, he wrote, i salute a great master. the year before, he had published his own plain book about the faith and called it simply christian.","status":"approved","title":"the bishop grades mere christianity, sixty years on.","when":"2007, in a magazine, two men who never met","where":"simply lewis: reflections on a master apologist after 60 years, touchstone magazine, march 2007. mere christianity open next to it.","who":["wright","cslewis"],"year":2007},{"also":[],"key":"wright-paul","lines":"[[paul]] wrote his letters in the middle of the first century to a handful of small churches. [[wright|tom wright]] has spent his whole working life on them. a lecture in 1978 offering a new way of looking at paul. a doctorate in 1981 on the letter to the romans. in 2013, paul and the faithfulness of god, more than sixteen hundred pages arguing that paul took the three things every jew of his day held to, one god, one people of god, one future for the world, and rebuilt each of them around jesus.\nthen in 2018, at sixty nine, he wrote the short one, paul: a biography, and went after the man. he thinks the trip to arabia after the road to damascus was a trip to mount sinai, the way elijah went. he gives paul ten quiet years back home in tarsus. and he proposes a jail term in ephesus, one the book of acts never mentions, where paul broke down and wrote some of his letters. that last one is wright's hypothesis, not something the bible records.\nhis paul didn't start a new religion and wasn't mainly telling people how to get to heaven. he was a jew announcing that the messiah had come and that god's kingdom was arriving on earth. some scholars of paul say wright leans on his favorite themes and leaves the rest out. read a man's mail for forty years and you may start finishing his sentences.","status":"approved","title":"a bishop spends forty years reading another man's letters.","when":"1978 to 2018, forty years on one man's mail","where":"paul: a biography, 2018. paul's own letters next to it, philippians first.","who":["wright","paul"],"year":2018},{"also":["laura huxley"],"key":"zurich-1961","lines":"one morning in august 1961 [[hofmann|albert hofmann]] got a phone call at the lab. it was [[huxley|aldous huxley]], passing through zurich with his wife, inviting the hofmanns to lunch at a hotel. huxley, who'd written the doors of perception about mescaline, knew exactly who had made lsd.\ntwo years later huxley, dying of cancer in los angeles, asked his wife laura for lsd, and she gave it to him. hofmann wrote about the lunch warmly in his book.","status":"approved","title":"the novelist calls the chemist at work.","when":"august 1961, zurich","where":"lsd: my problem child, chapter 8, meeting with aldous huxley.","who":["hofmann","huxley"],"year":1961}]};
